window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"NE-1":{"unit":"NE-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01","title":"Neural Tube Embryology and Its Malformations","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the central nervous system from a flat sheet of ectoderm to a closed tube, then to the vesicles that become the adult brain and ventricles, and to the three tissues that supply its cells. Each developmental step is paired with the malformation that follows when it fails: open neural tube defects when the neuropores do not close, holoprosencephaly when the forebrain does not divide, and the Chiari and Dandy-Walker malformations when the hindbrain and posterior fossa develop abnormally. Board questions give a screening result, a facial or skin finding, or an imaging description and ask you to name the step that failed. Timing, signals, and tissue of origin let you reason from finding to mechanism.","prerequisites":["Gastrulation, the trilaminar germ disc, and formation of the notochord (week 3)","Gross organization of the brain: hemispheres, diencephalon, brainstem, cerebellum, and ventricles","Folate and one-carbon metabolism in thymidylate and purine synthesis","Meiotic nondisjunction and the autosomal trisomies","Basic signal transduction: ligands, receptors, and transcription factors"],"connections":[{"to":"Neuron and nerve histology (NE-2)","how":"The glial cells whose origins are set here (oligodendrocytes, astrocytes, Schwann cells, microglia) respond differently to injury because they come from different tissues."},{"to":"CNS blood supply and CSF flow (NE-10)","how":"The ventricles are the persistent lumen of the neural tube, so the CSF pathway follows the vesicles and explains where hydrocephalus begins."},{"to":"Diseases of increased intracranial fluid (NE-20-C)","how":"Aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, and Dandy-Walker malformation are developmental causes of obstructive hydrocephalus."},{"to":"Neurogenetics (NE-16)","how":"Trisomy 13 and single-gene defects in the sonic hedgehog pathway are major causes of holoprosencephaly."},{"to":"Congenital gastrointestinal disease (GI-13-H)","how":"Hirschsprung disease is a failure of neural crest cells to colonize the distal colon, a direct consequence of crest migration described here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Neurulation","def":"Formation of the neural tube from the neural plate during weeks 3 and 4."},{"term":"Notochord","def":"A rod of axial mesoderm that induces the neural plate and later persists only as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs."},{"term":"Neural plate","def":"The thickened strip of dorsal ectoderm (neuroectoderm) that forms over the notochord and folds into the neural tube."},{"term":"Neural fold","def":"The raised lateral edge of the neural plate; paired folds meet and fuse in the midline to close the tube."},{"term":"Neural crest","def":"Cells that detach from the crests of the neural folds during fusion and migrate to form peripheral neurons, glia, and many non-neural tissues."},{"term":"Neuropore","def":"One of the two open ends of the neural tube, cranial and caudal, which are the last parts of the tube to close."},{"term":"Brain vesicle","def":"A dilation of the cranial neural tube; three primary vesicles divide into five secondary vesicles that form the adult brain."},{"term":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)","def":"The main fetal plasma protein, made by the yolk sac and fetal liver, which leaks into amniotic fluid and maternal blood from open neural tube defects."},{"term":"Sonic hedgehog (SHH)","def":"A secreted, cholesterol-modified signaling protein that specifies ventral midline tissue in the neural tube, forebrain, and face."},{"term":"Holoprosencephaly","def":"Failure of the prosencephalon to divide into two cerebral hemispheres, with accompanying midline facial defects."},{"term":"Syringomyelia","def":"A fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord, most often cervical and often associated with Chiari I malformation."},{"term":"Posterior fossa","def":"The compartment of the cranial cavity below the tentorium that holds the cerebellum, pons, medulla, and fourth ventricle and opens at the foramen magnum."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C01","obj":"NE-1.1","title":"Neurulation: induction, folding, fusion, and neuropore closure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neurulation is the process by which ectoderm on the dorsal surface of the embryo becomes the neural tube, the precursor of the brain and spinal cord. It begins in week 3, while gastrulation is still adding mesoderm at the caudal end of the embryo, and the tube is closed by the end of week 4."},{"type":"p","text":"The notochord, a rod of axial mesoderm formed during gastrulation, and the prechordal mesoderm in front of it induce the overlying ectoderm to become neural. Ectoderm exposed to bone morphogenetic protein 4 (BMP4) becomes epidermis. The notochord and prechordal mesoderm secrete the BMP antagonists noggin, chordin, and follistatin, which bind BMP4 and keep it from its receptors, and ectoderm released from BMP signaling adopts its default neural fate. The result, at about day 18 to 19, is the neural plate: a thickened strip of pseudostratified neuroectoderm lying directly over the notochord and broader at its cranial end."},{"type":"p","text":"The lateral edges of the plate rise as neural folds, leaving the neural groove in the midline between them. Elevation is driven partly by the plate cells themselves, which constrict their apical surfaces with actin and myosin and become wedge shaped at hinge points, and partly by expansion of the adjacent mesoderm and surface ectoderm. The folds first meet and fuse in the future cervical region, at about the level of the fifth somite, around day 22. Fusion then proceeds both cranially and caudally. As the folds fuse, surface ectoderm separates from the tube and closes over it as future epidermis, and cells at the crests of the folds detach as neural crest."},{"type":"flow","title":"Neurulation and neuropore closure","steps":["Notochord secretes noggin, chordin, and follistatin","BMP4 blocked; ectoderm forms neural plate (about day 18 to 19)","Plate edges rise as neural folds around the neural groove","Folds fuse first in future cervical region (about day 22)","Fusion spreads cranially and caudally; neural crest detaches","Cranial neuropore closes about day 25","Caudal neuropore closes about day 28"],"caption":"Fusion starts in the middle of the tube and spreads toward both ends, so the two neuropores are the last openings to seal and the commonest sites of failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Until fusion is complete, the two ends of the tube remain open to the amniotic cavity as the cranial (anterior) and caudal (posterior) neuropores. The cranial neuropore closes at about day 25 and the caudal neuropore at about day 28. As the last sites to close, they are the commonest sites of failure: failed cranial closure produces anencephaly, and failed caudal closure produces lumbosacral myelomeningocele (C04). Once both close, the lumen is a sealed cavity that becomes the ventricles and the central canal. The lowest sacral and coccygeal cord segments form afterward by secondary neurulation, in which a solid mass of tail bud cells hollows out and joins the primary tube."},{"type":"p","text":"The closed tube is then patterned dorsoventrally. Sonic hedgehog (SHH) from the notochord and from the floor plate of the tube directs ventral cells to become motor neurons (basal plate), while BMPs from the roof plate and overlying ectoderm specify dorsal sensory neurons (alar plate). This is why motor nuclei lie ventral, or medial in the brainstem, and sensory nuclei lie dorsal, or lateral."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A teratogen acts only on day 32 of development. Could it cause anencephaly or an open myelomeningocele?","explain":"No. Both neuropores have closed by about day 28, and an open neural tube defect requires interference with closure itself. An insult after closure can damage the formed brain or cord but cannot reopen the tube.","check":"Compare with the neuropore closure days in the flow and the paragraph that follows it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C02","obj":"NE-1.2","title":"Brain vesicles, their adult derivatives, and the ventricles","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"By the end of week 4, as the cranial neuropore closes, the cranial end of the tube expands into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), mesencephalon (midbrain), and rhombencephalon (hindbrain). The caudal tube stays narrow and becomes the spinal cord. The tube also bends at three flexures: the cephalic flexure at the midbrain and the cervical flexure at the hindbrain and cord junction, both concave ventrally, and the pontine flexure between them, which bends the opposite way and spreads the thin roof of the hindbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"In week 5 two of the primary vesicles divide, giving five secondary vesicles. The prosencephalon divides into the telencephalon, whose paired lateral outgrowths become the cerebral hemispheres, and the diencephalon. The rhombencephalon divides into the metencephalon and myelencephalon. The mesencephalon does not divide. Each secondary vesicle produces a fixed set of adult structures."},{"type":"table","title":"Brain vesicles and their adult derivatives","cols":["Primary vesicle","Secondary vesicle","Adult structures","Cavity"],"rows":[["Prosencephalon","Telencephalon","Cerebral cortex and white matter, caudate and putamen, hippocampus, amygdala, olfactory bulbs","Lateral ventricles"],["Prosencephalon","Diencephalon","Thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus with pineal gland, retina and optic nerve, posterior pituitary","Third ventricle"],["Mesencephalon","Mesencephalon","Midbrain: tectum (colliculi), tegmentum, cerebral peduncles","Cerebral aqueduct"],["Rhombencephalon","Metencephalon","Pons and cerebellum","Upper fourth ventricle"],["Rhombencephalon","Myelencephalon","Medulla oblongata","Lower fourth ventricle"],["Caudal neural tube","None","Spinal cord","Central canal"]],"caption":"The lumen of each vesicle persists as a ventricle, so the cavity identifies the vesicle of origin."},{"type":"p","text":"The lumen of the tube is never lost; it widens or narrows with the wall around it. The lateral ventricles are the cavities of the hemispheres and drain through the interventricular foramina (of Monro) into the third ventricle of the diencephalon. The walls of the mesencephalon thicken greatly, so its lumen narrows to the cerebral aqueduct, which joins the fourth ventricle of the rhombencephalon. The roof of the fourth ventricle, thinned by the pontine flexure, carries choroid plexus and later opens through the median and lateral apertures into the subarachnoid space. The aqueduct is the narrowest channel in the system, so aqueductal stenosis is a classic cause of congenital obstructive hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"Several structures that seem separate from the brain are outgrowths of the diencephalon. The optic vesicle forms the retina and optic nerve, so the optic nerve is a CNS tract myelinated by oligodendrocytes and can be affected by multiple sclerosis. The infundibulum grows down from the diencephalic floor to form the posterior pituitary, and the pineal gland arises from its roof. The anterior pituitary, by contrast, comes from Rathke pouch, an outgrowth of oral ectoderm, not from the neural tube."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an obstruction by which ventricles are enlarged","text":"A fetal MRI shows enlarged lateral and third ventricles and a normal fourth ventricle. CSF is made mainly by the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricles and flows through the interventricular foramina to the third ventricle, through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle, and out through its apertures. Every space upstream of a block dilates and every space downstream does not. The lateral and third ventricles are enlarged, so the block lies beyond the third ventricle; the fourth is normal, so the block lies before it. The only channel between them is the aqueduct, the lumen of the mesencephalon, so this is aqueductal stenosis. If the fourth ventricle were also greatly dilated, the obstruction would lie at its outlet, as in Dandy-Walker malformation (C06)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C03","obj":"NE-1.3","title":"Neuroectoderm, neural crest, and mesoderm derivatives","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nervous system draws its cells from three sources, and the source of a cell predicts where it lives, how it behaves, and which diseases arise from it. Neuroectoderm is the wall of the neural tube itself. Neural crest is the population that detaches from the crests of the neural folds during fusion. Mesoderm contributes the microglia, the dura, and the blood vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroepithelial cells lining the tube divide in the ventricular zone and produce, first, neuroblasts, which become all CNS neurons, and later glioblasts, which become astrocytes and oligodendrocytes. Radial glia act both as progenitors and as scaffolds along which young neurons migrate outward to form the cortex. Cells that remain lining the lumen become ependyma, the ciliated epithelium of the ventricles and central canal, and specialized ependyma forms the epithelium of the choroid plexus. The retina, the glia of the optic nerve, the posterior pituitary, and the pineal gland are also neuroectoderm because they grow out of the tube (C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"Neural crest cells undergo an epithelial to mesenchymal transition: they lose their E-cadherin junctions, become motile, and migrate along defined pathways. In the trunk they form the sensory neurons of the dorsal root ganglia, the postganglionic neurons of sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric ganglia, the Schwann cells and satellite cells of peripheral nerves and ganglia, the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, and melanocytes. In the head they also form much of the facial skeleton and connective tissue, sensory ganglia of several cranial nerves (with ectodermal placodes), odontoblasts, and, in cardiac crest, the aorticopulmonary septum. The adrenal medulla shows how origin explains function: chromaffin cells are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons, so they receive preganglionic cholinergic fibers directly and release mainly epinephrine into the blood rather than onto a target organ."},{"type":"p","text":"Microglia are the exception among CNS glia. They derive from yolk sac macrophage progenitors (mesoderm) that colonize the brain early, so they belong to the mononuclear phagocyte lineage: they phagocytose debris, present antigen, and are the main CNS cells infected by HIV, fusing into multinucleated giant cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Cell origins in the nervous system","cols":["Origin","Derivatives","Disease examples"],"rows":[["Neuroectoderm (wall of the neural tube)","CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependyma, choroid plexus epithelium, retina, posterior pituitary, pineal gland","Multiple sclerosis (oligodendrocytes), astrocytoma, ependymoma, retinoblastoma"],["Neural crest","Dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia, autonomic and enteric ganglia, Schwann and satellite cells, adrenal medulla, melanocytes, facial skeleton, odontoblasts, aorticopulmonary septum","Hirschsprung disease, neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma, melanoma, schwannoma"],["Mesoderm","Microglia (yolk sac macrophage lineage), dura mater, CNS blood vessels","Multinucleated giant cells of HIV encephalitis (microglia)"],["Surface ectoderm (for contrast)","Anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), lens, epidermis, inner ear and olfactory epithelium (placodes)","Craniopharyngioma (Rathke pouch remnant)"]],"caption":"Cells that stay inside the brain and cord come from the tube; cells scattered in the periphery come from crest; microglia come from mesoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"The two myelinating cells show why origin matters clinically. Oligodendrocytes (neuroectoderm) each myelinate segments of many CNS axons and are the target in multiple sclerosis. Schwann cells (neural crest) each myelinate a single internode of one peripheral axon, support axon regeneration, are the target in Guillain-Barre syndrome, and give rise to schwannomas. Because crest cells migrate, disorders of crest migration or proliferation appear far from the neural tube: Hirschsprung disease when crest fails to reach the distal colon, neuroblastoma and pheochromocytoma in the adrenal medulla, and melanoma in the skin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has an adrenal tumor that secretes catecholamines. Does it share a tissue of origin with an ependymoma of the fourth ventricle?","explain":"No. Adrenal medullary tumors (neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma) arise from neural crest cells that migrated away from the tube, whereas ependymoma arises from neuroectoderm that stayed lining the lumen.","check":"Compare with the neural crest and neuroectoderm rows of the cell origin table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C04","obj":"NE-1.4","title":"Neural tube defects, alpha-fetoprotein, and folate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural tube defects result from failure of the tube to close, or from incomplete formation of its coverings. The closed tube induces the surrounding mesoderm to form the vertebral arches and skull vault, so an open tube leaves the bony covering incomplete. The defects are ranked by how much neural tissue is involved and whether skin covers it, which determines both the neurological outcome and the screening result."},{"type":"table","title":"Neural tube defects compared","cols":["Defect","What is in the defect","Skin covered","Amniotic AFP and AChE","Typical findings"],"rows":[["Spina bifida occulta","Unfused vertebral arch only, usually L5 or S1","Yes","Normal","Usually silent; hair tuft, dimple, or lipoma over the defect"],["Meningocele","Meninges and CSF; cord in place","Usually","Normal if skin covered; raised if open","Neurological function usually normal"],["Myelomeningocele","Meninges plus cord or roots, usually lumbosacral","Usually not","Raised","Deficits below the level, neurogenic bladder, Chiari II, hydrocephalus"],["Anencephaly","No skull vault; exposed, degenerated forebrain","No","Raised","Polyhydramnios; not compatible with survival"]],"caption":"Only defects exposing neural tissue to amniotic fluid raise AFP and AChE."},{"type":"p","text":"Myelomeningocele is the most common serious open defect and usually reflects failed closure of the caudal neuropore. The exposed cord and roots are further damaged by amniotic fluid, so function is lost below the level: lower motor neuron leg weakness, sensory loss, and neurogenic bladder and bowel from involvement of S2 to S4. Nearly every child with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele also has a Chiari II malformation, and most develop hydrocephalus (C06). Anencephaly is the cranial counterpart: the exposed forebrain degenerates, the skull vault does not form, and impaired fetal swallowing causes polyhydramnios."},{"type":"p","text":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is the main fetal plasma protein, made by the yolk sac and then the fetal liver. Normally little reaches the amniotic fluid, mainly through fetal urine. In an open defect, fetal plasma leaks from exposed neural tissue and its vessels, raising amniotic AFP, and more AFP then crosses to the mother, raising maternal serum AFP (MSAFP), usually screened at about 15 to 20 weeks. Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), a neural enzyme not normally detectable in amniotic fluid, appears only when neural tissue is exposed, so it is more specific. Skin-covered lesions leave both values normal."},{"type":"flow","title":"From folate deficiency to raised maternal serum AFP","steps":["Low folate slows DNA synthesis in dividing neural folds","Caudal neuropore fails to close by about day 28","Vertebral arches cannot form over the open tube","Exposed neural tissue leaks AFP and acetylcholinesterase","Amniotic fluid AFP and acetylcholinesterase rise","AFP crosses to the mother, raising maternal serum AFP"],"caption":"Each step explains one finding."},{"type":"p","text":"Folate deficiency is the best established modifiable risk factor. Tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon units: N5,N10-methylene tetrahydrofolate donates the carbon thymidylate synthase uses to make dTMP, and N10-formyl tetrahydrofolate supplies two carbons of the purine ring, so limited folate slows DNA synthesis in the rapidly dividing neural folds. Folate also remethylates homocysteine to methionine for DNA methylation, and raised maternal homocysteine and some MTHFR variants increase risk. Because the tube closes by day 28, the mother's folate status at conception is what counts, and periconceptional folic acid substantially lowers risk. Other risk factors are valproate (which interferes with folate metabolism and inhibits histone deacetylases), pregestational diabetes, obesity, and hyperthermia in early pregnancy."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting a raised maternal serum AFP","text":"An MSAFP is reported as 2.8 multiples of the median (MoM). AFP rises through the second trimester, so each result is compared with the median for that gestational age; about 2.0 to 2.5 MoM is the usual cutoff. First ask whether the value is truly high: a pregnancy further along than recorded makes a normal value look raised, and twins double the fetal source. With both confirmed, the remaining causes are leaks of fetal plasma: an open neural tube defect, gastroschisis or omphalocele, or fetal death. Raised amniotic acetylcholinesterase points to exposed neural tissue. A low MSAFP is instead associated with trisomy 21 and 18."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C05","obj":"NE-1.5","title":"Holoprosencephaly, sonic hedgehog, and the midline face","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Holoprosencephaly is failure of the prosencephalon to divide into two cerebral hemispheres with two lateral ventricles, a division that normally takes place at about weeks 5 to 6. It is a spectrum. In alobar holoprosencephaly there is a single midline ventricle, fused thalami, and no interhemispheric fissure or corpus callosum. In semilobar forms the posterior hemispheres are partly separated, and in lobar forms the hemispheres are largely separate, with fusion limited to the basal frontal region. Olfactory bulbs and tracts are often absent in severe forms."},{"type":"p","text":"The forebrain divides because its ventral midline is specified by signals from the prechordal plate, the mesoderm just cranial to the notochord. The key signal is sonic hedgehog (SHH). SHH binds its receptor Patched, which relieves Patched inhibition of Smoothened and activates GLI transcription factors that drive midline gene expression. The resulting midline tissue separates the single forebrain field into right and left halves. Loss of function anywhere along this pathway can produce holoprosencephaly: mutations in SHH itself or in other midline genes such as ZIC2 and SIX3, defective cholesterol synthesis, and teratogens. Cholesterol matters because SHH is covalently attached to cholesterol during its processing and Smoothened activity depends on sterols, so Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (7-dehydrocholesterol reductase deficiency) can cause holoprosencephaly. Cyclopamine, a plant alkaloid that blocks Smoothened, produces cyclopia in grazing animals."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sonic hedgehog and division of the forebrain","steps":["Prechordal plate and ventral forebrain secrete cholesterol-modified SHH","SHH binds Patched, releasing Smoothened to activate GLI","Midline tissue forms in the ventral forebrain and midface","Single eye field splits into two eye primordia","Prosencephalon divides into two telencephalic hemispheres"],"caption":"Loss of any step (SHH, ZIC2, or SIX3 mutation, trisomy 13, defective cholesterol synthesis, or Smoothened blockade) leaves a single forebrain and a deficient midface."},{"type":"p","text":"Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome) is the most common chromosomal cause, and holoprosencephaly is one of its characteristic features alongside microphthalmia, cleft lip and palate, postaxial polydactyly, scalp defects (cutis aplasia), congenital heart disease, and severe intellectual disability. Holoprosencephaly also occurs with other chromosome abnormalities, including trisomy 18, and is more common in infants of mothers with pregestational diabetes."},{"type":"p","text":"The face is involved because the same midline signal shapes the midface. Early on, a single eye field lies across the anterior neural plate, and SHH from the prechordal plate suppresses its midline portion so that two eyes form. The nasal septum, philtrum, and premaxilla develop from the medial nasal prominences, which also depend on midline signaling. With too little midline tissue the eyes lie close together (hypotelorism) or fuse into a single median eye (cyclopia, often with a proboscis above it), the nose may have a single nostril, the premaxilla is absent, giving a median cleft lip, and a single central maxillary incisor may be the only sign of the mildest form. Because brain and face share one signal, the severity of the facial defect usually parallels the severity of the brain defect. The common lateral cleft lip is different: it reflects failed fusion of the maxillary prominence with the merged medial nasal prominences and does not by itself imply holoprosencephaly."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C06","obj":"NE-1.6","title":"Chiari I, Chiari II, and Dandy-Walker malformations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The three posterior fossa malformations are defined by where the cerebellum lies relative to the foramen magnum, by fossa size, and by the effect on CSF flow."},{"type":"table","title":"Posterior fossa malformations compared","cols":["Feature","Chiari I","Chiari II","Dandy-Walker"],"rows":[["Posterior fossa","Small","Small","Enlarged"],["Displaced or missing tissue","Tonsils at least about 5 mm below the foramen magnum","Vermis, fourth ventricle, and medulla","Vermis absent or hypoplastic; no herniation"],["Fourth ventricle","Normal position","Elongated, small, displaced downward","Dilated into a cyst"],["Neural tube defect","No","Lumbosacral myelomeningocele, almost always","No"],["Hydrocephalus","Uncommon","Common","Common"],["Usual presentation","Adolescence or adulthood; cough headache","Birth; open spine, stridor, apnea","Infancy; enlarging head"]],"caption":"Fossa size separates Dandy-Walker from both Chiari forms; the open spine and extra displaced structures separate Chiari II from Chiari I."},{"type":"p","text":"In Chiari I malformation the cerebellar tonsils extend at least about 5 mm below the foramen magnum, usually because the posterior fossa is too small for its contents (underdeveloped occipital bone, a paraxial mesoderm derivative). The brainstem and fourth ventricle stay in place, and there is no neural tube defect. It is often incidental or causes occipital headache worsened by coughing or straining, which raises intracranial pressure and pushes the tonsils down. Crowding at the foramen magnum impairs the normal pulsatile movement of CSF between cranial and spinal subarachnoid spaces, and the altered pressure waves are thought to drive fluid into the cord, forming a cervical syrinx (syringomyelia) in a substantial proportion of patients."},{"type":"p","text":"A cervical syrinx expands from the center of the cord. It first interrupts the pain and temperature fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure, giving bilateral loss of pain and temperature in a cape over the shoulders and arms while the dorsal columns, and therefore fine touch, vibration, and proprioception, are spared (dissociated sensory loss). As it enlarges it destroys anterior horn cells, causing lower motor neuron weakness and wasting of the hand muscles, and compresses the lateral corticospinal tracts, causing upper motor neuron signs in the legs."},{"type":"p","text":"Chiari II malformation is congenital and more severe. The posterior fossa is small, and the vermis, fourth ventricle, and medulla are displaced through the foramen magnum, with kinking of the medulla and beaking of the tectum. It occurs almost only with an open lumbosacral myelomeningocele, and the leading hypothesis links the two (flow below). Displaced tissue blocks outflow from the fourth ventricle, and aqueductal stenosis is common, causing obstructive hydrocephalus. Stretch on the brainstem and lower cranial nerves explains neonatal stridor from vocal cord paresis, swallowing difficulty, and apnea."},{"type":"flow","title":"Leading hypothesis for Chiari II malformation","steps":["Caudal neuropore fails: open lumbosacral myelomeningocele","CSF escapes through the open spinal defect","Ventricles stay undistended; posterior fossa fails to enlarge","Growing hindbrain herniates through the foramen magnum","Fourth ventricle outflow blocked; hydrocephalus develops"],"caption":"CSF lost through the open defect cannot distend the developing posterior fossa."},{"type":"p","text":"Dandy-Walker malformation is the opposite in fossa size. The vermis is absent or hypoplastic, especially its inferior part, the fourth ventricle is dilated into a large cyst filling an enlarged posterior fossa, and the tentorium and confluence of sinuses are displaced upward. It is thought to arise from abnormal development of the roof of the rhombencephalon, with delayed or failed opening of the fourth ventricle outflow. Most patients develop hydrocephalus and present in infancy with an enlarging head and prominent occiput; ataxia and developmental delay vary, and agenesis of the corpus callosum often coexists."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An adult has wasting of the hand muscles and lost pain and temperature over both shoulders, with normal vibration sense. Which malformation, and which fibers fail first?","explain":"Chiari I with a cervical syrinx. The cavity first cuts spinothalamic fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure, sparing the dorsal columns, then reaches the anterior horns.","check":"The Chiari I and syrinx paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural tube development links biochemistry, signaling, and cell biology. Folate one-carbon chemistry supplies thymidylate and purines for the dividing neural folds, so maternal folate status at conception, MTHFR variants, and drugs that interfere with folate, such as valproate, alter the risk of open defects. SHH, which depends on cholesterol, divides the forebrain and the face together, which is why trisomy 13, Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome, and Smoothened blockade all produce holoprosencephaly with midline facial defects. Cell origin predicts where disease appears: crest tumors and migration failures (neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma, melanoma, Hirschsprung disease) arise outside the CNS, oligodendrocyte disease spares peripheral nerve, Schwann cell disease spares the brain, and mesodermal microglia are the CNS cells HIV infects."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and pathology connect these steps to findings. The ventricles are the persistent lumen of the tube, so hydrocephalus is localized by which ventricles dilate, and the posterior fossa malformations are separated by fossa size and CSF flow. There are limits: MSAFP is a screening probability affected by dating, twins, and abdominal wall defects; the hydrodynamic explanations for Chiari II and for syrinx formation are well supported but not proven; and thresholds such as the 5 mm tonsillar descent are imaging conventions rather than sharp biological boundaries."}],"summary":["Notochord and prechordal mesoderm block BMP4 with noggin, chordin, and follistatin, so the overlying ectoderm becomes the neural plate in week 3.","Neural folds fuse first in the future cervical region and close toward both ends; the cranial neuropore closes about day 25 and the caudal about day 28.","Three primary vesicles become five secondary vesicles, and each lumen persists as a ventricle: lateral (telencephalon), third (diencephalon), aqueduct (mesencephalon), fourth (rhombencephalon).","Retina, optic nerve, posterior pituitary, and pineal are diencephalic outgrowths; the anterior pituitary is oral ectoderm.","Neuroectoderm forms CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma; neural crest forms peripheral ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, and melanocytes; microglia are mesodermal.","Open defects (myelomeningocele, anencephaly) raise amniotic and maternal AFP and amniotic acetylcholinesterase; skin-covered defects do not.","Folate supplies one-carbon units for thymidylate and purine synthesis in the rapidly dividing neural folds, so maternal folate status at conception sets risk.","Holoprosencephaly is failed forebrain division from defective SHH midline signaling (SHH mutation, trisomy 13, cholesterol synthesis defects), and the face parallels the brain.","Chiari I is tonsillar descent with a small fossa and syringomyelia; Chiari II is vermis and medulla descent with myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus.","Dandy-Walker is vermis agenesis with a cystic fourth ventricle in an enlarged posterior fossa, usually with hydrocephalus."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, malformations and developmental diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue and the Nervous System"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Folic acid and one-carbon metabolism; nucleotide synthesis"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, vertebral column and spinal cord; Head, cranial cavity"}]},"NE-10":{"unit":"NE-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01","title":"Blood Supply of the Central Nervous System and Cerebrospinal Fluid Flow","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows blood into the brain through the carotid and vertebrobasilar systems, maps each arterial territory onto the structures it feeds, and uses that map to predict cortical, lacunar, and brainstem stroke syndromes. It then traces cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from secretion to absorption, follows venous blood out through the dural sinuses, and explains how the brain holds its blood flow steady and what happens when intracranial pressure rises. Board questions give a deficit, a ventricle pattern, or a set of vital signs and ask you to name the vessel, the site of obstruction, or the mechanism. Localization depends on knowing what lies where and which artery reaches it.","prerequisites":["Lobes, gyri, motor and sensory homunculi, and language areas (NE-3)","Brainstem cross-sectional anatomy: cranial nerve nuclei and long tracts (NE-3, NE-7, NE-8)","Meninges, meningeal spaces, cisterns, and the blood-brain barrier (NE-4)","Mean arterial pressure, Poiseuille relationship, and the baroreceptor reflex (CV units)","CO2 transport and the bicarbonate buffer system"],"connections":[{"to":"Meninges and CSF compartments (NE-4)","how":"The spaces CSF fills and the hemorrhages that occupy them are described there; this chapter adds the flow path and what blocks it."},{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Atherosclerosis, embolism, and hypertensive arteriolosclerosis produce the territorial and lacunar infarcts whose deficits are predicted here."},{"to":"Diseases of increased intracranial fluid (NE-20-C)","how":"Hydrocephalus, cerebral edema, and herniation are the pathologies that raise intracranial pressure and lower perfusion pressure."},{"to":"Respiratory physiology","how":"PaCO2, set by alveolar ventilation, is the strongest local regulator of cerebral vessel diameter, so breathing pattern changes brain blood volume."},{"to":"Cardiovascular regulation (CV units)","how":"The Cushing reflex combines the CNS ischemic response with the arterial baroreceptor reflex."}],"terms":[{"term":"Circle of Willis","def":"The arterial ring at the base of the brain that links the two internal carotid systems with each other and with the vertebrobasilar system through communicating arteries."},{"term":"End artery","def":"An artery whose territory has no effective collateral supply, so its occlusion infarcts the tissue it feeds."},{"term":"Watershed zone","def":"The border region between two arterial territories, the area most vulnerable when overall perfusion pressure falls."},{"term":"Lacunar infarct","def":"A small deep infarct (under about 15 mm) from occlusion of a single penetrating artery, usually from hypertensive small vessel disease."},{"term":"Crossed findings","def":"Cranial nerve signs on the side of a brainstem lesion combined with long tract signs on the opposite side of the body."},{"term":"Noncommunicating hydrocephalus","def":"Ventricular dilation from obstruction within the ventricular system or its outlets, so the ventricles no longer communicate with the subarachnoid space."},{"term":"Communicating hydrocephalus","def":"Dilation of all four ventricles from impaired CSF absorption or flow in the subarachnoid space, with open ventricular pathways."},{"term":"Dural venous sinus","def":"A valveless, endothelium-lined channel between the periosteal and meningeal layers of the dura that collects cerebral venous blood and CSF."},{"term":"Bridging vein","def":"A superficial cerebral vein that crosses the subdural space to enter a dural sinus."},{"term":"Autoregulation","def":"The intrinsic ability of cerebral arterioles to keep blood flow nearly constant across a wide range of perfusion pressure."},{"term":"Cerebral perfusion pressure","def":"The pressure that drives blood through the brain, calculated as mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure."},{"term":"Cushing reflex","def":"Systemic hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular breathing produced when raised intracranial pressure makes the brainstem ischemic."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01-C01","obj":"NE-10.1","title":"The circle of Willis and the cerebral arterial territories","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brain receives about 15% of resting cardiac output through two paired inflows. The internal carotid arteries form the anterior circulation; the vertebral arteries, branches of the subclavian arteries that ascend through the transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae and enter the skull through the foramen magnum, form the posterior circulation. The two vertebral arteries unite at the pontomedullary junction to form the basilar artery, which runs up the ventral pons and divides at the midbrain into the two posterior cerebral arteries (PCA)."},{"type":"p","text":"Each internal carotid artery ends by dividing into the anterior cerebral artery (ACA) and the larger middle cerebral artery (MCA). The circle of Willis closes the ring: the anterior communicating artery joins the two ACAs, and each posterior communicating artery joins an internal carotid artery to the PCA on its side. A complete, well-sized circle is present in only a minority of people, so collateral capacity varies widely."},{"type":"flow","title":"Assembly of the circle of Willis","steps":["Vertebral arteries ascend and enter through the foramen magnum","Vertebrals unite at the pontomedullary junction as the basilar","Basilar divides at the midbrain into the two PCAs","Posterior communicating arteries join each PCA to the internal carotid","Each internal carotid divides into ACA and MCA","Anterior communicating artery joins the two ACAs"],"caption":"The ring links right and left carotid systems with the vertebrobasilar system."},{"type":"p","text":"The circle protects against slow, proximal occlusion. When one internal carotid artery narrows gradually in the neck, flow can arrive from the opposite side through the anterior communicating artery or from the basilar through a posterior communicating artery. It cannot protect against sudden occlusion distal to the ring. Branches beyond the circle overlap only at their borders, and the penetrating arteries that enter deep gray matter are functional end arteries, so an embolus there produces a predictable infarct."},{"type":"table","title":"Cerebral arterial territories","cols":["Artery","Origin","Territory"],"rows":[["ACA","Internal carotid","Medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes back to the parieto-occipital sulcus, including the paracentral lobule (leg and foot cortex); most of the corpus callosum"],["MCA","Internal carotid (larger terminal branch)","Most of the lateral convexity of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes, including face and arm cortex, Broca and Wernicke areas, and the insula"],["Lenticulostriate arteries","Proximal stem of the MCA","Putamen, part of the globus pallidus and caudate, and much of the internal capsule"],["PCA","Terminal branches of the basilar","Occipital lobe including primary visual cortex, inferior and medial temporal lobe, splenium of the corpus callosum, thalamus and midbrain through perforating branches"]],"caption":"Superficial branches supply cortex and underlying white matter; deep perforators supply the basal ganglia, internal capsule, and thalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"The lenticulostriate arteries leave the MCA stem at nearly right angles and are small, so they receive nearly the full pressure of a large parent artery. Chronic hypertension thickens their walls with hyaline arteriolosclerosis and lipohyalinosis, which leads either to occlusion (lacunar infarct) or to rupture (hypertensive hemorrhage, most often in the putamen). The watershed zones between ACA and MCA and between MCA and PCA lie at the farthest reach of each tree, so they infarct first when systemic pressure falls, as in cardiac arrest or severe hypotension."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01-C02","obj":"NE-10.2","title":"Predicting cerebral and lacunar stroke syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A stroke deficit is the function of the tissue that loses its blood supply. The motor and sensory homunculi are draped across two territories: the leg and foot area lies on the medial surface in the paracentral lobule, supplied by the ACA, while the trunk, arm, hand, and face areas lie on the lateral convexity, supplied by the MCA. The descending motor pathway crosses in the lower medulla and the ascending sensory pathways cross below the cortex, so a hemispheric infarct produces contralateral signs."},{"type":"p","text":"ACA occlusion therefore causes contralateral weakness and sensory loss of the leg more than the arm, with the face largely spared. Damage to the medial frontal lobe can add urinary incontinence and abulia (reduced drive and spontaneity). MCA occlusion causes contralateral face and arm weakness and sensory loss with less leg involvement. The face weakness is an upper motor neuron pattern that spares the forehead, because the facial nucleus neurons for the forehead receive corticobulbar input from both hemispheres. Damage to the frontal eye field turns the eyes toward the side of the lesion, and involvement of the optic radiation can add a contralateral visual field defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Which hemisphere is involved decides the cortical signs. Language is lateralized to the left hemisphere in nearly all right-handed people and most left-handed people. A dominant MCA infarct causes aphasia: superior division infarcts damage Broca area in the inferior frontal gyrus (nonfluent, effortful speech with relatively preserved comprehension), inferior division infarcts damage Wernicke area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus (fluent speech with poor comprehension), and stem occlusion causes global aphasia. A nondominant MCA infarct causes contralateral hemineglect, often with denial of the deficit."},{"type":"p","text":"PCA occlusion infarcts the primary visual cortex and causes a contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Central vision is often spared because the occipital pole, where the macula is represented, frequently receives collateral flow from the MCA. A dominant PCA infarct that also involves the splenium can cause alexia without agraphia, and thalamic involvement adds contralateral sensory loss."},{"type":"table","title":"Cerebral stroke syndromes","cols":["Vessel","Motor and sensory pattern","Other findings"],"rows":[["ACA","Contralateral leg and foot more than arm; face spared","Urinary incontinence, abulia, frontal release signs"],["MCA","Contralateral face and arm more than leg; lower face only","Aphasia if dominant; hemineglect if nondominant; gaze toward lesion; field defect"],["PCA","Usually none; thalamic sensory loss if perforators involved","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing"],["Lenticulostriate (lacune in posterior limb of internal capsule)","Pure motor hemiparesis of face, arm, and leg equally","No aphasia, neglect, or field defect"]],"caption":"Leg-predominant weakness points medially (ACA); face and arm predominance with cortical signs points laterally (MCA)."},{"type":"p","text":"A lacunar infarct in the posterior limb of the internal capsule shows the opposite pattern from a cortical stroke. Corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers for face, arm, and leg are packed into a few millimeters, so one small infarct weakens all three equally, while cortex is untouched and there is no aphasia, neglect, or field loss. The same small vessel disease in thalamic perforators causes pure sensory stroke."},{"type":"example","title":"Cortex or capsule?","text":"A right-handed man has sudden weakness of the right face and arm, with the right leg only mildly weak, and he speaks in short, halting phrases but follows commands. Step 1: face and arm more than leg places the lesion on the lateral convexity, the MCA territory, not the ACA. Step 2: the deficit is on the right, so the lesion is in the left hemisphere. Step 3: impaired speech output with preserved comprehension is Broca aphasia, the inferior frontal gyrus, supplied by the superior division of the left MCA. If his face, arm, and leg were equally weak with normal language, the lesion would instead be a lacune in the left internal capsule."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01-C03","obj":"NE-10.3","title":"Brainstem vascular syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The vertebrobasilar system feeds the brainstem through two kinds of branches. Paramedian branches (the anterior spinal artery in the medulla and pontine branches of the basilar) supply medial structures, and circumferential branches (posterior inferior cerebellar artery, PICA, from the vertebral; anterior inferior cerebellar artery, AICA, from the lower basilar; superior cerebellar artery from the upper basilar) supply the lateral brainstem and cerebellum."},{"type":"p","text":"Localization uses two rules. First, structures are arranged medially and laterally: the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus are medial, while the spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract, vestibular nuclei, nucleus ambiguus, cerebellar peduncles, and descending hypothalamic sympathetic fibers are lateral. Second, cranial nerve nuclei act on the same side, while the long tracts serve the opposite side of the body because they have already crossed (spinothalamic tract, medial lemniscus) or will cross below (corticospinal tract). One lesion therefore produces crossed findings, and the cranial nerve sign gives the level and side."},{"type":"table","title":"Brainstem stroke syndromes","cols":["Vessel and syndrome","Ipsilateral (cranial nerve and lateral) signs","Contralateral (long tract) signs","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["PICA or vertebral: lateral medullary","Loss of face pain and temperature (spinal trigeminal); Horner syndrome; dysphagia, hoarseness, reduced gag (nucleus ambiguus); limb ataxia (inferior cerebellar peduncle); vertigo, nystagmus, vomiting (vestibular nuclei)","Loss of body pain and temperature (spinothalamic)","Dysphagia and hoarseness; no limb weakness"],["Anterior spinal: medial medullary","Tongue weakness, deviates toward the lesion (CN XII)","Hemiparesis of arm and leg (pyramid); loss of vibration and position sense (medial lemniscus)","Tongue deviation"],["AICA: lateral pontine","Whole-face lower motor neuron paralysis (facial nucleus); hearing loss and tinnitus (cochlear nuclei, labyrinthine artery); vertigo; face pain and temperature loss; Horner syndrome; ataxia (middle cerebellar peduncle)","Loss of body pain and temperature","Facial paralysis and deafness"],["Basilar: ventral pons (locked-in)","Bilateral: loss of facial, tongue, and swallowing movement; horizontal gaze often lost","Bilateral: quadriplegia","Consciousness, blinking, and vertical eye movement preserved"]],"caption":"Medial syndromes take motor tracts and a motor cranial nerve; lateral syndromes take pain pathways, vestibular nuclei, cerebellar connections, and sympathetic fibers."},{"type":"p","text":"The lateral medullary syndrome is the classic crossed sensory pattern. Fibers for pain and temperature of the face descend in the spinal trigeminal tract on the same side before crossing, while body pain and temperature fibers crossed in the spinal cord and ascend in the lateral spinothalamic tract, so both lie in the lateral medulla and are lost on opposite sides. Weakness is absent because the pyramids are medial. Vertebral artery occlusion produces this syndrome at least as often as occlusion of PICA itself."},{"type":"p","text":"The medial medullary syndrome is a crossed motor pattern. The hypoglossal nerve exits medially between the pyramid and olive, so its fibers and the pyramid are damaged together: the tongue deviates toward the lesion while the opposite arm and leg are weak. The AICA syndrome resembles the PICA syndrome one level higher, with the facial nucleus and cochlear structures replacing the nucleus ambiguus. The facial weakness involves the forehead because the nucleus itself, the lower motor neuron, is destroyed."},{"type":"p","text":"In the locked-in syndrome, basilar occlusion infarcts the ventral pons bilaterally, interrupting corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers to all limbs and to the lower cranial nerves. The reticular activating system lies dorsally in the pontine and midbrain tegmentum and is spared, so the patient is fully awake. Vertical gaze and eyelid control depend on midbrain structures above the lesion, so blinking and vertical eye movement become the only means of communication."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01-C04","obj":"NE-10.4","title":"CSF production, circulation, and the site of obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The choroid plexus, a fold of pia and capillaries covered by specialized ependymal epithelium, lies in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles and produces most of the CSF, about 500 mL per day, so the roughly 150 mL in the system turns over several times daily. The epithelium secretes actively: Na+/K+-ATPase on the ventricular surface pumps Na+ into the ventricle, carbonic anhydrase supplies HCO3-, Cl- and HCO3- follow, and water moves through aquaporin-1 channels. Because secretion is active, production continues at nearly the same rate when outflow is blocked, so obstruction causes progressive dilation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of CSF","steps":["Choroid plexus secretes CSF into the lateral ventricles","Interventricular foramina (of Monro) to the third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain","Fourth ventricle","Median aperture (Magendie) and two lateral apertures (Luschka)","Subarachnoid space and cisterns around brain and cord","Arachnoid granulations into the superior sagittal sinus"],"caption":"CSF moves by bulk flow from secretion sites to venous absorption sites; the aqueduct is the narrowest channel."},{"type":"p","text":"Absorption occurs mainly through arachnoid granulations, tufts of arachnoid that project into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus. They act as one-way valves: CSF passes into venous blood when CSF pressure exceeds sinus pressure, and blood cannot pass back. Absorption therefore rises with CSF pressure and falls when sinus pressure rises. Some CSF also leaves along cranial and spinal nerve sheaths to lymphatics."},{"type":"p","text":"Hydrocephalus is enlargement of the ventricles from excess CSF. The rule for localizing a block is that every ventricle upstream of the obstruction dilates, while every ventricle downstream stays normal or small. A block within the ventricles or at the fourth ventricle outlets is noncommunicating hydrocephalus. When the ventricular path is open but absorption at the granulations or flow through the subarachnoid space is impaired, all four ventricles enlarge, which is communicating hydrocephalus."},{"type":"table","title":"Site of obstruction and ventricular pattern","cols":["Site","Example","Dilated","Not dilated"],"rows":[["One interventricular foramen","Tumor at the foramen","One lateral ventricle","Other lateral, third, fourth"],["Both interventricular foramina","Colloid cyst in the roof of the third ventricle","Both lateral ventricles","Third and fourth"],["Cerebral aqueduct","Congenital aqueductal stenosis; pineal region tumor","Both lateral and third","Fourth"],["Fourth ventricle outlets","Dandy-Walker malformation; posterior fossa tumor","Lateral, third, and fourth (unless the tumor fills the fourth)","None"],["Arachnoid granulations or subarachnoid space","Scarring after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage","All four (communicating)","None"]],"caption":"Walk the path from the lateral ventricles downstream: dilation stops at the block."},{"type":"p","text":"The age of the patient shapes the presentation. Before the cranial sutures fuse, ventricular enlargement expands the skull, producing a rapidly enlarging head and a bulging fontanelle. After fusion the skull cannot expand, so pressure rises and causes headache, vomiting, and papilledema. Normal pressure hydrocephalus is a chronic communicating form in older adults with normal or intermittently raised pressure and the triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and cognitive decline, explained by stretch of periventricular corona radiata fibers. Ventricles also enlarge passively when brain tissue is lost, as in Alzheimer disease (hydrocephalus ex vacuo); pressure is normal and no flow is blocked."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A pineal tumor compresses the cerebral aqueduct. Which ventricles enlarge, and why does CSF keep accumulating?","explain":"The lateral and third ventricles enlarge because they are upstream; the fourth stays normal because it lies downstream. The choroid plexus keeps secreting actively regardless of back pressure, so volume and pressure continue to rise.","check":"The CSF path figure and the cerebral aqueduct row."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01-C05","obj":"NE-10.5","title":"Venous drainage, subdural hematoma, and dural sinus thrombosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cerebral veins and dural sinuses have no valves, so blood moves along pressure gradients toward the internal jugular veins. The sinuses lie between the periosteal and meningeal layers of the dura, and their rigid walls keep them open even when venous pressure is low. Emissary and diploic veins connect the sinuses with scalp veins, and the ophthalmic veins connect facial veins with the cavernous sinus, which gives infection a path inward."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cerebral venous drainage","steps":["Superficial cerebral veins cross the subdural space as bridging veins","Superior sagittal sinus along the falx cerebri","Deep internal cerebral veins form the great cerebral vein","Great cerebral vein and inferior sagittal sinus form the straight sinus","Superior sagittal and straight sinuses meet at the confluence","Transverse sinuses, then sigmoid sinuses","Internal jugular veins at the jugular foramina"],"caption":"The cavernous sinus drains separately: superior petrosal sinus to the sigmoid sinus, inferior petrosal sinus to the internal jugular vein."},{"type":"p","text":"Bridging veins are the weak point. They run from the brain surface, which moves within the skull, to the sinus, which is fixed to the dura. Acceleration and deceleration of the head shear them. As the brain shrinks with age or chronic alcohol use, the veins are stretched across a wider subdural space and tear with milder force; in infants violent shaking produces the same injury. Blood collects between the dura and arachnoid at venous pressure, so it accumulates slowly over hours to weeks. Because the clot lies beneath the dura, the dural attachments at the sutures do not confine it, so it spreads along the convexity in a crescent and crosses suture lines, stopping only at dural reflections such as the falx and tentorium."},{"type":"table","title":"Subdural compared with epidural hematoma","cols":["Feature","Subdural","Epidural"],"rows":[["Vessel","Torn bridging veins","Middle meningeal artery, often with temporal bone fracture"],["Space","Between dura and arachnoid","Between skull and periosteal dura"],["Shape on CT","Crescent; crosses sutures","Biconvex (lens); limited by sutures"],["Tempo","Slow, venous pressure","Rapid, arterial pressure"]],"caption":"The bleeding vessel determines pressure and tempo; the dural attachments determine shape."},{"type":"p","text":"Dural venous sinus thrombosis follows the conditions of venous thrombosis in general: hypercoagulability (pregnancy and the postpartum period, estrogen-containing contraceptives, inherited thrombophilias, malignancy), slow flow (dehydration), and local injury or infection (mastoiditis spreading to the sigmoid or transverse sinus, facial infection spreading to the cavernous sinus). Occlusion raises pressure throughout the venous and capillary beds upstream."},{"type":"flow","title":"Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis raises intracranial pressure","steps":["Thrombus occludes the superior sagittal sinus","Sinus pressure rises","CSF absorption at arachnoid granulations falls","Cortical venous pressure and capillary pressure rise","Vasogenic edema and venous infarcts, often hemorrhagic","Intracranial pressure rises: headache, papilledema, seizures"],"caption":"Two mechanisms act together: CSF accumulates and venous congestion swells the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Venous infarcts differ from arterial infarcts. They do not follow an arterial territory, they are often bilateral and parasagittal when the superior sagittal sinus is involved, and they frequently bleed because arterial inflow continues into tissue whose outflow is blocked."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-10-01-C06","obj":"NE-10.6","title":"Regulation of cerebral blood flow, perfusion pressure, and the Cushing reflex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brain is about 2% of body weight but receives roughly 50 mL of blood per 100 g of tissue per minute and stores almost no glucose or oxygen, so consciousness is lost within seconds when flow stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoregulation keeps flow nearly constant while mean arterial pressure (MAP) stays between about 60 and 150 mm Hg. Arteriolar smooth muscle constricts when stretched by higher pressure and dilates when pressure falls (the myogenic response), reinforced by local metabolites. Outside the range flow follows pressure passively, with ischemia below it and hyperperfusion and edema above it. Chronic hypertension shifts the curve to higher pressures, so a pressure that is normal for others can leave such a brain underperfused."},{"type":"table","title":"Local regulators of cerebral arteriolar diameter","cols":["Stimulus","Effect on arterioles","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Rise in PaCO2 (hypercapnia)","Strong dilation; flow rises","CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier and lowers perivascular pH"],["Fall in PaCO2 (hypocapnia, hyperventilation)","Constriction; flow and cerebral blood volume fall","Perivascular pH rises"],["Fall in PaO2 below about 50 mm Hg","Dilation","Hypoxia; little effect above this level"],["Increased neuronal activity","Local dilation","K+, adenosine, nitric oxide, and H+ from active tissue"]],"caption":"PaCO2 is the dominant chemical regulator."},{"type":"p","text":"Carbon dioxide is the most powerful chemical regulator. It diffuses freely across the blood-brain barrier, carbonic anhydrase converts it to H+ and HCO3- in the perivascular fluid, and the fall in pH relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle. H+ in arterial blood crosses the barrier poorly, so a metabolic acidosis affects cerebral vessels much less than an equal respiratory acidosis. A rise in PaCO2 from 40 to about 70 mm Hg roughly doubles cerebral blood flow. Hyperventilation lowers PaCO2, constricts the arterioles, and reduces cerebral blood volume and therefore intracranial pressure (ICP) within minutes, but excessive hypocapnia can lower flow enough to cause ischemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the skull is rigid, the volumes of brain, blood, and CSF must total a constant (the Monro-Kellie doctrine). An added mass is first offset by pushing CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and venous blood out of the skull; once those reserves are used, small added volumes cause steep rises in pressure. Normal ICP in a supine adult is about 5 to 15 mm Hg. Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) equals MAP minus ICP, so as ICP climbs toward MAP, perfusion fails even when blood pressure is normal."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating perfusion pressure","text":"A patient with a large hemorrhage has a blood pressure of 120/75 mm Hg and an ICP of 35 mm Hg. Step 1: MAP is about diastolic plus one third of pulse pressure, 75 + 45/3 = 90 mm Hg. Step 2: CPP = 90 minus 35 = 55 mm Hg, below the normal of about 70 to 90 mm Hg and near the lower limit of autoregulation, so the brain is at the edge of ischemia. Step 3: if ICP rose to 60 mm Hg with MAP unchanged, CPP would fall to 30 mm Hg, which is the situation the Cushing reflex tries to correct by raising MAP."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The Cushing reflex","steps":["ICP rises toward MAP","CPP falls and the medulla becomes ischemic","Medullary vasomotor center drives sympathetic discharge","Systemic hypertension, often with wide pulse pressure","Baroreceptors sense hypertension; vagal output slows the heart","Higher MAP restores CPP until ICP rises further"],"caption":"Hypertension is the ischemic response; bradycardia is the baroreceptor response to it."},{"type":"p","text":"The resulting Cushing triad is systemic hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration, the last from compression or ischemia of the pontine and medullary respiratory centers. It is a late sign of brainstem compromise and impending herniation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does hyperventilation lower ICP, while an equivalent metabolic alkalosis has little effect?","explain":"CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier, so hypocapnia raises perivascular pH, constricts arterioles, and reduces cerebral blood volume. Plasma HCO3- and H+ cross the barrier slowly, so arteriolar pH changes little.","check":"The PaCO2 paragraph and the regulator table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology combine in every localization question. The arterial map tells which structures share a blood supply, cross-sectional anatomy tells which tracts and nuclei sit together, and the crossing points of the pathways turn a set of deficits into a side and a level. Pathology supplies the mechanism: atherosclerosis and embolism at the large arteries and the heart give territorial infarcts, hypertensive arteriolosclerosis of perforators gives lacunes and putaminal hemorrhage, falling systemic pressure gives watershed infarcts, and the Virchow triad gives venous sinus thrombosis. Microbiology and immunology enter through meningitis, whose inflammatory exudate scars the arachnoid granulations, and through infections that spread inward along valveless veins."},{"type":"p","text":"The fluid compartments are linked because they share one rigid box. A blocked aqueduct, a clotted sinus, an expanding hematoma, or vasodilation from hypercapnia all add volume, raise ICP, and lower CPP, and the respiratory system influences that balance through PaCO2. Limits matter: arterial anatomy varies, so classic syndromes are often incomplete, and numerical ranges for autoregulation and ICP are approximate."}],"summary":["Internal carotids give the ACA and MCA; vertebrals join as the basilar, which ends as the PCAs; communicating arteries complete the circle of Willis, which compensates only for slow proximal occlusion.","ACA feeds the medial frontal and parietal lobes (leg); MCA feeds the lateral convexity (face, arm, language); PCA feeds the occipital lobe and thalamus.","Lenticulostriate arteries are thin, high-pressure perforators damaged by hypertension, causing lacunes and putaminal hemorrhage.","Dominant MCA infarcts cause aphasia, nondominant MCA infarcts cause neglect, PCA infarcts cause homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing, and a capsular lacune causes pure motor hemiparesis.","Brainstem lesions give ipsilateral cranial nerve signs and contralateral long tract signs; PICA means dysphagia and hoarseness, AICA means facial paralysis and deafness, medial medulla means tongue deviation, and basilar occlusion means locked-in.","CSF flows from the choroid plexus through the foramina of Monro, third ventricle, aqueduct, fourth ventricle, and its apertures to the subarachnoid space and arachnoid granulations; ventricles upstream of a block dilate.","Bridging vein tears cause slowly accumulating crescentic subdural hematomas; sinus thrombosis raises ICP through impaired CSF absorption and venous congestion.","Autoregulation holds cerebral flow steady over a MAP of about 60 to 150 mm Hg; hypercapnia dilates and hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles.","CPP = MAP minus ICP; the Cushing reflex raises MAP to protect perfusion and produces hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular breathing."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Special Circulations (cerebral circulation); The Neuronal Microenvironment"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: cranial meninges, dural venous sinuses, and arterial supply of the brain"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System: cerebrovascular diseases, edema, herniation, and hydrocephalus"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders: stroke"}]},"NE-11":{"unit":"NE-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11-01","title":"Association Cortex, Language, and the EEG","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the association cortex, the large regions outside the primary sensory and motor areas that combine information into planned behavior, attention, language, and recognition. It explains the prefrontal cortex and its circuits, the parietal attention system, the dominant-hemisphere language network, the dorsal and ventral visual streams, and how the scalp electroencephalogram (EEG) reflects arousal and sleep. Board questions usually describe a behavior, speech pattern, or perceptual deficit after a stroke, injury, or degenerative disease and ask you to localize it, or give EEG features and ask for the state. If you can name the network a symptom depends on and the artery that supplies it, you can localize most cortical syndromes.","prerequisites":["Lobes, gyri, and sulci of the cerebral hemispheres and the main Brodmann areas (NE-3)","The visual pathway from retina to primary visual cortex and visual field defects (NE-6)","Territories of the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries (NE-10)","Basal ganglia and thalamic circuits (NE-7)","Postsynaptic potentials and their summation (NE-13)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hypothalamic and limbic function (NE-12)","how":"Orbitofrontal and cingulate cortex work with limbic circuits for reward and emotion, and hypothalamic nuclei switch the brain between the waking and sleeping states the EEG records."},{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Aphasia, neglect, Gerstmann syndrome, and prosopagnosia are classic stroke syndromes localized by the divisions of the MCA and by the PCA."},{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Frontotemporal dementia and Alzheimer disease are told apart by which association cortex degenerates first."},{"to":"Special senses (NE-6)","how":"Both visual streams begin in primary visual cortex, so higher visual deficits must be separated from field defects caused by lesions earlier in the pathway."},{"to":"Neurotransmitter synthesis and breakdown (NE-15)","how":"Dopamine tunes prefrontal working memory, and acetylcholine, norepinephrine, serotonin, histamine, and orexin set arousal and the timing of REM sleep."}],"terms":[{"term":"Association cortex","def":"Cortex outside the primary sensory and motor areas that integrates information across modalities to support cognition and behavior."},{"term":"Executive function","def":"The abilities that hold a goal in mind, plan steps toward it, monitor progress, and suppress responses that do not fit the context."},{"term":"Working memory","def":"The brief, active holding and manipulation of information needed for the task at hand."},{"term":"Perseveration","def":"Repeating a response or strategy after it is no longer appropriate or has been shown to fail."},{"term":"Abulia","def":"Loss of will and spontaneous action, with slowed and reduced behavior even though the person can act when prompted."},{"term":"Hemispatial neglect","def":"Failure to attend or respond to stimuli on the side opposite a brain lesion that a primary sensory or motor deficit does not explain."},{"term":"Anosognosia","def":"Unawareness or denial of one's own neurological deficit."},{"term":"Aphasia","def":"An acquired disorder of language affecting speaking, understanding, reading, or writing, distinct from a motor disorder of articulation."},{"term":"Paraphasia","def":"A speech error in which a wrong sound (phonemic) or a wrong word (semantic) is substituted for the intended one."},{"term":"Agnosia","def":"Failure to recognize a stimulus despite intact primary sensation, attention, and language."},{"term":"Sleep spindle","def":"A brief burst of about 11 to 16 Hz waves generated by thalamocortical circuits and characteristic of N2 sleep."},{"term":"K complex","def":"A large, sharp, biphasic EEG wave of N2 sleep that can occur spontaneously or after a sound."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11-01-C01","obj":"NE-11.1","title":"Prefrontal cortex, executive function, and frontotemporal dementia","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Normal structure and function"},{"type":"p","text":"The prefrontal cortex is the part of the frontal lobe in front of the motor and premotor areas. It receives highly processed input from posterior association cortex, the limbic system, and the mediodorsal nucleus of the thalamus, and projects back to all of them. Its work is executive function: holding a goal in working memory, planning the steps toward it, shifting strategy when the situation changes, and suppressing actions that do not fit the context. Orbitofrontal cortex adds judgment and social conduct, which observers recognize as personality."},{"type":"p","text":"Each prefrontal region runs a parallel loop through the basal ganglia and thalamus that sustains the selected plan and inhibits competing ones. Dopamine from the ventral tegmental area (the mesocortical pathway) modulates dorsolateral working memory, so prefrontal performance is sensitive to dopamine level."},{"type":"flow","title":"A frontal-subcortical loop","steps":["Prefrontal cortex excites the caudate nucleus","Caudate inhibits the internal globus pallidus","Pallidal inhibition of the thalamus is released","Mediodorsal and ventral anterior thalamus re-excite the same cortex","Selected plan is sustained; competing plans are suppressed"],"caption":"Simplified direct pathway. Damage anywhere in the loop, including the caudate or mediodorsal thalamus, can mimic a frontal lobe syndrome."},{"type":"table","title":"Prefrontal regions and the effects of damage","cols":["Region","Main role","Result of damage","Common causes"],"rows":[["Dorsolateral","Working memory, planning, shifting strategy","Poor planning and organization, perseveration, concrete thinking","Lateral frontal tumor or MCA infarct, FTD"],["Orbitofrontal (ventromedial)","Linking actions to reward and social rules","Disinhibition, impulsivity, poor judgment, socially inappropriate behavior","Contusion in head trauma, FTD"],["Medial frontal and anterior cingulate","Motivation and initiation of action","Apathy and abulia; akinetic mutism if bilateral","ACA infarct, FTD"]],"caption":"Most real lesions involve more than one region, so mixed pictures are common."},{"type":"h","text":"Pathophysiology and manifestations"},{"type":"p","text":"Frontal damage rarely lowers scores on simple tests of knowledge; it disrupts the use of knowledge. A patient can state the rules yet break them, repeat a strategy after it stops working, or sit inactive while remaining able to act when prompted. Orbitofrontal contusions are common after head injury because the orbital surface strikes the irregular floor of the anterior cranial fossa. Large frontal lesions also release primitive reflexes (grasp, snout, palmomental) that frontal inhibition normally suppresses after infancy."},{"type":"h","text":"Frontotemporal dementia"},{"type":"p","text":"Frontotemporal dementia (FTD) is a neurodegenerative disease that selectively destroys frontal and anterior temporal cortex, usually beginning between about 45 and 65 years. In the behavioral variant, disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsive behaviors, and hyperorality (overeating, a craving for sweets) appear while memory and visuospatial skill are relatively preserved early. This is the reverse of Alzheimer disease, which begins in the medial temporal lobe and presents with memory loss. Other variants begin with progressive language decline (primary progressive aphasia). Imaging shows frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, sometimes so severe that the gyri are knife-thin."},{"type":"p","text":"The pathology is frontotemporal lobar degeneration with neuronal inclusions of tau or TDP-43, and rarely FUS. Pick disease is a tau subtype with round, silver-staining Pick bodies and swollen (ballooned) neurons. Familial cases often involve mutations in MAPT (tau) or GRN (progranulin), or a hexanucleotide repeat expansion in C9orf72, which also causes amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and explains why FTD and motor neuron disease can occur together."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11-01-C02","obj":"NE-11.2","title":"Parietal association cortex, spatial attention, neglect, and Gerstmann syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Normal structure and function"},{"type":"p","text":"The posterior parietal cortex lies behind the postcentral gyrus. The intraparietal sulcus divides it into the superior parietal lobule and the inferior parietal lobule, which contains the supramarginal and angular gyri. It combines visual, somatosensory, proprioceptive, and vestibular input into maps of space centered on the body, head, and eyes, and, with the frontal eye fields, selects which part of that space receives attention and guides the eyes and hand toward it."},{"type":"p","text":"Attention is lateralized. The left parietal lobe directs attention mainly to the right half of space, whereas the right parietal lobe attends to both halves. A right-lateralized network centered on the temporoparietal junction also detects unexpected events and reorients attention toward them. A left parietal lesion is therefore largely compensated by the intact right hemisphere, but a right parietal lesion leaves the left half of space with little attention, and the unopposed left hemisphere pulls attention further to the right."},{"type":"h","text":"Hemispatial neglect"},{"type":"p","text":"Hemispatial neglect most often follows a right inferior parietal or temporoparietal infarct in the territory of the inferior division of the right MCA, although right frontal, thalamic, and basal ganglia lesions can also cause it. The patient eats from only the right side of the plate, shaves only the right face, marks the center of a line to the right of true center, and draws a clock with the numbers crowded onto the right. Extinction, missing the left stimulus only when both sides are stimulated together, is a milder form. Anosognosia often accompanies neglect, so the patient may deny left-sided weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"Neglect differs from homonymous hemianopia. A patient with a field cut knows that half the field is missing and turns the eyes and head to compensate, whereas a patient with neglect does not search the left side even when the visual fields are full. A large right MCA stroke can cause both."},{"type":"h","text":"Gerstmann syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"The dominant angular gyrus lies where the parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes meet, linking written symbols and numbers with language and the body schema. Damage, usually from an infarct in the inferior division of the left MCA or a tumor, causes Gerstmann syndrome: agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia (inability to identify one's own fingers), and left-right confusion. The pure tetrad is rare; most patients also have alexia, anomia, or a fluent aphasia because Wernicke area is adjacent. Damage to the nearby dominant supramarginal gyrus can cause ideomotor apraxia, inability to perform a learned movement on command despite intact strength and comprehension."},{"type":"table","title":"Nondominant versus dominant parietal lesions","cols":["Feature","Nondominant (usually right)","Dominant (usually left)"],"rows":[["Classic syndrome","Left hemispatial neglect","Gerstmann syndrome (angular gyrus)"],["Associated deficits","Anosognosia, extinction, dressing and constructional apraxia","Alexia, anomia, fluent aphasia, ideomotor apraxia"],["Visual field if optic radiation is involved","Left inferior quadrantanopia","Right inferior quadrantanopia"],["Cortical sensory loss","Left astereognosis and agraphesthesia","Right astereognosis and agraphesthesia"]],"caption":"Primary sensory and field deficits are contralateral on either side; the attention and symbol deficits depend on which hemisphere is damaged."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a large left parietal stroke rarely cause lasting right-sided neglect, while a right parietal stroke of the same size often causes left-sided neglect?","explain":"The right hemisphere attends to both halves of space, so after a left lesion it still covers the right side. The left hemisphere attends mainly to the right side, so after a right lesion nothing covers the left side well, and the unopposed left hemisphere biases attention rightward.","check":"The paragraph on lateralized attention."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11-01-C03","obj":"NE-11.3","title":"The language network and the aphasias","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Normal structure and function"},{"type":"p","text":"Aphasia affects speaking, understanding, reading, and writing. It differs from dysarthria, a motor disorder of articulation in which language is intact and writing is normal. Language is lateralized to the left hemisphere in about 95% of right-handed people and most left-handed people, and that hemisphere is called dominant."},{"type":"p","text":"Broca area occupies the pars opercularis and pars triangularis of the dominant inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann 44 and 45), just in front of the face and larynx region of the motor cortex, and assembles grammar and the motor plan for speech. Wernicke area lies in the posterior dominant superior temporal gyrus (posterior Brodmann 22), beside primary auditory cortex, and links sound patterns to word meaning. The arcuate fasciculus connects them, arching around the end of the lateral sulcus deep to the supramarginal gyrus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hearing and repeating a word","steps":["Primary auditory cortex receives the sound","Wernicke area decodes it into a word","Arcuate fasciculus carries the word to Broca area","Broca area builds the speech motor plan","Motor cortex drives larynx, tongue, and lips"],"caption":"Repetition needs the whole loop, so perisylvian lesions impair it."},{"type":"h","text":"Lesions and their manifestations"},{"type":"p","text":"A Broca lesion, usually from the superior division of the left MCA, produces nonfluent aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with few grammatical words, and impaired repetition and naming. Everyday comprehension is preserved, though grammatically complex sentences are often misunderstood. Because intact Wernicke area monitors the output, the patient recognizes the errors and is frustrated. Nearby motor cortex explains the frequent right face and arm weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"A Wernicke lesion, usually from the inferior division of the left MCA, produces fluent speech with normal rate and melody but little meaning, full of paraphasias and neologisms (invented words). Comprehension and repetition are impaired. Because the same area monitors the patient's own speech, the patient is often unaware of the problem and may seem confused. Weakness is usually absent, but involvement of the temporal optic radiation (Meyer loop) can cause a right superior quadrantanopia."},{"type":"p","text":"Conduction aphasia follows damage to the arcuate fasciculus or supramarginal gyrus. Speech is fluent with phonemic paraphasias and comprehension is good, but repetition is disproportionately poor because heard words cannot be passed forward; patients are aware of their errors. Global aphasia follows a large left MCA stem or internal carotid occlusion that destroys the whole network, usually with right hemiplegia and hemisensory loss. The transcortical aphasias, typically from watershed infarcts that spare the perisylvian loop, are the opposite case: repetition is preserved although fluency, comprehension, or both fail."},{"type":"table","title":"Classifying the aphasias","cols":["Type","Lesion","Fluency","Comprehension","Repetition"],"rows":[["Broca","Dominant inferior frontal gyrus (MCA superior division)","Nonfluent","Intact","Impaired"],["Wernicke","Dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus (MCA inferior division)","Fluent","Impaired","Impaired"],["Conduction","Arcuate fasciculus or supramarginal gyrus","Fluent","Intact","Impaired"],["Global","Whole perisylvian network (MCA stem or ICA)","Nonfluent","Impaired","Impaired"],["Transcortical","Watershed cortex around the perisylvian loop","Varies","Varies","Preserved"]],"caption":"Ask three questions: is speech fluent, is comprehension intact, and can the patient repeat?"},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an aphasia step by step","text":"A 70-year-old speaks rapidly with normal melody but says \"I took the plinder to the vorn,\" follows no spoken commands, and cannot repeat. Strength is normal, but the right upper quadrant of vision is lost. Fluent speech spares Broca area, and normal strength spares the motor strip. Impaired comprehension excludes conduction aphasia, and impaired repetition excludes transcortical sensory aphasia, leaving Wernicke aphasia. The quadrantanopia means the lesion reaches Meyer loop in the left temporal lobe: an infarct in the inferior division of the left MCA."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After prolonged hypotension during surgery, a patient speaks little and with effort but repeats long sentences perfectly. Why is repetition spared?","explain":"A watershed infarct between the ACA and MCA territories damaged frontal cortex near Broca area but spared the perisylvian loop itself. This is transcortical motor aphasia.","check":"The transcortical aphasias and the classification table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11-01-C04","obj":"NE-11.4","title":"Dorsal and ventral visual association streams","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Normal structure and function"},{"type":"p","text":"Primary visual cortex (V1, Brodmann 17), on the banks of the calcarine sulcus, maps the opposite visual field point by point but does not identify objects. Its output reaches association cortex along two broad routes. The dorsal stream runs upward into posterior parietal cortex and determines where an object is, how it moves, and how to act on it. The ventral stream runs downward into inferior occipitotemporal cortex and determines what the object is. The dorsal stream draws mainly on the magnocellular system (fast, motion-sensitive, little color), and the ventral stream mainly on the parvocellular system (fine detail and color), although the two interact."},{"type":"table","title":"The two visual association streams","cols":["Feature","Dorsal stream","Ventral stream"],"rows":[["Route","V1 to posterior parietal cortex","V1 to inferior temporal cortex"],["Question answered","Where is it, and how do I act on it?","What is it?"],["Main input","Mostly magnocellular","Mostly parvocellular"],["Key areas","MT (V5), intraparietal sulcus","V4, fusiform gyrus, inferior temporal cortex"],["Blood supply","MCA, with the parieto-occipital MCA and PCA border zone","PCA"],["Lesion results","Akinetopsia, optic ataxia, Balint syndrome","Prosopagnosia, achromatopsia, visual object agnosia"]],"caption":"Both streams start in V1; deficits in either occur with normal or near-normal acuity."},{"type":"h","text":"Dorsal stream lesions"},{"type":"p","text":"Key dorsal areas include the motion area MT (V5) near the occipitotemporal junction on the lateral surface, and regions along the intraparietal sulcus that convert visual location into coordinates for reaching and eye movements. Bilateral MT damage causes akinetopsia, in which motion is seen as a series of still frames. Bilateral parieto-occipital damage, often from watershed infarcts between the MCA and PCA, causes Balint syndrome: simultanagnosia (seeing only one object at a time), optic ataxia (misreaching for objects that are clearly seen), and oculomotor apraxia (difficulty directing gaze voluntarily)."},{"type":"h","text":"Ventral stream lesions and prosopagnosia"},{"type":"p","text":"The ventral stream passes through V4, which is important for color, to inferior temporal cortex, where neurons respond to whole objects regardless of size or position. On the ventral surface, the fusiform gyrus contains the fusiform face area, which responds selectively to faces and is usually larger on the right. This region is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery. Damage, typically bilateral or right-sided, causes prosopagnosia, inability to recognize familiar faces. The patient sees and can describe the face but cannot say whose it is, yet recognizes the same person instantly by voice, gait, or clothing because knowledge of the person is intact. Nearby lesions cause achromatopsia (cortical loss of color vision) and visual object agnosia, and damage to the left fusiform region contributes to alexia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Recognizing a familiar face","steps":["Retina, lateral geniculate nucleus, and V1 encode edges and contrast","V2 and V4 assemble shape and color","Fusiform face area builds the configuration of the face","Anterior temporal cortex matches it to a stored identity","Name and personal knowledge are retrieved"],"caption":"Prosopagnosia arises when the fusiform step fails; recognition by voice uses an auditory route and survives."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the streams are anatomically separate, lesions dissociate them. A patient with ventral damage may fail to recognize an object yet reach for it and shape the hand correctly, whereas a patient with optic ataxia names the object but misreaches. Normal acuity separates these higher deficits from lesions of the eye, optic nerve, or V1."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-11-01-C05","obj":"NE-11.5","title":"EEG patterns, arousal, and sleep stages","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"What the EEG records"},{"type":"p","text":"The scalp electroencephalogram records voltage produced by summed excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials in the apical dendrites of cortical pyramidal neurons, aligned perpendicular to the surface so their currents add. The pattern therefore reports synchrony: when many neurons receive the same rhythmic input, waves are slow and large; when each processes different information, waves are fast and small."},{"type":"h","text":"Regulation of arousal and sleep"},{"type":"p","text":"Wakefulness is maintained by the ascending arousal system: cholinergic neurons of the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei that activate the thalamus, and monoaminergic neurons (locus coeruleus norepinephrine, raphe serotonin, tuberomammillary histamine) that project widely to cortex, all stabilized by orexin (hypocretin) from the lateral hypothalamus. This input depolarizes thalamocortical neurons so they relay information faithfully, and the cortex desynchronizes into beta activity. At sleep onset, GABAergic neurons of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus inhibit the arousal centers, and the thalamus shifts into rhythmic burst firing."},{"type":"flow","title":"From wakefulness to slow-wave sleep","steps":["Ventrolateral preoptic GABA neurons inhibit arousal centers","Cholinergic and monoamine drive to the thalamus falls","Thalamocortical neurons hyperpolarize; T-type Ca2+ channels recover","Thalamic reticular nucleus paces bursts: spindles in N2","Deeper hyperpolarization slows the rhythm: delta waves in N3"],"caption":"Sleep synchronizes the cortex by changing thalamic firing mode."},{"type":"h","text":"Sleep stages"},{"type":"p","text":"Relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed produces posterior alpha, which disappears when the eyes open. N1 replaces alpha with theta and slow rolling eye movements. N2, the largest share of the night, adds spindles and K complexes. N3, or slow-wave sleep, has high-amplitude delta, the highest arousal threshold, and the nightly peak of growth hormone; sleepwalking and sleep terrors arise from it. REM sleep shows a low-voltage, mixed-frequency EEG resembling wakefulness, with rapid eye movements, dreaming, and atonia of skeletal muscle except the extraocular muscles and diaphragm."},{"type":"table","title":"EEG by state","cols":["State","Dominant activity","Defining features","Share of adult sleep"],"rows":[["Awake, alert, eyes open","Beta, over 13 Hz, low voltage","Desynchronized cortex","Not applicable"],["Awake, relaxed, eyes closed","Alpha, 8 to 13 Hz, posterior","Blocked by eye opening","Not applicable"],["N1","Theta, 4 to 7 Hz","Slow rolling eye movements","About 5%"],["N2","Theta background","Spindles and K complexes","About 45 to 55%"],["N3","Delta, under 4 Hz, high voltage","Highest arousal threshold","About 15 to 25%, less with age"],["REM","Low voltage, mixed, beta-like","Rapid eye movements, atonia","About 20 to 25%"]],"caption":"Amplitude rises and frequency falls as non-REM sleep deepens; REM returns to a waking-like pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"REM is generated by pontine cholinergic REM-on neurons when locus coeruleus and raphe REM-off neurons fall silent. Atonia comes from pontine neurons that drive medullary glycinergic and GABAergic inhibition of motor neurons; failure of this pathway causes dream enactment (REM sleep behavior disorder). Non-REM and REM alternate in cycles of about 90 minutes, with N3 concentrated in the first third and REM periods lengthening toward morning. REM latency, normally about 90 minutes, is shortened in narcolepsy (loss of orexin neurons) and in depression."},{"type":"example","title":"Identifying a REM epoch","text":"A 30-second epoch shows low-voltage, mixed-frequency activity, bursts of rapid eye movements, and an almost flat chin electromyogram. Low-voltage, mixed-frequency activity fits wakefulness, N1, or REM. The flat chin electromyogram means atonia, which wakefulness and N1 lack, and the eye movements are rapid rather than the slow rolling movements of N1, so the epoch is REM. If it appeared within 15 minutes of sleep onset, the short REM latency would suggest narcolepsy, in which loss of orexin lets REM intrude early."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why do sleepwalking and sleep terrors cluster in the first third of the night, while dream enactment in REM sleep behavior disorder clusters in the last third?","explain":"Sleepwalking and terrors are partial arousals from N3, which dominates the early cycles. REM periods lengthen toward morning, so a disorder of REM atonia appears mostly late.","check":"The sleep stage and REM regulation paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and pathology meet through blood supply and degeneration. The superior division of the left MCA supplies Broca area and the lateral frontal lobe, the inferior division supplies Wernicke area, the angular gyrus, and, on the right, the parietal cortex whose loss causes neglect. The ACA supplies medial frontal cortex (abulia), the PCA supplies the fusiform gyrus (prosopagnosia, alexia), and border zones produce transcortical aphasias and Balint syndrome. Degenerative disease follows networks rather than arteries: FTD begins in frontal and anterior temporal cortex with tau or TDP-43 inclusions, typical Alzheimer disease begins in the medial temporal lobe, and its posterior cortical variant can present with dorsal stream deficits."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and biochemistry explain the EEG and arousal. The EEG reflects synaptic currents and thalamic firing mode, which are set by acetylcholine, monoamines, histamine, orexin, and GABA, so drugs and metabolic disorders that change these transmitters change the EEG and the state of arousal. The localizations in this chapter are models: lateralization is statistical, the visual streams interact, and real lesions often combine features, so a clinical picture is localized by its dominant pattern rather than by a single sign."}],"summary":["Prefrontal cortex supports working memory, planning, judgment, and impulse control through loops with the basal ganglia and thalamus.","Dorsolateral damage causes poor planning and perseveration, orbitofrontal damage causes disinhibition, and medial frontal damage causes apathy and abulia.","Frontotemporal dementia begins with behavior or language change while memory is relatively spared, with tau (including Pick bodies) or TDP-43 inclusions.","The right parietal lobe attends to both sides of space, so a right parietal lesion causes left hemispatial neglect, often with anosognosia.","A dominant angular gyrus lesion causes Gerstmann syndrome: agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right confusion.","Broca aphasia is nonfluent with intact comprehension, Wernicke aphasia is fluent with impaired comprehension, conduction aphasia selectively impairs repetition, and global aphasia impairs all three.","Repetition fails with perisylvian lesions and is spared in transcortical aphasias from watershed infarcts.","The dorsal stream (occipitoparietal) handles location, motion, and reaching; the ventral stream (occipitotemporal) handles object and face recognition, and fusiform damage causes prosopagnosia.","The EEG reflects cortical synchrony: beta awake, alpha relaxed with eyes closed, theta in N1, spindles and K complexes in N2, delta in N3, and low-voltage beta-like activity in REM.","REM combines a waking-like EEG with rapid eye movements and atonia; N3 dominates early night and REM lengthens toward morning."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Cortex, Intellectual Functions of the Brain, Learning, and Memory"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"States of Brain Activity: Sleep, Brain Waves, Epilepsy, Psychoses, and Dementia"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the electroencephalogram, sleep, and arousal"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, cerebral hemispheres and arterial supply of the brain"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Central nervous system, frontotemporal lobar degeneration"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-12":{"unit":"NE-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12-01","title":"Hypothalamic and Limbic Function","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the hypothalamus, which holds body temperature, food intake, water balance, and daily timing near set points, and the limbic system, which forms new memories and attaches emotional meaning to experience. It then applies these circuits to fever, feeding disorders after hypothalamic lesions, amnesia syndromes, narcolepsy, and the common sleep disorders. Board questions usually describe a behavior or vital sign change and ask for the nucleus, circuit, molecule, or nutrient involved. If you can explain each region as a sensor and controller with a predictable lesion sign, and each sleep disorder by the sleep stage it comes from, you can reason through most of them.","prerequisites":["Gross anatomy of the diencephalon, third ventricle, and medial temporal lobe","Hypothalamic control of the anterior and posterior pituitary (portal system versus axonal transport)","Sympathetic and parasympathetic pathways, including sympathetic cholinergic sweat gland innervation","Innate immune cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha) and the arachidonic acid pathway","Normal sleep stages and their EEG features"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine system (pituitary and adrenal)","how":"Hypothalamic neurons release ADH and oxytocin directly and control the anterior pituitary through releasing and inhibiting hormones in portal blood."},{"to":"Immunology (innate immunity)","how":"Cytokines released by macrophages reach the hypothalamus and reset the temperature set point, linking infection to fever."},{"to":"Biochemistry (vitamins and amino acid derivatives)","how":"Thiamine pyrophosphate dependent enzymes explain Wernicke-Korsakoff lesions, and tryptophan gives rise to serotonin and melatonin."},{"to":"Basal ganglia and movement disorders (NE chapters)","how":"REM sleep behavior disorder is often the first sign of alpha-synuclein disease, and restless legs syndrome reflects reduced dopaminergic signaling."},{"to":"Microbiology (viral encephalitis)","how":"HSV-1 has a predilection for the medial temporal lobes, so it can destroy the hippocampus and amygdala."}],"terms":[{"term":"Set point","def":"The value of a regulated variable, such as core temperature, that a hypothalamic controller defends."},{"term":"Magnocellular neurons","def":"Large supraoptic and paraventricular neurons whose axons carry ADH and oxytocin to the posterior pituitary."},{"term":"Circumventricular organ","def":"A brain region with fenestrated capillaries and no complete blood-brain barrier, such as the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT)."},{"term":"Pyrogen","def":"A substance that causes fever; exogenous pyrogens such as LPS act by inducing endogenous pyrogenic cytokines."},{"term":"Leptin","def":"A hormone secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass that signals energy sufficiency to the arcuate nucleus."},{"term":"Papez circuit","def":"A loop linking hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus, and cingulate gyrus, involved in memory and emotion."},{"term":"Anterograde amnesia","def":"Inability to form new declarative memories after an injury, with older memories largely preserved."},{"term":"Confabulation","def":"Unintentional production of false memories to fill gaps, typical of Korsakoff syndrome."},{"term":"Orexin (hypocretin)","def":"A lateral hypothalamic neuropeptide that excites arousal centers and stabilizes wakefulness."},{"term":"Cataplexy","def":"Sudden emotion-triggered loss of muscle tone with consciousness preserved, caused by intrusion of REM atonia into waking."},{"term":"REM atonia","def":"Active inhibition of skeletal motor neurons during REM sleep that prevents movement during dreaming."},{"term":"Parasomnia","def":"Abnormal behavior or experience arising during sleep or at transitions between sleep and waking."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12-01-C01","obj":"NE-12.1","title":"Hypothalamic nuclei and their functions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The hypothalamus forms the floor and lower lateral walls of the third ventricle, below the hypothalamic sulcus, extending from the lamina terminalis and optic chiasm in front to the mammillary bodies behind. Although it weighs only about 4 g, it integrates sensory, hormonal, and limbic input and acts through three outputs: the pituitary, the autonomic nervous system, and behavior such as eating, drinking, and seeking warmth. Each nucleus works like a controller that compares a sensed value with a set point and corrects the difference."},{"type":"h","text":"Neuroendocrine nuclei"},{"type":"p","text":"Magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei synthesize ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin, package them with carrier proteins (neurophysins), and transport them down the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract to the posterior pituitary, where action potentials release them into the blood. Both nuclei make both hormones, but the supraoptic nucleus is the main source of ADH and the paraventricular nucleus of oxytocin. Osmoreceptors in the OVLT and adjacent anterior hypothalamus drive supraoptic firing when plasma osmolality rises, and destruction of these nuclei or of the stalk above the median eminence causes central diabetes insipidus. The paraventricular nucleus also contains parvocellular neurons that secrete CRH and TRH into portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"The arcuate nucleus, at the base of the third ventricle beside the median eminence, works through the hypophyseal portal system instead of axonal release. Its tuberoinfundibular dopamine neurons tonically inhibit prolactin secretion, and other arcuate neurons release GHRH. This is why a stalk lesion lowers most anterior pituitary hormones but raises prolactin: the inhibitory dopamine no longer arrives."},{"type":"h","text":"Homeostatic and timing nuclei"},{"type":"p","text":"Feeding is set by opposed regions. The lateral hypothalamic area promotes hunger and food seeking, and the ventromedial nucleus promotes satiety. The arcuate nucleus feeds both, reading leptin and other signals of energy balance. Temperature is likewise split: the anterior (preoptic) region drives heat loss through cutaneous vasodilation and sweating, and the posterior region drives heat conservation through sympathetic adrenergic output and shivering. The suprachiasmatic nucleus, directly above the optic chiasm, is the master circadian clock. A lesion removes one function and leaves its opponent unopposed."},{"type":"table","title":"Major hypothalamic nuclei","cols":["Nucleus or region","Main output or function","Result of a bilateral lesion"],"rows":[["Supraoptic","ADH (mainly) to posterior pituitary","Central diabetes insipidus"],["Paraventricular","Oxytocin (mainly) and ADH; CRH and TRH to portal blood","Impaired milk ejection; contributes to central DI and low ACTH and TSH"],["Arcuate","Dopamine (inhibits prolactin), GHRH; leptin sensing","Hyperprolactinemia, low GH, disordered appetite"],["Lateral hypothalamic area","Hunger; orexin neurons for wakefulness","Anorexia, weight loss"],["Ventromedial","Satiety","Hyperphagia, obesity"],["Anterior (preoptic)","Heat loss: cutaneous vasodilation, sweating","Hyperthermia"],["Posterior","Heat conservation: vasoconstriction, shivering","Hypothermia or poikilothermia"],["Suprachiasmatic","Circadian rhythm; drives pineal melatonin","Loss of day and night patterning"]],"caption":"Each region has a predictable lesion sign; opposed pairs (lateral and ventromedial, anterior and posterior) produce opposite signs."},{"type":"p","text":"Posterior lesions often cause poikilothermia, temperature that drifts with the environment, because descending heat-loss fibers from the anterior region pass through the posterior hypothalamus. Suprasellar masses such as craniopharyngioma, which press on the optic chiasm, stalk, and hypothalamus together, can combine bitemporal hemianopia, diabetes insipidus, hyperprolactinemia, and hyperphagia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12-01-C02","obj":"NE-12.2","title":"Thermoregulation, fever, and lesions of the feeding centers","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Core temperature is held near 37 °C (98.6 °F), with a normal daily swing of about 0.5 °C (0.9 °F), lowest in the early morning. Warm-sensitive neurons in the preoptic anterior hypothalamus act as the central sensor, and they also receive input from skin and deep body thermoreceptors. Their firing compared with the set point decides which effectors run."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Negative feedback control of core temperature","steps":["Core temperature rises above set point","Preoptic warm-sensitive neurons fire faster","Cutaneous vasodilation and sympathetic cholinergic sweating","Heat loss by radiation, convection, and evaporation","Core temperature falls back to set point"],"caption":"Heat loss responses switch off as temperature returns to the set point; cooling activates the opposite posterior responses."},{"type":"p","text":"When the body cools, the posterior hypothalamus raises sympathetic adrenergic tone to constrict skin vessels (alpha-1 receptors) and cause piloerection, and activates shivering, the largest source of extra heat. Infants also use nonshivering thermogenesis in brown fat, where beta-3 adrenergic stimulation opens uncoupling protein 1 (thermogenin) so the proton gradient becomes heat instead of ATP. Over weeks, cold exposure increases TRH and thyroid hormone, raising metabolic rate. Sweat glands are unusual in that their sympathetic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors."},{"type":"h","text":"Fever: a regulated rise in the set point"},{"type":"p","text":"In fever the controller works normally but defends a higher value. Exogenous pyrogens such as LPS bind Toll-like receptor 4 on macrophages, which release the endogenous pyrogens IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. Cytokines do not readily cross the blood-brain barrier, so they mainly act on endothelium at the OVLT and nearby vessels, inducing COX-2. Prostaglandin E2 then diffuses to preoptic neurons and acts on EP3 receptors, reducing warm-sensitive firing and raising the set point."},{"type":"flow","title":"How infection raises the temperature set point","steps":["LPS binds TLR4 on macrophages","IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha released into blood","Cytokines act on OVLT endothelium","COX-2 induced, PGE2 synthesized","PGE2 acts on preoptic EP3 receptors","Set point rises; heat conserved until core matches"],"caption":"Fever is mediated by PGE2 at the hypothalamus; blocking COX explains why aspirin-like drugs lower the set point."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: chills, then sweating","text":"A patient with pneumonia has a temperature of 37 °C (98.6 °F) when IL-1 raises the set point to 39 °C (102.2 °F). The hypothalamus now reads the body as 2 °C too cold, so the patient feels chilled, the skin is pale and cool from vasoconstriction, and shivering begins, even though temperature is normal. Once core reaches 39 °C, the patient feels warm and stable. When the infection resolves and PGE2 falls, the set point drops to 37 °C while the body is still at 39 °C, so the hypothalamus reads overheating: the skin flushes and the patient sweats. In heat stroke, by contrast, the set point is normal and heat gain exceeds heat loss, so COX inhibition does not lower the temperature."},{"type":"h","text":"Predicting lesions of the feeding centers"},{"type":"p","text":"Leptin rises with fat mass and acts on the arcuate nucleus, stimulating POMC neurons (whose product alpha-MSH acts on melanocortin-4 receptors to cause satiety) and inhibiting NPY and AgRP neurons, which promote hunger. Ghrelin from the empty stomach does the reverse. Destroying the ventromedial satiety region leaves hunger unopposed, so the animal or patient overeats and becomes obese. Destroying the lateral area removes the drive to eat, causing anorexia and wasting even with food available. Loss-of-function mutations in leptin, its receptor, or MC4R cause severe early obesity by the same logic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"What would happen to temperature and feeding after a tumor destroyed the ventromedial nucleus but spared the anterior hypothalamus?","explain":"Hunger from the lateral area would be unopposed, giving hyperphagia and obesity, while heat loss responses would remain intact.","check":"Compare with the lesion table in C01 and the feeding center paragraph above."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12-01-C03","obj":"NE-12.3","title":"The limbic system, memory, and emotion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The limbic system is a ring of cortical and subcortical structures on the medial edge of the cerebral hemisphere that links memory, emotion, and the hypothalamus. Its core parts are the hippocampus and amygdala in the medial temporal lobe, the parahippocampal and cingulate gyri, the fornix, the mammillary bodies of the hypothalamus, and the anterior nucleus of the thalamus. Through the hypothalamus, limbic activity produces the autonomic and endocrine signs of emotion, such as a racing heart with fear."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The Papez circuit","steps":["Hippocampus (subiculum)","Fornix","Mammillary bodies","Mammillothalamic tract","Anterior nucleus of thalamus","Cingulate gyrus","Cingulum to parahippocampal (entorhinal) cortex","Back to hippocampus"],"caption":"Damage anywhere in this loop, especially bilaterally, can impair formation of new declarative memories."},{"type":"p","text":"The hippocampus binds the elements of an experience into a declarative (explicit) memory trace, using NMDA receptor dependent long-term potentiation. Over months to years the trace is consolidated into neocortex and no longer needs the hippocampus. Bilateral hippocampal damage therefore causes anterograde amnesia: new facts and events cannot be stored, while older memories, language, intelligence, and procedural (skill) learning are spared. Retrograde loss, if present, is temporally graded, affecting the most recent memories that were not yet consolidated. Causes include global hypoxia (CA1 pyramidal neurons are especially vulnerable), HSV-1 encephalitis, bilateral posterior cerebral artery infarcts, and Alzheimer disease, which begins in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus."},{"type":"h","text":"Thiamine deficiency and the diencephalic memory relays"},{"type":"p","text":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase. In deficiency, usually with chronic alcohol use and poor intake, neurons with high oxidative demand fail to make ATP, lactate accumulates, and symmetric lesions with petechial hemorrhage form around the third ventricle, aqueduct, and fourth ventricle: mammillary bodies, medial dorsal thalamus, periaqueductal gray, ocular motor and vestibular nuclei, and the cerebellar vermis. A carbohydrate load increases flux through these enzymes and can unmask or worsen deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Wernicke encephalopathy is the acute stage: confusion, eye signs (nystagmus and ophthalmoplegia, classically lateral rectus weakness), and gait ataxia, each traceable to the structures above, though all three appear together in a minority of patients. Korsakoff syndrome is the chronic stage, with severe anterograde and some retrograde amnesia and confabulation; it reflects damage to the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus, which interrupts the Papez circuit and related thalamic pathways. Atrophic mammillary bodies are a characteristic imaging and autopsy finding."},{"type":"h","text":"The amygdala and Klüver-Bucy syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"The amygdala, deep in the anterior temporal lobe in front of the hippocampus, assigns emotional value to sensory input, especially fear, and drives fear responses through its central nucleus projections to the hypothalamus and brainstem. Bilateral destruction of the anterior temporal lobes including the amygdala, classically from HSV-1 encephalitis, trauma, or frontotemporal degeneration, produces Klüver-Bucy syndrome: hyperorality (placing objects in the mouth), hypersexuality and disinhibition, placidity with loss of fear, visual agnosia, and hypermetamorphosis (compulsive attention to every visual stimulus)."},{"type":"table","title":"Limbic lesions and their signs","cols":["Site","Typical cause","Main deficit"],"rows":[["Hippocampus, bilateral","Hypoxia, HSV-1, bilateral PCA infarcts, Alzheimer disease","Anterograde amnesia; skills and old memories spared"],["Mammillary bodies and medial thalamus","Thiamine deficiency","Wernicke triad acutely; Korsakoff amnesia with confabulation"],["Amygdala, bilateral","HSV-1, trauma, frontotemporal degeneration","Klüver-Bucy syndrome"],["Anterior cingulate gyrus","Infarct, tumor","Apathy, reduced motivation; akinetic mutism if bilateral"]],"caption":"Memory loss localizes to the hippocampus and its diencephalic relays; loss of fear and disinhibition localize to the amygdala."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with bilateral hippocampal infarcts still remember childhood events and can learn to ride a stationary bike?","explain":"Old memories are already consolidated in neocortex, and procedural learning uses the basal ganglia and cerebellum, not the hippocampus.","check":"Compare with the hippocampus paragraph and the lesion table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12-01-C04","obj":"NE-12.4","title":"Circadian rhythm, melatonin, and narcolepsy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) contains neurons that keep time on their own. In each cell, the CLOCK and BMAL1 proteins activate transcription of the Period and Cryptochrome genes, whose proteins accumulate and then inhibit CLOCK and BMAL1. This transcription and translation feedback loop takes slightly more or less than 24 hours, so the clock must be reset daily. Light provides the reset: intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells containing melanopsin, most sensitive to blue light, project through the retinohypothalamic tract to the SCN, independent of the visual pathway to the lateral geniculate body."},{"type":"flow","title":"From light to melatonin","steps":["Darkness: melanopsin ganglion cells quiet","SCN stops inhibiting paraventricular neurons","Descending fibers reach upper thoracic intermediolateral column","Superior cervical ganglion sends sympathetic fibers to pineal","Norepinephrine on beta-1 receptors raises pinealocyte cAMP","Serotonin N-acetyltransferase activity rises","Melatonin secreted into blood at night"],"caption":"Light at night reverses the chain and suppresses melatonin within minutes."},{"type":"p","text":"Melatonin is synthesized from tryptophan, which tryptophan hydroxylase converts to 5-hydroxytryptophan and aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase converts to serotonin. In the pineal gland, serotonin N-acetyltransferase (the rate-limiting, night-activated step) and a methyltransferase convert serotonin to melatonin. Plasma melatonin rises after dark and peaks in the middle of the night. It acts on MT1 and MT2 receptors, including receptors on the SCN itself, promoting sleep onset and shifting the clock. Because the pineal pathway runs through the cervical sympathetic chain, high spinal cord injury or interruption of that chain can abolish the melatonin rhythm, and people with no light perception may run on a clock that drifts away from the 24-hour day."},{"type":"h","text":"Orexin and the stability of waking"},{"type":"p","text":"Orexin A and B (hypocretin 1 and 2) are made only by neurons in the lateral and perifornical hypothalamus. They excite the arousal systems: locus coeruleus (norepinephrine), dorsal raphe (serotonin), tuberomammillary nucleus (histamine), and cholinergic pontine and basal forebrain nuclei. These arousal systems also suppress REM-generating neurons. Orexin therefore acts like a stabilizer on the switch between waking, non-REM, and REM sleep, keeping each state consolidated."},{"type":"p","text":"Narcolepsy type 1 results from selective loss of orexin neurons, thought to be autoimmune because of a strong association with HLA-DQB1*06:02, and is confirmed by low CSF hypocretin-1. Without orexin, states switch abruptly. Patients have excessive daytime sleepiness and fragmented night sleep, enter REM within minutes of falling asleep, and experience REM features during waking: hypnagogic or hypnopompic hallucinations (dream imagery at sleep onset or waking) and sleep paralysis. Cataplexy is the defining feature: strong positive emotion such as laughter triggers the brainstem REM atonia pathway, causing sudden loss of muscle tone, from jaw sagging to a full collapse, while consciousness is preserved. Narcolepsy type 2 lacks cataplexy and usually has normal hypocretin levels."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a sleep attack","text":"A 19-year-old falls asleep in class daily and drops his coffee cup when he laughs, though he remains aware. Sleep testing shows REM within 10 minutes of sleep onset. The awareness during collapse excludes a seizure or syncope and points to REM atonia intruding into waking. Early REM shows that REM is no longer held off by monoaminergic tone. The single lesion that destabilizes both is loss of orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, so CSF hypocretin-1 would be low. The SCN is intact, so the timing of his sleep across the 24 hours is normal; the problem is the stability of each state, not the clock."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-12-01-C05","obj":"NE-12.5","title":"REM sleep behavior disorder, N3 parasomnias, and restless legs syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sleep cycles about every 90 minutes through non-REM stages N1, N2 (sleep spindles and K complexes), and N3 (high-amplitude delta waves, the deepest stage), then REM sleep. N3 dominates the first third of the night, and REM periods lengthen toward morning. During REM the EEG resembles waking and the eyes move rapidly, but pontine neurons near the locus coeruleus drive medullary circuits that release glycine and GABA onto spinal and cranial motor neurons, producing REM atonia. Where a parasomnia arises in this architecture predicts its timing, recall, and behavior."},{"type":"h","text":"REM sleep behavior disorder"},{"type":"p","text":"In REM sleep behavior disorder, the atonia pathway is damaged, so dream content is acted out with punching, kicking, or shouting, often injuring the patient or bed partner. Episodes occur in the second half of the night, when REM is abundant, and the patient wakes quickly and can recount a vivid, often threatening dream. Polysomnography shows REM sleep without atonia. The disorder is most common in men over 50. Alpha-synuclein deposition reaches the lower brainstem early in Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, and multiple system atrophy, before the substantia nigra and cortex are heavily involved, so isolated REM sleep behavior disorder often precedes these diseases by years and most patients develop one of them on long follow-up."},{"type":"h","text":"N3 parasomnias of childhood"},{"type":"p","text":"Sleepwalking, sleep terrors, and confusional arousals are disorders of partial arousal from N3. Motor and limbic regions become active while frontal and parietal association cortex stays in slow-wave sleep, so the child walks or screams with eyes open but is unresponsive, hard to wake, confused if woken, and has no memory of the event. A sleep terror adds intense autonomic arousal: tachycardia, sweating, dilated pupils. Because N3 is plentiful in children and in the first third of the night, these events are common in childhood, cluster early in the night, increase after sleep deprivation (which deepens N3) or fever, and fade as N3 declines with age. Nightmares differ: they arise in REM, later in the night, and the child wakes fully and recalls the dream."},{"type":"h","text":"Restless legs syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"Restless legs syndrome is a sensorimotor disorder, not a parasomnia, defined by an urge to move the legs, often with uncomfortable creeping sensations, that begins or worsens at rest, is worse in the evening and at night, and is relieved by movement. Most patients also have periodic limb movements during sleep. The leading mechanism is brain iron deficiency: iron is the cofactor of tyrosine hydroxylase and is needed for dopamine signaling, and imaging and CSF studies show low iron in the substantia nigra. Dopaminergic activity normally falls in the evening, which fits the timing. Secondary causes include iron deficiency (low ferritin), chronic kidney disease, and pregnancy; dopamine-blocking drugs and sedating antihistamines worsen symptoms."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the major sleep disorders in this unit","cols":["Feature","REM sleep behavior disorder","N3 parasomnias","Restless legs syndrome"],"rows":[["Stage or state","REM","N3 (slow-wave)","Waking at rest, before sleep"],["Time of night","Second half","First third","Evening and bedtime"],["Typical patient","Older man","Child","Any age; iron deficiency, CKD, pregnancy"],["Memory of event","Recalls dream","None","Fully aware"],["Mechanism","Loss of REM atonia, brainstem synuclein","Partial arousal from deep sleep","Brain iron deficiency, reduced dopamine signaling"],["Associated condition","Parkinson disease, Lewy body dementia, MSA","Usually none; outgrown","Periodic limb movements of sleep"]],"caption":"Stage of origin predicts timing, recall, and the population affected."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 68-year-old man punches his wife during sleep at 4 am and describes fighting off an attacker in a dream. What sleep stage is involved, and what should be watched for over the next decade?","explain":"REM, because it occurs late in the night with dream recall and abnormal movement; the loss of atonia reflects brainstem alpha-synuclein disease that often progresses to Parkinson disease or Lewy body dementia.","check":"Compare with the REM sleep behavior disorder paragraph and the comparison table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain the hypothalamus as a set of controllers placed at the meeting point of blood, cerebrospinal fluid, and nerve. Circumventricular organs let it read plasma osmolality and circulating cytokines, portal vessels and axonal transport carry its hormones, and descending autonomic pathways reach sweat glands, skin vessels, brown fat, and the pineal gland. Immunology and biochemistry supply the signals: IL-1 and PGE2 reset temperature, leptin and ghrelin report energy balance, thiamine pyrophosphate keeps memory relays supplied with ATP, and tryptophan feeds serotonin and melatonin."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology ties lesions to signs. Suprasellar tumors, viral encephalitis, nutritional deficiency, autoimmune neuron loss, and alpha-synuclein deposition each damage a different node and produce a recognizable syndrome. The limits matter: hypothalamic centers are networks rather than discrete points, human lesions are rarely confined to one nucleus, and sleep disorders are confirmed by polysomnography and history rather than by a single test."}],"summary":["Supraoptic and paraventricular neurons release ADH and oxytocin from the posterior pituitary; arcuate dopamine inhibits prolactin and arcuate GHRH stimulates GH.","Lateral hypothalamic lesions cause anorexia and ventromedial lesions cause hyperphagia, because each removes one of two opposed controllers; leptin acts on the arcuate nucleus to promote satiety.","The anterior hypothalamus drives heat loss and the posterior drives heat conservation, so their lesions cause hyperthermia and hypothermia or poikilothermia.","Fever is a raised set point: IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha induce COX-2 at the OVLT, and PGE2 on preoptic EP3 receptors raises the set point; hyperthermia has a normal set point.","The Papez circuit runs hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus, and back.","Bilateral hippocampal damage causes anterograde amnesia with old memories and skills spared; thiamine deficiency damages mammillary bodies and medial thalamus, giving Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff amnesia with confabulation.","Bilateral amygdala damage, often from HSV-1, causes Klüver-Bucy syndrome with hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity, and visual agnosia.","Melanopsin retinal ganglion cells reset the SCN, which controls pineal melatonin through a sympathetic relay; melatonin rises in darkness.","Loss of lateral hypothalamic orexin neurons causes narcolepsy type 1 with cataplexy, sleep-onset REM, sleep paralysis, and low CSF hypocretin.","REM sleep behavior disorder (late night, dream recall) often precedes synucleinopathy; N3 parasomnias (early night, no recall) affect children; restless legs syndrome is linked to iron deficiency."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Behavioral and Motivational Mechanisms of the Brain: The Limbic System and the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body Temperature Regulation and Fever"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"States of Brain Activity: Sleep, Brain Waves, Epilepsy, Psychoses, and Dementia"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The autonomic nervous system and hypothalamus; regulation of body temperature"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins (thiamine) and amino acid conversion to specialized products (serotonin and melatonin)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System: metabolic and toxic disorders and neurodegenerative diseases"}]},"NE-13":{"unit":"NE-13","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01","title":"Membrane Potentials, Action Potentials, and Synaptic Transmission","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how excitable cells hold a resting voltage, fire action potentials, integrate synaptic input, and conduct signals along axons and across the neuromuscular junction, then uses those mechanisms to explain seizures and hypokalemic periodic paralysis. Board questions in this area rarely ask for a definition alone. They describe a change, such as a shift in serum potassium, loss of myelin, an antibody, a toxin, or an abnormal EEG, and ask you to predict the effect on excitability or transmission. If you can reason from equilibrium potentials, Na+ channel gating, cable properties, and the steps of vesicle release, most of these questions become a short chain of cause and effect.","prerequisites":["Diffusion, electrochemical gradients, and primary active transport","Neuron structure and the myelin-forming glial cells (NE-2)","Skeletal muscle structure and excitation-contraction coupling","Serum electrolyte reference ranges for K+, Na+, Ca2+, and Mg2+","Antibody-mediated (type II) hypersensitivity"],"connections":[{"to":"Sensory receptors and reflexes (NE-5)","how":"Receptor potentials are graded potentials, and nerve fiber class sets how fast sensory and reflex signals travel."},{"to":"Autonomic nervous system (NE-9)","how":"The same acetylcholine release machinery works at autonomic ganglia and parasympathetic endings, so botulism and Lambert-Eaton syndrome produce autonomic signs."},{"to":"Cardiac electrophysiology (CV-7)","how":"Potassium, Na+ channel inactivation, and refractoriness explain both the cardiac and the neuromuscular effects of electrolyte disorders."},{"to":"Renal physiology and potassium balance","how":"Insulin, catecholamines, and kidney handling of K+ set the serum K+ that determines the resting potential."},{"to":"Immunology and microbiology","how":"Autoantibodies (myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton), molecular mimicry (Guillain-Barré syndrome), and Clostridium botulinum toxin each attack a different step of conduction or transmission."}],"terms":[{"term":"Resting membrane potential","def":"The steady voltage across the membrane of an unstimulated cell, inside negative, about -70 mV in neurons and about -90 mV in skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Equilibrium potential","def":"The membrane voltage at which the electrical force on an ion exactly balances its concentration gradient, so it has no net movement; calculated with the Nernst equation."},{"term":"Threshold","def":"The membrane voltage at which inward Na+ current exceeds outward current, so depolarization becomes self-sustaining and an action potential fires."},{"term":"Inactivation gate","def":"The part of the voltage-gated Na+ channel that closes shortly after depolarization opens the channel and reopens only after the membrane repolarizes."},{"term":"Refractory period","def":"The interval after an action potential during which a second action potential cannot fire (absolute) or needs a stronger stimulus (relative)."},{"term":"Postsynaptic potential","def":"A graded change in postsynaptic voltage caused by neurotransmitter, depolarizing (EPSP) or moving the cell away from threshold (IPSP)."},{"term":"Length constant","def":"The distance over which a passively spreading voltage falls to about 37% of its starting value; longer when membrane resistance is high and internal resistance is low."},{"term":"Saltatory conduction","def":"Conduction in myelinated axons in which the action potential is regenerated only at nodes of Ranvier, with current spreading rapidly through the myelinated internodes between them."},{"term":"Safety factor","def":"The margin by which the depolarizing current available at a node or end plate exceeds the amount needed to reach threshold."},{"term":"Quantum","def":"The amount of acetylcholine released by fusion of one synaptic vesicle, several thousand molecules."},{"term":"Paroxysmal depolarization shift","def":"A large, prolonged neuronal depolarization that triggers a burst of action potentials; the cellular event behind epileptiform discharges."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C01","obj":"NE-13.1","title":"The resting membrane potential and extracellular potassium","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every living cell holds a voltage across its membrane, inside negative, called the resting membrane potential (RMP). It requires unequal ion distribution and selective permeability. The Na+/K+ ATPase pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP, concentrating K+ inside and Na+ outside. The pump is electrogenic, but its direct contribution is only a few millivolts; its main role is to maintain the gradients that the channels use."},{"type":"p","text":"At rest the membrane is far more permeable to K+ than to other cations because K+ leak channels (two-pore domain and inward rectifier channels) stay open. K+ diffuses outward down its concentration gradient and leaves behind negative charge, mostly on impermeant proteins and organic phosphates. The growing negativity pulls K+ back, and the voltage at which this electrical pull exactly balances the chemical push is the K+ equilibrium potential (EK). The Nernst equation gives the equilibrium potential of any single ion: E = (61.5 / z) x log10 ([ion] outside / [ion] inside) mV at 37 °C (98.6 °F), where z is the ion's valence."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical concentrations and equilibrium potentials","cols":["Ion","Outside (mmol/L)","Inside (mmol/L)","Equilibrium potential"],"rows":[["K+","About 4","About 140","About -90 to -95 mV"],["Na+","About 142","About 14","About +60 mV"],["Cl-","About 105 to 110","About 5 to 10 in neurons","About -70 to -90 mV, close to rest"],["Ca2+ (free)","About 1.2","About 0.0001","About +120 mV or more"]],"caption":"The membrane is pulled toward the equilibrium potential of its most permeant ion, which at rest is K+."},{"type":"p","text":"The actual membrane potential is a weighted average of the equilibrium potentials, each weighted by that ion's permeability, which is what the Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation expresses. Because K+ permeability dominates, the RMP lies near EK, and a small Na+ leak holds it a little less negative: about -70 mV in a typical neuron and about -90 mV in skeletal muscle, where a large resting Cl- conductance also stabilizes it."},{"type":"h","text":"Extracellular potassium and excitability"},{"type":"p","text":"Intracellular K+ is so large that clinical disorders barely change it, so serum K+ (reference range about 3.5 to 5.0 mmol/L) is the main lever on EK and therefore on the RMP. Hyperkalemia shrinks the gradient and depolarizes cells. A mild rise brings the membrane closer to threshold and raises excitability, but sustained depolarization keeps voltage-gated Na+ channels inactivated (C02), so fewer are available, upstrokes become smaller and slower, and excitability falls. The result is weakness, paresthesias, and in the heart slowed conduction and arrhythmias. Hypokalemia widens the gradient and hyperpolarizes nerve and skeletal muscle, moving them away from threshold and causing weakness or flaccid paralysis. The heart is an exception: low extracellular K+ reduces inward rectifier K+ conductance, which delays repolarization and favors arrhythmias."},{"type":"p","text":"Extracellular Ca2+ works differently. It barely changes the RMP but binds the outer surface of Na+ channels and raises the voltage needed to open them, so hypocalcemia lowers threshold and causes paresthesias and tetany, while hypercalcemia dulls excitability."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: how far does EK move?","text":"Take intracellular K+ as 140 mmol/L. At serum K+ 4 mmol/L, log10 (4/140) = -1.54, so EK = 61.5 x -1.54 = about -95 mV. At 8 mmol/L, log10 (8/140) = -1.24, so EK = about -76 mV. Doubling extracellular K+ moves EK by 61.5 x log10 2, about 18.5 mV, toward zero, and the RMP follows by somewhat less because of the small Na+ permeability. The cell first sits closer to threshold, but held there its Na+ channels stay inactivated, so severe hyperkalemia ends in weakness and cardiac standstill rather than continuous firing."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Serum K+ falls from 4.0 to 2.0 mmol/L. Predict the change in skeletal muscle RMP and excitability.","explain":"EK becomes about 18 mV more negative, the RMP hyperpolarizes away from threshold, and the muscle is harder to fire, so weakness follows.","check":"The extracellular potassium paragraph and worked example."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C02","obj":"NE-13.2","title":"The action potential, threshold, and refractory periods","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An action potential (AP) is a brief, all-or-none reversal of membrane voltage that regenerates itself as it travels along an axon or muscle fiber. It depends on voltage-gated Na+ channels, which have two gates: an activation gate that opens quickly when the membrane depolarizes and an inactivation gate that closes a fraction of a millisecond later and stays closed until the membrane repolarizes. Voltage-gated K+ channels (delayed rectifiers) also open with depolarization, but more slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"Threshold is the voltage at which inward Na+ current through newly opened channels exceeds outward K+ and leak current, so depolarization becomes self-sustaining. In a typical neuron it is about -55 mV, roughly 15 mV above rest. Below threshold a stimulus produces only a local response that decays. Above it, positive feedback drives the upstroke regardless of stimulus size, which is why the AP is all-or-none and why stimulus strength is coded by firing frequency rather than spike height."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Positive feedback of the upstroke","steps":["Membrane depolarizes to threshold","Voltage-gated Na+ channels open","Na+ enters the cell","Membrane depolarizes further","More Na+ channels open"],"caption":"The loop drives the membrane toward ENa in well under a millisecond. Na+ channel inactivation and delayed K+ efflux break it."},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of the neuronal action potential","cols":["Phase","Channel events","Voltage"],"rows":[["Rest","K+ leak dominates; Na+ channels closed but ready","About -70 mV"],["Upstroke","Na+ activation gates open; Na+ influx","Peak about +30 to +40 mV, short of ENa"],["Repolarization","Na+ inactivation gates close; delayed K+ channels open; K+ efflux","Falls back toward rest"],["Afterhyperpolarization","K+ channels close slowly, so K+ conductance stays above resting","Briefly more negative than rest, toward EK"],["Recovery","Na+ inactivation gates reset as the membrane repolarizes","Returns to rest"]],"caption":"Repolarization needs both events: Na+ inactivation stops the inward current, and K+ efflux supplies the outward current."},{"type":"p","text":"The absolute refractory period lasts from threshold through most of repolarization. During it nearly all Na+ channels are either already open or inactivated, so no stimulus of any strength can produce a second AP. The relative refractory period follows. Some Na+ channels have reset, but K+ conductance is still elevated and the membrane is hyperpolarized, so only a stronger than normal stimulus fires the cell, and the AP it produces is smaller. Refractoriness limits the maximum firing rate and makes conduction one-way, because the membrane just behind an advancing AP cannot be re-excited."},{"type":"p","text":"Very few ions cross during a single AP, so concentrations barely change. The Na+/K+ ATPase restores them over many spikes and is not needed to repolarize each one. Nerve APs last about 1 ms and skeletal muscle APs a few milliseconds. Two exceptions confirm the gating logic. When a cell is depolarized slowly, Na+ channels inactivate before enough open together (accommodation), so threshold rises or is never reached, which is the same reason sustained hyperkalemia silences cells. Blocking Na+ channels, as tetrodotoxin does from outside and lidocaine-type local anesthetics do from inside, prevents the upstroke, whereas blocking K+ channels prolongs the AP."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C03","obj":"NE-13.3","title":"Graded potentials and summation at the axon hillock","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Graded potentials are local voltage changes whose size varies with stimulus strength. At chemical synapses they arise when neurotransmitter opens ligand-gated (ionotropic) channels on dendrites and the cell body. An excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP) is a depolarization, typically from glutamate opening AMPA receptor cation channels, which let more Na+ in than K+ out. NMDA receptors add a slower, Ca2+-carrying EPSP, but only when glutamate is bound and depolarization has expelled the Mg2+ that blocks the pore. An inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) moves the membrane away from threshold: GABA-A and glycine receptors open Cl- channels, and GABA-B receptors open K+ channels through a G protein."},{"type":"p","text":"Graded potentials spread passively (electrotonically) and shrink with distance and time because current leaks out across the membrane. The length constant is the distance over which voltage falls to about 37% of its starting value, and the time constant is the time the membrane takes to charge or discharge by about 63%. A single EPSP at a central synapse is usually well under the 10 to 20 mV needed to reach threshold, so one input rarely fires a neuron."},{"type":"table","title":"Graded potentials compared with action potentials","cols":["Feature","Graded potential (EPSP or IPSP)","Action potential"],"rows":[["Site","Dendrites and cell body","Starts at the axon initial segment, travels down the axon"],["Channels","Ligand-gated (or sensory transduction) channels","Voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels"],["Size","Proportional to stimulus","All-or-none, fixed size"],["Direction","Depolarizing or hyperpolarizing","Always a depolarizing upstroke"],["Over distance","Decays","Regenerated without loss"],["Summation and refractoriness","Sums; no refractory period","Does not sum; absolute then relative refractory period"]],"caption":"Graded potentials carry information by amplitude over short distances; action potentials carry it by frequency over long distances."},{"type":"h","text":"Summation and integration"},{"type":"p","text":"Temporal summation occurs when one synapse fires repeatedly before each EPSP has decayed, so successive potentials stack; a long time constant favors it. Spatial summation occurs when many synapses at different sites are active together and their currents add; a long length constant favors it. EPSPs and IPSPs sum algebraically. The point of integration is the axon hillock and initial segment, which has the highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels and therefore the lowest threshold in the neuron. The cell fires when, and as often as, the net voltage there exceeds threshold, so the neuron converts many graded inputs into one frequency code."},{"type":"p","text":"Inhibitory synapses on or near the soma are especially powerful because their effect reaches the initial segment with little decay. Opening Cl- channels inhibits even when ECl is close to the RMP and the voltage barely changes, because the open channels hold the membrane near ECl and drain away excitatory current (shunting inhibition). Loss of inhibition shows how much it matters: tetanus toxin blocks glycine and GABA release from spinal inhibitory interneurons and strychnine blocks glycine receptors, and both leave motor neurons firing unopposed, with rigidity and spasms."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C04","obj":"NE-13.4","title":"Myelin, axon diameter, and conduction velocity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Conduction velocity depends on how quickly current from the active region brings the next stretch of membrane to threshold. That depends on how far current spreads before leaking out, which is the length constant (longer when membrane resistance is high and internal resistance is low), and on how fast the membrane charges, which is faster when membrane capacitance is low. A wider axon has lower internal (axial) resistance, so velocity rises with diameter: roughly with the square root of diameter in unmyelinated fibers and roughly in proportion to diameter in myelinated fibers."},{"type":"p","text":"Myelin is a spiral of glial membrane, formed by oligodendrocytes in the CNS (one cell myelinates segments of many axons) and Schwann cells in the PNS (one cell per internode). The many wrapped layers raise membrane resistance and lower capacitance, so little current leaks out or is spent charging the internode. Voltage-gated Na+ channels cluster at the nodes of Ranvier and are sparse under myelin, so the AP is regenerated only at nodes while current spreads rapidly through the internode to the next node. This saltatory conduction is faster and uses less energy, because ion exchange, and therefore pump work, is confined to the nodes."},{"type":"table","title":"Nerve fiber classes (Erlanger-Gasser)","cols":["Class","Myelin and diameter","Velocity","Examples"],"rows":[["A alpha","Heavy, about 12 to 20 µm","About 70 to 120 m/s","Alpha motor neurons; Ia and Ib afferents"],["A beta","Heavy, about 6 to 12 µm","About 30 to 70 m/s","Touch, pressure, vibration"],["A delta","Thin, about 1 to 5 µm","About 5 to 30 m/s","Fast sharp pain, cold"],["B","Thin, under about 3 µm","About 3 to 15 m/s","Preganglionic autonomic"],["C","Unmyelinated, about 0.2 to 1.5 µm","About 0.5 to 2 m/s","Slow burning pain, warmth, postganglionic autonomic"]],"caption":"Values are approximate and vary slightly between sources."},{"type":"p","text":"Normally the current reaching a node is several times what is needed to fire it (the safety factor). When myelin is lost, current leaks across the bare internode and is spent charging its now higher capacitance, and that membrane has too few Na+ channels to regenerate the spike. Conduction slows when the safety factor is reduced and blocks when too little current reaches the next node. Heat speeds Na+ channel inactivation and shortens the inward current, tipping marginal fibers into block, which explains the Uhthoff phenomenon, a transient worsening of symptoms with exercise or a hot bath."},{"type":"flow","title":"From demyelination to conduction block","steps":["Myelin lost from an internode","Membrane resistance falls and capacitance rises","Current leaks out before reaching the next node","Next node depolarizes slowly or not to threshold","Conduction slows, or fails completely"],"caption":"Slowing reflects a reduced safety factor; block occurs when the safety factor falls below one."},{"type":"p","text":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is immune-mediated destruction of CNS myelin and oligodendrocytes, producing plaques in white matter. Deficits follow plaque location, such as optic neuritis or internuclear ophthalmoplegia from a medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion, and evoked potentials show delayed latencies. CNS remyelination is limited and axons are lost over time, so disability accumulates. Guillain-Barré syndrome in its common demyelinating form targets peripheral nerve myelin and roots, often after Campylobacter jejuni or viral infection, probably through molecular mimicry. It causes ascending, symmetric flaccid weakness with lost reflexes, and nerve conduction studies show slowed velocity, prolonged latencies, and conduction block. CSF protein is high with a normal cell count, and because Schwann cells remyelinate, most patients recover over weeks to months."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In demyelinating Guillain-Barré syndrome, which fail first: reflexes and vibration sense, or pain and temperature sense?","explain":"Reflexes, vibration, and motor function depend on large myelinated fibers that rely on saltatory conduction. Unmyelinated C fibers never used myelin, so pain and temperature are relatively spared.","check":"Compare with the fiber class table and the paragraph on the safety factor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C05","obj":"NE-13.5","title":"Neuromuscular transmission and its disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The neuromuscular junction (NMJ) is a cholinergic synapse between a motor neuron terminal and the motor end plate, a folded region of the muscle membrane."},{"type":"flow","title":"Steps of neuromuscular transmission","steps":["Motor nerve AP depolarizes the terminal","P/Q-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open; Ca2+ enters","Ca2+ binds synaptotagmin; SNARE proteins fuse vesicles","Acetylcholine (ACh) diffuses across the cleft","ACh opens nicotinic receptor cation channels","End-plate potential fires a muscle AP","Acetylcholinesterase splits ACh; choline is recycled"],"caption":"Botulinum toxin blocks step 3, Lambert-Eaton antibodies step 2, and myasthenia gravis antibodies step 5."},{"type":"p","text":"ACh is made in the terminal from choline and acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase and packed into vesicles. Each vesicle releases one quantum of several thousand ACh molecules; a single spontaneous vesicle produces a miniature end-plate potential of about 0.5 mV. Fusion requires SNARE proteins: synaptobrevin on the vesicle zips together with syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the plasma membrane, and Ca2+ binding to synaptotagmin triggers fusion. Release rises steeply with Ca2+ entry, so a modest fall in Ca2+ influx sharply reduces release."},{"type":"p","text":"The adult muscle nicotinic receptor is a pentamer of two alpha-1 subunits with beta, delta, and epsilon subunits; ACh must bind both alpha subunits to open its nonselective cation channel. Na+ entry exceeds K+ exit, producing the end-plate potential, which fires a muscle AP in the adjacent Na+ channel-rich membrane. An impulse releases several dozen quanta or more, depending on the muscle, so the end-plate potential is normally several times larger than needed (the safety factor). Acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic basal lamina splits ACh into acetate and choline within milliseconds, and choline is recycled by a Na+-coupled transporter. If the enzyme is inhibited, as in organophosphate poisoning, the end plate stays depolarized, nearby Na+ channels inactivate, and fasciculations give way to weakness (depolarizing block)."},{"type":"table","title":"Disorders of neuromuscular transmission","cols":["Disorder","Mechanism","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Botulism (presynaptic)","Toxin, a zinc protease, cleaves SNARE proteins (A and E: SNAP-25; B: synaptobrevin); no ACh release","Descending flaccid paralysis starting in cranial nerves; dilated pupils, dry mouth; sensation intact"],["Myasthenia gravis (postsynaptic)","IgG against nicotinic receptors (some against MuSK); complement damage and receptor internalization","Ptosis, diplopia, bulbar and proximal weakness worse with use; pupils and reflexes normal; thymic hyperplasia or thymoma"],["Lambert-Eaton syndrome (presynaptic)","IgG against P/Q-type Ca2+ channels; less Ca2+ entry and release","Proximal leg weakness that briefly improves with effort; reduced reflexes; dry mouth; often small cell lung carcinoma"]],"caption":"Presynaptic disorders lower release; myasthenia gravis lowers the response to normal release."},{"type":"p","text":"Quanta per impulse normally decline during repeated firing as the readily releasable store depletes. This is harmless with a normal safety factor, but in myasthenia gravis more and more fibers fall below threshold, so weakness worsens with use. In Lambert-Eaton syndrome and botulism release is low, so baseline strength and reflexes fall, but rapid firing lets residual Ca2+ build up in the terminal and transiently increases release (facilitation). Botulinum toxin also blocks autonomic cholinergic endings, which explains the dilated pupils and dry mouth."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: repetitive nerve stimulation","text":"A motor nerve is stimulated at 3 Hz while the compound muscle action potential (CMAP) is recorded. Patient A has a normal first CMAP that falls by more than 10% by the fourth or fifth response. Patient B has a small first CMAP that more than doubles after 10 seconds of maximal contraction. In A, release is normal but receptors are few, so as quanta per impulse decline over the first stimuli, fibers drop below threshold one by one: a decrement, myasthenia gravis. In B, so few quanta are released at rest that many fibers fail from the start; exercise loads the terminal with Ca2+, release surges, and silent fibers are recruited: an increment, Lambert-Eaton syndrome."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would inhibiting acetylcholinesterase enlarge the end-plate potential more in myasthenia gravis or botulism?","explain":"Myasthenia gravis: normal ACh release, and a longer life lets each molecule reach the scarce receptors repeatedly. In botulism little ACh is released, so there is little to preserve.","check":"The transmission flow and disorders table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C06","obj":"NE-13.6","title":"Seizures: classification and mechanisms","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A seizure is a transient episode of signs or symptoms caused by abnormally excessive, synchronous neuronal activity in the brain; epilepsy is a lasting tendency to recurrent unprovoked seizures. Cortex is normally stable because glutamate excitation is balanced by GABA inhibition, much of it from interneurons that surround active neurons and keep activity from spreading (surround inhibition). Anything that raises excitation or lowers inhibition can tip neurons into synchronous firing."},{"type":"p","text":"The cellular hallmark is the paroxysmal depolarization shift: a large, prolonged depolarization driven by glutamate (AMPA and NMDA) and Ca2+ currents that fires a burst of APs and is normally ended by a GABA-mediated afterhyperpolarization. Many neurons doing this together produce an interictal EEG spike. A seizure begins when afterhyperpolarization and surround inhibition fail, so bursting recruits neighbors and spreads, helped by K+ that accumulates outside active cells (C01). Active inhibition, adenosine release, and metabolic exhaustion end the seizure and contribute to postictal depression."},{"type":"table","title":"Conditions that tip the excitation and inhibition balance","cols":["Condition","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hyponatremia","Water enters brain cells, causing cerebral edema, worst when the fall is rapid"],["Hypoglycemia","Energy failure impairs ion pumping and transmitter balance"],["Hypocalcemia","Na+ channels open at more negative voltages, lowering threshold"],["Hypomagnesemia","Mg2+ block of NMDA receptors is relieved; also lowers serum Ca2+"],["Alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal","GABA-A signaling is downregulated during chronic use, so inhibition suddenly falls"],["SCN1A loss of function (Dravet syndrome)","Inhibitory interneurons fire poorly, so inhibition fails"],["Structural lesions (scar, tumor, stroke, hippocampal sclerosis)","Interneurons are lost and excitatory circuits reorganize"]],"caption":"Metabolic causes provoke seizures until corrected; lesions create a lasting focus."},{"type":"table","title":"Major seizure types (ILAE 2017 terms)","cols":["Type","Onset","Features","Afterward and EEG"],"rows":[["Focal aware","One hemisphere network","Awareness kept; symptoms match the area (motor jerking that marches along the homunculus, sensory, autonomic, or psychic aura)","Todd paralysis may follow a motor seizure"],["Focal impaired awareness","One network, often mesial temporal lobe","Aura (rising epigastric feeling, deja vu), staring, automatisms such as lip smacking, 1 to 2 minutes","Postictal confusion"],["Focal to bilateral tonic-clonic","Focal, then both hemispheres","Focal signs, then tonic-clonic activity","Postictal confusion"],["Generalized tonic-clonic","Both hemispheres from onset","Sudden loss of consciousness, tonic stiffening, then clonic jerking; lateral tongue biting, incontinence","Postictal confusion and drowsiness"],["Absence (typical)","Bilateral thalamocortical","Seconds of staring with abrupt start and stop, many per day, provoked by hyperventilation; childhood onset","No postictal state; 3 Hz spike-and-wave"]],"caption":"Older terms: simple partial, complex partial, and secondarily generalized seizures."},{"type":"p","text":"Absence seizures arise from an oscillation in thalamocortical circuits that depends on T-type (low-threshold) Ca2+ channels in thalamic relay neurons. These channels can open only after hyperpolarization from the GABAergic thalamic reticular nucleus has removed their inactivation, as the cycle below shows. Blocking T-type channels, the action of ethosuximide, abolishes the rhythm."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The thalamocortical loop in absence seizures","steps":["Reticular nucleus GABA neurons inhibit thalamic relay neurons","Hyperpolarization removes T-type Ca2+ channel inactivation","Rebound Ca2+ spike triggers a burst of APs","Synchronous cortical excitation (EEG spike)","Cortex re-excites reticular nucleus; inhibition returns (EEG wave)"],"caption":"Each turn takes about a third of a second, giving 3 Hz spike-and-wave."},{"type":"p","text":"A seizure lasting 5 minutes or more, or repeated seizures without recovery between them, is status epilepticus; sustained firing injures neurons through NMDA-mediated Ca2+ entry (excitotoxicity). Febrile seizures occur in children about 6 months to 5 years old with a temperature of 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher, without CNS infection, metabolic cause, or earlier afebrile seizure. Fever raises excitability in an immature brain whose inhibition is still developing, and family history is common. A simple febrile seizure is generalized, lasts under 15 minutes, and does not recur within 24 hours; a complex one is focal, longer, or repeated. About a third recur with a later fever, but epilepsy risk after a simple febrile seizure is only slightly above the general population."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-13-01-C07","obj":"NE-13.7","title":"Hypokalemic periodic paralysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypokalemic periodic paralysis (HypoPP) is an inherited channelopathy of skeletal muscle, usually autosomal dominant with reduced penetrance in women. Most cases are caused by mutations in the skeletal muscle L-type Ca2+ channel (CACNA1S, Cav1.1, the dihydropyridine receptor), and a smaller group by mutations in the skeletal muscle Na+ channel (SCN4A, Nav1.4). Attacks usually begin in adolescence or early adulthood and bring flaccid weakness lasting hours to a day or more. Serum K+ is low during the attack, often below 3.0 mmol/L, and normal between attacks."},{"type":"p","text":"The mutations replace positively charged arginines in the S4 voltage-sensing segment, opening an abnormal leak path through the voltage sensor itself, the gating pore (omega) current, which carries a small inward cation current at rest. Normally, lowering extracellular K+ hyperpolarizes muscle (C01). However, inward rectifier K+ conductance falls as extracellular K+ falls, and in HypoPP fibers the inward gating pore current then outweighs the shrinking K+ current. Instead of hyperpolarizing, the fiber paradoxically depolarizes to about -60 mV. At that voltage its Na+ channels are inactivated (C02), so it cannot fire an AP and becomes flaccid and electrically inexcitable."},{"type":"flow","title":"How an attack develops","steps":["Carbohydrate meal raises insulin, or rest follows exercise","Na+/K+ ATPase drives K+ into cells; serum K+ falls","Inward rectifier K+ conductance decreases","Gating pore leak paradoxically depolarizes the fiber","Voltage-gated Na+ channels inactivate","Fiber cannot fire: flaccid weakness"],"caption":"Low serum K+ is both the trigger and a marker; the mutant channel turns a fall in K+ into depolarization."},{"type":"p","text":"Triggers act by shifting K+ into cells, not by removing it from the body. Insulin released after a carbohydrate-rich meal and catecholamines both stimulate the Na+/K+ ATPase, and K+ released from muscle during exercise is taken back up during rest. Attacks often begin on waking after a large evening meal or strenuous exercise. Weakness is mainly proximal, reflexes are reduced during the attack, sensation is normal, and respiratory and cranial muscles are usually spared. During an attack the ECG may show hypokalemic changes such as flattened T waves and U waves. Total body K+ is normal, and some patients later develop a fixed proximal myopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing causes of paralysis with abnormal potassium","cols":["Condition","Cause","Serum K+ in attack","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Hypokalemic periodic paralysis","Inherited CACNA1S or SCN4A gating pore mutation","Low","Carbohydrate meals or rest after exercise; normal K+ between attacks"],["Hyperkalemic periodic paralysis","Inherited SCN4A mutation that impairs Na+ channel inactivation","High or normal","Shorter attacks after K+-rich food, fasting, or rest after exercise; myotonia may occur"],["Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis","Acquired; thyroid hormone increases Na+/K+ ATPase activity","Low","Mostly young men of Asian descent; signs of hyperthyroidism; attacks stop when thyroid function normalizes"],["Hypokalemia from K+ loss","Renal or gastrointestinal loss, such as diuretics, vomiting, or diarrhea","Low","Total body K+ depleted; weakness persists until K+ is replaced"]],"caption":"Periodic paralyses are shifts of K+ across cell membranes in a muscle that responds abnormally; ordinary hypokalemia is a true deficit."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every topic in this chapter rests on the same few mechanisms. The Na+/K+ ATPase builds the gradients, K+ leak channels set the RMP, and voltage-gated Na+ channels with their inactivation gates decide whether a cell can fire. Hyperkalemia, accommodation, organophosphate poisoning, hyperkalemic periodic paralysis, and HypoPP all silence muscle the same way, by holding the membrane depolarized until Na+ channels inactivate. Anatomy and histology add the glial cells that make myelin, which explains why MS (oligodendrocytes) and Guillain-Barré syndrome (Schwann cells) differ in location and recovery."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and microbiology supply the agents that attack each step: autoantibodies against the nicotinic receptor or P/Q-type Ca2+ channel, molecular mimicry after Campylobacter infection, and the SNARE-cleaving toxins of Clostridium botulinum and Clostridium tetani. Pathology and genetics add channelopathies, from SCN1A in Dravet syndrome to CACNA1S in HypoPP, in which one altered channel changes excitability throughout a tissue. The limits matter as well: serum values describe the extracellular space only, nerve conduction and EEG findings support but do not by themselves make a diagnosis."}],"summary":["The RMP lies near EK because K+ leak channels dominate resting permeability; the Na+/K+ ATPase maintains the gradients and adds only a few millivolts directly.","Nernst: E = (61.5 / z) x log10 (outside / inside); EK is about -90 to -95 mV and ENa about +60 mV, and doubling serum K+ moves EK about 18 mV toward zero.","Hyperkalemia depolarizes and, if sustained, inactivates Na+ channels and lowers excitability; hypokalemia hyperpolarizes nerve and skeletal muscle and causes weakness.","The AP upstroke is Na+ positive feedback; Na+ inactivation plus delayed K+ efflux repolarize; inactivation sets the absolute refractory period and lingering K+ conductance the relative one.","Graded potentials decay and sum; the axon initial segment, with the densest Na+ channels, integrates EPSPs and IPSPs and fires when net voltage exceeds threshold.","Velocity rises with axon diameter and with myelin, which raises membrane resistance and lowers capacitance so APs regenerate only at nodes; demyelination slows or blocks conduction.","MS strips CNS myelin with limited repair; demyelinating Guillain-Barré syndrome strips peripheral myelin, gives ascending areflexic weakness and high CSF protein, and usually recovers.","At the NMJ, botulinum toxin cleaves SNAREs, Lambert-Eaton antibodies block P/Q Ca2+ channels (facilitation with effort), and myasthenia gravis antibodies remove nicotinic receptors (fatigue with use).","Seizures are synchronous firing when excitation outweighs inhibition; absence seizures come from T-type Ca2+ thalamocortical oscillation with 3 Hz spike-and-wave and no postictal state.","Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is a gating pore channelopathy in which an insulin or post-exercise K+ shift paradoxically depolarizes muscle, inactivating Na+ channels and causing flaccid weakness."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Electrophysiology of the cell membrane; synaptic transmission and the neuromuscular junction"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles; The Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spore-forming gram-positive bacilli, Clostridium botulinum"}]},"NE-14":{"unit":"NE-14","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01","title":"Neurological Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the nervous system's biochemistry makes it vulnerable to fuel shortage, vitamin deficiency, ammonia, inherited lipid storage, and lead. Neurons need a continuous supply of oxidizable fuel, depend on vitamin cofactors to make ATP, neurotransmitters, and myelin, and rely on astrocytes and lysosomes to remove what they cannot use. Board questions usually describe a neurologic picture with a dietary, laboratory, or family clue and ask for the enzyme or cofactor at fault. If you can follow each finding back to the reaction that failed, you can reason through hypoglycemia, Wernicke encephalopathy, subacute combined degeneration, hepatic encephalopathy, the sphingolipidoses, and lead poisoning.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, pyruvate dehydrogenase, the TCA cycle, and the pentose phosphate pathway","Ketone body synthesis in the liver during fasting","Urea cycle and amino acid transamination","Heme synthesis pathway","Myelin formation by oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells (NE-2) and spinal cord tracts (NE-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neuron and nerve histology (NE-2)","how":"Myelin structure explains why lipid storage disorders, B12 deficiency, and vitamin E deficiency injure white matter and long axons."},{"to":"Somatic motor and sensory pathways (NE-7)","how":"The dorsal columns, corticospinal tracts, and spinocerebellar tracts damaged by B12 and vitamin E deficiency produce the bedside signs used to localize these lesions."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal and hepatic physiology","how":"Liver failure, portosystemic shunting, ileal disease, and fat malabsorption are the usual routes to hyperammonemia and to B12 and vitamin E deficiency."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Megaloblastic anemia, sideroblastic anemia, and basophilic stippling share enzyme defects with the neurologic disorders in this chapter."},{"to":"Endocrine physiology","how":"Insulin, glucagon, and epinephrine control the blood glucose that the brain cannot store, and counterregulation produces the warning symptoms of hypoglycemia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Neuroglycopenia","def":"Brain dysfunction caused by too little glucose reaching neurons, ranging from confusion to seizures and coma."},{"term":"Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)","def":"The active form of vitamin B1, a cofactor that carries activated aldehyde groups in oxidative decarboxylation and transketolase reactions."},{"term":"Methyl-folate trap","def":"Accumulation of folate as N5-methyltetrahydrofolate when B12-dependent methionine synthase fails, starving DNA synthesis of usable folate."},{"term":"Subacute combined degeneration","def":"Demyelination of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts of the spinal cord in vitamin B12 deficiency."},{"term":"Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)","def":"The active form of vitamin B6, a cofactor for amino acid decarboxylases, transaminases, and ALA synthase."},{"term":"Glutamine synthetase","def":"The astrocyte enzyme that combines glutamate and ammonia into glutamine, the brain's main route for removing ammonia."},{"term":"Alzheimer type II astrocyte","def":"A gray matter astrocyte with an enlarged, pale, vesicular nucleus, the histologic marker of chronic hyperammonemia; unrelated to Alzheimer disease."},{"term":"Sphingolipid","def":"A membrane lipid built on ceramide (sphingosine plus a fatty acid), including sphingomyelin, cerebrosides, sulfatides, and gangliosides."},{"term":"Sphingolipidosis","def":"An inherited lysosomal storage disease in which one missing hydrolase lets its sphingolipid substrate accumulate."},{"term":"Leukodystrophy","def":"An inherited disorder in which myelin forms abnormally or is destroyed, causing progressive white matter loss."},{"term":"Zinc protoporphyrin","def":"Protoporphyrin IX carrying zinc instead of iron, which rises when ferrochelatase is inhibited or iron is scarce."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C01","obj":"NE-14.1","title":"Brain fuel: glucose, ketone bodies, and hypoglycemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brain is about 2% of body weight but uses about 20% of resting oxygen consumption and roughly 120 g of glucose a day in a fed adult. Almost all of this energy comes from complete oxidation of glucose. Brain glycogen, held mainly in astrocytes, would last only minutes without fresh supply, so neuronal function tracks blood glucose."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose crosses the blood-brain barrier by facilitated diffusion through GLUT1 on capillary endothelial cells (and on astrocytes), then enters neurons through GLUT3. Both transporters have a high affinity for glucose, so uptake is adequate across the normal range (fasting about 70 to 99 mg/dL, 3.9 to 5.5 mmol/L), and neither requires insulin. This lets the brain keep taking up glucose when insulin is low during fasting, while insulin-dependent GLUT4 in muscle and fat shuts down. The flip side is that the brain cannot acutely raise uptake when blood glucose falls, because transport is passive and follows the concentration gradient. In GLUT1 deficiency syndrome, too little glucose crosses the barrier, cerebrospinal fluid glucose is low while blood glucose is normal, and infants develop seizures and delayed development."},{"type":"p","text":"Long-chain fatty acids are not a meaningful neuronal fuel: they cross the barrier slowly while bound to albumin, and neurons have little capacity for beta-oxidation. Ketone bodies solve this problem in fasting. As insulin falls and glucagon rises, the liver oxidizes fatty acids to acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate. These cross the barrier through monocarboxylate transporters (MCT1), and brain mitochondria convert them back to acetyl-CoA using succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase, an enzyme the liver lacks (so the liver exports ketones rather than using them). Uptake rises with blood level, so ketones contribute little after an overnight fast but supply about two-thirds of brain energy after several weeks of starvation. This lowers the brain's glucose demand and therefore the amount of muscle protein broken down for gluconeogenesis."},{"type":"table","title":"Brain fuel across the fed and fasted states","cols":["State","Main brain fuel","Glucose source","Why"],"rows":[["Fed","Glucose","Dietary carbohydrate","Insulin high; ketogenesis suppressed"],["Overnight fast","Glucose","Hepatic glycogenolysis, then gluconeogenesis","Ketones still low, so uptake is small"],["Several days of fasting","Glucose plus rising ketones","Gluconeogenesis from amino acids, lactate, glycerol","Ketone levels rise and MCT1 uptake increases"],["Weeks of starvation","Ketones (about two-thirds), glucose (about one-third)","Gluconeogenesis, now reduced","Spares muscle protein"]],"caption":"The brain never stops needing some glucose, but ketone bodies replace most of it in prolonged fasting."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoglycemia produces symptoms in a predictable order because thresholds are hierarchical. Insulin secretion falls first (at about 80 mg/dL, 4.4 mmol/L), then glucagon and epinephrine rise at about 65 to 70 mg/dL (3.6 to 3.9 mmol/L). Autonomic symptoms follow at about 55 mg/dL (3.0 mmol/L): adrenergic tremor, palpitations, and anxiety, and cholinergic sweating, hunger, and tingling. Below about 50 mg/dL (2.8 mmol/L) neurons run short of ATP, and neuroglycopenic symptoms appear: confusion, difficulty concentrating, behavior change, blurred or double vision, slurred speech, focal deficits, then seizures and coma. Prolonged severe hypoglycemia causes selective neuronal death in the cortex, hippocampus, and striatum, driven partly by glutamate excitotoxicity. Recurrent episodes shift the thresholds lower, so neuroglycopenia can arrive without warning (hypoglycemia unawareness), and beta blockade masks adrenergic warnings but not cholinergic sweating."},{"type":"flow","title":"Falling blood glucose and the order of responses","steps":["Blood glucose falls","Insulin secretion falls (about 80 mg/dL)","Glucagon and epinephrine rise (about 65 to 70 mg/dL)","Autonomic warning symptoms (about 55 mg/dL)","Neuronal ATP falls (below about 50 mg/dL)","Confusion, behavior change, visual and speech disturbance","Seizures, coma, selective neuronal death"],"caption":"Counterregulation and autonomic warnings normally appear before neuroglycopenia; thresholds are approximate and shift with recurrent hypoglycemia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C02","obj":"NE-14.2","title":"Thiamine: TPP enzymes, Wernicke encephalopathy, and beriberi","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) is absorbed in the small intestine and phosphorylated inside cells by thiamine pyrophosphokinase to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP). Body stores are small, so a poor diet empties them within weeks. Chronic alcohol use is the usual cause in developed countries because it reduces intake, intestinal absorption, hepatic storage, and phosphorylation at the same time. Other settings include hyperemesis gravidarum, bariatric surgery, prolonged vomiting, refeeding after starvation, and diets of polished rice."},{"type":"table","title":"Enzymes that need thiamine pyrophosphate","cols":["Enzyme","Reaction","Effect of deficiency"],"rows":[["Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (links glycolysis to the TCA cycle)","Pyruvate diverted to lactate; less acetyl-CoA and ATP"],["Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA (TCA cycle)","TCA cycle slows; alpha-ketoglutarate and glutamate accumulate"],["Branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase","Breakdown of leucine, isoleucine, valine ketoacids","Minor role in acquired deficiency"],["Transketolase","Nonoxidative pentose phosphate pathway","Pentose and glycolytic intermediates interconvert poorly; red cell transketolase activity falls"]],"caption":"The two dehydrogenases dominate the neurologic picture because they control aerobic ATP production from glucose. Red cell transketolase activity is a functional test of thiamine status."},{"type":"p","text":"Without TPP, glucose can still be broken down to pyruvate, but the pyruvate cannot enter the TCA cycle, and the cycle itself stalls at alpha-ketoglutarate. Tissues that depend on aerobic glucose oxidation, chiefly neurons and myocardium, lose ATP and produce lactate. Neurons and astrocytes in regions with high metabolic turnover then fail through a combination of energy failure, focal lactic acidosis, glutamate excitotoxicity, and breakdown of the blood-brain barrier."},{"type":"p","text":"Wernicke encephalopathy is the acute result. The lesions are symmetric and lie around the third ventricle, aqueduct, and fourth ventricle: the mammillary bodies, medial thalamus, periaqueductal gray, and the ocular motor and vestibular nuclei. These sites explain the classic triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia with nystagmus (often lateral rectus weakness from abducens nucleus involvement), and gait ataxia (vestibular and superior vermis involvement). Only a minority of patients show all three, so the diagnosis is suspected from any part of the triad in an at-risk patient. Korsakoff syndrome is the chronic sequel when damage to the medial thalamus and mammillary bodies persists: severe anterograde amnesia, patchy retrograde amnesia, and confabulation with otherwise preserved alertness."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral nerves fail more slowly. Dry beriberi is a symmetric, length-dependent axonal sensorimotor neuropathy, worse in the legs, with distal weakness, wasting, and lost ankle reflexes. Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from peripheral vasodilation combined with impaired myocardial energy production."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why glucose before thiamine worsens Wernicke encephalopathy","steps":["Thiamine-depleted patient receives a glucose load","Glycolytic flux to pyruvate rises","PDH and alpha-KGDH consume remaining TPP","Pyruvate shunted to lactate; ATP falls further","Vulnerable periventricular neurons fail","Wernicke encephalopathy appears or worsens"],"caption":"A carbohydrate load raises demand for a cofactor that is already scarce, so thiamine must be replaced before or with glucose."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A malnourished patient with alcohol use disorder is given intravenous dextrose alone and becomes more confused, with new nystagmus. Which two enzymes are most responsible, and why did dextrose make things worse?","explain":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. The dextrose increased flux toward pyruvate and the TCA cycle, using up the last TPP, so neurons in the periventricular gray lost aerobic ATP production faster.","check":"The TPP enzyme table and the glucose-before-thiamine flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C03","obj":"NE-14.3","title":"Vitamin B12 and folate: two enzymes, two laboratory patterns, one myelopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is a cofactor for only two human enzymes, and both explain its clinical picture. Methionine synthase, in the cytosol, uses methylcobalamin to move a methyl group from N5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, forming methionine and regenerating tetrahydrofolate (THF). Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, in mitochondria, uses adenosylcobalamin to convert L-methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. That second reaction is the final step in breaking down propionyl-CoA, which comes from odd-chain fatty acids and from valine, isoleucine, methionine, and threonine."},{"type":"table","title":"The two B12-dependent enzymes","cols":["Enzyme","B12 form","Also needs folate?","Substrate that rises when blocked"],"rows":[["Methionine synthase","Methylcobalamin","Yes (N5-methyl-THF is the methyl donor)","Homocysteine"],["Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","Adenosylcobalamin","No","Methylmalonic acid (from methylmalonyl-CoA)"]],"caption":"Homocysteine rises in deficiency of either vitamin; methylmalonic acid rises only in B12 deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"When methionine synthase stalls, folate accumulates as N5-methyl-THF, which cannot be converted back to other folate forms (the methyl-folate trap). The cell then lacks N5,N10-methylene-THF for thymidylate synthesis, DNA replication lags behind cytoplasmic growth, and marrow precursors become megaloblastic. Folate deficiency reaches the same DNA defect directly, so the two anemias look identical on a smear: macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils, and ineffective erythropoiesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Only B12 deficiency damages the nervous system in adults. The exact mechanism is still debated. The leading explanations are reduced S-adenosylmethionine, which is needed to methylate myelin basic protein and phospholipids, and incorporation of abnormal odd-chain and branched fatty acids into myelin when methylmalonyl-CoA and propionyl-CoA accumulate. The characteristic lesion is subacute combined degeneration: vacuolar demyelination, then axonal loss, beginning in the dorsal columns of the cervical and upper thoracic cord and spreading to the lateral corticospinal tracts. Dorsal column loss produces impaired vibration and position sense, sensory ataxia, and a positive Romberg sign. Corticospinal loss produces spastic weakness and Babinski signs. A coexisting peripheral neuropathy can depress the ankle reflexes, so extensor plantar responses with absent ankle jerks is a classic combination. Cognitive decline, depression, and optic neuropathy can also occur, and neurologic damage can appear before, or without, anemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Folate supplements bypass the methyl-folate trap by supplying folate that is not methylated, so they restore DNA synthesis and correct the anemia of B12 deficiency. They do not restore methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or methionine synthase, so the myelopathy continues and the hematologic clue that would prompt testing disappears. Nitrous oxide oxidizes the cobalt in cobalamin and inactivates methionine synthase, which is why nitrous oxide exposure can precipitate subacute combined degeneration in someone with borderline stores. Folate deficiency in pregnancy matters for neural tube closure in the embryo, but that is a developmental effect, not myelin injury in the mother."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the laboratory pattern","text":"A 64-year-old has macrocytic anemia and hypersegmented neutrophils. Both deficiencies impair DNA synthesis, so the smear cannot separate them. Homocysteine is elevated, which fits either, because methionine synthase needs both vitamins. Methylmalonic acid is also elevated. Only methylmalonyl-CoA mutase can explain that, and it needs B12 but not folate, so this is B12 deficiency. Expect dorsal column and corticospinal signs now or later. If methylmalonic acid were normal with high homocysteine, folate deficiency would be the answer and the spinal cord would be spared. Note that renal failure can raise both metabolites, so the pattern is read with kidney function in mind."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C04","obj":"NE-14.4","title":"Vitamin B6, niacin, and vitamin E","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) acts as pyridoxal phosphate (PLP), which forms a Schiff base with amino acids and stabilizes the intermediates of decarboxylation and transamination. Neurotransmitter synthesis depends on it. Glutamate decarboxylase makes GABA, aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase makes dopamine from L-DOPA and serotonin from 5-hydroxytryptophan, and histidine decarboxylase makes histamine. PLP is also needed by ALA synthase, the first step of heme synthesis, and by kynureninase in the pathway that makes niacin from tryptophan."},{"type":"p","text":"When PLP falls, GABA synthesis is the most consequential loss: inhibition fails relative to excitation, and seizures follow, most dramatically in infants. Deficiency also produces a sensory-predominant peripheral neuropathy, irritability, and confusion, plus sideroblastic anemia from impaired ALA synthase and cheilosis and glossitis. Isoniazid causes deficiency by forming an inactive hydrazone with pyridoxal and inhibiting pyridoxal kinase. An important exception is that chronic high-dose pyridoxine is itself toxic to dorsal root ganglion neurons and causes a sensory neuronopathy, so both too little and too much B6 injure sensory nerves."},{"type":"p","text":"Niacin (vitamin B3) is the precursor of NAD+ and NADP+, the electron carriers for hundreds of oxidation-reduction reactions, including glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and the respiratory chain. It can also be made from tryptophan in a pathway that needs B6 and riboflavin, at roughly 60 mg of tryptophan per 1 mg of niacin. Deficiency, pellagra, therefore follows either low niacin intake or reduced tryptophan supply. High-turnover and high-energy tissues fail: photosensitive dermatitis in sun-exposed skin, diarrhea from intestinal epithelial loss, and dementia with depression, insomnia, and confusion progressing to delirium. Large cortical neurons, including Betz cells, show chromatolysis. Causes include a maize-based diet (niacin bound in an unavailable form, and maize is low in tryptophan), Hartnup disease (defective neutral amino acid transporter in gut and kidney), carcinoid syndrome (tryptophan diverted to serotonin), isoniazid, and alcohol use disorder."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) is a lipid-soluble, chain-breaking antioxidant that sits in membranes and stops lipid peroxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids. Deficiency is almost always secondary to fat malabsorption (cholestatic liver disease, cystic fibrosis, short bowel, abetalipoproteinemia) or to mutation of alpha-tocopherol transfer protein (ataxia with vitamin E deficiency). Large sensory neurons with long axons are most vulnerable, producing a picture that resembles Friedreich ataxia: spinocerebellar ataxia, dorsal column loss of vibration and position sense, areflexia, peripheral neuropathy, and sometimes ophthalmoplegia and retinopathy. Red cell membranes undergo peroxidation, causing hemolytic anemia. Because dorsal column signs overlap with B12 deficiency, the distinguishing clues are normal methylmalonic acid, no megaloblastic anemia, and evidence of malabsorption."},{"type":"table","title":"B6, niacin, and vitamin E deficiency compared","cols":["Vitamin","Active form and key role","Neurologic findings","Distinguishing clues"],"rows":[["B6 (pyridoxine)","PLP: glutamate decarboxylase, aromatic amino acid decarboxylase, ALA synthase","Seizures (low GABA), peripheral neuropathy, irritability","Isoniazid; sideroblastic anemia; excess B6 also causes sensory neuropathy"],["B3 (niacin)","NAD+ and NADP+ for redox reactions","Dementia, depression, delirium (pellagra)","Photosensitive dermatitis and diarrhea; maize diet, Hartnup, carcinoid"],["E (alpha-tocopherol)","Membrane antioxidant against lipid peroxidation","Spinocerebellar ataxia, dorsal column loss, neuropathy","Fat malabsorption; hemolytic anemia; normal methylmalonic acid"]],"caption":"Each deficiency is localized by which neural function depends most on the missing reaction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C05","obj":"NE-14.5","title":"Hyperammonemia and the astrocyte","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ammonia comes from amino acid catabolism, intestinal glutaminase, and bacterial urease in the colon. Portal blood carries it to the liver, where periportal hepatocytes convert it to urea and perivenous hepatocytes capture the remainder as glutamine. Normal plasma ammonia in adults is below about 50 micromol/L. Liver failure removes both systems, portosystemic shunting lets portal blood bypass them, and urea cycle defects block urea formation directly."},{"type":"p","text":"The brain has no complete urea cycle. Ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier readily as un-ionized NH3, and astrocytes, which hold nearly all brain glutamine synthetase, detoxify it by adding it to glutamate to form glutamine, using ATP. Because NH3 and NH4+ equilibrate with a pKa of about 9.2, alkalosis increases the NH3 fraction and therefore brain entry, which is why alkalosis and hypokalemia (which raises renal ammonia production) can precipitate encephalopathy."},{"type":"flow","title":"How excess ammonia injures the brain","steps":["Blood ammonia rises","NH3 diffuses into astrocytes","Glutamine synthetase makes glutamine from glutamate","Glutamine accumulates and draws in water","Glutamine enters mitochondria and releases ammonia","Mitochondrial dysfunction and oxidative stress","Astrocyte swelling and impaired glutamate cycling","Cerebral edema, encephalopathy"],"caption":"Glutamine acts both as an osmolyte and as a carrier that delivers ammonia into astrocyte mitochondria (the Trojan horse hypothesis)."},{"type":"p","text":"Glutamine is osmotically active, so astrocytes swell. In acute liver failure or a neonatal urea cycle crisis, the rise is fast, compensation cannot keep up, and cytotoxic edema raises intracranial pressure and can cause herniation. In chronic liver disease, astrocytes export other osmolytes such as myo-inositol, swelling stays mild, and dysfunction dominates over edema. Energy fails as well: fixing ammonia consumes glutamate and, through glutamate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate, which slows the TCA cycle. Diversion of glutamate also disrupts the glutamate-glutamine cycle that refills neuronal transmitter pools, and inhibitory GABAergic tone appears to be increased. The result is a fluctuating encephalopathy: inverted sleep pattern, confusion, asterixis (a flapping movement from brief lapses of postural tone, not a true tremor), somnolence, and coma."},{"type":"p","text":"The histologic hallmark is the Alzheimer type II astrocyte: an enlarged, pale, vesicular nucleus with marginated chromatin and little visible cytoplasm, found in the cortex, basal ganglia, thalamus, and cerebellar dentate nucleus. It reflects metabolic stress, is unrelated to Alzheimer disease, and also appears in Wilson disease, where liver failure coexists with copper toxicity."},{"type":"p","text":"Urea cycle defects present in neonates once protein feeding begins, with poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy, seizures, and hyperventilation (ammonia stimulates the respiratory center, causing respiratory alkalosis). Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency, the most common, is X-linked; unused carbamoyl phosphate spills into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid rises. Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I deficiency causes similar hyperammonemia without orotic aciduria."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with cirrhosis and stable mental status develops vomiting and diuretic-induced hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, then becomes confused with asterixis. Why did the alkalosis matter?","explain":"Alkalosis shifts NH4+ toward NH3, which crosses the blood-brain barrier freely, and hypokalemia increases renal ammonia production. More ammonia reaches astrocytes, glutamine accumulates, and encephalopathy follows.","check":"The NH3 and NH4+ equilibrium and the ammonia flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C06","obj":"NE-14.6","title":"Myelin lipids, sphingolipidoses, and leukodystrophies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myelin is a stack of compacted plasma membrane, about 70 to 80% lipid by dry weight, compared with roughly half for most cell membranes. This composition gives it the high electrical resistance and low capacitance that make saltatory conduction possible. Its main lipids are cholesterol (about a quarter of myelin lipid), galactocerebroside (about a fifth), phospholipids including ethanolamine plasmalogen, sphingomyelin, and a smaller amount of sulfatide. The fatty acids are long and highly saturated, which packs the bilayer tightly. Galactocerebroside and sulfatide are so characteristic of oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells that they serve as myelin markers."},{"type":"p","text":"All sphingolipids share a ceramide core made from serine and palmitoyl-CoA (a PLP-dependent step) plus a second fatty acid. Adding phosphocholine makes sphingomyelin, adding galactose makes galactocerebroside, sulfating galactocerebroside makes sulfatide, and adding sugar chains with sialic acid makes gangliosides, which are concentrated in neuronal membranes. Breakdown occurs in lysosomes, where specific hydrolases remove one group at a time from the outer end, so a single missing enzyme halts the whole sequence at its substrate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Lysosomal breakdown of the myelin glycolipids","steps":["Sulfatide","Arylsulfatase A removes sulfate (absent in metachromatic leukodystrophy)","Galactocerebroside","Galactocerebrosidase removes galactose (absent in Krabbe disease)","Ceramide","Ceramidase releases sphingosine and fatty acid"],"caption":"Each step needs the previous product, so a block at either hydrolase causes storage of the lipid just above it."},{"type":"p","text":"Where the stored lipid normally lives determines the disease. Gangliosides and sphingomyelin are abundant in neuronal membranes, so their storage diseases distend neuronal cell bodies, impair autophagy, and cause gray matter disease with loss of milestones, seizures, and blindness. Accumulation in retinal ganglion cells whitens the macula except at the fovea, where ganglion cells are absent, producing a cherry-red spot. Galactocerebroside and sulfatide belong to myelin, so their defects destroy oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells and cause leukodystrophies affecting both central and peripheral myelin. In Krabbe disease, the toxic product is psychosine (galactosylsphingosine), which galactocerebrosidase normally degrades; it kills myelinating cells, so little galactocerebroside actually accumulates in brain, and macrophages filled with undegraded lipid form multinucleated globoid cells. In metachromatic leukodystrophy, stored sulfatide stains a different color with certain dyes, which gives the disease its name. X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy is the peroxisomal exception: a defective ABCD1 transporter prevents very long chain fatty acids from entering peroxisomes for beta-oxidation, and they accumulate in myelin and the adrenal cortex."},{"type":"table","title":"Lipid storage disorders that injure the nervous system","cols":["Disease","Missing enzyme or protein","Stored substance","Inheritance","Nervous system findings"],"rows":[["Tay-Sachs disease","Hexosaminidase A","GM2 ganglioside","Autosomal recessive","Infantile neurodegeneration, exaggerated startle, cherry-red spot; no hepatosplenomegaly"],["Niemann-Pick disease type A","Sphingomyelinase","Sphingomyelin","Autosomal recessive","Neurodegeneration, cherry-red spot, hepatosplenomegaly, foam cells"],["Gaucher disease (types 2 and 3)","Glucocerebrosidase","Glucocerebroside","Autosomal recessive","Neurologic involvement only in types 2 and 3; type 1 spares the brain"],["Krabbe disease","Galactocerebrosidase","Psychosine (toxic), galactocerebroside in globoid cells","Autosomal recessive","Central and peripheral demyelination, optic atrophy, spasticity"],["Metachromatic leukodystrophy","Arylsulfatase A","Sulfatide","Autosomal recessive","Central and peripheral demyelination, ataxia, cognitive decline"],["Fabry disease","Alpha-galactosidase A","Globotriaosylceramide","X-linked recessive","Painful small fiber neuropathy; also kidney, heart, skin"],["X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","ABCD1 peroxisomal transporter","Very long chain fatty acids","X-linked recessive","Cerebral demyelination in boys, adrenal insufficiency"]],"caption":"Neuronal storage (gangliosides, sphingomyelin) causes gray matter disease; defects in galactocerebroside and sulfatide breakdown cause leukodystrophy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-14-01-C07","obj":"NE-14.7","title":"Lead poisoning: heme synthesis and the nervous system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lead binds sulfhydryl groups and displaces zinc and calcium from proteins. Two heme synthesis enzymes are especially sensitive. ALA dehydratase (porphobilinogen synthase), a cytosolic zinc enzyme, joins two molecules of delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) into porphobilinogen; lead displaces its zinc and blocks it, so ALA accumulates in blood and urine. Ferrochelatase, in mitochondria, inserts ferrous iron into protoporphyrin IX to make heme; when lead inhibits it, zinc is inserted instead, and zinc protoporphyrin accumulates in red cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"Heme synthesis and the steps lead blocks","steps":["Glycine plus succinyl-CoA","ALA synthase (needs PLP) makes ALA","ALA dehydratase makes porphobilinogen (blocked by lead)","Cytosolic steps to coproporphyrinogen III","Protoporphyrin IX in mitochondria","Ferrochelatase inserts iron to form heme (blocked by lead)"],"caption":"Blocks at the second and final steps raise ALA and zinc protoporphyrin and lower heme, giving a microcytic, sideroblastic anemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Falling heme output produces a microcytic hypochromic anemia with ringed sideroblasts, because iron enters mitochondria but cannot be incorporated. Lead also inhibits pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase, so ribosomal RNA is not degraded and aggregates as basophilic stippling. The accumulated ALA is structurally similar to GABA and may contribute to neurotoxicity, but most nervous system injury comes from lead's direct effects on calcium-dependent processes: it substitutes for calcium at voltage-gated channels, protein kinase C, and synaptic release machinery, and blocks NMDA receptors that are important for synaptic plasticity in the developing brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Children are more vulnerable for several reasons. They ingest dust and old paint chips through hand-to-mouth behavior, absorb a much larger fraction of ingested lead than adults, and have a developing blood-brain barrier and synapses that are still forming. At high blood levels lead damages capillary endothelium, causing vasogenic cerebral edema with headache, vomiting, ataxia, seizures, and coma. At much lower levels it produces lasting deficits in IQ, attention, and learning without any acute illness, and no safe threshold has been established. Dense metaphyseal bands on radiographs of long bones mark growth periods during exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"Adults, usually exposed through battery manufacture, smelting, renovation, or firearms, more often develop peripheral rather than central disease. Lead neuropathy is predominantly motor with little sensory loss, and it favors the most active extensor muscles, especially those supplied by the radial nerve (wrist drop) and the common fibular nerve (foot drop). Adults also develop abdominal colic, a gray-blue gingival line (lead sulfide), proximal tubular injury and chronic nephropathy, hypertension, and saturnine gout."},{"type":"table","title":"Lead poisoning in children and adults","cols":["Feature","Children","Adults"],"rows":[["Usual exposure","Old paint chips and dust, contaminated soil and water","Occupational: batteries, smelting, renovation, firing ranges"],["Main nervous system target","Brain: encephalopathy, cerebral edema, cognitive and behavioral deficits","Peripheral motor nerves: wrist drop and foot drop"],["Why","Higher absorption, immature barrier, developing synapses","Mature barrier; long motor axons to active extensors"],["Other findings","Lead lines at metaphyses, microcytic anemia, stippling","Colic, gingival line, nephropathy, gout, hypertension, anemia"]],"caption":"The same enzyme and calcium-pathway effects produce different patterns because the target tissues differ in maturity and exposure."},{"type":"example","title":"Linking the blood smear to the wrist","text":"A 45-year-old who recycles car batteries has abdominal pain and cannot extend his right wrist, with normal sensation. His smear shows microcytic red cells with basophilic stippling. Work backward. Microcytosis means less heme. Stippling means pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase failed, a lead effect. Elevated zinc protoporphyrin would confirm ferrochelatase inhibition, and urinary ALA would confirm ALA dehydratase inhibition. A purely motor radial neuropathy fits lead's preference for motor axons to active extensors. A child with the same exposure would instead present with behavioral change or encephalopathy, because the developing brain is the more sensitive target."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disorder in this chapter follows the same logic: a nervous system function depends on one reaction, and the reaction fails because its substrate, cofactor, or clearance pathway is missing. Energy metabolism links glucose, thiamine, niacin, and ammonia, because all of them converge on pyruvate, the TCA cycle, and NAD+. Myelin links B12, vitamin E, and the leukodystrophies, since long, heavily myelinated tracts such as the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts show damage first. Heme synthesis links B6 and lead, which cause sideroblastic anemia through ALA synthase and ferrochelatase, respectively. Hematology often gives the decisive clue: megaloblasts point to B12 or folate, stippling to lead, hemolysis to vitamin E."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology confirms the mechanism. Periventricular hemorrhagic lesions mark thiamine deficiency, vacuolar cord demyelination marks B12 deficiency, Alzheimer type II astrocytes mark hyperammonemia, and globoid cells, foam cells, and metachromatic material mark the lysosomal diseases. The limits matter: several mechanisms, especially the cause of myelin injury in B12 deficiency and the relative weight of edema and neurotransmitter change in hepatic encephalopathy, remain incompletely understood, and laboratory thresholds vary between sources and laboratories."}],"summary":["The brain takes up glucose through insulin-independent GLUT1 and GLUT3, stores almost none, cannot use fatty acids, and shifts to ketone bodies through MCT1 in prolonged fasting.","Hypoglycemia triggers counterregulation and autonomic warnings first; neuroglycopenia (confusion, behavior change, seizures, coma) appears below about 50 mg/dL (2.8 mmol/L).","TPP is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase; deficiency causes Wernicke encephalopathy, Korsakoff syndrome, and beriberi, and glucose before thiamine worsens it.","B12 runs methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase; high methylmalonic acid separates B12 from folate deficiency, and only B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration.","B6 deficiency causes seizures (low GABA) and neuropathy; niacin deficiency causes pellagra with dementia; vitamin E deficiency causes spinocerebellar ataxia and dorsal column loss.","Astrocytes clear brain ammonia through glutamine synthetase; glutamine accumulation swells astrocytes, alpha-ketoglutarate depletion slows the TCA cycle, and Alzheimer type II astrocytes mark the injury.","Myelin is lipid-rich with cholesterol, galactocerebroside, sulfatide, and sphingomyelin; lysosomal sphingolipid breakdown proceeds one step at a time.","Neuronal lipid storage (Tay-Sachs, Niemann-Pick A) causes gray matter disease; defects in galactocerebroside and sulfatide breakdown (Krabbe, metachromatic leukodystrophy) cause leukodystrophy.","Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, causing microcytic sideroblastic anemia with basophilic stippling, encephalopathy in children, and motor wrist and foot drop in adults."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins; Metabolism of sphingolipids; Conversion of amino acids to specialized products (heme synthesis); Disposal of nitrogen"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral blood flow and brain metabolism; Insulin, glucagon, and diabetes mellitus"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Brain energy metabolism and the blood-brain barrier"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System: metabolic and toxic disorders, leukodystrophies; Genetic disorders: lysosomal storage diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver disease: hepatic encephalopathy; Nervous system disorders"}]},"NE-15":{"unit":"NE-15","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01","title":"Neurotransmitter Synthesis and Breakdown","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the major small-molecule neurotransmitters from precursor to breakdown product: the catecholamines, serotonin and melatonin, glutamate, GABA, glycine, and acetylcholine. For each one you will learn the rate-limiting enzyme and its cofactor, where in the cell each step happens, how signaling ends, and which urinary or plasma products reveal disease. The chapter closes by placing each transmitter in its source nucleus and explaining how losing those neurons produces Parkinson disease, Huntington disease, and the cholinergic deficit of Alzheimer disease. Board questions usually give a deficiency, a tumor, a lab result, or a lesion and ask you to work back to the enzyme, cofactor, or nucleus responsible.","prerequisites":["Amino acid structure, transamination, and the role of pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)","Synaptic vesicle release, ionotropic and metabotropic receptors, EPSPs and IPSPs (NE-13)","Phenylalanine and tyrosine metabolism, including phenylketonuria (NE-14)","Adrenal gland structure and blood supply (EN-3, EN-4)","Basal ganglia anatomy and brainstem landmarks (NE-3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Autonomic nervous system (NE-9)","how":"Sympathetic neurons release norepinephrine, the adrenal medulla releases epinephrine, and all preganglionic neurons release acetylcholine, so these pathways set autonomic tone."},{"to":"Endocrine hormone synthesis (EN-8, EN-12-G)","how":"Cortisol induces PNMT in the adrenal medulla, and pheochromocytoma and carcinoid tumors are identified by the catecholamine and serotonin products described here."},{"to":"Neurological biochemistry and nutrition (NE-14, NE-20-D)","how":"Tetrahydrobiopterin, pyridoxal phosphate, ascorbate, copper, and thiamine deficiencies each disable a specific synthetic step."},{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Glutamate excitotoxicity explains why neurons die within minutes of ischemia and why some neuron types die first."},{"to":"Degenerative diseases and neoplasms (NE-20-B, NE-20-F)","how":"Parkinson, Huntington, and Alzheimer disease are defined by the loss of specific transmitter-producing neurons, and neuroblastoma is diagnosed partly by catecholamine metabolites."}],"terms":[{"term":"Catecholamine","def":"A monoamine with a catechol ring (a benzene ring carrying two adjacent hydroxyl groups) and an amine side chain: dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine."},{"term":"Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4)","def":"The reduced pterin cofactor that supplies electrons for the aromatic amino acid hydroxylases (phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan hydroxylase)."},{"term":"Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)","def":"The active form of vitamin B6, a cofactor for amino acid decarboxylases and transaminases, including the enzymes that make dopamine, serotonin, and GABA."},{"term":"Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC)","def":"The PLP-dependent enzyme, also called DOPA decarboxylase, that converts L-DOPA to dopamine and 5-hydroxytryptophan to serotonin."},{"term":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)","def":"A flavin enzyme of the outer mitochondrial membrane that removes the amine group of monoamines by oxidative deamination."},{"term":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)","def":"A cytosolic enzyme that transfers a methyl group from S-adenosylmethionine to the 3-hydroxyl of a catechol ring."},{"term":"VMAT2","def":"The vesicular monoamine transporter that loads cytosolic monoamines into vesicles in exchange for protons."},{"term":"Excitotoxicity","def":"Neuronal death caused by excessive glutamate receptor activation, mainly through NMDA receptor Ca2+ entry."},{"term":"Glutamate-glutamine cycle","def":"The shuttle in which astrocytes take up synaptic glutamate, convert it to glutamine, and return glutamine to neurons to be made back into glutamate."},{"term":"Nucleus basalis of Meynert","def":"A group of large cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain (substantia innominata) that supplies most of the acetylcholine in the neocortex."},{"term":"Locus coeruleus","def":"A pigmented nucleus in the dorsal rostral pons that is the main source of norepinephrine in the brain."},{"term":"Medium spiny neuron","def":"The GABAergic projection neuron of the striatum, the cell type lost first in Huntington disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01-C01","obj":"NE-15.1","title":"Catecholamine synthesis from tyrosine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine are made by one pathway that starts with tyrosine. Each cell type runs the pathway only as far as its enzymes allow: dopaminergic neurons lack dopamine beta-hydroxylase and stop at dopamine, sympathetic postganglionic neurons and the locus coeruleus lack PNMT and stop at norepinephrine, and adrenal medullary chromaffin cells carry it through to epinephrine."},{"type":"flow","title":"Catecholamine synthesis","steps":["Phenylalanine hydroxylase (liver, BH4) makes tyrosine from phenylalanine","Tyrosine hydroxylase (BH4, Fe2+, O2) makes L-DOPA: rate limiting","AADC (pyridoxal phosphate) removes CO2 to make dopamine","VMAT2 loads dopamine into vesicles","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase (copper, ascorbate) makes norepinephrine in vesicles","PNMT (SAM, induced by cortisol) makes epinephrine in cytosol"],"caption":"Each step changes one group on the tyrosine skeleton. Where the enzyme sits (cytosol or vesicle) decides the order in which the cell handles the product."},{"type":"p","text":"Tyrosine comes from the diet or from phenylalanine through hepatic phenylalanine hydroxylase. Tyrosine hydroxylase, a cytosolic enzyme, adds a second ring hydroxyl to form L-DOPA, using O2, ferrous iron, and tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) as the electron donor. It is the rate-limiting step for three reasons. It is normally close to saturated with tyrosine, so eating more tyrosine adds little. Catecholamines inhibit it by competing with BH4, so a full terminal slows its own production. Neural activity speeds it within minutes through phosphorylation by protein kinase A and other kinases, and sustained stress induces more enzyme over hours to days. Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC) then removes the carboxyl group so quickly that L-DOPA barely accumulates."},{"type":"p","text":"The last two steps depend on location. Dopamine beta-hydroxylase sits inside synaptic and chromaffin vesicles, so dopamine must first be pumped in by VMAT2, and norepinephrine is made inside the vesicle. The enzyme contains copper and uses ascorbate (vitamin C) to supply electrons as it hydroxylates the beta carbon of the side chain. It is released with norepinephrine during exocytosis, and its activity falls when copper transport fails, as in Menkes disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PNMT) is cytosolic, so norepinephrine must leak out of the vesicle, accept a methyl group from S-adenosylmethionine (SAM), and be repackaged. Chromaffin cells have high PNMT because blood leaving the adrenal cortex drains through the medulla before reaching the systemic circulation, bathing the medulla in cortisol at concentrations far above those in systemic plasma. Cortisol induces PNMT, so about 80% of adrenal medullary output is epinephrine. Paragangliomas outside the adrenal gland lack this cortisol supply and secrete mainly norepinephrine."},{"type":"table","title":"Enzymes of catecholamine synthesis","cols":["Enzyme","Cofactors","Location","What a defect or deficiency does"],"rows":[["Tyrosine hydroxylase","BH4, Fe2+, O2","Cytosol","BH4 deficiency lowers dopamine and norepinephrine (and serotonin)"],["AADC (DOPA decarboxylase)","Pyridoxal phosphate","Cytosol","AADC deficiency lowers dopamine and serotonin together"],["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Copper, ascorbate","Vesicle","Norepinephrine and epinephrine nearly absent; dopamine high"],["PNMT","SAM","Cytosol, adrenal medulla","Low epinephrine when cortisol is low or medulla is lost"]],"caption":"Ring hydroxylases need BH4, decarboxylases need B6, side-chain hydroxylation needs copper and vitamin C, and methylation needs SAM."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In congenital dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency, what happens to plasma dopamine and norepinephrine, and to blood pressure on standing?","explain":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine are nearly undetectable and plasma dopamine is high, because sympathetic vesicles fill with dopamine that cannot be converted. Sympathetic nerves then release dopamine in place of norepinephrine, and dopamine is a poor alpha-1 agonist at these concentrations, so the baroreflex cannot raise vascular resistance on standing and severe orthostatic hypotension results.","check":"The vesicular location of dopamine beta-hydroxylase and the enzyme table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01-C02","obj":"NE-15.2","title":"Catecholamine breakdown and tumor markers","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most released catecholamine is removed by reuptake, not by enzymes. The Na+-coupled norepinephrine transporter (NET) and dopamine transporter (DAT) return transmitter to the terminal, where VMAT2 repackages much of it. Blocking reuptake, as cocaine does, lengthens the synaptic signal. Transmitter that escapes reuptake, leaks from vesicles, or is secreted into blood is degraded by two enzymes."},{"type":"p","text":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO) is a flavin enzyme on the outer mitochondrial membrane. It removes the amine group by oxidative deamination, producing an aldehyde that aldehyde dehydrogenase oxidizes to an acid. MAO-A prefers norepinephrine and serotonin and is abundant in gut and liver, where it destroys dietary amines such as tyramine. MAO-B prefers dopamine and is concentrated in astrocytes. Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) is cytosolic and abundant in liver, kidney, and adrenal chromaffin cells. It transfers a methyl group from SAM to the 3-hydroxyl of the ring. The two enzymes can act in either order, and both routes converge on the same end products."},{"type":"table","title":"Catecholamine breakdown products","cols":["Parent","After COMT alone","After MAO and COMT","Clinical use"],"rows":[["Dopamine","3-methoxytyramine","Homovanillic acid (HVA)","Urine HVA high in neuroblastoma"],["Norepinephrine","Normetanephrine","Vanillylmandelic acid (VMA); MHPG in brain","Urine VMA high in neuroblastoma"],["Epinephrine","Metanephrine","VMA","Plasma or urine metanephrines high in pheochromocytoma"]],"caption":"HVA comes from dopamine; VMA and the metanephrines come from norepinephrine and epinephrine. In the CNS, norepinephrine is mainly reduced rather than oxidized after deamination, giving MHPG."},{"type":"p","text":"These products become tumor markers because tumors of neural crest origin make catecholamines faster than they store them. Neuroblastoma is an embryonal tumor of sympathetic neuroblasts, usually in a child under 5 years, arising in the adrenal medulla or along the paraspinal sympathetic chain. Its immature cells make mostly dopamine and norepinephrine, which are metabolized inside the tumor and liver to HVA and VMA, so urinary HVA, VMA, or both are raised in about 90% of cases. Because much of the transmitter is degraded before it reaches receptors, hypertension is less prominent than in pheochromocytoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of mature chromaffin cells, usually in adults. Release is episodic, so plasma catecholamines can be normal between attacks of headache, sweating, palpitations, and hypertension. The tumor cells contain COMT, however, and convert leaking catecholamine to metanephrines continuously, which is why plasma free or urinary fractionated metanephrines are the most sensitive tests."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the metabolite pattern","text":"A 3-year-old has an abdominal mass and raised urinary HVA and VMA with normal blood pressure. HVA points to excess dopamine, typical of immature cells that convert little of it onward, and VMA shows norepinephrine is also being made and degraded. That pattern fits neuroblastoma. A 45-year-old with spells of headache, sweating, and blood pressure of 210/120 mm Hg during spells, normal plasma norepinephrine between spells, and high plasma metanephrines fits pheochromocytoma: tumor COMT keeps producing metanephrines even when release pauses. If metanephrine (from epinephrine) dominates, the tumor is likely adrenal, because only cortisol-exposed tissue makes much PNMT; a pure normetanephrine rise suggests an extra-adrenal paraganglioma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01-C03","obj":"NE-15.3","title":"Serotonin synthesis, melatonin, and 5-HIAA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) is an indoleamine made from the essential amino acid tryptophan in two steps that mirror catecholamine synthesis. Tryptophan hydroxylase uses BH4, O2, and Fe2+ to hydroxylate the 5 position of the indole ring, forming 5-hydroxytryptophan, and the same PLP-dependent AADC then removes the carboxyl group. Tryptophan hydroxylase is rate limiting, but unlike tyrosine hydroxylase it is not saturated at normal brain tryptophan levels, so tryptophan supply does change serotonin output. Tryptophan enters the brain on the large neutral amino acid transporter, competing with leucine, phenylalanine, and tyrosine."},{"type":"p","text":"Two isoforms divide the work. TPH2 operates in the raphe nuclei, clusters of neurons along the midline of the brainstem that supply all brain serotonin. TPH1 operates in the pineal gland and in enterochromaffin cells of the gut, which hold about 90% of the body's serotonin. Platelets take serotonin up through the serotonin transporter (SERT) but cannot make it. Serotonin in blood does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so the brain relies entirely on its own synthesis."},{"type":"table","title":"Serotonin synthesis and its two fates","cols":["Step","Enzyme","Cofactor or donor","Site"],"rows":[["Tryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptophan","Tryptophan hydroxylase (rate limiting)","BH4","Raphe neurons, enterochromaffin cells, pineal"],["5-hydroxytryptophan to serotonin","AADC","Pyridoxal phosphate","Same cells"],["Serotonin to N-acetylserotonin","Arylalkylamine N-acetyltransferase (rate limiting for melatonin)","Acetyl-CoA","Pineal gland"],["N-acetylserotonin to melatonin","Hydroxyindole-O-methyltransferase","SAM","Pineal gland"],["Serotonin to 5-HIAA","MAO-A, then aldehyde dehydrogenase","FAD, then NAD+","Neurons, liver, lung"]],"caption":"Serotonin is either converted to melatonin in the pineal gland or deaminated to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), which is excreted in urine."},{"type":"p","text":"In the pineal gland, the output of serotonin as melatonin follows the light and dark cycle. Melatonin signals night to the body and peaks in the middle of the night. Light suppresses it through a multisynaptic sympathetic pathway, and darkness releases it."},{"type":"flow","title":"From darkness to melatonin release","steps":["Melanopsin retinal ganglion cells sense light","Retinohypothalamic tract signals the suprachiasmatic nucleus","In darkness, SCN inhibition of the paraventricular nucleus falls","Paraventricular nucleus drives the intermediolateral column (upper thoracic cord)","Superior cervical ganglion releases norepinephrine onto pinealocytes","Beta-1 receptors raise cAMP and N-acetyltransferase activity","Melatonin is made and secreted"],"caption":"Light at night silences this pathway within minutes. Damage to the cervical sympathetic chain abolishes the melatonin rhythm."},{"type":"p","text":"Synaptic serotonin is removed by SERT and then deaminated by MAO-A to 5-HIAA. Urinary 5-HIAA therefore measures whole-body serotonin turnover. In carcinoid syndrome, a well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor derived from enterochromaffin cells makes large amounts of serotonin, and 24-hour urinary 5-HIAA rises well above the reference range. Liver MAO normally inactivates serotonin carried in portal blood, so flushing, diarrhea, and bronchospasm usually appear only after liver metastases release serotonin directly into the hepatic veins. Normally about 1% of tryptophan becomes serotonin; a carcinoid tumor can divert most of it, leaving too little for niacin synthesis and producing pellagra."},{"type":"example","title":"Why one cofactor defect lowers two transmitters","text":"An infant has high plasma phenylalanine that does not fall on a low-phenylalanine diet, plus hypotonia and seizures. Cerebrospinal fluid HVA and 5-HIAA are low. Phenylalanine hydroxylase itself is normal, so the defect lies in its cofactor, BH4. Tyrosine hydroxylase and tryptophan hydroxylase use the same cofactor, so dopamine and serotonin synthesis fail together, and their breakdown products in CSF fall. The same logic applies to AADC deficiency, except phenylalanine stays normal because AADC acts after the hydroxylases, and CSF L-DOPA and 5-hydroxytryptophan accumulate."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01-C04","obj":"NE-15.4","title":"Glutamate, GABA, glycine, and excitotoxicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter of the CNS. It crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so neurons make it from glutamine with glutaminase and by transamination of the Krebs cycle intermediate alpha-ketoglutarate. After release, Na+-dependent excitatory amino acid transporters, mostly on astrocytes, clear it from the cleft. Astrocytes convert it to glutamine with glutamine synthetase, which consumes ATP and ammonia, and return glutamine to neurons. This glutamate-glutamine cycle keeps extracellular glutamate low, which matters because glutamate at high concentration is toxic."},{"type":"p","text":"GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is made from glutamate in one step: glutamate decarboxylase (GAD) removes the alpha-carboxyl group, using pyridoxal phosphate. The brain's main inhibitory transmitter is therefore built from its main excitatory one. GABA is cleared by GABA transporters and degraded by GABA transaminase, also PLP dependent, to succinic semialdehyde and then succinate, which enters the Krebs cycle. Glycine, made from serine, is the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord and brainstem, used by Renshaw cells and by the interneurons that relax antagonist muscles."},{"type":"table","title":"Amino acid transmitter receptors","cols":["Receptor","Type","Ions or effector","Result"],"rows":[["AMPA (glutamate)","Ionotropic","Na+ in, K+ out","Fast EPSP"],["NMDA (glutamate)","Ionotropic","Na+ and Ca2+; Mg2+ blocks at rest; glycine is co-agonist","Slow EPSP and Ca2+ signal when cell is already depolarized"],["GABA-A","Ionotropic","Cl- channel","Fast IPSP or shunting inhibition"],["GABA-B","Metabotropic (Gi/o)","Opens K+ channels; closes presynaptic Ca2+ channels","Slow IPSP; less transmitter release"],["Glycine","Ionotropic","Cl- channel","Fast IPSP in cord and brainstem"]],"caption":"Excitatory receptors pass cations; fast inhibitory receptors pass Cl-, which holds the membrane near the Cl- equilibrium potential of about -70 mV."},{"type":"p","text":"The NMDA receptor opens only when glutamate is bound and the membrane is already depolarized, usually by AMPA receptors, enough to expel Mg2+ from the pore. This coincidence detection makes it the trigger for long-term potentiation and learning. GABA-A and glycine receptors inhibit because Cl- entry drives the membrane toward a potential at or below rest. In immature neurons, intracellular Cl- is high, so opening GABA-A channels lets Cl- leave and depolarizes the cell instead. Strychnine blocks glycine receptors, and tetanus toxin blocks GABA and glycine release from spinal inhibitory interneurons; both remove inhibition and cause rigidity and spasms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient taking isoniazid, or an infant fed a formula lacking vitamin B6, develops seizures. Which transmitter falls, and why?","explain":"GABA. Isoniazid forms an inactive hydrazone with pyridoxal and depletes PLP, and dietary deficiency removes it directly. Without PLP, glutamate decarboxylase cannot make GABA, inhibition fails, and glutamate-driven excitation is left unopposed.","check":"The GABA synthesis paragraph and the receptor table."},{"type":"p","text":"Excitotoxicity is the main reason neurons die within minutes of ischemia. Glutamate uptake is powered by the Na+ gradient, so when ATP runs out the transporters stall or run backward, and depolarized terminals keep releasing glutamate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ischemia to excitotoxic neuronal death","steps":["Blood flow falls; ATP is depleted","Na+/K+ ATPase fails; neurons depolarize","Glutamate release rises; uptake stalls or reverses","Depolarization removes Mg2+ block; NMDA channels admit Ca2+","Ca2+ activates calpains, phospholipase A2, endonucleases, nitric oxide synthase","Free radicals and peroxynitrite damage membranes and mitochondria","Necrosis in the core; apoptosis in the penumbra"],"caption":"Na+ and water entry also cause cytotoxic edema. Each dying neuron releases more glutamate, spreading injury outward."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurons with dense glutamate input die first after global ischemia: CA1 pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus, cerebellar Purkinje cells, and pyramidal neurons of neocortical layers 3, 5, and 6, which produces laminar necrosis. About 12 to 24 hours after the insult these neurons appear as red neurons, shrunken cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm and pyknotic nuclei. Early memory loss after cardiac arrest reflects CA1 injury."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01-C05","obj":"NE-15.5","title":"Acetylcholine synthesis, breakdown, and Alzheimer disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acetylcholine is the transmitter of motor neurons, all preganglionic autonomic neurons, postganglionic parasympathetic neurons, sympathetic neurons to sweat glands, and several CNS systems. Choline acetyltransferase (ChAT), a cytosolic enzyme in the nerve terminal, transfers the acetyl group of acetyl-CoA to choline. Acetyl-CoA comes from pyruvate through pyruvate dehydrogenase in terminal mitochondria, a thiamine-dependent step that ties cholinergic function to glucose metabolism."},{"type":"p","text":"Choline, not ChAT, limits the rate of synthesis. Neurons make little choline, so they depend on the Na+-dependent high-affinity choline transporter in the terminal membrane, which recaptures choline from the cleft and takes it up from plasma, where it comes from the diet and from phosphatidylcholine. The vesicular acetylcholine transporter (VAChT) loads acetylcholine into vesicles in exchange for protons, and Ca2+ entry triggers release through SNARE proteins, which botulinum toxin cleaves."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The cholinergic synapse","steps":["High-affinity transporter takes choline into the terminal (rate limiting)","ChAT joins acetyl-CoA and choline","VAChT loads acetylcholine into vesicles","Ca2+ entry triggers exocytosis","Acetylcholinesterase splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline","Choline returns to the terminal"],"caption":"About half the choline released by hydrolysis is recaptured; the rest is replaced from plasma."},{"type":"p","text":"Acetylcholinesterase ends the signal by hydrolysis rather than reuptake. It is anchored in the synaptic cleft (in the basal lamina at the neuromuscular junction) and is among the fastest enzymes known, so a given acetylcholine molecule acts for only about a millisecond. A serine in its active site attacks the ester bond, forming a brief acetyl-enzyme intermediate that water quickly releases. Organophosphates phosphorylate that serine almost irreversibly, so acetylcholine accumulates and overstimulates both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors. Butyrylcholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase), made by the liver, hydrolyzes choline esters in plasma."},{"type":"p","text":"The nucleus basalis of Meynert is a sheet of large cholinergic neurons in the substantia innominata of the basal forebrain, beneath the globus pallidus and anterior commissure. It supplies most of the acetylcholine in the neocortex and amygdala, while the nearby medial septal nucleus and diagonal band supply the hippocampus. Cortical acetylcholine sharpens attention and helps encode new memories."},{"type":"p","text":"In Alzheimer disease these basal forebrain neurons form neurofibrillary tangles and die early. Cortical ChAT activity and acetylcholine release fall, and the size of the fall correlates with cognitive decline. This is the cholinergic deficit of Alzheimer disease. It is a consequence of the neurodegeneration, which is driven by amyloid-beta plaques and tau tangles, rather than its cause. The deficit explains why drugs that block muscarinic receptors can worsen confusion and memory in older adults: they further reduce an already depleted signal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-15-01-C06","obj":"NE-15.6","title":"Source nuclei and the transmitter changes in Parkinson and Huntington disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each monoamine and acetylcholine system starts in a small group of neurons with very widespread axons, so losing a few thousand cells can change transmitter levels across much of the brain. The locus coeruleus and substantia nigra contain neuromelanin, a dark byproduct of catecholamine oxidation, which makes them visible as pigmented areas on gross brain sections."},{"type":"table","title":"Main source nuclei","cols":["Transmitter","Source nucleus","Location","Main targets and roles"],"rows":[["Dopamine","Substantia nigra pars compacta","Midbrain","Striatum (nigrostriatal): starting and scaling movement"],["Dopamine","Ventral tegmental area","Midbrain, medial to the nigra","Nucleus accumbens and limbic areas (mesolimbic), prefrontal cortex (mesocortical): reward and motivation"],["Dopamine","Arcuate nucleus","Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary (tuberoinfundibular): inhibits prolactin"],["Norepinephrine","Locus coeruleus","Dorsal rostral pons, floor of fourth ventricle","Whole cortex, cerebellum, spinal cord: arousal and vigilance"],["Serotonin","Raphe nuclei","Midline brainstem","Rostral groups to forebrain (mood, sleep); caudal groups to cord (pain modulation)"],["Acetylcholine","Nucleus basalis of Meynert","Basal forebrain","Neocortex: attention and memory"]],"caption":"The locus coeruleus and raphe fire fastest in wakefulness, slow in non-REM sleep, and fall nearly silent in REM sleep."},{"type":"p","text":"These nuclei act on the basal ganglia through two opposing striatal pathways. Striatal medium spiny neurons are GABAergic. Direct pathway neurons carry D1 receptors and, when excited, inhibit the internal globus pallidus (GPi), releasing the thalamus to drive movement. Indirect pathway neurons carry D2 receptors and, through the external globus pallidus and subthalamic nucleus, increase GPi output and suppress movement. Dopamine excites the direct pathway and inhibits the indirect pathway, so both effects promote movement. Striatal cholinergic interneurons oppose dopamine."},{"type":"p","text":"In Parkinson disease, pigmented neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta die, many containing Lewy bodies of aggregated alpha-synuclein. Motor signs appear only after about 60% of these neurons and about 80% of striatal dopamine are lost. The direct pathway loses drive and the indirect pathway loses inhibition, so GPi output rises and the thalamus is held back, giving bradykinesia and rigidity, plus a resting tremor. Acetylcholine becomes relatively excessive. Neurons in the locus coeruleus and raphe are also lost, which helps explain the depression and sleep disturbance of the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Huntington disease is an autosomal dominant expansion of CAG repeats in the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4. Striatal medium spiny neurons die, beginning in the caudate, and the caudate atrophies so that the lateral ventricles enlarge. Indirect pathway neurons are lost first, so the brake on movement fails and chorea results. Striatal GABA and ChAT activity fall, while nigral dopamine neurons survive and dopamine signaling becomes relatively excessive. Mutant huntingtin appears to enhance NMDA receptor signaling in response to corticostriatal glutamate, so excitotoxicity is thought to contribute."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does Parkinson disease produce too little movement and early Huntington disease too much, when both damage the striatal circuit?","explain":"Parkinson disease removes dopamine, which normally excites the direct pathway and inhibits the indirect one, so net GPi output rises and the thalamus is suppressed. Early Huntington disease destroys indirect pathway neurons themselves, so GPi output falls, the thalamus is released, and chorea appears.","check":"The direct and indirect pathway paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry links these pathways through shared cofactors. BH4 serves all three aromatic hydroxylases, PLP serves AADC, GAD, and GABA transaminase, SAM serves PNMT, COMT, and melatonin synthesis, and copper and ascorbate serve dopamine beta-hydroxylase, so one nutritional or genetic defect can lower several transmitters at once. Anatomy and endocrinology meet in the adrenal gland, where cortical venous drainage induces PNMT, and in the pineal gland, where a sympathetic pathway from the cervical cord turns darkness into melatonin. Pathology reads the same pathways backward: HVA, VMA, metanephrines, and 5-HIAA identify neural crest and enterochromaffin tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"In neuropathology, transmitter changes follow selective neuron loss rather than isolated enzyme defects. Parkinson, Huntington, and Alzheimer disease each remove a defined population, and ischemia kills the neurons with the heaviest glutamate input first. The transmitter-centered view has limits. Each disease involves many systems, protein aggregation and inflammation drive the degeneration, and transmitter levels are inferred from enzyme activity and metabolite measurements rather than seen directly."}],"summary":["Tyrosine hydroxylase (BH4) is rate limiting for catecholamines; AADC needs PLP, dopamine beta-hydroxylase needs copper and vitamin C inside vesicles, and PNMT needs SAM.","Cortisol from the adrenal cortex induces PNMT, so the adrenal medulla secretes mostly epinephrine and extra-adrenal paragangliomas secrete mostly norepinephrine.","Reuptake ends most catecholamine action; MAO deaminates and COMT methylates, giving HVA from dopamine and VMA and metanephrines from norepinephrine and epinephrine.","Neuroblastoma in a young child raises urinary HVA and VMA; pheochromocytoma is detected by metanephrines because tumor COMT forms them continuously.","Tryptophan hydroxylase (BH4) is rate limiting for serotonin; raphe neurons supply the brain, the pineal makes melatonin in darkness, and MAO forms urinary 5-HIAA, which rises in carcinoid syndrome.","GABA is made from glutamate by PLP-dependent GAD, so B6 deficiency or isoniazid causes seizures; glycine inhibits in the cord and brainstem.","NMDA receptors need glutamate plus depolarization to relieve Mg2+ block; in ischemia, failed uptake and Ca2+ overload kill CA1, Purkinje, and cortical pyramidal neurons first.","ChAT joins acetyl-CoA and choline, choline uptake is rate limiting, and acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine to acetate and choline.","Loss of nucleus basalis neurons lowers cortical acetylcholine in Alzheimer disease.","Parkinson disease is nigral dopamine loss with relative cholinergic excess; Huntington disease is striatal GABA and acetylcholine loss with relative dopamine excess."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Conversion of amino acids to specialized products (catecholamines, serotonin, melatonin, GABA)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of the Nervous System, Basic Functions of Synapses, and Transmitter Substances"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Synaptic transmission in the nervous system; the adrenal medulla"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System: hypoxia and ischemia, neurodegenerative diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood (neuroblastoma); The Endocrine System (adrenal medulla)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-16":{"unit":"NE-16","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01","title":"Neurogenetics","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how specific kinds of genetic error produce neurologic disease: unstable trinucleotide repeats, an extra chromosome 21, inherited loss of tumor suppressor genes, a somatic activating mutation, loss of a motor neuron survival gene, and the driver mutations of nervous system tumors. Board questions usually give a pedigree, an age of onset, or a cluster of skin, eye, and brain findings and ask for the gene, the inheritance pattern, or the molecular mechanism. If you can say what the normal gene product does, how the mutation changes its amount or behavior, and why the cells that suffer are the ones that depend on it, you can reason through almost every question in this area.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance patterns, penetrance, and expressivity","Meiosis I and II, chromosome segregation, and karyotype notation","Transcription, pre-mRNA splicing, and DNA methylation as gene silencing","Cell cycle regulation at the G1 to S checkpoint and the Ras and mTOR growth pathways","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs (NE-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Huntington disease, Alzheimer disease, and Friedreich ataxia are degenerative disorders whose cause is genetic, and Down syndrome is the clearest proof that amyloid-beta dosage drives Alzheimer pathology."},{"to":"Neurological neoplasms (NE-20-F)","how":"Vestibular schwannoma, hemangioblastoma, oligodendroglioma, retinoblastoma, and neuroblastoma are defined or predicted by the genes taught here."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal genetics (MS-9)","how":"Myotonic dystrophy is both a repeat disorder and a muscle disease, and spinal muscular atrophy must be told apart from primary myopathies in the floppy infant."},{"to":"Neural tube embryology (NE-1)","how":"Neural crest gives rise to melanocytes, Schwann cells, and the adrenal medulla, which is why skin pigment, nerve sheath tumors, and neuroblastoma recur in this chapter."},{"to":"Neoplasia and cell cycle control","how":"The two-hit model, tumor suppressor loss, and oncogene amplification are general principles of cancer biology applied here to the nervous system."}],"terms":[{"term":"Trinucleotide repeat expansion","def":"Lengthening of a tandem run of three bases beyond a stable size, causing disease once a threshold is passed."},{"term":"Anticipation","def":"Earlier onset or greater severity of a disease in successive generations, explained in repeat disorders by progressive repeat lengthening."},{"term":"Premutation","def":"A repeat longer than normal but below the disease range that is unstable and can expand to a full mutation in the next generation."},{"term":"Nondisjunction","def":"Failure of homologous chromosomes or sister chromatids to separate at meiosis or mitosis, producing cells with an extra or missing chromosome."},{"term":"Robertsonian translocation","def":"Fusion of the long arms of two acrocentric chromosomes (13, 14, 15, 21, 22) with loss of their short arms, giving a balanced carrier 45 chromosomes."},{"term":"Mosaicism","def":"Presence of two or more genetically different cell lines in one person, arising from an error after fertilization."},{"term":"Tumor suppressor gene","def":"A gene whose product restrains growth or division, so disease requires loss of function of both copies in a cell."},{"term":"Two-hit hypothesis","def":"Knudson's model that a tumor suppressor tumor needs two inactivating events, the first of which may be inherited in every cell."},{"term":"Loss of heterozygosity","def":"Loss of the remaining normal allele at a locus in a cell that started with one normal and one mutant copy, a common form of the second hit."},{"term":"Oncogene","def":"A mutated or overexpressed gene whose gain of function drives growth, so one altered copy or amplification is enough."},{"term":"Somatic mosaic mutation","def":"A mutation that arises after conception in one cell and is passed only to that cell's descendants, so it is not inherited."},{"term":"Gene amplification","def":"Increase in the number of copies of a gene within a tumor cell, raising its product and acting as a gain of function."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01-C01","obj":"NE-16.1","title":"Trinucleotide repeat expansion disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A trinucleotide repeat is a tandem run of the same three bases. Short runs are inherited stably, but once a run passes a threshold it can fold into hairpins and other unusual structures that make DNA polymerase slip during replication and repair, so the tract tends to lengthen when passed on. Longer repeats are both more unstable and more harmful, so each generation tends to carry a longer tract, show symptoms earlier, and be more severely affected. This is anticipation."},{"type":"p","text":"Where the repeat sits decides how it causes disease. A repeat inside the coding sequence is translated into an abnormal protein (gain of function), a repeat in an untranslated region or intron can silence the gene (loss of function) or produce a toxic RNA."},{"type":"table","title":"The four repeat disorders compared","cols":["Disorder","Repeat, location, gene","Mechanism","Inheritance and anticipation","Key findings"],"rows":[["Huntington disease","CAG in exon 1 of HTT (chromosome 4)","Polyglutamine huntingtin misfolds and aggregates (toxic protein)","Autosomal dominant; anticipation, largest expansions through the father","Chorea, psychiatric change, dementia, caudate atrophy"],["Fragile X syndrome","CGG in the 5' untranslated region of FMR1 (Xq27.3)","Full mutation hypermethylates the promoter; FMRP absent","X-linked (often called X-linked dominant with reduced penetrance in females); anticipation, expansion through the mother","Intellectual disability, long face, large ears and jaw, macroorchidism, mitral valve prolapse"],["Myotonic dystrophy type 1","CTG in the 3' untranslated region of DMPK (chromosome 19)","Expanded CUG RNA traps splicing regulators (toxic RNA)","Autosomal dominant; anticipation, congenital form through the mother","Myotonia, distal and facial weakness, cataracts, frontal balding, testicular atrophy, heart block"],["Friedreich ataxia","GAA in intron 1 of FXN (chromosome 9)","Repeat blocks transcription; frataxin deficient (loss of function)","Autosomal recessive; no anticipation across generations","Ataxia, areflexia, lost position sense, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, diabetes, scoliosis"]],"caption":"Coding repeats make a toxic protein; noncoding repeats silence the gene or make a toxic RNA."},{"type":"p","text":"In Huntington disease about 40 or more CAG repeats are fully penetrant (36 to 39 with reduced penetrance). The polyglutamine tract makes huntingtin misfold and aggregate and disturbs transcription, and the GABAergic medium spiny neurons of the striatum die first. Early loss of the indirect pathway removes a brake on movement, producing chorea, and caudate atrophy widens the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles. CAG tracts are most unstable in spermatogenesis, so juvenile cases usually inherit from the father."},{"type":"p","text":"In fragile X a full mutation (more than about 200 CGG repeats) triggers methylation of the FMR1 promoter, which silences transcription and removes FMRP, an RNA-binding protein that restrains local protein synthesis at synapses. A premutation (about 55 to 200) expands to a full mutation only during female meiosis, so risk depends on the mother's repeat size. Premutation carriers make excess FMR1 mRNA that is toxic, which explains the late-onset tremor and ataxia syndrome in older men and primary ovarian insufficiency in women."},{"type":"p","text":"In myotonic dystrophy the CTG repeat is transcribed but not translated. The expanded CUG RNA forms nuclear foci that sequester the splicing regulator muscleblind-like 1, so many transcripts revert to fetal splicing. Missplicing of the muscle chloride channel (ClC-1) lowers sarcolemmal chloride conductance, so fibers keep firing after a voluntary contraction and relax slowly, which is myotonia, and missplicing of the insulin receptor causes insulin resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"In Friedreich ataxia both FXN alleles usually carry GAA expansions that pack the gene into silent chromatin. Frataxin is a mitochondrial protein needed to build iron-sulfur clusters, so its deficiency cripples respiratory chain enzymes, iron accumulates in mitochondria, and oxidative injury follows. Dorsal root ganglion neurons, dorsal columns, spinocerebellar tracts, cardiomyocytes, and pancreatic beta cells are hit hardest. Because carrier parents are unaffected, there is no generation-to-generation anticipation, even though longer repeats predict earlier onset."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01-C02","obj":"NE-16.2","title":"Down syndrome: chromosomal mechanisms and early Alzheimer disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Down syndrome results from three copies of the long arm of chromosome 21 (21q). The phenotype is a dosage effect: genes on 21q are expressed at about 1.5 times the normal level, which alters development of the brain, face, heart (atrioventricular septal defects), and gut (duodenal atresia). The three ways a person can end up with extra 21q differ in karyotype, cause, link to maternal age, and recurrence risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of Down syndrome","cols":["Mechanism","Frequency","Karyotype","Cause","Maternal age link","Recurrence"],"rows":[["Meiotic nondisjunction","About 95%","47,+21","Failed separation, mostly maternal meiosis I","Strong","Low"],["Robertsonian translocation","About 3 to 4%","46, with a fused chromosome carrying 21q (often 14;21)","De novo or inherited from a balanced carrier parent","None","High if a parent carries the translocation"],["Mosaicism","About 1 to 2%","Mixture of 46 and 47,+21 cell lines","Mitotic nondisjunction after fertilization","None","Low"]],"caption":"Only the translocation form depends on parental karyotype, so it is the one that can recur in a family regardless of maternal age."},{"type":"p","text":"Most trisomy 21 arises in the mother because oocytes enter prophase I before birth and stay arrested until ovulation, sometimes for more than 40 years. Over that time the cohesin that holds chromosomes together deteriorates, and homologs joined by few or poorly placed crossovers are more likely to move to the same pole. This is why risk climbs steeply after about age 35."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a balanced 14;21 carrier has a child with Down syndrome","steps":["Parent has 45 chromosomes, including fused 14q21q","Parent is balanced and phenotypically normal","Gamete receives fused chromosome plus a free 21","Fertilization adds another normal 21","Child has 46 chromosomes but three copies of 21q"],"caption":"The count looks normal, but 21q is present in triplicate; parental karyotyping is required after any translocation case."},{"type":"p","text":"In mosaicism a nondisjunction during an early mitotic division leaves only some cell lines trisomic, so features are often milder and vary with the proportion of trisomic cells in each tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"The amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene lies on 21q. With three copies, cells make about 1.5 times the normal APP, and sequential cleavage by beta-secretase and then gamma-secretase releases more amyloid-beta, especially the aggregation-prone 42 amino acid form. Amyloid-beta deposits as extracellular plaques, followed by tau neurofibrillary tangles and neuron loss, the same cascade as in Alzheimer disease in the general population. Plaques are present in nearly all people with Down syndrome by about age 40, and most develop clinical dementia in their 50s or 60s. The link is specific to APP dosage: duplication of APP alone, without trisomy 21, causes familial early-onset Alzheimer disease."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: recurrence after a translocation case","text":"A 26-year-old woman has a child with Down syndrome whose karyotype shows 46 chromosomes, including a fused 14;21 chromosome. Step 1: 46 chromosomes with extra 21q means translocation Down syndrome, unrelated to maternal age. Step 2: the parents must be karyotyped, because about a quarter of these translocations are inherited. Step 3: if the mother has 45 chromosomes with the 14;21 fusion, she is a balanced carrier, and her observed recurrence risk is about 10 to 15%, far above the risk for her age. Step 4: if both parents have normal karyotypes, the translocation arose de novo and recurrence is low. A parent carrying a 21;21 fusion can have only liveborn children with Down syndrome, because conceptions lacking 21q are lost."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a woman with Down syndrome caused by mosaicism be expected to show Alzheimer pathology as early as a woman with full trisomy 21?","explain":"Probably later or less uniformly, because only her trisomic cells carry three APP copies, so amyloid-beta production depends on the proportion of trisomic cells.","check":"The APP dosage paragraph and the mosaicism row of the mechanisms table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01-C03","obj":"NE-16.3","title":"Neurocutaneous syndromes from tumor suppressor loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1), neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2), tuberous sclerosis complex, and von Hippel-Lindau disease share one logic. Each is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait, yet each tumor arises only when a cell loses its remaining normal allele. Every cell carries one inherited inactive copy; a somatic second hit, often a deletion or mitotic recombination that produces loss of heterozygosity, removes the other. Because second hits occur randomly across millions of cells, affected people develop multiple tumors in several organs, and the dominant pedigree reflects the near certainty that some cell will be hit. About half of NF1 cases arise from new mutations, so a negative family history does not exclude these disorders."},{"type":"table","title":"Autosomal dominant tumor suppressor syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Gene, protein, chromosome","Normal function lost","Nervous system lesions","Other findings"],"rows":[["NF1","NF1, neurofibromin, 17","Ras GTPase-activating protein; Ras stays on","Cutaneous and plexiform neurofibromas, optic pathway glioma","Cafe-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, Lisch nodules, pheochromocytoma"],["NF2","NF2, merlin, 22","Links membrane to actin for contact inhibition","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, spinal ependymomas","Juvenile posterior subcapsular cataracts"],["Tuberous sclerosis","TSC1 hamartin (9); TSC2 tuberin (16)","Complex inactivates Rheb; mTORC1 stays on","Cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, giant cell astrocytoma; seizures, intellectual disability","Ash-leaf macules, facial angiofibromas, cardiac rhabdomyoma, renal angiomyolipoma"],["von Hippel-Lindau","VHL, VHL protein, 3","Targets HIF for degradation; HIF accumulates","Hemangioblastomas of cerebellum, brainstem, spinal cord, and retina","Bilateral clear cell renal carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, pancreatic cysts"]],"caption":"Same inheritance and two-hit mechanism; the lost pathway determines which tumors form."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurofibromin accelerates hydrolysis of GTP on Ras. Without it Ras remains GTP-bound and keeps the RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways active, driving Schwann cell growth in neurofibromas and melanocyte expansion in cafe-au-lait macules. Lisch nodules are pigmented iris hamartomas that do not affect vision."},{"type":"p","text":"Merlin links membrane receptors to the actin cytoskeleton and transmits the signal that stops growth when cells touch, partly through the Hippo pathway. Schwann and meningothelial cells depend most on this brake, so NF2 produces schwannomas, classically on the vestibular division of cranial nerve VIII at the cerebellopontine angle, causing sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and imbalance."},{"type":"p","text":"Hamartin and tuberin form a complex whose GTPase-activating activity keeps the small G protein Rheb inactive. Loss of either releases Rheb-GTP to activate mTORC1, which increases protein synthesis and cell size. The resulting lesions are mostly hamartomas, disorganized overgrowths of tissue native to the site. Cortical tubers contain abnormal giant cells and disrupted lamination that act as seizure foci, explaining infantile spasms and epilepsy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Loss of VHL protein produces pseudohypoxia","steps":["Normal oxygen: prolyl hydroxylases hydroxylate HIF-alpha","VHL protein recognizes hydroxylated HIF and ubiquitinates it","Proteasome degrades HIF","VHL lost: HIF escapes degradation despite oxygen","HIF induces VEGF, erythropoietin, and glucose transporters","Hemangioblastomas, clear cell carcinoma, possible erythrocytosis"],"caption":"Cells lacking VHL behave as if hypoxic, which explains the highly vascular tumors."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01-C04","obj":"NE-16.4","title":"Sturge-Weber syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sturge-Weber syndrome is a congenital capillary-venous malformation affecting the skin of the face, the leptomeninges, and the eye. It is usually grouped with the neurocutaneous syndromes, but its genetics are the opposite of NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, and von Hippel-Lindau disease. It is sporadic, it is caused by an activating (gain of function) mutation rather than loss of a tumor suppressor, and the mutation is present only in a patch of tissue rather than in every cell. It is not inherited, and affected people do not pass it on."},{"type":"p","text":"The mutation lies in GNAQ, which encodes Gq alpha, the G protein subunit that couples receptors to phospholipase C beta. The usual change, arginine 183 to glutamine, impairs the intrinsic GTPase that switches Gq alpha off, so downstream signaling, including the MAPK pathway, stays active in affected endothelial cells. The mutation arises after conception in a progenitor cell, and only that cell's descendants carry it (somatic mosaicism). Timing matters: a mutation early enough to reach progenitors shared by the upper facial skin, the overlying leptomeninges, and the eye produces the full syndrome, whereas a later mutation produces an isolated port-wine stain, which carries the same GNAQ change. The territory of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V1) is used clinically to describe the facial stain; the lesion reflects the embryonic vascular field rather than the nerve itself."},{"type":"table","title":"Lesions of Sturge-Weber syndrome and their consequences","cols":["Lesion","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Facial port-wine stain (nevus flammeus)","Dilated dermal capillaries and venules in the CN V1 territory","Present at birth, usually unilateral; may darken and thicken with age"],["Leptomeningeal angioma, ipsilateral","Capillary-venous malformation of the pia-arachnoid, often occipital and parietal","Seizures in infancy, contralateral hemiparesis, homonymous hemianopia, intellectual disability"],["Cortical injury beneath the angioma","Venous stasis and poor cortical drainage cause chronic ischemia","Cortical atrophy and gyriform (tram-track) calcification on imaging"],["Eye involvement, ipsilateral","Raised episcleral venous pressure and a malformed drainage angle","Glaucoma, sometimes congenital with an enlarged eye (buphthalmos); choroidal hemangioma"]],"caption":"The brain and eye injuries follow from abnormal venous drainage, not from tumor growth."},{"type":"flow","title":"From somatic GNAQ mutation to seizures","steps":["Activating GNAQ mutation in an early progenitor cell","Capillary-venous malformation in face, leptomeninges, and eye","Abnormal pial vessels drain cortex poorly","Venous stasis and chronic cortical hypoxia","Neuron loss, gliosis, calcification","Focal seizures and contralateral deficits"],"caption":"Progressive ischemic injury explains why neurologic findings can worsen after birth."},{"type":"p","text":"Despite the name, the leptomeningeal angioma is a vascular malformation, not a neoplasm, which distinguishes it from the tumors of the tumor suppressor syndromes. A port-wine stain on the forehead or upper eyelid raises the likelihood of brain and eye involvement, but most children with a facial port-wine stain do not have Sturge-Weber syndrome."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01-C05","obj":"NE-16.5","title":"Spinal muscular atrophy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is an autosomal recessive degeneration of the alpha motor neurons in the anterior horns of the spinal cord and the motor nuclei of the lower brainstem. About 95% of patients lack both copies of exon 7 of SMN1 (survival motor neuron 1) on chromosome 5q, usually by deletion or by conversion of SMN1 into its neighbor SMN2. It is one of the most common lethal recessive disorders of infancy, with a carrier frequency of roughly 1 in 40 to 60."},{"type":"p","text":"SMN protein assembles the small nuclear ribonucleoproteins of the spliceosome and helps move mRNA along axons. All cells need it, but alpha motor neurons are the most vulnerable; why they are selectively lost is not fully explained, though their very long axons and high demands on RNA processing are thought to contribute."},{"type":"p","text":"Severity is set by SMN2, a nearly identical gene that everyone carries in variable copy number. A single C to T change in exon 7 of SMN2 disrupts a splicing enhancer, so most SMN2 transcripts skip exon 7 and encode a truncated, unstable protein. Each SMN2 copy therefore contributes only about 10% of full-length SMN, and patients with more copies have more functional protein, later onset, and milder disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why SMN2 copy number sets severity in SMA","steps":["Both SMN1 copies deleted or converted","SMN2 C to T change disrupts an exon 7 splicing enhancer","About 90% of SMN2 mRNA skips exon 7","Each SMN2 copy yields about 10% full-length SMN","Fewer SMN2 copies, less SMN, earlier severe disease"],"caption":"The modifier gene is the only source of SMN protein once SMN1 is lost."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical types of SMA","cols":["Type","Onset","Highest motor milestone","Usual SMN2 copies"],"rows":[["Type 1 (Werdnig-Hoffmann)","Before 6 months","Never sits","Usually 2"],["Type 2","6 to 18 months","Sits, never walks","Usually 3"],["Type 3 (Kugelberg-Welander)","After 18 months","Walks, may lose walking later","3 or 4"],["Type 4","Adulthood","Normal walking, mild weakness","4 or more"]],"caption":"Types form a continuum defined by age of onset and best motor function achieved."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of anterior horn cells removes the final common pathway to muscle, so every sign is a lower motor neuron sign. The severe infantile form presents as a floppy infant with symmetric proximal weakness, frog-leg posture, and absent deep tendon reflexes. Tongue fasciculations reflect degeneration of hypoglossal motor neurons. Intercostal muscles weaken before the diaphragm, so the chest is drawn in while the abdomen rises (paradoxical breathing) and the chest becomes bell-shaped; respiratory failure is the usual cause of death in untreated type 1. Sensory neurons, cortex, and the extraocular muscles are spared, so the infant is alert, with normal sensation and eye movements. Electromyography shows denervation, muscle biopsy shows grouped atrophy, and creatine kinase is normal or only mildly raised."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions are frequently tested. Central (upper motor neuron) causes of infant hypotonia preserve or increase reflexes and often impair alertness. Infant botulism also produces a floppy baby, but blocked acetylcholine release adds constipation, ptosis, poor suck, and sluggish pupils, all absent in SMA."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two siblings both lack SMN1 entirely, yet one sits unsupported at 9 months and the other never achieves head control. What genetic difference best explains this?","explain":"The milder sibling most likely carries more SMN2 copies. SMN1 status is identical, so the amount of full-length SMN made from SMN2 decides how many motor neurons survive.","check":"The SMN2 splicing flow and the clinical types table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-16-01-C06","obj":"NE-16.6","title":"Driver genes in nervous system tumors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tumors arise when mutations inactivate tumor suppressor genes or activate oncogenes. A tumor suppressor acts recessively at the level of the cell, so both copies must be lost; an oncogene acts dominantly, so one activating event, such as amplification, is enough. Retinoblastoma, oligodendroglioma, and neuroblastoma each illustrate a different way a driver gene shapes a tumor's behavior and its laboratory definition."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rb and the G1 to S checkpoint","steps":["Hypophosphorylated Rb binds E2F and represses S-phase genes","Growth signals raise cyclin D, activating CDK4 and CDK6","CDK4/6, then cyclin E-CDK2, hyperphosphorylate Rb, freeing E2F","E2F drives transcription of DNA synthesis genes","Cell enters S phase","Both RB1 copies lost: E2F always free, G1 control lost"],"caption":"Rb is the brake on entry into DNA synthesis; losing both copies removes the brake permanently."},{"type":"p","text":"RB1 on chromosome 13q14 encodes Rb, the protein that holds cells in G1. Knudson's two-hit model came from retinoblastoma. In hereditary cases, about 40%, a germline RB1 mutation is present in every retinal cell, so one somatic hit is enough. Tumors therefore tend to be bilateral, multifocal, and diagnosed earlier, and survivors carry a lifelong risk of second cancers, especially osteosarcoma and other sarcomas. In sporadic cases both hits must occur in the same developing retinal cell, so the tumor is usually unilateral, single, and later. Inheritance appears autosomal dominant with high penetrance, although the gene is recessive within the cell. Retinoblastoma presents as leukocoria, a white pupillary reflex, often with strabismus."},{"type":"p","text":"Oligodendroglioma is now defined molecularly by an IDH1 or IDH2 mutation plus whole-arm codeletion of 1p and 19q. Mutant isocitrate dehydrogenase gains a new activity, converting alpha-ketoglutarate to 2-hydroxyglutarate. This oncometabolite inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent DNA and histone demethylases, producing widespread hypermethylation that blocks glial differentiation. The tumor typically arises in the frontal lobe of adults and presents with seizures; classic histology shows round nuclei with perinuclear halos (fried-egg cells), branching chicken-wire capillaries, and calcification. IDH mutation with 1p/19q codeletion predicts slower growth and longer survival than IDH-wildtype gliomas."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroblastoma arises from neural crest cells of the sympathetic system, most often in the adrenal medulla of children younger than about 5 years. Tumor cells secrete catecholamines, so urinary homovanillic acid and vanillylmandelic acid are raised, and histology shows small round blue cells with Homer Wright rosettes. MYCN on chromosome 2p encodes N-Myc, a transcription factor that drives proliferation and blocks differentiation. Amplification, often more than 10 copies, occurs in about a quarter of tumors and marks aggressive disease with poor prognosis independent of age and stage."},{"type":"table","title":"Driver gene defects in nervous system tumors","cols":["Tumor","Driver","Type of alteration","Behavior within the cell","Significance"],"rows":[["Retinoblastoma","RB1 (13q14)","Two-hit loss: mutation, deletion, or loss of heterozygosity","Recessive tumor suppressor","Germline first hit: bilateral, early, later osteosarcoma"],["Oligodendroglioma","IDH1 or IDH2 with 1p/19q codeletion","Point mutation (neomorphic enzyme) plus chromosome arm loss","Gain of a new function","Defining marker; better prognosis than IDH-wildtype glioma"],["Neuroblastoma","MYCN (2p)","Gene amplification","Dominant oncogene","Aggressive disease, poor prognosis"]],"caption":"The type of alteration predicts how many events are needed and how the tumor behaves."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: bilateral versus unilateral retinoblastoma","text":"A 9-month-old has tumors in both eyes; a 2-year-old has one tumor in one eye. Step 1: bilateral, early disease implies a germline RB1 mutation, because every retinal cell already carries the first hit and only a common somatic second hit is needed. Step 2: this child will pass the mutation to about 50% of children, and most carriers develop retinoblastoma because second hits are very likely. Step 3: the second child probably has two somatic hits in one cell, which is rare, explaining one tumor at a later age. Step 4: unilateral disease does not fully exclude a germline mutation, which is present in a minority of unilateral cases, so germline testing is still informative."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"This chapter applies a few molecular rules across very different diseases. Loss of function needs both copies in a cell (Friedreich ataxia, SMA, tumor suppressor syndromes, retinoblastoma), whereas gain of function needs one (Huntington disease, myotonic dystrophy RNA toxicity, Sturge-Weber, MYCN). Dosage alone can cause disease, as an extra APP copy shows, and timing of a mutation decides whether it is inherited in every cell, confined to one tissue, or limited to a tumor. Anatomy and embryology explain distribution: neural crest derivatives link pigment, nerve sheath, and adrenal lesions, and the territory of an early progenitor defines the Sturge-Weber field."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and biochemistry connect these genes to familiar pathways: Ras, mTOR, HIF, Rb and E2F, mitochondrial iron-sulfur assembly, and splicing. The limits matter. Repeat thresholds overlap, penetrance in the intermediate ranges is uncertain, phenotype severity within one genotype still varies, and tumor classifications now rest on molecular criteria that are revised over time, so definitions should be checked against current references."}],"summary":["Repeat instability increases with length, causing anticipation in Huntington disease (paternal), fragile X (maternal), and myotonic dystrophy (congenital form maternal), but not in recessive Friedreich ataxia.","Huntington CAG makes toxic polyglutamine huntingtin; fragile X CGG silences FMR1 by hypermethylation; myotonic dystrophy CTG makes toxic RNA that missplices ClC-1; Friedreich GAA blocks frataxin transcription.","About 95% of Down syndrome is nondisjunction, mostly maternal meiosis I and age-related; Robertsonian translocation can recur regardless of maternal age; mosaicism is usually milder.","Three copies of APP on chromosome 21 raise amyloid-beta, giving plaques by about age 40 and early-onset Alzheimer disease.","NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, and von Hippel-Lindau are autosomal dominant but need a second somatic hit in each tumor cell.","Neurofibromin turns off Ras, merlin mediates contact inhibition, hamartin-tuberin restrains mTORC1 through Rheb, and VHL protein targets HIF for degradation.","Sturge-Weber is a sporadic somatic activating GNAQ mutation: CN V1 port-wine stain, ipsilateral leptomeningeal angioma, seizures, and glaucoma.","SMA is recessive loss of SMN1 with pure lower motor neuron signs; SMN2 copy number sets severity.","RB1 needs two hits, oligodendroglioma is defined by IDH mutation with 1p/19q codeletion, and MYCN amplification marks aggressive neuroblastoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Regulation of gene expression and RNA processing"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Genetic Disease"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central nervous system and neural crest derivatives"}]},"NE-17":{"unit":"NE-17","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17-01","title":"Nervous and Endocrine System Interactions","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the brain runs the endocrine system. The hypothalamus controls the anterior pituitary with hormones carried in portal blood and controls the posterior pituitary with axons that end there, so damage to the hypothalamus, stalk, or sella produces predictable hormonal and visual findings. It then follows two neuroendocrine systems outward: the stress response, with its fast sympathoadrenal arm and slow HPA arm, and thyroid hormone, which builds the developing brain and sets the speed of adult neuromuscular function. Board questions usually describe a sellar mass, a head injury, a postoperative sodium problem, or a reflex finding and ask you to name the structure or hormone responsible.","prerequisites":["Hypothalamic nuclei and the gross anatomy of the sella turcica, optic chiasm, and cavernous sinus","Retinotopic organization of the visual pathway and crossing of nasal retinal fibers","Autonomic preganglionic and postganglionic organization and adrenergic receptors (NE-9)","G protein signaling (Gs, Gi, Gq) and nuclear hormone receptor action","Renal water handling in the collecting duct and plasma osmolality"],"connections":[{"to":"Autonomic nervous system (NE-9)","how":"The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion, so the fast stress response is ordinary preganglionic sympathetic signaling ending on chromaffin cells."},{"to":"Visual pathway","how":"Because nasal retinal fibers cross in the chiasm just above the sella, an expanding pituitary mass removes the temporal visual fields."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"ADH acting on V2 receptors in the collecting duct links hypothalamic injury to serum sodium and urine concentration."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF activate the HPA axis, and cortisol in turn suppresses the inflammatory response."},{"to":"Endocrine pathology","how":"Pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma, adrenal insufficiency, and thyroid disease each present with neurological findings explained by the pathways in this chapter."}],"terms":[{"term":"Median eminence","def":"The floor of the third ventricle at the base of the infundibulum, a circumventricular organ with fenestrated capillaries where hypothalamic hormones enter portal blood."},{"term":"Hypophyseal portal system","def":"A capillary bed in the median eminence drained by portal veins to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary."},{"term":"Parvocellular neurons","def":"Small hypothalamic neurons that release releasing and inhibiting hormones into the median eminence."},{"term":"Magnocellular neurons","def":"Large neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei whose axons carry ADH and oxytocin to the posterior pituitary."},{"term":"Neurophysin","def":"A carrier protein cleaved from the same precursor as ADH or oxytocin that travels with the hormone in secretory granules."},{"term":"Tuberoinfundibular dopamine neurons","def":"Arcuate nucleus neurons that release dopamine into portal blood to inhibit prolactin secretion tonically."},{"term":"Stalk effect","def":"Mild hyperprolactinemia caused by interruption of dopamine delivery down a compressed or damaged pituitary stalk."},{"term":"Bitemporal hemianopia","def":"Loss of the temporal half of the visual field in both eyes from damage to crossing fibers in the optic chiasm."},{"term":"Chromaffin cell","def":"A neural crest derived adrenal medullary cell, equivalent to a postganglionic sympathetic neuron, that secretes catecholamines into blood."},{"term":"PNMT","def":"Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase, the cortisol-induced enzyme that converts norepinephrine to epinephrine in the adrenal medulla."},{"term":"Type 2 deiodinase","def":"An enzyme in glial cells that converts T4 to active T3 inside the brain."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17-01-C01","obj":"NE-17.1","title":"The hypophyseal portal system and hypothalamic control of the anterior pituitary","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) develops from Rathke pouch, an outgrowth of oral ectoderm, so it has no nerve supply from the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus controls it through blood instead. Parvocellular neurons release small peptides and dopamine into fenestrated capillaries of the median eminence, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier. These capillaries, supplied by the superior hypophyseal arteries from the internal carotid, drain into long portal veins that run down the pituitary stalk and open into a second capillary bed (sinusoids) around the endocrine cells. Most of the anterior lobe's blood arrives this way rather than from a direct artery."},{"type":"p","text":"The portal arrangement explains why hypothalamic hormones work in such small amounts. They reach their target before dilution in the systemic circulation, at concentrations far higher than in peripheral blood. It also explains a key weakness: the anterior pituitary depends on an intact stalk for both its signals and much of its blood supply."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypothalamic signal to anterior pituitary output","steps":["Parvocellular neuron releases hormone in median eminence","Hormone enters fenestrated primary capillary plexus","Long portal veins carry it down the stalk","Secondary capillary plexus bathes anterior pituitary cells","Pituitary hormone enters systemic circulation","Target gland hormone feeds back on hypothalamus and pituitary"],"caption":"Two capillary beds in series let tiny amounts of hypothalamic hormone act at high concentration."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypothalamic hormones that control the anterior pituitary","cols":["Hormone","Main source","Receptor signal","Effect on anterior pituitary"],"rows":[["TRH (tripeptide)","Paraventricular nucleus","Gq","Raises TSH; also raises prolactin"],["CRH","Paraventricular nucleus","Gs","Raises ACTH (from POMC); ADH acts with it"],["GnRH","Preoptic area and arcuate nucleus","Gq","Raises LH and FSH only when pulsatile"],["GHRH","Arcuate nucleus","Gs","Raises GH"],["Somatostatin","Periventricular nucleus","Gi","Lowers GH and TSH"],["Dopamine","Arcuate nucleus (tuberoinfundibular)","Gi (D2)","Lowers prolactin"]],"caption":"Four stimulatory and two inhibitory signals; only prolactin is controlled mainly by inhibition."},{"type":"p","text":"GnRH illustrates why the pattern of delivery matters. Gonadotrophs respond to pulses, roughly hourly to every few hours depending on cycle phase. Continuous GnRH exposure downregulates and desensitizes its receptors, so LH and FSH fall, which is the mechanism behind the suppressive effect of continuous GnRH agonists."},{"type":"h","text":"Why dopamine is the main control of prolactin"},{"type":"p","text":"Lactotrophs secrete prolactin at a high rate on their own, so their control has to be a brake. Dopamine from tuberoinfundibular neurons acts on D2 receptors, which couple to Gi: cAMP falls, K+ channels open and hyperpolarize the cell, voltage-gated Ca2+ entry drops, and prolactin release, gene transcription, and lactotroph proliferation are all suppressed. Prolactin has no target gland hormone to feed back on it, so it regulates itself by a short loop, raising dopamine output from the same arcuate neurons. Stimulators such as TRH, estrogen, and suckling exist but are secondary. The result is a distinctive rule: if the stalk is cut or the hypothalamus destroyed, every other anterior pituitary hormone falls while prolactin rises. Drugs that block D2 receptors raise prolactin for the same reason, and primary hypothyroidism can raise it through excess TRH."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A car crash transects the pituitary stalk. Predict the direction of change for GH, ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, and prolactin.","explain":"Five hormones lose their stimulatory releasing hormone and fall; prolactin loses its dopamine brake and rises.","check":"Compare with the hormone table and the paragraph on dopamine control of prolactin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17-01-C02","obj":"NE-17.2","title":"ADH and oxytocin, central diabetes insipidus, and SIADH","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is a downgrowth of the diencephalon and contains no endocrine cells. Magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic nucleus (SON) and paraventricular nucleus (PVN) make the hormones; ADH (vasopressin) comes mainly from the SON and oxytocin mainly from the PVN, although each nucleus makes some of both. Each hormone is synthesized as a preprohormone with its own carrier protein (neurophysin II with ADH, neurophysin I with oxytocin), packaged into granules, and moved by fast axonal transport down the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract through the stalk. Granules are stored in swollen axon terminals (Herring bodies) and released by action potential triggered Ca2+ entry into fenestrated capillaries."},{"type":"flow","title":"ADH response to rising plasma osmolality","steps":["Water loss raises plasma osmolality above about 285 mOsm/kg","Hypothalamic osmoreceptors shrink and fire","SON and PVN neurons release ADH from posterior pituitary","ADH binds V2 receptors, Gs raises cAMP","Aquaporin-2 inserts into collecting duct apical membrane","Water is reabsorbed and osmolality falls"],"caption":"Osmoreceptors near the lamina terminalis respond to changes of about 1%; a fall in blood volume of roughly 5 to 10% is needed before baroreceptors become a strong stimulus."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxytocin contracts myoepithelial cells for milk ejection, a neuroendocrine reflex triggered by suckling, and uterine smooth muscle in labor, where cervical stretch drives a positive feedback loop. ADH also constricts vessels through V1 receptors (Gq), which matters mainly in severe hypovolemia."},{"type":"h","text":"How CNS injury disturbs ADH"},{"type":"p","text":"Central diabetes insipidus (DI) is too little ADH. The collecting duct stays impermeable to water, so large volumes of dilute urine (usually below about 300 mOsm/kg) are lost. Thirst usually compensates, so serum sodium is high normal; it rises clearly when the patient cannot drink or the thirst center is also damaged. Causes include head trauma, pituitary surgery, craniopharyngioma, and infiltrative or metastatic disease of the stalk. Permanent DI requires loss of most magnocellular cell bodies, so a high stalk or hypothalamic lesion is more likely to be permanent, while a low stalk lesion often recovers because cut axons can release ADH from their proximal ends. When ADH is given, urine concentrates in central DI but not in nephrogenic DI, which locates the defect."},{"type":"p","text":"SIADH is ADH secretion that persists despite low plasma osmolality. Water is retained, serum sodium falls, and urine stays inappropriately concentrated. Patients look euvolemic because the modest volume expansion triggers natriuresis, so urine sodium stays high. CNS causes include head trauma, subarachnoid hemorrhage, meningitis, encephalitis, stroke, and tumors; small cell lung cancer is the classic ectopic source. Falling osmolality draws water into brain cells, causing headache, confusion, and seizures. Cerebral salt wasting after subarachnoid hemorrhage also gives hyponatremia with high urine sodium, but the patient is volume depleted."},{"type":"table","title":"Central DI and SIADH compared","cols":["Feature","Central DI","SIADH"],"rows":[["ADH","Low","Inappropriately present"],["Urine","Large volume, dilute","Inappropriately concentrated (above about 100 mOsm/kg)"],["Serum sodium (about 135 to 145 mEq/L)","High or high normal","Low"],["Plasma osmolality (about 275 to 295 mOsm/kg)","High or high normal","Low"],["Volume status","Normal or depleted","Euvolemic, urine sodium high"],["Response to ADH","Urine concentrates","Not applicable; already excess"]],"caption":"Plasma and urine osmolality read together locate the problem; one value alone does not."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: sodium after pituitary surgery","text":"After transsphenoidal surgery, a patient passes large volumes of dilute urine on day 1 with sodium 147 mEq/L. Injured axons have stopped conducting, so ADH release fails (phase 1, DI). Around day 6, urine output drops and sodium falls to 128 mEq/L despite normal volume. Degenerating terminals are now leaking their stored ADH without regulation (phase 2, SIADH pattern). Once the stores are exhausted, polyuria returns and persists if most SON and PVN cell bodies have died (phase 3, permanent DI). Each phase follows from where ADH is made, stored, and released."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17-01-C03","obj":"NE-17.3","title":"Sellar masses: chiasmal compression and the stalk effect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pituitary sits in the sella turcica, roofed by the diaphragma sellae. The optic chiasm lies above the diaphragma, separated from it by the suprasellar cistern, and the hypothalamus and third ventricle lie just behind and above the chiasm. The cavernous sinuses flank the sella; CN III, CN IV, V1, and V2 run in their lateral walls, and CN VI and the internal carotid artery run through them. A mass's direction of growth therefore predicts its signs."},{"type":"h","text":"Visual field loss"},{"type":"p","text":"In the chiasm, fibers from the nasal hemiretinas cross. Nasal retina sees the temporal field, so midline compression removes both temporal fields (bitemporal hemianopia) while sparing the uncrossed temporal retinal fibers. Inferior crossing fibers come from the inferior nasal retina, which sees the upper temporal field. A pituitary adenoma growing up from below presses these first, so loss begins in the upper temporal quadrants. A craniopharyngioma often presses from above and can begin with lower temporal loss. Because loss is gradual and central vision is spared early, patients often notice it late. If the chiasm sits unusually far forward or back, the same mass may compress an optic tract (homonymous hemianopia) or an optic nerve (monocular loss)."},{"type":"table","title":"Pituitary adenoma and craniopharyngioma compared","cols":["Feature","Pituitary adenoma","Craniopharyngioma"],"rows":[["Origin","Anterior pituitary cells","Rathke pouch remnants"],["Age","Mainly adults","Childhood peak and a second peak in older adults"],["Position","Intrasellar, grows upward","Usually suprasellar, presses down"],["Early field loss","Upper temporal quadrants","Often lower temporal quadrants"],["Pathology","Monomorphic cells; macroadenoma 10 mm or more","Adamantinomatous (children): cysts, calcification, CTNNB1; papillary (adults): BRAF V600E"],["Hypothalamic signs and DI","Uncommon","Common: DI, growth failure, obesity, hydrocephalus"]],"caption":"Diabetes insipidus at presentation points away from a plain adenoma, because an adenoma rarely damages the hypothalamus or high stalk."},{"type":"h","text":"Hormonal effects and the stalk effect"},{"type":"p","text":"A nonfunctioning mass compresses the normal gland and the portal vessels, producing hypopituitarism, classically with GH and gonadotropins failing first and ACTH last. Compression of the stalk also blocks dopamine delivery, so lactotrophs are released from inhibition and prolactin rises, usually only mildly. Raised prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH release (largely through hypothalamic kisspeptin neurons), causing amenorrhea, infertility, galactorrhea, low libido, and erectile dysfunction. A prolactinoma, the most common hormone-secreting adenoma, produces prolactin in proportion to its size, so a large mass with only mild hyperprolactinemia suggests stalk effect."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stalk effect","steps":["Sellar mass compresses pituitary stalk","Dopamine delivery in portal blood falls","Lactotrophs lose D2 inhibition","Prolactin rises modestly","Prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH","LH and FSH fall, causing hypogonadism"],"caption":"A nonfunctioning mass can produce galactorrhea and amenorrhea through loss of dopamine inhibition alone."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: adenoma or stalk effect?","text":"A man has headaches, bitemporal field loss, low libido, and a 3 cm sellar mass. Prolactin is about 60 ng/mL (normal roughly 20 or less). A prolactinoma this large would usually push prolactin into the hundreds or thousands, because output tracks tumor mass. A level this modest fits a nonfunctioning adenoma blocking dopamine flow down the stalk, with low gonadotropins from compression and prolactin-induced GnRH suppression. One caution: an extremely high prolactin can saturate the immunoassay and read falsely low (hook effect), so a large mass with a modest level is often retested on a diluted sample."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17-01-C04","obj":"NE-17.4","title":"The neuroendocrine stress response","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A stressor, whether hemorrhage, hypoglycemia, infection, pain, or fear, reaches the hypothalamus through brainstem visceral pathways, the amygdala, and circulating cytokines. The paraventricular nucleus (PVN) then drives two outputs that differ in speed because one is neural and the other is a hormonal cascade."},{"type":"p","text":"In the fast arm, PVN and other hypothalamic neurons project to brainstem sympathetic centers and directly to preganglionic neurons in the intermediolateral cell column of the thoracolumbar cord. Preganglionic fibers in the splanchnic nerves pass through the sympathetic chain without synapsing and end directly on adrenal medullary chromaffin cells. These neural crest derived cells are modified postganglionic neurons: acetylcholine opens their nicotinic receptors, Ca2+ enters, and catecholamines are released into blood within seconds, about 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine. Epinephrine raises heart rate and contractility (beta 1), dilates bronchi and skeletal muscle vessels (beta 2), and mobilizes fuel through glycogenolysis and lipolysis, while alpha 2 effects on beta cells hold insulin down."},{"type":"p","text":"In the slow arm (the HPA axis), parvocellular PVN neurons release CRH, often with ADH, which strengthens the response. In the anterior pituitary, CRH acts through Gs to stimulate corticotrophs, which cleave POMC to ACTH. ACTH binds MC2R in the zona fasciculata; cAMP increases StAR-mediated cholesterol transport to the inner mitochondrial membrane, the rate-limiting step. Cortisol is not stored, so its rise lags ACTH by minutes."},{"type":"table","title":"The two arms of the stress response","cols":["Feature","Sympathoadrenal arm","HPA axis"],"rows":[["Signal path","Hypothalamus, preganglionic sympathetic fibers, adrenal medulla","PVN, CRH in portal blood, ACTH, adrenal cortex"],["Hormone","Epinephrine (with norepinephrine)","Cortisol"],["Onset","Seconds","Minutes to hours"],["Mechanism","Membrane receptors (adrenergic)","Intracellular glucocorticoid receptor changes gene transcription"],["Main effects","Cardiac output, bronchodilation, glucose and fatty acid release","Gluconeogenesis, protein catabolism, permissive vascular tone, anti-inflammation"]],"caption":"The neural arm meets an emergency; the hormonal arm sustains fuel supply and circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol raises glucose through hepatic gluconeogenesis supplied by muscle protein breakdown and lipolysis. It is permissive for catecholamines, maintaining adrenergic receptor expression and vascular responsiveness. It suppresses inflammation by inducing annexin A1, which limits phospholipase A2, and by inhibiting NF-kappa B, which lowers IL-1, IL-2, and TNF production. Cortisol also feeds back on the PVN, corticotrophs, and hippocampus to end the response."},{"type":"p","text":"The arms are linked anatomically and chemically. Blood draining the cortex carries high cortisol into the medulla, where it induces PNMT, the enzyme that converts norepinephrine to epinephrine; that is why the medulla secretes mostly epinephrine. CRH activates the locus coeruleus, and norepinephrine stimulates CRH neurons, so each arm reinforces the other. When cortisol is absent, as in adrenal insufficiency, catecholamines lose much of their vascular effect and stress can cause shock that resists pressors. When the axis stays active for weeks, sustained cortisol produces hyperglycemia, muscle wasting, immunosuppression, and hypertension."},{"type":"cycle","title":"HPA axis negative feedback","steps":["Stressor activates PVN CRH neurons","CRH reaches corticotrophs through portal blood","Corticotrophs release ACTH","Zona fasciculata synthesizes cortisol","Cortisol inhibits CRH and ACTH release"],"caption":"Cortisol shuts off its own drive; loss of this feedback raises ACTH in primary adrenal failure."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on long-term glucocorticoid therapy has surgery. Predict the adrenal cortex response to the stress.","explain":"Prolonged feedback suppression of CRH and ACTH atrophies the zona fasciculata, so cortisol cannot rise and catecholamine responses lose their permissive support.","check":"Compare with the HPA cycle and the paragraph on permissive effects."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-17-01-C05","obj":"NE-17.5","title":"Thyroid hormone and the nervous system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormone acts on the brain mainly as T3, but most of that T3 is made inside the brain. T4 crosses the blood-brain barrier on transporters (including MCT8 and OATP1C1), and type 2 deiodinase in glial cells (astrocytes and tanycytes, third ventricle glia) converts it to T3, which then enters neurons. Neurons express type 3 deiodinase, which inactivates T3. This local control lets the brain keep T3 relatively stable when serum T4 changes. T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors, mainly TR alpha 1 in the brain, which pair with the retinoid X receptor on thyroid response elements and change transcription of genes for neuronal migration, dendrite and axon growth, synapse formation, and myelin proteins."},{"type":"h","text":"Congenital hypothyroidism"},{"type":"p","text":"These gene programs run during a critical period from late fetal life through about the first two to three years. In the first trimester the fetus depends on maternal T4; the fetal thyroid begins making hormone at about 10 to 12 weeks. A newborn without a working thyroid still receives some maternal T4 through the placenta, so cord blood T4 is roughly 25 to 50% of normal and the baby often looks normal at birth. Damage accumulates after delivery, when that supply is gone. Early signs are lethargy, poor feeding, prolonged jaundice, hypotonia, macroglossia, umbilical hernia, and a large posterior fontanelle. If the deficit continues through the critical period, the result is permanent intellectual disability and short stature, because neurons that missed their window of growth and myelination cannot recover it later. This is why newborns are screened. In iodine-sufficient regions most cases come from thyroid dysgenesis; worldwide, iodine deficiency remains the leading preventable cause of intellectual disability."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why congenital hypothyroidism damages the brain permanently","steps":["Neonatal thyroid fails; maternal T4 supply ends at birth","Brain T3 falls despite increased deiodinase activity","Fewer TR alpha 1 target genes are transcribed","Dendrite growth, synapse formation, and myelination slow","Critical period closes","Permanent intellectual disability"],"caption":"The timing of the deficit, not its severity alone, decides whether the damage is reversible."},{"type":"h","text":"Adult hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism"},{"type":"p","text":"In the adult brain, thyroid hormone no longer builds circuits, so its effects are on speed and arousal. The most testable sign is the delayed relaxation phase of the deep tendon reflex in hypothyroidism. The cause is in muscle, not nerve: T3 normally drives expression of SERCA, the sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ pump, and fast myosin isoforms. With less T3, Ca2+ is pumped back slowly and cross-bridges detach slowly, so the limb returns slowly after a normal jerk. In hyperthyroidism the reverse occurs, and T3 also increases beta-adrenergic receptor number and responsiveness. Catecholamine levels are normal or low, yet sympathetic effects are exaggerated, producing a fine, fast postural tremor, brisk reflexes, anxiety, and insomnia."},{"type":"table","title":"Neurological effects of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency","cols":["Feature","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism"],"rows":[["Reflexes","Normal contraction, slow relaxation phase","Brisk, with fast relaxation"],["Tremor","Absent","Fine postural tremor (enhanced physiological tremor)"],["Mood and cognition","Slowing, poor memory, depression, somnolence","Anxiety, irritability, insomnia"],["Muscle","Myopathy with cramps and raised CK","Proximal weakness; rare hypokalemic periodic paralysis"],["Peripheral nerve","Carpal tunnel syndrome from myxedematous tissue","Usually unaffected"],["Lab link","Hyponatremia from impaired free water excretion; high TRH can raise prolactin","Low TSH (about 0.4 to 4.0 mU/L normal) with high free T4"]],"caption":"Most adult findings reflect altered metabolic rate, Ca2+ handling in muscle, and beta-adrenergic sensitivity rather than structural brain injury."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An adult who develops severe hypothyroidism and a newborn with untreated congenital hypothyroidism both have low T3 for one year. Predict which brain changes will reverse when thyroid hormone is restored.","explain":"The adult's slowing reverses because the circuits are already built; the infant's deficits largely persist because the critical period for growth and myelination has passed.","check":"Compare with the congenital hypothyroidism flow and the paragraph on adult effects."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology explain most of this chapter. The anterior pituitary comes from oral ectoderm and receives no axons, so it depends on portal blood, while the posterior pituitary is brain tissue that only stores hormone, so injury at different levels of the stalk produces different syndromes. The chiasm above the sella turns a pituitary mass into a visual field problem, and the adrenal medulla, built from neural crest, is effectively a sympathetic ganglion that secretes into blood. Renal physiology and immunology complete the picture: ADH errors appear as serum sodium disorders, and cytokines drive the HPA axis that cortisol uses to restrain inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory cutoffs for prolactin, sodium, and osmolality vary between laboratories and sources, field defects do not always follow textbook order because chiasm position varies, and the triphasic ADH response after surgery is a pattern only some patients complete. Reason from mechanism first, then check the value against the stated reference range."}],"summary":["Parvocellular hypothalamic hormones enter fenestrated median eminence capillaries and reach the anterior pituitary through portal veins at high concentration.","TRH, CRH, GnRH (pulsatile), and GHRH stimulate; somatostatin inhibits GH and TSH, and dopamine through D2 and Gi is the main control of prolactin.","Cutting the stalk lowers every anterior pituitary hormone except prolactin, which rises.","ADH (mainly SON) and oxytocin (mainly PVN) travel with neurophysins down axons and are released from the posterior pituitary.","Central DI gives dilute polyuria and high normal to high sodium; SIADH gives euvolemic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine and high urine sodium.","Pituitary surgery or stalk injury can produce DI, then an SIADH pattern from leaking terminals, then permanent DI.","Midline chiasm compression causes bitemporal hemianopia; adenomas from below take the upper temporal fields first.","Mild hyperprolactinemia with a large mass suggests stalk effect; prolactinoma levels track tumor size.","The stress response pairs seconds-fast epinephrine from chromaffin cells with slower cortisol from the HPA axis; cortisol induces PNMT and is permissive for catecholamines.","Brain T3 is made locally by type 2 deiodinase; congenital deficiency causes permanent intellectual disability, while adult hypothyroidism slows reflex relaxation and hyperthyroidism causes tremor and hyperreflexia."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones; Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation, the hypothalamic-pituitary axis and the adrenal medulla"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, sections on the pituitary and the thyroid; The Central Nervous System, sellar tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, the pituitary gland, cavernous sinus, and optic chiasm"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Hypothalamus and Pituitary Gland"}]},"NE-18":{"unit":"NE-18","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01","title":"Immune Responses in Neurological Disease","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The nervous system keeps most of the immune system at a distance, yet many neurologic diseases are driven by immune responses. This chapter explains how the blood-brain barrier and microglia shape CNS defense and how cytokines in bacterial meningitis cause cerebral edema. It then covers autoimmune attack on CNS myelin (multiple sclerosis), peripheral nerve (Guillain-Barré syndrome), and the neuromuscular junction (myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome), paraneoplastic syndromes, and the immunology of vaccines against meningitis and polio. Board questions usually give a CSF result, an antibody, or an antecedent infection and ask you to name the target, the effector, and the hypersensitivity type.","prerequisites":["Innate immunity: toll-like receptors, NF-kB, IL-1 and TNF, neutrophil recruitment (IM-13)","Antibody classes, class switching, and T cell help to B cells (IM-5)","Gell and Coombs hypersensitivity types (IM-15-C)","Myelination by oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells, and saltatory conduction (NE-2, NE-13)","CSF formation, flow, and normal composition (NE-4, NE-10)"],"connections":[{"to":"Synaptic transmission (NE-13)","how":"Myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome are explained by the quantal release of acetylcholine and the safety factor of the end-plate potential."},{"to":"Neurological pathogens (NE-19) and CNS infections (NE-20-G)","how":"The organisms that cause meningitis and polio are the targets of the vaccines covered here, and their structures trigger the cytokine response."},{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"The pathology of multiple sclerosis plaques and ADEM builds on the T cell mechanisms taught in this chapter."},{"to":"Pulmonary and reproductive neoplasia","how":"Small cell lung cancer, ovarian teratoma, and gynecologic cancers supply the shared antigens behind paraneoplastic syndromes."},{"to":"Diseases of increased intracranial fluid (NE-20-C)","how":"Vasogenic, cytotoxic, and interstitial edema from meningitis raise intracranial pressure and reduce cerebral perfusion."}],"terms":[{"term":"Blood-brain barrier","def":"The tight junction-sealed, nonfenestrated capillary endothelium of the CNS, supported by basement membrane, pericytes, and astrocyte end-feet, that restricts passage of cells, proteins, and polar solutes."},{"term":"Microglia","def":"Resident macrophages of the CNS, derived from yolk sac precursors, that survey tissue, phagocytose debris, present antigen, and release cytokines."},{"term":"Vasogenic edema","def":"Extracellular accumulation of plasma water and protein in brain tissue after the blood-brain barrier opens."},{"term":"Cerebral perfusion pressure","def":"The pressure driving blood through the brain, equal to mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure."},{"term":"Oligoclonal bands","def":"Discrete IgG bands on CSF electrophoresis, absent from paired serum, that indicate antibody synthesis by a few B cell clones inside the CNS."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of an immune response raised against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar host molecule."},{"term":"Albuminocytologic dissociation","def":"Raised CSF protein with a normal CSF cell count, typical of Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"term":"Type II hypersensitivity","def":"Injury or dysfunction caused by antibody binding to antigen fixed on a cell surface or in tissue matrix."},{"term":"Safety factor","def":"The margin by which the end-plate potential normally exceeds the depolarization needed to trigger a muscle action potential."},{"term":"Onconeural antigen","def":"A protein normally restricted to neurons that is also expressed by a tumor and becomes the target of a paraneoplastic immune response."},{"term":"Linked recognition","def":"Help given by a T cell to a B cell when both recognize different parts of the same physical antigen, the basis of conjugate vaccines."},{"term":"Secretory IgA","def":"Dimeric IgA joined by J chain and transported across mucosal epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor, which protects mucosal surfaces."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01-C01","obj":"NE-18.1","title":"The blood-brain barrier, microglia, and cytokine-driven edema in bacterial meningitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is formed by continuous, nonfenestrated capillary endothelium whose cells are sealed by tight junctions (claudin-5 and occludin) and carry out very little transcytosis. A basement membrane, pericytes, and astrocyte end-feet surround the capillary and maintain these properties. Solutes cross only if they are lipid soluble or have a carrier, such as GLUT1 for glucose. The barrier is absent at the circumventricular organs (for example the area postrema and posterior pituitary), and the choroid plexus has fenestrated capillaries, so its barrier lies in the choroid epithelium instead."},{"type":"p","text":"The barrier also limits immune surveillance. Normal CSF holds fewer than 5 cells/µL, very little complement, and IgG at well under 1% of the plasma level, so the subarachnoid space is poor at opsonizing bacteria. Innate defense rests mainly on microglia. Unlike neurons and macroglia, which arise from neuroectoderm, microglia derive from yolk sac (mesodermal) myeloid precursors. At rest they are ramified and survey the tissue with their processes. When their pattern recognition receptors are engaged, they retract their processes, become amoeboid, phagocytose, increase MHC class II, and release cytokines. Perivascular and meningeal macrophages play the same role at the borders of the brain."},{"type":"h","text":"How cytokines open the barrier in bacterial meningitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Bacteria usually reach the subarachnoid space from the bloodstream after nasopharyngeal colonization, then multiply freely in CSF. Their cell wall products activate toll-like receptors (TLR4 for the lipopolysaccharide or lipooligosaccharide of N. meningitidis and H. influenzae, TLR2 and NOD-like receptors for pneumococcal lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan). Through NF-kB, microglia, macrophages, and endothelium release IL-1 beta and TNF-alpha, with IL-6 and chemokines."},{"type":"flow","title":"From bacterial products to cerebral edema","steps":["Bacteria multiply in CSF lacking complement and antibody","Cell wall products activate TLRs on microglia and macrophages","NF-kB drives IL-1 and TNF release","Endothelial E-selectin and ICAM-1 recruit neutrophils","Neutrophil proteases and oxidants loosen tight junctions","Plasma water and protein leak: vasogenic edema","Intracranial pressure rises; perfusion falls; herniation risk"],"caption":"IL-1 and TNF convert a local infection into barrier failure, edema, and raised intracranial pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"Edema has three components. Vasogenic edema comes from the open barrier. Cytotoxic edema follows when bacterial toxins, neutrophil products, excess glutamate, and ischemia fail the Na+/K+-ATPase, so cells swell. Interstitial edema appears when purulent exudate blocks CSF absorption at the arachnoid granulations, producing hydrocephalus. Together they raise intracranial pressure (ICP), which lowers cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = mean arterial pressure minus ICP); the resulting ischemia adds more cytotoxic edema, a self-reinforcing cycle. The same mechanisms explain the CSF: many neutrophils, high protein from the leaky barrier, and low glucose because GLUT1 transport is impaired while neutrophils, bacteria, and brain tissue consume it."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: intracranial pressure and perfusion","text":"A patient with pneumococcal meningitis has a mean arterial pressure of 90 mmHg. With a normal ICP of about 10 mmHg, CPP is 90 minus 10, or 80 mmHg, within the range where autoregulation holds cerebral blood flow steady. As edema raises ICP to 40 mmHg, CPP falls to 50 mmHg, near the lower limit of autoregulation (about 50 to 60 mmHg). Any further rise in ICP or fall in blood pressure now reduces flow passively, the brain becomes ischemic, cells swell, and ICP rises further. The Cushing reflex (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) reflects the brainstem trying to restore CPP by raising arterial pressure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01-C02","obj":"NE-18.2","title":"Multiple sclerosis: T cell-mediated injury to CNS myelin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a chronic immune-mediated disease that destroys CNS myelin and the oligodendrocytes that make it, producing lesions separated in time and in space. Peripheral myelin, made by Schwann cells, is spared, which is one feature that separates MS from Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of an MS plaque","steps":["Myelin-reactive CD4 T cells escape tolerance","Activated in peripheral lymphoid tissue, possibly by mimicry","VLA-4 binds endothelial VCAM-1; T cells cross the barrier","Reactivated by microglia presenting myelin peptides on MHC II","Th1 releases IFN-gamma; Th17 releases IL-17","Macrophages and microglia strip and phagocytose myelin","Demyelinated axons conduct slowly or fail"],"caption":"CD4 T cells start the lesion; macrophages, microglia, CD8 T cells, B cells, and antibody carry out the injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Th1 cells secrete interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages and microglia. Th17 cells secrete IL-17, which recruits neutrophils and further loosens the barrier. The candidate antigens are myelin proteins such as myelin basic protein, proteolipid protein, and myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein. Experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis, induced in animals by immunization with myelin antigens and transferred by CD4 T cells, supports this model. Epstein-Barr virus infection is strongly associated with later MS, consistent with mimicry or bystander activation."},{"type":"p","text":"The strongest genetic risk is the HLA-DR2 class II haplotype (HLA-DRB1*15:01, serologically DR15). Class II molecules present peptides to CD4 T cells, which fits a CD4-driven disease. Other risks are female sex (about 2 to 3 times more common), onset between 20 and 40 years, higher latitude, low vitamin D, and smoking."},{"type":"p","text":"B cells and plasma cells that settle in the CNS make IgG from a limited number of clones. CSF electrophoresis therefore shows oligoclonal IgG bands that are absent from paired serum, proving intrathecal synthesis, in more than 90% of patients, with a raised IgG index. The cell count is normal or mildly raised with lymphocytes. Bands are not specific, because CNS infections can also produce them."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammation starts around small veins, so plaques cluster around the periventricular veins and appear on MRI as ovoid lesions perpendicular to the lateral ventricles, as well as in juxtacortical, infratentorial, spinal cord, and optic nerve white matter. Active plaques contain perivenular lymphocytes and lipid-laden macrophages with relatively preserved axons; old plaques are gliotic scars with few oligodendrocytes and progressive axon loss, which drives lasting disability. Demyelinated internodes have few Na+ channels and leak current, so saltatory conduction slows or blocks. Raising body temperature shortens Na+ channel opening, so marginal axons fail and symptoms worsen with heat or exercise (Uhthoff phenomenon)."},{"type":"table","title":"MS lesion sites and their findings","cols":["Lesion site","Finding"],"rows":[["Optic nerve","Optic neuritis: painful monocular vision loss, relative afferent pupillary defect"],["Medial longitudinal fasciculus","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia: weak adduction on the side of the lesion, nystagmus of the abducting eye"],["Cervical dorsal columns","Lhermitte sign (electric sensation down the spine on neck flexion), loss of vibration sense"],["Corticospinal tracts","Spastic weakness, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign"],["Cerebellum and its connections","Ataxia, intention tremor, scanning dysarthria"]],"caption":"Upper motor neuron and central sensory signs reflect CNS white matter lesions."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a patient with MS have antibody bands in serum that match the CSF bands?","explain":"No. The defining bands come from plasma cells inside the CNS, so they appear in CSF but not in paired serum.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on oligoclonal IgG bands and intrathecal synthesis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01-C03","obj":"NE-18.3","title":"Guillain-Barré syndrome and molecular mimicry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) is an acute immune-mediated polyradiculoneuropathy, an attack on peripheral nerves and spinal nerve roots. The demyelinating form, acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (AIDP), is most common in North America and Europe; axonal forms such as acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN) are more common in Asia and after Campylobacter jejuni infection."},{"type":"p","text":"About two thirds of patients report an infection 1 to 4 weeks earlier, most often C. jejuni enteritis, with cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, and Zika virus also implicated. The outer core of C. jejuni lipooligosaccharide (LOS) carries sialylated sugars structurally identical to the carbohydrate of gangliosides such as GM1 and GD1a. Antibodies made against the bacterium therefore bind gangliosides concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier and paranodes. Bound IgG activates complement, whose membrane attack complex disrupts the nodal axolemma and its Na+ channel clusters (AMAN), or macrophages enter the nerve and strip myelin (AIDP). The LOS structure of the infecting strain helps decide which ganglioside is targeted."},{"type":"flow","title":"Molecular mimicry in Guillain-Barré syndrome","steps":["C. jejuni enteritis exposes lipooligosaccharide","B cells make IgG against ganglioside-like sugars","IgG binds gangliosides at nodes and paranodes","Complement and macrophages damage myelin or axolemma","Conduction slows or blocks in roots and nerves","Ascending weakness and areflexia"],"caption":"The response is appropriate against the bacterium but cross-reacts with peripheral nerve gangliosides."},{"type":"p","text":"Weakness is symmetric, flaccid, and usually ascending, reaching its worst within 4 weeks. Reflexes disappear because both arms of the reflex arc run in peripheral nerves. Cranial nerves (often both facial nerves), the phrenic and intercostal nerves (respiratory failure), and autonomic fibers (arrhythmia, labile blood pressure) can be involved. These are lower motor neuron signs, unlike the upper motor neuron signs of MS. Schwann cells remyelinate, so most patients recover over weeks to months, more slowly in axonal forms. Nerve conduction studies show slowed velocity, prolonged latencies, and conduction block in AIDP, but reduced amplitudes with preserved velocity in AMAN."},{"type":"p","text":"The CSF shows albuminocytologic dissociation. Inflamed nerve roots lie in the subarachnoid space and leak protein, but few leukocytes enter the CSF, so protein rises with fewer than 10 cells/µL; protein may still be normal in the first week."},{"type":"p","text":"In the Miller Fisher variant, about 90% of patients have IgG against ganglioside GQ1b, which is concentrated in the paranodal regions of the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves and in large sensory neurons that carry muscle spindle input. The antibody target explains the triad: ophthalmoplegia, ataxia from loss of proprioceptive input, and areflexia, usually with little limb weakness."},{"type":"table","title":"CSF patterns in immune neurologic disease","cols":["Condition","Cells","Protein","Glucose","Distinctive finding"],"rows":[["Normal adult","0 to 5/µL, mononuclear","About 15 to 45 mg/dL","About 60% of serum","Clear, opening pressure about 10 to 20 cm H2O"],["Bacterial meningitis","Often 1,000 to 5,000/µL, neutrophils","Markedly raised","Low, CSF to serum ratio 0.4 or less","Raised opening pressure, organisms on Gram stain"],["Multiple sclerosis","Normal or mildly raised lymphocytes","Normal or mildly raised","Normal","Oligoclonal IgG bands absent from serum"],["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Usually fewer than 10/µL","Raised","Normal","Albuminocytologic dissociation"]],"caption":"Cells reflect leukocyte entry, protein reflects barrier or root leak, and glucose falls only when transport fails and consumption rises."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01-C04","obj":"NE-18.4","title":"Classifying neuroimmune disease: antibody-mediated versus T cell-mediated","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In type II hypersensitivity, IgG or IgM binds an antigen fixed on a cell surface or in matrix. The antibody then activates complement, opsonizes the target for phagocytes, or changes the function of a receptor by blocking or stimulating it without killing the cell. In type IV hypersensitivity, T cells cause the injury without any need for antibody: CD4 cells by releasing cytokines that activate macrophages, and CD8 cells by direct cytotoxicity."},{"type":"h","text":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"type":"p","text":"About 85% of patients with generalized myasthenia gravis have IgG against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (AChR) on the postsynaptic membrane; most others have antibodies to muscle-specific kinase (MuSK) or LRP4, which cluster the receptor. Anti-AChR IgG acts in three ways: cross-linking speeds internalization and degradation of receptors, complement damages the postsynaptic folds (flattening them and widening the cleft), and some antibodies block the acetylcholine binding site. The end-plate potential (EPP) normally exceeds threshold by a wide safety factor. With fewer receptors the EPP is smaller, and because acetylcholine release normally declines with repeated firing, more junctions fall below threshold as activity continues. Weakness is therefore fatigable and worse late in the day, favoring the extraocular muscles and levator (diplopia, ptosis), then bulbar and proximal limb muscles, with normal sensation and reflexes. An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor briefly improves strength because acetylcholine lingers in the cleft."},{"type":"p","text":"Most AChR-positive patients have a thymic abnormality: lymphoid follicular hyperplasia with germinal centers in about 60 to 70% and thymoma in about 10 to 15%. Thymic myoid cells express AChR, and the thymus is thought to be where tolerance to the receptor breaks down."},{"type":"h","text":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"In Lambert-Eaton syndrome (LEMS), IgG binds presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels at motor and autonomic nerve terminals. Less Ca2+ enters with each action potential, fewer quanta are released, and the EPP is small. Patients have proximal leg weakness, reduced reflexes, and autonomic symptoms such as dry mouth, with little ocular involvement. About half have small cell lung cancer, which expresses the same channel. During brief maximal effort, Ca2+ accumulates in the terminal and release rises, so strength and reflexes improve for a short time."},{"type":"h","text":"T cell-mediated demyelination"},{"type":"p","text":"MS and acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) are classed as type IV. ADEM is usually a single episode of multifocal perivenous demyelination with encephalopathy, most often in a child days to weeks after a viral infection or, rarely, vaccination. All its lesions are the same age, unlike the lesions of different ages in MS. GBS is mainly antibody-mediated, although T cells and macrophages contribute."},{"type":"table","title":"Immune mechanisms in neurologic disease","cols":["Disorder","Target","Effector","Type","Signature finding"],"rows":[["Myasthenia gravis","Postsynaptic AChR","IgG, complement","II","Fatigable ptosis and diplopia; decrement on repetitive stimulation"],["Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Presynaptic P/Q Ca2+ channel","IgG","II","Strength improves briefly with effort; small cell lung cancer"],["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Peripheral nerve gangliosides","IgG, complement, macrophages","Mainly II","Ascending weakness, areflexia, albuminocytologic dissociation"],["Multiple sclerosis","CNS myelin","CD4 Th1 and Th17, macrophages","IV","Relapses; periventricular plaques; oligoclonal bands"],["ADEM","CNS myelin","T cells","IV","Monophasic, postinfectious, lesions of the same age"]],"caption":"Antibody-mediated disorders act at the neuromuscular junction and peripheral nerve; T cell-mediated disorders demyelinate the CNS."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: repetitive nerve stimulation","text":"Stimulating a motor nerve at 3 Hz normally lowers acetylcholine release over the first few shocks, but the safety factor keeps every fiber firing, so the compound muscle action potential (CMAP) is stable. In myasthenia gravis the reduced safety factor means fibers drop out as release declines, and the CMAP decreases progressively (decrement). In LEMS the first CMAP is already small because few quanta are released. After 10 seconds of maximal contraction, or stimulation at 20 to 50 Hz, Ca2+ builds up in the terminal and the CMAP can more than double (increment). A postsynaptic defect gives decrement; a presynaptic Ca2+ defect gives facilitation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01-C05","obj":"NE-18.5","title":"Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome is nervous system injury caused by a tumor at a distance, not by invasion, metastasis, metabolic effects, or treatment. The tumor expresses an onconeural antigen, a protein normally restricted to neurons. The neurons have been largely hidden from the immune system behind the barrier, while the tumor presents the antigen in an immunologically active setting. The response raised against the tumor then crosses into the nervous system. Because this response may hold the tumor in check, the neurologic syndrome often appears months before the cancer is found, and the tumor is frequently small."},{"type":"p","text":"The location of the antigen decides the effector and the outcome. When the antigen is intracellular, as with Hu and Yo, antibodies cannot reach it in living cells; they serve as markers, and CD8 cytotoxic T cells that recognize antigen peptides on MHC class I kill the neurons. Neuron loss is therefore largely permanent. When the antigen sits on the cell surface, as with the NMDA receptor or the Ca2+ channel in LEMS, the antibody itself causes the dysfunction, and recovery is possible when antibody levels fall."},{"type":"p","text":"Anti-Hu antibodies (ANNA-1) recognize RNA-binding proteins in neuronal nuclei and are most often linked to small cell lung cancer. Dorsal root ganglion neurons are especially exposed because their capillaries are more permeable than those of the CNS. Their loss causes sensory neuronopathy: asymmetric loss of all sensory modalities, pain, sensory ataxia, and areflexia. Inflammation of the hippocampus and amygdala causes limbic encephalitis with short-term memory loss, mood and behavior change, and seizures."},{"type":"p","text":"Anti-Yo antibodies (PCA-1) recognize a cytoplasmic protein of Purkinje cells and occur almost entirely in women with ovarian, breast, or other gynecologic cancers. Purkinje cell loss causes subacute cerebellar degeneration: gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, and nystagmus that develop over weeks to months, followed by cerebellar atrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is caused by IgG against the extracellular GluN1 subunit. In young women it is often associated with an ovarian teratoma containing neural tissue that expresses the receptor. The antibody cross-links and internalizes receptors, reducing NMDA receptor current, so the illness resembles the effects of NMDA antagonists such as phencyclidine and ketamine. A viral-like prodrome is followed by psychiatric symptoms (anxiety, psychosis, agitation), seizures, reduced consciousness, orofacial dyskinesias, autonomic instability, and central hypoventilation. CSF often shows lymphocytic pleocytosis and oligoclonal bands, and receptor density can recover once the antibody falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic neurologic paraneoplastic antibodies","cols":["Antibody","Antigen location","Usual tumor","Syndrome","Prognosis"],"rows":[["Anti-Hu (ANNA-1)","Neuronal nucleus (intracellular)","Small cell lung cancer","Sensory neuronopathy, limbic encephalitis, encephalomyelitis","Poor; T cell-mediated neuron loss"],["Anti-Yo (PCA-1)","Purkinje cell cytoplasm (intracellular)","Ovarian, breast, gynecologic","Subacute cerebellar degeneration","Poor; Purkinje cells lost"],["Anti-NMDA receptor","Cell surface (GluN1)","Ovarian teratoma","Psychiatric change, seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability","Often good; antibody effect reversible"],["Anti-P/Q Ca2+ channel","Presynaptic surface","Small cell lung cancer","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Often improves; antibody effect reversible"]],"caption":"Intracellular antigens predict cytotoxic T cell injury and permanent deficits; surface antigens predict antibody-mediated, reversible dysfunction."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman has new psychosis, seizures, and orofacial dyskinesias. Would you expect permanent neuron loss?","explain":"Not usually. Anti-NMDA receptor antibodies target a surface receptor and cause reversible internalization rather than T cell killing of neurons.","check":"Compare with the paragraph contrasting intracellular and surface antigens."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-18-01-C06","obj":"NE-18.6","title":"Conjugate polysaccharide vaccines and polio vaccine immunity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"H. influenzae type b (Hib), S. pneumoniae, and N. meningitidis are encapsulated. The polysaccharide capsule resists phagocytosis and complement deposition, so protection depends on anticapsular antibody that opsonizes the organism for splenic and hepatic phagocytes and, for Neisseria, fixes complement for lysis. Risk peaks in infancy after maternal IgG wanes."},{"type":"p","text":"A plain polysaccharide is a T cell-independent antigen. Its repeating units cross-link many B cell receptors on marginal zone and B-1 B cells, but it contains no protein, so no peptide can be presented on MHC class II to a helper T cell. The response is mainly IgM, short-lived, without germinal centers or significant memory, and repeat doses give no booster effect. Children under about 2 years respond poorly because their splenic marginal zone is immature and their B cells express little CD21 (complement receptor 2)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Linked recognition in a conjugate vaccine","steps":["Polysaccharide is covalently linked to a carrier protein","B cell receptor binds the polysaccharide portion","B cell internalizes and processes the whole conjugate","Carrier peptides are presented on MHC class II","Carrier-specific helper T cell gives CD40L and cytokines","Germinal center: class switch, affinity maturation, memory"],"caption":"The antibody is against the polysaccharide; the T cell help is against the carrier, such as tetanus toxoid or nontoxic diphtheria toxin."},{"type":"p","text":"Conjugate vaccines against Hib, pneumococcus, and meningococcus (serogroups A, C, W, Y) therefore produce high-affinity IgG and memory from infancy and boost on later doses. They also reduce nasopharyngeal carriage, which lowers transmission to unvaccinated people. Serogroup B is the exception: its capsule is polysialic acid identical to that on human neural cell adhesion molecule (NCAM), so it is tolerated as self and poorly immunogenic, and serogroup B vaccines use outer membrane proteins instead."},{"type":"h","text":"Polio: serum IgG versus mucosal IgA"},{"type":"p","text":"Poliovirus spreads by the fecal-oral route, replicates in the oropharynx and intestinal lymphoid tissue, and occasionally reaches the CNS through viremia, where it destroys anterior horn motor neurons. Serum neutralizing IgG blocks the viremic step; intestinal secretory IgA blocks infection and shedding. Mucosal IgA requires antigen delivered at the mucosa, where it is made by lamina propria plasma cells and carried across the epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor. A live virus replicating in the gut provides this; an injected killed virus mostly does not."},{"type":"table","title":"Inactivated (Salk) versus live oral (Sabin) polio vaccine","cols":["Feature","Inactivated (Salk, IPV)","Live attenuated oral (Sabin, OPV)"],"rows":[["Route","Injection","Oral"],["Serum neutralizing IgG","Strong; prevents paralytic disease","Strong"],["Intestinal secretory IgA","Weak; vaccinee can still be infected and shed virus","Strong; blocks gut infection and transmission"],["Spread to contacts","None","Vaccine virus is shed and can immunize contacts"],["Reversion risk","None","Rare vaccine-associated paralytic polio and vaccine-derived strains"],["Immunodeficient host","No live virus, so no infection","Prolonged gut replication; risk of paralytic disease"]],"caption":"Both vaccines protect the vaccinee; only the live oral vaccine reliably interrupts intestinal spread."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a plain pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine fail to give a booster response on a second dose, while a conjugate vaccine does?","explain":"Without protein there is no peptide on MHC class II, no T cell help, no germinal center, and no memory B cells; the carrier in the conjugate supplies that help.","check":"Compare with the linked recognition flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology decide where an immune attack shows itself. The blood-brain barrier keeps antibody and complement low in the CNS, which is why bacteria multiply freely in CSF and why a CNS antibody response such as oligoclonal bands must be made locally. Myelin from oligodendrocytes (MS, ADEM) gives upper motor neuron and central sensory signs, while myelin and nodes from Schwann cells (GBS) give lower motor neuron signs and areflexia. At the neuromuscular junction, the safety factor and the Ca2+ dependence of transmitter release explain why a postsynaptic receptor loss fatigues while a presynaptic Ca2+ channel loss briefly facilitates."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology, biochemistry, and immunology supply the triggers and the effectors. Sialylated sugars link C. jejuni to gangliosides and serogroup B meningococcus to NCAM, and the location of an antigen predicts whether antibody or cytotoxic T cells cause the damage, and therefore whether recovery is possible. The type II and type IV labels are teaching simplifications: MS plaques contain B cells and antibody, and GBS involves T cells and macrophages, so classification names the dominant mechanism, not the only one."}],"summary":["Tight junctions in nonfenestrated endothelium keep cells, complement, and antibody out of the CNS, leaving microglia as the main resident innate defense.","In bacterial meningitis, TLR signaling drives IL-1 and TNF release, neutrophil recruitment, barrier breakdown, vasogenic edema, raised ICP, and falling cerebral perfusion pressure.","MS is CD4 Th1 and Th17 attack on CNS myelin, linked to HLA-DR2, with CSF oligoclonal bands absent from serum and perivenular periventricular plaques.","GBS follows C. jejuni infection when antibodies to lipooligosaccharide cross-react with nerve gangliosides, causing ascending weakness, areflexia, and albuminocytologic dissociation.","Anti-GQ1b antibodies target the ocular motor nerves and proprioceptive neurons, producing the Miller Fisher triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia.","Myasthenia gravis (postsynaptic AChR, thymic hyperplasia or thymoma) and LEMS (presynaptic P/Q Ca2+ channel, small cell lung cancer) are type II; MS and ADEM are type IV.","Paraneoplastic antibodies to intracellular antigens (Hu, Yo) mark cytotoxic T cell injury with permanent deficits; antibodies to surface antigens (NMDA receptor) act directly and are often reversible.","Conjugate vaccines turn a T cell-independent polysaccharide into a T cell-dependent antigen through linked recognition, giving IgG, memory, and protection in infants.","Inactivated polio vaccine gives serum IgG that prevents paralysis; live oral vaccine adds intestinal secretory IgA that blocks gut infection and spread, at a rare risk of reversion."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System; peripheral nerve and neuromuscular junction disorders"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders; immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity; B cell activation and antibody production"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism; neuromuscular transmission"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Haemophilus, Neisseria, and Streptococcus pneumoniae; picornaviruses and polio vaccines"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-19":{"unit":"NE-19","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01","title":"Neurological Pathogens","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the organisms that infect the meninges, brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves: the encapsulated bacteria of meningitis, the neonatal pathogens, the two clostridial neurotoxins, the neurotropic DNA and RNA viruses, the fungi that reach the central nervous system, and the spirochetes and leprosy bacillus. Board questions usually give one decisive clue (a Gram stain, a capsule test, an exposure, a vector, or a toxin effect) and ask you to name the organism or explain the virulence factor behind the finding. The aim here is to connect each clue to the structure or mechanism that produces it, so the answer can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Complement pathways, opsonization, and the membrane attack complex","Humoral versus cell-mediated immunity, including T cell-independent polysaccharide antigens","Synaptic vesicle release at the neuromuscular junction and spinal inhibitory interneurons","Formation of CSF, the blood-brain barrier, and the meninges","Basic microbial classification: Gram stain, acid-fast stain, viral genome type and envelope"],"connections":[{"to":"Immune responses in neurological disease (NE-18)","how":"Conjugate vaccines against the three capsular meningitis bacteria work by turning a T cell-independent polysaccharide into a T cell-dependent antigen."},{"to":"Infections of the CNS and PNS (NE-20-G)","how":"The organisms described here are the causes of the meningitis, encephalitis, abscess, and neuropathy syndromes taught in pathology."},{"to":"Synaptic transmission (NE-13)","how":"Botulinum toxin and tetanospasmin are explained entirely by which SNARE protein they cleave and which neuron they enter."},{"to":"Complement and acquired immunodeficiency (IM-7, IM-15-B)","how":"Terminal complement deficiency, asplenia, and low CD4 counts each predict a specific set of nervous system pathogens."},{"to":"CSF compartments and flow (NE-4, NE-10)","how":"CSF cell type, protein, and glucose distinguish bacterial, viral, and fungal meningitis, and basilar exudates or capsular polysaccharide raise intracranial pressure."}],"terms":[{"term":"Capsule","def":"A loose polysaccharide layer outside the bacterial or fungal cell wall that blocks complement deposition and phagocytosis."},{"term":"Opsonization","def":"Coating of a microbe with antibody or C3b so that phagocyte receptors can recognize and ingest it."},{"term":"Lipooligosaccharide (LOS)","def":"The Neisseria form of endotoxin, made of lipid A and a short core oligosaccharide without the long O antigen chain of lipopolysaccharide."},{"term":"Aseptic meningitis","def":"Meningeal inflammation with a lymphocytic CSF and no organism on routine Gram stain or culture, most often caused by enteroviruses."},{"term":"SNARE proteins","def":"Synaptobrevin on the vesicle and syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the presynaptic membrane, which zip together to fuse the vesicle and release transmitter."},{"term":"Retrograde axonal transport","def":"Dynein-driven movement of cargo from the axon terminal back toward the neuronal cell body, used by rabies virus, herpesviruses, and tetanospasmin."},{"term":"Latency","def":"Persistence of a viral genome inside a host cell without production of infectious virus, with the ability to reactivate later."},{"term":"Dimorphic fungus","def":"A fungus that grows as a mold in the environment and takes a different form, such as a yeast or spherule, in tissue."},{"term":"Spherule","def":"The large, thick-walled tissue form of Coccidioides, filled with endospores that are released when it ruptures."},{"term":"Nontreponemal test","def":"A syphilis screening test (VDRL or RPR) that detects antibody to a cardiolipin-lecithin-cholesterol antigen rather than to Treponema itself."},{"term":"Acid-fast","def":"Able to retain carbolfuchsin dye after an acid-alcohol wash because of a mycolic acid-rich cell wall, as in mycobacteria."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C01","obj":"NE-19.1","title":"The encapsulated meningitis bacteria: S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, and Hib","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three bacteria cause most community-acquired bacterial meningitis after the newborn period, and they share a route: nasopharyngeal colonization, passage across the mucosa, survival in blood, and seeding of the subarachnoid space. Survival in blood depends on a polysaccharide capsule, which limits C3b deposition and masks any bound C3b from phagocyte receptors, so the bacteria escape opsonization. Clearance then needs anticapsular antibody and the spleen, whose macrophages remove poorly opsonized bacteria, so asplenia raises the risk from all three."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is a gram-positive, lancet-shaped diplococcus: alpha-hemolytic, optochin sensitive, bile soluble. Its capsule has about 100 serotypes and swells with type-specific antiserum (the quellung reaction). Pneumolysin damages ependymal and cochlear cells, IgA1 protease cleaves mucosal IgA, and autolysin releases cell wall fragments that drive inflammation. It is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in adults and leaves the most sequelae, including hearing loss."},{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria meningitidis is a gram-negative, kidney-bean-shaped diplococcus, oxidase positive and a fermenter of maltose as well as glucose (gonococcus ferments only glucose). Pili attach it to nasopharyngeal epithelium, and it spreads by respiratory droplets in crowded settings such as dormitories and barracks. Its capsule resists complement, and the serogroup B capsule is polysialic acid resembling host neural cell adhesion molecule, so it is poorly immunogenic. LOS shed in outer membrane blebs activates TLR4, releasing TNF and IL-1 that cause capillary leak, shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation, purpura, and adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome). Because Neisseria is killed mainly by the membrane attack complex, C5 to C9 deficiency predisposes to recurrent neisserial infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) is a small gram-negative coccobacillus needing factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD), so it grows on chocolate agar or as satellite colonies around Staphylococcus aureus. Its polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP) capsule is the main virulence factor, and anti-PRP antibody is protective. Infants respond poorly to plain polysaccharide, so before conjugate vaccines Hib meningitis was concentrated between about 6 months, when maternal IgG wanes, and 5 years (NE-18)."},{"type":"table","title":"The three capsular meningitis bacteria","cols":["Feature","S. pneumoniae","N. meningitidis","H. influenzae type b"],"rows":[["Gram stain and shape","Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci","Gram-negative kidney-bean diplococci","Gram-negative coccobacillus"],["Capsule","Polysaccharide, about 100 serotypes","Polysaccharide, serogroups A, B, C, W, Y","PRP (type b)"],["Other virulence factors","Pneumolysin, IgA1 protease, autolysin","LOS endotoxin, pili, IgA1 protease","IgA1 protease"],["Key lab clues","Alpha-hemolytic, optochin sensitive, bile soluble","Oxidase positive, ferments maltose and glucose","Needs factors X and V"],["Typical host","Adults and older adults, asplenia","Adolescents and young adults, C5 to C9 deficiency","Unvaccinated children under about 5"]],"caption":"All three need a capsule to survive in blood; LOS gives meningococcus its shock and purpura."},{"type":"flow","title":"From nasopharynx to purulent meningitis","steps":["Bacteria colonize nasopharyngeal mucosa","They cross the mucosa into the blood","Capsule blocks opsonization, so bacteremia persists","Bacteria cross the blood-brain barrier into the subarachnoid space","Cell wall products and endotoxin trigger cytokines","Neutrophils enter CSF; protein rises and glucose falls","Cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure"],"caption":"Low CSF glucose reflects consumption by neutrophils and bacteria and impaired glucose transport into inflamed CSF."},{"type":"table","title":"CSF patterns in meningitis","cols":["Finding","Normal","Bacterial","Viral","Fungal or tuberculous"],"rows":[["Opening pressure","10 to 20 cm H2O","Raised","Normal or slightly raised","Raised, often very high"],["White cells","0 to 5/microliter","Hundreds to thousands, neutrophils","Tens to hundreds, lymphocytes","Tens to hundreds, lymphocytes"],["Protein","15 to 45 mg/dL","Markedly raised","Normal or slightly raised","Raised"],["Glucose","About 60% of plasma","Low","Normal","Low"]],"caption":"Viral meningitis can show neutrophils on day one; glucose discriminates best."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One patient has had a splenectomy and another has an inherited C7 deficiency. Which meningitis organisms threaten each?","explain":"The asplenic patient cannot clear poorly opsonized encapsulated bacteria and is at risk from all three; the C7-deficient patient cannot form the membrane attack complex and is at particular risk from Neisseria.","check":"Compare with the first and third paragraphs of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C02","obj":"NE-19.2","title":"Neonatal meningitis: group B Streptococcus, E. coli K1, and Listeria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Newborns meet a different set of organisms because their defenses are immature. Complement levels are low, neutrophil reserves and chemotaxis are limited, and antibody consists almost entirely of maternal IgG carried across the placenta, mostly in the third trimester, so preterm infants receive less. The organisms that exploit this come from the mother: from the birth canal and perineum, or across the placenta."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae (group B Streptococcus, GBS) is a gram-positive coccus in chains that is beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, CAMP test positive, and hippurate positive. It colonizes the vagina and rectum of a sizable minority of pregnant women. Its capsule is rich in sialic acid, which recruits the host regulator factor H and inactivates C3b on the bacterial surface, so the alternative pathway fails. An infant is protected only if the mother has transferred anticapsular IgG. Early-onset disease, in the first week and often the first day, follows aspiration of infected amniotic fluid or vaginal secretions and presents as sepsis or pneumonia; late-onset disease, from about 1 week to 3 months, more often presents as meningitis. GBS is the most common cause of neonatal meningitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Escherichia coli carrying the K1 capsule is the second most common cause and is especially important in preterm infants. The K1 capsule is the same polysialic acid found on meningococcus serogroup B and on host neural cell adhesion molecule, so it is poorly immunogenic and resists complement. A high-grade bacteremia is followed by invasion of brain microvascular endothelial cells, which carries the organism into the CSF."},{"type":"p","text":"Listeria monocytogenes is a gram-positive rod (sometimes mistaken for a diphtheroid contaminant) that is catalase positive, narrowly beta-hemolytic, and shows tumbling motility at room temperature. It grows at refrigerator temperature, about 4 °C (39 °F), so cold storage enriches it in deli meats, soft cheeses, and unpasteurized milk. It is a facultative intracellular organism that spends its life inside host cells, out of reach of antibody. Clearance therefore needs cell-mediated immunity (Th1 cells, interferon gamma-activated macrophages, and CD8 T cells), which is why neonates, pregnant women, older adults, and people on corticosteroids or transplant immunosuppression are at risk. Internalin binds E-cadherin, which helps Listeria cross the intestinal epithelium and the placenta, leading to amnionitis, fetal loss, or disseminated granulomas in the newborn (granulomatosis infantiseptica). In adults it can cause rhombencephalitis, an infection of the brainstem."},{"type":"flow","title":"Intracellular life cycle of Listeria","steps":["Internalin binds E-cadherin and triggers uptake","Listeriolysin O lyses the phagosome membrane","Bacteria multiply free in the cytosol","ActA recruits host Arp2/3 to build actin tails","Actin tails push bacteria into protrusions entering the neighbor cell","Bacteria escape the double-membrane vacuole and repeat"],"caption":"Cell-to-cell spread never exposes the organism to extracellular antibody or complement."},{"type":"table","title":"Neonatal meningitis pathogens","cols":["Organism","Stain and lab clues","Key virulence factor","Source"],"rows":[["Group B Streptococcus","Gram-positive cocci in chains; beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, CAMP positive","Sialic acid-rich capsule that recruits factor H","Maternal vaginal and rectal colonization"],["E. coli K1","Gram-negative rod, lactose fermenter","K1 polysialic acid capsule","Maternal gut flora at delivery"],["Listeria monocytogenes","Gram-positive rod; tumbling motility, grows at 4 °C (39 °F)","Internalin, listeriolysin O, ActA","Transplacental or at birth; mother ate deli meat, soft cheese, or unpasteurized dairy"]],"caption":"Gram-positive cocci in chains point to GBS, gram-negative rods to E. coli, and gram-positive rods to Listeria."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C03","obj":"NE-19.3","title":"Botulinum toxin and tetanospasmin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Transmitter release requires the SNARE proteins. Synaptobrevin on the vesicle zips with syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the plasma membrane to dock it, and when Ca2+ enters through voltage-gated channels and binds synaptotagmin, the vesicle fuses and releases its contents. Botulinum toxin and tetanospasmin are closely related AB toxins: the heavy chain binds gangliosides and protein receptors on the nerve terminal and mediates entry, and the light chain is a zinc endopeptidase that cleaves a SNARE protein. Their opposite clinical pictures come from which neuron they end up in, not from a different molecular action. Binding is irreversible, so function returns only as new terminals and SNAREs are made over weeks to months."},{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium botulinum is an anaerobic, spore-forming gram-positive rod. Of its serotypes (A to G), A, B, and E cause most human disease; A and E cleave SNAP-25 and B cleaves synaptobrevin. Toxin absorbed into the blood acts at peripheral cholinergic terminals: the neuromuscular junction, autonomic ganglia, and parasympathetic endings. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so consciousness and sensation are preserved. Weakness is symmetric and descending, beginning with cranial nerve muscles (diplopia, ptosis, dysarthria, dysphagia) and spreading to the limbs and diaphragm, without fever. Cholinergic autonomic failure adds dilated, poorly reactive pupils, dry mouth, constipation, and urinary retention. Adults usually ingest preformed toxin in home-canned low-acid food; the toxin is heat labile but the spores are heat resistant. In infant botulism, spores (classically from honey) germinate in a gut that lacks mature flora and make toxin there, giving constipation and a floppy baby. Wound botulism follows contaminated wounds, including injection drug use."},{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium tetani is an anaerobic gram-positive rod with a terminal spore. Spores in soil enter puncture or necrotic wounds, where low oxygen lets them germinate. Tetanospasmin enters motor nerve terminals but is sorted into retrograde transport, carried to motor neuron cell bodies in the spinal cord and brainstem, and passed across the synapse into the inhibitory interneurons that synapse on them, including Renshaw cells. There it cleaves synaptobrevin, stopping release of glycine and GABA. Motor neurons lose their brake and reciprocal inhibition fails, so agonist and antagonist muscles contract together: trismus (lockjaw), risus sardonicus, opisthotonos as the stronger back extensors win, and painful generalized spasms triggered by stimuli, with the patient awake. Loss of inhibition of sympathetic neurons causes autonomic instability. Neonatal tetanus follows infection of the umbilical stump."},{"type":"flow","title":"Tetanus from wound to spasm","steps":["Spores germinate in an anaerobic wound","Tetanospasmin enters motor nerve terminals","Retrograde transport carries toxin to the spinal cord","Toxin crosses into inhibitory interneurons","Light chain cleaves synaptobrevin","Glycine and GABA release stops","Motor neurons fire unchecked: spastic paralysis"],"caption":"The same SNARE cleavage that paralyzes a muscle in botulism causes spasm here because it silences the inhibitor."},{"type":"table","title":"Botulinum toxin versus tetanospasmin","cols":["Feature","Botulinum toxin","Tetanospasmin"],"rows":[["Usual source","Preformed toxin in food; spores in infant gut or wounds","Spores in a contaminated wound"],["Site of action","Peripheral cholinergic terminals","Spinal and brainstem inhibitory interneurons, reached by retrograde transport"],["SNARE cleaved","SNAP-25 (A, E) or synaptobrevin (B)","Synaptobrevin"],["Transmitter blocked","Acetylcholine","Glycine and GABA"],["Paralysis","Flaccid, symmetric, descending from cranial nerves","Spastic: trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonos"],["Autonomic signs","Dilated pupils, dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention","Sympathetic overactivity: tachycardia, labile blood pressure, sweating"]],"caption":"Same enzyme class, opposite effects, determined by the neuron that takes up the toxin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two alert patients have difficulty swallowing. One has ptosis, dilated unreactive pupils, and weakness spreading down to the arms; the other has a clenched jaw, an arched back, and painful spasms. Which toxin is in each?","explain":"Dilated pupils with descending flaccid weakness means acetylcholine release has failed at peripheral cholinergic terminals; trismus and opisthotonos mean inhibitory interneurons in the cord have been silenced.","check":"Compare the site of action row in the toxin table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C04","obj":"NE-19.4","title":"DNA viruses of the nervous system: herpesviruses and JC polyomavirus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both groups of DNA viruses that matter in the nervous system persist for life after the first infection, and both cause neurological disease mainly when they come out of latency. They differ in where they hide and in what they damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Herpesviruses are enveloped, linear double-stranded DNA viruses that replicate in the nucleus and take their envelope from nuclear and Golgi membranes. Herpes simplex virus (HSV) and varicella zoster virus (VZV) enter at epithelial surfaces, infect the sensory nerve endings beneath, and travel by retrograde axonal transport to the sensory ganglion. There the genome circularizes and persists as an episome in the neuronal nucleus, with transcription limited mainly to latency-associated transcripts and no infectious virus made, so the neuron displays little viral antigen to cytotoxic T cells. Reactivation, provoked by fever, ultraviolet light, stress, or falling cell-mediated immunity, sends virus by anterograde transport back down the axon to the skin or mucosa supplied by that neuron, which is why lesions recur in the same place and, for VZV, in a single dermatome."},{"type":"p","text":"HSV-1 is latent in the trigeminal ganglion and is the leading cause of sporadic fatal encephalitis, with a characteristic preference for the medial temporal and orbitofrontal lobes. The result is a hemorrhagic necrotizing encephalitis that produces fever, personality change, aphasia, olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, and temporal lobe seizures, with red cells and raised protein in a lymphocytic CSF; nuclear Cowdry A inclusions may be seen. HSV-2 is latent in sacral ganglia, causes recurrent genital lesions and benign recurrent lymphocytic meningitis, and causes severe disseminated or meningoencephalitic disease in neonates infected during vaginal delivery. VZV causes chickenpox on first infection, then persists in dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia; reactivation gives dermatomal zoster, and because inflammation damages the ganglion and dorsal root it can leave postherpetic neuralgia. Reactivation in the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve threatens the eye, and in the geniculate ganglion it causes facial palsy with vesicles in the ear canal (Ramsay Hunt syndrome). VZV can also cause a granulomatous vasculitis with stroke."},{"type":"p","text":"JC polyomavirus is a small nonenveloped virus with a circular double-stranded DNA genome. Most adults are infected silently in childhood and carry it latently in kidney tubular epithelium and lymphoid tissue. When cellular immunity fails, as in untreated HIV with a low CD4 count, hematologic malignancy, or therapy that blocks lymphocyte trafficking into the brain, the virus reaches the brain and infects oligodendrocytes. Because each oligodendrocyte myelinates many axons, lysis of these cells produces progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML): patchy, asymmetric demyelination of subcortical white matter with enlarged oligodendrocyte nuclei containing inclusions, bizarre reactive astrocytes, and little inflammation. Clinically it gives subacute, stepwise focal deficits such as hemiparesis, visual field loss, ataxia, and cognitive decline, with nonenhancing white matter lesions that spare the cortex."},{"type":"table","title":"DNA viruses of the nervous system","cols":["Virus","Structure","Site of latency","Cell damaged and disease"],"rows":[["HSV-1","Enveloped dsDNA","Trigeminal ganglion","Neurons of medial temporal and orbitofrontal lobes: necrotizing encephalitis"],["HSV-2","Enveloped dsDNA","Sacral ganglia","Meninges (recurrent lymphocytic meningitis); neonatal meningoencephalitis"],["VZV","Enveloped dsDNA","Dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia","Sensory ganglion and nerve: dermatomal zoster, postherpetic neuralgia, vasculitis"],["JC polyomavirus","Nonenveloped circular dsDNA","Kidney tubular epithelium and lymphoid tissue","Oligodendrocytes: progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"]],"caption":"Herpesviruses are latent in neurons and travel along axons; JC virus is latent outside the nervous system and destroys myelinating glia."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Herpesvirus latency and reactivation","steps":["Epithelial infection and local replication","Entry into sensory nerve endings","Retrograde transport to the sensory ganglion","Latent episome, only latency transcripts made","Trigger lowers cell-mediated immunity or stresses the neuron","Reactivation and anterograde transport down the axon","Lesions recur in the same site; ganglion latency persists"],"caption":"Latency in a long-lived neuron with no antigen display explains lifelong carriage and recurrence in a fixed territory."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C05","obj":"NE-19.5","title":"RNA viruses of the nervous system: enteroviruses, West Nile virus, and rabies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"RNA viruses reach the nervous system by one of two routes. Most arrive through the blood after replicating elsewhere, so they need a viremia high enough and long enough to cross the blood-brain barrier or choroid plexus. Rabies is the exception, travelling inside peripheral nerves instead."},{"type":"p","text":"Enteroviruses (poliovirus, coxsackieviruses A and B, echoviruses, and enterovirus 71) are nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA picornaviruses. Lacking an envelope, they tolerate stomach acid, bile, and detergents, which allows fecal-oral spread and makes them the most common cause of aseptic meningitis, typically in summer and early autumn. They replicate in pharyngeal and intestinal lymphoid tissue, then spread through the blood to the meninges, causing a self-limited lymphocytic meningitis. Poliovirus is tropic for anterior horn motor neurons and brainstem motor nuclei; destroying those cell bodies gives asymmetric flaccid paralysis with areflexia, wasting, and no sensory loss, because only the lower motor neuron is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"West Nile virus is an enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA flavivirus maintained between birds and Culex mosquitoes; humans and horses are dead-end hosts whose viremia is too low to infect a feeding mosquito. Most infections are silent or febrile only; neuroinvasive disease is far more likely in older adults and the immunosuppressed, because control depends on early interferon and antibody responses. It causes meningitis, encephalitis with tremor and parkinsonian features, and a poliomyelitis-like asymmetric flaccid paralysis from infection of anterior horn cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Rabies virus is an enveloped, bullet-shaped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA rhabdovirus, so it carries its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. US reservoirs are bats, raccoons, skunks, and foxes; worldwide, dog bites dominate. It replicates in muscle, binds receptors at the neuromuscular junction including the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, then moves by dynein-driven retrograde transport to the dorsal root ganglion, cord, and brain. Because transport is slow and the virus stays shielded inside the axon, incubation is long and variable, typically one to three months and shorter for bites on the face and neck than on a distal limb, and little antigen is seen until the virus is central. Encephalitis follows, with agitation, hydrophobia, autonomic storms, then paralysis, coma, and death; about one fifth of cases take a paralytic form. Eosinophilic cytoplasmic Negri bodies appear in cerebellar Purkinje cells and hippocampal neurons. Centrifugal spread along nerves then puts virus in saliva."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rabies from bite to brain","steps":["Virus replicates in muscle at the bite site","Binds receptors at the neuromuscular junction","Retrograde dynein transport up the peripheral axon","Reaches dorsal root ganglion, cord, and brain","Neuronal infection: encephalitis with Negri bodies","Centrifugal spread along nerves to salivary glands"],"caption":"Travel inside the axon explains both the long incubation and the poor immune response before symptoms begin."},{"type":"table","title":"RNA viruses of the nervous system","cols":["Virus","Genome and envelope","Route to the nervous system","Disease"],"rows":[["Enteroviruses","Positive-sense ssRNA, nonenveloped","Fecal-oral, then viremia","Aseptic meningitis; polio destroys anterior horn cells"],["West Nile virus","Positive-sense ssRNA, enveloped","Culex mosquito bite, then viremia","Meningitis, encephalitis with tremor, flaccid paralysis"],["Rabies virus","Negative-sense ssRNA, enveloped, bullet-shaped","Retrograde axonal transport from a bite","Encephalitis with hydrophobia; Negri bodies"]],"caption":"Nonenveloped structure allows fecal-oral spread; negative-sense genome requires a virion polymerase."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two causes of flaccid paralysis","text":"A previously well child develops fever, then asymmetric weakness of one leg with absent reflexes, wasting, and normal sensation. A 78-year-old man in late summer develops fever, confusion, tremor, and asymmetric arm weakness with areflexia. Both pictures localize to the anterior horn, because loss of the lower motor neuron gives flaccid weakness, areflexia, and atrophy without sensory loss. Age, season, and mosquito exposure favor West Nile virus in the older man, while an unimmunized child with fecal-oral exposure favors poliovirus. Botulinum toxin would also give flaccid weakness, but it is symmetric, descending, and accompanied by dilated pupils rather than fever and asymmetry."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C06","obj":"NE-19.6","title":"Fungi that reach the nervous system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fungi reach the nervous system through the blood after inhalation or by direct invasion from the sinuses. Which fungus causes disease depends less on exposure, which is common, than on which host defense has failed: neutrophils handle hyphae, so neutropenia favors invasive molds, while T cells and macrophages contain yeasts and spherules, so a low CD4 count favors Cryptococcus."},{"type":"p","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans is a narrow-based budding yeast with a thick polysaccharide capsule of glucuronoxylomannan. It lives in soil enriched with pigeon droppings. Inhaled yeast reaches the alveoli, where macrophages and CD4 T cells normally contain it; when CD4 counts fall below about 100 cells per microliter it disseminates, with marked tropism for the central nervous system, where little complement or antibody is present to oppose the capsule. The capsule blocks phagocytosis, sheds soluble antigen, and obstructs CSF outflow so pressure rises. The result is a subacute meningoencephalitis with headache, fever, and little neck stiffness, because the capsule provokes weak inflammation, plus cranial nerve palsies and visual loss from high pressure. India ink shows a clear halo because the capsule excludes the ink, and the capsular antigen test on CSF or serum is the most sensitive marker. Gelatinous pseudocysts along perivascular spaces in the basal ganglia give the soap-bubble appearance."},{"type":"p","text":"Mucor and Rhizopus, in the order Mucorales, grow as broad, ribbon-like hyphae that are nonseptate (or sparsely septate) and branch at wide, nearly right angles. They are angioinvasive: they penetrate arterial walls, causing thrombosis and bloodless necrosis of the tissue downstream. Diabetic ketoacidosis is the classic setting because acidemia releases iron from transferrin, giving the fungus free iron that its siderophores capture, while hyperglycemia and ketones support growth and impair neutrophil function. Infection begins in the nose and paranasal sinuses and extends into the orbit, then through the cribriform plate or orbital apex into the frontal lobe and cavernous sinus, giving facial pain, orbital swelling, proptosis, cranial nerve palsies, and a black necrotic eschar on the palate or turbinate. Iron overload, neutropenia, and transplant immunosuppression are the other settings. Aspergillus, by contrast, has narrow septate hyphae branching at acute angles and usually arrives from the lung."},{"type":"p","text":"Coccidioides is a dimorphic fungus of dry soil in the southwestern United States, Mexico, and parts of Latin America. Soil disruption by wind or construction aerosolizes arthroconidia, which are inhaled and convert in the lung into thick-walled spherules packed with endospores; when a spherule ruptures, each endospore forms a new spherule, so one inhaled particle multiplies locally. Most infections are a self-limited pneumonia (Valley fever) with erythema nodosum and eosinophilia. Dissemination to skin, bone, and meninges is far more likely with defective cellular immunity, and also in pregnancy. Coccidioidal meningitis is chronic and basilar: granulomatous exudate at the base of the brain obstructs CSF flow, causing hydrocephalus and cranial nerve palsies, with lymphocytic CSF, low glucose, and high protein."},{"type":"table","title":"Fungi of the nervous system","cols":["Fungus","Tissue form","Setting and host defect","Route and disease"],"rows":[["Cryptococcus neoformans","Encapsulated narrow-based budding yeast","Soil with pigeon droppings; low CD4 count","Inhaled, then blood to meninges: subacute meningoencephalitis with high pressure"],["Mucor and Rhizopus","Broad nonseptate hyphae, wide-angle branching","Ketoacidosis, iron overload, neutropenia","Sinus to orbit to brain: angioinvasion, thrombosis, necrosis"],["Coccidioides","Spherule filled with endospores","Southwestern US desert soil; weak cellular immunity","Inhaled arthroconidia, then blood: chronic basilar meningitis with hydrocephalus"]],"caption":"Each diagnostic clue follows from the tissue form: a capsule that excludes ink, hyphae that cross vessel walls, a spherule that multiplies in place."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does cryptococcal meningitis cause severe headache and visual loss with only mild neck stiffness, while coccidioidal meningitis causes hydrocephalus?","explain":"The cryptococcal capsule is poorly inflammatory but obstructs CSF outflow mechanically, so pressure rises without much meningeal irritation; Coccidioides provokes a granulomatous basilar exudate that blocks CSF pathways and traps cranial nerves.","check":"Compare the second and fourth paragraphs of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-19-01-C07","obj":"NE-19.7","title":"Spirochetes and the leprosy bacillus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"These three organisms damage nerves slowly, over weeks to years, and all are hard to see or to grow."},{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum is a thin, tightly coiled spirochete about 0.2 micrometers wide, below the resolution of ordinary light microscopy, so it is found by darkfield examination of fresh exudate, by silver or immunofluorescent stain in tissue, or by serology; it cannot be grown on artificial media. Its outer membrane has very few surface proteins, giving antibody few targets. It spreads through blood and lymph within hours, so even the primary chancre is systemic disease, and it enters the nervous system early."},{"type":"p","text":"Serology uses two classes of test. Nontreponemal tests (VDRL and RPR) detect antibody to a cardiolipin antigen released from damaged host cells; titers follow disease activity, and biologic false positives occur in pregnancy, lupus, antiphospholipid syndrome, and viral infections. Treponemal tests (FTA-ABS and newer immunoassays) detect antibody to the organism and stay positive for life."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurosyphilis takes several forms: early meningitis, meningovascular syphilis with endarteritis and stroke, and late parenchymal disease. Tabes dorsalis is degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots, with lightning pains, sensory ataxia, areflexia, and loss of position and vibration sense; general paresis is cortical neuronal loss with dementia and personality change. The Argyll Robertson pupil accommodates but does not react to light."},{"type":"p","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi is a larger, loosely coiled spirochete visible with Giemsa stain, transmitted by Ixodes ticks; nymphs feed on white-footed mice, the reservoir, and adults on deer, which sustain the tick population but are not a reservoir for the bacterium. Transmission usually needs prolonged attachment. It spreads outward in the skin as erythema migrans, then disseminates, varying its surface lipoproteins and binding host complement regulators so it persists while antibody accumulates. Neurologic disease, often weeks after the bite, combines lymphocytic meningitis, cranial neuropathy (most often facial palsy), and painful radiculoneuritis; carditis with AV block belongs to the same stage, and late disease brings arthritis. Antibody takes weeks to appear, so early serology can be negative."},{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium leprae is an acid-fast bacillus that cannot be cultured in vitro, grown only in armadillos or mouse footpads; wild armadillos are a US reservoir. It grows best at about 30 °C (86 °F), below core temperature, so it colonizes cooler tissue: skin, nasal mucosa, anterior eye, and superficial nerves. It is an obligate intracellular organism that binds laminin-2 in the Schwann cell basal lamina, and infection plus the immune response destroys the nerve. Because damage follows superficial nerve trunks, sensory loss is patchy or follows a thickened palpable nerve rather than a dermatome, and loss of pain leads to unnoticed injury and loss of digits. The T cell response sets the form: strong Th1 gives tuberculoid leprosy with few organisms, granulomas, and a few sharply bordered anesthetic patches, while weak Th1 with dominant Th2 gives lepromatous leprosy with abundant bacilli in foamy macrophages, diffuse symmetric skin thickening, and widespread nerve involvement."},{"type":"table","title":"Slow nerve pathogens compared","cols":["Feature","Treponema pallidum","Borrelia burgdorferi","Mycobacterium leprae"],"rows":[["Organism","Very thin spirochete, unseen on Gram stain","Larger spirochete, Giemsa visible","Acid-fast bacillus"],["Transmission","Sexual contact, transplacental","Ixodes tick bite","Prolonged close contact; armadillo reservoir in the US"],["Detection","Darkfield; VDRL or RPR then FTA-ABS","Two-step serology, delayed weeks","Acid-fast bacilli in skin or nerve biopsy"],["Nerve damage","Meningitis, stroke, tabes dorsalis, general paresis","Meningitis, facial palsy, radiculoneuritis","Schwann cells of cool superficial nerves"]],"caption":"All three injure nerves through chronic immune-mediated inflammation, not an exotoxin."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why tabes dorsalis gives sensory ataxia","steps":["Treponemes invade meninges and dorsal roots","Chronic inflammation destroys dorsal root and dorsal column fibers","Position and vibration sense lost","Reflex afferents lost, so reflexes disappear","Balance now depends on vision: positive Romberg sign"],"caption":"The deficit maps onto the fiber population destroyed; pain and temperature are relatively spared."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and immunology together predict which organism a given patient will get. The capsule is the central theme of extracellular bacterial and cryptococcal disease, so the defenses that handle capsules (anticapsular antibody, complement, and the spleen) define the risk groups: asplenia for all three capsular meningitis bacteria, terminal complement deficiency for Neisseria, and infancy for any plain polysaccharide, which is why conjugation to a protein carrier matters (NE-18). Intracellular organisms invert the logic: Listeria, Mycobacterium leprae, JC virus, Cryptococcus, and Coccidioides become dangerous when T cells and macrophages fail, which is why pregnancy, corticosteroids, transplant immunosuppression, and a low CD4 count select for them. Route explains the rest. Organisms that travel inside axons (rabies virus, herpesviruses, and tetanospasmin) bypass antibody entirely, which gives rabies its long silent incubation and herpesviruses their lifelong latency, while organisms that depend on viremia are limited by interferon and antibody responses that weaken with age."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and physiology explain the findings used to identify these infections. Neutrophil influx with bacterial cell wall products gives the high-protein, low-glucose, neutrophilic CSF of pyogenic meningitis, while lymphocytic CSF with normal glucose points to a virus, and lymphocytic CSF with low glucose points to a fungus or mycobacterium. Mechanical and vascular consequences follow structure: the cryptococcal capsule and the coccidioidal basilar exudate obstruct CSF outflow and raise pressure, Mucorales hyphae invade arteries and cause ischemic necrosis, and treponemal endarteritis causes stroke in a young adult. There are limits to this reasoning. Clinical pictures overlap widely, several organisms cause the same syndrome in the same host, and identification rests on stain, antigen, serology, or nucleic acid testing rather than on pattern alone; serologic tests also carry their own false positives and false negatives, which is why nontreponemal and treponemal syphilis tests are interpreted together."}],"summary":["The capsule is the shared virulence factor of S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, Hib, group B Streptococcus, E. coli K1, and Cryptococcus: it blocks opsonization and phagocytosis.","Meningococcal LOS drives shock, DIC, purpura, and adrenal hemorrhage; terminal complement deficiency predisposes specifically to Neisseria because killing needs the membrane attack complex.","In neonatal CSF, gram-positive cocci in chains mean group B Streptococcus, gram-negative rods mean E. coli K1, and gram-positive rods mean Listeria.","Listeria lives in the cytosol and moves cell to cell on actin tails, so clearance requires cell-mediated immunity, which explains the risk in pregnancy, infancy, age, and immunosuppression.","Botulinum toxin and tetanospasmin are both zinc proteases that cleave SNARE proteins; botulinum blocks acetylcholine peripherally for flaccid descending weakness, while tetanospasmin is carried retrograde to block glycine and GABA for spastic paralysis.","Herpesviruses are latent in sensory ganglia and move along axons in both directions; JC virus is latent in the kidney and lyses oligodendrocytes to produce PML.","Enteroviruses are nonenveloped and spread fecal-oral as the leading cause of aseptic meningitis, and poliovirus and West Nile virus both destroy anterior horn cells to give asymmetric flaccid paralysis without sensory loss.","Rabies travels retrograde inside axons, so incubation is long and depends on the distance from bite to brain, and Negri bodies appear in Purkinje and hippocampal neurons.","Cryptococcus gives high-pressure meningitis detected by capsular antigen and an India ink halo; Mucorales invade vessels from the sinus in ketoacidosis because acidemia frees iron; Coccidioides forms spherules and a chronic basilar meningitis with hydrocephalus.","Nontreponemal titers follow syphilis activity but give false positives, treponemal tests stay positive for life, Borrelia causes meningitis with facial palsy and radiculoneuritis, and M. leprae infects Schwann cells in cool superficial nerves."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Neisseriae; Haemophilus; Streptococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spore-forming gram-positive bacilli (Clostridium); Spirochetes and other spiral microorganisms; Mycobacteria"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses; Picornaviruses; Arthropod-borne and rodent-borne viral diseases; Rabies and slow virus infections"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Mycology"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, infections of the nervous system"}]},"NE-2":{"unit":"NE-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01","title":"Neuron and Nerve Histology","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic organization of nervous tissue: the parts and shapes of neurons, the connective tissue wrappings of a peripheral nerve, the glial cells that myelinate, support, defend, and line the nervous system, and the way neurons and axons respond to injury. Board questions usually show a cell or a lesion and ask you to name the cell involved, the disease that targets it, or what happens after an axon is cut or a neuron loses its oxygen supply. Knowing why the axon hillock lacks Nissl substance, why one oligodendrocyte matters more than one Schwann cell, and why peripheral axons regrow answers most questions here.","prerequisites":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum, ribosomes, and the cytoskeleton (microtubules, motor proteins)","Resting membrane potential and the Na+/K+-ATPase (IN-3)","Formation of the neural tube and neural crest (NE-1)","Basic tissue types: epithelium, loose and dense connective tissue, basal lamina","Reversible versus irreversible cell injury and patterns of necrosis (IN-8)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neural tube embryology (NE-1)","how":"Neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma come from the neural tube, while Schwann cells and sensory ganglion neurons come from neural crest."},{"to":"Synaptic transmission and action potentials (NE-13)","how":"Myelin, nodes of Ranvier, and the axon initial segment explain where action potentials start and how fast they travel."},{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Multiple sclerosis and the leukodystrophies are diseases of oligodendrocytes and CNS myelin described here at the cell level."},{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Red neurons, liquefactive necrosis, and gliosis are the tissue stages of ischemic stroke and global hypoxia."},{"to":"PNS trauma and compression (NE-20-I)","how":"Wallerian degeneration, Schwann cell tubes, and the nerve sheaths decide whether a crushed or cut nerve recovers."}],"terms":[{"term":"Nissl substance","def":"Clumps of rough endoplasmic reticulum and free ribosomes in the neuronal soma and proximal dendrites that stain blue with basic dyes."},{"term":"Axon initial segment","def":"The first part of the axon beyond the hillock, packed with voltage-gated Na+ channels, where action potentials normally begin."},{"term":"Pseudounipolar neuron","def":"A sensory neuron with one process that divides into a peripheral and a central branch, found in dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia."},{"term":"Perineurium","def":"Layers of flattened epithelioid cells joined by tight junctions that surround each fascicle and form the main diffusion barrier of a nerve."},{"term":"Internode","def":"The myelinated segment of an axon between two nodes of Ranvier."},{"term":"Saltatory conduction","def":"Propagation of an action potential from node to node along a myelinated axon."},{"term":"Reactive gliosis","def":"Hypertrophy and proliferation of astrocytes with increased GFAP after CNS injury; the CNS equivalent of a scar."},{"term":"Microglia","def":"Resident phagocytic and antigen-presenting cells of the CNS, of myeloid rather than neuroectodermal origin."},{"term":"Wallerian degeneration","def":"Breakdown of the axon and its myelin distal to a transection, followed by clearance of the debris."},{"term":"Central chromatolysis","def":"Reaction of the cell body to axonal injury: swelling, dispersal of Nissl substance, and displacement of the nucleus to the periphery."},{"term":"Bands of Büngner","def":"Columns of proliferating Schwann cells inside the old basal lamina tubes that guide regenerating peripheral axons."},{"term":"Red neuron","def":"A shrunken neuron with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm and a pyknotic nucleus, the light-microscopic sign of irreversible hypoxic or ischemic injury."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C01","obj":"NE-2.1","title":"Parts of a neuron and the three neuron shapes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A neuron is built to receive signals, decide whether to fire, and send the result over a long distance. The large lower motor neuron of the spinal cord anterior horn shows every part clearly, so it is the standard example. Its soma (cell body) contains a large, pale, euchromatic nucleus with a prominent nucleolus, a sign of very active protein synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"The soma cytoplasm is filled with Nissl substance, which is stacked rough endoplasmic reticulum and free polyribosomes. Ribosomal RNA binds basic dyes such as cresyl violet, so Nissl substance appears as dark blue clumps. It extends into the proximal dendrites but is absent from the axon hillock and the axon, so the hillock looks pale on a Nissl stain. This tells you the axon makes little protein of its own; it depends on axonal transport, with kinesin carrying cargo toward the terminal (anterograde) and dynein carrying cargo back to the soma (retrograde). Retrograde transport is also the route by which tetanus toxin, rabies virus, and herpes simplex virus reach the cell body."},{"type":"table","title":"Parts of a multipolar motor neuron","cols":["Part","Structure","Function"],"rows":[["Soma","Nucleus with prominent nucleolus; Nissl substance; Golgi; lipofuscin with age","Makes nearly all neuronal proteins; integrates input"],["Dendrites","Many tapering branches; contain Nissl substance; may bear spines","Receive synaptic input"],["Axon hillock","Cone where the axon leaves the soma; no Nissl substance","Transition zone to the axon"],["Axon initial segment","Highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels; lowest threshold","Site where an action potential starts"],["Axon","Single, uniform diameter; microtubules and neurofilaments; no ribosomes","Conducts the action potential; relies on transport"],["Synaptic terminals","Boutons with vesicles and mitochondria","Release neurotransmitter onto the target"]],"caption":"The presence or absence of Nissl substance separates dendrites from the axon on a stained section."},{"type":"p","text":"Synaptic input on dendrites and soma spreads passively, and the neuron fires only if the summed depolarization reaches threshold at the initial segment."},{"type":"h","text":"Neuron shapes"},{"type":"p","text":"Neurons are classified by the number of processes that leave the soma. Multipolar neurons have one axon and many dendrites and make up motor neurons, autonomic ganglion neurons, and most CNS neurons. Pseudounipolar neurons begin as bipolar cells in development; the two processes fuse near the soma, leaving one stem that divides in a T into a peripheral branch running to a receptor and a central branch entering the spinal cord or brainstem. Both branches are structurally axons, so the impulse can pass from one to the other without crossing the soma. Their round somas sit in dorsal root ganglia and cranial sensory ganglia, each surrounded by a ring of satellite cells. Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon at opposite poles and are found in special sensory pathways."},{"type":"table","title":"Neuron shapes and where they are found","cols":["Type","Processes","Locations","Note"],"rows":[["Multipolar","One axon, many dendrites","Anterior horn motor neurons, autonomic ganglia, most CNS neurons","Most common type"],["Pseudounipolar","One stem dividing into peripheral and central branches","Dorsal root ganglia, most cranial sensory ganglia","Impulse bypasses the soma; mesencephalic nucleus of CN V is a CNS exception"],["Bipolar","One dendrite, one axon","Retinal bipolar cells, olfactory receptor neurons, spiral and vestibular ganglia","Olfactory neurons are replaced from basal stem cells throughout life"]],"caption":"Shape predicts function: many inputs for integration, a direct path for sensation, a simple relay for special senses."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A section stained with cresyl violet shows a large neuron with one pale cone-shaped region leaving the soma. Which part is it, and why is it pale?","explain":"It is the axon hillock. It lacks rough endoplasmic reticulum and ribosomes, so there is no RNA to take up the basic dye, whereas the soma and dendrites stain blue.","check":"The paragraph on Nissl substance and the parts table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C02","obj":"NE-2.2","title":"Connective tissue layers of a peripheral nerve and the blood-nerve barrier","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A peripheral nerve is a bundle of axons, each with its Schwann cells, organized by three connective tissue sheaths. Reading from inside out, the endoneurium surrounds each nerve fiber, the perineurium surrounds each fascicle (a bundle of fibers), and the epineurium surrounds the whole nerve."},{"type":"table","title":"The three sheaths of a peripheral nerve","cols":["Layer","Surrounds","Structure","Main role"],"rows":[["Endoneurium","Each nerve fiber (axon plus Schwann cell)","Loose connective tissue with thin collagen and reticular fibers, fibroblasts, capillaries; lies outside the Schwann cell basal lamina","Supports fibers; holds endoneurial fluid and capillaries"],["Perineurium","Each fascicle","Several concentric layers of flattened epithelioid cells, each layer with basal lamina and tight junctions","Diffusion barrier; part of the blood-nerve barrier"],["Epineurium","Whole nerve and spaces between fascicles","Dense irregular connective tissue with type I collagen and fat","Protects against stretch and compression; carries the vasa nervorum"]],"caption":"Endo, peri, epi: fiber, fascicle, whole nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"The perineurium is the most specialized layer. Its cells behave like an epithelium: they rest on basal laminae and are sealed to one another by tight junctions, so substances in the surrounding tissue cannot diffuse freely into the fascicle. This keeps the endoneurial fluid around the axons stable in ionic composition and protects axons from toxins, drugs, and infectious agents. The perineurium also gives the fascicle much of its tensile strength."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood-nerve barrier has two parts. The perineurium blocks entry from the surrounding tissue, and the endoneurial capillaries, which are continuous (nonfenestrated) and joined by tight junctions, block entry from the blood. Together they are the peripheral counterpart of the blood-brain barrier. The barrier is weaker in the dorsal root ganglia and at the nerve roots, where capillaries are more permeable. This helps explain why some neurotoxins and immune attacks concentrate in sensory ganglia and roots, as in the root inflammation of Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"At the spinal and cranial roots the sheaths are continuous with the meninges: the epineurium with the dura mater and the perineurium with the arachnoid. The epineurial vasa nervorum feed the nerve, so diseases of small vessels, such as diabetic microangiopathy and vasculitis, cause ischemic damage to nerves."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the sheaths matter after injury"},{"type":"p","text":"The layers that survive an injury decide how well the nerve recovers. If the endoneurial tubes and Schwann cell basal laminae stay intact, regenerating axons are guided back to their original targets. If the perineurium and epineurium are cut through, axons sprout into scar tissue and may form a painful neuroma or reach the wrong target."},{"type":"table","title":"Seddon classification of nerve injury","cols":["Grade","What is damaged","Wallerian degeneration","Recovery"],"rows":[["Neurapraxia","Focal conduction block, often from segmental demyelination; axon and sheaths intact","No","Complete, usually within weeks once myelin is repaired"],["Axonotmesis","Axon cut; endoneurial tubes and outer sheaths intact (typical crush)","Yes","Good; axons regrow along their own tubes"],["Neurotmesis","Axon and all sheaths divided","Yes","Poor; without guidance tubes, axons form a neuroma or reach the wrong target"]],"caption":"The more connective tissue that survives, the better the guidance for regrowth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C03","obj":"NE-2.3","title":"Schwann cells, oligodendrocytes, and diseases of myelin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myelin is glial plasma membrane wrapped many times around an axon and compacted, about 70 to 80% lipid (cholesterol, galactocerebroside, sphingomyelin). Two cell types make it, and the difference between them explains the pattern of each demyelinating disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Schwann cells versus oligodendrocytes","cols":["Feature","Schwann cell","Oligodendrocyte"],"rows":[["Location","PNS","CNS"],["Embryonic origin","Neural crest","Neuroectoderm of the neural tube"],["Myelin per cell","One internode of one axon","Internodes on many axons, up to about 50"],["Unmyelinated axons","One nonmyelinating cell wraps several small axons (C fibers)","Not ensheathed"],["Basal lamina and outer cytoplasm","Present (neurilemma)","Absent"],["Major myelin proteins","P0, PMP22, myelin basic protein","Proteolipid protein, myelin basic protein, MOG"],["Disease targets","Guillain-Barré syndrome, CMT1A (PMP22 duplication)","Multiple sclerosis, PML, leukodystrophies"]],"caption":"One-to-one in the PNS, one-to-many in the CNS."},{"type":"p","text":"Myelin speeds conduction by acting as electrical insulation. It raises the resistance across the internodal membrane and lowers its capacitance, so less current leaks out and less charge is spent changing membrane voltage. Current therefore spreads passively and quickly along the internode to the next node of Ranvier, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are clustered at very high density."},{"type":"flow","title":"Saltatory conduction","steps":["Action potential opens dense Na+ channels at a node","Inward current spreads rapidly along the insulated internode","Next node depolarizes to threshold and fires","Impulse jumps node to node at high velocity"],"caption":"Only the nodes regenerate the action potential; the internode conducts passively."},{"type":"p","text":"When myelin is lost, current leaks across the bare internode, whose membrane has few Na+ channels, so the next node is reached slowly (slowing) or not at all (conduction block). Block causes weakness and sensory loss; uneven slowing desynchronizes the volley and abolishes tendon reflexes. Demyelinated axons are also heat sensitive because Na+ channels inactivate faster at higher temperature, so MS symptoms worsen with a hot bath or fever (Uhthoff phenomenon)."},{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barré syndrome, in its classic demyelinating form, is an acute immune attack on Schwann cell myelin, often 1 to 3 weeks after Campylobacter jejuni enteritis or a viral illness. The mechanism is molecular mimicry (microbial antigens resembling nerve components), and macrophages strip myelin from internodes. Root inflammation raises CSF protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation). The result is ascending symmetric weakness with areflexia, sparing the CNS."},{"type":"p","text":"Multiple sclerosis is a T cell mediated autoimmune attack on CNS myelin and oligodendrocytes, producing plaques in periventricular white matter, optic nerves, brainstem, and spinal cord, with oligoclonal IgG bands in the CSF. Because one oligodendrocyte supports many axons, each lost cell demyelinates many fibers, and remyelination is incomplete. Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is lytic JC polyomavirus infection of oligodendrocytes when cellular immunity fails (advanced HIV, transplant immunosuppression, or natalizumab, which blocks lymphocyte entry into the CNS). Infected oligodendrocytes have enlarged nuclei with ground-glass inclusions, and multifocal white matter lesions follow."},{"type":"example","title":"Demyelinating versus axonal nerve injury on nerve conduction studies","text":"Two patients have leg weakness. In patient A, motor conduction velocity is 25 m/s (normal is roughly 40 m/s or more), distal latency is prolonged, and the response is much smaller with proximal than with distal stimulation. In patient B, velocity is 42 m/s but the response is small. Velocity depends on myelin, so marked slowing with focal block means damaged internodes: patient A has a demyelinating neuropathy such as classic Guillain-Barré syndrome. Amplitude reflects how many axons conduct, so a small response at near-normal speed means axon loss with intact myelin on the survivors: patient B has an axonal neuropathy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a small MS plaque often cause more deficit than loss of a similar number of Schwann cells?","explain":"One oligodendrocyte myelinates internodes on many axons, so its loss demyelinates many fibers, and CNS remyelination is limited. Each Schwann cell serves one internode, and surviving Schwann cells can remyelinate.","check":"The comparison table and the multiple sclerosis paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C04","obj":"NE-2.4","title":"Astrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Astrocytes are the most numerous glial cells. They arise from neuroectoderm and contain glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP), an intermediate filament used to identify them and their tumors. Protoplasmic astrocytes lie in gray matter and fibrous astrocytes in white matter. Their processes contact synapses, nodes of Ranvier, and capillaries, and neighboring astrocytes are linked by gap junctions into a network."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood-brain barrier itself is formed by brain capillary endothelium: continuous, nonfenestrated cells joined by tight junctions, with few transport vesicles. Astrocyte end-feet cover nearly the whole capillary surface and, with pericytes, induce and maintain these endothelial properties. The end-feet carry aquaporin-4 water channels, the target of the antibody in neuromyelitis optica, which links an astrocyte protein to a demyelinating disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Astrocytes also control the fluid around neurons. Each action potential releases K+ into the small extracellular space, and if K+ accumulated it would depolarize nearby neurons. Astrocytes take up K+ through inward rectifier channels (Kir4.1), pass it through gap junctions, and release it where concentration is lower, often near capillaries; this is spatial buffering. They also remove synaptic glutamate with high-affinity transporters, convert it to glutamine with glutamine synthetase, and return glutamine to neurons. Because this enzyme is also the brain's main way to detoxify ammonia, hyperammonemia in liver failure loads astrocytes with glutamine, causing swelling and Alzheimer type II astrocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Spatial buffering of potassium","steps":["Repeated firing raises extracellular K+ near active neurons","Astrocytes take up K+ through Kir4.1 channels","K+ moves through gap junctions across the astrocyte network","K+ is released at end-feet near capillaries","Extracellular K+ falls; neuronal excitability returns to normal"],"caption":"Failure of this system lowers seizure threshold."},{"type":"p","text":"After injury, astrocytes enlarge, proliferate, and increase GFAP, a response called reactive gliosis. Because the brain parenchyma has almost no fibroblasts, the glial scar is the CNS form of scarring. It walls off damaged tissue and restores the barrier, but its proteoglycans also block axonal regrowth (C05)."},{"type":"p","text":"Microglia are the resident macrophages of the CNS. Unlike the other glia, they are of myeloid (mesodermal) origin, derived from yolk sac progenitors that colonize the brain early in development. At rest they have small elongated nuclei and branched processes; when activated they phagocytose debris and present antigen on MHC class II. In HIV encephalitis, the virus enters microglia and perivascular macrophages through CD4 and the CCR5 coreceptor, and infected cells fuse through viral envelope proteins into multinucleated giant cells within microglial nodules. Neurons are not infected; they are injured by cytokines and viral proteins released from these cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Ependymal cells are ciliated cuboidal to columnar epithelial cells that line the ventricles and central canal of the spinal cord. Their cilia help move CSF, and the junctions between them are leaky, so CSF and brain extracellular fluid exchange freely. Over the choroid plexus, modified ependymal cells sealed by tight junctions secrete CSF and form the blood-CSF barrier."},{"type":"table","title":"Glial cells of the CNS","cols":["Cell","Origin","Marker or feature","Key functions","Pathology"],"rows":[["Astrocyte","Neuroectoderm","GFAP","Barrier induction, K+ buffering, glutamate uptake, scar","Gliosis, astrocytoma, Alzheimer type II cells"],["Oligodendrocyte","Neuroectoderm","Perinuclear halo (fried-egg look) on routine sections","CNS myelin","MS, PML, oligodendroglioma"],["Microglia","Myeloid (yolk sac)","Small, dense, elongated nucleus; CD68 when active","Phagocytosis, antigen presentation","Microglial nodules and giant cells in HIV"],["Ependymal cell","Neuroectoderm","Cilia and microvilli","Lines ventricles, moves CSF; choroid plexus secretes CSF","Ependymoma"]],"caption":"Only microglia are not derived from the neural tube."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C05","obj":"NE-2.5","title":"Axon transection: Wallerian degeneration, chromatolysis, and regeneration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because the axon has no ribosomes, everything it needs comes from the soma by axonal transport. When an axon is cut, the distal segment loses that supply and dies, while the cell body survives and attempts repair."},{"type":"p","text":"Distal to the cut, the axon undergoes Wallerian degeneration. For a few days the distal stump still conducts, then the axon swells and breaks into fragments and its myelin collapses into ovoids. This is an active process: loss of a short-lived survival enzyme (NMNAT2) supplied by the soma triggers a self-destruct program that depletes NAD+. In the PNS, Schwann cells stop making myelin, begin breaking it down, and recruit macrophages, so debris is cleared within weeks. Proximal to the cut, the axon degenerates only back to the nearest node or two."},{"type":"p","text":"In the cell body, central chromatolysis begins within 1 to 2 days and peaks at about 1 to 3 weeks. The soma swells and rounds, Nissl substance disperses from the center toward the periphery, and the nucleus moves to the edge of the cell. The rough endoplasmic reticulum is not lost; it is reorganized as the neuron shifts from making neurotransmitter-related proteins to making structural proteins such as tubulin and actin for regrowth. Chromatolysis is reversible if the axon reconnects; if it does not, the neuron may atrophy or die."},{"type":"flow","title":"Peripheral axon injury and regrowth","steps":["Axon is cut; distal segment loses transport from the soma","Distal axon and myelin degenerate (Wallerian degeneration)","Schwann cells and macrophages clear debris","Cell body shows central chromatolysis and makes growth proteins","Schwann cells proliferate in basal lamina tubes (bands of Büngner)","Growth cones advance along the tubes about 1 mm per day","Axon reaches target; Schwann cells remyelinate it"],"caption":"Regrowth depends on surviving endoneurial tubes and Schwann cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral axons regrow because the environment supports them. Denervated Schwann cells divide inside the preserved basal lamina tubes, forming bands of Büngner, and secrete neurotrophic factors such as nerve growth factor and adhesive molecules such as laminin. Sprouts from the proximal stump enter these tubes and grow at about 1 mm per day, roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) per month. Regenerated internodes are shorter and thinner, so conduction velocity usually stays somewhat below normal. Axons that enter the wrong tube reach the wrong target, which explains synkinesis after facial nerve injury."},{"type":"table","title":"Why PNS axons regrow and CNS axons generally do not","cols":["Factor","PNS","CNS"],"rows":[["Myelinating cell response","Schwann cells dedifferentiate, divide, and guide","Oligodendrocytes do not form guidance tubes"],["Basal lamina tubes","Present around each fiber","Absent"],["Debris clearance","Fast (Schwann cells and macrophages)","Slow; myelin debris persists for months"],["Growth inhibitors","Few","Myelin proteins (Nogo-A, MAG, OMgp) and glial scar proteoglycans"],["Usual outcome","Useful regrowth if tubes are aligned","Little long-distance regrowth"]],"caption":"Adult CNS neurons also have less intrinsic capacity to grow, so CNS injuries leave permanent deficits even when cell bodies survive."},{"type":"example","title":"Estimating recovery time after a nerve injury","text":"A crush of the ulnar nerve at the elbow leaves the endoneurial tubes intact (axonotmesis). The first dorsal interosseous lies roughly 30 cm (300 mm) away along the nerve. At about 1 mm per day, regrowth takes about 300 days, roughly 10 months, plus a short delay to cross the injury site. Denervated muscle and motor end plates deteriorate over roughly 12 to 18 months, so the hand muscles are reinnervated close to that limit and recovery is often incomplete. Distance from target, not only severity, predicts outcome."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C06","obj":"NE-2.6","title":"Red neurons and acute hypoxic-ischemic neuronal injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neurons are the cells most sensitive to loss of oxygen and glucose. They depend almost entirely on oxidative phosphorylation, store little glycogen, and spend a large share of their ATP running the Na+/K+-ATPase. A few minutes of complete ischemia can start irreversible injury, but the light microscope cannot show that injury until about 12 to 24 hours later, when red neurons appear."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic-ischemic neuronal injury","steps":["Ischemia stops oxidative phosphorylation; ATP falls","Na+/K+-ATPase fails; neuron depolarizes","Glutamate is released and not taken back up","NMDA receptors open; Ca2+ floods the neuron","Ca2+ activates proteases, phospholipases, and nitric oxide synthase","Mitochondria fail; free radicals form","Red neurons visible about 12 to 24 hours later"],"caption":"Excess glutamate signaling (excitotoxicity) links energy failure to Ca2+ overload."},{"type":"p","text":"A red neuron is shrunken and angular, with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm, a small dark pyknotic nucleus, loss of the nucleolus, and loss of Nissl substance. Each feature has a cause. Nissl substance is basophilic because of its RNA, and when ribosomes break down the cytoplasm loses its blue tint. Denatured cytoplasmic proteins then bind eosin strongly, so the cell looks bright red. The nucleus condenses (pyknosis) as chromatin collapses, a sign that the cell is dead rather than injured."},{"type":"p","text":"Not all neurons are equally vulnerable. In global hypoxia or hypoperfusion, such as cardiac arrest, the first to die are pyramidal cells of hippocampal sector CA1 (Sommer sector), cerebellar Purkinje cells, and pyramidal neurons in neocortical layers 3, 5, and 6, giving a band-like pattern called pseudolaminar necrosis. These cells receive heavy glutamatergic input and have high metabolic demand. Regional hypoperfusion also targets watershed zones between arterial territories."},{"type":"table","title":"Tissue stages after a brain infarct","cols":["Time","Microscopic finding","Cause"],"rows":[["About 12 to 24 hours","Red neurons","Irreversible neuronal death"],["About 1 to 3 days","Neutrophil infiltration","Acute inflammation at the necrotic tissue"],["About 3 days to weeks","Macrophages laden with lipid (gitter cells)","Phagocytosis of myelin and debris"],["About 1 week onward","Reactive gliosis around the lesion","Astrocyte hypertrophy and proliferation"],["Weeks to months","Cyst walled off by glial scar","Liquefactive necrosis removed"]],"caption":"Times are approximate and vary with lesion size and source."},{"type":"p","text":"The brain undergoes liquefactive necrosis rather than the coagulative necrosis typical of other organs after ischemia, because it is rich in lipid, has little supporting collagen, and is digested by enzymes from microglia and macrophages. Red neurons must also be distinguished from central chromatolysis: chromatolysis is a reversible reaction to axonal injury with a swollen soma and peripheral nucleus, whereas a red neuron is shrunken, eosinophilic, and dead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient dies 6 hours after cardiac arrest with prolonged resuscitation. Would red neurons be expected in the hippocampus at autopsy?","explain":"Probably not. Irreversible injury has begun, but red neurons usually take about 12 to 24 hours to become visible, so neurons can look normal on routine sections at 6 hours.","check":"The opening paragraph and the infarct stage table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and cell biology explain the cell types. Neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma come from the neural tube; Schwann cells, satellite cells, and sensory ganglion neurons come from neural crest; microglia are myeloid immigrants. Because the axon lacks ribosomes, it depends on transport from the soma, which is why a cut axon degenerates distally, why viruses and toxins can travel retrograde to the cell body, and why distal axons are the first to fail in many metabolic neuropathies."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology, microbiology, and pathology act on specific cells. Schwann cell myelin is the target in Guillain-Barré syndrome, oligodendrocytes in MS and PML, astrocyte aquaporin-4 in neuromyelitis optica, and microglia in HIV encephalitis. Myelin determines conduction velocity, so demyelination shows as slowing and block while axon loss shows as reduced amplitude. Light microscopy lags behind events: red neurons appear hours after irreversible injury, and nerve conduction distal to a cut stays normal for several days, so timing must be considered before interpreting findings."}],"summary":["Nissl substance (rough ER and ribosomes) fills the soma and dendrites but is absent from the axon hillock and axon, which depend on kinesin and dynein transport.","Action potentials begin at the axon initial segment, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are densest.","Multipolar neurons are motor and most CNS neurons; pseudounipolar neurons sit in dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia; bipolar neurons serve the retina, olfaction, and the spiral and vestibular ganglia.","Endoneurium surrounds each fiber, perineurium each fascicle, and epineurium the whole nerve; perineurial tight junctions and endoneurial capillaries form the blood-nerve barrier.","Schwann cells (neural crest) myelinate one internode of one axon; oligodendrocytes (neural tube) myelinate many axons, so CNS demyelination spreads widely.","Guillain-Barré syndrome attacks Schwann cell myelin; MS attacks CNS myelin; JC virus kills oligodendrocytes in PML.","Astrocytes induce the blood-brain barrier, buffer K+, recycle glutamate, detoxify ammonia, and form the glial scar; microglia are myeloid macrophages that form giant cells in HIV encephalitis; ependyma lines the ventricles.","Wallerian degeneration occurs distal to a cut, central chromatolysis in the cell body; PNS axons regrow about 1 mm per day along bands of Büngner.","CNS axons rarely regrow because of absent guide tubes, inhibitory myelin proteins, slow debris clearance, and the glial scar.","Red neurons (shrunken, eosinophilic, pyknotic) mark irreversible hypoxic injury about 12 to 24 hours after the insult; CA1, Purkinje cells, and cortical layers 3, 5, and 6 die first."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue and the Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Central Nervous System, cellular pathology and cerebrovascular disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral nerve injury and demyelinating neuropathies"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials, myelinated nerve fibers and saltatory conduction"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Glial cells and the neuronal microenvironment"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-20-A":{"unit":"NE-20-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01","title":"Neurological Vascular Disease","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the brain and spinal cord are injured when blood flow stops, when vessels rupture, or when venous drainage or autoregulation fails. It covers ischemic infarction and its evolution under the microscope, global hypoxic injury, the four intracranial hemorrhages, vascular malformations, arterial dissection and subclavian steal, spinal cord infarction, venous sinus thrombosis, and posterior reversible encephalopathy. Board questions usually give a patient, a time course, an image description, or a tissue finding and ask for the vessel, the mechanism, or the next complication. If you can tie each lesion to the vessel that fails, the pressure behind it, and the space it fills, most of these questions become anatomy problems.","prerequisites":["Circle of Willis and the territories of the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries (NE-10)","Meninges, dural venous sinuses, and CSF production and absorption (NE-4)","Spinal cord tracts and where each decussates (NE-3, NE-5)","Glutamate receptors and neuronal membrane potential (NE-13)","Atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, thrombosis, and embolism (CV-18-I)"],"connections":[{"to":"CNS blood supply and CSF flow (NE-10)","how":"Arterial territories predict the deficit from each infarct, and CSF pathways explain hydrocephalus after subarachnoid hemorrhage."},{"to":"Diseases of increased intracranial fluid (NE-20-C)","how":"Hematomas, infarct edema, and venous outflow obstruction all raise intracranial pressure and can cause herniation."},{"to":"Hypertensive and vascular disease (CV-18-A, CV-18-I)","how":"Hypertension drives lacunes, Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms, and PRES, while atherosclerosis and dissection cause large artery strokes."},{"to":"Cardiac rhythm and valve disease (CV-7, CV-18-D)","how":"Atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, and endocarditis are the main sources of cerebral emboli."},{"to":"Neurogenetics and connective tissue disorders (NE-16, CV-14)","how":"ADPKD, vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia explain aneurysms and malformations in young patients."}],"terms":[{"term":"Infarct","def":"An area of tissue necrosis caused by loss of its blood supply."},{"term":"Ischemic penumbra","def":"Tissue around an infarct core that is electrically silent from reduced flow but still viable if flow returns."},{"term":"Hemorrhagic conversion","def":"Bleeding into an infarct when blood reenters vessels whose walls were damaged by ischemia."},{"term":"Lacune","def":"A small cavitated infarct, less than about 15 mm across, from occlusion of a deep penetrating artery."},{"term":"Liquefactive necrosis","def":"Necrosis in which enzymatic digestion turns dead tissue into fluid, typical of the brain."},{"term":"Excitotoxicity","def":"Neuronal death caused by excess glutamate overstimulating receptors and loading the cell with Ca2+."},{"term":"Watershed (border zone) infarct","def":"Infarction at the far ends of two adjacent arterial territories, where perfusion pressure is lowest."},{"term":"Saccular (berry) aneurysm","def":"A thin-walled outpouching at an arterial branch point of the circle of Willis, lacking media and internal elastic lamina in the sac."},{"term":"Xanthochromia","def":"Yellow discoloration of CSF from bilirubin formed as red cells in the subarachnoid space break down."},{"term":"Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysm","def":"A tiny dilation of a small penetrating artery weakened by chronic hypertension."},{"term":"Cerebral autoregulation","def":"Arteriolar constriction and dilation that hold cerebral blood flow nearly constant across a range of arterial pressure."},{"term":"Vasogenic edema","def":"Extracellular brain edema from blood-brain barrier leak, in contrast to cytotoxic edema from cell swelling."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C01","obj":"NE-20-A.1","title":"Thrombotic, embolic, and lacunar infarction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brain receives about 15% of the cardiac output and uses about 20% of resting oxygen, yet it stores almost no glucose or glycogen. When an artery closes, function in its territory stops within seconds and irreversible injury begins within minutes. How the artery closes predicts the onset, the vessel, and the appearance of the infarct."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrombotic infarction occurs where a thrombus forms on an ulcerated or fissured atherosclerotic plaque. Plaques gather where flow is turbulent and shear stress is low, so the classic sites are the carotid bifurcation (origin of the internal carotid artery), the middle cerebral artery (MCA), and the basilar artery. Narrowing progresses slowly while collaterals partly compensate, so the deficit often develops in a stuttering or stepwise pattern. Because the vessel usually stays occluded, the infarct is typically pale (anemic)."},{"type":"p","text":"Embolic infarction occurs when material formed elsewhere lodges in a cerebral artery. Most emboli are cardiac: left atrial appendage thrombus in atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus after myocardial infarction or in dilated cardiomyopathy, and vegetations on diseased valves. Artery-to-artery emboli from carotid plaque and paradoxical emboli through a patent foramen ovale also occur. The MCA is the usual destination because it continues the straight course of the internal carotid artery. The deficit is maximal at onset. Emboli often fragment or lyse within hours to days, and blood returning to vessels whose walls are already ischemic leaks into the infarct, producing hemorrhagic (red) conversion, often as petechiae along the cortex."},{"type":"p","text":"Lacunar infarcts arise in small penetrating arteries, such as the lenticulostriate branches of the MCA and the paramedian branches of the basilar artery, which leave large vessels at right angles and are exposed to near full arterial pressure. Chronic hypertension, and diabetes to a lesser degree, cause hyaline arteriolosclerosis: plasma proteins leak into the wall, the lumen narrows, and the vessel finally closes. Because these arteries supply densely packed tracts and nuclei, a lesion only millimeters wide can cause a pure motor stroke (posterior limb of the internal capsule) or a pure sensory stroke (ventral posterior thalamus), without cortical signs such as aphasia or neglect."},{"type":"table","title":"Three mechanisms of ischemic infarction","cols":["Feature","Thrombotic","Embolic","Lacunar"],"rows":[["Vessel lesion","Thrombus on atherosclerotic plaque","Clot or vegetation from heart or proximal artery","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of a penetrating artery"],["Typical sites","Carotid bifurcation, MCA, basilar artery","MCA branches, often cortical","Putamen, internal capsule, thalamus, pons"],["Onset","Stepwise or stuttering","Sudden, maximal at onset","Sudden or stepwise"],["Infarct","Pale, territorial","Often hemorrhagic, may be multiple territories","Small cavity under about 15 mm"],["Key risk factor","Atherosclerosis risk factors","Atrial fibrillation","Hypertension"]],"caption":"The mechanism of occlusion predicts onset, location, and whether the infarct becomes hemorrhagic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does an embolic infarct often become hemorrhagic while a thrombotic infarct usually stays pale?","explain":"The embolus fragments or lyses and flow returns into vessels whose walls are already necrotic, so blood leaks into the tissue. A thrombus on a plaque usually keeps the artery occluded, so no blood reaches the damaged bed.","check":"The paragraph on embolic infarction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C02","obj":"NE-20-A.2","title":"Evolution of a cerebral infarct","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The appearance of an infarct follows the order of events after ATP fails: neurons die first, dead tissue attracts neutrophils, macrophages remove the debris, and astrocytes seal off what is left. The timing is predictable, so the microscopic findings date the infarct."},{"type":"p","text":"For the first several hours little is visible, even though neurons may already be irreversibly injured. By about 12 to 24 hours the typical sign is the red neuron (acute ischemic neuronal change): the cell body shrinks, the cytoplasm becomes intensely eosinophilic as Nissl substance (rough endoplasmic reticulum) is lost, and the nucleus becomes small and dark (pyknotic). Edema develops at the same time, first cytotoxic as failing ion pumps let cells swell, then vasogenic as damaged capillaries leak. Swelling is greatest within the first few days, which is when a large MCA infarct can cause herniation."},{"type":"p","text":"Neutrophils enter during the first few days. Macrophages, derived from blood monocytes and activated microglia, then take over and dominate for weeks. Myelin is rich in lipid, so these cells fill with lipid and become foamy macrophages (gitter cells); in a hemorrhagic infarct they also hold hemosiderin. Reactive astrocytes enlarge and multiply at the margin from about the end of the first week."},{"type":"p","text":"The end result differs from infarction in the heart or kidney, where coagulative necrosis preserves tissue outlines and fibroblasts lay down collagen. Brain parenchyma has abundant lipid and lysosomal enzymes, little supporting collagen, and almost no fibroblasts. Digestion therefore liquefies the dead tissue (liquefactive necrosis), and over weeks to months the infarct becomes a fluid-filled cyst crossed by small vessels and walled by a glial scar of astrocyte processes (gliosis) rather than a collagen scar."},{"type":"table","title":"Time course of an ischemic cerebral infarct","cols":["Time after onset (about)","Microscopic finding","Gross appearance"],"rows":[["0 to 12 hours","Little visible change","Normal or slightly swollen"],["12 to 24 hours","Red neurons; cytotoxic then vasogenic edema","Slight swelling, otherwise near normal"],["1 to 3 days","Neutrophil infiltrate","Pale, soft, swollen; gray-white junction indistinct"],["3 days to weeks","Macrophages (microglia and monocytes) become foamy as they clear myelin","Gelatinous, friable, then liquefying"],["1 week onward","Reactive astrocytes, new capillaries","Infarct begins to cavitate"],["Weeks to months","Cyst lined by gliosis","Fluid-filled cavity"]],"caption":"Times are approximate and vary with infarct size and source; the order of events is what boards test."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C03","obj":"NE-20-A.3","title":"Ischemic stroke, hemorrhagic stroke, and transient ischemic attack","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"About 85% of strokes are ischemic (thrombotic, embolic, or lacunar, as in C01) and about 15% are hemorrhagic, divided between intraparenchymal hemorrhage (C07) and subarachnoid hemorrhage (C06). Ischemic stroke injures tissue by withdrawing oxygen and glucose. Hemorrhagic stroke injures it by mass effect, raised intracranial pressure, and the toxic effects of blood, so it more often brings early headache, vomiting, and declining consciousness, although examination alone cannot reliably separate the two."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal cerebral blood flow is about 50 mL per 100 g of brain per minute. When an artery closes, flow in the center of its territory falls so low that ion pumps fail and cells die within minutes (the infarct core). Around the core, collateral flow keeps tissue alive but electrically silent; this ischemic penumbra causes part of the early deficit and can recover if flow returns, or join the infarct if it does not."},{"type":"p","text":"A transient ischemic attack (TIA) is a brief episode of focal brain, spinal cord, or retinal dysfunction caused by ischemia without infarction on imaging. Most resolve within an hour. The current definition is tissue based: if MRI shows an infarct, the event is a stroke even when the symptoms disappear. A TIA indicates an unstable source, such as an ulcerated carotid plaque or a cardiac thrombus, and stroke risk is highest in the first days after it. Transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax) from emboli to the ophthalmic artery is a retinal TIA that points to the ipsilateral carotid artery."},{"type":"p","text":"The two types share risk factors because both reflect vessel wall damage. Age is the strongest nonmodifiable factor and hypertension the strongest modifiable one, driving atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, and microaneurysm rupture. Atrial fibrillation supplies emboli, diabetes and dyslipidemia accelerate atherosclerosis, and smoking injures endothelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Noncontrast CT is the first image because it answers the question that changes everything: is there blood? Fresh blood is bright (hyperdense) on CT at once, because a clot packs hemoglobin and protein densely. Early ischemia, by contrast, often looks normal for the first several hours, then shows loss of gray-white differentiation and, over about a day, a dark (hypodense) territory. Contrast is omitted because enhancing vessels are also bright and could hide or mimic blood. A clot-dissolving drug given into a hemorrhage would enlarge it, which is why blood must be excluded before reperfusion is considered. MRI diffusion imaging detects ischemic cell swelling within minutes but takes longer to obtain."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has right arm weakness for 40 minutes that resolves completely, but MRI shows a small acute infarct in the left internal capsule. TIA or stroke?","explain":"Stroke. The tissue-based definition depends on infarction, not on how long symptoms last.","check":"The TIA paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C04","obj":"NE-20-A.4","title":"Global hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy and watershed infarcts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Global hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy results when the whole brain loses its supply of oxygen, as in cardiac arrest, shock, severe hypotension, drowning, or carbon monoxide poisoning. Consciousness is lost within about 10 seconds of absent flow, and in a normothermic adult, irreversible neuronal injury begins after about 4 to 5 minutes. Ischemia (no flow) is more harmful than hypoxia with preserved flow, because flow still delivers glucose and removes lactate and other metabolites."},{"type":"flow","title":"Excitotoxic neuronal injury","steps":["Oxygen and glucose delivery stop","ATP falls and Na+/K+ ATPase fails","Neurons depolarize and release glutamate; reuptake fails","NMDA receptor activation floods neurons with Ca2+","Ca2+ activates proteases, phospholipases, and free radical production","Membranes and cytoskeleton break down; neuron dies"],"caption":"This cascade drives both focal infarction and global hypoxic injury; neurons with the most glutamate input are lost first."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurons are more vulnerable than glia, and some neurons are more vulnerable than others. The classic sites are pyramidal neurons of hippocampal sector CA1 (Sommer sector), cerebellar Purkinje cells, and pyramidal neurons of the neocortex. These cells receive heavy glutamatergic input, have high metabolic demand, and handle Ca2+ loads poorly. Hippocampal injury explains the severe anterograde memory loss of many survivors of cardiac arrest, and CA1 neurons can die over the following days (delayed neuronal death). In the cortex, damage is selective for the deeper layers 3, 5, and 6, producing bands of necrosis parallel to the surface called pseudolaminar (laminar) necrosis. Severe injury leaves widespread cortical loss and can end in a persistent vegetative state or brain death."},{"type":"p","text":"Watershed (border zone) infarcts form where two arterial territories meet. These regions lie at the farthest ends of both arterial trees, so their perfusion pressure is lowest. A fall in systemic pressure starves them first, even when no artery is blocked. The ACA and MCA border zone is most affected: a sickle-shaped strip over the convexity a few centimeters lateral to the midline, which contains the motor cortex for the proximal arm and shoulder (and hip). Bilateral lesions give proximal limb weakness with sparing of the face and hands, sometimes called man in a barrel. The MCA and PCA border zone in the parieto-occipital region causes visual and higher cortical deficits."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a cardiac arrest, a patient has bilateral weakness of the shoulders and upper arms with normal hand grip and facial movement. Where is the lesion and why there?","explain":"Bilateral ACA and MCA watershed zones. The shoulder and proximal arm are represented on the upper convexity, between the medial ACA strip (leg) and the lateral MCA region (hand and face), at the ends of both arterial trees, where pressure fell most.","check":"The watershed paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C05","obj":"NE-20-A.5","title":"Epidural and subdural hematoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The dura is the key to both lesions. Its outer layer is the periosteum of the inner skull and is bound most tightly at the sutures; its inner surface faces the arachnoid across a potential space crossed by bridging veins. Each hematoma takes its shape and speed from the vessel that tears and the space the blood must open."},{"type":"p","text":"Epidural hematoma lies between skull and dura and is usually arterial. The middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery that enters through the foramen spinosum, runs in a groove on the inner surface of the thin squamous temporal bone near the pterion. A fracture there tears it, and blood at arterial pressure strips the dura from bone. The dura holds firm at the sutures, so the clot bulges inward as a biconvex (lens-shaped) collection that does not cross sutures. The patient is often briefly knocked out by the impact, may then recover for minutes to hours (the lucid interval), and then deteriorates as the clot expands. It is uncommon in older adults, whose dura is more firmly attached to the skull."},{"type":"flow","title":"From temporal fracture to herniation","steps":["Temporal bone fracture tears the middle meningeal artery","Arterial blood strips dura from bone, stopping at sutures","Brief unconsciousness, then a lucid interval in some patients","Expanding biconvex clot raises intracranial pressure","Uncus herniates and compresses CN III and midbrain","Coma, brainstem compression, and death if pressure is not relieved"],"caption":"Arterial pressure makes the epidural clot grow over hours, so decline after the lucid interval is rapid."},{"type":"p","text":"Subdural hematoma is usually venous. Bridging veins pass from the cortical surface to the dural sinuses, chiefly the superior sagittal sinus. When the brain moves relative to the skull in acceleration and deceleration, these veins stretch and tear. Brain atrophy in older adults and in chronic alcohol use lengthens and tenses them, so minor trauma suffices; anticoagulation adds risk. In shaken infants, violent rotation tears them, often with retinal hemorrhages. Low-pressure bleeding spreads freely along the hemisphere, crossing sutures as a crescent, but it is stopped by dural reflections such as the falx and tentorium, so it does not cross the midline. Acute subdural hematoma follows major trauma and often accompanies brain contusion. A chronic subdural hematoma develops over weeks; granulation tissue and fragile new vessels in its membranes rebleed, causing headache, confusion, or slowly progressive deficits that can be mistaken for dementia."},{"type":"table","title":"Epidural versus subdural hematoma","cols":["Feature","Epidural","Subdural"],"rows":[["Vessel","Middle meningeal artery","Bridging veins"],["Usual cause","Temporal bone fracture","Acceleration and deceleration; minor trauma in atrophy"],["Typical patient","Young adult after a blow to the side of the head","Older adult, chronic alcohol use, shaken infant"],["Shape on CT","Biconvex, limited by sutures","Crescent, crosses sutures, not the midline"],["Speed","Minutes to hours","Hours (acute) to weeks (chronic)"],["CT density over time","Bright when acute","Bright when acute, about isodense at 1 to 3 weeks, dark when chronic"]],"caption":"Arterial blood under high pressure confined by sutures versus venous blood under low pressure spreading freely."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an expanding epidural hematoma","text":"A 22-year-old is struck on the left temple, loses consciousness briefly, then talks normally for two hours before becoming drowsy. His left pupil is dilated and unreactive, and his right arm and leg are weak. The lucid interval and temporal blow suggest an epidural hematoma from the left middle meningeal artery. The expanding clot pushes the left uncus over the tentorial edge, compressing the left oculomotor nerve, whose superficial parasympathetic fibers are affected first, so the pupil dilates before the eye deviates. The uncus also compresses the left cerebral peduncle, whose corticospinal fibers cross below in the medulla, causing right-sided weakness."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C06","obj":"NE-20-A.6","title":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage from a saccular aneurysm","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Trauma is the most common cause of subarachnoid blood, but most spontaneous cases come from rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm. About 90% of these aneurysms arise in the anterior circulation, most often at the junction of the anterior communicating artery with the anterior cerebral artery. Other common sites are the MCA bifurcation and the origin of the posterior communicating artery, where an aneurysm can compress the oculomotor nerve and dilate the pupil."},{"type":"p","text":"Saccular aneurysms are acquired over life rather than present at birth. At branch points the tunica media is thin or absent, and the internal elastic lamina is fragmented; where flow strikes the apex of a bifurcation, hemodynamic stress gradually balloons the weak wall outward. Within the sac, the wall is thickened hyalinized intima and adventitia without media. Risk rises with conditions that weaken the vessel wall or raise wall stress: autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD), vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (defective type III collagen), coarctation of the aorta, smoking, hypertension, and a family history."},{"type":"p","text":"Rupture drives arterial blood into the CSF around the brain. Intracranial pressure rises abruptly, producing a sudden, severe headache described as the worst of the patient's life, often with vomiting and brief loss of consciousness. Blood irritates the meninges, causing neck stiffness. CT shows blood in the basal cisterns. If CT is negative, lumbar puncture shows many red cells that do not clear from the first tube to the last, and xanthochromia: red cells lyse, and macrophages convert heme to bilirubin, which colors the supernatant yellow after several hours (reliably by about 12 hours). A traumatic tap gives red cells that decrease across tubes and clear supernatant."},{"type":"flow","title":"Aneurysm rupture and its complications","steps":["Thin media at a branch point balloons into a sac","Rupture fills the subarachnoid space with arterial blood","Thunderclap headache, meningismus, xanthochromic CSF","First day: highest risk of rebleeding","About days 3 to 14: vasospasm causes delayed ischemic infarcts","Blood blocks arachnoid granulations: communicating hydrocephalus"],"caption":"Complications follow a time course, so the day after rupture predicts which one is most likely."},{"type":"p","text":"The complications follow from blood in the CSF. Rebleeding from the unsecured aneurysm is most likely in the first day. Vasospasm develops over about days 3 to 14, peaking near the end of the first week: oxyhemoglobin and its breakdown products scavenge nitric oxide, increase endothelin, and stimulate smooth muscle contraction, narrowing arteries bathed in clot and causing delayed ischemic infarcts. Blood products also clog the arachnoid granulations, impairing CSF absorption and causing communicating hydrocephalus, while clot within the ventricles can obstruct flow. Hyponatremia from SIADH or cerebral salt wasting may also follow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C07","obj":"NE-20-A.7","title":"Hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage and cerebral amyloid angiopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In intraparenchymal hemorrhage, location usually identifies the cause: deep bleeds point to chronic hypertension, and superficial lobar bleeds in an older adult point to cerebral amyloid angiopathy. Other causes include anticoagulation, arteriovenous malformations (C09), bleeding into tumors, sympathomimetic drugs such as cocaine, and hemorrhagic conversion of infarcts."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypertensive hemorrhage arises from the same small penetrating arteries that cause lacunes (C01). Years of high pressure produce hyaline arteriolosclerosis and degeneration of smooth muscle in the media. The weakened wall can either narrow and occlude, producing a lacune, or dilate into Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms, tiny outpouchings of vessels less than about 300 micrometers in diameter, and rupture. The usual sites, in order of frequency in most series, are the putamen and basal ganglia, the thalamus, the pons, and the cerebellar hemispheres. The arterial bleed can keep expanding over the first hours, raising intracranial pressure. Survivors are left with a slit-like cavity lined by hemosiderin-laden macrophages and gliosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The deficit reflects the structures in the path. A putaminal hemorrhage compresses the adjacent internal capsule and causes contralateral hemiparesis and hemisensory loss. A thalamic bleed causes contralateral sensory loss and can extend into the third ventricle. A pontine hemorrhage disrupts the reticular activating system and descending tracts, producing coma, quadriparesis, and pinpoint pupils because descending sympathetic fibers are cut while parasympathetic pupillary tone is preserved. A cerebellar hemorrhage causes ataxia, vertigo, and vomiting and is dangerous because it can compress the fourth ventricle and brainstem in the confined posterior fossa."},{"type":"p","text":"Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA) is deposition of beta-amyloid, the same peptide found in Alzheimer plaques, in the walls of small and medium arteries of the leptomeninges and cortex. The amyloid replaces smooth muscle, leaving rigid, brittle vessels that stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence under polarized light. These vessels lie near the surface, so bleeds are lobar (cortical and subcortical), most often parietal and occipital, and tend to recur in different lobes; small cortical microbleeds accumulate as well. CAA is mainly a disease of older adults, often coexists with Alzheimer disease, and is more likely in carriers of the APOE e4 and e2 alleles."},{"type":"table","title":"Deep versus lobar intraparenchymal hemorrhage","cols":["Feature","Hypertensive","Amyloid angiopathy"],"rows":[["Vessel","Deep penetrating arteries","Leptomeningeal and cortical arteries"],["Wall change","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis, Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms","Beta-amyloid replacing smooth muscle"],["Location","Putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum","Lobar, cortex and subcortical white matter"],["Patient","Long-standing hypertension","Older adult, often with cognitive decline"],["Pattern","Usually a single deep bleed","Recurrent bleeds in different lobes"]],"caption":"Location predicts cause because the two diseases damage different vessels."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C08","obj":"NE-20-A.8","title":"Spinal cord blood supply and anterior spinal artery syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three longitudinal arteries supply the spinal cord. A single anterior spinal artery, formed by branches of both vertebral arteries, runs in the anterior median fissure and supplies the anterior two-thirds of the cord through central (sulcal) branches. Two posterior spinal arteries, from the vertebral or posterior inferior cerebellar arteries, supply the posterior third, mainly the dorsal columns and the back of the dorsal horns. Below the cervical cord these vessels are too small to carry blood the whole length of the cord, so they depend on segmental medullary (radicular) arteries that arrive along spinal nerve roots from the vertebral, intercostal, and lumbar arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"The largest reinforcing vessel is the great anterior segmental medullary artery (artery of Adamkiewicz). It usually arises on the left from a lower posterior intercostal or upper lumbar artery, most often between about T9 and T12, and it supplies much of the lower thoracic and lumbosacral cord. The mid-thoracic cord, between the cervical supply and this artery, receives few feeders and is a vascular border zone."},{"type":"table","title":"Spinal arteries and the structures they supply","cols":["Artery","Structures supplied","Loss when infarcted"],"rows":[["Anterior spinal artery","Anterior horns, lateral and anterior columns (corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts), autonomic pathways","Paralysis, pain and temperature loss, bladder and bowel dysfunction"],["Posterior spinal arteries","Dorsal columns, posterior dorsal horns","Vibration and position sense loss"],["Artery of Adamkiewicz","Lower thoracic and lumbosacral cord via the anterior spinal artery","Infarction of the lower cord with anterior cord syndrome"]],"caption":"The single anterior artery supplies most of the cord, which is why anterior infarction is far more common than posterior infarction."},{"type":"p","text":"Anterior spinal artery syndrome follows interruption of flow to the anterior two-thirds of the cord. The classic causes are aortic surgery with cross-clamping, aortic dissection that shears off intercostal origins, and severe hypotension; atherosclerosis and embolism are less common causes. Onset is sudden, often with back pain. Below the lesion, loss of the lateral corticospinal tracts causes bilateral paralysis, flaccid at first (spinal shock) and later spastic with hyperreflexia and Babinski signs, while loss of the anterior horn cells at the infarcted segments causes lower motor neuron weakness at that level. Loss of the spinothalamic tracts removes pain and temperature sensation bilaterally below the lesion, and loss of descending autonomic pathways causes urinary retention and bowel dysfunction. The dorsal columns, supplied by the posterior arteries, are spared, so vibration and joint position sense remain."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a spinal cord infarct","text":"A 70-year-old wakes after repair of a thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm unable to move his legs. He cannot feel pinprick or cold below the umbilicus, and his bladder is distended, yet he feels a tuning fork at both great toes and knows when they are moved up or down. Bilateral motor and spinothalamic loss with intact dorsal columns places the lesion in the anterior two-thirds of the cord. The umbilicus is the T10 dermatome, matching loss of flow through the artery of Adamkiewicz, whose intercostal origin was excluded during aortic clamping."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C09","obj":"NE-20-A.9","title":"Arteriovenous and other vascular malformations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vascular malformations are developmental errors in how arteries, capillaries, and veins connect. They are a leading cause of intracerebral hemorrhage and new seizures in children and young adults, in whom hypertension and amyloid angiopathy are unlikely."},{"type":"p","text":"An arteriovenous malformation (AVM) is a tangle of abnormal vessels, the nidus, in which arteries drain directly into veins with no capillary bed between them. Without the high resistance of arterioles and capillaries, flow through the shunt is fast and arterial pressure is transmitted into the draining veins. These veins become dilated and arterialized but remain thin-walled, so they can rupture, causing intraparenchymal hemorrhage, sometimes with subarachnoid or intraventricular extension. The low-resistance shunt can also divert flow from surrounding brain (steal), and the gliotic, hemosiderin-stained tissue between the vessels is irritable, so seizures are a common first sign. AVMs most often present between about 10 and 30 years of age, are more common in males, and arise most often in the MCA territory. A bruit may be heard over the skull. Multiple AVMs, including in the lung and liver, suggest hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia, an autosomal dominant disorder of TGF-beta signaling genes."},{"type":"p","text":"Other malformations differ in flow and in whether brain tissue lies between the vessels, which determines their behavior."},{"type":"table","title":"Cerebral vascular malformations compared","cols":["Malformation","Structure","Flow","Usual presentation"],"rows":[["Arteriovenous malformation","Arteries draining directly to veins, gliotic brain between vessels","High","Hemorrhage and seizures in young adults"],["Cavernous malformation","Packed thin-walled sinusoidal channels, no brain between them, hemosiderin rim","Low","Seizures and small recurrent bleeds; may be familial (CCM genes)"],["Capillary telangiectasia","Dilated capillaries separated by normal brain, often in the pons","Low","Usually incidental"],["Developmental venous anomaly (venous angioma)","Enlarged veins draining normal brain","Low","Usually incidental, rarely bleeds"]],"caption":"Flow and the presence or absence of intervening brain explain why AVMs bleed most dangerously."},{"type":"p","text":"Ectatic cerebral vessels are elongated, dilated, and tortuous arteries, most often the basilar artery (dolichoectasia), usually in older adults with long-standing hypertension and atherosclerosis. They cause symptoms by compressing cranial nerves or brainstem, or by allowing thrombus to form in slow, disturbed flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C10","obj":"NE-20-A.10","title":"Cervical artery dissection and subclavian steal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dissection of the internal carotid or vertebral artery is one of the most common causes of stroke in young and middle-aged adults. A tear in the intima lets blood enter the media, forming an intramural hematoma. The hematoma can narrow or occlude the lumen, but stroke more often results from thrombus that forms on the exposed subintimal surface and embolizes downstream. Triggers range from obvious trauma to minor neck movements such as sports, sudden rotation, or neck manipulation. Underlying wall weakness from fibromuscular dysplasia, vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, or Marfan syndrome increases risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Internal carotid dissection usually causes pain in the neck, face, or around the eye, and an ipsilateral partial Horner syndrome. The third-order sympathetic fibers to the eye run in the plexus on the internal carotid wall, so the expanding wall injures them, causing ptosis and miosis. Facial sweating is usually spared because the sudomotor fibers to most of the face travel with the external carotid artery. Emboli then reach the MCA territory. Vertebral dissection, often where the artery winds around the atlas, causes posterior neck or occipital pain followed by posterior circulation ischemia, classically the lateral medullary syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Subclavian steal is a reversal of flow in a vertebral artery. When the subclavian artery is stenosed or occluded proximal to the origin of the vertebral artery, usually by atherosclerosis and more often on the left, pressure in the distal subclavian falls below pressure at the basilar junction. Blood then flows up the opposite vertebral artery, across the basilar junction, and down the vertebral artery on the stenosed side to supply the arm. Exercising the arm lowers its resistance and increases the reversed flow, which can briefly reduce brainstem perfusion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Subclavian steal","steps":["Stenosis of subclavian artery before the vertebral origin","Pressure in distal subclavian falls below basilar pressure","Flow reverses down the ipsilateral vertebral artery","Arm exercise lowers arm resistance and increases reversed flow","Arm claudication, weak pulse, lower blood pressure on that side","Dizziness, vertigo, or syncope from vertebrobasilar insufficiency"],"caption":"The arm draws blood from the posterior circulation because the stenosis lowers pressure in its own supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Findings follow from the pressure drop: systolic pressure lower by more than about 15 to 20 mm Hg in the affected arm, a weaker radial pulse, and sometimes a supraclavicular bruit. Many patients are asymptomatic because collateral flow compensates. Takayasu arteritis is a cause in young women."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-01-C11","obj":"NE-20-A.11","title":"Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, hypertensive encephalopathy, and PRES","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis is clotting in the dural venous sinuses, most often the superior sagittal and transverse sinuses, or in the cortical veins that drain into them. It follows Virchow's triad. Hypercoagulable states dominate: pregnancy and especially the postpartum period, estrogen-containing contraceptives, inherited thrombophilias such as factor V Leiden and the prothrombin G20210A mutation, antiphospholipid syndrome, and malignancy. Dehydration and nearby infection, such as mastoiditis spreading to the sigmoid and transverse sinuses, contribute through stasis and local inflammation. It affects younger adults, women more than men."},{"type":"p","text":"Obstructed outflow raises venous and capillary pressure upstream. Fluid leaks into the brain, capillaries rupture, and the result is edema and hemorrhagic venous infarcts that do not match any arterial territory and are often bilateral and parasagittal when the superior sagittal sinus is blocked. Because the arachnoid granulations drain CSF into the sinuses, absorption also falls, so intracranial pressure rises. Headache is the most common symptom, often with papilledema, vomiting, and sometimes abducens palsy; seizures are more common than in arterial stroke because venous infarcts involve cortex and contain blood. Contrast imaging may show a filling defect within the sinus."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypertensive encephalopathy and posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) are failures of autoregulation. Cerebral arterioles normally hold flow nearly constant across a mean arterial pressure of about 60 to 150 mm Hg by constricting as pressure rises; chronic hypertension shifts this range upward. When pressure rises suddenly above the upper limit, the constriction is overcome, arterioles are forcibly dilated, and the capillary bed is exposed to high pressure. The blood-brain barrier leaks and vasogenic edema forms. The posterior circulation is affected most, probably because vertebrobasilar arterioles receive less sympathetic innervation to limit dilation. In severe hypertensive encephalopathy, fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and petechial hemorrhages appear."},{"type":"flow","title":"Autoregulatory breakthrough in PRES","steps":["Sudden severe hypertension or endothelial injury","Pressure exceeds the upper limit of autoregulation","Arterioles are forcibly dilated; capillary pressure rises","Blood-brain barrier leaks plasma into the interstitium","Vasogenic edema of parieto-occipital white matter","Headache, confusion, visual loss, seizures; usually reversible"],"caption":"The edema is extracellular and reversible if the cause is removed before infarction or hemorrhage develops."},{"type":"p","text":"PRES also occurs without extreme pressure when the endothelium is injured, as in preeclampsia and eclampsia, renal failure, and exposure to calcineurin inhibitors (cyclosporine, tacrolimus) or cytotoxic chemotherapy. Patients have headache, confusion, visual disturbance up to cortical blindness, and seizures. MRI shows bright T2 and FLAIR signal in the parieto-occipital white matter that typically resolves, distinguishing it from infarction."},{"type":"table","title":"Cytotoxic versus vasogenic edema","cols":["Feature","Cytotoxic","Vasogenic"],"rows":[["Mechanism","ATP and Na+/K+ ATPase failure; cells swell","Blood-brain barrier leak; fluid in interstitium"],["Main location","Gray and white matter of the injured territory","Mainly white matter"],["Typical causes","Ischemic infarct, global hypoxia","PRES, hypertensive encephalopathy, tumor, abscess"],["Reversibility","In ischemia, usually marks dying cells","Often reversible"]],"caption":"Infarcts begin with cytotoxic edema and add vasogenic edema as capillaries fail; PRES is primarily vasogenic."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology predict nearly every lesion in this chapter. Arterial territories decide the deficit of an infarct, the ends of those territories decide where hypotension strikes, the dura and its sutures shape extra-axial bleeds, and the single anterior spinal artery explains why the dorsal columns survive cord infarction. Cerebral autoregulation explains why the brain tolerates a wide range of pressures and why both very low pressure (watershed infarcts) and very high pressure (PRES) cause injury. Biochemistry supplies the shared cell death pathway: ATP failure, glutamate excitotoxicity, and Ca2+ overload explain why CA1 and Purkinje neurons die first."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology links the vessel wall to the outcome. Hypertension produces hyaline arteriolosclerosis that ends either in a lacune or a Charcot-Bouchard rupture at the same deep sites, atherosclerosis produces thrombosis and artery-to-artery emboli, and connective tissue and genetic disorders such as ADPKD, vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia explain aneurysms and malformations in young patients. The limits are worth keeping in mind: CT is insensitive to early ischemia, the time course of an infarct varies with its size, and examination alone cannot separate ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke."}],"summary":["Thrombotic infarcts arise on atherosclerotic plaques at the carotid bifurcation, MCA, or basilar artery; embolic infarcts come mainly from the heart, are maximal at onset, and often turn hemorrhagic when flow returns.","Lacunes are small deep infarcts from hypertensive hyaline arteriolosclerosis of penetrating arteries, causing pure motor or pure sensory strokes.","An infarct evolves from red neurons at about 12 to 24 hours to neutrophils, foamy macrophages over weeks, and finally a cyst walled by gliosis from liquefactive necrosis.","About 85% of strokes are ischemic; a TIA leaves no infarct; noncontrast CT comes first because acute blood is immediately bright while early ischemia is often invisible.","Global hypoxia kills CA1 pyramidal cells, Purkinje cells, and cortical layers 3, 5, and 6 first through excitotoxicity; hypotension infarcts the ACA and MCA watershed.","Epidural hematoma is arterial, biconvex, limited by sutures, and may show a lucid interval; subdural hematoma is venous, crescentic, and crosses sutures in atrophic or shaken brains.","Saccular aneurysms at the anterior communicating artery cause thunderclap headache and xanthochromia, then rebleeding, vasospasm at about days 3 to 14, and hydrocephalus.","Deep intraparenchymal hemorrhage points to hypertensive Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms; recurrent lobar hemorrhage in older adults points to amyloid angiopathy.","Anterior spinal artery infarction after aortic surgery causes paralysis, pain and temperature loss, and bladder dysfunction with vibration and position sense spared.","AVMs cause hemorrhage and seizures in young adults; dissection causes stroke with neck pain and Horner syndrome; venous sinus thrombosis and PRES cause edema outside arterial territories."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, cerebrovascular diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, cranial meninges and blood supply of the brain; Back, vasculature of the spinal cord"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral circulation and its autoregulation"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders, stroke"}]},"NE-20-B":{"unit":"NE-20-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02","title":"Degenerative and Demyelinating Diseases of the Nervous System","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers two groups of disease. In neurodegenerative disease, a selected population of neurons dies slowly, usually as a misfolded protein accumulates: Alzheimer, Lewy body, frontotemporal, and supranuclear palsy dementias, ALS, Parkinson, Huntington, and Friedreich ataxia. In demyelinating disease, the immune system strips myelin from axons: multiple sclerosis, ADEM, and transverse myelitis in the central nervous system, and Guillain-Barré syndrome in the peripheral nerves. Vascular dementia, mild cognitive impairment, essential tremor, and cervical dystonia are distinguished from these diseases. Board questions give a pattern of signs and ask for the protein, gene, cell population, or tract responsible. If you know which cells each disease destroys and what those cells normally do, the clinical picture follows.","prerequisites":["Upper versus lower motor neuron anatomy and signs (NE-7)","Basal ganglia direct and indirect pathways and dopamine receptors (NE-3, NE-15)","Myelin, oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells, and saltatory conduction (NE-2, NE-13)","Ascending and descending spinal cord tracts and their crossing points (NE-3, NE-5)","Trinucleotide repeat expansion and patterns of inheritance (NE-16)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neurogenetics (NE-16)","how":"Huntington disease and Friedreich ataxia are the model dominant and recessive trinucleotide repeat disorders, and APP and presenilin genes show how gene dosage alters protein processing."},{"to":"Immune responses in neurological disease (NE-18)","how":"Molecular mimicry and autoreactive T cells explain Guillain-Barré syndrome, ADEM, transverse myelitis, and multiple sclerosis."},{"to":"Diseases of amyloids (IM-15-E)","how":"Amyloid beta is a beta-pleated sheet protein that stains with Congo red, so Alzheimer plaques and amyloid angiopathy follow the general rules of amyloid deposition."},{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Vascular dementia is the cumulative result of infarcts, and amyloid angiopathy causes lobar hemorrhage in older adults."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and endocrine systems","how":"Frataxin deficiency injures cardiomyocytes and pancreatic beta cells, so Friedreich ataxia brings hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and diabetes."}],"terms":[{"term":"Neurodegeneration","def":"Progressive loss of a specific population of neurons, usually associated with intracellular or extracellular aggregates of a misfolded protein."},{"term":"Amyloid beta","def":"A 38 to 43 amino acid peptide cut from amyloid precursor protein by beta and gamma secretase that aggregates into the core of neuritic plaques."},{"term":"Tau","def":"A microtubule-associated protein that stabilizes axonal microtubules and, when hyperphosphorylated, aggregates into neurofibrillary tangles."},{"term":"Lewy body","def":"An eosinophilic intracytoplasmic neuronal inclusion composed mainly of alpha-synuclein."},{"term":"Anticipation","def":"Earlier onset or greater severity of a genetic disease in successive generations, caused by further expansion of an unstable repeat."},{"term":"Hydrocephalus ex vacuo","def":"Passive enlargement of the ventricles to fill space left by brain atrophy, with normal cerebrospinal fluid pressure."},{"term":"Demyelination","def":"Loss of the myelin sheath with relative preservation of the axon, which slows or blocks saltatory conduction."},{"term":"Albuminocytologic dissociation","def":"Raised cerebrospinal fluid protein with a normal white cell count, typical of Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"term":"Oligoclonal bands","def":"Discrete IgG bands on CSF electrophoresis that are absent from serum, showing antibody synthesis within the central nervous system."},{"term":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia","def":"Failure of adduction of one eye on lateral gaze, with nystagmus of the abducting eye, caused by a lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus."},{"term":"Sensory level","def":"A dermatome below which sensation is reduced or lost on both sides, marking a lesion of the spinal cord at or above that segment."},{"term":"Dystonia","def":"Sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contraction that produces twisting movements or abnormal postures."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C01","obj":"NE-20-B.1","title":"Alzheimer disease pathogenesis: amyloid beta, tau, and risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Alzheimer disease (AD) is the most common cause of dementia, meaning an acquired decline in memory and other cognitive abilities severe enough to remove independence in daily life. Two abnormal proteins define it: extracellular amyloid beta, which forms plaques, and intracellular hyperphosphorylated tau, which forms neurofibrillary tangles."},{"type":"h","text":"Processing of amyloid precursor protein"},{"type":"p","text":"Amyloid precursor protein (APP) is a transmembrane neuronal protein encoded on chromosome 21. Most APP is cut first by alpha secretase, which cleaves inside the amyloid beta sequence and so prevents amyloid beta from forming. When beta secretase (BACE1) cuts first, just outside the membrane, gamma secretase then cuts within the membrane and releases amyloid beta. Presenilin is the catalytic subunit of gamma secretase. Most of the peptide is 40 amino acids long, but the more hydrophobic 42 amino acid form aggregates readily, first into soluble oligomers that impair synapses and then into beta-pleated sheet fibrils that build plaques."},{"type":"flow","title":"Amyloidogenic processing of APP","steps":["APP spans the neuronal plasma membrane","Beta secretase cleaves APP just outside the membrane","Gamma secretase (presenilin) cleaves within the membrane","Amyloid beta 40 and 42 are released","Amyloid beta 42 forms oligomers, then fibrils","Fibrils deposit as plaques and in vessel walls"],"caption":"Alpha secretase cleavage, the main normal route, cuts inside the amyloid beta sequence and bypasses this pathway."},{"type":"h","text":"Tau and neuronal death"},{"type":"p","text":"Tau binds and stabilizes axonal microtubules, the tracks for axonal transport. In AD, kinases add excess phosphate groups, so tau detaches, self-aggregates into paired helical filaments, and fills the cell body as a neurofibrillary tangle. Microtubules then fall apart, transport of organelles and vesicles fails, and the neuron dies. Amyloid beta is thought to drive tau pathology, but tangle burden and neuron loss correlate with the severity of dementia much better than plaque burden does."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for Alzheimer disease","cols":["Factor","Mechanism","Pattern"],"rows":[["Age","Cumulative protein aggregation and reduced clearance","Strongest risk; most cases after age 65"],["APOE e4 allele (chromosome 19)","Less efficient clearance and greater deposition of amyloid beta","Late onset; raises risk in a dose-dependent way but does not determine disease; e2 is protective"],["Down syndrome (trisomy 21)","Three copies of APP raise amyloid beta production","Plaques and tangles by about age 40; dementia often in the 40s or 50s"],["APP mutation","Increased total amyloid beta or a higher share of the 42 amino acid form","Autosomal dominant, early onset"],["PSEN1 (chromosome 14) or PSEN2 (chromosome 1) mutation","Altered gamma secretase raises the ratio of amyloid beta 42 to 40","Autosomal dominant, early onset; PSEN1 is the most common familial cause"]],"caption":"Autosomal dominant mutations account for only a small minority of all Alzheimer disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A rare adult with Down syndrome carries a partial trisomy of chromosome 21 that does not include the APP gene. Would you expect early Alzheimer disease?","explain":"No. The early disease in trisomy 21 comes from APP gene dosage, so without an extra APP copy amyloid beta production is not increased.","check":"Compare with the Down syndrome row of the risk factor table and the APP processing flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C02","obj":"NE-20-B.2","title":"Alzheimer disease: gross, microscopic, and clinical findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Grossly, the AD brain shows diffuse cortical atrophy with narrowed gyri and widened sulci. Atrophy is most severe in the medial temporal lobe (hippocampus and entorhinal cortex) and the association cortex of the temporal, parietal, and frontal lobes, while primary motor, sensory, and visual cortex are relatively spared until late. As brain tissue is lost, the ventricles enlarge passively. This is hydrocephalus ex vacuo: cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pressure is normal and there is no obstruction. It differs from normal pressure hydrocephalus, in which the ventricles are enlarged out of proportion to the sulci and the triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and dementia appears."},{"type":"table","title":"Microscopic lesions of Alzheimer disease","cols":["Lesion","Composition and location","Significance"],"rows":[["Neuritic plaque","Extracellular amyloid beta core surrounded by dystrophic tau-containing neurites, microglia, and astrocytes","Density is scored in the pathologic diagnosis; amyloid shows apple-green birefringence with Congo red under polarized light"],["Diffuse plaque","Amyloid beta without surrounding dystrophic neurites","Common in cognitively normal older adults; less specific"],["Neurofibrillary tangle","Intracellular paired helical filaments of hyperphosphorylated tau, flame-shaped in pyramidal neurons; shown by silver stains","Correlates best with dementia; persists as a ghost tangle after the neuron dies"],["Cerebral amyloid angiopathy","Amyloid beta in walls of small and medium cortical and leptomeningeal arteries","Weakens vessels; cause of lobar hemorrhage and microbleeds in older adults"],["Granulovacuolar degeneration and Hirano bodies","Cytoplasmic changes in hippocampal pyramidal neurons","Characteristic but not specific"],["Neuron loss in the nucleus basalis of Meynert","Cholinergic neurons projecting to the cortex","Lowers cortical acetylcholine"]],"caption":"Definitive diagnosis rests on neuropathology showing plaques and tangles in sufficient density and distribution."},{"type":"h","text":"Why memory fails first"},{"type":"p","text":"Tangles appear first in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus and then spread through the limbic system into the neocortex. The hippocampus is needed to form new declarative memories, so the first symptom is loss of short-term memory for recent events, with repetitive questions and misplaced items, while remote memories are preserved. As the neocortex becomes involved, word-finding difficulty (anomia), visuospatial disorientation such as getting lost, impaired judgment and executive function, and apraxia follow. Late disease brings mutism, incontinence, and immobility, and death often results from aspiration pneumonia or other infection, usually several years to a decade after diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Biomarkers reflect the same pathology. CSF amyloid beta 42 is low because the peptide is trapped in plaques rather than circulating, while total and phosphorylated tau are raised as neurons die and release tau. Amyloid PET shows cortical deposits, MRI shows medial temporal atrophy, and FDG-PET shows reduced glucose metabolism in the temporoparietal cortex."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C03","obj":"NE-20-B.3","title":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is progressive degeneration of both upper motor neurons (UMNs) in the motor cortex, with their corticospinal and corticobulbar axons, and lower motor neurons (LMNs) in the anterior horn and brainstem motor nuclei. The name describes the pathology: amyotrophic refers to muscle atrophy from denervation, and lateral sclerosis refers to gliosis and hardening of the lateral columns, where the corticospinal tracts run."},{"type":"p","text":"About 90% of cases are sporadic and about 10% are familial. Mutations in SOD1, which encodes copper-zinc superoxide dismutase on chromosome 21, act through a toxic gain of function: the mutant protein misfolds and aggregates rather than simply losing enzyme activity. A GGGGCC hexanucleotide repeat expansion in C9orf72 is the most common familial cause and also causes frontotemporal dementia, which explains why the two diseases overlap. In nearly all sporadic cases, motor neurons contain cytoplasmic inclusions of the protein TDP-43. Glutamate excitotoxicity, oxidative stress, and impaired axonal transport contribute to cell death."},{"type":"table","title":"Upper versus lower motor neuron signs in ALS","cols":["Feature","UMN loss","LMN loss"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Loss of descending inhibition of spinal reflex circuits","Denervation of muscle fibers"],["Tone","Spasticity","Flaccidity"],["Reflexes","Hyperreflexia, clonus, Babinski sign","Hyporeflexia or areflexia"],["Muscle bulk","Little atrophy (disuse only)","Marked atrophy"],["Spontaneous activity","None","Fasciculations; fibrillation potentials on EMG"],["Bulbar signs","Brisk jaw jerk, spastic dysarthria, pseudobulbar affect","Tongue atrophy and fasciculations, dysphagia"]],"caption":"The diagnostic clue in ALS is UMN and LMN signs together in the same region, often in the same limb."},{"type":"p","text":"ALS is a pure motor disease. Sensory neurons are not part of the motor system, so sensation is normal. The oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nuclei and Onuf nucleus in the sacral cord (S2 to S4), which supplies the voluntary external urethral and anal sphincters, are relatively resistant, so eye movements and bladder and bowel control are usually preserved until very late. A sensory level, sphincter loss, or abnormal eye movements therefore points to another diagnosis, such as cervical myelopathy from spinal canal narrowing, which can also mix UMN and LMN signs."},{"type":"p","text":"Weakness typically begins asymmetrically in a limb or in bulbar muscles and spreads. EMG shows active denervation in several body regions with normal sensory nerve conduction. As phrenic (C3 to C5) and intercostal motor neurons die, ventilation fails, and respiratory failure, often with aspiration, is the usual cause of death, with median survival of about 3 to 5 years from symptom onset."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C04","obj":"NE-20-B.4","title":"Parkinson disease and secondary parkinsonism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta project to the striatum (caudate and putamen). Dopamine excites the direct pathway through D1 receptors and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2 receptors; both effects lower inhibitory output from the globus pallidus internus to the thalamus, so dopamine facilitates movement."},{"type":"p","text":"In Parkinson disease (PD), these pigmented neurons die, so the substantia nigra looks pale on gross section. Surviving neurons contain Lewy bodies, round eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions with a pale halo, composed mainly of aggregated alpha-synuclein. Lewy pathology also affects the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, the olfactory system, and the locus ceruleus, often before the nigra, which explains prodromal loss of smell, constipation, and REM sleep behavior disorder. Motor signs appear only after roughly half of nigral neurons, and most striatal dopamine, are lost. Most PD is sporadic, with misfolded alpha-synuclein, mitochondrial complex I dysfunction, and oxidative stress from dopamine metabolism as central mechanisms; a minority is familial (for example SNCA or LRRK2 mutations, dominant, and PRKN, recessive and early onset)."},{"type":"flow","title":"From nigral cell loss to bradykinesia","steps":["Substantia nigra pars compacta dopaminergic neurons die","Striatal dopamine falls","Direct (D1) pathway weakens; indirect (D2) pathway strengthens","Globus pallidus internus inhibits the thalamus more","Thalamocortical drive to motor cortex falls","Movements become slow, small, and hard to start"],"caption":"Both pathway changes push in the same direction, toward greater thalamic inhibition and less movement."},{"type":"table","title":"Cardinal motor signs of Parkinson disease","cols":["Sign","Description"],"rows":[["Resting tremor","About 4 to 6 Hz pill-rolling tremor, usually asymmetric at onset, that lessens with voluntary movement"],["Rigidity","Increased tone through the full range of passive movement (lead pipe); cogwheel when tremor is superimposed"],["Bradykinesia","Slow, small movements: masked face, reduced blinking, micrographia, soft voice, shuffling gait with reduced arm swing"],["Postural instability","Loss of postural reflexes, usually later in the disease, causing falls"]],"caption":"Bradykinesia plus either resting tremor or rigidity is required to call a syndrome parkinsonism."},{"type":"h","text":"Secondary parkinsonism"},{"type":"p","text":"MPTP, a contaminant of an illicitly synthesized opioid in the early 1980s, produced sudden parkinsonism in young users. It crosses the blood-brain barrier, is oxidized by monoamine oxidase B (MAO-B) in astrocytes to MPP+, and is taken up selectively into nigral neurons by the dopamine transporter. MPP+ inhibits complex I of the electron transport chain, causing ATP failure and free radical injury, a finding that supports the mitochondrial theory of PD. Drug-induced parkinsonism follows D2 receptor blockade (antipsychotics such as haloperidol, and antiemetics such as metoclopramide) or presynaptic dopamine depletion through VMAT inhibition (reserpine, tetrabenazine). Because the nigral neurons are intact, drug-induced parkinsonism tends to be symmetric, lacks Lewy bodies, and usually resolves weeks to months after the drug is stopped."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C05","obj":"NE-20-B.5","title":"Huntington disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Huntington disease (HD) is an autosomal dominant disease caused by expansion of a CAG repeat in the HTT gene on the short arm of chromosome 4. CAG encodes glutamine, so the mutant huntingtin protein carries a long polyglutamine tract. It misfolds and forms nuclear inclusions, disrupts transcription and mitochondrial function, and makes striatal neurons vulnerable to glutamate excitotoxicity through NMDA receptors. This is a toxic gain of function, which is why a single mutant allele causes disease."},{"type":"table","title":"HTT CAG repeat length","cols":["Repeat number","Meaning"],"rows":[["26 or fewer","Normal"],["27 to 35","Intermediate: no disease in the carrier, but may expand into the disease range in offspring, mainly through the father"],["36 to 39","Disease with reduced penetrance"],["40 or more","Full penetrance; longer repeats bring earlier onset"]],"caption":"Repeat length correlates inversely with age of onset; juvenile HD usually has more than about 60 repeats."},{"type":"p","text":"The CAG repeat is unstable during spermatogenesis, which involves many more cell divisions than oogenesis, so it tends to lengthen when passed from a father. The child then develops disease earlier and more severely than the parent, which is anticipation. Juvenile HD, often inherited paternally, presents with rigidity and bradykinesia rather than chorea."},{"type":"p","text":"The neurons lost first are the GABAergic medium spiny neurons of the striatum, the caudate more than the putamen. Caudate atrophy flattens the normally convex lateral walls of the frontal horns, so they look enlarged and box-shaped on imaging. Striatal GABA and acetylcholine fall, while dopamine is relatively preserved, so dopaminergic signaling is relatively excessive. The cortex is affected later."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms usually begin between about 30 and 50 years of age. Chorea consists of brief, irregular, flowing involuntary movements that move from one body part to another, with motor impersistence, such as an inability to keep the tongue protruded or maintain a steady grip. Cognitive decline is a subcortical dementia with slowed thinking and executive failure. Depression, irritability, apathy, and psychosis are common, and the suicide rate is high. Death usually follows about 15 to 20 years after onset."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why Parkinson disease is slow and Huntington disease is excessive","text":"Both diseases act on the same circuit. The globus pallidus internus (GPi) tonically inhibits the thalamus, and the thalamus drives the motor cortex. The direct pathway inhibits GPi, which releases the thalamus. The indirect pathway, through the external pallidum and subthalamic nucleus, excites GPi, which restrains the thalamus. In Parkinson disease, loss of dopamine weakens the direct pathway and disinhibits the indirect pathway, so GPi output rises, the thalamus is suppressed, and movement is reduced. In early Huntington disease, the striatal neurons of the indirect pathway die first. The external pallidum is released and suppresses the subthalamic nucleus, so GPi output falls, the thalamus is released, and unwanted movements (chorea) appear. Later, as direct pathway neurons also die, rigidity and bradykinesia emerge."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C06","obj":"NE-20-B.6","title":"Friedreich ataxia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Friedreich ataxia is the most common inherited ataxia. It is autosomal recessive, and most patients carry an expanded GAA repeat in the first intron of FXN on chromosome 9 on both alleles. Because the repeat lies in an intron, it does not alter the protein sequence. Instead, the long GAA tract forms abnormal DNA structures and silences the gene, so too little normal frataxin is made. This is loss of function, the opposite of the toxic gain of function in Huntington disease, and it explains the recessive inheritance."},{"type":"p","text":"Frataxin is a mitochondrial protein needed to assemble iron-sulfur clusters, which are cofactors for aconitase and for complexes I, II, and III of the electron transport chain. Without it, iron accumulates inside mitochondria, iron-sulfur enzymes fail, ATP production falls, and free iron generates hydroxyl radicals through the Fenton reaction. The cells hurt most are large, metabolically active, nondividing cells: large dorsal root ganglion neurons, cardiomyocytes, and pancreatic beta cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"From GAA expansion to cell injury","steps":["GAA repeats expand in intron 1 of both FXN alleles","Transcription of FXN is silenced","Frataxin is deficient in mitochondria","Iron-sulfur cluster enzymes fail; mitochondrial iron accumulates","ATP falls and oxidative damage rises","Dorsal root ganglion neurons, cardiomyocytes, and beta cells degenerate"],"caption":"Loss of frataxin injures tissues with high oxidative demand and little capacity to regenerate."},{"type":"table","title":"Structures damaged in Friedreich ataxia and the resulting findings","cols":["Structure","Finding"],"rows":[["Dorsal root ganglion neurons and dorsal columns","Loss of vibration and joint position sense, positive Romberg sign, sensory ataxia"],["Large peripheral sensory axons","Areflexia, because the afferent limb of the stretch reflex is lost"],["Spinocerebellar tracts and dentate nucleus","Gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus"],["Corticospinal tracts","Leg weakness and extensor plantar responses (Babinski sign)"],["Myocardium","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia, the most common cause of death"],["Pancreatic beta cells","Diabetes mellitus"],["Musculoskeletal system","Kyphoscoliosis and pes cavus (high arch) with hammer toes, from muscle imbalance during growth"]],"caption":"Absent ankle reflexes with a Babinski sign is a characteristic combination: afferent loss abolishes the reflex while corticospinal loss produces the extensor plantar response."},{"type":"p","text":"Onset is usually in late childhood or adolescence, typically before age 25, with progressive gait ataxia as the first sign. Longer repeats, particularly on the shorter of the two alleles, bring earlier onset and more frequent cardiomyopathy. Most patients need a wheelchair within about 10 to 15 years of onset. Cognition is generally preserved."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C07","obj":"NE-20-B.7","title":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) is an acute immune-mediated polyradiculoneuropathy, meaning it injures spinal nerve roots and peripheral nerves together. Its most common form in North America and Europe is acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (AIDP). About two thirds of patients report an infection 1 to 4 weeks earlier. Campylobacter jejuni gastroenteritis is the most commonly identified trigger; others include cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, influenza, and Zika virus, and vaccination is a rare trigger."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism is molecular mimicry. Campylobacter lipooligosaccharide resembles gangliosides such as GM1 and GD1a on peripheral nerve, so antibodies raised against the bacterium bind the nerve. Complement activation and macrophages then strip myelin from Schwann cells, mainly in AIDP, or attack the axolemma at the nodes of Ranvier in the axonal variants (acute motor axonal neuropathy, linked to anti-GM1 antibodies after Campylobacter infection). Nerve roots are especially vulnerable because their blood-nerve barrier is weaker. Schwann cells can remyelinate, so most patients recover over weeks to months."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of Guillain-Barré syndrome","steps":["Infection, often Campylobacter jejuni, 1 to 4 weeks earlier","Microbial antigens resemble nerve gangliosides","Antibodies, complement, and macrophages attack roots and nerves","Myelin is stripped; conduction slows or blocks","Ascending symmetric flaccid weakness with areflexia","Phrenic and autonomic fibers involved in severe cases"],"caption":"The same immune attack explains the weakness, the lost reflexes, and the life-threatening complications."},{"type":"p","text":"Weakness is symmetric, usually begins in the legs, and ascends over days, reaching its worst point within 4 weeks. It is flaccid with reduced or absent reflexes, because conduction fails in both the afferent and efferent limbs of the stretch reflex. Distal tingling and back pain are common, but objective sensory loss is mild, with no sensory level and no early sphincter loss. Bilateral facial weakness is frequent. The Miller Fisher variant, associated with anti-GQ1b antibodies, presents with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications and diagnostic findings","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Respiratory failure","Demyelination of phrenic (C3 to C5) and intercostal nerves weakens the diaphragm and chest wall; a substantial minority of patients need ventilation"],["Autonomic instability","Involvement of autonomic fibers causes tachycardia or bradycardia, labile blood pressure, ileus, and urinary retention"],["CSF albuminocytologic dissociation","Inflamed roots leak protein into CSF, but few cells enter; protein may still be normal in the first week"],["Nerve conduction studies","Slowed conduction velocity, prolonged distal latencies, conduction block, and delayed or absent F waves (proximal root involvement)"]],"caption":"Autonomic instability and respiratory failure are the main causes of death in GBS."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with weakness of both legs has a sharp sensory level at the umbilicus and urinary retention. Is this Guillain-Barré syndrome?","explain":"Unlikely. A sensory level and early sphincter dysfunction localize to the spinal cord, whereas GBS damages roots and nerves, causing symmetric weakness and areflexia with little objective sensory loss and no level.","check":"Compare with the clinical paragraph here and with transverse myelitis (NE-20-B.12)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C08","obj":"NE-20-B.8","title":"Multiple sclerosis: risk, course, and findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a chronic immune-mediated disease of the central nervous system (CNS) in which plaques of demyelination appear in different places at different times, described as dissemination in space and time. Autoreactive CD4 T cells of the Th1 and Th17 types cross the blood-brain barrier, recognize myelin antigens, and recruit macrophages and microglia, while B cells and antibodies contribute. Oligodendrocytes, which make CNS myelin, are injured, and axons are transected within plaques. Axonal loss, not demyelination alone, accounts for permanent disability."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for multiple sclerosis","cols":["Factor","Comment"],"rows":[["Female sex and age 20 to 40","Women are affected about two to three times as often as men"],["Higher latitude","Prevalence rises with distance from the equator, linked to lower sunlight exposure and vitamin D"],["Epstein-Barr virus infection","Prior infection is strongly associated with MS"],["HLA-DR2 (DRB1 15:01) and Northern European ancestry","Strongest genetic risk factor"],["Smoking","Raises risk and progression"]],"caption":"MS is thought to arise when genetically susceptible people meet environmental triggers."},{"type":"p","text":"About 85% of patients begin with a relapsing-remitting course. A relapse develops over days and resolves partly or fully over weeks as inflammation subsides, sodium channels redistribute along the demyelinated axon, and some remyelination occurs. Many later enter a secondary progressive phase, and about 10 to 15% have a primary progressive course from the start."},{"type":"p","text":"Myelin allows saltatory conduction because sodium channels are concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier. When myelin is lost, current leaks across the bare internode, so conduction slows or fails. Raised body temperature shortens the action potential and reduces the current available to cross the lesion, so symptoms worsen with exercise, fever, or a hot bath (Uhthoff phenomenon). Bending the neck stretches demyelinated cervical dorsal columns and produces an electric sensation down the spine or limbs (Lhermitte sign)."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in multiple sclerosis","cols":["Finding","Lesion and explanation"],"rows":[["Optic neuritis","Optic nerve: painful monocular vision loss, central scotoma, reduced color vision, relative afferent pupillary defect"],["Internuclear ophthalmoplegia","Medial longitudinal fasciculus; bilateral INO in a young adult strongly suggests MS"],["Sensory loss and paresthesias","Dorsal columns and spinothalamic tracts"],["Weakness, spasticity, Babinski sign","Corticospinal tracts"],["Charcot triad: nystagmus, intention tremor, scanning speech","Cerebellum and its connections"],["Urinary urgency and incontinence","Spinal pathways to the bladder; detrusor overactivity"],["Heat-worsened symptoms","Uhthoff phenomenon, from conduction block in demyelinated axons"]],"caption":"Symptoms map to white matter tracts, not to vascular territories."},{"type":"p","text":"MRI shows ovoid T2 and FLAIR hyperintense plaques in periventricular white matter, often oriented perpendicular to the ventricles (Dawson fingers), and in juxtacortical, infratentorial, and spinal cord regions. Gadolinium enhancement marks active blood-brain barrier breakdown, so enhancing and nonenhancing lesions on one scan show dissemination in time. CSF shows oligoclonal IgG bands absent from serum and a raised IgG index, evidence of antibody synthesis within the CNS."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing internuclear ophthalmoplegia","text":"A 28-year-old woman looks to the right. Her right eye abducts with nystagmus, but her left eye fails to adduct. Convergence is normal. For rightward gaze, the right abducens nucleus fires the right lateral rectus and, through interneurons whose axons cross and ascend in the left medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), the left oculomotor nucleus to fire the left medial rectus. Adduction works during convergence, so the left oculomotor nucleus and nerve are intact. The lesion is therefore in the left MLF, named for the side of the eye that cannot adduct: a left INO. In a young adult, especially if bilateral, the classic cause is an MS plaque; in an older adult, a brainstem infarct is more likely."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C09","obj":"NE-20-B.9","title":"Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis versus multiple sclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is an acute, usually single episode of immune-mediated inflammatory demyelination of the brain and spinal cord. It occurs mostly in children and typically follows a viral infection by days to a few weeks; measles, varicella, rubella, mumps, influenza, and nonspecific upper respiratory infections are classic antecedents. Vaccination is a rare trigger. Older rabies vaccines grown in animal nervous tissue caused it by injecting myelin antigens, the same principle used to induce experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis in animals."},{"type":"p","text":"Molecular mimicry or nonspecific activation of the immune system generates T cells that react against myelin, and the attack occurs once. Microscopically, sleeves of lymphocytes and macrophages surround small veins, with demyelination confined to these perivenular zones, and all lesions are the same age. MS, by contrast, forms confluent plaques with sharp borders, and lesions of different ages coexist. Acute hemorrhagic leukoencephalitis is a rare, hyperacute and often fatal form with necrotizing vasculitis of venules and hemorrhage."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically, ADEM produces sudden multifocal deficits together with encephalopathy (confusion, drowsiness, or behavioral change), often with fever, headache, and seizures. Encephalopathy is required for the diagnosis and is unusual in an MS relapse. A subset of children have antibodies against myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG). New demyelinating events more than about 3 months later prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis, including MS."},{"type":"table","title":"ADEM compared with multiple sclerosis","cols":["Feature","ADEM","Multiple sclerosis"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Child","Young adult woman"],["Trigger","Recent viral infection, rarely vaccination","None identifiable; genetic and environmental risk"],["Course","Monophasic","Relapsing-remitting or progressive"],["Encephalopathy","Required","Rare"],["Pathology","Perivenular demyelination, lesions all the same age","Confluent sharply bordered plaques of different ages"],["MRI","Large, poorly marginated, bilateral lesions, often involving the thalami and basal ganglia; all enhance alike if they enhance","Ovoid periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, and cord lesions; mixed enhancing and nonenhancing lesions"],["CSF","Lymphocytic pleocytosis and raised protein; oligoclonal bands usually absent or transient","Persistent oligoclonal bands and raised IgG index"],["Outcome","Usually good recovery","Accumulating disability over years"]],"caption":"One attack after an infection in a child favors ADEM; repeated attacks over years in a young adult favor MS."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C10","obj":"NE-20-B.10","title":"Dementia with Lewy bodies, frontotemporal dementia, and progressive supranuclear palsy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each neurodegenerative dementia is defined by the protein that aggregates and the region where it collects. Alpha-synuclein diseases are synucleinopathies, tau diseases are tauopathies, and a third group accumulates TDP-43. The region involved first decides the earliest symptoms."},{"type":"h","text":"Dementia with Lewy bodies"},{"type":"p","text":"In dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB), alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies form in limbic and neocortical neurons as well as in the brainstem. Cortical Lewy bodies are less distinct than nigral ones and lack the halo, and coexisting AD pathology is common. Core features are fluctuating cognition with marked swings in attention and alertness, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behavior disorder, and parkinsonism. Attention, executive, and visuospatial deficits appear early, while memory is relatively spared at first. Patients are highly sensitive to D2-blocking drugs, which can sharply worsen rigidity and confusion. By convention, dementia that begins before or within 1 year of parkinsonism is DLB, while dementia that appears more than a year after established PD is Parkinson disease dementia."},{"type":"h","text":"Frontotemporal dementia"},{"type":"p","text":"Frontotemporal dementia (FTD) results from frontotemporal lobar degeneration, with atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes that can thin gyri to a knife edge. Onset is often between 45 and 65, younger than typical AD. The behavioral variant causes early disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsive behavior, and hyperorality; language variants cause progressive aphasia. Memory and visuospatial skills are relatively preserved early, the reverse of AD. Pathology is either tau, including Pick disease with round silver-staining Pick bodies and swollen Pick cells, or TDP-43 inclusions, the group that includes C9orf72 cases and links FTD with ALS."},{"type":"h","text":"Progressive supranuclear palsy"},{"type":"p","text":"Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) is a tauopathy with neurofibrillary tangles and tufted astrocytes in the subthalamic nucleus, globus pallidus, substantia nigra, midbrain tectum, and dentate nucleus. Midbrain atrophy produces the hummingbird sign on sagittal MRI. Damage to midbrain vertical gaze centers causes vertical gaze palsy, downgaze first; it is supranuclear because the oculocephalic reflex still moves the eyes. Axial rigidity with neck extension and early unexplained backward falls, usually within the first year, are typical, with dysarthria, dysphagia, and frontal executive decline. Tremor is uncommon, and response to dopamine replacement is poor because striatal and pallidal neurons are also lost."},{"type":"table","title":"Dementias defined by protein and region","cols":["Disease","Protein","Main region","Early clue"],"rows":[["Alzheimer disease","Amyloid beta and tau","Hippocampus, temporoparietal cortex","Short-term memory loss"],["Dementia with Lewy bodies","Alpha-synuclein","Limbic and neocortex, brainstem","Visual hallucinations, fluctuating attention, parkinsonism"],["Frontotemporal dementia","Tau (Pick bodies) or TDP-43","Frontal and anterior temporal lobes","Personality and behavior change or aphasia"],["Progressive supranuclear palsy","Tau","Midbrain, subthalamic nucleus, pallidum, dentate","Vertical gaze palsy and early backward falls"]],"caption":"Lewy bodies are alpha-synuclein; Pick bodies are tau."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C11","obj":"NE-20-B.11","title":"Vascular dementia and mild cognitive impairment","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vascular dementia is cognitive decline caused by cerebrovascular disease and is the second most common dementia after AD. In multi-infarct dementia, repeated cortical or subcortical infarcts each remove a block of tissue, so the course is stepwise: an abrupt drop after each stroke, then a plateau or partial recovery. A single infarct in a strategic site, such as the thalamus, can also cause dementia. Diffuse small vessel disease from hypertensive arteriolosclerosis causes lacunes and widespread ischemic white matter injury, the subcortical form also called Binswanger disease, and can produce a more gradual decline. Risk factors are those of stroke: hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, smoking, and atrial fibrillation."},{"type":"p","text":"Vascular injury mainly disconnects frontal-subcortical circuits, so slowed processing, poor attention, and executive dysfunction dominate, and memory retrieval is impaired more than storage. In AD, hippocampal damage prevents encoding, so cues and recognition do not rescue recall. Vascular dementia is accompanied by focal signs such as hemiparesis, a Babinski sign, a short-stepped gait, pseudobulbar affect, and urinary urgency, and imaging shows infarcts and white matter hyperintensities. Many older adults have both AD and vascular pathology (mixed dementia)."},{"type":"p","text":"Mild cognitive impairment (MCI) is a decline in one or more cognitive domains greater than expected for age and education, reported by the person or an informant and confirmed by testing, with independence in daily activities preserved. Complex tasks may take longer, but the person still manages them. That preserved function is what separates MCI from dementia. MCI is a risk state rather than a disease: the amnestic type in particular often progresses to AD, at about 10 to 15% per year in clinic populations, while some people remain stable or improve. Reversible contributors such as depression, hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, sedating medications, and sleep apnea must be considered."},{"type":"table","title":"Normal aging, mild cognitive impairment, and dementia compared","cols":["Feature","Normal aging","MCI","Alzheimer disease","Vascular dementia"],"rows":[["Cognitive testing","Within norms; slower recall","Below expected for age and education","Impaired in memory and other domains","Impaired, especially executive and processing speed"],["Daily function","Independent","Independent","Lost","Lost"],["Onset and course","Very slow","Gradual; may progress, remain stable, or revert","Insidious and steadily progressive","Often abrupt and stepwise; gradual with small vessel disease"],["Memory pattern","Occasional retrieval lapses","Often amnestic","Encoding failure; cues do not help","Retrieval failure; cues help"],["Neurologic examination","Normal","Normal","Normal early","Focal signs common"]],"caption":"Function decides dementia versus MCI; time course and focal signs separate vascular from Alzheimer dementia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C12","obj":"NE-20-B.12","title":"Transverse myelitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Transverse myelitis is acute or subacute inflammation across both halves of one or a few segments of the spinal cord, most often in the thoracic cord. Deficits develop over hours to days and peak within about 3 weeks. Many cases are postinfectious or idiopathic, following viral infections (herpes simplex, varicella zoster, Epstein-Barr, cytomegalovirus, enteroviruses) or Mycoplasma. Others occur with MS, neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (aquaporin-4 antibodies), MOG antibody disease, or systemic autoimmune disease such as systemic lupus erythematosus, Sjögren syndrome, and sarcoidosis. Lymphocytes and macrophages infiltrate the cord, damaging myelin, axons, and gray matter."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the lesion crosses the cord, every long tract passing through that level is interrupted on both sides, which explains the three signature findings."},{"type":"table","title":"Cord structures injured and resulting findings","cols":["Structure","Finding below the lesion"],"rows":[["Both lateral corticospinal tracts","Bilateral weakness (paraparesis with thoracic lesions, quadriparesis with cervical lesions); flaccid with absent reflexes during spinal shock (motor neurons below the lesion are briefly unexcitable), later spastic with hyperreflexia and Babinski signs"],["Spinothalamic tracts and dorsal columns","Sensory level: reduced pain, temperature, vibration, and position sense below a dermatome, often with a band of altered sensation at the level"],["Descending autonomic pathways to sacral and thoracolumbar centers","Bladder dysfunction: early urinary retention, later urgency and incontinence from detrusor overactivity, plus bowel and sexual dysfunction"],["Inflamed segment itself","Back pain at the level of the lesion"]],"caption":"Bilateral weakness, a sensory level, and bladder dysfunction together localize to the spinal cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Compression of the cord by an epidural abscess, tumor, hematoma, or disc produces the same picture and must be excluded with MRI. In transverse myelitis, MRI shows T2 hyperintensity within the cord, often enhancing, and CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis and raised protein, sometimes with a raised IgG index. Lesion length helps identify the cause: MS typically produces partial, asymmetric lesions shorter than two vertebral segments in the peripheral cord, whereas neuromyelitis optica produces complete, central lesions spanning three or more segments (longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis). The sensory level distinguishes transverse myelitis from GBS, which has no level, and an anterior spinal artery infarct differs by its sudden onset and sparing of the dorsal columns."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why are reflexes absent in the legs in the first days after severe transverse myelitis, even though the corticospinal tracts are cut?","explain":"Spinal shock: abrupt loss of descending input leaves spinal motor neurons and interneurons below the lesion transiently unexcitable. As they recover over days to weeks, UMN signs of spasticity and hyperreflexia appear.","check":"Compare with the corticospinal row of the cord structures table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-02-C13","obj":"NE-20-B.13","title":"Essential tremor, other tremors, and cervical dystonia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A tremor is an involuntary, rhythmic oscillation of a body part produced by alternating or synchronous contraction of opposing muscles. Tremors are classified by when they occur: at rest, while holding a posture against gravity, or during movement (kinetic tremor, which includes intention tremor that worsens near a target). The condition under which a tremor appears points to the circuit at fault."},{"type":"p","text":"Essential tremor is the most common movement disorder. It is often familial with an autosomal dominant pattern. It causes a bilateral, fairly symmetric postural and action tremor of the hands and forearms, often with head tremor (yes-yes or no-no) and voice tremor. It worsens with stress, fatigue, and caffeine, and in many patients it improves briefly after a small amount of alcohol. Other neurologic signs are classically absent. Handwriting is large and shaky, in contrast to the small handwriting (micrographia) of Parkinson disease. The tremor is thought to arise from abnormal oscillation in the cerebello-thalamo-cortical circuit."},{"type":"table","title":"Tremors compared","cols":["Feature","Parkinsonian resting tremor","Essential tremor","Cerebellar intention tremor"],"rows":[["When present","At rest; lessens with voluntary movement","Holding a posture and during action","During goal-directed movement; worse near the target"],["Frequency","About 4 to 6 Hz","About 4 to 12 Hz","Usually below 5 Hz"],["Distribution","Asymmetric hand, pill-rolling; may involve jaw or legs, rarely head","Bilateral hands; head and voice often","Limb on the same side as the cerebellar lesion"],["Associated signs","Rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability","None classically","Dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, ataxia, nystagmus"],["Lesion or cause","Loss of substantia nigra dopaminergic neurons","Often familial; cerebello-thalamo-cortical oscillation","Ipsilateral cerebellar hemisphere, dentate nucleus, or superior cerebellar peduncle (MS, stroke, alcohol)"],["Effect of alcohol","No consistent effect","Often transient improvement","Worsens with alcohol intoxication"]],"caption":"Ask when the tremor appears: at rest, while holding a posture, or while reaching a target."},{"type":"p","text":"Dystonia is sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contraction that produces twisting movements and abnormal postures. Cervical dystonia is the most common focal dystonia in adults, usually beginning between about 30 and 50 years and more often in women. Rotation of the head is torticollis; tilt, flexion, and extension are laterocollis, anterocollis, and retrocollis. Contraction of one sternocleidomastoid turns the face toward the opposite side, usually with the contralateral splenius capitis. Neck pain and a jerky head tremor are common, and a light touch to the chin or face (a sensory trick) can briefly reduce the contraction. Imaging is usually normal; the disorder reflects abnormal basal ganglia output, reduced inhibition in motor circuits, and faulty sensorimotor integration. It differs from congenital muscular torticollis, fibrosis of one sternocleidomastoid in an infant, and from acute dystonic reactions caused by D2-blocking drugs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Across this chapter, the same few principles recur. Neurodegenerative diseases are protein-folding diseases: amyloid beta and tau in AD, alpha-synuclein in PD and DLB, tau in PSP and some FTD, TDP-43 in most ALS and the rest of FTD, and polyglutamine huntingtin in HD. Biochemistry explains the selectivity: nigral neurons handle oxidizable dopamine, frataxin-deficient cells fail because their mitochondria cannot build iron-sulfur clusters, and long neurons depend on intact microtubule transport. Genetics explains the inheritance: toxic gain of function (HTT, SOD1, APP) acts dominantly, while loss of function (FXN) is recessive, and unstable repeats explain anticipation in HD. Anatomy then turns each lesion into signs, from the basal ganglia pathways to the MLF and the long tracts of the cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology unifies the demyelinating diseases, but the target cell sets the pattern. Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons, so MS, ADEM, and transverse myelitis cause tract signs, sensory levels, and UMN findings, while Schwann cells myelinate peripheral nerves, so GBS causes flaccid areflexic weakness. Molecular mimicry links infection to GBS and ADEM, and intrathecal antibody synthesis gives MS its oligoclonal bands. Limits matter: overlap pathology is common in older brains, clinical diagnosis during life is probabilistic, and certainty about protein type often comes only at autopsy."}],"summary":["In AD, beta then gamma secretase (presenilin) cleave APP into amyloid beta 42, which forms plaques; hyperphosphorylated tau forms tangles that correlate with dementia.","AD risk rises with age, APOE e4, trisomy 21 (extra APP), and dominant APP, PSEN1, or PSEN2 mutations; hippocampal involvement makes short-term memory loss the first symptom.","AD atrophy enlarges the ventricles passively (hydrocephalus ex vacuo), and amyloid angiopathy causes lobar hemorrhage in older adults.","ALS combines UMN and LMN signs in the same region with sensation, sphincters, and eye movements spared, and ends in respiratory failure.","PD is loss of nigral dopaminergic neurons with alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies, increasing thalamic inhibition; MPTP (via MPP+ and complex I) and D2 blockade cause secondary parkinsonism.","HD is an autosomal dominant CAG expansion in HTT on chromosome 4 that destroys caudate GABAergic neurons, causing chorea, dementia, and depression, with paternal anticipation.","Friedreich ataxia is an autosomal recessive intronic GAA expansion in FXN causing frataxin loss and mitochondrial iron overload, with ataxia, areflexia plus Babinski signs, and fatal hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","GBS is post-infectious molecular mimicry against peripheral myelin, causing ascending flaccid areflexic weakness and high CSF protein with normal cells; respiratory and autonomic failure kill.","MS affects women aged 20 to 40 at higher latitudes with relapsing plaques of different ages: optic neuritis, INO, heat-worsened symptoms, periventricular MRI lesions, and oligoclonal bands; ADEM is a single perivenular attack in a child after infection.","Lewy bodies with hallucinations and fluctuations mean DLB, early personality change means FTD, vertical gaze palsy with falls means PSP, stepwise decline means vascular dementia, and preserved function means MCI; tremor at rest, with posture, or near a target points to PD, essential tremor, or cerebellar disease."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System: neurodegenerative diseases and demyelinating diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles: Guillain-Barré syndrome"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contributions of the Cerebellum and Basal Ganglia to Overall Motor Control"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immunologic Tolerance and Autoimmunity"},{"book":"Lippincott Biochemistry","where":"Structure of Proteins: protein misfolding and amyloid diseases"}]},"NE-20-C":{"unit":"NE-20-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-03","title":"Diseases of Increased Intracranial Fluid: Cerebral Edema and Hydrocephalus","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The adult skull is a rigid container, so any extra water in brain tissue (cerebral edema) or extra cerebrospinal fluid (hydrocephalus) must displace something else before pressure rises. This chapter explains the three main types of cerebral edema, how rising intracranial pressure produces papilledema, the Cushing triad, and herniation, and how blocked flow or failed absorption of CSF enlarges the ventricles. It then contrasts normal pressure hydrocephalus and idiopathic intracranial hypertension, two disorders that look paradoxical until the pressure physics is clear. Board questions usually give a scan, a pupil, a gait, or an opening pressure and ask you to name the mechanism or the site of obstruction.","prerequisites":["CSF production, circulation, and absorption, and the ventricular system (NE-4, NE-10)","Structure of the blood-brain barrier: endothelial tight junctions, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet","Na+/K+ ATPase and osmotic water movement across cell membranes","Dural folds (falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli) and the foramen magnum (NE-3, NE-4)","Course and functions of CN II, III, and VI (NE-8)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Large infarcts and intracerebral or subarachnoid hemorrhage cause edema, raised pressure, and later communicating hydrocephalus."},{"to":"Neurological neoplasms (NE-20-F)","how":"Tumors cause vasogenic edema and, when they sit in or near the ventricles, obstructive hydrocephalus."},{"to":"Infections of the CNS (NE-20-G)","how":"Abscesses produce vasogenic edema, and meningitis scars the arachnoid granulations and causes communicating hydrocephalus."},{"to":"Congenital neurological diseases (NE-20-E, NE-1)","how":"Aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II malformation with myelomeningocele, and Dandy-Walker malformation are developmental causes of noncommunicating hydrocephalus."},{"to":"Cardiovascular physiology","how":"Cerebral perfusion pressure depends on mean arterial pressure, and the Cushing reflex uses sympathetic outflow and the baroreceptor reflex."}],"terms":[{"term":"Intracranial pressure (ICP)","def":"The pressure inside the skull, normally about 5 to 15 mmHg in a resting supine adult."},{"term":"Monro-Kellie doctrine","def":"Because the skull volume is fixed, an increase in brain, blood, CSF, or a mass must be offset by a decrease in one of the others."},{"term":"Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)","def":"The pressure driving blood through the brain, equal to mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure."},{"term":"Vasogenic edema","def":"Extracellular brain edema caused by breakdown of the blood-brain barrier, mainly in white matter."},{"term":"Cytotoxic edema","def":"Intracellular swelling of neurons and glia when energy failure stops the Na+/K+ ATPase, with the barrier initially intact."},{"term":"Interstitial edema","def":"Movement of CSF across the ependyma into periventricular white matter when ventricular pressure is high, as in obstructive hydrocephalus."},{"term":"Papilledema","def":"Swelling of the optic discs caused by raised intracranial pressure, usually bilateral."},{"term":"Cushing triad","def":"Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular breathing caused by brainstem compression and ischemia, a late sign of raised ICP."},{"term":"Herniation","def":"Displacement of brain tissue across a dural fold or through the foramen magnum by a pressure gradient between compartments."},{"term":"Communicating hydrocephalus","def":"Ventricular enlargement in which CSF passes freely from the ventricles to the subarachnoid space but is not absorbed normally."},{"term":"Hydrocephalus ex vacuo","def":"Enlargement of the ventricles that simply fills the space left by lost brain tissue, with normal pressure."},{"term":"False localizing sign","def":"A deficit, such as a CN VI palsy, caused by raised pressure or brain shift rather than by a lesion at the site the deficit suggests."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-03-C01","obj":"NE-20-C.1","title":"Cerebral edema, raised intracranial pressure, and its complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adult cranial vault holds roughly 80% brain, 10% blood, and 10% CSF. By the Monro-Kellie doctrine, added volume is first buffered by shifting CSF into the spinal canal and blood out of the venous sinuses. Once that reserve is used up, each further milliliter raises ICP steeply, so patients can deteriorate abruptly after a stable period."},{"type":"h","text":"Three edema types, defined by where water sits"},{"type":"p","text":"In vasogenic edema the tight junctions of brain capillaries open and protein-rich plasma leaks into the extracellular space. The fluid spreads along loosely packed white matter tracts while dense cortex is relatively spared. This is the edema around tumors (whose new vessels lack a normal barrier), abscesses, and contusions, and it is why glucocorticoids, which reduce tumor capillary permeability, shrink peritumoral edema but not ischemic edema."},{"type":"p","text":"In cytotoxic edema the barrier is initially intact. Ischemia or hypoxia depletes ATP, the Na+/K+ ATPase stops, Na+ accumulates inside neurons and astrocytes, and water follows. Both gray and white matter swell within minutes, and because water is trapped inside cells, diffusion-weighted MRI shows restricted diffusion, the earliest imaging sign of infarction. Within days the injured barrier leaks, so a large infarct becomes mixed edema that usually peaks around days 3 to 5. Interstitial edema arises when high ventricular pressure in obstructive hydrocephalus drives CSF across the ependyma into periventricular white matter."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of cerebral edema","cols":["Feature","Vasogenic","Cytotoxic","Interstitial"],"rows":[["Defect","Barrier breakdown","Na+/K+ ATPase failure","High ventricular pressure"],["Water location","Extracellular, white matter","Intracellular, gray and white","Periventricular white matter"],["Causes","Tumor, abscess, contusion","Ischemia, hypoxia","Obstructive hydrocephalus"],["Diffusion MRI","Increased","Restricted","Increased"]],"caption":"Osmotic edema (acute hyponatremia) is sometimes listed as a fourth type."},{"type":"h","text":"Complications explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Papilledema develops because the subarachnoid space extends along the optic nerve sheath. Raised CSF pressure compresses axons at the lamina cribrosa and blocks axoplasmic flow, so the axons and disc swell over hours to days, enlarging the blind spot while sparing acuity until late. Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure (MAP) minus ICP, so rising ICP threatens brainstem perfusion, and the Cushing reflex raises MAP to restore it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rising ICP to the Cushing triad","steps":["CSF and venous buffering exhausted","ICP rises, cerebral perfusion pressure falls","Brainstem ischemia drives sympathetic discharge: hypertension","Baroreceptors respond: vagal bradycardia","Medullary compression: irregular respiration"],"caption":"The hypertension is compensatory; the bradycardia is a baroreflex."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: perfusion pressure as ICP rises","text":"With MAP 90 mmHg and ICP 15 mmHg, CPP is 75 mmHg. A hematoma raises ICP to 45 mmHg; with MAP unchanged, CPP falls to 45 mmHg, below the autoregulatory range, and the brainstem becomes ischemic. Sympathetic discharge raises MAP to 130 mmHg, restoring CPP to 85 mmHg, and the baroreflex slows the heart. The hypertension protects perfusion rather than causing harm."},{"type":"p","text":"Herniation occurs when pressure differs across the falx, tentorium, or foramen magnum, and each syndrome follows from neighboring structures. Pupillary fibers lie on the outer surface of CN III, so in uncal herniation an ipsilateral dilated pupil precedes ptosis and a down-and-out eye."},{"type":"table","title":"Herniation syndromes","cols":["Type","What moves","Structure compressed","Result"],"rows":[["Subfalcine","Cingulate gyrus under falx","Anterior cerebral artery","Contralateral leg weakness"],["Uncal","Medial temporal lobe over tentorium","Ipsilateral CN III, PCA, cerebral peduncle","Ipsilateral dilated pupil, contralateral hemianopia, contralateral hemiparesis"],["Kernohan notch","Midbrain against opposite tentorium","Contralateral peduncle","Hemiparesis on side of mass (false localizing)"],["Central","Diencephalon, midbrain downward","Paramedian basilar branches","Duret hemorrhages, coma"],["Tonsillar","Tonsils through foramen magnum","Medulla","Respiratory arrest"]],"caption":"Lumbar puncture with a mass present can drive tonsillar herniation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A left temporal mass causes a dilated left pupil and right weakness. What does a new right homonymous hemianopia mean?","explain":"The uncus can compress the left posterior cerebral artery, infarcting the left occipital lobe, which serves the right visual field.","check":"The uncal row of the herniation table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-03-C02","obj":"NE-20-C.2","title":"Noncommunicating, communicating, and ex vacuo hydrocephalus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hydrocephalus is an excess of CSF within the ventricles caused by obstructed flow or failed absorption. An adult has about 150 mL of CSF and makes about 500 mL a day, mostly from the choroid plexus, so the whole volume turns over about three times daily. Production is largely independent of pressure, so any block causes fluid to accumulate upstream within hours to days."},{"type":"flow","title":"CSF circulation","steps":["Choroid plexus secretes CSF into the lateral ventricles","Interventricular foramina (of Monro) to the third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle","Median and lateral apertures (Magendie, Luschka) to the subarachnoid space","Arachnoid granulations drain CSF into the dural venous sinuses"],"caption":"A block anywhere from the foramina of Monro to the fourth ventricle apertures causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus; failure at the granulations causes communicating hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"In noncommunicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus, CSF cannot leave the ventricular system, so only the ventricles upstream of the block enlarge. Aqueductal stenosis is a classic cause of congenital hydrocephalus; it may be inherited in an X-linked form (L1CAM mutation) or follow gliosis after intrauterine infection or hemorrhage. In Chiari II malformation a small posterior fossa forces the cerebellar vermis and the medulla down through the foramen magnum, almost always with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele, and outflow from the fourth ventricle is obstructed. Dandy-Walker malformation combines a hypoplastic or absent vermis with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle and an enlarged posterior fossa. Tumors obstruct at predictable sites: a colloid cyst at the foramen of Monro, a pineal tumor compressing the aqueduct, and posterior fossa tumors of childhood compressing the fourth ventricle."},{"type":"p","text":"In communicating hydrocephalus, CSF passes freely into the subarachnoid space but is not absorbed. Blood after subarachnoid hemorrhage, or inflammatory exudate after meningitis, scars the arachnoid granulations and the basal subarachnoid space, so all four ventricles enlarge. Raised venous sinus pressure reduces absorption by the same logic, because CSF drains only while its pressure exceeds sinus pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"Hydrocephalus ex vacuo is not a CSF disorder. When neurons are lost, as in Alzheimer disease, frontotemporal dementia, or Huntington disease (where caudate atrophy enlarges the frontal horns), the ventricles and sulci enlarge together to fill the space, and pressure is normal. True hydrocephalus shows ventricles out of proportion to the sulci, often with periventricular interstitial edema."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: locating the obstruction on imaging","text":"Enlarged lateral and third ventricles with a normal fourth ventricle place the block in the cerebral aqueduct. Enlargement of only the lateral ventricles, sometimes on one side, points to the foramina of Monro, as with a colloid cyst. Enlargement of all four ventricles means either an outlet block of the fourth ventricle, as in Dandy-Walker malformation, or failed absorption at the granulations. The history separates them: a posterior fossa cyst suggests outlet block, while prior meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage suggests communicating hydrocephalus. Enlarged ventricles with equally widened sulci and no periventricular edema suggest ex vacuo change."},{"type":"p","text":"Head enlargement occurs only in infants because the cranial sutures are unfused and the anterior fontanelle stays open until about 12 to 18 months. The skull expands under pressure, so head circumference crosses percentiles, the fontanelle bulges, sutures separate, and scalp veins distend. A dilated third ventricle presses on the dorsal midbrain and impairs upgaze, producing the setting-sun sign. Because the skull can expand, papilledema is less common in infants than in older children and adults, who instead present with headache, vomiting, papilledema, and CN VI palsy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An infant with an enlarging head has dilated lateral and third ventricles and a small fourth ventricle. Where is the block, and why is the fourth ventricle spared?","explain":"The cerebral aqueduct; CSF made upstream cannot reach the fourth ventricle, which receives only its own small choroid plexus output and drains normally.","check":"Compare with the CSF circulation flow and the worked example."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-03-C03","obj":"NE-20-C.3","title":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) is a chronic communicating hydrocephalus of older adults, usually after age 60, in which the ventricles enlarge while lumbar opening pressure is normal or only mildly raised. Most cases are idiopathic; secondary NPH can follow subarachnoid hemorrhage, meningitis, or head trauma, which scar the absorptive pathways. The name is somewhat misleading, because continuous monitoring often shows intermittent pressure waves, especially during sleep, superimposed on a normal baseline."},{"type":"p","text":"The leading explanation is that CSF absorption is impaired and the brain becomes less compliant, so each arterial pulse transmits a larger pressure swing into the ventricles. By the law of Laplace, wall tension rises with radius as well as pressure, so once the ventricles have enlarged, a near-normal pressure can keep stretching them. This explanation, first proposed by Hakim, is widely taught but not proven."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the triad appears"},{"type":"p","text":"The enlarged lateral ventricles stretch and compress the surrounding white matter, and the clinical triad follows from which fibers lie closest. Gait disturbance is usually first and most prominent. Corticospinal and frontal-subcortical fibers serving the legs pass close to the ventricular walls, so walking becomes slow, broad-based, and short-stepped, with the feet barely clearing the floor, as though held by a magnet. Starting and turning are especially hard, while arm swing and upper limb function are relatively preserved, and there is no rest tremor."},{"type":"p","text":"Urinary urgency and then incontinence follow because frontal pathways that normally inhibit the spinal and pontine micturition reflexes are disrupted, producing an uninhibited bladder. The dementia is subcortical in pattern: slowed thinking, apathy, and poor attention and executive function, with less memory loss and none of the aphasia or apraxia typical of Alzheimer disease. Imaging shows ventricles enlarged out of proportion to the cortical sulci; an Evans index (frontal horn width divided by maximal inner skull width) above 0.3 indicates ventriculomegaly, and many patients have tight high-convexity sulci with widened Sylvian fissures."},{"type":"flow","title":"From impaired absorption to the NPH triad","steps":["CSF absorption at granulations is impaired","Ventricles enlarge slowly; baseline pressure stays near normal","Enlarged ventricles stretch periventricular white matter","Leg-related fibers disrupted: magnetic gait","Frontal bladder inhibition lost: urgency, incontinence","Frontal-subcortical circuits disrupted: slowed cognition, apathy"],"caption":"Gait usually appears first and responds best to CSF removal; cognition responds least."},{"type":"p","text":"Removing 30 to 50 mL of CSF by lumbar puncture (the tap test) reduces the stretch, and a measurable improvement in gait within hours to a day supports the diagnosis. A negative test does not exclude NPH, because the test is specific but not very sensitive."},{"type":"table","title":"NPH compared with its common mimics","cols":["Feature","NPH","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo (Alzheimer type)","Parkinson disease"],"rows":[["Earliest feature","Gait","Memory loss","Rest tremor or bradykinesia"],["Gait","Broad-based, magnetic, arms swing","Normal until late","Narrow-based, shuffling, reduced arm swing"],["Ventricles and sulci","Ventricles out of proportion to sulci","Both enlarged in proportion","Usually normal"],["Response to CSF removal","Gait often improves","None","None"]],"caption":"The comparison rests on which structures are affected: periventricular fibers, cortex, or the nigrostriatal pathway."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-03-C04","obj":"NE-20-C.4","title":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH), also called pseudotumor cerebri, is raised ICP with no mass, no hydrocephalus, no venous sinus thrombosis, and normal CSF composition. About 90% of patients are women, typically of childbearing age with obesity or recent weight gain. It is the mirror image of NPH: pressure is clearly high, yet the ventricles are normal or small. A common explanation is that the pressure is raised evenly throughout the subarachnoid space and ventricles, so there is no pressure gradient across the brain to push the ventricles outward."},{"type":"h","text":"Proposed mechanisms"},{"type":"p","text":"The cause is not fully known, and several mechanisms likely combine. CSF absorption into the dural sinuses depends on CSF pressure exceeding venous sinus pressure. Most patients have narrowing of the transverse sinuses, and raised ICP itself can compress these thin-walled sinuses, raising venous pressure and further reducing absorption. Obesity may contribute by raising intra-abdominal, intrathoracic, and therefore cerebral venous pressure, and adipose tissue is hormonally active. Excess vitamin A and retinoid drugs, tetracycline-class antibiotics, growth hormone, and withdrawal of corticosteroids are recognized associations; when such a trigger is present, many authors call the condition secondary intracranial hypertension."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Proposed venous feedback loop in IIH","steps":["ICP rises","Thin-walled transverse sinuses are compressed","Venous sinus pressure rises","CSF absorption at granulations falls","CSF volume and ICP rise further"],"caption":"A proposed self-sustaining loop; it explains why sinus narrowing can be both a cause and a consequence of raised pressure."},{"type":"h","text":"Findings explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Headache is daily and often worse when lying down. Papilledema is bilateral and causes transient visual obscurations, blackouts of vision lasting seconds with standing or bending, as swollen axons briefly lose perfusion. Field loss begins as an enlarged blind spot and peripheral, often inferonasal, constriction, while central acuity is preserved until late. Pulsatile tinnitus reflects turbulent flow through narrowed transverse sinuses. Horizontal diplopia comes from CN VI palsy, a false localizing sign: the abducens nerve has a long course up the clivus and bends over the petrous apex, so raised pressure stretches it without a focal lesion along the nerve."},{"type":"table","title":"Diagnostic findings in IIH and their meaning","cols":["Finding","Typical result","Mechanistic meaning"],"rows":[["Brain parenchyma on MRI","Normal; no mass or hydrocephalus","Raised pressure without a space-occupying cause"],["Subtle MRI signs","Partially empty sella, flattened posterior globes, distended optic nerve sheaths","Chronically transmitted CSF pressure"],["MR venography","Transverse sinus narrowing; no thrombosis","Venous outflow contribution; thrombosis would make it secondary"],["Lumbar opening pressure","At or above 25 cm H2O in adults (normal about 10 to 20)","Confirms raised ICP"],["CSF composition","Normal cells, protein, and glucose","No infection, inflammation, or tumor"]],"caption":"Opening pressure is measured with the patient lying on one side; 1 mmHg equals about 1.36 cm H2O."},{"type":"p","text":"The major complication is permanent vision loss. Sustained papilledema leads to ischemia and death of optic nerve axons, and the swollen disc gives way to a pale, atrophic one. A disc that looks less swollen over time can therefore signal axon loss rather than recovery, which is why visual fields, not disc appearance alone, track the risk."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An obese 28-year-old woman taking minocycline for acne has headache, horizontal diplopia, and bilateral disc swelling. Imaging is normal. Predict the opening pressure and CSF content, and explain the diplopia.","explain":"Opening pressure is raised and CSF content normal; raised pressure stretches CN VI along its long course over the petrous apex, weakening abduction.","check":"Compare with the findings paragraph and the IIH table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disorder in this chapter is a problem of volume in a fixed box, so anatomy predicts the signs. The dural folds and foramen magnum define the herniation routes, the optic nerve sheath carries CSF pressure to the disc, and the long course of CN VI makes it vulnerable to any rise in pressure. Physiology supplies the numbers: CPP equals MAP minus ICP, and CSF drains only while its pressure exceeds venous sinus pressure, which links IIH and communicating hydrocephalus to venous outflow."},{"type":"p","text":"The most useful distinction is between pressure and ventricle size, which do not always move together. Obstructive and communicating hydrocephalus raise both; IIH raises pressure with normal ventricles; NPH enlarges ventricles with near-normal pressure; ex vacuo change enlarges ventricles only because brain is lost. Edema type is decided by where water sits, which is why diffusion MRI separates early ischemia from tumor edema. These are teaching categories, and real lesions such as large infarcts and contusions often combine more than one."}],"summary":["The skull is a fixed volume, so added brain water, blood, CSF, or mass raises ICP steeply once CSF and venous buffering is exhausted.","Vasogenic edema is extracellular, from barrier breakdown around tumors, abscesses, and contusions; cytotoxic edema is intracellular, from Na+/K+ ATPase failure in ischemia; interstitial edema is CSF forced across the ependyma in obstructive hydrocephalus.","CPP equals MAP minus ICP; the Cushing triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) is a late compensatory sign of brainstem ischemia.","Uncal herniation compresses ipsilateral CN III (pupil first), the posterior cerebral artery, and the cerebral peduncle; tonsillar herniation compresses the medulla.","Noncommunicating hydrocephalus enlarges only ventricles upstream of a block (aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker, tumor); communicating hydrocephalus enlarges all ventricles after granulation scarring from meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo is ventricular enlargement from brain loss with normal pressure and proportionally widened sulci.","Infants with open sutures develop an enlarging head, bulging fontanelle, and setting-sun eyes rather than early papilledema.","NPH in older adults causes magnetic gait, urinary incontinence, and subcortical dementia from periventricular stretch, with gait improving after CSF removal.","IIH in obese women of childbearing age causes headache, papilledema, transient visual obscurations, and CN VI palsy, with normal imaging, normal CSF content, and opening pressure of 25 cm H2O or more.","Vitamin A excess, retinoids, and tetracyclines are classic associations of IIH, and permanent vision loss from optic atrophy is its main complication."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on cerebral edema, herniation, hydrocephalus, and malformations"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Neuronal Microenvironment"},{"book":"Moore Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, sections on the cranial meninges, ventricles, and cerebrospinal fluid"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System, section on hydrocephalus and Arnold-Chiari malformation"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disease"}]},"NE-20-D":{"unit":"NE-20-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04","title":"Metabolic and Nutritional Neurological Diseases","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how systemic metabolic problems injure the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves: ammonia in liver failure, hyperglycemia in diabetes, missing vitamin B12 or thiamine, and a sodium level corrected too fast. It also classifies peripheral neuropathy and covers Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, the commonest inherited neuropathy. Board questions usually describe a pattern of deficits and ask for the biochemical cause, the tract or nerve involved, or the trigger. If you can connect each enzyme or osmotic failure to the cells that need it most, the findings follow logically.","prerequisites":["Urea cycle, glutamine synthetase, and ammonia handling in liver, muscle, and kidney","Cofactor roles of thiamine pyrophosphate, methylcobalamin, and adenosylcobalamin (NE-14)","Myelin, Schwann cells, oligodendrocytes, and saltatory conduction (NE-2, NE-13)","Dorsal column and corticospinal tract anatomy (NE-5, NE-7)","Course of the oculomotor nerve and the autonomic supply of the pupil and viscera (NE-8, NE-9)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hepatic diseases (GI-13-C)","how":"Cirrhosis and portal hypertension create the portosystemic shunting that lets gut-derived ammonia reach the brain."},{"to":"Deficiency and malabsorption conditions (GI-13-E)","how":"Gastric and ileal disease, bariatric surgery, and pancreatic insufficiency explain most acquired B12 and thiamine deficiency."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell production (HE-9-A)","how":"B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia through the same methionine synthase failure that contributes to cord injury."},{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Guillain-Barre syndrome and multiple sclerosis are the immune counterparts to the metabolic and inherited demyelination taught here."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology (UR-6, UR-7)","how":"Renal ammoniagenesis, potassium balance, and water handling explain hypokalemic encephalopathy and the osmotic injury of rapid sodium correction."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hepatic encephalopathy","def":"Reversible brain dysfunction caused by liver insufficiency or portosystemic shunting, mainly through ammonia and other gut-derived toxins."},{"term":"Asterixis","def":"Negative myoclonus: brief lapses of postural muscle tone that make outstretched, dorsiflexed hands flap."},{"term":"Length-dependent neuropathy","def":"Neuropathy that affects the longest axons first, producing stocking-glove sensory loss that begins in the feet."},{"term":"Mononeuritis multiplex","def":"Damage to two or more separately named nerves at different sites and times, typically from ischemia of the vasa nervorum."},{"term":"Segmental demyelination","def":"Loss of myelin from individual internodes with the axon preserved, which slows or blocks conduction."},{"term":"Polyol pathway","def":"Reduction of glucose to sorbitol by aldose reductase using NADPH, followed by slow oxidation of sorbitol to fructose."},{"term":"Advanced glycation end products (AGEs)","def":"Irreversible adducts formed when glucose reacts nonenzymatically with proteins, which cross-link matrix and activate the receptor for AGEs (RAGE)."},{"term":"Subacute combined degeneration","def":"Vacuolar demyelination of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts caused by vitamin B12 deficiency."},{"term":"Romberg sign","def":"Loss of balance when a patient stands with feet together and eyes closed, indicating impaired proprioception rather than cerebellar disease."},{"term":"Confabulation","def":"Production of false memories without intent to deceive, typical of Korsakoff syndrome."},{"term":"Organic osmolytes","def":"Small solutes such as myo-inositol, taurine, and glutamine that brain cells shed or accumulate to defend their volume."},{"term":"Onion bulb","def":"Concentric layers of Schwann cell processes around an axon, produced by repeated demyelination and remyelination."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C01","obj":"NE-20-D.1","title":"Hepatic encephalopathy: ammonia, precipitants, and asterixis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ammonia is produced mainly in the gut. Colonic bacteria split urea and dietary protein with urease and deaminases, and small intestinal enterocytes release ammonia when glutaminase breaks down glutamine. Portal blood carries it to the liver, where periportal hepatocytes convert it to urea and perivenous hepatocytes trap the remainder as glutamine. Skeletal muscle also takes up ammonia through glutamine synthetase, so muscle wasting in cirrhosis removes a second buffer."},{"type":"p","text":"In cirrhosis, two defects coexist: fewer functioning hepatocytes and portosystemic collaterals (or a surgical or transjugular shunt) that carry portal blood straight into the systemic circulation. Ammonia, mercaptans, manganese, and other gut-derived compounds reach the brain. Uncharged NH3 diffuses across the blood-brain barrier, and astrocytes, which lack a urea cycle, detoxify it with glutamine synthetase."},{"type":"flow","title":"From the gut to the astrocyte","steps":["Gut bacteria and enterocytes generate ammonia","Failing liver and shunts let ammonia bypass urea synthesis","NH3 crosses the blood-brain barrier","Astrocyte glutamine synthetase forms glutamine","Glutamine draws in water; astrocytes swell and malfunction","Neurotransmission shifts toward inhibition: confusion, asterixis, coma"],"caption":"Low-grade astrocyte swelling dominates in cirrhosis; in acute liver failure the same process can cause cerebral edema and herniation."},{"type":"p","text":"Glutamine is osmotically active, and its transport into astrocyte mitochondria generates ammonia and oxidative stress there. Swollen astrocytes handle glutamate and potassium poorly, and the brain shifts toward inhibition through increased GABAergic tone (partly from neurosteroids made by astrocytes). Systemic inflammation acts synergistically with ammonia, and manganese deposition in the basal ganglia adds parkinsonian features."},{"type":"p","text":"Each precipitant raises ammonia production, increases its entry into the brain, or adds sedation. Ammonia exists as NH4+ and NH3 with a pKa of about 9.2, so alkalosis shifts more toward diffusible NH3."},{"type":"table","title":"Precipitants of hepatic encephalopathy and their mechanisms","cols":["Precipitant","Mechanism"],"rows":[["GI bleeding","Blood in the gut is a large protein load digested to ammonia; hypovolemia also lowers hepatic perfusion"],["Infection","Cytokines amplify ammonia neurotoxicity and increase blood-brain barrier permeability"],["High protein intake","More substrate for bacterial and enterocyte ammonia production"],["Constipation","Longer colonic transit allows more bacterial ammonia production and absorption"],["Hypokalemia","Raises proximal tubule ammoniagenesis and causes metabolic alkalosis, favoring NH3"],["Diuretics, vomiting, dehydration","Hypokalemia, alkalosis, and prerenal azotemia (more urea diffusing into the gut)"],["Sedatives (benzodiazepines)","Enhance the already increased GABA-A inhibitory tone"],["Portosystemic shunt placement","Diverts more portal blood around the liver"]],"caption":"Ask whether the trigger adds nitrogen, adds NH3 transfer into the brain, or adds inhibition."},{"type":"p","text":"Findings range from subtle inattention and reversal of the sleep-wake cycle to confusion, somnolence, and coma, graded 1 to 4 (West Haven criteria). Asterixis appears in the intermediate grades: when the patient holds the arms out with wrists dorsiflexed, brief losses of tone let the hands drop and recover. It reflects disordered motor control rather than a muscle problem, and it is not specific, appearing in uremia and carbon dioxide retention. Fetor hepaticus, a musty breath odor from sulfur-containing compounds, may be present. Blood ammonia supports the diagnosis but correlates only loosely with severity."},{"type":"example","title":"Why low potassium worsens encephalopathy","text":"A patient with cirrhosis on a loop diuretic becomes confused with a serum K+ of 2.9 mEq/L (reference about 3.5 to 5.0). Potassium leaves cells in exchange for H+, so proximal tubule cells become relatively acidic, which stimulates glutaminase and ammonia production; more ammonia enters the renal vein. Volume loss and hypokalemia also generate metabolic alkalosis. At higher blood pH, a larger fraction of total ammonia is uncharged NH3, which diffuses into the brain more easily. Ammonia generation and brain entry both rise even though protein intake is unchanged."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C02","obj":"NE-20-D.2","title":"Classifying peripheral neuropathy by pattern and type of injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral neuropathy is classified by the pattern of nerves involved and by whether the axon or the myelin is injured; together these narrow the cause."},{"type":"h","text":"Patterns"},{"type":"p","text":"Distal symmetric polyneuropathy is length dependent. A neuron must synthesize and transport proteins and mitochondria along its entire axon, so systemic metabolic, toxic, and nutritional insults fail the longest axons first. Symptoms begin in the toes and rise to the knees before the fingers are affected, giving a stocking-glove distribution, and ankle reflexes are lost early. A mononeuropathy affects one nerve, usually from compression at a fixed site or focal ischemia. Mononeuritis multiplex affects several separately named nerves at random sites, often stepwise, most often because vasculitis of the vasa nervorum infarcts nerve segments supplied by different vessels; with time the lesions can merge and mimic a symmetric neuropathy."},{"type":"h","text":"Axonal versus demyelinating injury"},{"type":"p","text":"In axonal injury, the axon degenerates distal to the damage (Wallerian degeneration after a focal lesion, distal dying back in metabolic disease). Fewer fibers conduct, so the amplitude of the compound action potential falls, while the surviving fibers conduct at nearly normal speed. Recovery requires regrowth at about 1 mm per day and is slow and often incomplete. In demyelinating injury, internodes lose myelin while axons survive, so saltatory conduction slows or fails; conduction velocity falls, distal latencies lengthen, and focal conduction block may appear. Remyelination can restore function within weeks. Uniform slowing along every nerve suggests an inherited myelin disorder, whereas patchy slowing with conduction block suggests an acquired immune attack."},{"type":"table","title":"Major causes of peripheral neuropathy","cols":["Cause","Usual pattern","Injury type and mechanism"],"rows":[["Diabetes mellitus","Distal symmetric; also mononeuropathy and autonomic","Mainly axonal; metabolic and microvascular (C03)"],["Alcohol","Distal symmetric, often painful","Axonal; direct toxicity plus thiamine and other nutritional deficiency"],["B12 deficiency","Distal symmetric sensory, with cord disease","Axonal; failed cobalamin-dependent metabolism (C04)"],["Thiamine deficiency (dry beriberi)","Distal symmetric sensorimotor","Axonal; energy failure in long axons"],["Vasculitis (polyarteritis nodosa, ANCA-associated, cryoglobulinemia)","Mononeuritis multiplex","Axonal; ischemic infarction of nerve segments"],["Leprosy (Mycobacterium leprae)","Mononeuritis multiplex or patchy sensory loss in cool skin","Bacilli invade Schwann cells; granulomas in thickened nerves"],["HIV","Painful distal symmetric sensory neuropathy; less often demyelinating or multifocal","Mainly axonal; viral, inflammatory, and older antiretroviral toxicity"],["Chemotherapy (vincristine, taxanes, platinum compounds, bortezomib)","Distal symmetric, dose dependent","Axonal; microtubule disruption or dorsal root ganglion toxicity"],["Guillain-Barre syndrome and CIDP (NE-20-B)","Symmetric, proximal and distal weakness","Demyelinating; immune attack on myelin"]],"caption":"Most metabolic and toxic neuropathies are axonal and length dependent; ischemia gives multifocal axonal loss; immune and inherited myelin disorders are demyelinating (C06)."},{"type":"p","text":"Leprosy deserves a note on mechanism. M. leprae grows best below core body temperature and binds Schwann cells through laminin on their basal lamina, so it damages nerves near the skin surface (ulnar, common fibular, great auricular), which become palpably thickened. Tuberculoid disease, with strong Th1 immunity, damages a few nerves beside hypopigmented anesthetic patches; lepromatous disease, with weak cell-mediated immunity, causes widespread symmetric sensory loss. Vincristine blocks microtubule assembly and taxanes freeze microtubules, and either way fast axonal transport to the distal axon fails."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C03","obj":"NE-20-D.3","title":"Diabetic neuropathy: mechanisms and findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Diabetic neuropathy is the commonest neuropathy in developed countries. Schwann cells, neurons, and endothelial cells take up glucose through insulin-independent transporters, so intracellular glucose rises with blood glucose. Several pathways then convert excess glucose into injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"The polyol pathway in Schwann cells","steps":["Hyperglycemia raises glucose inside Schwann cells","Aldose reductase reduces glucose to sorbitol, using NADPH","Low sorbitol dehydrogenase activity traps sorbitol","Sorbitol draws in water; myo-inositol and Na+/K+-ATPase activity fall","NADPH loss limits glutathione regeneration; oxidative stress rises","Schwann cells and axons degenerate, longest fibers first"],"caption":"The same pathway contributes to diabetic cataract and retinopathy in the lens and retina."},{"type":"p","text":"Advanced glycation end products form when glucose binds proteins nonenzymatically. They cross-link collagen and basement membrane, and their receptor (RAGE) activates NF-kB and generates reactive oxygen species. Hyperglycemia also activates protein kinase C through diacylglycerol. Together these thicken the basement membrane of endoneurial capillaries and narrow the vasa nervorum, so endoneurial blood flow falls and the nerve becomes hypoxic. Metabolic injury explains the slow symmetric neuropathy; acute vascular occlusion explains the sudden mononeuropathies."},{"type":"table","title":"Diabetic neuropathy syndromes explained by fiber type","cols":["Syndrome","Fibers or site injured","Findings"],"rows":[["Distal symmetric polyneuropathy","Small fibers (pain, temperature) early; large fibers (vibration, position) later","Burning feet, stocking-glove loss, lost ankle reflexes, sensory ataxia"],["Autonomic neuropathy","Small myelinated and unmyelinated autonomic fibers","Gastroparesis, orthostatic hypotension, fixed resting tachycardia, erectile dysfunction, neurogenic bladder, distal anhidrosis"],["Cranial mononeuropathy","Ischemic infarct of the nerve core","Pupil-sparing CN III palsy; CN VI palsy"],["Lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy (diabetic amyotrophy)","Ischemic and inflammatory injury to roots and plexus","Painful asymmetric proximal thigh weakness and wasting"]],"caption":"The longest fibers of every type fail first, which is why the vagus and the sweat gland fibers of the feet are early autonomic targets."},{"type":"p","text":"Autonomic findings follow the lost efferents. Vagal damage slows gastric emptying and leaves the resting heart rate high and fixed. Damage to sympathetic vasoconstrictor fibers to the splanchnic and limb vessels prevents the reflex vasoconstriction that normally holds blood pressure on standing, giving orthostatic hypotension."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuropathic foot ulcers form under the metatarsal heads and heel because three losses combine. Without protective sensation, repeated pressure goes unnoticed. Motor loss of the intrinsic foot muscles produces claw toes that load the metatarsal heads. Sympathetic loss causes dry, cracked skin. Peripheral arterial disease then slows healing, and unfelt fractures can destroy the midfoot (Charcot neuroarthropathy)."},{"type":"p","text":"In the oculomotor nerve, parasympathetic pupillomotor fibers run superficially and are supplied by surface pial vessels, while the core depends on penetrating vasa nervorum. Diabetic microvascular infarction of the core causes ptosis and a down-and-out eye with a normal pupil, often with pain, and usually recovers over about 3 months. A compressive lesion such as a posterior communicating artery aneurysm presses on the surface first, so the pupil dilates early."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has sudden ptosis, a down-and-out eye, and a fixed dilated pupil. Is diabetic infarction the likely cause?","explain":"No. Pupillary fibers lie on the surface of CN III with their own surface blood supply, so infarction of the core spares them. A dilated pupil indicates external compression, classically a posterior communicating artery aneurysm or uncal herniation.","check":"The CN III paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C04","obj":"NE-20-D.4","title":"Vitamin B12 deficiency and subacute combined degeneration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) comes only from animal foods. Absorption has several steps, and each named cause of deficiency breaks one of them. Stomach acid and pepsin free B12 from food protein; it binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva; pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin in the duodenum; B12 then binds intrinsic factor from gastric parietal cells; and the complex is absorbed by cubilin receptors in the terminal ileum. Transcobalamin carries it in plasma. The liver stores several milligrams, enough for about 3 to 5 years, so dietary deficiency develops slowly."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of B12 deficiency by step","cols":["Step lost","Causes"],"rows":[["Intake","Strict vegan diet without supplementation"],["Release from food and intrinsic factor","Pernicious anemia; gastrectomy or gastric bypass; atrophic gastritis; long-term acid suppression"],["Release from haptocorrin","Pancreatic insufficiency"],["Competition in the lumen","Diphyllobothrium latum (fish tapeworm from raw freshwater fish); bacterial overgrowth in blind loops"],["Ileal uptake","Crohn disease of the terminal ileum, ileal resection; metformin reduces uptake"],["Enzyme function","Nitrous oxide oxidizes cobalamin and inactivates methionine synthase"]],"caption":"Nitrous oxide can unmask marginal stores quickly."},{"type":"p","text":"Pernicious anemia is autoimmune gastritis of the gastric body and fundus. T cells and antibodies against the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase destroy parietal cells, causing achlorhydria and loss of intrinsic factor; antibodies to intrinsic factor are less sensitive but highly specific. Gastrin rises because acid no longer inhibits antral G cells. It is associated with other autoimmune diseases such as thyroiditis and type 1 diabetes."},{"type":"p","text":"B12 is a cofactor for two enzymes. Methionine synthase uses methylcobalamin to transfer a methyl group from N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, forming methionine (then S-adenosylmethionine, the main methyl donor) and regenerating tetrahydrofolate for DNA synthesis. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase uses adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. Deficiency therefore raises both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid (MMA) and causes megaloblastic anemia. Folate deficiency raises homocysteine but not MMA. How the nervous system is injured is not fully settled: reduced S-adenosylmethionine impairs methylation of myelin proteins and lipids, and accumulated methylmalonyl-CoA and propionyl-CoA may lead to abnormal fatty acids in myelin. Folate can correct the anemia but not the neurologic injury, so folate alone can mask B12 deficiency while the cord degenerates."},{"type":"p","text":"Subacute combined degeneration is vacuolar swelling and loss of myelin, then axons, in the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts, usually beginning in the cervical and upper thoracic cord. Dorsal column loss removes vibration and joint position sense, so the patient relies on vision to balance and falls when the eyes close (positive Romberg sign) and walks with a broad-based sensory ataxia. Corticospinal loss gives spastic weakness, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses. A coexisting axonal neuropathy causes distal paresthesias and can abolish ankle reflexes. Cerebral involvement causes memory loss, depression, psychosis, or dementia, and optic neuropathy may occur. Neurologic signs can appear without anemia or macrocytosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a mixed upper and lower motor neuron picture","text":"A 62-year-old with a partial gastrectomy has tingling feet, falls in the dark, absent ankle reflexes, brisk knee reflexes, and bilateral Babinski signs. Vibration is absent at the toes and reduced at the knees. Falling in the dark and lost vibration localize to large-fiber sensory pathways (dorsal columns and peripheral nerve). Babinski signs and brisk knee reflexes require corticospinal disease, while absent ankle reflexes reflect the peripheral neuropathy interrupting the longest reflex arc. One lesion cannot explain both, but B12 deficiency damages cord and nerve together. Raised MMA and homocysteine with low serum B12 (reference about 200 to 900 pg/mL) would confirm it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C05","obj":"NE-20-D.5","title":"Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) is converted to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway, and branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase. Without TPP, pyruvate cannot enter the TCA cycle and the cycle itself slows, so glucose oxidation and ATP production fail and lactate accumulates. The brain depends almost entirely on glucose oxidation, so it is vulnerable. Body stores are small (about 30 mg) and can be depleted in about 2 to 3 weeks of poor intake."},{"type":"p","text":"Alcohol use disorder is the commonest setting: diet is poor, alcohol reduces intestinal thiamine absorption, and liver disease impairs storage and conversion to TPP. Other causes are malnutrition, hyperemesis gravidarum and other prolonged vomiting, bariatric surgery, malignancy, and refeeding after starvation. A glucose load in a depleted patient increases flux through the thiamine-dependent steps and can precipitate or worsen an attack."},{"type":"flow","title":"From thiamine depletion to Wernicke lesions","steps":["Thiamine intake, absorption, or storage falls","TPP-dependent dehydrogenases and transketolase lose activity","Glucose oxidation fails; ATP falls and lactate rises","Ion gradients fail; cytotoxic edema and glutamate excitotoxicity","High-turnover periventricular regions are damaged first","Confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia"],"caption":"Damage is symmetric around the third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, and fourth ventricle."},{"type":"p","text":"Wernicke encephalopathy is the acute stage. Lesions involve the mammillary bodies, the medial (dorsomedial) thalamus, the periaqueductal gray, the oculomotor, abducens, and vestibular nuclei in the brainstem, and the superior cerebellar vermis. Acutely they show edema, endothelial swelling, and petechial hemorrhage; later the mammillary bodies shrink and contain hemosiderin. Only a minority of patients show the full triad, so any one feature in a malnourished patient should raise the question."},{"type":"table","title":"The Wernicke triad and its anatomy","cols":["Finding","Lesion responsible"],"rows":[["Confusion, apathy, inattention","Medial thalamus, mammillary bodies, diffuse energy failure"],["Ophthalmoplegia: nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy, conjugate gaze palsy","Abducens and oculomotor nuclei, vestibular nuclei, gaze centers"],["Gait ataxia, broad-based","Superior cerebellar vermis and vestibular dysfunction"]],"caption":"Eye findings and confusion are often reversible early; the ataxia and memory loss may persist."},{"type":"p","text":"Korsakoff syndrome is the chronic stage, usually following untreated or recurrent Wernicke encephalopathy. Damage to the mammillary bodies, the mammillothalamic tract, and the anterior and dorsomedial thalamic nuclei interrupts the diencephalic memory circuits, including the Papez circuit, that consolidate new declarative memories. The result is severe anterograde amnesia, variable retrograde amnesia, and confabulation, which fills gaps with false memories produced without intent to deceive. Attention, immediate recall, and other intellectual functions are relatively preserved, which distinguishes it from global dementia. It is largely irreversible because neurons have been lost."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral thiamine deficiency causes beriberi. Dry beriberi is an axonal, length-dependent sensorimotor neuropathy; wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from peripheral vasodilation as tissue energy fails."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A malnourished man with vomiting is given intravenous glucose alone and within hours develops nystagmus and confusion. Why did glucose make him worse?","explain":"Glucose increases flux toward pyruvate dehydrogenase and the TCA cycle, which require TPP. With little thiamine available, the load consumes what remains and the blocked steps fail completely in high-demand periventricular neurons.","check":"The thiamine enzymes and precipitant paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C06","obj":"NE-20-D.6","title":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth (CMT) disease, also called hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy, is a genetically diverse group of inherited neuropathies and the commonest inherited peripheral neuropathy. All forms are length dependent, so distal leg muscles weaken first, and all progress slowly, typically beginning in the first or second decade."},{"type":"p","text":"The most common form, CMT1A, is autosomal dominant and caused by a duplication on chromosome 17p12 that contains PMP22 (peripheral myelin protein 22). The extra copy raises PMP22 expression, a gene dosage effect, and the excess protein misfolds and destabilizes compact myelin. Schwann cells repeatedly demyelinate and remyelinate, layering their processes around axons as onion bulbs, so nerves can become palpably enlarged. Conduction slows uniformly in every nerve, unlike the patchy slowing of acquired demyelination, and secondary axonal loss eventually produces the weakness. Deletion of the same region produces the opposite dosage problem, hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsies."},{"type":"table","title":"Main forms of Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","cols":["Form","Inheritance and gene","Injury and conduction"],"rows":[["CMT1A","Autosomal dominant; PMP22 duplication","Demyelinating; uniformly slow velocity"],["CMT1B","Autosomal dominant; MPZ (myelin protein zero)","Demyelinating"],["CMT2A","Autosomal dominant; MFN2 (mitofusin 2)","Axonal; near-normal velocity, low amplitude"],["CMTX1","X-linked; GJB1 (connexin 32)","Mixed; males more severely affected"]],"caption":"CMT1 means demyelinating and CMT2 means axonal; the clinical picture is similar."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings come from distal muscle imbalance. Weak tibialis anterior and fibular (peroneal) muscles cause foot drop, so the patient lifts the knee high to clear the toes (steppage gait) and trips easily. Wasting of the distal leg with preserved thigh bulk gives the inverted champagne bottle appearance. The intrinsic foot muscles weaken early; the long toe extensors and flexors then pull unopposed, clawing the toes into hammer toes. Relatively stronger fibularis longus and tibialis posterior acting against weaker antagonists raise and invert the arch, giving pes cavus. Ankle reflexes are reduced or absent, and distal sensory loss is usually mild compared with the weakness. Hands become involved later."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-04-C07","obj":"NE-20-D.7","title":"Osmotic demyelination syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Water crosses cell membranes freely, so a fall in plasma osmolality from hyponatremia drives water into brain cells. Within hours the brain limits swelling by exporting Na+, K+, and Cl-. Over about 48 hours it also sheds organic osmolytes such as myo-inositol, taurine, glutamine, and glutamate, which returns cell volume nearly to normal. Hyponatremia present this long is called chronic, and the adapted brain tolerates it relatively well."},{"type":"p","text":"Restoring osmolytes takes days, much longer than losing them. If serum sodium is then raised quickly, extracellular osmolality rises before the cells can rebuild their osmolyte content, so water leaves and the cells shrink. Shrinkage disrupts endothelial tight junctions and the blood-brain barrier, and oligodendrocytes undergo apoptosis, stripping myelin while neurons and axons are relatively spared. Risk rises when sodium increases by more than about 10 to 12 mEq/L in 24 hours, and lower thresholds apply to high-risk patients: those with a starting sodium of 105 mEq/L or less (reference about 135 to 145), alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, hypokalemia, or advanced liver disease. The syndrome is rare when the starting sodium is above about 120 mEq/L."},{"type":"flow","title":"How rapid correction demyelinates the pons","steps":["Chronic hyponatremia: brain cells shed organic osmolytes","Serum sodium is raised too quickly","Water leaves cells faster than osmolytes return","Cells shrink; blood-brain barrier is disrupted","Oligodendrocytes in the central pons die","Days later: quadriparesis, dysarthria, dysphagia"],"caption":"The same mechanism explains extrapontine lesions in the basal ganglia, thalamus, and cerebellum."},{"type":"p","text":"The central basis pontis is most vulnerable, possibly because its many oligodendrocytes lie interwoven with gray matter in the pontine nuclei; extrapontine lesions are common, with or without pontine involvement. The course is biphasic: the confusion of hyponatremia improves as sodium rises, then about 2 to 6 days later the patient deteriorates. Corticobulbar fiber loss produces dysarthria, dysphagia, and pseudobulbar palsy; corticospinal loss produces quadriparesis that is flaccid early and spastic later. A large lesion of the basis pontis that spares the tegmentum produces locked-in syndrome, in which the patient is awake and aware, because the reticular activating system is intact, but can move only the eyes vertically and blink. Magnetic resonance imaging shows a symmetric central pontine T2 hyperintensity that spares the periphery, often appearing a week or two after symptoms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does raising sodium by 15 mEq/L in 24 hours cause demyelination in a patient whose hyponatremia developed over 3 weeks, but not in one whose hyponatremia developed over 6 hours?","explain":"After 3 weeks the brain has shed organic osmolytes and cannot regain them quickly, so rapid correction shrinks its cells. After 6 hours, swelling has been opposed mainly by electrolyte shifts that reverse quickly, so the brain is still swollen and correction restores normal volume.","check":"The osmolyte adaptation and correction paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each disease in this chapter is a biochemical or osmotic failure in the cells that depend most on the missing function. Astrocytes alone detoxify brain ammonia, so they swell in liver failure. Periventricular regions around the third ventricle, aqueduct, and fourth ventricle have the highest thiamine-dependent oxidative turnover, so Wernicke lesions cluster there. Cobalamin-dependent methylation and odd-chain metabolism support myelin, so B12 deficiency strikes the long myelinated tracts of the cord and nerves. The longest axons have the greatest transport and energy burden, so diabetic, alcoholic, toxic, and inherited neuropathies begin in the feet. Oligodendrocytes in the pons fail when cell volume changes faster than osmolytes can follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Overlapping findings are separated by mechanism. Confusion with asterixis suggests metabolic encephalopathy from liver, kidney, or carbon dioxide retention; confusion with ophthalmoplegia suggests thiamine deficiency; dorsal column loss with corticospinal signs suggests B12 deficiency, copper deficiency, or nitrous oxide exposure. Nerve conduction studies separate axonal from demyelinating neuropathy, but many disorders are mixed. Blood ammonia, serum B12, and imaging all correlate imperfectly with severity, so they support rather than replace the clinical pattern."}],"summary":["In cirrhosis, gut ammonia bypasses the urea cycle; astrocytes convert it to glutamine, swell, and shift the brain toward inhibition.","GI bleeding, infection, protein load, constipation, hypokalemia with alkalosis, and sedatives precipitate hepatic encephalopathy; asterixis is negative myoclonus.","Length-dependent neuropathy gives stocking-glove loss; mononeuritis multiplex reflects vasa nervorum ischemia, as in vasculitis.","Axonal injury lowers conduction amplitude and recovers slowly; demyelination slows conduction velocity and can recover quickly.","Diabetic neuropathy results from sorbitol accumulation with NADPH loss, AGEs, and microvascular injury; the pupil is spared in diabetic CN III infarction.","B12 deficiency raises homocysteine and methylmalonic acid and causes subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts, even without anemia.","Thiamine deficiency impairs PDH, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase; Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia) can progress to Korsakoff amnesia with confabulation.","CMT1A is an autosomal dominant PMP22 duplication causing uniform demyelination, foot drop, pes cavus, and hammer toes.","Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia shrinks osmolyte-depleted brain cells and demyelinates the central pons, causing quadriparesis, dysarthria, dysphagia, or locked-in syndrome."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles; The Central Nervous System, acquired metabolic and toxic disturbances"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body fluid compartments and regulation of extracellular fluid osmolarity and sodium concentration"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins; Amino acids: disposal of nitrogen; Metabolism of monosaccharides (polyol pathway)"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cellular volume regulation and the physiology of neurons and glia"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders; Liver Disease, hepatic encephalopathy"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Mycobacteria, Mycobacterium leprae"}]},"NE-20-E":{"unit":"NE-20-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05","title":"Congenital and Genetic Neurological Diseases","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers inherited and prenatal disorders that injure the nervous system: chromosomal and repeat disorders (Down syndrome, fragile X), leukodystrophies, lysosomal storage diseases, amino acid and urea cycle defects, acute intermittent porphyria, Wilson disease, and the congenital infections and toxins that cause microcephaly. Board questions give an infant or child with a pattern of findings and ask for the missing enzyme or gene, the substance that accumulates, the mode of inheritance, or the single clue that separates two look-alike diseases. If you can trace each disease from the defect to what builds up, which cells store it, and why those cells fail, the clinical picture follows logically.","prerequisites":["Meiosis, nondisjunction, and patterns of Mendelian and X-linked inheritance (NE-16)","Lysosomal and peroxisomal function and sphingolipid structure","Amino acid catabolism, the urea cycle, and heme synthesis","Myelin formation by oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells","Basal ganglia anatomy and motor circuits"],"connections":[{"to":"Medical genetics (NE-16)","how":"Nondisjunction, Robertsonian translocation, trinucleotide repeat expansion, and X inactivation explain how these diseases arise and who expresses them."},{"to":"Biochemistry of metabolism","how":"Each inborn error is a blocked step in a pathway, so the substrate before the block accumulates and the product after it becomes deficient."},{"to":"Hepatic encephalopathy and metabolic brain injury (NE-14, NE-20-D)","how":"Ammonia neurotoxicity in urea cycle defects uses the same astrocyte glutamine mechanism as liver failure."},{"to":"Neurodegenerative disease (NE-20)","how":"Amyloid in Down syndrome, basal ganglia injury in Wilson disease, and GBA variants in Parkinson disease link genetics to adult degeneration."},{"to":"TORCH infections and teratology","how":"Congenital CMV, rubella, toxoplasmosis, Zika, and ethanol injure neural progenitors during brain growth, producing microcephaly."}],"terms":[{"term":"Nondisjunction","def":"Failure of homologous chromosomes or sister chromatids to separate during meiosis or mitosis, producing gametes or cells with an extra or missing chromosome."},{"term":"Lysosomal storage disease","def":"A disorder in which loss of a lysosomal hydrolase traps its undegraded substrate inside lysosomes, enlarging and damaging the cells that turn that substrate over."},{"term":"Leukodystrophy","def":"An inherited disorder of white matter in which myelin is abnormally formed or progressively destroyed."},{"term":"Peroxisome","def":"A membrane-bound organelle that, among other tasks, shortens very long chain fatty acids by beta oxidation before mitochondria finish them."},{"term":"Sphingolipid","def":"A membrane lipid built on a ceramide backbone; gangliosides, cerebrosides, sulfatides, and sphingomyelin are examples."},{"term":"Cherry-red spot","def":"A red fovea framed by pale, swollen retina, seen when lipid-filled ganglion cells surround the fovea, which has no ganglion cells."},{"term":"Trinucleotide repeat expansion","def":"An increase in the number of copies of a three-base sequence beyond a stable range, often growing further in successive generations (anticipation)."},{"term":"Hyperammonemia","def":"An elevated blood ammonia concentration, toxic to the brain, from failure of urea synthesis or liver clearance."},{"term":"Ceruloplasmin","def":"A liver-made ferroxidase that carries most circulating copper; copper must be loaded onto it by ATP7B, or the apoprotein is rapidly degraded."},{"term":"Microcephaly","def":"A head circumference more than 2 standard deviations below the mean for age and sex, usually reflecting a small brain."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C01","obj":"NE-20-E.1","title":"Down syndrome: neurologic and associated features","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the most common chromosomal cause of intellectual disability. About 95% of cases arise from meiotic nondisjunction, most often in maternal meiosis I, which is why risk rises with maternal age. About 4% result from a Robertsonian translocation, usually between chromosomes 14 and 21, which can be inherited from a balanced carrier parent and is independent of maternal age; the rest are mosaic, with milder and more variable features."},{"type":"p","text":"The extra copy of chromosome 21 raises the dosage of every gene on it by about 50%. Brain growth and neuronal development are altered, producing intellectual disability that is usually mild to moderate, and hypotonia that is often the first sign in the newborn. Gene dosage also explains early-onset Alzheimer disease. The amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene lies on chromosome 21, so lifelong overproduction of APP yields more amyloid beta. Plaques and tangles are present in nearly all adults with Down syndrome by about age 40, and clinical dementia commonly appears in the 50s."},{"type":"p","text":"Several features reflect abnormal connective tissue and embryonic development. Laxity of the transverse ligament of the atlas allows atlantoaxial instability, so C1 can slip on C2 and compress the cervical cord, causing neck pain, gait change, hyperreflexia, or weakness. Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse produces the atrioventricular septal defect, the most characteristic heart lesion. Failed recanalization of the duodenum gives duodenal atresia, with bilious vomiting and a double bubble on radiograph. Leukemia risk is increased: acute lymphoblastic leukemia is more frequent overall, while acute megakaryoblastic leukemia is especially increased in children under 5 and may be preceded in newborns by a transient myeloproliferative disorder linked to GATA1 mutations."},{"type":"table","title":"Down syndrome: features and their basis","cols":["Feature","Basis"],"rows":[["Intellectual disability, hypotonia","Altered brain development from chromosome 21 gene dosage"],["Early-onset Alzheimer disease","Extra APP copy; more amyloid beta"],["Atlantoaxial instability","Transverse ligament laxity; risk of cord compression"],["Atrioventricular septal defect","Endocardial cushion defect"],["Duodenal atresia; Hirschsprung disease","Failed recanalization; failed neural crest migration"],["ALL and acute megakaryoblastic leukemia","Altered hematopoiesis; GATA1 mutation in megakaryoblastic disease"],["Flat face, upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single palmar crease, Brushfield spots","Dysmorphic features used for clinical recognition"],["Hypothyroidism","Increased autoimmune and congenital thyroid disease"]],"caption":"Most findings trace to gene dosage or to defects in specific embryonic structures."},{"type":"p","text":"Prenatal screening reflects the same biology. In the second trimester quadruple screen, alpha-fetoprotein and estriol are low while hCG and inhibin A are high; in the first trimester, nuchal translucency is increased, hCG is high, and PAPP-A is low. Karyotype distinguishes free trisomy from translocation, which matters because a translocation carrier parent has a high recurrence risk."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 45-year-old with Down syndrome develops progressive memory loss. Why would her Alzheimer pathology appear decades earlier than in the general population?","explain":"The APP gene is on chromosome 21. Three copies raise APP and amyloid beta production from birth, so plaques form early and nearly all adults have Alzheimer neuropathology by about 40.","check":"The paragraph on gene dosage and APP."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C02","obj":"NE-20-E.2","title":"Leukodystrophies: adrenoleukodystrophy, metachromatic leukodystrophy, and Krabbe disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myelin is a lipid-rich membrane made by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system and by Schwann cells in peripheral nerves, and it is turned over continuously. A leukodystrophy results when a defect in lipid handling makes myelin unstable or poisons the cells that make it. The three classic disorders differ in the organelle involved (peroxisome or lysosome), the stored lipid, the inheritance, and whether peripheral nerve and adrenal gland are also affected."},{"type":"h","text":"X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy"},{"type":"p","text":"ABCD1 encodes a transporter in the peroxisomal membrane that imports very long chain fatty acids (VLCFA, 22 or more carbons) for beta oxidation. Mitochondria cannot shorten these fatty acids, so when ABCD1 fails they accumulate in plasma, myelin, and the adrenal cortex and Leydig cells. In the childhood cerebral form, a previously normal boy aged about 4 to 10 develops behavior change, school decline, visual and hearing loss, and spasticity, with inflammatory demyelination that usually begins in the posterior (parieto-occipital) white matter. Adrenal insufficiency with hyperpigmentation may precede neurologic signs, so primary adrenal insufficiency in a boy should prompt VLCFA measurement. Adult men may instead develop adrenomyeloneuropathy, a slowly progressive spastic paraparesis, and many carrier women develop a milder myelopathy."},{"type":"h","text":"Metachromatic leukodystrophy and Krabbe disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Both are autosomal recessive lysosomal diseases of galactolipids that destroy central and peripheral myelin, so upper motor neuron signs coexist with peripheral neuropathy (reduced reflexes, slowed nerve conduction). In metachromatic leukodystrophy, arylsulfatase A deficiency leaves sulfatides (cerebroside sulfate) undegraded; the stored material stains metachromatically, a color shift with certain dyes that gives the disease its name, and urinary sulfatides are increased. The late infantile form presents in the second year with gait disturbance and regression. In Krabbe disease, galactocerebrosidase deficiency allows psychosine (galactosylsphingosine) to accumulate, which is directly toxic to oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells. Myelin collapses early, so infants at about 3 to 6 months show irritability, stiffness, optic atrophy, and neuropathy. Galactocerebroside is phagocytosed by macrophages that fuse into multinucleated globoid cells, the histologic hallmark."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the three leukodystrophies","cols":["Feature","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","Metachromatic leukodystrophy","Krabbe disease"],"rows":[["Inheritance","X-linked","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal recessive"],["Defect","ABCD1 peroxisomal VLCFA transporter","Arylsulfatase A","Galactocerebrosidase"],["Organelle","Peroxisome","Lysosome","Lysosome"],["Accumulates","VLCFA","Sulfatides","Galactocerebroside and psychosine"],["Peripheral nerve","Mainly spared in cerebral form","Demyelinated","Demyelinated"],["Distinguishing clue","Adrenal insufficiency in a boy","Metachromatic granules; urine sulfatides","Globoid cells; infant onset"]],"caption":"Peroxisomal disease adds adrenal failure; lysosomal galactolipid diseases add peripheral neuropathy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C03","obj":"NE-20-E.3","title":"Phenylketonuria: phenylalanine hydroxylase and BH4 deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), a liver enzyme, converts phenylalanine to tyrosine using tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) as cofactor. In classic phenylketonuria (PKU), an autosomal recessive PAH deficiency, phenylalanine accumulates and tyrosine can no longer be made, so tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid. Excess phenylalanine is transaminated to phenylpyruvate and further to phenyllactate and phenylacetate, the phenylketones that appear in urine; phenylacetate gives sweat and urine a musty or mousy odor."},{"type":"flow","title":"From PAH block to brain injury","steps":["PAH or BH4 deficient; phenylalanine cannot become tyrosine","Plasma phenylalanine rises; tyrosine falls","Phenylalanine saturates the LAT1 transporter at the blood-brain barrier","Brain uptake of tyrosine, tryptophan, and other large neutral amino acids falls","Brain protein, myelin, dopamine, and serotonin synthesis impaired","Intellectual disability, seizures, behavior problems"],"caption":"High phenylalanine competes with other large neutral amino acids for the same transporter during brain development."},{"type":"p","text":"The brain injury comes mainly from high phenylalanine itself. It competes for the large neutral amino acid transporter (LAT1) at the blood-brain barrier, starving developing neurons of tyrosine and tryptophan needed for protein, dopamine, and serotonin synthesis, and it impairs myelination. Untreated infants appear normal at birth because maternal metabolism cleared phenylalanine in utero; over months they develop intellectual disability, seizures, hyperactivity, and eczema. Fair skin, hair, and blue eyes reflect reduced melanin, because less tyrosine is available and phenylalanine competitively inhibits tyrosinase. Newborn screening measures blood phenylalanine after protein feeding has begun."},{"type":"p","text":"About 1 to 2% of cases are caused by defects in BH4 synthesis or recycling (for example dihydropteridine reductase deficiency). BH4 is also the cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase and tryptophan hydroxylase, so these infants lack dopamine and serotonin even when phenylalanine is controlled, and neurologic deterioration is more severe. The classic exam point is that a BH4 defect causes neurologic decline out of proportion to the phenylalanine level, because neurotransmitter synthesis fails directly."},{"type":"p","text":"Maternal PKU affects a genetically normal fetus. If a woman with PKU has high phenylalanine during pregnancy, it crosses the placenta, which actively concentrates amino acids, and acts as a teratogen. The infant shows microcephaly, intellectual disability, growth restriction, and congenital heart defects, even though the infant does not have PKU."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic PKU versus BH4 deficiency","cols":["Feature","PAH deficiency","BH4 deficiency"],"rows":[["Phenylalanine","High","High"],["Tyrosine","Low; essential","Low; essential"],["Dopamine and serotonin synthesis","Reduced indirectly by transport competition","Directly reduced (cofactor missing)"],["Neurologic injury","Tracks phenylalanine level","Exceeds what phenylalanine level explains"]],"caption":"BH4 is shared by three aromatic amino acid hydroxylases, so its loss affects neurotransmitter synthesis directly."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C04","obj":"NE-20-E.4","title":"Tay-Sachs disease versus Niemann-Pick disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lysosomal hydrolases remove one group at a time from complex lipids. When one is missing, its substrate is trapped in lysosomes of whichever cells normally degrade it, so the distribution of the substrate predicts which organs fail. Tay-Sachs and Niemann-Pick disease both cause infantile neurodegeneration and a cherry-red macula, and both are autosomal recessive with a higher carrier frequency in Ashkenazi Jewish populations, so the distinction rests on the enzyme, the substrate, and the presence of hepatosplenomegaly."},{"type":"p","text":"In Tay-Sachs disease, mutations in HEXA eliminate hexosaminidase A, and GM2 ganglioside accumulates. Gangliosides are concentrated in neuronal membranes, so neurons swell with lysosomes containing concentric lamellar (onion-skin) membranous bodies. Infants are normal until about 3 to 6 months, then show weakness, loss of milestones, an exaggerated startle to sound, blindness, and seizures, with death in early childhood. Because visceral macrophages handle little GM2, the liver and spleen are not enlarged."},{"type":"p","text":"In Niemann-Pick disease types A and B, deficiency of acid sphingomyelinase (gene SMPD1) leaves sphingomyelin undegraded. Sphingomyelin is abundant in cell membranes that macrophages engulf, so lipid-laden foam cells fill the liver, spleen, marrow, and lungs, producing massive hepatosplenomegaly. Type A is the infantile neuronopathic form, with feeding difficulty, regression, and death by about age 3; type B spares the brain and presents with hepatosplenomegaly and lung disease. Niemann-Pick type C is a different disease, a defect in cholesterol trafficking (NPC1 or NPC2) that typically presents later with ataxia, vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, and dementia."},{"type":"p","text":"The cherry-red spot arises the same way in both. Retinal ganglion cells swollen with stored lipid make the perimacular retina pale and opaque, while the fovea, which contains no ganglion cells, lets the red choroid show through."},{"type":"table","title":"Tay-Sachs versus Niemann-Pick type A","cols":["Feature","Tay-Sachs","Niemann-Pick type A"],"rows":[["Enzyme","Hexosaminidase A (HEXA)","Acid sphingomyelinase (SMPD1)"],["Substrate","GM2 ganglioside","Sphingomyelin"],["Main storing cells","Neurons","Neurons and macrophages"],["Hepatosplenomegaly","Absent","Marked"],["Histology","Onion-skin lysosomes in neurons","Foam cells; zebra bodies on electron microscopy"],["Shared findings","Cherry-red macula, regression","Cherry-red macula, regression"]],"caption":"Hepatosplenomegaly is the single most useful discriminator."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does Niemann-Pick disease enlarge the spleen while Tay-Sachs does not, when both enzymes are missing from every cell?","explain":"Organ involvement follows substrate turnover, not enzyme distribution. Macrophages degrade large amounts of sphingomyelin from engulfed membranes, so they fill with it. GM2 ganglioside is concentrated in neurons, so only neurons store it.","check":"The paragraphs on substrate distribution in each disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C05","obj":"NE-20-E.5","title":"Fabry, Gaucher, Hurler, and Hunter diseases","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"These lysosomal diseases follow the same rule as the previous section: the substrate collects in the cells that degrade it, and symptoms follow from those cells. Two of them are X-linked (Fabry and Hunter); the others, like most lysosomal diseases, are autosomal recessive."},{"type":"h","text":"Fabry disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Deficiency of alpha-galactosidase A (gene GLA) causes globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) to accumulate in vascular endothelium and smooth muscle, dorsal root ganglion neurons, podocytes, and cardiac myocytes. Storage in small sensory fibers and their ganglia produces the earliest symptom, a small fiber neuropathy with burning pain in the hands and feet (acroparesthesias) that begins in childhood and is provoked by heat, fever, or exercise. Autonomic involvement reduces sweating, so heat intolerance is common. Skin vessels form angiokeratomas, small dark red papules clustered between the umbilicus and knees. Vasculopathy causes early stroke, often in the posterior circulation, along with proteinuria progressing to kidney failure and left ventricular hypertrophy. Corneal whorls (verticillata) are a useful eye clue. Because of random X inactivation, heterozygous women are often symptomatic, usually later and more mildly than men."},{"type":"h","text":"Gaucher disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Glucocerebrosidase deficiency (gene GBA) leaves glucocerebroside (glucosylceramide) in macrophages, which become Gaucher cells with a crumpled tissue paper cytoplasm. Type 1, the most common form and frequent among Ashkenazi Jewish people, spares the brain: hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia from marrow replacement and hypersplenism, bone pain, avascular necrosis of the femoral head, and Erlenmeyer flask deformity of the distal femur. Types 2 and 3 are neuronopathic. Type 2 is acute infantile disease with rapid brainstem dysfunction and death in early childhood; type 3 progresses more slowly, with impaired horizontal saccades (supranuclear gaze palsy), seizures, and ataxia. Heterozygous GBA variants also raise the risk of Parkinson disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Hurler and Hunter syndromes"},{"type":"p","text":"These mucopolysaccharidoses store dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate, glycosaminoglycans of connective tissue and cell surfaces, so they distort bone, face, airway, heart valves, viscera, and brain, and glycosaminoglycans appear in urine. Hurler syndrome (MPS I) is autosomal recessive alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency with coarse facies, corneal clouding, progressive developmental decline, hepatosplenomegaly, skeletal dysostosis multiplex, and airway obstruction. Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is X-linked recessive iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency, generally milder and more variable, with aggressive behavior in some boys and no corneal clouding."},{"type":"table","title":"Fabry, Gaucher, Hurler, and Hunter compared","cols":["Disease","Inheritance","Enzyme","Substrate","Key neurologic or distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Fabry","X-linked","Alpha-galactosidase A","Globotriaosylceramide","Painful small fiber neuropathy, angiokeratomas, hypohidrosis, early stroke"],["Gaucher","Autosomal recessive","Glucocerebrosidase","Glucocerebroside","Type 1 spares brain; types 2 and 3 neuronopathic"],["Hurler (MPS I)","Autosomal recessive","Alpha-L-iduronidase","Dermatan and heparan sulfate","Corneal clouding, severe developmental decline"],["Hunter (MPS II)","X-linked recessive","Iduronate-2-sulfatase","Dermatan and heparan sulfate","No corneal clouding, milder, aggressive behavior"]],"caption":"Inheritance and corneal clouding separate the two mucopolysaccharidoses, which store the same glycosaminoglycans."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C06","obj":"NE-20-E.6","title":"Wilson disease: copper transport and basal ganglia injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dietary copper is absorbed in the intestine and carried to the liver, which excretes it almost entirely into bile. In the hepatocyte, the copper-transporting ATPase ATP7B, encoded on chromosome 13, does two jobs: it loads copper onto apoceruloplasmin in the trans-Golgi network, and it moves excess copper into bile. Wilson disease (hepatolenticular degeneration) is autosomal recessive loss of ATP7B. Copper cannot be excreted, so it accumulates first in the liver, and apoceruloplasmin that receives no copper is rapidly degraded, so serum ceruloplasmin is low."},{"type":"flow","title":"Copper overload in Wilson disease","steps":["ATP7B mutation in hepatocytes","Copper not excreted into bile or loaded onto ceruloplasmin","Hepatocyte copper rises; free radical injury; hepatitis, cirrhosis","Free (non-ceruloplasmin) copper spills into blood","Copper deposits in basal ganglia, cornea, and kidney","Movement disorder, psychiatric change, Kayser-Fleischer rings"],"caption":"The liver fails first; extrahepatic deposition follows when hepatic storage is overwhelmed."},{"type":"p","text":"Free copper catalyzes formation of hydroxyl radicals (Fenton chemistry), injuring mitochondria and membranes. Hepatic disease, from asymptomatic transaminase elevation to chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, or acute liver failure, usually appears in childhood or adolescence. Sudden release of copper from necrotic hepatocytes can cause Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia, and renal tubular deposits can produce a Fanconi-type defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurologic disease usually presents in the late teens to 30s. Copper damages the lenticular nucleus (putamen and globus pallidus), along with thalamus and brainstem, disrupting basal ganglia motor circuits. The result is tremor (classically a coarse, proximal wing-beating tremor), dystonia, rigidity and bradykinesia, dysarthria, drooling, and incoordination. Injury to frontal-striatal circuits explains psychiatric change, including depression, personality change, impulsivity, and psychosis, which may precede the motor signs. Kayser-Fleischer rings are golden-brown copper deposits in Descemet membrane at the corneal margin; they are present in nearly all patients with neurologic disease but in only about half of those with hepatic disease alone."},{"type":"example","title":"Why serum copper is low in a copper overload disease","text":"A 19-year-old with tremor and dysarthria has low serum ceruloplasmin, low total serum copper, and high 24-hour urine copper. Step 1: about 90% of serum copper is normally bound to ceruloplasmin. Step 2: without ATP7B, apoceruloplasmin is not loaded and is degraded, so ceruloplasmin and the bound copper fall, lowering total serum copper. Step 3: the unbound (free) copper fraction is actually increased, because the overloaded liver releases it. Step 4: free copper is filtered by the kidney, so urine copper rises, and it deposits in tissues. Total serum copper therefore misleads; ceruloplasmin, urine copper, and liver copper reflect the true overload."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory and imaging findings in Wilson disease","cols":["Test","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Serum ceruloplasmin","Low (often below about 20 mg/dL)","Apoceruloplasmin degraded without copper loading"],["Total serum copper","Usually low","Less ceruloplasmin-bound copper"],["Free serum copper","High","Release from overloaded liver"],["24-hour urine copper","High","Filtered free copper"],["Hepatic copper content","High","Failed biliary excretion"],["Slit lamp","Kayser-Fleischer rings","Copper in Descemet membrane"],["Brain MRI","Basal ganglia signal change","Copper deposition and injury"]],"caption":"Ceruloplasmin is low because it is unloaded, not because copper is scarce."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C07","obj":"NE-20-E.7","title":"Fragile X syndrome: repeat expansion and gene silencing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fragile X syndrome is the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability, and the most common single-gene cause of autism. Down syndrome is more common overall, but it is usually a new nondisjunction event rather than an inherited change. Fragile X results from expansion of a CGG trinucleotide repeat in the 5 prime untranslated region of FMR1 on the X chromosome."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal alleles carry fewer than about 45 repeats. Premutation alleles (about 55 to 200 repeats) are unstable and tend to expand when passed through a mother's meiosis, which explains anticipation: severity increases over generations, but only through female transmission. A full mutation, above about 200 repeats, triggers methylation of the repeat and the CpG island of the FMR1 promoter. The methylated promoter is silenced, so no FMR1 protein (FMRP) is made. The disease is therefore caused by loss of function through epigenetic silencing, not by a toxic protein."},{"type":"flow","title":"From CGG expansion to synaptic dysfunction","steps":["Premutation expands during maternal meiosis","More than about 200 CGG repeats in FMR1","Promoter CpG island hypermethylated","FMR1 transcription silenced; FMRP absent","Unregulated synaptic protein synthesis; immature dendritic spines","Intellectual disability, autism features, seizures"],"caption":"Fragile X is a loss of function disorder caused by epigenetic silencing."},{"type":"p","text":"FMRP is an RNA-binding protein that normally restrains local translation of specific messenger RNAs at synapses, in part downstream of group I metabotropic glutamate receptors. Without it, protein synthesis at synapses is excessive and unregulated, dendritic spines remain long, thin, and immature, and synaptic plasticity is abnormal. Boys with the full mutation show moderate intellectual disability, autism features (gaze avoidance, hand flapping, social anxiety), hyperactivity, and seizures in some. Physical features include a long narrow face, prominent jaw, large protruding ears, joint laxity, and macroorchidism that becomes evident after puberty; mitral valve prolapse also occurs. Females with a full mutation are affected more mildly and variably because of X inactivation."},{"type":"p","text":"Premutation carriers do not have fragile X syndrome but have distinct risks: primary ovarian insufficiency in women and fragile X-associated tremor and ataxia syndrome in older men, both attributed to toxic effects of the overproduced repeat-containing RNA. Diagnosis uses PCR and Southern blot to size the repeat and assess methylation; the cytogenetic fragile site that named the disease is no longer used for diagnosis."},{"type":"table","title":"FMR1 repeat categories","cols":["Category","CGG repeats (about)","Consequence"],"rows":[["Normal","Fewer than 45","Stable"],["Premutation","55 to 200","Unstable in female meiosis; ovarian insufficiency or late-onset tremor and ataxia"],["Full mutation","More than 200","Methylation, silencing, fragile X syndrome"]],"caption":"Alleles of about 45 to 54 repeats form a gray zone with uncertain stability."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C08","obj":"NE-20-E.8","title":"Maple syrup urine disease and ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both disorders present as a neonatal encephalopathy: an infant who is well at birth becomes lethargic, feeds poorly, vomits, and may seize or become comatose within days, once protein feeding delivers a substrate load the blocked pathway cannot handle. The toxic molecule differs."},{"type":"h","text":"Maple syrup urine disease"},{"type":"p","text":"The branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine are first transaminated to their alpha-ketoacids, which are then oxidatively decarboxylated by the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex. This mitochondrial complex resembles pyruvate dehydrogenase and uses the same cofactors (thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoate, coenzyme A, FAD, and NAD+). In maple syrup urine disease, an autosomal recessive deficiency of this complex, the amino acids and their ketoacids accumulate. Leucine and its ketoacid are the most neurotoxic: leucine competes with other large neutral amino acids for brain uptake, and accumulation causes cerebral edema. Infants show poor feeding, alternating hypotonia and hypertonia, opisthotonus, seizures, and ketoacidosis. The sweet, maple syrup odor of urine and cerumen comes from a compound derived from isoleucine; plasma alloisoleucine is diagnostic."},{"type":"h","text":"Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency"},{"type":"p","text":"Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) combines carbamoyl phosphate with ornithine in the mitochondria to form citrulline. OTC deficiency is the most common urea cycle disorder and the only urea cycle disorder inherited in an X-linked pattern, so affected boys present as newborns while heterozygous girls may have episodes later, after protein loads or illness. Ammonia rises because nitrogen cannot be converted to urea, and unused carbamoyl phosphate leaves the mitochondrion and enters cytosolic pyrimidine synthesis, producing excess orotic acid in urine."},{"type":"flow","title":"How hyperammonemia injures the brain","steps":["Urea cycle blocked; blood ammonia rises","Ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier","Astrocyte glutamine synthetase converts ammonia and glutamate to glutamine","Glutamine accumulates; astrocytes swell osmotically","Cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure","Vomiting, lethargy, seizures, coma"],"caption":"Ammonia also stimulates the respiratory center, so early hyperammonemia often causes respiratory alkalosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Separating OTC deficiency from look-alikes","text":"A 3-day-old boy has vomiting, lethargy, high ammonia, normal anion gap, and high urine orotic acid. Step 1: high ammonia without ketoacidosis points to a urea cycle defect rather than an organic acidemia or maple syrup urine disease. Step 2: carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I deficiency also causes hyperammonemia, but carbamoyl phosphate is not made, so orotic acid is not increased. Step 3: hereditary orotic aciduria (UMP synthase deficiency) raises orotic acid but causes megaloblastic anemia, not hyperammonemia. Step 4: hyperammonemia plus orotic aciduria in a boy fits OTC deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C09","obj":"NE-20-E.9","title":"Acute intermittent porphyria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heme synthesis begins in the mitochondria, where aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthase joins glycine and succinyl CoA to form ALA; this is the rate-limiting step. In the liver, the ALAS1 isoform is normally held in check by heme through negative feedback. ALA dehydratase then forms porphobilinogen (PBG), and porphobilinogen deaminase (also called hydroxymethylbilane synthase) joins four PBG molecules into the linear tetrapyrrole hydroxymethylbilane, which later steps convert to porphyrins and heme."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is autosomal dominant partial deficiency of porphobilinogen deaminase, about 50% of normal activity. Most carriers never have symptoms, because the remaining enzyme meets ordinary demand. Attacks occur when hepatic heme demand rises and ALAS1 is induced. Cytochrome P450 enzymes are heme proteins, so drugs that induce them (barbiturates, carbamazepine, phenytoin, rifampin, griseofulvin) consume heme and release ALAS1 from feedback. Alcohol and smoking act similarly. Fasting and low carbohydrate intake induce ALAS1 through the same signals that drive gluconeogenesis, which is also why glucose suppresses it. Progesterone induces ALAS1, so attacks are more common in women after puberty and in the luteal phase. With the deaminase step limiting, ALA and PBG pile up behind the block."},{"type":"flow","title":"Trigger to attack in acute intermittent porphyria","steps":["Trigger: P450 inducer, alcohol, fasting, or progesterone","Hepatic heme falls or demand rises; ALAS1 induced","ALA production increases sharply","Partial PBG deaminase deficiency becomes rate limiting","ALA and PBG accumulate in blood and urine","Neurovisceral attack: pain, neuropathy, psychiatric change"],"caption":"The block is partial, so symptoms appear only when ALA synthesis is driven above what the remaining enzyme can process."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations are neurologic. ALA is neurotoxic and resembles GABA, and heme deficiency in neurons may also contribute. Autonomic neuropathy produces the dominant symptom, severe poorly localized abdominal pain with nausea, vomiting, and constipation, often with tachycardia and hypertension; the abdomen is soft because the pain is neuropathic, not inflammatory. A motor axonal neuropathy can follow, often proximal and in the arms first, and can progress to respiratory weakness. Central effects include anxiety, confusion, hallucinations, and seizures, and hypothalamic involvement can cause SIADH with hyponatremia. Urine may darken to red-brown on standing as PBG converts to porphobilin and porphyrins. The key laboratory finding during an attack is markedly elevated urine PBG."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does acute intermittent porphyria cause no skin photosensitivity, unlike porphyria cutanea tarda?","explain":"Photosensitivity requires porphyrins, which absorb light and generate reactive oxygen species in skin. The AIP block lies before any porphyrin ring is formed, so ALA and PBG accumulate instead, and these are neurotoxic but not photoactive.","check":"The description of the heme pathway and where the deaminase acts."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-05-C10","obj":"NE-20-E.10","title":"Causes of congenital microcephaly","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Microcephaly is a head circumference more than 2 standard deviations below the mean for age and sex; more than 3 standard deviations is severe. Because the skull grows in response to the growing brain, a small head almost always means a small brain. Brain size depends on how many neurons neural progenitor cells produce during the first and second trimesters and on how well those neurons survive. Anything that kills progenitors, halts their division, or destroys developing neurons in that window can cause microcephaly, so the causes are genetic (single-gene primary microcephaly, chromosomal syndromes), infectious, toxic, and metabolic. Microcephaly present at birth points to injury in utero; acquired microcephaly, in which head growth slows after birth, suggests later injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital infections are the best-tested causes. Zika virus has strong tropism for neural progenitor cells and kills them, producing severe microcephaly, sometimes with a collapsed skull and excess scalp skin, along with subcortical calcifications, ventriculomegaly, eye abnormalities, and joint contractures from loss of motor neurons. Cytomegalovirus is the most common congenital infection; it replicates in the germinal matrix around the ventricles, producing periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, chorioretinitis, and sensorineural hearing loss. Congenital rubella, most damaging in the first trimester, inhibits cell division and damages vessels, causing cataracts, sensorineural deafness, patent ductus arteriosus, and microcephaly. Congenital toxoplasmosis causes diffuse intracranial calcifications and chorioretinitis; it more often causes hydrocephalus, but destructive disease can leave microcephaly."},{"type":"p","text":"Ethanol is the most common preventable teratogenic cause. It triggers apoptosis of neural progenitors and disrupts neuronal migration and neural crest cells, producing fetal alcohol syndrome: microcephaly, growth restriction, intellectual disability, and a smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, and short palpebral fissures. Maternal PKU, described earlier, injures the fetus through high maternal phenylalanine."},{"type":"table","title":"Prenatal causes of microcephaly and their distinguishing clues","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Distinguishing clues"],"rows":[["Zika virus","Infects and kills neural progenitors","Severe microcephaly, subcortical calcifications, joint contractures, eye lesions"],["Cytomegalovirus","Periventricular germinal matrix injury","Periventricular calcifications, sensorineural hearing loss, chorioretinitis"],["Rubella","Inhibits cell division; vascular injury","Cataracts, deafness, patent ductus arteriosus; first trimester"],["Toxoplasma gondii","Necrotizing parenchymal infection","Diffuse calcifications, chorioretinitis; hydrocephalus more typical"],["Fetal alcohol syndrome","Progenitor apoptosis; migration defects","Smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, short palpebral fissures, growth restriction"],["Maternal PKU","Placental transfer of high phenylalanine","Infant without PKU; heart defects, growth restriction"]],"caption":"Calcification pattern separates the infections: periventricular in CMV, diffuse in toxoplasmosis, subcortical in Zika."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The diseases in this chapter share one logic. A single defect, an extra chromosome, a silenced gene, a missing enzyme, or a failed transporter, either removes a needed product (tyrosine, FMRP, ceruloplasmin, heme) or lets a toxic substrate accumulate (phenylalanine, leucine, ammonia, copper, ALA, stored lipid). Anatomy tells you where it lands: substrate collects in the cells that turn it over, so gangliosides fill neurons, sphingomyelin and glucocerebroside fill macrophages, galactolipids destroy myelinating cells, and copper damages the liver before the lenticular nucleus. Genetics explains who is affected: X-linked disorders (adrenoleukodystrophy, Fabry, Hunter, OTC, fragile X) strike boys, with variable disease in heterozygous girls through X inactivation, and the autosomal dominant AIP depends on environmental triggers to become symptomatic."},{"type":"p","text":"Timing explains the clinical picture. Metabolic toxins cleared by the placenta (phenylalanine, leucine, ammonia) spare the fetus and injure the newborn once feeding begins, whereas teratogens and congenital infections act in utero and leave microcephaly at birth. Classic exam distinctions rest on single discriminators: hepatosplenomegaly for Niemann-Pick versus Tay-Sachs, corneal clouding for Hurler versus Hunter, orotic acid for OTC versus CPS I deficiency, and adrenal failure for adrenoleukodystrophy. Real patients show wide variation in onset and severity within each disease, so these patterns describe the classic forms rather than every case."}],"summary":["Trisomy 21 usually reflects maternal meiosis I nondisjunction; the extra APP gene causes Alzheimer pathology by about age 40, and endocardial cushion, duodenal, and ligament defects explain AV septal defect, duodenal atresia, and atlantoaxial instability.","Adrenoleukodystrophy is X-linked ABCD1 failure of peroxisomal VLCFA import with demyelination and adrenal insufficiency; metachromatic leukodystrophy (arylsulfatase A, sulfatides) and Krabbe disease (galactocerebrosidase, psychosine, globoid cells) are lysosomal and also demyelinate peripheral nerve.","PKU blocks phenylalanine to tyrosine conversion; high phenylalanine starves the brain of other large neutral amino acids, tyrosine becomes essential, and BH4 defects also impair dopamine and serotonin synthesis.","Maternal hyperphenylalaninemia is teratogenic, causing microcephaly and heart defects in an infant who does not have PKU.","Tay-Sachs (hexosaminidase A, GM2) and Niemann-Pick A (sphingomyelinase) both give a cherry-red macula; only Niemann-Pick has hepatosplenomegaly and foam cells.","Fabry disease is X-linked alpha-galactosidase A deficiency with painful small fiber neuropathy, angiokeratomas, and early stroke; Gaucher types 2 and 3 are neuronopathic; Hurler has corneal clouding and Hunter, X-linked, does not.","Wilson disease is autosomal recessive ATP7B loss: no biliary copper excretion, low ceruloplasmin, high urine copper, lenticular nucleus injury, and Kayser-Fleischer rings.","Fragile X is FMR1 CGG expansion above about 200 repeats, with promoter hypermethylation and loss of FMRP; expansion occurs through maternal transmission.","Maple syrup urine disease (branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase) and OTC deficiency (X-linked, hyperammonemia with orotic aciduria) cause neonatal encephalopathy after feeding begins.","AIP is autosomal dominant PBG deaminase deficiency unmasked by ALAS1 inducers, causing neurovisceral attacks with high urine PBG and no photosensitivity."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases; The Central Nervous System, sections on leukodystrophies and inborn errors of metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Amino Acids: Degradation and Synthesis; Conversion of Amino Acids to Specialized Products; Phospholipid, Glycosphingolipid, and Eicosanoid Metabolism; Glycosaminoglycans, Proteoglycans, and Glycoproteins"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System; Birth Defects and Prenatal Diagnosis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Cytomegalovirus, rubella virus, flaviviruses, and Toxoplasma"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Genetic disease (fragile X syndrome) and nervous system disorders"}]},"NE-20-F":{"unit":"NE-20-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06","title":"Neoplasms of the Nervous System","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the primary tumors of the brain, meninges, and peripheral nerves, the neural crest tumor neuroblastoma, brain metastases and primary CNS lymphoma, the two classic eye tumors, and the inherited syndromes that predispose to nervous system tumors. Board questions usually give three clues: the patient's age, the tumor's location, and one histologic or molecular feature. This chapter explains why each clue appears: why childhood tumors favor the posterior fossa, why glioblastoma necroses and enhances in a ring, why a germline tumor suppressor mutation produces bilateral or multiple tumors, and why the location of a mass, more than its grade, decides the symptoms.","prerequisites":["Cell types of nervous tissue: neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependymal cells, Schwann cells, and meningothelial cells","CSF production and flow through the ventricles, foramina, and subarachnoid space","Neural crest derivatives: sympathetic ganglia, adrenal medulla, Schwann cells, and melanocytes","Oncogenes, tumor suppressor genes, and the Knudson two-hit hypothesis","Catecholamine synthesis and breakdown by MAO and COMT"],"connections":[{"to":"General neoplasia (Robbins)","how":"Ras, RB, HIF, and mTOR pathways explain how single gene losses in NF1, retinoblastoma, VHL, and tuberous sclerosis produce tumors."},{"to":"Neurogenetics (NE-16)","how":"NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, VHL, and hereditary retinoblastoma are autosomal dominant at the family level but need a second hit in each tumor cell."},{"to":"Raised intracranial pressure and hydrocephalus (NE-20-C)","how":"Many tumors cause symptoms by blocking CSF pathways or adding volume to a closed skull rather than by destroying tissue."},{"to":"HIV and opportunistic infection","how":"Primary CNS lymphoma and toxoplasmosis both appear as ring-enhancing lesions when CD4 counts are very low."},{"to":"Renal and endocrine pathology","how":"Clear cell renal carcinoma and pheochromocytoma link VHL and NF1 to the kidney and adrenal medulla."}],"terms":[{"term":"Extra-axial","def":"Located outside the brain parenchyma, as with a dura-based meningioma, so the tumor compresses rather than infiltrates the brain."},{"term":"Diffuse (infiltrative) glioma","def":"A glioma whose cells migrate individually into surrounding brain, so it has no true margin and cannot be removed completely."},{"term":"Small round blue cell tumor","def":"A tumor of primitive cells with dark nuclei and scant cytoplasm on H and E stain, such as medulloblastoma, neuroblastoma, and retinoblastoma."},{"term":"Homer Wright rosette","def":"A ring of tumor nuclei around a central tangle of fibrillary cell processes without a lumen, a sign of neuronal differentiation."},{"term":"Pseudopalisading necrosis","def":"A zone of necrosis bordered by tumor cells lined up at its edge, the defining necrosis pattern of glioblastoma."},{"term":"Psammoma body","def":"A laminated, concentric calcification formed by dystrophic calcification within a tumor, typical of meningioma."},{"term":"Rosenthal fiber","def":"A brightly eosinophilic, corkscrew-shaped aggregate of GFAP and other proteins in astrocytic processes, typical of pilocytic astrocytoma."},{"term":"Verocay body","def":"Two rows of palisaded Schwann cell nuclei separated by a fibrillary zone, found in the Antoni A areas of schwannoma."},{"term":"Drop metastasis","def":"Seeding of tumor cells through the CSF onto the spinal cord, nerve roots, or cauda equina."},{"term":"IDH mutation","def":"A mutation in isocitrate dehydrogenase that makes the enzyme produce 2-hydroxyglutarate and defines the astrocytoma and oligodendroglioma lineage."},{"term":"Mass effect","def":"Displacement and compression of brain by a tumor and its edema within the fixed volume of the skull."},{"term":"Hamartoma","def":"A disorganized overgrowth of cell types native to a site, as in the cortical tubers of tuberous sclerosis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C01","obj":"NE-20-F.1","title":"Medulloblastoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant brain tumor of childhood, and most patients are diagnosed before age 10. It is an embryonal tumor, meaning it is made of primitive precursor cells that have not differentiated, and it is classified as CNS WHO grade 4. The classic site of medulloblastoma is the cerebellar vermis, in the roof of the fourth ventricle; tumors in older children and adults more often arise laterally in a cerebellar hemisphere."},{"type":"p","text":"Under the microscope the tumor forms dense sheets of small cells with dark, crowded nuclei and very little cytoplasm, the pattern called a small round blue cell tumor. Some cells arrange themselves in Homer Wright rosettes, rings of nuclei around a central tangle of neuritic processes with no lumen. These rosettes and staining for synaptophysin show that the tumor is attempting neuronal differentiation. Medulloblastoma is now also divided into molecular groups: WNT-activated tumors have the best prognosis, SHH-activated tumors arise from cerebellar granule cell precursors and occur in Gorlin syndrome (germline PTCH1) and Li-Fraumeni syndrome (germline TP53), and group 3 tumors, often MYC amplified, do worst."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the symptoms appear"},{"type":"p","text":"A vermian mass presses directly on the fourth ventricle, the outflow path for CSF from the lateral and third ventricles. Blocking it produces obstructive (noncommunicating) hydrocephalus. Headache and vomiting are typically worst on waking because intracranial pressure rises when the child lies flat and when nocturnal hypoventilation raises arterial CO2, which dilates cerebral vessels. Raised pressure also causes papilledema and sometimes a sixth nerve palsy, a false localizing sign from stretching of the long intracranial course of the abducens nerve. In infants with open sutures, the head enlarges instead. Damage to the vermis itself causes truncal ataxia with a wide-based, unsteady gait, while limb coordination may be relatively spared."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the tumor sits against the CSF pathway, cells shed into the CSF and settle by gravity on the spinal cord, nerve roots, and cauda equina as drop metastases. For this reason staging examines the entire neuraxis and the CSF, not only the posterior fossa."},{"type":"flow","title":"Medulloblastoma: from vermis mass to raised pressure","steps":["Embryonal tumor grows in the cerebellar vermis","Mass compresses the fourth ventricle","CSF outflow is blocked","Lateral and third ventricles dilate","Intracranial pressure rises","Morning headache, vomiting, papilledema, sixth nerve palsy"],"caption":"Obstructive hydrocephalus explains the early pressure symptoms; vermis damage separately explains truncal ataxia."},{"type":"table","title":"Common posterior fossa tumors of childhood","cols":["Tumor","Usual site","Key features","Behavior"],"rows":[["Medulloblastoma","Vermis, roof of fourth ventricle","Small round blue cells, Homer Wright rosettes","Grade 4, CSF seeding"],["Pilocytic astrocytoma","Cerebellar hemisphere","Rosenthal fibers, cyst with mural nodule","Grade 1, often cured by resection"],["Ependymoma","Floor of fourth ventricle","Perivascular pseudorosettes, ependymal rosettes","Variable; can block CSF flow"]],"caption":"Children have mostly infratentorial tumors and adults mostly supratentorial ones; location and histology together identify the tumor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C02","obj":"NE-20-F.2","title":"Meningioma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Meningioma is the most common primary intracranial tumor in adults. It arises from arachnoid cap (meningothelial) cells, which cluster in the arachnoid villi and granulations along the dural venous sinuses. The tumor is attached to the dura and lies outside the brain parenchyma, so it is extra-axial: it pushes the brain aside rather than growing into it. It is most common in middle-aged and older adults, and intracranial meningiomas are about twice as common in women as in men. Many tumors express progesterone receptors, which fits the female predominance and the occasional growth during pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"The two best established risk factors both damage growth control in meningothelial cells. Loss of the NF2 gene on chromosome 22, which encodes the tumor suppressor merlin, is found in about half of sporadic meningiomas, and patients with NF2 often have multiple meningiomas. Ionizing radiation to the head, for example childhood cranial irradiation, increases risk after a latency of many years, and radiation-induced tumors are more often multiple and higher grade."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic histology is whorls of meningothelial cells, some of which calcify in concentric layers to form psammoma bodies. The cells stain for epithelial membrane antigen. Most meningiomas are CNS WHO grade 1. Brain invasion or high mitotic activity defines atypical (grade 2) tumors, and frankly malignant histology defines anaplastic (grade 3) tumors. On imaging the tumor is a well-circumscribed, uniformly enhancing dural mass, often with a tapering dural tail and thickening (hyperostosis) of the overlying bone."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms come from compression and depend on location. Irritation of the underlying cortex causes focal seizures, and pressure on cortex or cranial nerves causes focal deficits. Because growth is slow, the brain adapts, so meningiomas can become large before causing symptoms and many are found incidentally."},{"type":"table","title":"Meningioma location predicts the deficit","cols":["Location","Structure compressed","Typical finding"],"rows":[["Parasagittal or falx (near the paracentral lobule)","Leg area of motor and sensory cortex","Contralateral leg weakness or numbness; both legs if bilateral"],["Cerebral convexity","Adjacent cortex","Focal seizures, focal weakness, or language deficit"],["Olfactory groove","Olfactory tracts and frontal lobes","Anosmia, personality change; later optic nerve compression"],["Sphenoid wing or cavernous sinus","Optic nerve, CN III, IV, VI","Visual loss, diplopia, proptosis"],["Cerebellopontine angle","CN VIII, V, VII","Hearing loss and facial numbness, mimicking schwannoma"],["Spinal (often thoracic, in women)","Spinal cord","Slowly progressive myelopathy"]],"caption":"Meningioma causes symptoms by mass effect, so the site of the dural attachment predicts the deficit."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C03","obj":"NE-20-F.3","title":"Neuroblastoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural crest cells migrate from the dorsal neural tube to form the sympathetic chain ganglia and the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. Neuroblastoma is a malignant tumor of these sympathetic precursor cells. It is the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood and the most common cancer of infancy; the median age at diagnosis is about 18 months, and about 90% of cases are found before age 5. The adrenal medulla is the most common primary site, but the tumor can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain, including the paraspinal abdomen, posterior mediastinum, neck, and pelvis."},{"type":"p","text":"Histologically it is another small round blue cell tumor, with Homer Wright rosettes and a fibrillary background of neuronal processes (neuropil). The cells stain for neuronal markers such as synaptophysin and chromogranin. Maturation toward ganglion cells and Schwannian stroma (ganglioneuroblastoma, ganglioneuroma) improves behavior."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the cells are sympathetic precursors, most tumors synthesize catecholamines. Much of the catecholamine is metabolized inside the tumor, so the urine contains high homovanillic acid (HVA) and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) in about 90% of patients, while sustained hypertension is less prominent than in pheochromocytoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why urinary HVA and VMA rise","steps":["Tyrosine converted to L-dopa by tyrosine hydroxylase","L-dopa decarboxylated to dopamine","Dopamine broken down by MAO and COMT to HVA","Dopamine converted to norepinephrine (some to epinephrine)","Norepinephrine and epinephrine broken down to VMA","Both metabolites excreted in urine"],"caption":"HVA reflects dopamine and VMA reflects norepinephrine and epinephrine, so both rise when sympathetic precursor cells proliferate."},{"type":"p","text":"The usual presentation is a firm, irregular abdominal mass in a young child. A retroperitoneal neuroblastoma grows around the aorta and its branches and often crosses the midline. Metastases explain many presentations: bone and marrow (bone pain, limp, cytopenias), liver (hepatomegaly in infants), skin (bluish nodules in infants), and the orbits (proptosis and periorbital bruising). A paraspinal tumor can grow through a neural foramen and compress the spinal cord, and a cervical or upper thoracic tumor can interrupt the sympathetic chain and cause Horner syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Opsoclonus-myoclonus (rapid chaotic eye movements, limb jerks, and ataxia) is a paraneoplastic syndrome in a small percentage of children, thought to result from antitumor immunity that cross-reacts with neurons. Amplification of the MYCN oncogene, found in about a fifth of tumors, drives proliferation and predicts aggressive disease regardless of age. At the other extreme, some infants with metastatic disease limited to skin, liver, and marrow have tumors that regress spontaneously."},{"type":"table","title":"Neuroblastoma compared with Wilms tumor","cols":["Feature","Neuroblastoma","Wilms tumor"],"rows":[["Cell of origin","Neural crest sympathetic precursor","Metanephric blastema of the kidney"],["Usual age","Median about 18 months","Usually 2 to 5 years"],["Mass","Irregular, often crosses midline, encases vessels","Smooth, within the kidney, rarely crosses midline"],["Calcification on imaging","Common","Uncommon"],["Urine catecholamine metabolites","HVA and VMA elevated","Normal"],["Key genetics","MYCN amplification (poor prognosis)","WT1 mutation or 11p15 (WT2) alterations in some cases"]],"caption":"Both present as abdominal masses in young children; origin outside the kidney explains the neuroblastoma features."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C04","obj":"NE-20-F.4","title":"Glioblastoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Glioblastoma is the most common primary malignant brain tumor in adults, usually in people older than 50. Under the current WHO classification it is defined as a diffuse astrocytic glioma of adults that is IDH-wildtype, and it is always CNS WHO grade 4. Its astrocytic nature is shown by staining for glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP), the intermediate filament of astrocytes. IDH-mutant grade 4 astrocytomas, once called secondary glioblastoma, are now classified separately because they arise in younger patients and have a better prognosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The tumor arises in the cerebral hemispheres, centered in white matter. Its cells migrate individually along myelinated tracts, so a glioblastoma can cross the corpus callosum to the opposite hemisphere and form a butterfly-shaped mass. Tumor cells are present well beyond the enhancing edge seen on imaging, which is why the tumor recurs even after extensive resection."},{"type":"h","text":"Necrosis, new vessels, and the ring"},{"type":"p","text":"Glioblastoma outgrows its blood supply. The hypoxic center dies, and tumor cells line up along the edge of each necrotic zone (pseudopalisading necrosis), probably as they migrate away from the hypoxic core. Hypoxia stabilizes the transcription factor HIF-1 alpha, which induces vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). VEGF drives microvascular proliferation, tufts of multilayered endothelial cells that can look like renal glomeruli. These new vessels lack a normal blood-brain barrier. On contrast imaging this produces a thick, irregular rim of enhancement around a dark necrotic center, surrounded by vasogenic edema from leaky vessels."},{"type":"flow","title":"Glioblastoma: from hypoxia to ring enhancement","steps":["Tumor outgrows its blood supply","Central hypoxia and necrosis","Cells palisade around necrotic zones","HIF-1 alpha stabilized, VEGF secreted","Leaky microvascular proliferation","Ring enhancement and vasogenic edema"],"caption":"The same hypoxia-VEGF chain explains the two defining histologic features and the imaging appearance."},{"type":"p","text":"Common molecular changes include EGFR amplification, PTEN loss (unchecked PI3K-AKT signaling), CDKN2A deletion (loss of the p16 brake on the RB pathway), and TERT promoter mutation (sustained telomerase). In an IDH-wildtype diffuse astrocytic tumor, TERT promoter mutation, EGFR amplification, or gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10 is enough for the diagnosis even before necrosis or microvascular proliferation appears. Methylation of the MGMT promoter silences a DNA repair enzyme that removes alkyl groups from the O6 position of guanine; such tumors repair alkylating DNA damage poorly and have longer survival."},{"type":"p","text":"Patients develop headache, seizures, personality change, or focal deficits over weeks to months as the mass and edema expand. Median survival is only about 15 months even with maximal therapy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C05","obj":"NE-20-F.5","title":"Pilocytic astrocytoma versus IDH-mutant diffuse gliomas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gliomas divide into two behaviors. Circumscribed gliomas such as pilocytic astrocytoma form a mass with a border and can often be cured by removal. Diffuse gliomas send single cells into the surrounding brain, so even a slow-growing, low-grade diffuse glioma cannot be completely removed and tends to recur and progress. This growth pattern, more than grade at diagnosis, explains the difference in outcome."},{"type":"p","text":"Pilocytic astrocytoma is CNS WHO grade 1 and is the most common brain tumor of children overall. The classic picture is a cerebellar cyst with an enhancing mural nodule; other sites are the optic nerve and chiasm (especially in NF1), hypothalamus, and brainstem. Histology is biphasic: compact areas of elongated, hair-like (piloid) cells containing Rosenthal fibers, and loose microcystic areas with eosinophilic granular bodies. Rosenthal fibers are not specific, since they also appear in chronic reactive gliosis and Alexander disease. Almost all pilocytic astrocytomas carry an alteration that activates the MAPK pathway, most often a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion in cerebellar tumors, less often BRAF V600E or loss of NF1. Prognosis is excellent."},{"type":"p","text":"IDH-mutant diffuse gliomas arise mainly in the cerebral hemispheres of young and middle-aged adults, most often the frontal lobe, and frequently present with seizures because they infiltrate cortex. Mutant isocitrate dehydrogenase (most often IDH1 R132H) converts alpha-ketoglutarate to 2-hydroxyglutarate. This oncometabolite inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate dependent enzymes, including TET DNA demethylases and histone demethylases, so DNA becomes widely hypermethylated and cells are held in an undifferentiated, proliferative state."},{"type":"p","text":"Two tumors share this IDH-mutant origin. Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, usually has loss of ATRX and mutation of TP53 and is graded 2 to 4. Oligodendroglioma is defined by IDH mutation plus codeletion of whole chromosome arms 1p and 19q, is graded 2 or 3, and often calcifies. Its cells have round nuclei and clear cytoplasm, the fried-egg appearance (an artifact of delayed fixation), separated by a delicate branching chicken-wire capillary network."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the three gliomas","cols":["Feature","Pilocytic astrocytoma","Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant","Oligodendroglioma"],"rows":[["Age","Children and young adults","Young to middle-aged adults","Adults, often 40s to 50s"],["Site","Cerebellum, optic pathway","Cerebral hemispheres","Frontal lobe, cortex and white matter"],["Growth","Circumscribed, cyst with mural nodule","Diffuse","Diffuse, often calcified"],["Histology","Piloid cells, Rosenthal fibers","Infiltrating atypical astrocytes","Fried-egg cells, chicken-wire capillaries"],["Molecular","BRAF fusion or mutation","IDH, ATRX loss, TP53","IDH plus 1p/19q codeletion"],["Grade and outlook","Grade 1, excellent","Grade 2 to 4, progresses","Grade 2 or 3, better than astrocytoma"]],"caption":"Growth pattern and molecular markers separate these tumors more reliably than appearance alone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 9-year-old has a grade 1 cerebellar tumor and a 35-year-old has a grade 2 frontal tumor. Which patient is more likely to have recurrence after surgery?","explain":"The adult, because an IDH-mutant diffuse glioma infiltrates beyond any visible margin and tends to progress, while a circumscribed pilocytic astrocytoma can be removed whole.","check":"Reread the first paragraph on circumscribed versus diffuse growth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C06","obj":"NE-20-F.6","title":"Neuronal and mixed neuronal-glial tumors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most brain tumors arise from glia, because mature neurons do not divide. A small group of tumors, however, contains neoplastic cells with neuronal differentiation, either alone or mixed with neoplastic glia. These tumors are usually low grade, and their symptoms depend on where they sit: in the cortex they cause epilepsy, and in a ventricle they block CSF."},{"type":"p","text":"Ganglioglioma is the classic example. It is a slow-growing CNS WHO grade 1 tumor of children and young adults, most often in the temporal lobe. It contains two neoplastic populations: large, abnormal (dysmorphic) ganglion cells, sometimes with two nuclei, and a glial component that is usually astrocytic. Eosinophilic granular bodies, cuffs of lymphocytes around vessels, and calcification are common, and many tumors carry BRAF V600E. On imaging it is often a cortical cyst with an enhancing nodule and little surrounding edema."},{"type":"p","text":"The usual presentation is a long history of focal seizures that resist anticonvulsant drugs. The temporal lobe, particularly its mesial structures, has a low seizure threshold, and the abnormal neurons within and around the tumor form excitable networks. Because the tumor grows slowly, raised intracranial pressure is unusual. Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumor (DNT) is another low-grade cortical tumor that presents the same way."},{"type":"p","text":"Central neurocytoma is a CNS WHO grade 2 tumor of young adults that grows inside a lateral ventricle, often attached to the septum pellucidum near the foramen of Monro. Its uniform round cells with clear perinuclear halos resemble oligodendroglioma, but they stain for synaptophysin, showing neuronal differentiation, and the tumor arises within the ventricle rather than infiltrating brain. Symptoms come from obstruction of one or both foramina of Monro: the lateral ventricles enlarge and patients develop headache, vomiting, and papilledema. Prognosis is generally favorable."},{"type":"table","title":"Neuronal and mixed neuronal-glial tumors","cols":["Tumor","Patient and site","Distinguishing features","Presentation"],"rows":[["Ganglioglioma","Children and young adults; temporal lobe","Dysmorphic ganglion cells plus neoplastic glia; often BRAF V600E","Chronic drug-resistant focal seizures"],["Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumor","Children and young adults; temporal cortex","Multinodular cortical tumor with neurons floating in mucoid matrix","Chronic drug-resistant focal seizures"],["Central neurocytoma","Young adults; lateral ventricle near foramen of Monro","Uniform round cells with halos; synaptophysin positive","Obstructive hydrocephalus"]],"caption":"Cortical tumors cause epilepsy; ventricular tumors cause hydrocephalus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C07","obj":"NE-20-F.7","title":"Schwannoma and vestibular schwannoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Schwann cells, derived from the neural crest, myelinate peripheral axons, including cranial nerve axons beyond the short segment myelinated by oligodendrocytes. A schwannoma is a benign, encapsulated CNS WHO grade 1 tumor of Schwann cells. It grows eccentrically from one side of the nerve and pushes the axons aside, so the parent nerve can often be separated from it. Most sporadic schwannomas, like those in NF2, have lost function of the NF2 gene product merlin."},{"type":"p","text":"The histology has two patterns. Antoni A areas are densely cellular, with spindle cells whose nuclei line up in palisades; two rows of palisaded nuclei separated by a pink fibrillary zone form a Verocay body. Antoni B areas are loose, hypocellular, and myxoid. The cells stain strongly and uniformly for S-100, a marker of neural crest derived cells."},{"type":"h","text":"Vestibular schwannoma at the cerebellopontine angle"},{"type":"p","text":"The most common intracranial schwannoma arises from the vestibular division of CN VIII inside the internal acoustic canal; the old name acoustic neuroma is inaccurate on both counts. It grows out of the canal into the cerebellopontine angle, the space between the cerebellum, pons, and petrous bone. Compression of the cochlear nerve and its blood supply in the canal causes slowly progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss, the most common presenting complaint, often with tinnitus and speech discrimination worse than the pure-tone loss suggests. Vestibular input is lost so slowly that the brain compensates, so patients report imbalance more than true vertigo."},{"type":"p","text":"A larger tumor stretches CN V, causing facial numbness and a reduced corneal reflex through loss of its afferent limb. CN VII travels with CN VIII but its motor fibers tolerate stretch, so facial weakness is usually late. Further growth compresses the cerebellar peduncles and hemisphere (ipsilateral limb ataxia) and the fourth ventricle (hydrocephalus)."},{"type":"p","text":"Bilateral vestibular schwannomas are the hallmark of NF2, an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutation of NF2 on chromosome 22. Merlin mediates contact inhibition of growth, so a Schwann cell that loses its second copy proliferates. NF2 patients also develop meningiomas, spinal ependymomas, and early posterior subcapsular cataracts."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a cerebellopontine angle mass","text":"A 48-year-old has a year of worsening left-sided hearing loss and ringing. The Weber test lateralizes to the right ear, and air conduction exceeds bone conduction in both ears on the Rinne test. Step 1: Weber lateralizing away from the affected ear with a normal Rinne means sensorineural loss on the left, not conductive loss. Step 2: unilateral sensorineural loss with tinnitus points behind the cochlea to CN VIII. Step 3: the left corneal reflex is reduced, so the mass has grown far enough to stretch CN V, while facial movement is normal because CN VII motor fibers resist stretch. Step 4: an enhancing mass widening the internal acoustic canal fits vestibular schwannoma; a dural tail without canal widening would favor meningioma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C08","obj":"NE-20-F.8","title":"Neurofibromatosis type 1","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is one of the most common autosomal dominant disorders, affecting about 1 in 3,000 people. About half of cases are new mutations. Penetrance is essentially complete, but expressivity varies widely, even within one family. The NF1 gene on chromosome 17 encodes neurofibromin, a Ras GTPase-activating protein that speeds the hydrolysis of active Ras-GTP to inactive Ras-GDP. When a cell carrying the inherited mutation loses its second NF1 allele, Ras stays active and drives growth through the MAPK and PI3K pathways."},{"type":"p","text":"Most features involve tissues derived from the neural crest. Melanocyte changes produce cafe-au-lait macules (usually the first sign, in early childhood), freckling in the axillae and groin, and Lisch nodules, pigmented hamartomas of the iris that do not affect vision. Schwann cell proliferation produces neurofibromas."},{"type":"p","text":"A neurofibroma differs from a schwannoma in a way that explains its behavior. It is a mixture of Schwann cells, fibroblasts, perineurial-like cells, and mast cells, with the nerve's axons running through the tumor, so it cannot be separated from the nerve. Cutaneous neurofibromas are soft nodules that appear around puberty and increase with age. Plexiform neurofibromas are usually congenital, grow along long segments of a nerve trunk or plexus, and feel like a bag of worms under the skin. They can transform into a malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST), a high-grade sarcoma that NF1 patients develop at a lifetime risk of roughly 10%; new pain or rapid growth of a plexiform tumor suggests this change."},{"type":"p","text":"Other tumors follow from the same Ras activation. Optic pathway glioma, a pilocytic astrocytoma of the optic nerve or chiasm in young children, causes visual loss or proptosis, and hypothalamic involvement can cause precocious puberty. Pheochromocytoma, a tumor of adrenal chromaffin cells, occurs in a small percentage of patients and causes episodic or sustained hypertension from catecholamine release. Skeletal dysplasia (sphenoid wing, tibial pseudarthrosis, scoliosis) and learning disabilities are also common."},{"type":"table","title":"NF1 compared with NF2","cols":["Feature","NF1","NF2"],"rows":[["Gene and chromosome","NF1, chromosome 17","NF2, chromosome 22"],["Protein and role","Neurofibromin, Ras GTPase-activating protein","Merlin, cytoskeletal linker for contact inhibition"],["Nerve sheath tumor","Neurofibromas, cutaneous and plexiform","Schwannomas, bilateral vestibular"],["Other tumors","Optic pathway glioma, pheochromocytoma, MPNST","Meningiomas, spinal ependymomas"],["Skin and eye","Cafe-au-lait macules, freckling, Lisch nodules","Few skin findings; posterior subcapsular cataract"],["Inheritance","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal dominant"]],"caption":"Both are tumor suppressor disorders with a second hit in each tumor; the lost protein and the cells it controls explain the different tumors."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a child with NF1 develop tumors in only some Schwann cells, even though every cell carries the mutation?","explain":"One working NF1 allele still makes enough neurofibromin to control Ras; a tumor forms only in a cell that loses the second allele by a somatic event.","check":"Reread the first paragraph of this section on loss of the second NF1 allele."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C09","obj":"NE-20-F.9","title":"Brain metastases and primary CNS lymphoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Metastases are the most common intracranial tumors in adults. The brain receives about 15% of cardiac output, so tumor cells that reach the arterial circulation, usually after passing through or arising in the lung, are delivered there in large numbers. Lung cancer is the most frequent source, followed by breast cancer and melanoma; kidney and colorectal cancers are less common sources. Melanoma has the highest tendency to reach the brain relative to its frequency."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumor emboli lodge where arterioles narrow abruptly at the junction of gray and white matter and in the arterial border zones. Deposits are distributed roughly by blood flow, so most are in the cerebral hemispheres, fewer in the cerebellum, and few in the brainstem. They are often multiple, round, and sharply bordered, because they push brain aside rather than infiltrate it. New tumor vessels lack a blood-brain barrier, so even small metastases enhance and cause vasogenic edema out of proportion to their size. Melanoma, renal cell carcinoma, choriocarcinoma, and thyroid carcinoma metastases tend to bleed. Histology resembles the primary tumor and lacks GFAP. Leptomeningeal spread causes headache and multiple cranial nerve palsies."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary CNS lymphoma is a non-Hodgkin lymphoma confined to the brain, leptomeninges, spinal cord, or eyes, and almost all cases are diffuse large B cell lymphoma (CD20 positive). In immunocompetent patients it occurs mainly in older adults as a solitary, uniformly enhancing mass in the deep periventricular white matter, basal ganglia, or corpus callosum. The tumor cells cluster around blood vessels in concentric cuffs. Glucocorticoids trigger apoptosis of lymphoma cells, so a lesion can shrink after steroid exposure and a later biopsy may be nondiagnostic."},{"type":"p","text":"In AIDS with CD4 counts below about 50 cells per microliter, the tumor is almost always driven by Epstein-Barr virus. EBV latent proteins push infected B cells to proliferate and resist apoptosis, and without T cell surveillance they expand unchecked. These lesions are more often multiple, necrotic, and ring-enhancing, so they resemble cerebral toxoplasmosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Ring-enhancing lesions in advanced HIV","cols":["Feature","Primary CNS lymphoma","Toxoplasmosis"],"rows":[["Number","Often single","Usually multiple"],["Typical site","Periventricular white matter, corpus callosum","Basal ganglia and gray-white junction"],["Laboratory support","EBV DNA in CSF by PCR","Positive anti-Toxoplasma IgG"],["Metabolic imaging","Increased uptake (hypermetabolic)","Low uptake"]],"caption":"These are tendencies rather than rules; tissue diagnosis is definitive."},{"type":"example","title":"A ring-enhancing lesion in AIDS","text":"A 38-year-old with untreated HIV (CD4 count 30 cells per microliter) has confusion and right arm weakness. MRI shows one ring-enhancing lesion beside the left lateral ventricle extending into the corpus callosum. Step 1: at this CD4 count the two leading causes are toxoplasmosis and primary CNS lymphoma. Step 2: a single periventricular lesion involving the corpus callosum favors lymphoma; toxoplasmosis usually gives multiple basal ganglia and gray-white junction lesions. Step 3: EBV DNA in the CSF supports lymphoma because nearly all AIDS-related cases are EBV driven. Step 4: the tissue would show large atypical B cells cuffing vessels, CD20 positive, a diffuse large B cell lymphoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C10","obj":"NE-20-F.10","title":"Retinoblastoma and uveal melanoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Retinoblastoma is the most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood. It arises from primitive retinal cells and is usually diagnosed before age 5, at a younger age when bilateral. The RB1 gene on chromosome 13q14 encodes the retinoblastoma protein (pRb). In its unphosphorylated form pRb binds the transcription factor E2F and blocks entry from G1 into S phase; cyclin D with CDK4 or CDK6 phosphorylates pRb to release E2F when the cell should divide."},{"type":"p","text":"Retinoblastoma was the basis of the Knudson two-hit hypothesis. In hereditary cases, about 40% of the total, every cell carries a germline RB1 mutation, so only one somatic hit is needed in any retinal cell. Tumors are therefore earlier, bilateral, and multifocal. Most germline mutations are new, so many children have no family history, but each child of a hereditary survivor has about a 50% chance of inheriting it. In sporadic cases both hits must occur in the same retinal cell, so the tumor is unilateral, single, and later. Survivors of hereditary disease remain at risk for second cancers, most characteristically osteosarcoma, along with other sarcomas, particularly within a radiation field."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two hits in hereditary and sporadic retinoblastoma","steps":["Hereditary: germline RB1 mutation in every cell","One somatic hit in any retinal cell","Bilateral, multifocal, early tumors and later sarcomas","Sporadic: both RB1 copies normal at birth","Two somatic hits needed in one retinal cell","Unilateral, single, later tumor"],"caption":"The inherited first hit explains why the disease behaves as autosomal dominant in families while each tumor cell has lost both copies."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic sign is leukocoria: the white tumor reflects light where the normal red fundus reflex should be. Strabismus is the second most common sign, because a blind eye loses its fixation. The tumor often calcifies, and histology shows small round blue cells with Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes (rings around a true central lumen, reflecting photoreceptor differentiation) and Homer Wright rosettes. It can spread along the optic nerve to the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Uveal melanoma arises from melanocytes of the uveal tract, most often the choroid, and is the most common primary intraocular malignancy in adults (metastases to the eye are more common overall). Risk is higher in people with light skin and light irises. Unlike cutaneous melanoma it is not driven by ultraviolet damage or BRAF; it usually carries activating GNAQ or GNA11 mutations, and loss of BAP1 with monosomy 3 predicts metastasis. It causes blurred vision, flashes, floaters, or a field defect from retinal detachment, or is found on routine examination. The interior of the eye has no lymphatic drainage, so uveal melanoma spreads through the blood, most often to the liver, sometimes years after the eye tumor is found."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has one tumor in one eye at age 3 and no family history. Can you be sure the mutation is not hereditary?","explain":"No. Most germline RB1 mutations are new, so a hereditary case often has no family history; a single unilateral tumor only makes it less likely.","check":"Compare with the two-hit flow and the paragraph on hereditary cases."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-06-C11","obj":"NE-20-F.11","title":"Tuberous sclerosis and von Hippel-Lindau disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tuberous sclerosis complex is autosomal dominant, and about two thirds of cases are new mutations. It is caused by mutation of TSC1 (hamartin, chromosome 9) or TSC2 (tuberin, chromosome 16). Hamartin and tuberin form a complex that inactivates the small GTPase Rheb, which keeps mTORC1 switched off until growth signals arrive. Without the complex, mTORC1 stays active, driving protein synthesis and cell growth, so hamartomas form in many organs."},{"type":"p","text":"In the brain, cortical tubers are firm hamartomas of disorganized dysmorphic neurons, large balloon cells, and gliosis. They are highly epileptogenic, which explains infantile spasms, later focal epilepsy, intellectual disability, and autism. Subependymal nodules form along the walls of the lateral ventricles and often calcify. A subependymal giant cell astrocytoma (CNS WHO grade 1) grows from a nodule near the foramen of Monro and can block it, causing obstructive hydrocephalus. Skin findings include hypopigmented ash-leaf macules (accentuated under Wood lamp), facial angiofibromas, and shagreen patches. Cardiac rhabdomyomas are often detected before birth and usually regress afterward, and renal angiomyolipomas can bleed."},{"type":"p","text":"Von Hippel-Lindau disease is an autosomal dominant syndrome caused by mutation of VHL on chromosome 3p. The VHL protein is part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase. In normal oxygen, HIF-alpha is hydroxylated, VHL binds it, and the proteasome destroys it. Without VHL, HIF accumulates even when oxygen is normal, and cells act as if they were hypoxic, secreting VEGF, PDGF, and erythropoietin and increasing glucose uptake."},{"type":"flow","title":"VHL loss and pseudohypoxia","steps":["Second VHL allele lost in a susceptible cell","Hydroxylated HIF-alpha not ubiquitinated","HIF accumulates despite normal oxygen","VEGF, PDGF, and erythropoietin genes switched on","Highly vascular tumors and sometimes polycythemia"],"caption":"Constant HIF signaling explains why VHL tumors are richly vascular and why hemangioblastomas can raise the hematocrit."},{"type":"p","text":"The CNS lesion is the hemangioblastoma, a CNS WHO grade 1 tumor of lipid-laden stromal cells within a dense capillary network. It occurs in the cerebellum, brainstem, spinal cord, and retina. Cerebellar tumors are often a cyst with a mural nodule, causing ataxia and hydrocephalus, and some secrete erythropoietin. Retinal hemangioblastomas leak and cause retinal detachment and visual loss. Clear cell renal cell carcinoma, often multiple and bilateral, is a leading cause of death; pheochromocytoma and pancreatic cysts and neuroendocrine tumors also occur."},{"type":"table","title":"Tuberous sclerosis compared with von Hippel-Lindau disease","cols":["Feature","Tuberous sclerosis","Von Hippel-Lindau"],"rows":[["Genes","TSC1 (9q), TSC2 (16p)","VHL (3p)"],["Pathway","Loss of Rheb control, mTORC1 overactive","HIF not degraded, pseudohypoxia"],["CNS lesions","Cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, giant cell astrocytoma","Cerebellar, brainstem, spinal hemangioblastomas"],["Eye","Retinal hamartomas","Retinal hemangioblastomas"],["Skin and heart","Ash-leaf macules, angiofibromas; cardiac rhabdomyoma","No characteristic skin lesions"],["Kidney and other","Angiomyolipomas","Clear cell renal carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, pancreatic lesions"]],"caption":"Both are autosomal dominant tumor suppressor syndromes; the pathway each gene controls predicts the lesions."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Nervous system tumors are best approached by asking three questions. Anatomy answers where the mass is and therefore what it compresses or irritates: posterior fossa masses block the fourth ventricle, intraventricular masses block the foramina of Monro, cortical tumors cause seizures, and cerebellopontine angle masses affect CN VIII, then V, then VII. Histology identifies the cell lineage through rosettes, Rosenthal fibers, Verocay bodies, whorls, and pseudopalisading necrosis, supported by stains such as GFAP, synaptophysin, S-100, and epithelial membrane antigen. Molecular pathology now defines the main gliomas, so IDH status and 1p/19q codeletion carry as much weight as appearance."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and genetics explain why tumors form. Loss of neurofibromin leaves Ras active, loss of pRb removes the G1 checkpoint, loss of VHL creates pseudohypoxia, loss of TSC1 or TSC2 activates mTORC1, and mutant IDH produces an oncometabolite. Immunology links primary CNS lymphoma to loss of T cell surveillance of EBV and links opsoclonus-myoclonus to antitumor immunity that cross-reacts with neurons. A limit to remember is that age and site are probabilities, not rules: adults can have medulloblastoma and children can have glioblastoma, so the histologic and molecular findings decide the diagnosis when they are given."}],"summary":["Children have mostly posterior fossa tumors (medulloblastoma, pilocytic astrocytoma, ependymoma); adults have mostly supratentorial tumors, and metastases are the most common intracranial tumors in adults.","Medulloblastoma is a grade 4 embryonal small round blue cell tumor of the vermis that causes obstructive hydrocephalus, truncal ataxia, and drop metastases through the CSF.","Meningioma is an extra-axial, dura-based tumor of arachnoid cap cells with whorls and psammoma bodies, linked to NF2 loss and prior radiation, and its symptoms depend on what it compresses.","Neuroblastoma is a neural crest tumor of young children that crosses the midline, raises urinary HVA and VMA, can cause opsoclonus-myoclonus, and behaves aggressively with MYCN amplification.","Glioblastoma is an IDH-wildtype grade 4 astrocytic tumor of older adults; hypoxia drives pseudopalisading necrosis, VEGF-driven microvascular proliferation, ring enhancement, and recurrence after resection.","Pilocytic astrocytoma is circumscribed, BRAF-altered, and curable; IDH-mutant astrocytoma and oligodendroglioma (1p/19q codeleted) are diffuse, seizure-producing tumors that cannot be fully removed.","Ganglioglioma causes chronic temporal lobe epilepsy, and central neurocytoma causes hydrocephalus by blocking the foramen of Monro.","Schwannomas push the nerve aside (Antoni A and B, Verocay bodies, S-100); vestibular schwannoma causes unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, and bilateral tumors define NF2.","NF1 (neurofibromin, a Ras GAP) causes cafe-au-lait macules, Lisch nodules, neurofibromas with MPNST risk, optic glioma, and pheochromocytoma; RB1, TSC1 or TSC2, and VHL losses explain retinoblastoma, tuberous sclerosis, and hemangioblastoma with renal carcinoma.","Primary CNS lymphoma is a diffuse large B cell lymphoma, EBV driven in AIDS, that must be separated from toxoplasmosis; uveal melanoma spreads by blood to the liver because the eye lacks lymphatics."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, section on tumors and familial tumor syndromes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, section on peripheral nerve sheath tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood, section on neuroblastic tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Eye, sections on retinoblastoma and uveal melanoma"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, sections on tumor suppressor genes and the two-hit hypothesis"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central nervous system, neural crest derivatives"}]},"NE-20-G":{"unit":"NE-20-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07","title":"Infections of the Central and Peripheral Nervous System","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how bacteria, viruses, fungi, parasites, toxins, and prions reach the nervous system and why each produces its own pattern of injury. It covers meningitis, encephalitis, abscess, opportunistic infections in AIDS, toxin-mediated paralysis, latent and slow viral infections, prion disease, and infections of peripheral nerves. Board questions usually give the host (age, immune status, exposure) and the lesion (CSF profile, imaging, histology) and ask for the organism or mechanism. If you can trace the route of entry, the cell the agent targets, and the immune response it provokes, you can reason through nearly every item in this unit.","prerequisites":["Meninges, CSF production, circulation, and absorption at the arachnoid granulations","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs and the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways","Neuromuscular junction transmission and SNARE-mediated vesicle release","Th1 and Th2 helper T cell responses and granuloma formation","Basic virology: genome type, envelope, latency, and replication strategy"],"connections":[{"to":"Neuromuscular junction and motor control (NE chapters on synaptic transmission)","how":"Botulinum and tetanus toxins cleave the same SNARE proteins but act at different synapses, producing flaccid versus spastic paralysis."},{"to":"Immunology (IM)","how":"CD4 count, Th1 versus Th2 polarization, and complement and antibody deficiency predict which opportunistic or encapsulated organism invades."},{"to":"Cardiovascular infections (CV-18-K)","how":"Infective endocarditis and right-to-left shunts seed the brain with septic emboli that become abscesses."},{"to":"Congenital infections (NE-20-E)","how":"Congenital CMV and Toxoplasma produce different calcification patterns and hearing and visual deficits in the newborn brain."},{"to":"Demyelinating and neurodegenerative disease (NE-20 units)","how":"PML, SSPE, and prion disease mimic primary demyelinating and dementing disorders and must be told apart by tempo, histology, and CSF."}],"terms":[{"term":"Meningitis","def":"Inflammation of the leptomeninges (pia and arachnoid) and the CSF in the subarachnoid space."},{"term":"Encephalitis","def":"Inflammation of the brain parenchyma, usually viral, causing altered mental status, seizures, or focal deficits."},{"term":"Aseptic meningitis","def":"Meningitis with a lymphocytic CSF and negative routine bacterial culture, most often caused by enteroviruses or other viruses."},{"term":"Dead-end host","def":"A host in which a pathogen does not reach blood levels high enough to infect the next vector, so transmission stops there."},{"term":"Neuronophagia","def":"Engulfment of dying neurons by microglia and macrophages, a hallmark of viral destruction of neurons."},{"term":"Microglial nodule","def":"A small cluster of activated microglia around an infected or dying neuron in viral encephalitis."},{"term":"Ring-enhancing lesion","def":"A lesion with a contrast-enhancing rim on CT or MRI, seen with abscess, toxoplasmosis, necrotic tumor, and lymphoma."},{"term":"Retrograde axonal transport","def":"Dynein-driven movement of material from the axon terminal toward the cell body, used by rabies virus, poliovirus, and tetanus toxin."},{"term":"Latency","def":"Persistence of a viral genome in host cells without production of infectious virus, with the possibility of later reactivation."},{"term":"Opening pressure","def":"CSF pressure measured at lumbar puncture in the lateral decubitus position, normally about 10 to 20 cm H2O in adults."},{"term":"SNARE proteins","def":"Membrane proteins (synaptobrevin, SNAP-25, syntaxin) that dock synaptic vesicles and fuse them with the presynaptic membrane."},{"term":"Spongiform change","def":"Fine vacuoles in the gray matter neuropil, the characteristic lesion of prion disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C01","obj":"NE-20-G.1","title":"Arboviral encephalitis and West Nile acute flaccid paralysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Arboviruses (arthropod-borne viruses) are maintained in nature by a cycle between a vector and an amplifying host. For the major North American encephalitis viruses, the vector is a mosquito and the amplifying host is a wild bird. Birds develop a high-titer viremia, so a mosquito that feeds on them becomes infected and passes the virus on at its next blood meal. Humans and horses become infected when bitten by the same mosquitoes, but their viremia is low and brief, too low to infect a feeding mosquito. That is why they are called dead-end hosts: they can become ill, but they do not spread the virus, and cases cluster in late summer and early fall when mosquitoes are most active."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Arbovirus transmission cycle","steps":["Infected mosquito bites a bird","Bird develops high-titer viremia","New mosquitoes feed and become infected","Infected mosquito bites another bird","Occasional bite of human or horse (dead-end host)"],"caption":"Birds amplify the virus; humans and horses have viremia too low to infect mosquitoes, so the cycle continues only through birds."},{"type":"p","text":"Most infections are asymptomatic. With West Nile virus (WNV), about 80% of infected people have no symptoms, about 20% develop a febrile illness with headache, myalgia, and sometimes a rash, and fewer than 1% develop neuroinvasive disease (meningitis, encephalitis, or acute flaccid paralysis). The risk of neuroinvasive disease rises steeply with age and with impaired cell-mediated immunity. Once the virus crosses into the CNS it infects neurons, producing the typical viral encephalitis histology (C04), with a predilection for the deep gray matter (thalamus and basal ganglia), brainstem, and anterior horns of the spinal cord. Involvement of the deep gray matter explains the tremor, myoclonus, and parkinsonian features some patients show."},{"type":"p","text":"West Nile acute flaccid paralysis is caused by infection and destruction of anterior horn motor neurons, the same lesion as poliomyelitis (C12). Weakness is therefore of the lower motor neuron type: sudden, often asymmetric, flaccid, with reduced or absent reflexes and no sensory loss. If the cervical cord or brainstem is involved, respiratory failure can follow. CSF typically shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with elevated protein and normal glucose. Detection of WNV-specific IgM in CSF supports the diagnosis, because IgM is a large molecule that does not readily cross the blood-brain barrier and so indicates antibody made within the CNS."},{"type":"table","title":"Mosquito-borne encephalitis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family","Reservoir and vector","Key features"],"rows":[["West Nile virus","Flaviviridae","Birds; Culex mosquitoes","Most common US arboviral neuroinvasive disease; older adults; acute flaccid paralysis"],["St. Louis encephalitis virus","Flaviviridae","Birds; Culex mosquitoes","Encephalitis mainly in older adults; similar to WNV"],["Eastern equine encephalitis virus","Togaviridae (alphavirus)","Birds in swamp habitats; mosquitoes","Rare but most severe, with high fatality and frequent lasting deficits"],["Western equine encephalitis virus","Togaviridae (alphavirus)","Birds; Culex tarsalis","Western US and Canada; disease most often in infants and older adults"]],"caption":"All four are enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses carried by mosquitoes from birds; humans and horses are dead-end hosts."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C02","obj":"NE-20-G.2","title":"Botulism: foodborne, infant, and wound forms","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium botulinum is a gram-positive, spore-forming, obligate anaerobic rod found in soil. Its spores survive boiling, but they germinate and make toxin only in a low-oxygen, low-acid, moist environment. Botulinum neurotoxin is the most potent biological toxin known. Its heavy chain binds receptors on peripheral cholinergic nerve terminals, the toxin is taken up by endocytosis, and its light chain, a zinc-dependent protease, enters the cytosol and cleaves a SNARE protein (SNAP-25 for type A, synaptobrevin for type B). Without intact SNAREs, acetylcholine vesicles cannot fuse with the membrane, so release stops at the neuromuscular junction and at autonomic cholinergic synapses."},{"type":"p","text":"The three main forms differ in where the toxin is made. In foodborne botulism, toxin is already formed in improperly home-canned or preserved low-acid food, so symptoms begin within hours to a few days of eating it; the toxin itself is heat-labile, which is why thorough cooking of the food destroys it. In infant botulism, the infant swallows spores (from honey or environmental dust), and because the immature gut flora of an infant under about 12 months cannot suppress them, the spores germinate and make toxin within the colon. Constipation is often the first sign, followed by a weak cry, poor suck, ptosis, and generalized hypotonia (floppy baby). In wound botulism, spores germinate in devitalized tissue, classically from injection drug use, so there are no gastrointestinal symptoms."},{"type":"p","text":"All three produce the same syndrome because the same toxin acts on the same terminals. Paralysis is symmetric and descending, beginning with cranial nerves (diplopia, ptosis, dysarthria, dysphagia) and moving to the arms, trunk, diaphragm, and legs. Blocked parasympathetic transmission gives dilated, poorly reactive pupils, dry mouth, constipation, and urinary retention. The patient is afebrile and alert, and sensation is normal because the toxin acts only on cholinergic terminals, and sensory pathways do not depend on them. These features distinguish botulism from Guillain-Barre syndrome, which is usually ascending with sensory symptoms, and from myasthenia gravis, which spares the pupils."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of botulism","cols":["Form","Source of toxin","Typical setting","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Foodborne","Preformed toxin in food","Home-canned low-acid vegetables, fermented fish","Onset hours to days after ingestion; may begin with nausea and vomiting; often several people affected"],["Infant","Spores swallowed and germinate in colon","Age under about 12 months; honey, soil, dust","Constipation first, then poor feeding, weak cry, hypotonia"],["Wound","Spores germinate in an anaerobic wound","Injection drug use, contaminated trauma","No gastrointestinal phase; wound may look unremarkable"]],"caption":"Each form ends in the same descending, symmetric flaccid paralysis without fever or sensory loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C03","obj":"NE-20-G.3","title":"Brain abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A brain abscess is a focal collection of pus within the brain parenchyma. Organisms reach the brain in three ways, and the route predicts both the location and the organism. Contiguous spread from a nearby infection travels through bone or along emissary veins: frontal sinusitis and dental infection lead to frontal lobe abscesses, while otitis media and mastoiditis lead to temporal lobe and cerebellar abscesses. Hematogenous seeding from infective endocarditis, lung abscess, or bronchiectasis produces abscesses that are often multiple and lie at the gray-white junction in middle cerebral artery territory, where emboli lodge as arterioles narrow. Direct inoculation follows penetrating trauma or neurosurgery."},{"type":"p","text":"Right-to-left shunts deserve special attention. Normally the pulmonary capillaries filter small septic emboli and bacteria from venous blood. In cyanotic congenital heart disease (for example tetralogy of Fallot) or with pulmonary arteriovenous malformations, venous blood bypasses this filter and enters the systemic arteries directly. Chronic hypoxemia and polycythemia may also create small areas of low perfusion where bacteria can settle. Once bacteria lodge, the lesion evolves over about two weeks from cerebritis (neutrophils, edema, softening) to a necrotic center walled off by a collagen capsule laid down by fibroblasts. The capsule is often thinner on the ventricular side, where an abscess can rupture into the ventricle."},{"type":"p","text":"The organisms reflect the source. Streptococci, especially the Streptococcus anginosus (milleri) group of viridans streptococci, are the most common, often mixed with anaerobes such as Bacteroides, Prevotella, and Fusobacterium from sinus, dental, or ear infection. Staphylococcus aureus dominates after trauma or surgery and with endocarditis. In immunocompromised hosts, Toxoplasma, Nocardia, and fungi become important."},{"type":"p","text":"On contrast CT or MRI the capsule appears as a smooth enhancing ring because its new vessels leak contrast, with low-density pus centrally and vasogenic edema around it. Pus restricts diffusion on diffusion-weighted MRI, which helps separate an abscess from a necrotic tumor. Clinically, headache is the most common symptom, and fever and focal deficits are each present in only about half of patients, so the full triad is often absent. Seizures and signs of raised intracranial pressure may occur. Lumbar puncture is avoided because releasing CSF below a mass lesion can precipitate herniation, and the CSF is often nondiagnostic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 9-year-old with tetralogy of Fallot develops headache, fever, and a new left hemiparesis. Where did the organisms most likely bypass a normal defense, and what will imaging show?","explain":"Venous bacteria crossed the right-to-left shunt without passing through the pulmonary capillary filter and lodged in the right cerebral hemisphere, forming a ring-enhancing lesion with surrounding edema.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on right-to-left shunts and the imaging paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C04","obj":"NE-20-G.4","title":"Histology of viral encephalitis and rabies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Viral encephalitis has a recognizable histological pattern regardless of the virus. Lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages accumulate in the Virchow-Robin spaces around small vessels, forming perivascular cuffs. Within the parenchyma, activated microglia gather into small clusters (microglial nodules) around infected neurons. When a neuron dies, microglia and macrophages engulf it, a process called neuronophagia. Some viruses add a specific clue in the form of inclusion bodies: intranuclear Cowdry type A inclusions in herpes simplex encephalitis and SSPE, enlarged cells with owl's eye inclusions in CMV, and Negri bodies in rabies."},{"type":"p","text":"Rabies virus is an enveloped, bullet-shaped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA virus of the family Rhabdoviridae (genus Lyssavirus). It is transmitted in the saliva of an infected mammal, usually by a bite. Worldwide, dogs cause nearly all human deaths. In the United States, wildlife reservoirs include raccoons, skunks, foxes, and bats, and bats account for most human cases, often because the bite was small and went unnoticed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of rabies virus from bite to brain","steps":["Virus replicates in muscle at bite site","Binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptor near nerve terminals","Enters peripheral nerve","Retrograde axonal transport to spinal cord and brain","Spreads through neurons, especially limbic system and brainstem","Travels outward along nerves to salivary glands"],"caption":"Because the virus moves inside axons, it is shielded from antibody, and the incubation period depends on the distance from the bite to the CNS."},{"type":"p","text":"The incubation period is usually 1 to 3 months but ranges from days to more than a year, and bites on the face and head have shorter incubations. A prodrome of fever, malaise, and pain or paresthesia at the healed bite site is attributed to inflammation of the dorsal root ganglion that supplies the bite. Most patients then develop the encephalitic (furious) form: agitation, confusion, hallucinations, hypersalivation, and autonomic instability. Hydrophobia is the hallmark, consisting of painful spasms of the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles when the patient attempts to drink, later triggered by the sight of water or a draft of air (aerophobia). A minority develop a paralytic form with ascending flaccid weakness. Once symptoms appear, the disease is almost always fatal."},{"type":"p","text":"Negri bodies are round, eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions composed of viral nucleocapsid material, found most often in Purkinje cells of the cerebellum and pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus. Inflammation is often surprisingly mild for such a lethal disease, because rabies kills largely by disrupting neuronal function rather than by destroying large numbers of neurons."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C05","obj":"NE-20-G.5","title":"Parasitic infections: Toxoplasma, neurocysticercosis, and Naegleria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Toxoplasma gondii is an obligate intracellular protozoan whose definitive host is the cat. Humans become infected by swallowing oocysts from cat feces (soil, litter, unwashed produce) or tissue cysts in undercooked meat. In an immunocompetent person the infection is controlled by cell-mediated immunity (Th1 cells, interferon-gamma, activated macrophages), but the parasite persists as dormant bradyzoite cysts in brain and muscle, which is why many adults are IgG seropositive. When the CD4 count falls below about 100 cells per microliter in AIDS, bradyzoites convert back to rapidly dividing tachyzoites and destroy brain tissue. Imaging shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions with edema, typically in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction, with headache, fever, confusion, seizures, and focal deficits. The main alternative in this setting is EBV-associated primary CNS lymphoma, which is more often solitary and periventricular (C07)."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurocysticercosis is caused by the larval stage of the pork tapeworm Taenia solium. The route of exposure decides the disease. Eating undercooked pork containing cysticerci produces an adult tapeworm in the intestine (taeniasis). Swallowing eggs, shed in the feces of a human tapeworm carrier and spread by contaminated hands, food, or water, produces cysticercosis: the eggs hatch, the embryos penetrate the intestinal wall, and they travel in the blood to brain, muscle, and eye, where they form fluid-filled cysts. A carrier can also infect themselves. Living cysts provoke little inflammation, but as a cyst degenerates the host response produces edema and seizures, and the cyst later calcifies. Neurocysticercosis is a leading cause of acquired epilepsy worldwide. Imaging shows cysts, some with a visible scolex, and scattered calcifications; cysts in the ventricles can block CSF flow and cause hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"Naegleria fowleri is a free-living amoeba of warm freshwater, such as lakes, hot springs, and poorly chlorinated pools. When contaminated water is forced into the nose during swimming or nasal rinsing, the amoeba attaches to the olfactory mucosa and migrates along the olfactory nerve fibers through the cribriform plate into the olfactory bulbs and frontal lobes. The result is primary amebic meningoencephalitis, a hemorrhagic necrotizing infection in previously healthy children and young adults. Early changes in smell or taste are followed by severe headache, fever, stiff neck, and rapid progression to coma, and death usually occurs within about one to two weeks. CSF resembles bacterial meningitis (neutrophils, low glucose, high protein), and motile trophozoites may be seen on a wet mount of fresh CSF."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C06","obj":"NE-20-G.6","title":"Fungal infections: cryptococcal meningitis and rhinocerebral mucormycosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans is an encapsulated yeast found in soil contaminated with bird, especially pigeon, droppings. Infection begins when the yeast is inhaled. Its thick polysaccharide capsule (mainly glucuronoxylomannan) resists phagocytosis and suppresses the inflammatory response, so control depends on CD4 T cells activating macrophages. When the CD4 count drops below about 100 cells per microliter, the yeast spreads from the lung by the blood and has a strong tropism for the CNS, causing a subacute meningoencephalitis. Patients have headache, fever, and altered mental status that develop over days to weeks, and neck stiffness is often mild or absent because the inflammatory response is weak."},{"type":"p","text":"The CSF opening pressure is often markedly raised, frequently above 25 cm H2O, because yeast and shed capsular polysaccharide clog the arachnoid granulations and impair CSF absorption. The CSF shows low glucose, elevated protein, and a lymphocytic pleocytosis that may be minimal in advanced AIDS. Cryptococcal capsular antigen in CSF or serum is highly sensitive and specific. India ink shows yeast surrounded by a clear halo (the capsule), and mucicarmine stains the capsule red in tissue. In the brain, yeast can fill dilated perivascular spaces of the basal ganglia, producing gelatinous soap bubble lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Mucormycosis is caused by molds of the order Mucorales, including Rhizopus and Mucor, which form broad, ribbon-like hyphae with few or no septa that branch at wide, nearly right angles. These organisms are angioinvasive. Diabetic ketoacidosis is the classic setting because acidosis lowers the iron-binding capacity of transferrin, releasing free iron that the fungus needs for growth, and because hyperglycemia and acidosis impair neutrophil chemotaxis and killing. Severe neutropenia and iron overload are other risk factors. In rhinocerebral disease, inhaled spores germinate in the nose and sinuses, and hyphae invade arterial walls, causing thrombosis and ischemic necrosis. This produces a black eschar on the turbinates or palate, then spread into the orbit (proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, vision loss), the cavernous sinus, and the frontal lobe within days."},{"type":"table","title":"Cryptococcus versus Mucorales","cols":["Feature","Cryptococcus neoformans","Mucorales (Rhizopus, Mucor)"],"rows":[["Form in tissue","Encapsulated budding yeast","Broad nonseptate hyphae branching at wide angles"],["Main host risk","Low CD4 count (AIDS), other T cell defects","Diabetic ketoacidosis, neutropenia, iron overload"],["Route to CNS","Inhalation, then blood to meninges","Direct extension from sinuses through vessels and bone"],["Tempo","Subacute, days to weeks","Fulminant, hours to days"],["Key findings","High opening pressure; capsular antigen; India ink halo","Black nasal or palatal eschar; orbital and brain invasion"]],"caption":"The yeast causes a meningitis through the bloodstream; the mold invades vessels and destroys tissue by infarction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C07","obj":"NE-20-G.7","title":"Herpesvirus diseases of the CNS","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All herpesviruses are enveloped, double-stranded DNA viruses that establish lifelong latency. Their CNS diseases follow from where each virus becomes latent and from the state of the host's cell-mediated immunity, so the key questions are the patient's age and immune status."},{"type":"p","text":"Herpes simplex virus type 1 (HSV-1) is the most common cause of sporadic, nonepidemic fatal viral encephalitis in adults. The virus is thought to reach the brain along the olfactory or trigeminal pathways, either during primary infection or on reactivation from the trigeminal ganglion. It produces a hemorrhagic, necrotizing encephalitis of the medial temporal lobes and orbitofrontal cortex, usually asymmetric. Because these are limbic regions, patients show fever with confusion, personality and behavioral change, memory loss, aphasia, olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, and temporal lobe seizures. CSF shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis, often with red blood cells from the hemorrhagic necrosis, and HSV DNA by PCR. MRI shows temporal lobe signal change, and EEG may show periodic lateralized discharges over the temporal region. Histology adds Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusions to the general viral pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"HSV-2 behaves differently. In adults it typically causes a self-limited aseptic (lymphocytic) meningitis, often with primary genital infection, and it is the main cause of recurrent benign lymphocytic meningitis. In the newborn, who lacks mature cell-mediated immunity, HSV acquired from the maternal genital tract at delivery (usually HSV-2) can cause localized skin, eye, and mouth disease, diffuse encephalitis, or disseminated infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) causes CNS disease at the two extremes of immunity. In advanced AIDS, usually with CD4 counts below about 50 cells per microliter, it causes encephalitis that favors the cells lining the ventricles (ventriculoencephalitis), together with retinitis and polyradiculopathy. Congenital CMV, the most common congenital infection, injures the germinal matrix around the ventricles, leaving periventricular calcifications with microcephaly, chorioretinitis, and sensorineural hearing loss. Congenital toxoplasmosis, by contrast, leaves calcifications scattered through the parenchyma with hydrocephalus. Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) drives primary CNS lymphoma in AIDS: latently infected B cells express viral proteins that promote proliferation, and when T cell surveillance is lost they expand into a diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, usually a solitary periventricular lesion with weak or irregular ring enhancement."},{"type":"table","title":"Herpesviruses and CNS disease","cols":["Virus","Host","Lesion","Clue"],"rows":[["HSV-1","Any age, usually immunocompetent","Hemorrhagic necrosis of temporal and orbitofrontal lobes","Behavior change, seizures, red cells in CSF"],["HSV-2","Adults; neonates at delivery","Aseptic meningitis; neonatal encephalitis or dissemination","Recurrent lymphocytic meningitis in adults"],["CMV","Advanced AIDS; fetus","Ventriculoencephalitis; congenital periventricular calcifications","Owl's eye inclusions; hearing loss in newborn"],["EBV","Advanced AIDS","Primary CNS lymphoma (B cell)","Usually solitary periventricular mass"],["VZV","Older or immunosuppressed adults","Ganglionitis with dermatomal zoster","See C17"]],"caption":"Latency site and host immunity predict which herpesvirus causes which CNS lesion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C08","obj":"NE-20-G.8","title":"Leprosy: tuberculoid and lepromatous forms","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium leprae is an acid-fast, obligate intracellular bacillus that cannot be grown in standard culture and divides very slowly, so incubation lasts years. It grows best below core body temperature, at roughly 27 to 30 °C (81 to 86 °F), which is why it attacks the skin, nasal mucosa, testes, anterior eye, and superficial peripheral nerves such as the ulnar, common fibular, and great auricular nerves. Transmission is thought to be mainly by respiratory droplets during prolonged close contact, and armadillos are an animal reservoir in the southern United States. The bacillus binds the basal lamina of Schwann cells and lives inside them, so nerve damage is central to the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical form depends on the helper T cell response, and the two poles of the spectrum illustrate how immunity shapes pathology. In tuberculoid leprosy, a strong Th1 response (IL-2 and interferon-gamma) activates macrophages and forms well-organized epithelioid granulomas that contain the infection, so bacilli are scarce (paucibacillary) and the lepromin skin test is positive. Lesions are few, asymmetric, well-demarcated, hypopigmented plaques that are anesthetic, because the granulomas destroy the small cutaneous nerves within them. Nearby peripheral nerves are thickened and palpable."},{"type":"p","text":"In lepromatous leprosy, a Th2-dominant response (IL-4 and IL-10) gives antibody but weak macrophage activation, so bacilli multiply freely inside foamy macrophages (lepra cells), and the disease is multibacillary with a negative lepromin test. Diffuse nodular infiltration of the cool skin of the face produces leonine facies, with loss of the eyebrows and destruction of the nasal cartilage. Bacilli spread through the blood and invade nerves symmetrically, so sensory loss begins in the coolest distal regions, giving a glove-and-stocking pattern. Testicular involvement can cause atrophy and gynecomastia, and immune complex deposition can cause erythema nodosum leprosum. In both forms, painless injuries, burns, and ulcers accumulate in anesthetic hands and feet, leading to deformity and loss of digits."},{"type":"table","title":"Tuberculoid versus lepromatous leprosy","cols":["Feature","Tuberculoid","Lepromatous"],"rows":[["T cell response","Th1 (IL-2, interferon-gamma)","Th2 (IL-4, IL-10)"],["Bacilli in tissue","Few (paucibacillary)","Many (multibacillary), in foamy macrophages"],["Histology","Well-formed epithelioid granulomas","Sheets of foamy macrophages, poorly formed granulomas"],["Lepromin skin test","Positive","Negative"],["Skin lesions","Few, asymmetric, anesthetic hypopigmented plaques","Widespread nodules and infiltration; leonine facies"],["Nerve involvement","Thickened nerves near lesions, asymmetric","Symmetric glove-and-stocking sensory loss"]],"caption":"The same organism causes opposite pictures depending on whether the host mounts a cell-mediated (Th1) or humoral (Th2) response."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a patient with tuberculoid leprosy develops a condition that weakens Th1 immunity, how would the skin biopsy and lesion pattern change?","explain":"Granulomas would become poorly formed, bacilli would multiply inside macrophages, and lesions would become more numerous and symmetric, shifting the disease toward the lepromatous pole.","check":"Compare with the table on tuberculoid versus lepromatous leprosy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C09","obj":"NE-20-G.9","title":"Bacterial meningitis: organisms by age and pathogenesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most bacterial meningitis begins with colonization of a mucosal surface, followed by invasion of the blood and crossing of the blood-brain or blood-CSF barrier at the choroid plexus or cerebral capillaries. A polysaccharide capsule is the key virulence factor, because it lets the organism resist complement and phagocytosis in the bloodstream. Once in the subarachnoid space, bacteria multiply quickly because CSF contains very little complement, antibody, or phagocytic activity. Bacterial cell wall components and endotoxin then trigger release of IL-1, TNF-alpha, and other cytokines, drawing in neutrophils and opening the blood-brain barrier. Much of the damage comes from this host response."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of bacterial meningitis","steps":["Nasopharyngeal or birth canal colonization","Invasion into bloodstream; capsule resists clearance","Crossing into CSF at choroid plexus or capillaries","Rapid multiplication in CSF with weak defenses","Cytokine release and neutrophil exudate","Edema, raised intracranial pressure, vascular and nerve injury"],"caption":"The immune response to bacterial products, more than the bacteria themselves, injures the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"The likely organism follows the patient's age and immunity. Neonates acquire group B streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) and Escherichia coli (especially strains with the K1 capsule) from the maternal genital and intestinal tract, and Listeria monocytogenes can cross the placenta or be acquired at birth. After the neonatal period, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis dominate in children and adults. N. meningitidis is especially important in adolescents and young adults in crowded settings such as dormitories and military barracks. Haemophilus influenzae type b was once the leading cause in young children and is now rare where conjugate vaccination is routine. In older adults, waning cell-mediated immunity makes Listeria, an intracellular organism controlled by T cells, important again alongside S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis; Listeria also affects pregnant and immunosuppressed patients. Gram-negative bacilli matter in older adults and after neurosurgery."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical CSF profiles","cols":["Cause","Opening pressure","Cells","Glucose","Protein"],"rows":[["Normal adult","About 10 to 20 cm H2O","0 to 5 lymphocytes per microliter","About 45 to 80 mg/dL (about 60% of serum)","About 15 to 45 mg/dL"],["Bacterial","Raised","Hundreds to thousands, neutrophils","Low (below about 40 mg/dL)","High"],["Viral (aseptic)","Normal or mildly raised","Tens to hundreds, lymphocytes","Normal","Normal or mildly raised"],["Tuberculous or fungal","Raised","Tens to hundreds, lymphocytes","Low","High, often very high in TB"]],"caption":"Bacteria and neutrophils consume glucose and the barrier leaks protein; viruses usually leave glucose normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Gram stain clues","cols":["Gram stain","Organism","Typical host"],"rows":[["Gram-positive cocci in chains","Group B streptococcus","Neonate"],["Gram-negative rods","E. coli","Neonate; older adult"],["Gram-positive rods","Listeria monocytogenes","Neonate, pregnancy, older or immunosuppressed adult"],["Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci","S. pneumoniae","All ages beyond neonate; older adults"],["Gram-negative diplococci","N. meningitidis","Adolescents and young adults"]],"caption":"The Gram stain links the CSF finding back to the age group."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C10","obj":"NE-20-G.10","title":"Findings and complications of meningitis, including tuberculous meningitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The classic findings of meningitis come from inflammation of the meninges and the raised intracranial pressure that follows. Inflamed meninges are pain-sensitive, giving severe headache, and stretching them by flexing the neck triggers protective muscle spasm, felt as neck stiffness. The Kernig and Brudzinski signs test the same irritation but are insensitive. Photophobia is thought to reflect irritation of meningeal pain fibers carried by the trigeminal nerve. Fever and altered mental status reflect cytokine release and cortical dysfunction. In infants these signs are unreliable, and irritability, poor feeding, lethargy, and a bulging fontanelle may be the only clues."},{"type":"p","text":"Meningococcal disease adds a petechial or purpuric rash. Endotoxin (lipooligosaccharide) shed by N. meningitidis damages endothelium and activates coagulation, producing disseminated intravascular coagulation, skin hemorrhage, shock, and in the worst cases bilateral adrenal hemorrhage with acute adrenal failure (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome)."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications and their mechanisms","cols":["Complication","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Sensorineural hearing loss","Infection and inflammation spread from the subarachnoid space to the cochlea (labyrinthitis) and damage the eighth nerve; most frequent with pneumococcus"],["Hydrocephalus","Purulent exudate obstructs CSF absorption at the arachnoid granulations or flow through the basal cisterns and ventricular outlets"],["Seizures","Cortical irritation, infarction, venous sinus thrombosis, and hyponatremia"],["Infarction","Inflammation of vessels crossing the subarachnoid space causes vasospasm and thrombosis"],["Cerebral edema and herniation","Vasogenic, cytotoxic, and interstitial edema raise intracranial pressure"],["Hyponatremia","Inappropriate ADH secretion"],["Cranial nerve palsies","Nerves crossing the inflamed subarachnoid space are compressed or ischemic"]],"caption":"Each complication follows from inflammation in the subarachnoid space and the structures that pass through it."},{"type":"p","text":"Tuberculous meningitis has a different tempo and distribution. During earlier hematogenous spread, Mycobacterium tuberculosis forms small caseating foci (Rich foci) beneath the pia or ependyma; when one ruptures into the subarachnoid space, it releases bacilli and antigen that provoke a thick, gelatinous, fibrinous exudate concentrated at the base of the brain. That location explains the three main complications. Cranial nerves leaving the brainstem become entrapped, so palsies of the abducens (most common), oculomotor, and facial nerves are typical. The exudate obstructs CSF flow through the basal cisterns, causing communicating hydrocephalus. Arteries passing through the exudate develop obliterative endarteritis, producing infarcts, especially in the basal ganglia and internal capsule supplied by the lenticulostriate arteries. Onset is subacute over weeks, with headache, low-grade fever, weight loss, and confusion. CSF shows lymphocytes, very high protein, and low glucose."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the CSF","text":"A 45-year-old man has three weeks of headache, night sweats, and double vision; he cannot abduct the right eye. CSF: opening pressure 30 cm H2O, 220 cells per microliter (85% lymphocytes), glucose 25 mg/dL with serum glucose 110 mg/dL, protein 280 mg/dL. Step 1: lymphocytes and a subacute course argue against typical bacterial meningitis. Step 2: a CSF to serum glucose ratio of about 0.23 is low, which argues against most viral meningitis. Step 3: very high protein, a cranial nerve VI palsy, and raised pressure point to a thick basilar exudate. Conclusion: tuberculous meningitis, with cryptococcal meningitis the main alternative if the patient is immunosuppressed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C11","obj":"NE-20-G.11","title":"Neurosyphilis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum invades the CSF early in infection in many patients, often without symptoms. If the infection is not cleared, it can produce neurologic disease months to decades later. The tertiary forms reflect where the spirochetes and the chronic lymphocyte and plasma cell inflammation they provoke settle: in the meninges and vessels, in the cerebral cortex, or in the dorsal roots and dorsal columns of the spinal cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Meningovascular neurosyphilis, appearing years after infection, is a chronic meningitis that involves the base of the brain and causes obliterative endarteritis, with plasma cells and lymphocytes around small vessels. The narrowed arteries cause ischemic strokes in relatively young adults, often in middle cerebral artery territory. General paresis results from invasion of the cerebral cortex, especially the frontal lobes, typically one to several decades after infection. Neuronal loss, microglial proliferation, gliosis, and iron deposits in microglia produce a progressive dementia with personality change, poor judgment, mood disturbance, and sometimes grandiose delusions."},{"type":"p","text":"Tabes dorsalis, also a late form, damages the sensory nerve roots where they enter the spinal cord, with secondary degeneration of the dorsal columns, mainly in the lumbosacral region. The losses follow directly from the anatomy. Loss of proprioception produces sensory ataxia with a wide-based, stamping gait and a positive Romberg sign: the patient can stand with eyes open by using vision but falls when the eyes are closed. Loss of the afferent limb of the stretch reflex abolishes knee and ankle reflexes. Irritation of dorsal roots causes sudden lancinating (lightning) pains. Loss of pain and position sense in large joints allows repeated unrecognized injury, producing destructive neuropathic (Charcot) joints, classically the knee. Bladder sensation is lost, causing overflow retention. Argyll Robertson pupils are small, irregular pupils that constrict to accommodation but not to light, attributed to a lesion in the dorsal midbrain that interrupts the light reflex pathway while sparing the more ventral accommodation pathway."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of neurosyphilis","cols":["Form","Site of injury","Main findings"],"rows":[["Asymptomatic","CSF and meninges","Abnormal CSF only"],["Meningovascular","Basal meninges and small arteries (endarteritis)","Stroke in a young adult, cranial nerve palsies"],["General paresis","Cerebral cortex, especially frontal","Dementia, personality change, delusions"],["Tabes dorsalis","Dorsal roots and dorsal columns","Sensory ataxia, positive Romberg, areflexia, lightning pains, Charcot joints, Argyll Robertson pupils"]],"caption":"The location of chronic spirochetal inflammation determines the syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"CSF in neurosyphilis typically shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis and elevated protein. The CSF VDRL test, which detects antibodies to cardiolipin, is highly specific: a reactive result essentially confirms neurosyphilis. It is not sensitive, however, and misses a substantial fraction of cases, so a negative CSF VDRL does not exclude the diagnosis. Serum treponemal tests usually remain positive for life once a patient has been infected."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C12","obj":"NE-20-G.12","title":"Poliomyelitis and post-polio syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Poliovirus is a nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus of the genus Enterovirus (family Picornaviridae), with three serotypes. Because it lacks an envelope, it survives stomach acid and the environment and spreads by the fecal-oral route. The virus replicates first in the oropharynx and in intestinal lymphoid tissue (tonsils and Peyer patches), then enters the blood. In a small minority it reaches the CNS, either across the blood-brain barrier or by retrograde transport along peripheral nerves, using its cell receptor CD155. Within the CNS it is strongly tropic for motor neurons, destroying anterior horn cells of the spinal cord and, in bulbar polio, motor nuclei of the brainstem."},{"type":"p","text":"Most infections are silent. Roughly 70% are asymptomatic, about a quarter cause a mild febrile illness, 1% to 5% cause aseptic meningitis, and fewer than 1% cause paralysis. Paralytic disease follows the loss of lower motor neurons. Weakness is flaccid and asymmetric, often affecting one leg more than the other and proximal muscles more than distal, because the virus destroys motor neuron pools patchily. Loss of the motor neuron interrupts the efferent limb of the stretch reflex, so reflexes are reduced or absent. Dying motor units produce fasciculations, and denervated muscles atrophy over weeks. Sensation is normal because the dorsal horns and dorsal roots are spared. Bulbar involvement can cause dysphagia and respiratory failure. CSF shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose. Histology shows the viral encephalitis pattern (C04) centered on the anterior horns."},{"type":"p","text":"Recovery after acute polio depends on surviving motor neurons sprouting new axon branches to reinnervate orphaned muscle fibers, creating enlarged motor units. Post-polio syndrome appears about 15 to 40 years later as new, slowly progressive weakness, fatigue, and atrophy, usually in previously affected muscles. It is not reactivation of the virus. The best-supported explanation is that the oversized motor units, under long-term metabolic stress, gradually lose terminal sprouts and fail. Vaccines that prevent polio are the inactivated vaccine and the live attenuated oral vaccine; the oral strains can rarely revert to neurovirulence, which is the biomedical basis of vaccine-associated and vaccine-derived paralytic polio."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with West Nile acute flaccid paralysis look so much like a patient with paralytic polio?","explain":"Both viruses destroy anterior horn motor neurons, so both produce asymmetric lower motor neuron weakness with areflexia and preserved sensation.","check":"Compare the lesion described here with West Nile paralysis in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C13","obj":"NE-20-G.13","title":"Prion disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Prion diseases are transmissible neurodegenerative disorders caused by a misfolded host protein rather than by a microorganism with nucleic acid. Normal prion protein (PrPc), encoded by the PRNP gene on chromosome 20, is a membrane protein rich in alpha-helices that is readily digested by proteases. The disease form (PrPSc) has the same amino acid sequence but is folded into beta-pleated sheets. It is insoluble, resistant to proteases, and able to bind PrPc and template its conversion into more PrPSc. Each conversion adds template, so PrPSc accumulates exponentially, aggregates, and kills neurons."},{"type":"p","text":"The initial misfolding can arise in three ways. Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD), the most common form at about 85% of cases, arises spontaneously, usually around age 60. Inherited forms (about 10% to 15%) result from PRNP mutations that make the protein prone to misfold and include familial CJD, Gerstmann-Straussler-Scheinker syndrome, and fatal familial insomnia. Acquired forms are rare: iatrogenic CJD from contaminated neurosurgical instruments, dura mater or corneal grafts, and cadaveric pituitary growth hormone; kuru from ritual cannibalism; and variant CJD from eating beef contaminated with bovine spongiform encephalopathy prions. Variant CJD affects younger patients, begins with psychiatric and sensory symptoms, runs a longer course, and shows prominent amyloid plaques."},{"type":"p","text":"Sporadic CJD causes a rapidly progressive dementia over weeks to months, with startle-sensitive myoclonus, ataxia, and visual or behavioral disturbance, and death usually within about a year. The pathology explains the name spongiform encephalopathy: fine vacuoles in the gray matter neuropil, neuronal loss, and reactive astrocytosis, with no inflammatory infiltrate, because PrPSc is a self protein and the immune system does not recognize it as foreign. Supporting tests reflect rapid neuronal destruction or the abnormal protein itself: periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG, raised CSF 14-3-3 protein and tau, a positive RT-QuIC assay that amplifies PrPSc seeding activity, and cortical and basal ganglia signal change on diffusion-weighted MRI. Prions resist conventional sterilization by heat, formalin, and radiation, which is why instruments have transmitted disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Categories of human prion disease","cols":["Category","Examples","Origin of PrPSc"],"rows":[["Sporadic","Sporadic CJD","Spontaneous misfolding of normal PrP"],["Inherited","Familial CJD, Gerstmann-Straussler-Scheinker syndrome, fatal familial insomnia","PRNP mutation, autosomal dominant"],["Acquired","Iatrogenic CJD, kuru, variant CJD","Exposure to exogenous prions"]],"caption":"All forms share protease-resistant beta-sheet PrP and spongiform change without inflammation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C14","obj":"NE-20-G.14","title":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is a demyelinating disease caused by JC virus, a small, nonenveloped, double-stranded DNA polyomavirus. Most people are infected silently in childhood, after which the virus remains latent, mainly in the kidney, and is shed in the urine without harm. Healthy T cell immunity keeps it in check. When cell-mediated immunity is profoundly impaired, the virus reactivates, and rearranged, neurotropic variants reach the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"The settings all share severe loss of T cell surveillance of the brain. Advanced AIDS was the classic cause, usually with a CD4 count below about 200 and often below 100 cells per microliter. Other settings include hematologic malignancy, organ transplantation, and specific immunomodulating drugs. Natalizumab, an antibody to alpha-4 integrin, blocks lymphocytes from crossing into the CNS, so the brain loses immune surveillance even when blood counts look normal. Rituximab, an anti-CD20 antibody, depletes B cells, which may serve as a reservoir and vehicle for the virus as well as supporting T cell responses."},{"type":"p","text":"JC virus infects and lyses oligodendrocytes, the cells that make and maintain CNS myelin, and also infects astrocytes. As oligodendrocytes die, myelin breaks down in patches that enlarge and merge, most often in the subcortical white matter of the parietal and occipital lobes, including the subcortical U-fibers. Histology shows oligodendrocytes with enlarged nuclei filled with glassy, purple viral inclusions at the edges of the lesions, and bizarre, enlarged astrocytes that can resemble tumor cells, with little inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical course reflects lesion location rather than any meningeal process. Patients develop subacute, steadily worsening focal deficits over weeks: hemiparesis, visual field loss or cortical blindness, aphasia, ataxia, and cognitive decline. Fever and headache are uncommon. MRI shows multifocal, asymmetric white matter lesions that are bright on T2-weighted images, with little or no mass effect and usually no enhancement. Detection of JC virus DNA in CSF by PCR supports the diagnosis. Without restoration of immunity, death typically follows within months. If immunity recovers rapidly, the returning inflammatory response can briefly worsen lesions and make them enhance (immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C15","obj":"NE-20-G.15","title":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) is a rare, progressive, fatal encephalitis caused by persistent measles virus infection of the brain. Measles (rubeola) virus is an enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA paramyxovirus. The greatest risk follows measles acquired in early childhood, especially before about age 2, when the immature immune system may fail to clear virus that has entered the CNS. SSPE then appears after a latent period of several years, typically about 7 to 10, most often in late childhood or adolescence."},{"type":"p","text":"The persisting virus is defective. Mutations, most consistently in the gene for the matrix (M) protein and often in the fusion and hemagglutinin genes, prevent assembly and budding of complete virions. The viral nucleocapsids instead spread directly from cell to cell across synapses, so no free virus is released for antibody to neutralize. The host continues to make large amounts of anti-measles antibody, yet the infection persists inside neurons and oligodendrocytes. Inflammation and neuronal loss slowly destroy both gray and white matter, which is the meaning of panencephalitis, and gliosis (sclerosis) follows. Histology shows perivascular cuffs, microglial nodules, gliosis, and intranuclear and cytoplasmic viral inclusions in neurons and oligodendrocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"The disease starts insidiously with personality change, irritability, and declining school performance, then progresses to periodic myoclonic jerks, seizures, visual loss from chorioretinitis, and finally rigidity, dementia, and a vegetative state, with death usually within about one to three years of onset. EEG shows periodic, high-amplitude complexes that repeat every several seconds and coincide with the myoclonic jerks. CSF has few or no cells and normal glucose, but the immunoglobulin findings are striking: markedly raised anti-measles antibody titers in CSF relative to serum, an elevated IgG index, and oligoclonal bands, all reflecting antibody production within the CNS. SSPE is prevented by measles vaccination, because preventing early childhood measles removes the source of persistent virus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C16","obj":"NE-20-G.16","title":"Tetanus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium tetani is a gram-positive, spore-forming, obligate anaerobic rod with a terminal spore (drumstick appearance). Spores are widespread in soil and animal feces and survive for years. When they enter a wound with low oxygen tension, such as a puncture wound, crush injury, burn, or wound with dead tissue or a foreign body, they germinate and the bacteria release tetanospasmin. The organism stays at the wound and does not invade, and the wound may look trivial. In neonatal tetanus, spores enter the umbilical stump when the cord is cut with an unsterile instrument or dressed with contaminated material, in infants whose mothers lack protective antibody."},{"type":"flow","title":"How tetanospasmin causes spastic paralysis","steps":["Toxin binds motor nerve terminals at the wound","Retrograde axonal transport to spinal cord and brainstem","Transfer into inhibitory interneurons","Light chain cleaves synaptobrevin","Glycine and GABA release blocked","Alpha motor neurons fire unopposed: rigidity and spasms"],"caption":"Removing inhibition, not adding excitation, produces sustained muscle contraction."},{"type":"p","text":"Like botulinum toxin, tetanospasmin is a zinc protease that cleaves a SNARE protein, but after retrograde transport it acts centrally, in the inhibitory interneurons (including Renshaw cells) that normally restrain motor neurons. With glycine and GABA release blocked, alpha motor neurons discharge without inhibition, so agonist and antagonist muscles contract together. Loss of inhibition of sympathetic neurons causes autonomic instability with tachycardia, hypertension, and sweating."},{"type":"p","text":"The muscles with the shortest nerve pathways are often affected first. Trismus (lockjaw) from masseter spasm is usually the first sign. Sustained facial muscle contraction produces risus sardonicus, a fixed grin with raised eyebrows. Generalized spasms, triggered by noise, touch, or light, arch the back into opisthotonus. Laryngeal spasm and rigidity of respiratory muscles threaten breathing. The patient remains conscious and in pain throughout. Neonatal tetanus appears in the first one to two weeks of life with poor sucking, irritability, rigidity, and spasms."},{"type":"p","text":"Tetanus is prevented by toxoid immunization. Toxoid is tetanospasmin inactivated with formaldehyde, which destroys its toxicity but preserves its antigenic structure, so it induces neutralizing IgG antitoxin. Maternal immunization protects the newborn because IgG crosses the placenta. Recovery from tetanus does not confer immunity, because the amount of toxin that causes disease is too small to stimulate an antibody response."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: botulism or tetanus?","text":"Two farm workers present on the same day. The first has a stiff jaw, painful back arching set off by noise, and a healing puncture wound on the foot. The second has double vision, drooping eyelids, dilated pupils, and weakness spreading down from the face after eating home-canned beans. Step 1: both toxins are zinc proteases that cleave SNARE proteins. Step 2: ask which synapse is silenced. Botulinum toxin stays at the peripheral cholinergic terminal and blocks acetylcholine release, so muscles go limp and pupils dilate: flaccid paralysis in the second patient. Step 3: tetanospasmin travels to the spinal cord and blocks inhibitory glycine and GABA release, so motor neurons fire unopposed: spastic paralysis in the first patient."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C17","obj":"NE-20-G.17","title":"Varicella zoster virus: latency, reactivation, and herpes zoster syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary varicella zoster virus (VZV) infection is chickenpox. The virus is spread by respiratory droplets and contact with vesicle fluid, replicates in the respiratory tract and lymphoid tissue, and reaches the skin through the blood, producing a widespread vesicular rash. During this illness the virus enters sensory nerve endings in the skin and travels by retrograde axonal transport to the sensory ganglia, and it may also reach them through the blood. It then becomes latent in neurons of the dorsal root ganglia and cranial nerve ganglia, including the trigeminal and geniculate ganglia. Cell-mediated immunity, specifically VZV-specific T cells, keeps it latent."},{"type":"cycle","title":"VZV latency and reactivation","steps":["Chickenpox: virus enters sensory nerve endings","Retrograde transport to sensory ganglion","Latency held by VZV-specific T cells","T cell immunity declines with age or immunosuppression","Reactivation and anterograde spread to the dermatome"],"caption":"Shingles is reactivation of the patient's own latent virus, not a new infection."},{"type":"p","text":"VZV-specific T cell immunity declines with age, especially after about 50, and with immunosuppression. The virus then reactivates in one ganglion, inflames and damages it, and travels anterograde down the sensory axons to the skin of that dermatome. This explains the features of herpes zoster (shingles): burning or aching pain often precedes the rash by several days, and the vesicles cluster in a band on one side of the body that respects the midline. Thoracic dermatomes are the most common, followed by the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. Vesicle scrapings show multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions."},{"type":"p","text":"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus is reactivation in the trigeminal ganglion affecting the ophthalmic division (V1). Vesicles on the tip or side of the nose (Hutchinson sign) signal involvement of the nasociliary branch, which also supplies the cornea and is therefore a warning of keratitis and uveitis. Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus) is reactivation in the geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve. It causes an ipsilateral lower motor neuron facial palsy with vesicles in the external ear canal or auricle, sometimes with loss of taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and, when the adjacent vestibulocochlear nerve is involved, hearing loss and vertigo. VZV can also infect cerebral arteries after zoster, causing a vasculopathy with stroke."},{"type":"p","text":"Postherpetic neuralgia is pain that persists in the affected dermatome for more than about three months after the rash heals. Its risk rises with age and with the severity of the acute pain and rash. Inflammation and necrosis in the ganglion and nerve destroy many sensory neurons, and the injured, surviving neurons fire ectopically while central pain pathways become sensitized. The result is burning pain, allodynia (pain from light touch), and sometimes numbness in the same area."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-07-C18","obj":"NE-20-G.18","title":"Spirochetal meningitis: Lyme neuroborreliosis and leptospirosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Spirochetes are thin, helical bacteria that move by corkscrew motion powered by periplasmic flagella (endoflagella) lying beneath the outer membrane. This motility lets them penetrate tissue and cross barriers, including the blood-brain barrier, and they classically produce a lymphocytic (aseptic-type) meningitis rather than the neutrophilic meningitis of pyogenic bacteria."},{"type":"p","text":"Lyme disease is caused by Borrelia burgdorferi, transmitted in North America by Ixodes ticks (the blacklegged or deer tick). White-footed mice and other small mammals are the main reservoir for the bacteria, while deer support the adult ticks. Transmission usually requires prolonged tick attachment, and most cases occur in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States in late spring and summer. Early localized disease is erythema migrans, an expanding red rash at the bite site. Over the following weeks the organism spreads in the blood, and early disseminated disease can involve the heart (AV block), skin (multiple erythema migrans), and nervous system."},{"type":"p","text":"Lyme neuroborreliosis classically presents as a triad that can occur alone or together. Lymphocytic meningitis causes headache and mild neck stiffness, with CSF lymphocytes, raised protein, normal glucose, and intrathecal production of antibody to Borrelia. Cranial neuritis most often affects the facial nerve, and the palsy is bilateral in a notable minority of cases. Because bilateral facial palsy has few causes, Lyme disease is a leading consideration in an endemic area. Painful radiculoneuritis causes burning, shooting pain in a dermatomal distribution, sometimes with weakness. Late disease can cause a mild encephalopathy and peripheral neuropathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Leptospirosis is caused by Leptospira interrogans, a spirochete with hooked ends. Rodents, especially rats, are the most important reservoir, though dogs and livestock also carry it, shedding the organism in their urine into water and soil. Humans are infected when contaminated water enters through abraded skin or mucous membranes, so cases follow flooding, freshwater swimming, and occupational exposure, especially in tropical regions. The illness is often biphasic. A leptospiremic phase brings abrupt fever, severe headache, calf myalgia, and conjunctival suffusion (red eyes without discharge). After a brief improvement, an immune phase follows as antibodies appear, and aseptic lymphocytic meningitis is its most common neurologic feature. Severe disease (Weil disease) adds jaundice, acute kidney injury, and hemorrhage, including pulmonary hemorrhage."},{"type":"table","title":"Spirochetes that cause meningitis","cols":["Feature","Borrelia burgdorferi","Leptospira interrogans","Treponema pallidum"],"rows":[["Transmission","Ixodes tick bite","Water or soil contaminated by animal urine","Sexual contact or vertical"],["Reservoir","White-footed mice and small mammals","Rats, dogs, livestock","Humans only"],["Neurologic pattern","Meningitis, facial palsy (often bilateral), radiculoneuritis","Aseptic meningitis in the immune phase","Meningovascular disease, general paresis, tabes dorsalis (C11)"],["Other clues","Erythema migrans, AV block","Conjunctival suffusion, calf pain, jaundice and renal failure","Chancre, secondary rash"]],"caption":"All three cause lymphocytic meningitis; the exposure and associated findings identify the organism."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Nearly every infection in this chapter can be solved by linking three facts: how the agent reaches the nervous system, which cell it damages, and how the host responds. Agents that travel inside axons (rabies virus, poliovirus, tetanus toxin, VZV) are shielded from antibody, and Naegleria reaches the brain by migrating along olfactory nerve fibers; both routes bypass the bloodstream. Agents that reach the subarachnoid space through the blood (pneumococcus, meningococcus, Cryptococcus) cause meningitis whose character depends on whether the host response is neutrophilic, lymphocytic, or weak. The target cell predicts the deficit: anterior horn cells give flaccid weakness (polio, West Nile), oligodendrocytes give demyelination (PML), Schwann cells give peripheral neuropathy (leprosy), inhibitory interneurons give spasticity (tetanus), and cholinergic terminals give flaccid paralysis with autonomic failure (botulism)."},{"type":"p","text":"Immune status narrows the list, and pathology reveals the tempo. Very low CD4 counts invite reactivation or opportunistic disease from Toxoplasma, Cryptococcus, CMV, EBV, and JC virus; Th1 strength decides the form of leprosy; and neonates and older adults share Listeria because both have weaker cell-mediated immunity. Disorders without inflammation (prion disease) or with persistent intrathecal antibody (SSPE) show that inflammation itself can be absent, present but ineffective, or the main cause of injury, as in bacterial and tuberculous meningitis. A limit of this approach is overlap: ring-enhancing lesions, lymphocytic CSF, and subacute dementia each have several causes, so the full picture, not one finding, should drive the answer."}],"summary":["Arboviruses cycle between mosquitoes and birds; humans are dead-end hosts, and West Nile virus can destroy anterior horn cells to cause polio-like flaccid paralysis in older adults.","Botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release at peripheral cholinergic terminals (descending flaccid paralysis, dilated pupils, no fever or sensory loss), while tetanus toxin blocks glycine and GABA release from spinal inhibitory interneurons (spastic paralysis).","Brain abscess location and organisms follow the route of spread, and the collagen capsule forms the enhancing ring; lumbar puncture risks herniation.","Viral encephalitis shows perivascular cuffs, microglial nodules, and neuronophagia; inclusions (Negri bodies, Cowdry A, owl's eye) point to rabies, herpesviruses, and CMV.","Low CD4 counts permit Toxoplasma (multiple ring lesions), Cryptococcus (high opening pressure, capsular antigen), CMV, EBV lymphoma, and JC virus (PML); ketoacidosis frees iron for angioinvasive Mucorales.","Bacterial meningitis organisms follow age: group B streptococcus, E. coli, and Listeria in neonates; S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis afterward; Listeria returns in older adults.","Tuberculous meningitis forms a basilar exudate that traps cranial nerves, blocks CSF flow, and causes endarteritis with infarcts.","Th1-dominant tuberculoid leprosy is paucibacillary with few anesthetic plaques; Th2-dominant lepromatous leprosy is multibacillary with leonine facies and symmetric neuropathy.","Tabes dorsalis destroys dorsal roots and columns (sensory ataxia, Romberg sign, areflexia, Charcot joints, Argyll Robertson pupils); a reactive CSF VDRL is specific but insensitive.","Prions (beta-sheet PrP templating its own formation), PML (lytic oligodendrocyte infection), and SSPE (defective measles virus) are slow progressive CNS infections with distinctive pathology and CSF findings."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, section on infections"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles; Infectious Diseases, sections on leprosy and syphilis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Clostridia; Mycobacteria; Spirochetes; Herpesviruses; Picornaviruses; Rabies and arboviruses; Prions; Medical mycology"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Effector mechanisms of cell-mediated immunity; immunity to microbes"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious diseases and nervous system disorders"}]},"NE-20-H":{"unit":"NE-20-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-08","title":"Trauma to the Brain and Spinal Cord","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains what mechanical force does to the brain and spinal cord and how each injury produces its signs. It moves from functional injury (concussion) to structural injury (contusion and diffuse axonal injury), then to the expanding hematoma that kills through rising intracranial pressure and herniation, and finally to spinal cord transection and compression. Board questions give a mechanism of injury, a time course, or a pattern of signs and ask you to name the lesion, its level, or what happens next. Reasoning from the vessel torn, the tissue sheared, and the structure compressed answers most of them.","prerequisites":["Meninges, dural partitions, and the potential epidural and subdural spaces (NE-4)","Course of the oculomotor nerve and the corticospinal tract through the midbrain (NE-7, NE-8)","Cerebral blood flow, autoregulation, and cerebral perfusion pressure (NE-10)","Spinal cord segments, the reflex arc, and upper versus lower motor neuron signs (NE-3, NE-5)","Sympathetic outflow from T1 to L2 and sacral parasympathetic outflow from S2 to S4 (NE-9)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"The anatomy of epidural and subdural hematomas is taught there; this chapter follows what happens once either becomes a mass."},{"to":"Diseases of increased intracranial fluid (NE-20-C)","how":"Traumatic hematomas and contusions raise intracranial pressure by the same pressure-volume rules and herniate through the same dural openings."},{"to":"Degenerative diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy is a tauopathy whose distribution of tau separates it from Alzheimer disease."},{"to":"Neoplasms and infections (NE-20-F, NE-19)","how":"Vertebral metastases and spinal epidural abscess are the main nontraumatic causes of cord and cauda equina compression."},{"to":"Autonomic nervous system (NE-9)","how":"Loss of descending control of sympathetic and sacral parasympathetic neurons explains neurogenic shock, bladder failure, and autonomic dysreflexia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Concussion","def":"A traumatic, rapidly resolving disturbance of brain function with no lesion on standard CT or MRI."},{"term":"Contusion","def":"A bruise of the brain: hemorrhage and necrosis in cortex, centered on the crests of gyri, where the brain struck the skull."},{"term":"Coup and contrecoup","def":"Contusion beneath the point of impact (coup) and on the opposite side of the brain (contrecoup)."},{"term":"Diffuse axonal injury","def":"Widespread axonal damage caused by rotational shear, producing coma without a mass lesion."},{"term":"Axonal spheroid","def":"A bulbous swelling where transported material accumulates at a point of axonal damage; also called a retraction ball."},{"term":"Monro-Kellie doctrine","def":"Because the skull is rigid, any increase in one intracranial volume (brain, blood, or CSF) must be offset by a decrease in another or pressure rises."},{"term":"Cerebral perfusion pressure","def":"The pressure driving blood through the brain, equal to mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure."},{"term":"Uncal herniation","def":"Displacement of the medial temporal lobe through the tentorial notch, compressing the oculomotor nerve and midbrain."},{"term":"Duret hemorrhage","def":"Hemorrhage in the midline midbrain and pons from tearing of paramedian basilar branches as the brainstem is pushed downward."},{"term":"Spinal shock","def":"The temporary loss of all reflex activity, tone, and autonomic function below an acute cord lesion."},{"term":"Sensory level","def":"The dermatome below which sensation is lost on the trunk or limbs, marking a lesion of the spinal cord."},{"term":"Cauda equina","def":"The bundle of lumbar and sacral nerve roots within the thecal sac below the end of the spinal cord."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-08-C01","obj":"NE-20-H.1","title":"Concussion, postconcussion syndrome, and chronic traumatic encephalopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Concussion is a traumatic brain injury in which force transmitted to the head causes a rapid-onset, short-lived disturbance of neurologic function that resolves on its own. Typical features are confusion, amnesia for the event, headache, dizziness, nausea, and slowed thinking. Loss of consciousness, when it happens, is brief and is not required. Standard CT and MRI are normal, and that is the defining point: concussion is a disorder of function, not a visible lesion. A focal deficit or an abnormal scan means contusion, hemorrhage, or axonal injury instead."},{"type":"p","text":"The disturbance is metabolic. Sudden acceleration stretches neuronal membranes and axons, mechanically opened channels let K+ leave and Na+ and Ca2+ enter, and depolarized neurons release a surge of glutamate that spreads the depolarization. Restoring ion gradients drives the Na+/K+ ATPase hard and sharply increases glucose use, at the same time that cerebral blood flow falls. Ca2+ taken up by mitochondria further limits ATP production. This supply and demand mismatch explains symptoms in a structurally intact brain, and it is why a second blow before recovery is complete can cause disproportionate injury. Most people recover within days to a few weeks."},{"type":"flow","title":"Neurometabolic cascade of concussion","steps":["Acceleration stretches neuronal membranes and axons","K+ efflux, Na+ and Ca2+ influx","Glutamate release spreads depolarization","Na+/K+ ATPase and glucose use surge","Cerebral blood flow falls: energy crisis","Ion balance and flow recover over days to weeks"],"caption":"Concussion is a temporary energy mismatch, not structural damage, which is why imaging stays normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Postconcussion syndrome is the persistence of these symptoms well beyond the usual recovery, often for months: headache, dizziness, fatigue, irritability, poor concentration and memory, insomnia, anxiety, and low mood. Imaging remains normal. The mechanism is not settled; lingering metabolic and microstructural axonal changes and psychological factors probably both contribute, and severity correlates poorly with the force of the original blow."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE), called dementia pugilistica when first described in boxers, is a progressive tauopathy linked to repeated head impacts, including many that never caused symptoms, in contact sports and military service. Its defining lesion is hyperphosphorylated tau in neurons, with or without astrocytes, clustered around small vessels at the depths of cortical sulci in a patchy pattern. The brain shows frontal and temporal atrophy, enlarged ventricles, a fenestrated cavum septum pellucidum, a thinned corpus callosum, and a pale substantia nigra. Clinically, mood and behavior change (depression, impulsivity, aggression) and memory loss progress to dementia, sometimes with parkinsonism, years after exposure ends. Diagnosis is confirmed only at autopsy. Alzheimer disease differs because its tangles begin in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus and are accompanied by amyloid plaques."},{"type":"table","title":"Three outcomes of head impact without a mass lesion","cols":["Feature","Concussion","Postconcussion syndrome","Chronic traumatic encephalopathy"],"rows":[["Exposure","Single impact","Follows a concussion","Repeated impacts over years"],["Time course","Minutes to weeks, resolves","Persists for months","Progressive, years after exposure"],["Pathology","Functional and metabolic","No defined lesion","Perivascular tau at sulcal depths, atrophy"],["Imaging","Normal","Normal","Atrophy, enlarged ventricles"],["Core features","Confusion, amnesia, headache","Headache, dizziness, poor concentration, mood change","Behavior change, memory loss, dementia, parkinsonism"]],"caption":"All three can follow head impact with a normal acute CT; time course and tau pathology separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-08-C02","obj":"NE-20-H.2","title":"Coup and contrecoup contusion and diffuse axonal injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A contusion is a bruise of the brain: blood leaks from small vessels into cortex that has struck the inside of the skull, with necrosis and edema around it. The crests of gyri touch bone first, so the hemorrhage is wedge-shaped with its base at the crest and the depths of sulci are relatively spared."},{"type":"p","text":"The brain floats in CSF and lags behind the skull when the head speeds up or stops. A coup contusion forms beneath the point of impact; a contrecoup contusion forms on the opposite side, where the brain strikes the skull as it rebounds. Coup lesions dominate when a stationary head is struck by a moving object. Contrecoup lesions are often the larger ones when a moving head hits a fixed surface, as in a fall backward onto the occiput that bruises the frontal and temporal poles. Whatever the impact site, the orbital frontal surfaces and anterior temporal poles are contused most, because they rest against the ridged orbital roofs, sphenoid wings, and middle cranial fossa. Injury there explains disinhibition, poor judgment, and personality change (orbitofrontal) and memory problems and seizures (temporal). Contusions can enlarge and swell over the first 1 to 3 days, and healed ones leave depressed yellow-brown scars at gyral crests (plaques jaunes) containing hemosiderin and gliosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Diffuse axonal injury (DAI) is caused by rotational (angular) acceleration and deceleration, as in high-speed vehicle crashes, rather than by contact. Tissues of different density move at different rates, so shear concentrates at the gray-white junction, corpus callosum, internal capsule, and dorsolateral upper brainstem. Axons are rarely torn outright. Stretch disrupts the axolemma, admitting Ca2+ that activates proteases (calpains), which break down microtubules and neurofilaments. Fast axonal transport halts at the damaged point, transported organelles and proteins pile up, and within hours the axon swells into an axonal spheroid, then disconnects and undergoes Wallerian degeneration. Swellings are shown by immunostaining for amyloid precursor protein or by silver stains."},{"type":"p","text":"Because damage is spread through the hemispheric white matter and the reticular activating system of the upper brainstem, the patient is in coma from the moment of injury, yet CT shows no mass lesion and may be normal or show only small hemorrhages in the corpus callosum or dorsolateral brainstem. MRI is more sensitive. DAI is a leading cause of persistent coma or a vegetative state after trauma. A hematoma behaves differently: consciousness is lost later, as the mass expands."},{"type":"table","title":"Contusion versus diffuse axonal injury","cols":["Feature","Contusion","Diffuse axonal injury"],"rows":[["Force","Brain striking skull (linear impact)","Rotational acceleration and deceleration"],["Lesion","Hemorrhagic necrosis at gyral crests","Axonal swellings, small white matter hemorrhages"],["Typical sites","Orbital frontal and anterior temporal poles","Gray-white junction, corpus callosum, dorsolateral brainstem"],["Consciousness","Often preserved; focal deficits","Coma from the moment of injury"],["CT","Hemorrhagic foci, edema","Normal or nearly normal; no mass"],["Late result","Plaques jaunes, seizures, behavior change","Persistent coma or lasting cognitive disability"]],"caption":"Impact bruises the surface of the brain; rotation shears the axons deep within it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A driver in a high-speed rollover is unresponsive from the scene, but CT shows no hematoma or midline shift. What lesion explains this, and where would you expect to find it?","explain":"Rotational shear stretches long axons, halting transport and producing axonal swellings in the corpus callosum and upper brainstem; disconnection of the reticular activating system and hemispheric white matter causes coma without a mass.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on diffuse axonal injury and the contusion versus diffuse axonal injury table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-08-C03","obj":"NE-20-H.3","title":"The expanding hematoma: rising pressure, uncal herniation, and Duret hemorrhage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An epidural hematoma, usually from a fracture of the thin squamous temporal bone near the pterion that tears the middle meningeal artery, expands quickly under arterial pressure; in the classic pattern, seen in only a minority, a brief loss of consciousness is followed by a lucid interval of hours, then decline. An acute subdural hematoma from torn bridging veins collects more slowly. Either becomes a mass, and the sequence that follows is the same (the anatomy of each bleed is in NE-20-A)."},{"type":"p","text":"The adult skull is a rigid box holding brain (about 80% of its volume), blood, and CSF (about 10% each). By the Monro-Kellie doctrine, an enlarging clot is first offset by displacing CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and venous blood out of the skull, so intracranial pressure (ICP, normally about 5 to 15 mm Hg) barely rises. Once these reserves are used up, the pressure-volume curve turns steep and each added milliliter raises ICP sharply. This is why the patient can look well during the lucid interval: compensation is still holding ICP down. Because cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) equals mean arterial pressure (MAP) minus ICP, rising ICP lowers perfusion, and ischemic brain swells, which raises ICP further."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Vicious cycle of rising intracranial pressure","steps":["Expanding clot adds volume to the fixed skull","CSF and venous reserves exhausted; ICP rises steeply","CPP (MAP minus ICP) falls","Brain ischemia causes cytotoxic edema","Edema adds volume and raises ICP further"],"caption":"Once compensation is exhausted, ischemia and edema raise pressure faster than the clot alone."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure gradients then push tissue across dural partitions. A lateral mass forces the medial temporal lobe (uncus and parahippocampal gyrus) through the tentorial notch, compressing midbrain-level structures in a predictable order. Parasympathetic pupilloconstrictor fibers run on the outer surface of the oculomotor nerve, so the ipsilateral pupil dilates and stops reacting before ptosis and a down-and-out eye appear."},{"type":"table","title":"Structures compressed in uncal herniation and the resulting signs","cols":["Structure compressed","Sign","Side relative to mass"],"rows":[["Oculomotor nerve (superficial parasympathetic fibers first)","Fixed dilated pupil, then ptosis and down-and-out eye","Same side"],["Cerebral peduncle (corticospinal fibers above the decussation)","Hemiparesis, Babinski sign","Opposite side"],["Opposite peduncle against tentorial edge (Kernohan notch)","Hemiparesis, a false localizing sign","Same side"],["Posterior cerebral artery","Occipital infarct with homonymous hemianopia","Infarct same side, field loss opposite"],["Midbrain reticular activating system","Falling consciousness, coma","Bilateral"]],"caption":"The pupil marks the side of the mass more reliably than the weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"As the brainstem is pushed downward, the basilar artery, anchored to the circle of Willis, cannot follow. Its paramedian perforating branches tear, producing flame-shaped Duret hemorrhages in the midline midbrain and pons, a sign of terminal brainstem injury. Brainstem ischemia evokes the Cushing reflex: a sympathetic surge raises blood pressure, baroreceptors slow the heart, and breathing becomes irregular. Tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum then compresses the medullary centers, causing apnea and death unless the clot is removed in time."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a herniating hematoma","text":"A 22-year-old is struck on the left side of the head, is dazed briefly, then talks normally for 3 hours before becoming drowsy. The left pupil is now dilated and unreactive and the right limbs are weak. Step 1: deterioration after a lucid interval points to a left epidural hematoma from a torn middle meningeal artery. Step 2: the left pupil fails first because the herniating left uncus presses on the outer, parasympathetic fibers of the left oculomotor nerve. Step 3: the left cerebral peduncle carries corticospinal fibers that have not yet crossed, so its compression weakens the right side."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In the patient above, weakness appears on the left, the same side as the blown pupil. Has the mass moved?","explain":"No. The midbrain is pushed against the opposite tentorial edge, compressing the right cerebral peduncle (Kernohan notch); the pupil still marks the side of the mass.","check":"Compare with the uncal herniation table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-08-C04","obj":"NE-20-H.4","title":"Spinal cord transection: spinal shock, spasticity, and the level of injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complete transection severs every ascending and descending tract at one level. At the level itself, anterior horn cells and exiting roots are destroyed, giving lower motor neuron signs in that segment's muscles. Below it, motor neurons survive but lose all input from the brain. Sensation, voluntary movement, and conscious bladder and bowel control are lost below the lesion, and the picture below the level changes over time in two phases."},{"type":"p","text":"The first phase is spinal shock. Spinal motor neurons and interneurons depend on continuous facilitation from descending reticulospinal, vestibulospinal, and corticospinal fibers. When that input stops abruptly, they lose their resting excitability and stop responding, so everything below the level is flaccid and areflexic, including the bulbocavernosus reflex, and autonomic reflexes fail as well: the bladder is atonic with urinary retention and the gut develops ileus. It resembles a lower motor neuron lesion, but it is a temporary failure of neurons that are still intact."},{"type":"p","text":"With a lesion above about T6, neurogenic shock accompanies it. Sympathetic preganglionic neurons (T1 to L2) lose their brain input, so vascular tone falls and blood pools in the splanchnic and limb beds, while vagal output from the brainstem continues unopposed. The result is hypotension with bradycardia and warm, dry skin, the opposite of the tachycardia and cold skin of hemorrhagic shock."},{"type":"p","text":"Over days to weeks, reflexes return, the bulbocavernosus reflex among the first, and then become exaggerated. Motor neurons below the lesion become intrinsically more excitable, denervated synapses become supersensitive, surviving segmental afferents sprout new connections, and descending inhibition is permanently gone. The result is the upper motor neuron picture: spastic paralysis, hyperreflexia, clonus, and a Babinski sign. The bladder becomes reflexively spastic, often contracting against a sphincter that fails to relax (detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia). In lesions at or above T6, a stimulus below the level such as a distended bladder can trigger massive reflex sympathetic vasoconstriction (autonomic dysreflexia); baroreceptors respond by slowing the heart through the vagus and reducing sympathetic outflow above the lesion, which flushes the skin there, but this inhibition cannot descend past the cut, so hypertension persists."},{"type":"flow","title":"Complete spinal cord transection over time","steps":["Cord severed at one level","Descending facilitation lost; spinal neurons lose excitability","Spinal shock: flaccid, areflexic, atonic bladder","Above T6: neurogenic hypotension with bradycardia","Days to weeks: spinal reflexes return","Spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski, spastic bladder"],"caption":"The same lesion looks like a lower motor neuron injury at first and an upper motor neuron injury later."},{"type":"p","text":"The level decides breathing and limb function. The phrenic nerve arises from C3 to C5, mainly C4, so complete injury at or above C3 paralyzes the diaphragm and the patient cannot breathe without a ventilator. Lower cervical injury leaves the diaphragm working but paralyzes intercostal and abdominal muscles, reducing vital capacity and cough. Any complete cervical injury causes quadriplegia (tetraplegia); thoracic and lower injury spares the arms and causes paraplegia."},{"type":"table","title":"Level of complete cord injury and its consequences","cols":["Level","Limbs","Breathing"],"rows":[["C1 to C3","Quadriplegia","Diaphragm paralyzed; ventilator dependent"],["C4","Quadriplegia","Diaphragm partly spared; breathing often weak and may need support"],["C5 to C8","Quadriplegia with some arm function, more at lower levels","Diaphragm intact; intercostals lost, weak cough"],["T1 to T12","Paraplegia; arms spared","Intercostal and abdominal loss lessens as level falls"],["Lumbar and sacral cord","Paraplegia of legs","Normal"]],"caption":"C3, C4, and C5 supply the phrenic nerve; the brachial plexus (C5 to T1) separates quadriplegia from paraplegia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two days after a complete T4 transection, the legs are flaccid with absent reflexes. Will they stay that way?","explain":"No. This is spinal shock from loss of descending facilitation; as spinal neurons regain and then exceed their excitability, spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign appear over days to weeks.","check":"Compare with the spinal shock paragraph and the transection flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-08-C05","obj":"NE-20-H.5","title":"Spinal cord compression versus cauda equina syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In adults the spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris at about the L1 to L2 vertebral level; below this, the thecal sac contains only the lumbar and sacral roots of the cauda equina. A mass above L1 compresses central tracts and produces upper motor neuron signs, while a mass below L2 compresses roots and produces lower motor neuron signs."},{"type":"p","text":"Spinal cord compression comes from an extradural mass pressing on the cord. Pressure first obstructs epidural venous drainage, causing congestion and vasogenic edema of the white matter, then reduces arterial flow, so ischemia and eventually infarction follow. Back pain is usually first, often for weeks; pain from vertebral metastasis is typically worse lying down and at night. Weakness of the legs follows, with a sensory level on the trunk, hyperreflexia, and Babinski signs. Bladder and bowel dysfunction tend to appear late. Rapid compression can cause initial flaccidity, similar to spinal shock, before upper motor neuron signs emerge."},{"type":"p","text":"Three causes dominate. Vertebral metastasis, most often from lung, breast, or prostate cancer (also kidney, lymphoma, and myeloma), grows from the vertebral body into the epidural space, most commonly in the thoracic spine. Spinal epidural abscess, usually Staphylococcus aureus, occurs mainly with injection drug use, diabetes, or recent spinal procedures and causes fever, back pain, and then deficit. Central disc herniation compresses the cord in the cervical or thoracic spine, whereas a large central lumbar disc (L4 to L5 or L5 to S1) compresses the cauda equina."},{"type":"flow","title":"Metastatic spinal cord compression","steps":["Hematogenous tumor seeds vertebral body","Tumor or collapsed bone enters epidural space","Epidural venous obstruction and vasogenic cord edema","Arterial flow falls; white matter ischemia","Weakness, sensory level, UMN signs, late bladder loss","Infarction makes the deficit permanent"],"caption":"Compression injures the cord largely through venous congestion and ischemia, so deficits can progress over hours."},{"type":"p","text":"Cauda equina syndrome damages multiple lumbar and sacral roots. Loss of the S2 to S5 dermatomes gives saddle anesthesia over the perineum, buttocks, and inner thighs. The S2 to S4 roots carry parasympathetic fibers to the detrusor and the afferents of bladder filling, so the bladder fails to contract and urine is retained, with overflow incontinence. Loss of the S2 to S4 reflex arcs abolishes the anal wink and bulbocavernosus reflex and lowers anal sphincter tone. Because roots are lower motor neuron axons, the legs show flaccid, often asymmetric weakness, reduced or absent ankle reflexes, no Babinski sign, and radicular pain. Conus medullaris lesions sit between the two pictures."},{"type":"table","title":"Cord compression, conus medullaris lesion, and cauda equina syndrome","cols":["Feature","Spinal cord compression","Conus medullaris","Cauda equina"],"rows":[["Structure","Cord above the conus","Sacral cord at about L1 to L2","Lumbar and sacral roots below L2"],["Motor","UMN: spastic, hyperreflexic, Babinski","Mixed UMN and LMN","LMN: flaccid, often asymmetric"],["Sensory","Sensory level on the trunk","Saddle anesthesia, symmetric","Saddle anesthesia, often asymmetric; radicular pain"],["Bladder and bowel","Usually late","Early and prominent","Retention with overflow; lost anal wink and tone"],["Typical causes","Metastasis, epidural abscess, cervical or thoracic disc","Tumor, trauma, abscess","Central lumbar disc, metastasis, abscess"]],"caption":"Above the conus the lesion hits tracts (upper motor neuron); below it the lesion hits roots (lower motor neuron)."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: tracts or roots?","text":"A 64-year-old man with prostate cancer has 4 weeks of thoracic back pain, worse at night, then 2 days of leg weakness. He has brisk knee reflexes, bilateral Babinski signs, and lost pinprick below the umbilicus. Step 1: upper motor neuron signs mean the cord itself is involved, above the conus. Step 2: a sensory level at the umbilicus (T10 dermatome) places the lesion at or above about T10. Step 3: night pain with prostate cancer points to vertebral metastasis. Saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, a lax anal sphincter, and absent ankle reflexes would instead place the lesion in the cauda equina."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"In every trauma case, anatomy determines what tears, physiology determines what follows, and the time course shows which process is operating. Imaging measures structure, not function, so a normal CT can accompany a confused patient (concussion, metabolic failure) or a comatose one (diffuse axonal injury, microscopic shear). Herniation signs follow from where parasympathetic fibers lie on the oculomotor nerve and from corticospinal fibers crossing below the midbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"In the spinal cord, the segmental origin of the phrenic, sympathetic, and sacral parasympathetic outflows and the vertebral level where the cord ends predict breathing, blood pressure, and bladder function. Nontraumatic compression links this chapter to oncology (vertebral metastasis) and microbiology (Staphylococcus aureus epidural abscess). The main limit is time: spinal shock and the lucid interval both make an early examination look different from what the lesion will become."}],"summary":["Concussion is a functional, metabolic injury (ion flux, glutamate surge, energy crisis with reduced blood flow) with normal CT and MRI.","Postconcussion syndrome is persistence of headache, dizziness, poor concentration, and mood change for months; chronic traumatic encephalopathy is a progressive tauopathy after repeated impacts, with perivascular tau at the depths of sulci.","Contusions occur at gyral crests beneath the impact (coup) and opposite it (contrecoup), most often in the orbital frontal and anterior temporal poles.","Diffuse axonal injury results from rotational shear; axonal swellings in the corpus callosum and upper brainstem cause coma without a mass on CT.","An expanding hematoma is compensated by displacing CSF and venous blood until reserve is exhausted, after which ICP rises steeply and CPP (MAP minus ICP) falls.","Uncal herniation compresses the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve (fixed dilated pupil first) and cerebral peduncle (contralateral hemiparesis); a Kernohan notch can give same-side weakness.","Downward brainstem shift tears paramedian basilar branches (Duret hemorrhages); the Cushing reflex and tonsillar herniation precede death unless the clot is removed.","Spinal shock (flaccid, areflexic, atonic bladder) gives way over days to weeks to spasticity, hyperreflexia, and Babinski signs; lesions above about T6 cause neurogenic hypotension with bradycardia.","The phrenic nerve arises from C3 to C5, so complete injury at or above C3 requires a ventilator; cervical injury causes quadriplegia and thoracic injury paraplegia.","Cord compression gives back pain, a sensory level, and upper motor neuron signs; cauda equina syndrome gives saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, lost anal reflex, and lower motor neuron leg weakness."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on trauma, raised intracranial pressure and herniation, and neurodegenerative diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes, section on spinal cord transection and spinal shock"},{"book":"Moore Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, sections on the spinal cord, conus medullaris, and cauda equina; Head, section on the cranial meninges"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-20-I":{"unit":"NE-20-I","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09","title":"Peripheral Nerve and Root Compression: Cranial Neuropathies, Entrapments, Radiculopathy, and Neuropathic Pain","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers injury to cranial nerves, spinal roots, and peripheral nerves from compression, inflammation, and trauma: Bell palsy, trigeminal neuralgia, carpal and cubital tunnel syndromes, thoracic outlet syndrome, disc herniation, radiculopathy, sciatica, lower limb entrapments, spinal stenosis, complex regional pain syndrome, and phantom limb pain. Nearly every question here is an anatomy problem. The stem gives a pattern of numbness, weakness, and reflex change, and you must decide whether the lesion lies in the cord, a root, a plexus trunk, or a single nerve, and at what level. The mechanism of compression (ischemia, demyelination, then axon loss) explains the order in which symptoms appear and why some recover quickly and others do not.","prerequisites":["Dermatomes, myotomes, and the segmental levels of the major stretch reflexes","Brachial and lumbosacral plexus organization and the course of the median, ulnar, tibial, and common fibular nerves","Course and branches of the facial and trigeminal nerves","Myelin, saltatory conduction, and Wallerian degeneration","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs"],"connections":[{"to":"CNS trauma and cord compression (NE-20-H)","how":"A central disc or stenosis that compresses the cord or cauda equina produces the cord and cauda equina syndromes taught there, rather than a single radiculopathy."},{"to":"Demyelinating disease (multiple sclerosis)","how":"A pontine plaque at the trigeminal root entry zone causes trigeminal neuralgia in a young adult."},{"to":"Endocrine and renal disease","how":"Hypothyroidism, diabetes, acromegaly, pregnancy, and beta-2 microglobulin amyloid in long-term dialysis add volume to the carpal tunnel or make nerves more vulnerable to pressure."},{"to":"Infectious disease","how":"HSV reactivation, VZV (Ramsay Hunt syndrome), and Lyme disease are infectious causes of lower motor neuron facial palsy."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal degeneration","how":"Disc degeneration, facet and uncovertebral osteophytes, and ligamentum flavum thickening are the structural causes of root entrapment and stenosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Entrapment neuropathy","def":"Focal nerve injury where a nerve passes through a tight anatomical space, caused by pressure, ischemia, and traction."},{"term":"Radiculopathy","def":"Dysfunction of a spinal nerve root producing dermatomal pain or sensory change, myotomal weakness, and reduced reflexes."},{"term":"Dermatome","def":"The area of skin supplied by the sensory fibers of a single spinal root."},{"term":"Myotome","def":"The group of muscles innervated by the motor fibers of a single spinal root."},{"term":"Neurapraxia","def":"Conduction block from focal demyelination with intact axons, which recovers as myelin is repaired."},{"term":"Axonotmesis","def":"Axon disruption with preserved connective tissue sheaths, followed by Wallerian degeneration and slow regrowth along the original path."},{"term":"Ephaptic transmission","def":"Abnormal electrical crosstalk between adjacent demyelinated axons, so activity in one fiber excites its neighbor."},{"term":"Neurogenic claudication","def":"Leg pain, numbness, or weakness brought on by standing or walking and relieved by flexion, caused by lumbar canal narrowing."},{"term":"Myelopathy","def":"Spinal cord dysfunction, producing upper motor neuron signs and tract sensory loss below the level of the lesion."},{"term":"Allodynia","def":"Pain evoked by a stimulus that is normally not painful, such as light touch."},{"term":"Hyperacusis","def":"Abnormal sensitivity to ordinary sound, here from paralysis of the stapedius muscle."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C01","obj":"NE-20-I.1","title":"Bell palsy: lower motor neuron facial nerve palsy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The facial nerve (CN VII) leaves the pons, crosses the cerebellopontine angle with CN VIII, and enters the internal acoustic meatus. It then runs through the facial canal in the temporal bone, a long bony tunnel whose narrowest part is the labyrinthine segment near the geniculate ganglion, and exits at the stylomastoid foramen to supply the muscles of facial expression. Along the way it gives off branches for tearing, sound damping, and taste."},{"type":"flow","title":"Facial nerve path and the branches given off in the canal","steps":["CN VII leaves the pons and enters the internal acoustic meatus","Geniculate ganglion: greater petrosal nerve to the lacrimal gland","Nerve to stapedius: damps loud sound","Chorda tympani: taste, anterior two-thirds of tongue; submandibular secretion","Stylomastoid foramen: motor branches to facial muscles"],"caption":"A lesion loses every function whose branch leaves at or distal to the lesion, so the extra deficits localize the level."},{"type":"p","text":"Bell palsy is an acute, unilateral, lower motor neuron facial palsy with no other identified cause. The leading explanation is inflammation of the nerve, most often linked to reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus in the geniculate ganglion. The inflamed nerve swells inside the rigid canal, which compresses its own blood supply, so ischemia and demyelination follow. Diabetes, pregnancy (especially late pregnancy and the early postpartum period), and Lyme disease are associated risks; Lyme disease is a classic cause of bilateral facial palsy. Weakness develops over hours to about 3 days, often with ear pain."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the nerve itself is damaged, the entire half of the face is weak: the forehead cannot wrinkle, the eye cannot close, and the mouth droops. On attempted closure the eye rolls upward (Bell phenomenon), and the blink of the corneal reflex is lost on that side although the afferent limb (V1) is intact. A stroke behaves differently. The part of the facial nucleus that supplies the forehead receives corticobulbar input from both hemispheres, so an upper motor neuron lesion weakens only the contralateral lower face."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing a facial palsy","cols":["Finding","Mechanism","Points to"],"rows":[["Whole half of face weak, forehead included","Final common pathway to all facial muscles damaged","Lower motor neuron lesion (nucleus or nerve)"],["Lower face weak, forehead spared","Bilateral cortical input to upper face nucleus","Contralateral upper motor neuron lesion"],["Hyperacusis","Stapedius paralyzed, ossicles not damped","Lesion proximal to the nerve to stapedius"],["Loss of taste, anterior two-thirds of tongue","Chorda tympani fibers interrupted","Lesion proximal to the chorda tympani"],["Reduced tearing","Greater petrosal fibers interrupted","Lesion at or proximal to the geniculate ganglion"],["Vesicles in ear canal, ear pain","VZV reactivation in the geniculate ganglion","Ramsay Hunt syndrome, not Bell palsy"]],"caption":"A lesion at the stylomastoid foramen causes facial weakness alone; lesions higher in the canal add the branch deficits."},{"type":"p","text":"Most patients recover over weeks to months because the usual injury is demyelination with intact axons. When axons degenerate, regrowth can be misdirected: fibers meant for one muscle reach another (synkinesis, such as eye closure with smiling), and salivary fibers can reach the lacrimal gland, causing tearing while eating. Incomplete eye closure with reduced tearing exposes the cornea to drying and ulceration."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient cannot raise the right eyebrow or close the right eye, and loud sounds are uncomfortable in the right ear, but tearing is normal. Where is the lesion?","explain":"The whole right face is weak, so this is a lower motor neuron lesion. Hyperacusis places it proximal to the nerve to stapedius, and normal tearing places it distal to the geniculate ganglion, in the facial canal between the two.","check":"The facial nerve flow and the localization table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C02","obj":"NE-20-I.2","title":"Carpal tunnel syndrome: median nerve compression at the wrist","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The carpal tunnel is a rigid channel with the carpal bones forming its floor and walls and the flexor retinaculum (transverse carpal ligament) forming its roof. Through it pass the median nerve and nine flexor tendons: four of flexor digitorum superficialis, four of flexor digitorum profundus, and flexor pollicis longus. Because the walls cannot stretch, anything that adds volume to the contents raises pressure on the nerve, which is the most compressible structure in the tunnel."},{"type":"flow","title":"From added volume to axon loss","steps":["Added volume or swelling within the rigid tunnel","Pressure rises on the median nerve","Venous congestion and ischemia of the nerve's small vessels","Focal demyelination slows conduction across the wrist","Intermittent paresthesia, worse at night","Sustained pressure causes axon loss: numbness, thenar atrophy"],"caption":"Early symptoms are reversible because they reflect ischemia and demyelination; constant numbness and atrophy mean axons have been lost."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors and how each raises tunnel pressure","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Pregnancy","Fluid retention swells tunnel contents"],["Hypothyroidism","Glycosaminoglycan (myxedematous) deposits in connective tissue"],["Rheumatoid arthritis","Tenosynovitis of the flexor tendons"],["Diabetes mellitus","Glycation and microangiopathy make the nerve less tolerant of pressure"],["Long-term dialysis","Beta-2 microglobulin amyloid deposits"],["Acromegaly","Soft tissue and synovial overgrowth"],["Repetitive forceful wrist use","Tendon sheath thickening and repeated pressure peaks"]],"caption":"Every listed risk either adds volume to a fixed space or lowers the nerve's tolerance of pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"Distal to the retinaculum the median nerve supplies sensation to the palmar thumb, index, middle, and lateral half of the ring finger. Its recurrent (motor) branch supplies the thenar muscles abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis, and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis, and digital branches supply the lateral two lumbricals. The result is paresthesia in that finger distribution and, later, weak thumb abduction and opposition with flattening of the thenar eminence."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms are typically worse at night because the wrist often rests in flexion during sleep, which raises tunnel pressure, and patients wake and shake the hand to relieve them. Tinel sign is paresthesia produced by tapping over the nerve at the wrist, where demyelinated segments are mechanosensitive. Phalen sign is paresthesia produced by holding the wrist fully flexed for about a minute. Nerve conduction studies show prolonged median sensory and motor latencies across the wrist."},{"type":"p","text":"Sensation over the thenar eminence and central palm is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch leaves the median nerve proximal to the wrist and runs superficial to the retinaculum. This is a key distinction: numbness of the palm itself places a median nerve lesion in the forearm or above, not in the carpal tunnel."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C03","obj":"NE-20-I.3","title":"Intervertebral disc herniation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each intervertebral disc has two parts. The annulus fibrosus is a ring of concentric fibrocartilage lamellae rich in type I collagen, whose fibers alternate in direction to resist twisting. The nucleus pulposus is a central gel, a remnant of the notochord, made of water-binding proteoglycans (mainly aggrecan) in a type II collagen network, which spreads compressive load to the annulus. The adult disc is largely avascular and depends on diffusion from the vertebral end plates."},{"type":"p","text":"With age, proteoglycan content and water fall, the nucleus loses turgor, and the annulus develops fissures under repeated flexion and axial load. Herniation occurs when nucleus material pushes through a torn annulus. It is most common in the lumbar spine at L4 to L5 and L5 to S1, the levels that bear the most load and motion. The posterior longitudinal ligament is strong centrally but narrow in the lumbar region, leaving the posterolateral annulus unsupported, so most herniations are posterolateral."},{"type":"p","text":"Pain has two sources. The outer annulus is innervated (by the sinuvertebral nerve), so annular tears cause axial back pain. Displaced nucleus material compresses the root mechanically and, because it is normally sequestered from the immune system, provokes an inflammatory reaction around the root; this chemical radiculitis explains why pain can be severe with a small herniation and why many herniations shrink and improve as macrophages resorb them."},{"type":"p","text":"Which root is hit depends on how roots exit. In the lumbar spine each root exits through the foramen below the pedicle of its own vertebra, and that root leaves high in the foramen, above the disc. The next root down crosses the back of the disc in the lateral recess on its way to exit one level lower. A posterolateral L4 to L5 herniation therefore compresses the traversing L5 root, and an L5 to S1 herniation compresses S1. A far lateral (foraminal) herniation is the exception and compresses the exiting root (L4 at L4 to L5). A large central herniation can compress several roots of the cauda equina."},{"type":"p","text":"In the cervical spine, roots C1 to C7 exit above the vertebra of the same number and C8 exits between C7 and T1, so a C5 to C6 herniation compresses C6 and a C6 to C7 herniation compresses C7."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the root from the disc level","text":"MRI shows a posterolateral herniation at L4 to L5. The L4 root exits through the L4 to L5 foramen above the disc, so it is usually spared. The L5 root is traveling down in the lateral recess across the back of this disc, so it is compressed. Expect L5 findings: pain and numbness over the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot, weak great toe extension and ankle dorsiflexion, and no change in the knee or ankle reflex. If the same disc herniated far laterally into the foramen, it would compress L4 instead, with a reduced knee reflex."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C04","obj":"NE-20-I.4","title":"Radiculopathy from nerve root entrapment","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A spinal root is formed from a dorsal (sensory) and ventral (motor) rootlet bundle that join near the intervertebral foramen; the dorsal root ganglion, containing the sensory cell bodies, usually lies within the foramen. Roots are vulnerable because they have thinner connective tissue sheaths than peripheral nerves and run through bony openings that narrow with degeneration. In younger adults the usual cause of entrapment is a herniated disc. In older adults it is spondylosis: osteophytes from the uncovertebral and facet joints, disc height loss, and ligament thickening narrow the foramen."},{"type":"p","text":"Root compression produces a predictable triad in the distribution of that single root: sharp, radiating pain and paresthesia in the dermatome, weakness in the myotome, and a reduced or absent reflex if the root carries one. Signs are lower motor neuron signs, because the injury is to the axons of the final motor pathway. Weakness is usually partial, because most muscles receive fibers from two or more roots. Maneuvers that narrow the foramen worsen the pain: in the neck, extension with rotation and axial loading toward the affected side (Spurling test); in the low back, coughing or straining, which raise epidural venous and cerebrospinal fluid pressure."},{"type":"table","title":"Root lesions to predict","cols":["Root","Sensory loss or pain","Weakness","Reflex reduced"],"rows":[["C6","Lateral forearm, thumb, index finger","Elbow flexion, wrist extension","Biceps, brachioradialis"],["C7","Middle finger","Elbow extension, wrist flexion, finger extension","Triceps"],["L4","Anterior knee, medial leg to medial malleolus","Knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion","Patellar"],["L5","Lateral leg, dorsum of foot, great toe","Great toe extension, dorsiflexion, inversion, hip abduction","None reliable"],["S1","Posterior calf, lateral foot and sole, little toe","Plantarflexion, hip extension, eversion","Achilles"]],"caption":"Dermatomes overlap, so sensory loss is often smaller than the full dermatome; reflex and motor changes are more localizing."},{"type":"p","text":"Electrodiagnosis confirms the level by mechanism. Because most root compression lies proximal to the dorsal root ganglion, it cuts the central process of the sensory neuron but leaves the cell body and its peripheral axon intact. Sensory nerve action potentials recorded from the limb therefore remain normal despite numbness, whereas a plexus or peripheral nerve lesion reduces them. Denervation of the paraspinal muscles, supplied by the posterior rami that branch just beyond the root, also points to a root rather than a plexus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has numbness of the thumb and index finger with a reduced biceps reflex, yet the median and radial sensory nerve action potentials are normal. Root or peripheral nerve?","explain":"Root (C6). The lesion is proximal to the dorsal root ganglion, so the peripheral sensory axons stay connected to their cell bodies and conduct normally even though signals no longer reach the cord.","check":"The paragraph on electrodiagnosis and the root table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C05","obj":"NE-20-I.5","title":"Sciatica and the straight leg raise","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The sciatic nerve forms from the L4 to S3 roots, leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen usually below piriformis, and descends in the posterior thigh before dividing into the tibial and common fibular nerves. Sciatica is radicular pain in this territory: pain that radiates from the buttock down the posterior or posterolateral thigh and below the knee, often with paresthesia in the foot. In most cases the cause is compression of the L5 or S1 root by a lumbar disc herniation."},{"type":"flow","title":"From L5 to S1 disc herniation to sciatica","steps":["Degeneration and load tear the posterolateral annulus","Nucleus pulposus herniates posterolaterally at L5 to S1","Traversing S1 root compressed and inflamed","Ectopic firing in the damaged root","Pain radiates buttock to posterior leg, below the knee","Weak plantarflexion, lateral foot numbness, reduced ankle reflex"],"caption":"The pain is felt in the root's territory, not at the site of compression, because ectopic impulses are read by the brain as coming from the root's normal receptive field."},{"type":"p","text":"Radiation below the knee is the key feature. Pain from facet joints, the sacroiliac joint, or muscle is referred somatic pain, which is usually deep, poorly localized, and stays above the knee. Radicular pain follows a dermatome into the leg and foot and is often sharper and shooting."},{"type":"p","text":"The straight leg raise test exploits root mobility. With the patient supine and the knee straight, the examiner flexes the hip. This pulls the sciatic nerve and draws the L5 and S1 roots a few millimeters through their foramina. A normal root slides freely, but an inflamed root tethered over a herniation is stretched, reproducing the radiating pain. The test is positive when pain below the knee appears between about 30 and 70 degrees of hip flexion; below 30 degrees the root barely moves, and beyond 70 degrees hamstring and lumbar joint strain cause nonspecific discomfort. Dorsiflexing the foot at the point of pain adds tension and increases it. The test is sensitive for L5 or S1 root irritation but not specific. Pain in the affected leg when the opposite leg is raised (crossed straight leg raise) is less sensitive but much more specific for herniation. Upper lumbar roots (L2 to L4), which form the femoral nerve, are tested instead by hip extension with the patient prone."},{"type":"p","text":"Not all sciatic pain is radicular. Compression of the sciatic nerve by piriformis in the buttock, pelvic masses, or trauma can mimic it, but these spare the paraspinal muscles and produce findings in a nerve rather than root pattern. Sciatica with saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, or bilateral weakness suggests cauda equina compression, covered in NE-20-H."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C06","obj":"NE-20-I.6","title":"Thoracic outlet syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thoracic outlet is the passage from the root of the neck to the axilla. The brachial plexus trunks and the subclavian artery pass through the interscalene triangle, bounded by anterior scalene in front, middle scalene behind, and the first rib below. The subclavian vein passes in front of anterior scalene, then through the costoclavicular space between the clavicle and first rib. Distally, the neurovascular bundle runs beneath pectoralis minor. Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) is compression of these structures at any of these sites."},{"type":"p","text":"The lower trunk, formed by C8 and T1, lies lowest in the plexus and rests directly on the first rib, so it is the part most often compressed. The usual culprits are a cervical rib (an elongated costal element of C7) or the fibrous band that extends from it to the first rib, scalene muscle hypertrophy or anomalous insertion, and post-traumatic scarring. Raising the arm overhead narrows the costoclavicular space and tightens the structures over the rib, so symptoms are characteristically worse with overhead activity."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of thoracic outlet syndrome","cols":["Form","Structure compressed","Mechanism of findings","Findings"],"rows":[["Neurogenic (most cases)","Lower trunk, C8 to T1","Chronic compression and traction of the lowest trunk","Paresthesia of medial forearm, ring and little fingers; weak, wasted intrinsic hand muscles"],["Arterial (rare)","Subclavian artery","Repeated compression causes stenosis, post-stenotic dilation, thrombus, and distal emboli","Arm pallor, coolness, fatigue with use, reduced pulse; digital ischemia"],["Venous","Subclavian vein at costoclavicular space","Repeated compression injures the vein wall and causes thrombosis, often after strenuous overhead effort","Arm swelling, cyanosis, heaviness, distended superficial chest and shoulder veins"]],"caption":"Neurogenic symptoms follow C8 and T1, so they resemble ulnar nerve disease; vascular forms add color, temperature, or swelling changes."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurogenic TOS produces paresthesia and aching along the ulnar side of the hand and the medial forearm (the C8 and T1 dermatomes) and weakness of the intrinsic hand muscles, which are T1 dominant. Because the median nerve also carries C8 and T1 fibers to the thenar muscles, thenar wasting can be prominent even though sensory symptoms are ulnar sided."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions matter on exams. First, ulnar neuropathy at the elbow does not cause medial forearm numbness, because the medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve arises from the medial cord, above the elbow; medial forearm sensory loss therefore points to the lower trunk or the C8 and T1 roots. Second, an apical lung (Pancoast) tumor can invade the same lower trunk, and it adds a Horner syndrome from involvement of the sympathetic chain, along with weight loss or a smoking history. A lower trunk traction injury from forced arm abduction (Klumpke palsy) produces a similar distribution acutely."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C07","obj":"NE-20-I.7","title":"Trigeminal neuralgia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) carries general sensation from the face, cornea, oral cavity, and anterior scalp through its ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular (V3) divisions, and V3 also carries motor fibers to the muscles of mastication. Its sensory root enters the lateral pons. Near the brainstem there is a short segment where myelin changes from central (oligodendrocyte) to peripheral (Schwann cell), and this root entry zone is mechanically and metabolically vulnerable."},{"type":"p","text":"Trigeminal neuralgia is paroxysmal, unilateral, electric shock or stabbing pain lasting from a fraction of a second to about 2 minutes, in one or more divisions, most often V2 and V3 and rarely V1 alone. Attacks are triggered by innocuous stimulation of trigger zones around the lips, nose, or cheek: light touch, chewing, talking, shaving, brushing teeth, or a breeze. Between attacks the patient is usually pain free, and a brief refractory period may follow an attack. In the classic form the sensory examination is normal. It is most common after age 50 and more common in women."},{"type":"flow","title":"From vascular compression to triggered pain","steps":["Artery loop (often superior cerebellar) pulses against the root entry zone","Focal demyelination of trigeminal root axons","Ectopic impulses arise at the demyelinated segment","Ephaptic crosstalk from touch fibers to nociceptive fibers","Afterdischarges amplify into a burst","Light touch or chewing triggers a paroxysm of pain"],"caption":"Demyelinated axons become hyperexcitable and electrically coupled, so ordinary touch input recruits pain fibers."},{"type":"p","text":"The usual cause is compression of the root near the pons by a blood vessel, most often a loop of the superior cerebellar artery. Chronic pulsation demyelinates the root, and large touch fibers (A-beta) can then excite nociceptive fibers, so a light touch sets off a burst of pain. A short silent period follows while the hyperexcitable membrane recovers. The paroxysms respond to drugs that produce use-dependent blockade of voltage-gated Na+ channels, which supports the idea that repetitive ectopic firing drives the attacks."},{"type":"p","text":"In a younger adult, especially with bilateral symptoms, consider multiple sclerosis: a demyelinating plaque at the trigeminal root entry zone in the pons produces the same mechanism without a vessel. Other secondary causes include cerebellopontine angle tumors such as meningioma, epidermoid tumor, or vestibular schwannoma. Persistent sensory loss, an absent corneal reflex, weakness of chewing, or constant background pain all suggest a structural lesion rather than classic neuralgia. Postherpetic neuralgia differs because it follows a zoster rash, most often in V1, and causes continuous burning pain with allodynia rather than brief shocks."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C08","obj":"NE-20-I.8","title":"Cubital tunnel, tarsal tunnel, and common fibular nerve entrapment","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"These three entrapments follow the same rules as carpal tunnel syndrome: a nerve passes a fixed bony or fibrous point, and pressure, traction, or both cause ischemia, demyelination, and eventually axon loss in the territory distal to that point."},{"type":"table","title":"Three entrapments compared","cols":["Feature","Cubital tunnel","Tarsal tunnel","Common fibular at fibular neck"],"rows":[["Nerve and site","Ulnar nerve behind medial epicondyle, under fibrous arch of flexor carpi ulnaris","Tibial nerve under flexor retinaculum behind medial malleolus","Common fibular nerve winding around fibular neck"],["Typical causes","Leaning on elbow, prolonged elbow flexion, arthritis, old fractures","Ganglion, varicose veins, flat foot, ankle trauma, tenosynovitis","Leg crossing, squatting, tight cast, fibular fracture, weight loss"],["Sensory loss","Little finger and ulnar half of ring finger, palmar and dorsal","Sole (medial and lateral plantar nerves), burning, worse standing","Lateral leg and dorsum of foot"],["Weakness","Interossei, hypothenar muscles, adductor pollicis, medial lumbricals","Intrinsic foot muscles, often subtle","Dorsiflexion and toe extension (foot drop), eversion"],["Spared","Medial forearm sensation","Ankle movement; heel if calcaneal branch arises above tunnel","Inversion, plantarflexion, ankle reflex"]],"caption":"The spared functions are as diagnostic as the deficits, because they show which branches leave above the compression."},{"type":"p","text":"The ulnar nerve is stretched by elbow flexion and the tunnel beneath the flexor carpi ulnaris arch narrows, so symptoms worsen with the elbow bent, as when holding a phone or sleeping with arms flexed. Weak interossei cause difficulty spreading and closing the fingers, and weak adductor pollicis makes the patient flex the thumb tip with flexor pollicis longus (median nerve) to grip paper (Froment sign). Clawing of the ring and little fingers is milder with an elbow lesion than with a wrist lesion, because a lesion at the elbow also weakens the ulnar half of flexor digitorum profundus, removing the pull that flexes the fingertips. Numbness of the dorsal ulnar hand places the lesion above the wrist, because the dorsal cutaneous branch leaves in the forearm and bypasses the Guyon canal."},{"type":"p","text":"The common fibular nerve lies just under the skin against bone at the fibular neck. Its deep branch supplies the ankle and toe dorsiflexors, and its superficial branch supplies fibularis longus and brevis (evertors) and the lateral leg skin. Injury causes foot drop with a high-stepping gait to clear the toes."},{"type":"p","text":"In tarsal tunnel syndrome the tibial nerve is compressed beneath the flexor retinaculum behind the medial malleolus, where it divides into the medial and lateral plantar nerves. The result is burning paresthesia of the sole that worsens with standing and walking, while ankle strength and the ankle reflex are preserved because the calf muscles are supplied above the tunnel."},{"type":"example","title":"Fibular neuropathy or L5 radiculopathy?","text":"Both cause foot drop and numbness over the dorsum of the foot, because the L5 root supplies the dorsiflexors through the fibular nerve. Test inversion. Tibialis posterior inverts the foot; it is innervated by the tibial nerve but carries L5 fibers. In a fibular neuropathy at the knee, inversion is normal because the tibial nerve is untouched. In L5 radiculopathy, inversion is weak, and hip abduction (gluteus medius, superior gluteal nerve, L5) may also be weak. Back pain radiating below the knee and a positive straight leg raise further favor the root. Paraspinal denervation on electromyography confirms it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C09","obj":"NE-20-I.9","title":"Lumbar and cervical spinal stenosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Spinal stenosis is narrowing of the central canal, lateral recesses, or foramina. In older adults it is acquired: disc height loss with posterior bulging, facet joint hypertrophy and osteophytes, thickening and infolding of the ligamentum flavum, and degenerative spondylolisthesis all crowd the canal. Congenitally short pedicles, as in achondroplasia, lower the threshold. What is compressed depends on the level. In the lumbar spine the canal contains the cauda equina, so stenosis affects multiple roots. In the cervical spine it contains the cord, so stenosis causes myelopathy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanism of neurogenic claudication","steps":["Standing or walking extends the lumbar spine","Ligamentum flavum buckles inward; canal and foramina narrow","Epidural pressure rises; venous outflow from the roots is impaired","Walking raises the metabolic demand of crowded roots","Root ischemia: leg pain, numbness, heaviness","Sitting or bending forward widens the canal; symptoms ease"],"caption":"Posture changes canal size, so lumbar flexion relieves symptoms and extension provokes them."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurogenic claudication is bilateral or unilateral buttock and leg pain, numbness, or weakness brought on by standing or walking and relieved within minutes by sitting or leaning forward. Patients walk farther pushing a shopping cart or going uphill, and they tolerate cycling, all of which flex the spine. The examination at rest is often near normal, because compression is positional. Vascular claudication must be distinguished (table)."},{"type":"table","title":"Neurogenic versus vascular claudication","cols":["Feature","Neurogenic (lumbar stenosis)","Vascular (arterial disease)"],"rows":[["Cause of pain","Positional root ischemia and compression","Muscle ischemia from fixed arterial narrowing"],["Provoked by","Standing as well as walking; downhill worse","Walking or exercise only; uphill worse"],["Relieved by","Sitting or flexing the spine","Standing still"],["Cycling","Usually tolerated","Provokes pain"],["Pulses and skin","Normal","Reduced pulses, cool, hairless, shiny skin"]],"caption":"Relief by standing still points to arterial disease; relief only by flexion points to the spine."},{"type":"p","text":"Cervical spondylotic myelopathy is the most common cause of spinal cord dysfunction in older adults. The cord is squeezed between disc and osteophyte in front and a buckled ligamentum flavum behind, especially in extension, and repeated motion adds ischemic injury. Compression of the lateral corticospinal tracts gives upper motor neuron signs below the level: spastic, stiff gait, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign, and a positive Hoffmann sign. Dorsal column involvement causes poor proprioception, unsteady gait, and clumsy hands. Roots at the compressed level may add lower motor neuron signs, such as a reduced biceps reflex with brisk reflexes below. Neck flexion may send an electric sensation down the spine (Lhermitte sign), and bladder symptoms appear late. Sensory findings separate it from amyotrophic lateral sclerosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 70-year-old has leg pain after walking two blocks that eases when he leans on a shopping cart, with normal foot pulses. Why does leaning forward help?","explain":"Lumbar flexion stretches the ligamentum flavum flat and opens the canal and foramina, lowering epidural pressure and restoring venous drainage and blood flow to the cauda equina roots. Normal pulses argue against arterial claudication.","check":"The neurogenic claudication flow and comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-09-C10","obj":"NE-20-I.10","title":"Complex regional pain syndrome and phantom limb pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) is continuing pain in a limb after an injury, out of proportion in severity and duration to the original event and not confined to one nerve or dermatome. It most often follows a fracture (distal radius fracture is a classic setting), surgery, a sprain, or immobilization. Type I, formerly called reflex sympathetic dystrophy, occurs without an identifiable nerve injury. Type II, formerly called causalgia, follows a documented injury to a nerve. Diagnosis is clinical, using the Budapest criteria, which require symptoms and signs across the categories below with no better explanation."},{"type":"table","title":"Features of CRPS and their mechanisms","cols":["Category","Findings","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Sensory","Burning pain, allodynia, hyperalgesia","Peripheral and central sensitization of pain pathways"],["Vasomotor","Temperature asymmetry, red or blue skin","Altered sympathetic vasomotor control and neurogenic vasodilation"],["Sudomotor and edema","Swelling, increased or decreased sweating","Neuropeptide-driven plasma extravasation; autonomic dysfunction"],["Motor and trophic","Weakness, stiffness, tremor; changes in hair, nails, skin; patchy bone loss","Disuse, altered cortical motor control, and trophic effects of inflammation"]],"caption":"No single test confirms CRPS; the pattern of findings across categories makes the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Several mechanisms combine. Injured nociceptor terminals release substance P and calcitonin gene-related peptide, causing vasodilation, plasma leakage, and swelling (exaggerated neurogenic inflammation) along with local cytokines. Repeated nociceptive input sensitizes dorsal horn neurons (central sensitization), so touch becomes painful. After injury, nociceptors can express adrenergic receptors, coupling sympathetic activity to pain. Early disease often shows a warm, red limb, and later disease more often a cool, blue one. The limb's representation in the somatosensory cortex shrinks and distorts. Imaging may show patchy periarticular osteopenia and increased uptake on bone scan."},{"type":"p","text":"Phantom limb pain is pain perceived in a limb that has been amputated, often burning, cramping, or shooting. It must be distinguished from phantom sensation, the nonpainful feeling that the limb is still present, which most amputees experience, and from residual limb (stump) pain, which arises in the remaining tissue, for example from a neuroma. Mechanisms operate at three levels. Cut axons form neuromas that fire spontaneously, and dorsal root ganglion neurons become hyperexcitable. In the dorsal horn, loss of normal input and central sensitization amplify signals. In the cortex, neighboring regions expand into the deafferented area; after arm amputation the face area may invade the hand area, so touching the face can evoke sensation in the phantom hand, and greater remapping is associated with more pain. Severe pain before amputation increases the risk."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy decides the localization in every condition in this chapter. Where a branch leaves relative to the compression explains spared palm sensation in carpal tunnel syndrome, preserved tearing in a distal facial palsy, preserved inversion in fibular neuropathy, and medial forearm numbness in thoracic outlet syndrome. The level of the dorsal root ganglion explains why root lesions spare sensory nerve action potentials. The developmental origin of a cervical rib and the course of the roots relative to the discs explain who is compressed and where."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and physiology explain the time course. Pressure first impairs microvascular flow and myelin, which is reversible, and only later destroys axons, which recover slowly and sometimes with misdirected regrowth. Endocrine disease, pregnancy, dialysis amyloid, and rheumatoid synovitis act by adding volume to fixed spaces; HSV, VZV, and Borrelia act by inflaming nerves in bony canals; multiple sclerosis acts by demyelinating a vulnerable root entry zone. Chronic pain syndromes show the limit of a purely structural view: in CRPS and phantom limb pain the nervous system itself becomes the generator, through peripheral sensitization, central sensitization, and cortical reorganization."}],"summary":["Bell palsy is a lower motor neuron CN VII palsy that weakens the whole half of the face (upper motor neuron lesions spare the forehead); reduced tearing, hyperacusis, and lost anterior tongue taste localize the lesion along the canal.","Carpal tunnel syndrome is median nerve compression under the flexor retinaculum, with nocturnal paresthesia of the thumb to the lateral ring finger, thenar weakness, and spared palm sensation.","Posterolateral lumbar herniation compresses the traversing lower root (L4 to L5 disc affects L5); cervical roots exit above their vertebra (C5 to C6 disc affects C6).","Radiculopathy gives dermatomal pain, myotomal weakness, and reflex loss with lower motor neuron signs; sensory nerve action potentials stay normal because the lesion is proximal to the dorsal root ganglion.","Sciatica is radicular pain below the knee, usually from L5 or S1; the straight leg raise stretches these roots and is sensitive, while the crossed test is specific.","Thoracic outlet syndrome compresses the C8 to T1 lower trunk, subclavian artery, or vein, worse with the arm overhead; medial forearm numbness separates it from ulnar neuropathy at the elbow.","Trigeminal neuralgia is brief, triggered V2 or V3 pain from vascular demyelination of the root entry zone; in young or bilateral cases, suspect multiple sclerosis.","Fibular neuropathy causes foot drop with preserved inversion; L5 radiculopathy weakens inversion and hip abduction as well.","Lumbar stenosis causes neurogenic claudication relieved by flexion with normal pulses; cervical stenosis causes myelopathy with upper motor neuron signs below the level.","CRPS is limb pain out of proportion to an injury with sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor, and trophic changes (type II after nerve injury); phantom limb pain reflects neuroma firing, central sensitization, and cortical remapping."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, intervertebral discs and spinal nerves; Upper limb and Lower limb, nerve courses and entrapment sites; Head, facial and trigeminal nerves"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, compressive neuropathies"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: Pain, Headache, and Thermal Sensations"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory transduction and somatic sensory pathways"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-20-J":{"unit":"NE-20-J","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10","title":"Diseases of the Special Senses","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the common diseases of the eye and ear: lid and conjunctival disease, the red eye, glaucoma, lens and retinal disorders, vascular loss of vision, refractive error and amblyopia, middle and outer ear infection, vertigo, hearing loss, orbital infection, and eye injury. Board items in this area are usually recognition questions built on one or two mechanistic clues, such as pain versus no pain, watery versus purulent discharge, sudden versus gradual loss, or vertigo lasting seconds, hours, or days. Each section explains where the clue comes from in the anatomy and physiology, so that an unfamiliar vignette can be reasoned through rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Anatomy of the eyelid, conjunctiva, cornea, uvea, lens, retina, and orbit","Aqueous humor production and drainage, and the autonomic control of the pupil and lens","Retinal layers, the retinal pigment epithelium, and the dual retinal and choroidal blood supply","Sound conduction through the outer and middle ear, cochlear hair cell transduction, and the vestibular labyrinth","Hypersensitivity types and the major pyogenic bacteria and herpesviruses"],"connections":[{"to":"Microbiology","how":"S. aureus, Pseudomonas, the otitis media trio, N. gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis, HSV, CMV, Toxoplasma, and Acanthamoeba each produce a characteristic eye or ear infection."},{"to":"Biochemistry","how":"Aldose reductase explains diabetic and galactosemic cataract, and fibrillin and cystathionine beta-synthase explain ectopia lentis."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and endocrine pathology","how":"The retina is the only place where arterioles can be viewed directly, so hypertension, diabetes, and carotid atheroembolism leave readable signs there."},{"to":"Rheumatology and immunology","how":"HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies, sarcoidosis, and juvenile idiopathic arthritis present with anterior uveitis, and AIDS permits CMV retinitis."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy of vision and hearing","how":"Papilledema, amblyopia, and the central versus peripheral vertigo distinction depend on optic nerve, visual cortex, and brainstem anatomy."}],"terms":[{"term":"Red eye","def":"Visible dilation of conjunctival or ciliary vessels, a shared sign of conjunctivitis, keratitis, uveitis, and angle-closure glaucoma."},{"term":"Ciliary flush","def":"A ring of injection at the limbus from dilated deep anterior ciliary vessels, signaling corneal or intraocular inflammation rather than conjunctivitis."},{"term":"Hypopyon","def":"A layer of leukocytes settled in the inferior anterior chamber."},{"term":"Optic disc cupping","def":"Enlargement of the central depression of the optic disc as retinal ganglion cell axons are lost."},{"term":"Drusen","def":"Yellow extracellular deposits between the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch membrane."},{"term":"Endolymph","def":"The potassium-rich fluid of the membranous labyrinth, secreted by the stria vascularis."},{"term":"Otoconia","def":"Calcium carbonate crystals on the utricular and saccular maculae that make those organs sensitive to gravity and linear acceleration."},{"term":"Conductive hearing loss","def":"Hearing loss caused by failure of sound to reach the cochlea through the canal, tympanic membrane, or ossicles."},{"term":"Sensorineural hearing loss","def":"Hearing loss caused by damage to cochlear hair cells, the cochlear nerve, or central auditory pathways."},{"term":"Relative afferent pupillary defect","def":"A weaker pupillary response to light shone in one eye than in the other, indicating asymmetric retinal or optic nerve damage."},{"term":"Amblyopia","def":"Reduced vision in a structurally normal eye because the visual cortex suppressed its input during the critical period of development."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C01","obj":"NE-20-J.1","title":"Blepharitis, hordeolum, and chalazion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The eyelid margin carries three gland systems. Glands of Zeis are sebaceous glands opening into lash follicles, glands of Moll are apocrine sweat glands beside the lashes, and meibomian glands are long sebaceous glands embedded in the tarsal plate that secrete the lipid layer of the tear film. That lipid layer slows tear evaporation, so meibomian disease shows up as dryness and burning."},{"type":"p","text":"Blepharitis is chronic inflammation of the lid margin, and it is divided by location. Anterior blepharitis involves the lash base. Staphylococcal disease produces crusts and collarettes around the lashes, and seborrheic disease produces greasy scales, often with seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and face. Posterior blepharitis is meibomian gland dysfunction: the glands secrete thick, turbid oil and their orifices plug, the tear film evaporates quickly, and the patient has gritty, burning, red eyes that are often worse in the morning. It is strongly associated with rosacea. Both forms are chronic, recurrent, and usually bilateral."},{"type":"p","text":"A hordeolum is an acute suppurative infection of a lid gland, usually by S. aureus. An external hordeolum (stye) involves a gland of Zeis or Moll and points at the lash line; an internal hordeolum involves a meibomian gland and points toward the conjunctiva. Acute neutrophilic inflammation makes it red, warm, and tender. A chalazion is different in kind: a blocked meibomian gland releases its lipid into the surrounding tarsus, and the lipid provokes a chronic sterile lipogranuloma with macrophages and giant cells. The result is a firm, painless nodule within the lid that develops over weeks. A chalazion may follow a hordeolum. A chalazion that keeps recurring in the same place in an older adult raises concern for sebaceous carcinoma, which can mimic it."},{"type":"table","title":"Lid margin lesions","cols":["Feature","Hordeolum","Chalazion"],"rows":[["Gland","Zeis or Moll (external), meibomian (internal)","Meibomian"],["Process","Acute bacterial infection, usually S. aureus","Chronic sterile lipogranulomatous inflammation"],["Pain","Painful, tender, warm","Painless, firm"],["Time course","Days","Weeks to months"],["Histology","Neutrophils, abscess","Lipid vacuoles, macrophages, giant cells"]],"caption":"Infection is acute and painful; retained lipid is granulomatous and painless."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C02","obj":"NE-20-J.2","title":"Cataract","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lens is transparent because its fibers have lost their nuclei and organelles and are packed with crystallin proteins arranged in a highly ordered, closely spaced array. The lens has no blood supply and lives on glucose and antioxidants from the aqueous humor, and its fibers are never shed, so the proteins must last for life. A cataract is any opacity of the lens. It forms when crystallins are oxidized, glycated, cross-linked, and aggregated into particles large enough to scatter light, or when fluid enters the fibers and disrupts their ordered packing."},{"type":"p","text":"Age is the dominant cause. Nuclear cataract yellows and hardens the central lens and raises its refractive index, which can produce a myopic shift. Cortical cataract forms spokes in the outer fibers and is linked to ultraviolet exposure. Posterior subcapsular cataract forms just under the back of the capsule and is typical of corticosteroid use, diabetes, and radiation. Smoking adds oxidative stress. Congenital rubella infects the developing lens and causes cataract together with sensorineural deafness and patent ductus arteriosus."},{"type":"p","text":"Diabetes and galactosemia share an osmotic mechanism. Aldose reductase in the lens reduces excess glucose to sorbitol and excess galactose to galactitol. The lens has little sorbitol dehydrogenase to oxidize sorbitol further, and galactitol cannot be metabolized at all, so these polyols accumulate, draw water into the fibers, and cause swelling and opacity. Infants with galactosemia (classic galactose 1-phosphate uridyltransferase deficiency or galactokinase deficiency) can develop cataracts in the first weeks of life."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms follow directly from scattering. Vision blurs slowly and painlessly, contrast falls, and glare and halos appear around lights, especially at night. The opacity blocks light returning from the fundus, so the red reflex dims or disappears. In an infant an absent red reflex or white pupil (leukocoria) must be distinguished from retinoblastoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Polyol pathway cataract","steps":["High glucose or galactose enters lens fibers freely","Aldose reductase forms sorbitol or galactitol","Polyol cannot be cleared efficiently and is trapped","Osmotic water influx swells lens fibers","Fiber disruption and protein aggregation scatter light","Painless blurred vision, glare, loss of red reflex"],"caption":"The same osmotic pathway explains diabetic and galactosemic cataract."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C03","obj":"NE-20-J.3","title":"Conjunctivitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The conjunctiva is a mucous membrane lining the inner lids and covering the sclera up to the limbus. When inflamed, its superficial vessels dilate most in the fornices and fade toward the cornea, vision stays normal, and the pupil reacts normally, which separates conjunctivitis from the more dangerous red eyes in the next sections. The type of discharge and the type of conjunctival reaction point to the cause. Follicles are lymphoid aggregates and suggest viral or chlamydial infection; papillae are swellings with a central vascular core and suggest bacterial or allergic disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Viral conjunctivitis is most often adenovirus. It produces a watery discharge, follicles, a tender preauricular lymph node, and often spreads to the second eye within days. It is highly contagious through hands and instruments; some serotypes cause epidemic keratoconjunctivitis and others pharyngoconjunctival fever. Bacterial conjunctivitis (S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, Moraxella) produces purulent discharge that glues the lids shut in the morning. Neisseria gonorrhoeae causes hyperacute conjunctivitis with copious pus and lid swelling; it can invade intact corneal epithelium and perforate the cornea within days, which makes it an emergency."},{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D to K cause adult inclusion conjunctivitis, a chronic follicular conjunctivitis transmitted sexually or from genital secretions, often with urethritis or cervicitis. Serovars A to C cause trachoma, spread by hands, flies, and shared cloths in areas with poor water access. Repeated infection scars the upper tarsal conjunctiva, the scar contracts and turns the lid inward (entropion), lashes rub the cornea (trichiasis), and corneal scarring follows. Trachoma is the leading infectious cause of blindness worldwide. In neonates, gonococcal conjunctivitis typically appears at 2 to 5 days and chlamydial at about 5 to 14 days."},{"type":"p","text":"Allergic conjunctivitis is a type I hypersensitivity reaction: allergen cross-links IgE on conjunctival mast cells, and histamine causes itching, vasodilation, and chemosis (conjunctival edema). Itching is the most specific symptom, the discharge is watery or stringy, and both eyes are affected, often with allergic rhinitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing conjunctivitis","cols":["Cause","Discharge","Key clues"],"rows":[["Adenovirus","Watery","Follicles, preauricular node, recent URI, spreads to other eye"],["Common bacteria","Purulent","Lids stuck shut, papillae"],["N. gonorrhoeae","Copious purulent, hyperacute","Marked lid swelling, risk of corneal perforation"],["C. trachomatis D to K","Mucopurulent, chronic","Follicles, genital infection"],["C. trachomatis A to C (trachoma)","Variable","Tarsal scarring, entropion, trichiasis, blindness"],["Allergic (type I)","Watery or stringy","Itching, chemosis, bilateral, atopy"]],"caption":"Discharge, follicles versus papillae, and itching identify the cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C04","obj":"NE-20-J.4","title":"Glaucoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aqueous humor is secreted by the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium into the posterior chamber, a process that depends on carbonic anhydrase and Na+/K+ ATPase. It flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber and leaves mainly through the trabecular meshwork in the angle between iris and cornea, then through the canal of Schlemm into episcleral veins; a smaller fraction leaves by the uveoscleral route. Intraocular pressure (IOP) reflects the balance between production and outflow resistance, and the normal range is about 10 to 21 mmHg."},{"type":"p","text":"Glaucoma is an optic neuropathy, not simply high pressure. Retinal ganglion cell axons are lost where they bend through the lamina cribrosa at the optic disc, so the central cup of the disc enlarges (a cup-to-disc ratio above about 0.6, or asymmetry between eyes, is suspicious) and the rim thins. Raised IOP is the main modifiable risk factor, but glaucoma can occur at normal pressure, and some eyes tolerate high pressure without damage. Arcuate nerve fiber bundles are lost first, so visual field loss begins in the periphery and central acuity is spared until late."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary open-angle glaucoma is the common form. The angle looks open, but outflow resistance in the trabecular meshwork rises, IOP climbs slowly, and the patient notices nothing until much of the peripheral field is gone. Risk rises with age, African ancestry, family history, and myopia. Acute angle-closure glaucoma is mechanically different. In a small hyperopic eye with a shallow anterior chamber and an enlarging lens, the iris presses against the lens and blocks flow through the pupil (pupillary block). Pressure builds behind the iris, bows it forward, and the peripheral iris seals the trabecular meshwork. Mid-dilation of the pupil, as in dim light or with an anticholinergic or sympathomimetic drug, maximizes iris-lens contact and slackens the peripheral iris, which is why it triggers attacks."},{"type":"p","text":"In an attack, IOP rises abruptly, often above 40 mmHg. Pressure forces water into the corneal epithelium, and the edematous cornea looks hazy and diffracts light into halos around lights. Severe pain and headache arise from the trigeminal nerve, and nausea and vomiting are reflex vagal effects. Ischemia of the iris sphincter leaves the pupil fixed and mid-dilated, the eye is red with ciliary flush, and the globe feels hard."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acute angle closure","steps":["Shallow anterior chamber in a hyperopic, older eye","Pupil mid-dilates in dim light or with drugs","Iris-lens contact blocks aqueous flow through pupil","Iris bows forward and seals trabecular meshwork","Outflow stops and IOP rises sharply","Corneal edema, halos, pain, vomiting, fixed mid-dilated pupil"],"caption":"Pupillary block converts normal aqueous production into a pressure emergency."},{"type":"table","title":"Open-angle versus acute angle-closure glaucoma","cols":["Feature","Open-angle","Acute angle-closure"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Increased trabecular outflow resistance","Iris occludes the angle"],["Onset","Insidious over years","Sudden, hours"],["Pain and redness","None","Severe pain, red eye, nausea"],["Pupil","Normal","Fixed, mid-dilated"],["Vision","Peripheral field loss first","Blurring with halos, rapid loss if untreated"]],"caption":"Both end in optic nerve damage, by very different routes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can a patient with open-angle glaucoma have 20/20 vision while losing a large part of the visual field?","explain":"Ganglion cell axons from the arcuate bundles above and below the fovea are lost first, while the papillomacular bundle that carries central vision is preserved until late. Acuity tests the fovea only.","check":"The paragraph on optic neuropathy and field loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C05","obj":"NE-20-J.5","title":"Keratitis and anterior uveitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cornea is avascular and has the densest sensory innervation in the body (trigeminal V1), so corneal disease causes pain, tearing, photophobia, and a foreign body sensation, and it threatens vision because any scar lies in the visual axis. Keratitis is corneal inflammation, and an ulcer is an epithelial defect with an underlying stromal infiltrate. Risks for ulcer include contact lens wear (hypoxia, microtrauma, and organisms carried on the lens or in its case), trauma, dry eye, poor lid closure, and topical steroid use."},{"type":"p","text":"Herpes simplex virus (usually HSV-1) reactivates from the trigeminal ganglion and infects corneal epithelium. Infected cells die and slough in a branching line, the dendritic ulcer, which stains with fluorescein and ends in small terminal bulbs. Viral damage to corneal nerves reduces corneal sensation. Recurrences can involve the stroma, where immune inflammation causes scarring, so HSV is a leading infectious cause of corneal blindness in developed countries. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the classic contact lens organism: it adheres to damaged epithelium, secretes proteases, and can produce a rapidly enlarging ulcer with stromal melting and perforation. Acanthamoeba is a free-living amoeba in tap water, pools, and hot tubs; infection follows rinsing lenses in tap water or swimming in them. It causes pain out of proportion to the findings, a ring infiltrate, and inflammation along corneal nerves, and its cysts are very resistant."},{"type":"p","text":"Anterior uveitis (iritis or iridocyclitis) is inflammation of the iris and ciliary body. The injection is a ciliary flush concentrated at the limbus. Inflammation breaks the blood-aqueous barrier, so protein and cells leak into the anterior chamber and are seen on slit lamp as flare and cells, and leukocytes may deposit on the corneal endothelium (keratic precipitates) or layer as a hypopyon. The inflamed iris sphincter goes into spasm, making the pupil small, and sticky exudate glues the iris to the lens (posterior synechiae), making it irregular when it later dilates. Light causes pain because constriction of the inflamed iris and ciliary muscle hurts; this pain occurs even when light is shone in the opposite eye, through the consensual reflex."},{"type":"table","title":"The red eye","cols":["Feature","Conjunctivitis","Keratitis","Anterior uveitis","Acute angle closure"],"rows":[["Pain","Mild irritation","Marked, foreign body sense","Moderate, aching","Severe, with nausea"],["Vision","Normal","Reduced if central","Mildly reduced","Markedly reduced, halos"],["Injection","Diffuse, fornix","Ciliary","Ciliary flush","Diffuse and ciliary"],["Pupil","Normal","Normal or small","Small, irregular","Fixed, mid-dilated"],["Cornea","Clear","Staining defect or opacity","Keratic precipitates","Hazy, edematous"],["IOP","Normal","Normal","Low, normal, or high","Very high"]],"caption":"Pain, reduced vision, an abnormal pupil, or a corneal change mark a red eye as more than conjunctivitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C06","obj":"NE-20-J.6","title":"Age-related macular degeneration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The macula, and the fovea at its center, are packed with cones and provide central, high-acuity, color vision. Photoreceptors depend on the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), which recycles visual pigment, phagocytoses shed outer segment discs, and transports nutrients from the choriocapillaris across Bruch membrane. Each RPE cell digests an enormous load of lipid-rich membrane over a lifetime, and incompletely digested material accumulates."},{"type":"p","text":"Age-related macular degeneration (AMD) is the leading cause of irreversible central vision loss in older adults in developed countries. Risk factors are age (usually over 50), smoking, family history, and variants in complement genes such as complement factor H, which point to chronic complement-mediated injury at Bruch membrane. Oxidative stress, including that from smoking, adds to it."},{"type":"p","text":"Dry (nonexudative) AMD is about 85 to 90% of cases. Drusen, yellow deposits of lipid, protein, and complement components, collect between the RPE basal lamina and Bruch membrane and impair exchange between the choroid and the outer retina. The RPE and overlying photoreceptors gradually atrophy, ending in sharply bordered patches of geographic atrophy. Central vision fades slowly over years, with difficulty reading and recognizing faces."},{"type":"p","text":"Wet (neovascular or exudative) AMD is less common but causes most severe vision loss. Stressed and hypoxic RPE releases vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), and new vessels grow from the choroid through breaks in Bruch membrane beneath the RPE or retina (choroidal neovascularization). These vessels are fragile and leaky, so fluid and blood collect under the macula within days to weeks. Photoreceptors displaced by fluid make straight lines look bent (metamorphopsia), a central dark spot appears, and untreated lesions organize into a fibrovascular scar. In both forms the peripheral retina is spared, so patients keep navigational vision. Dry AMD can convert to wet AMD, which is why sudden distortion in a patient with drusen is significant."},{"type":"table","title":"Dry versus wet AMD","cols":["Feature","Dry","Wet"],"rows":[["Share of cases","About 85 to 90%","About 10 to 15%"],["Lesion","Drusen, RPE atrophy","Choroidal neovascularization, fluid, hemorrhage"],["Key mediator","Chronic RPE and Bruch membrane injury","VEGF"],["Tempo","Gradual, years","Rapid, days to weeks"],["Symptom","Slow central blur","Distortion, sudden central loss"]],"caption":"VEGF-driven leakage makes wet AMD fast."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C07","obj":"NE-20-J.7","title":"Uveitis in systemic disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The uvea (iris, ciliary body, and choroid) is highly vascular, so it is a frequent site of immune-mediated and hematogenous disease. Uveitis is classified anatomically: anterior uveitis involves the iris and ciliary body and presents as a painful red eye with photophobia; posterior uveitis involves the choroid and retina and presents as painless floaters and blurred vision, because the iris and ciliary muscle, whose spasm causes the pain and photophobia, are spared, and inflammatory cells in the vitreous cast shadows on the retina. Knowing the location narrows the systemic cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute anterior uveitis is the most common extra-articular feature of the HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies: ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and the arthritis of inflammatory bowel disease. Attacks are sudden, usually unilateral, and recurrent, and may alternate between eyes, often with a fibrinous exudate or hypopyon. Sarcoidosis causes granulomatous uveitis, often bilateral and chronic, with large greasy keratic precipitates made of epithelioid macrophages; it can involve the anterior or posterior segment and also the lacrimal glands. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis, particularly the oligoarticular form in young ANA-positive girls, causes chronic anterior uveitis that is quiet: the eye is white and painless, so cataract, glaucoma, and band keratopathy can develop before anyone notices."},{"type":"p","text":"Toxoplasma gondii is the most common cause of infectious posterior uveitis in immunocompetent people. Tissue cysts in the retina, often from congenital infection, reactivate at the edge of an old pigmented scar and produce a focus of necrotizing retinochoroiditis with overlying vitritis, which looks like a headlight in fog. Cytomegalovirus retinitis occurs in advanced AIDS, typically with a CD4 count below 50 cells per microliter. Without cell-mediated immunity, the virus spreads cell to cell through the retina, producing yellow-white necrosis with hemorrhage along vessels and little vitreous inflammation because there are too few T cells to mount it. It is painless, progresses relentlessly, and can cause retinal detachment."},{"type":"table","title":"Systemic causes of uveitis","cols":["Disease","Location","Character"],"rows":[["HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy","Anterior","Acute, unilateral, recurrent, painful, hypopyon"],["Sarcoidosis","Anterior or posterior","Granulomatous, bilateral, mutton-fat keratic precipitates"],["Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Anterior","Chronic, painless, white eye, ANA-positive"],["Toxoplasmosis","Posterior","Retinochoroiditis beside old scar, dense vitritis"],["CMV in AIDS","Posterior","Hemorrhagic necrotizing retinitis, CD4 below 50, little vitritis"]],"caption":"Location and tempo of the uveitis point to the systemic disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C08","obj":"NE-20-J.8","title":"Meniere disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The membranous labyrinth is filled with endolymph, an unusual extracellular fluid with a high potassium concentration (about 150 mmol/L) and low sodium. Endolymph is secreted in the cochlea by the stria vascularis and resorbed largely by the endolymphatic sac. When transduction channels open, potassium flows from endolymph into hair cells, driven mainly by the electrical gradient (in the cochlea, the endocochlear potential of about +80 mV), so the volume and composition of endolymph must be held tightly."},{"type":"p","text":"Meniere disease is idiopathic endolymphatic hydrops: excess endolymph distends the cochlear duct and vestibular compartments, most likely because resorption by the endolymphatic sac is impaired. Reissner membrane bulges into the scala vestibuli. Episodes are thought to occur when distension or small ruptures let potassium-rich endolymph mix with perilymph, which first depolarizes and then temporarily silences hair cells and nerve endings. While the vestibular input from one ear is abnormal, the brain perceives rotation, so the patient has violent spinning vertigo with nausea, vomiting, and nystagmus."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical pattern follows from this mechanism. Attacks of vertigo last from about 20 minutes to 12 hours, longer than BPPV and shorter than vestibular neuritis. Hearing loss is sensorineural and fluctuating, and it affects low frequencies first, probably because the apex of the cochlea, which encodes low frequencies, has the widest and most compliant basilar membrane and is the first region distorted by excess volume. Tinnitus is typically low-pitched and roaring, and a sense of fullness or pressure in the ear reflects distension. Early in the disease hearing recovers between attacks; with repeated episodes hair cells are lost, and hearing loss becomes permanent and extends across frequencies. Most cases are unilateral at onset."},{"type":"table","title":"The Meniere tetrad","cols":["Symptom","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Episodic vertigo, about 20 minutes to 12 hours","Asymmetric vestibular hair cell firing during the attack"],["Fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss","Hydrops thought to distort the compliant cochlear apex first"],["Tinnitus, low-pitched","Abnormal cochlear output"],["Aural fullness","Distension of the membranous labyrinth"]],"caption":"Each element of the tetrad traces to hydrops."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C09","obj":"NE-20-J.9","title":"Otitis media and its complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The middle ear is an air-filled space whose only connection to the outside is the eustachian tube, which opens during swallowing through contraction of the tensor veli palatini. The tube ventilates the middle ear, drains its secretions into the nasopharynx, and protects it from nasopharyngeal contents. In young children the tube is shorter, more horizontal, and floppier, and adenoid tissue sits near its opening, so it obstructs easily. Children with cleft palate or Down syndrome have particularly poor tube function."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute otitis media (AOM) usually follows a viral upper respiratory infection. Mucosal swelling closes the tube, the trapped middle ear gas is absorbed into the blood, and pressure in the middle ear falls. Negative pressure draws in transudate and nasopharyngeal secretions, and bacteria colonizing the nasopharynx multiply in the static fluid. The three main pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae (unencapsulated, so not covered by the Hib vaccine), and Moraxella catarrhalis. Pus under pressure produces ear pain, fever, irritability, and a red, bulging, immobile tympanic membrane."},{"type":"p","text":"Otitis media with effusion (OME) is fluid in the middle ear without signs of acute infection. It often follows AOM or persistent tube dysfunction. The membrane is dull, retracted or neutral, and moves poorly, sometimes with an air-fluid level. Because fluid damps the vibration of the membrane and ossicles, OME causes conductive hearing loss, and persistent loss in a young child can delay speech and language."},{"type":"p","text":"Complications follow the anatomy. Pressure can rupture the membrane (perforation), which relieves pain and releases discharge. The middle ear connects with the mastoid air cells through the aditus and antrum, so infection can spread there; mastoiditis causes postauricular pain and swelling that pushes the auricle forward and outward. Chronic negative pressure can draw a pocket of the membrane inward, and keratinizing squamous epithelium trapped in the pocket grows into a cholesteatoma, a collection of keratin that erodes bone, including the ossicles. Cholesteatoma causes painless, foul-smelling discharge and conductive loss, and it can reach the facial nerve, labyrinth, or cranial cavity. Spread to meninges, brain, or the sigmoid sinus is rare but serious."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of acute otitis media","steps":["Viral URI swells nasopharynx and eustachian tube","Tube closes and middle ear gas is absorbed","Negative pressure draws in fluid and secretions","Nasopharyngeal bacteria multiply in trapped fluid","Pus under pressure: pain, fever, bulging membrane"],"caption":"Tube dysfunction comes first; infection exploits the trapped fluid."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a child with acute otitis media often have sudden relief of pain followed by ear discharge?","explain":"Pus under pressure stretches the pain-sensitive tympanic membrane. When the membrane perforates, pressure falls at once and the pus drains into the canal.","check":"The complications paragraph on perforation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C10","obj":"NE-20-J.10","title":"Otitis externa and perichondritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The external canal is lined by skin. Its outer third rests on cartilage and contains hair follicles and ceruminous glands; its inner two thirds lies on bone. Cerumen is acidic and water-repellent and contains antimicrobial lipids and peptides, so it protects the canal skin. Moisture from swimming, humidity, or sweating removes cerumen and macerates the skin, and scratching or cotton swabs break the barrier. That combination permits otitis externa (swimmer's ear), usually caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa or Staphylococcus aureus, and less often by fungi such as Aspergillus."},{"type":"p","text":"The canal skin is tightly bound to the underlying perichondrium and periosteum, so even small amounts of swelling stretch sensitive tissue. Pain on pressing the tragus or pulling the auricle is therefore the characteristic sign, and it helps separate otitis externa from otitis media. The canal is swollen and red with discharge, and conductive hearing loss occurs when the canal closes."},{"type":"p","text":"Malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa is a Pseudomonas osteomyelitis of the temporal bone and skull base. It occurs mainly in older adults with diabetes, whose microangiopathy and impaired neutrophil function allow infection to spread through fissures in the cartilaginous canal and into bone. Severe, persistent, often nocturnal pain and granulation tissue on the floor of the canal at the bony-cartilaginous junction are typical. As infection spreads medially it involves cranial nerves at their foramina: the facial nerve at the stylomastoid foramen is affected first and most often, and further spread reaches the jugular foramen (IX, X, XI) and hypoglossal canal (XII). The inflammatory markers are elevated, and the disease can be fatal."},{"type":"p","text":"Auricular perichondritis is infection of the perichondrium of the ear cartilage. Cartilage has no blood vessels and depends on diffusion from the perichondrium, so when pus separates the two, cartilage necroses and can collapse into a deformed ear. Piercing through the upper ear cartilage is a classic route, and Pseudomonas is the usual organism. The ear is red, hot, and tender, but the lobule is spared because it contains no cartilage."},{"type":"table","title":"Infections of the external ear","cols":["Condition","Host and organism","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Otitis externa","Swimmers, canal trauma; Pseudomonas, S. aureus","Tragal tenderness, canal edema and discharge"],["Malignant otitis externa","Older diabetics, immunosuppressed; Pseudomonas","Severe pain, canal granulation tissue, skull base osteomyelitis, CN VII then IX to XII palsies"],["Perichondritis","Upper ear cartilage piercing, trauma; Pseudomonas","Red, tender auricle with lobule spared, cartilage necrosis risk"]],"caption":"Host factors and anatomy separate a superficial infection from bone and cartilage infection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C11","obj":"NE-20-J.11","title":"Peripheral vertigo","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"At rest, the vestibular nerves on both sides fire at equal tonic rates, and the brain reads the difference between the sides as head rotation. Any sudden one-sided loss of input mimics a turn toward the healthy side, causing vertigo and a horizontal-torsional nystagmus whose slow phase drifts toward the damaged ear and whose fast phase beats away from it. The duration of vertigo and whether hearing is affected identify the peripheral cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Vestibular neuritis is inflammation of the vestibular nerve, presumed viral (reactivated HSV-1 is suspected), usually involving the superior division. The patient has sudden, continuous, severe vertigo with nausea and vomiting lasting days, then gradual improvement over weeks as the brain recalibrates. Hearing is normal because the cochlear nerve is spared. The head impulse test shows a corrective saccade when the head is turned quickly toward the affected side, because the vestibulo-ocular reflex on that side fails, and the patient tends to fall toward the affected side. Labyrinthitis is inflammation of the labyrinth itself, including the cochlea, so the same vertigo is accompanied by sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus. An acute continuous vertigo can also be a cerebellar or brainstem stroke; a normal head impulse test, nystagmus that changes direction with gaze, a vertical skew deviation, or inability to stand points to a central cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) is the most common cause of vertigo. Otoconia detach from the utricular macula, often with aging or head trauma, and drift into a semicircular canal, most often the posterior canal because it is the most dependent when the person is upright or lying. When the head moves in the plane of that canal, the loose particles sink, drag endolymph, and deflect the cupula, so a canal that should sense only rotation now responds to gravity. Vertigo starts a few seconds after the position change, lasts less than a minute, and fades with repetition. The Dix-Hallpike maneuver reproduces it with an upbeating, torsional nystagmus. Hearing is normal."},{"type":"flow","title":"Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","steps":["Otoconia detach from the utricular macula","Particles settle in the posterior semicircular canal","Head moves in the plane of that canal","Sinking particles drag endolymph and deflect the cupula","Brief vertigo with upbeating torsional nystagmus"],"caption":"Free otoconia make a rotation sensor respond to gravity."},{"type":"table","title":"Timing separates peripheral vertigo","cols":["Condition","Duration","Hearing","Trigger"],"rows":[["BPPV","Seconds, under 1 minute","Normal","Head position change"],["Meniere disease","About 20 minutes to 12 hours","Fluctuating low-frequency loss","Spontaneous"],["Vestibular neuritis","Continuous for days","Normal","Spontaneous, often after viral illness"],["Labyrinthitis","Continuous for days","Sensorineural loss, tinnitus","Spontaneous, often after viral illness"]],"caption":"Duration and hearing status sort the four main peripheral causes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C12","obj":"NE-20-J.12","title":"Retinal vascular occlusion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The retina has two blood supplies. The central retinal artery, a branch of the ophthalmic artery (the first major branch of the internal carotid), enters through the optic nerve and supplies the inner retina, including the ganglion cells. The choroid supplies the outer retina, including the photoreceptors. The central retinal artery is an end artery, so its occlusion leaves the inner retina with no collateral flow."},{"type":"p","text":"Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) causes sudden, painless, severe monocular vision loss. Most cases are embolic, usually cholesterol or platelet-fibrin emboli from carotid atherosclerosis or emboli from the heart; giant cell arteritis is an important nonembolic cause in older adults. The ischemic inner retina becomes edematous and opaque, so the fundus looks pale white. The fovea has no inner retinal layers, so it stays thin and transparent, and the normal red of the underlying choroid shows through as a cherry-red spot. Arterioles are narrowed, the blood column may break into segments, and there is a relative afferent pupillary defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Amaurosis fugax is transient monocular vision loss, often described as a curtain coming down, lasting minutes and then clearing. It is usually caused by small emboli from an ipsilateral internal carotid plaque passing through retinal arterioles, and a bright refractile cholesterol embolus (Hollenhorst plaque) may be seen at an arteriolar branch. It is a retinal transient ischemic attack and signals a raised risk of stroke."},{"type":"p","text":"Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO) is thrombosis of the central retinal vein, usually at the lamina cribrosa, where the artery and vein share an adventitial sheath and a stiff atherosclerotic artery can compress the vein. Risks include age, hypertension, diabetes, glaucoma, and hypercoagulable states. Blood still enters but cannot leave, so veins become dilated and tortuous, and hemorrhages spread through all four quadrants with disc swelling and cotton wool spots. Vision loss is painless but usually less abrupt and less complete than in CRAO. Ischemic CRVO drives VEGF release, which can cause iris neovascularization and neovascular glaucoma weeks to months later."},{"type":"table","title":"Painless monocular vision loss from vascular disease","cols":["Feature","CRAO","Amaurosis fugax","CRVO"],"rows":[["Lesion","Arterial occlusion, usually embolic","Transient arteriolar embolus","Venous thrombosis at the lamina cribrosa"],["Onset and course","Sudden, persistent","Sudden, resolves in minutes","Sudden or subacute, persistent"],["Fundus","Pale retina, cherry-red spot, narrow arterioles","Often normal, possible Hollenhorst plaque","Diffuse hemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins, disc swelling"],["Significance","Stroke equivalent; consider giant cell arteritis","Carotid TIA, stroke risk","Later neovascular glaucoma"]],"caption":"Arterial occlusion empties the retina; venous occlusion floods it."},{"type":"example","title":"Pale retina or bloody retina","text":"A 72-year-old with hypertension wakes with painless loss of vision in the right eye. If the fundus is pale with a cherry-red spot, arterial inflow has stopped: the inner retina is ischemic, the fovea lets the choroid show through, and the likely sources are a carotid or cardiac embolus or giant cell arteritis, so jaw claudication or scalp tenderness matter. If instead the fundus shows hemorrhages in every quadrant with engorged veins, outflow is blocked and the arteries are still delivering blood into a congested retina. One lesion is upstream of the capillaries and the other downstream, so the same symptom gives opposite fundus pictures."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C13","obj":"NE-20-J.13","title":"Hypertensive and diabetic retinopathy and papilledema","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Flame hemorrhages lie in the superficial nerve fiber layer, where blood spreads along the axons; dot and blot hemorrhages lie deeper, where cells are packed vertically. Cotton wool spots are microinfarcts of the nerve fiber layer in which axoplasmic transport stops and organelles accumulate in swollen axons. Hard exudates are yellow deposits of lipid and protein that leaked from abnormal capillaries."},{"type":"p","text":"In hypertensive retinopathy, retinal arterioles first constrict (autoregulatory narrowing) and then thicken by hyaline arteriolosclerosis, so the light reflex widens (copper or silver wiring). Where a thickened arteriole crosses a vein within their shared sheath, it compresses the vein (arteriovenous nicking). Severe pressure breaks down the blood-retina barrier, producing flame hemorrhages, cotton wool spots, and hard exudates, sometimes in a star around the macula. Swelling of the optic disc marks malignant hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"In diabetic retinopathy, chronic hyperglycemia damages capillaries through advanced glycation end products, the polyol pathway, and oxidative stress. Pericytes are lost and basement membranes thicken. The weakened capillary wall balloons into microaneurysms, the earliest visible sign, and leaks to form dot and blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, and macular edema (nonproliferative retinopathy). Capillary closure makes the retina ischemic, and ischemic retina releases VEGF, which drives new vessels on the disc or retinal surface (proliferative retinopathy). These vessels bleed into the vitreous and pull on the retina as they scar, causing tractional detachment."},{"type":"p","text":"Papilledema is optic disc swelling caused by raised intracranial pressure. The subarachnoid space extends along the optic nerve sheath, so CSF pressure is transmitted to the nerve behind the eye, compresses it, and blocks axoplasmic flow at the lamina cribrosa. Axons swell, the disc margins blur and elevate, veins engorge, spontaneous venous pulsations are lost (though some normal people lack them), and the blind spot enlarges. It is usually bilateral, and acuity is preserved early, which separates it from optic neuritis, where vision falls early."},{"type":"table","title":"Fundus findings and their meaning","cols":["Condition","Characteristic findings","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hypertensive retinopathy","Arteriolar narrowing, AV nicking, flame hemorrhages, cotton wool spots, exudates","Arteriolar constriction and sclerosis, then barrier breakdown"],["Nonproliferative diabetic retinopathy","Microaneurysms, dot and blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, macular edema","Pericyte loss and capillary leakage"],["Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Neovascularization, vitreous hemorrhage, traction","Ischemia-driven VEGF"],["Papilledema","Bilateral disc swelling, blurred margins, venous engorgement, enlarged blind spot","Raised ICP blocks axoplasmic flow"]],"caption":"Pressure, leakage, ischemia, and stalled axonal transport each leave a distinct sign."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why do cotton wool spots appear in hypertension, diabetes, and CRVO alike?","explain":"Each condition can cut off capillary flow to the nerve fiber layer (arteriolar damage in hypertension and diabetes, stagnant venous flow in CRVO), and the ischemic axons stop axoplasmic transport and swell with accumulated material.","check":"The first paragraph on fundus lesions, and the CRVO paragraph in the retinal vascular occlusion section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C14","obj":"NE-20-J.14","title":"Retinal detachment and retinitis pigmentosa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The neurosensory retina is held against the RPE only loosely, by the pump function of the RPE and the matrix between photoreceptor outer segments. The two layers are the remnants of the two walls of the embryonic optic cup, and the potential space between them reopens in retinal detachment. Detached photoreceptors are separated from the choroid, which nourishes them, and they begin to degenerate."},{"type":"p","text":"Rhegmatogenous detachment, the common form, starts with a retinal tear. With age the vitreous liquefies and separates from the retina (posterior vitreous detachment), and where it remains firmly attached, it pulls a tear in the retina. Liquid vitreous then passes through the tear and lifts the retina off the RPE. Traction on the retina stimulates photoreceptors mechanically, which the patient sees as flashes (photopsia). Pigment and blood released into the vitreous cast shadows as a shower of floaters. As fluid spreads, a shadow or curtain advances over the visual field opposite the detached area, and central vision fails when the macula detaches. Myopia is a major risk because the elongated eye has a thin, stretched peripheral retina, often with lattice degeneration. Other risks are cataract surgery, trauma, and Marfan syndrome. Tractional detachment follows fibrovascular scarring in proliferative diabetic retinopathy, and exudative detachment follows fluid leakage from inflammation or tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"Retinitis pigmentosa is a group of inherited degenerations of photoreceptors and RPE with autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, and X-linked forms; rhodopsin mutations are a common dominant cause. Rods are lost first, so the first symptom is night blindness, followed by loss of the midperipheral field that narrows to tunnel vision. Cones fail later, and central vision is lost last. As photoreceptors die, RPE cells migrate into the retina around vessels and form bone spicule pigment, the arterioles narrow, and the optic disc becomes waxy and pale. It can occur with sensorineural deafness in Usher syndrome and with vitamin A and E deficiency in abetalipoproteinemia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","steps":["Vitreous liquefies and separates with age or myopia","Vitreous traction tears the peripheral retina","Traction stimulates photoreceptors: flashes","Blood and pigment enter vitreous: floaters","Liquid vitreous lifts retina off RPE: curtain","Macula detaches and central vision fails"],"caption":"Each symptom marks a stage, from traction to macular involvement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C15","obj":"NE-20-J.15","title":"Refractive error, strabismus, and amblyopia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The eye focuses light with about 60 diopters of total power. The cornea supplies about two thirds of it (roughly 43 diopters) and the lens about one third, and only the lens can change its power. To focus on a near object, the ciliary muscle contracts under parasympathetic control (oculomotor nerve, muscarinic receptors). Contraction moves the ciliary ring inward, slackens the zonular fibers, and lets the elastic capsule round up the lens, increasing its power. This is accommodation. Emmetropia means parallel light from a distant object focuses on the retina with accommodation relaxed."},{"type":"p","text":"In myopia the eye is too long for its optical power (or, less often, the cornea or lens is too strong), so distant images focus in front of the retina and far vision blurs while near vision stays clear. Diverging (minus) lenses neutralize it. In hyperopia the eye is too short, so images focus behind the retina. A young hyperopic eye can pull the image forward by accommodating, at the cost of eye strain, and converging (plus) lenses neutralize it. In astigmatism the cornea (or lens) is curved more steeply in one meridian than the other, like the back of a spoon, so no single point focus forms and vision blurs at all distances; cylindrical lenses neutralize it. Presbyopia is the age-related loss of accommodation: the lens keeps adding fibers, becomes larger and stiffer, and no longer rounds up when the zonules slacken. The near point recedes, and reading becomes difficult, usually from the early to mid forties."},{"type":"p","text":"Strabismus is misalignment of the eyes, inward (esotropia) or outward (exotropia). In adults new strabismus causes double vision. In a young child, whose visual cortex is still being wired during a critical period, the cortex instead suppresses the image from the deviating eye. Its inputs then lose competitive ground in the ocular dominance columns of primary visual cortex, and the eye becomes amblyopic: vision is reduced although the eye itself is normal. Unequal refractive error between the eyes (anisometropia) and a congenital cataract or ptosis that blocks the image cause amblyopia the same way. Hyperopia links the two topics: a hyperopic child who accommodates hard also converges, through the near reflex, and can develop accommodative esotropia. Because cortical wiring becomes fixed after early childhood, amblyopia that is not corrected early can become permanent."},{"type":"table","title":"Refractive errors","cols":["Error","Defect","Image falls","Blurred","Neutralizing lens"],"rows":[["Myopia","Eye too long or too powerful","In front of retina","Distance","Diverging (minus)"],["Hyperopia","Eye too short or too weak","Behind retina","Near more than distance","Converging (plus)"],["Astigmatism","Unequal meridian curvature","No single focus","All distances","Cylindrical"],["Presbyopia","Stiff lens, lost accommodation","Behind retina for near objects","Near","Converging (plus) for near"]],"caption":"Myopia and hyperopia are mismatches between length and power; presbyopia is loss of accommodation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C16","obj":"NE-20-J.16","title":"Conductive and sensorineural hearing loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sound in air must enter the fluid-filled cochlea, and most of its energy would reflect at an air-fluid interface. The middle ear matches impedance: the tympanic membrane has about 17 to 20 times the area of the stapes footplate, and the ossicular lever adds a little more, so pressure at the oval window increases about 20-fold (roughly 25 to 30 dB). The basilar membrane is tuned by place, high frequencies at the base and low at the apex."},{"type":"p","text":"Conductive hearing loss occurs anywhere from the canal to the footplate. Cerumen impaction and otitis externa block the canal. A perforated tympanic membrane loses area and the pressure gain. Middle ear effusion and eustachian tube dysfunction damp the membrane and ossicles. In barotrauma, during a plane descent or a dive, outside pressure rises faster than a blocked tube can equalize, so the membrane is pushed inward and blood or fluid may enter the middle ear. Otosclerosis is abnormal remodeling of the otic capsule in which new spongy bone forms in front of the oval window and fixes the stapes footplate; it is often familial, more common in women, begins in young adults, and may worsen in pregnancy. The tympanic membrane looks normal. Cholesteatoma erodes the ossicles."},{"type":"p","text":"Sensorineural loss arises in the cochlea or the nerve. Presbycusis is gradual, bilateral, symmetric loss beginning at high frequencies, because hair cells at the basal turn are lost first; speech becomes hard to follow in noisy rooms. Noise damages outer hair cells mechanically and metabolically and typically produces a notch near 4 kHz on the audiogram. Tinnitus accompanies both types of loss. When cochlear input falls, neurons in the central auditory pathway raise their gain and fire spontaneously, which is heard as sound."},{"type":"table","title":"Tuning fork tests","cols":["Finding","Conductive loss (left ear)","Sensorineural loss (left ear)"],"rows":[["Weber (fork on midline)","Louder in left (affected) ear","Louder in right (better) ear"],["Rinne, left ear","Bone conduction louder than air","Air louder than bone, both reduced"],["Rinne, right ear","Air louder than bone","Air louder than bone"]],"caption":"Weber lateralizes toward a conductive loss and away from a sensorineural loss."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading Weber and Rinne","text":"A 30-year-old woman has gradual hearing loss in the left ear that worsened during pregnancy; the eardrums look normal. Weber is louder in the left ear. That alone could mean left conductive loss or right sensorineural loss. Rinne on the left is negative: she hears the fork better on the mastoid than in front of the canal, so air conduction is impaired and the cochlea is working. Rinne on the right is normal. The loss is conductive on the left. A conductive ear lateralizes Weber because room noise no longer masks bone-conducted sound and sound energy escapes less through the blocked path. A normal membrane, young female patient, and worsening in pregnancy point to otosclerosis fixing the stapes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C17","obj":"NE-20-J.17","title":"Orbital infection, dacryocystitis, and endophthalmitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The orbital septum is a fibrous sheet running from the bony orbital rim to the tarsal plates. It divides the eyelid tissues in front from the orbital fat, muscles, and optic nerve behind, and it is a barrier to spread of infection. The medial orbital wall is the lamina papyracea, a paper-thin plate of the ethmoid bone, and the orbital veins communicate without valves with the facial veins, the sinuses, and the cavernous sinus."},{"type":"p","text":"Preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis is infection in front of the septum, usually after skin trauma, an insect bite, or a hordeolum, sometimes from sinusitis. The lids are red and swollen, but because the orbit is spared, vision, pupils, and eye movements are normal and there is no proptosis. Orbital cellulitis lies behind the septum and most often spreads from ethmoid sinusitis through the lamina papyracea, typically in children. Inflamed orbital contents cause pain with eye movement, proptosis, restricted movement (ophthalmoplegia), chemosis, and fever, and pressure on the optic nerve reduces vision and produces an afferent pupillary defect. Organisms include S. aureus, streptococci, and anaerobes; in diabetic ketoacidosis, mucormycosis must be considered. Complications are subperiosteal or orbital abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis through the valveless veins, and meningitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Dacryocystitis is infection of the lacrimal sac. Tears drain through the puncta and canaliculi into the sac and down the nasolacrimal duct to the inferior meatus. Obstruction of the duct, congenital in infants (an imperforate membrane at its lower end) or acquired in older adults, causes stasis and infection, usually by S. aureus or streptococci. A tender red swelling appears at the inner corner of the eye below the medial canthal tendon, and pressure may express pus from the punctum."},{"type":"p","text":"Endophthalmitis is infection of the vitreous and aqueous inside the globe. Exogenous cases follow intraocular surgery such as cataract extraction (often coagulase-negative staphylococci from the patient's own lid flora) or penetrating trauma (Bacillus cereus is notoriously destructive). Endogenous cases come from bacteremia or fungemia, as with Candida in people with central lines or who inject drugs. Pain, rapidly falling vision, a hypopyon, and hazy vitreous develop, and the eye can be lost within days."},{"type":"table","title":"Preseptal versus orbital cellulitis","cols":["Feature","Preseptal","Orbital"],"rows":[["Location","Anterior to orbital septum","Posterior to septum"],["Usual source","Skin break, bite, hordeolum","Ethmoid sinusitis via lamina papyracea"],["Eye movement","Full and painless","Painful and restricted"],["Proptosis","Absent","Present"],["Vision and pupil","Normal","May be reduced, afferent pupillary defect"],["Serious complications","Rare","Abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis"]],"caption":"Signs that come from orbital contents (motility, proptosis, vision) mean infection is behind the septum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C18","obj":"NE-20-J.18","title":"Pterygium and ectopia lentis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pterygium is a triangular, wing-shaped growth of fibrovascular conjunctival tissue that extends from the bulbar conjunctiva across the limbus onto the cornea, usually on the nasal side. It is linked to chronic ultraviolet exposure, as in people who work outdoors in sunny, dry, dusty climates. Ultraviolet light damages limbal cells and conjunctival collagen, producing elastotic degeneration of the stroma and growth of fibrovascular tissue. A pinguecula is a similar yellow, sun-related degeneration of the conjunctiva that does not cross onto the cornea. A pterygium causes irritation and redness; as it advances it flattens the cornea in one meridian, causing astigmatism, and if it reaches the pupil it blocks the visual axis."},{"type":"p","text":"The lens is suspended from the ciliary body by zonular fibers, whose main structural protein is fibrillin-1. Ectopia lentis, displacement of the lens, occurs when these fibers are weak or broken, and patients notice blurred or double vision in one eye, large changes in refraction, or a trembling iris (iridodonesis) because the iris loses its support behind it."},{"type":"p","text":"In Marfan syndrome, an autosomal dominant mutation in FBN1 produces defective fibrillin-1, so zonules stretch. The lens usually shifts upward and outward (superotemporally), typically in both eyes, and Marfan eyes also tend to be long and myopic, which adds to the risk of retinal detachment. In homocystinuria, usually autosomal recessive cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, homocysteine accumulates. Zonular fibrillin is rich in cysteine, and excess homocysteine is thought to disrupt its disulfide bonds, so the zonules degrade and break, and the lens tends to fall downward and inward (inferonasally). Homocystinuria also causes a marfanoid habitus, intellectual disability, and arterial and venous thrombosis, which help separate the two diseases."},{"type":"table","title":"Ectopia lentis","cols":["Feature","Marfan syndrome","Homocystinuria"],"rows":[["Defect","FBN1 mutation, defective fibrillin-1","Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency"],["Inheritance","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive"],["Usual lens displacement","Upward, superotemporal","Downward, inferonasal"],["Other features","Aortic root dilation, tall habitus, joint laxity","Thrombosis, intellectual disability, marfanoid habitus"]],"caption":"Both weaken fibrillin-rich zonules; the direction of displacement differs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-20-10-C19","obj":"NE-20-J.19","title":"Eye injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A corneal abrasion is loss of corneal epithelium from a scratch, fingernail, contact lens, or foreign body. Because the cornea is so densely innervated, the patient has severe pain, tearing, photophobia, and a foreign body sensation. Intact epithelium, sealed by tight junctions and covered with lipid-rich membranes, excludes the water-soluble dye fluorescein, but where epithelium is missing the dye enters the stroma and glows green under cobalt blue light, outlining the defect. Vertical linear abrasions suggest a foreign body under the upper lid. An embedded iron fragment leaves a brown rust ring."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyphema is blood in the anterior chamber, usually from blunt trauma that tears vessels at the iris root or ciliary body. Red cells settle inferiorly as a level and can block the trabecular meshwork, raising IOP. In sickle cell disease or trait, red cells sickle in the low-oxygen, acidic aqueous, stiffen, and obstruct the meshwork even more readily, so pressure and optic nerve damage occur at lower levels. A subconjunctival hemorrhage, by contrast, is blood beneath the conjunctiva from rupture of a small vessel during coughing, straining, minor trauma, or anticoagulant use. It is bright red, sharply bordered, and painless, vision is normal, and it clears within about 1 to 2 weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"An orbital floor (blowout) fracture occurs when a blunt object larger than the orbital opening, such as a fist or ball, strikes the eye. Pressure in the orbit rises suddenly and breaks its thinnest walls, the floor over the maxillary sinus or the medial wall. Orbital fat and the inferior rectus can herniate into the fracture and become trapped, so the eye cannot look up and the patient has vertical double vision. Injury to the infraorbital nerve in its canal numbs the cheek and upper lip, and the globe may sink (enophthalmos). In children a trapdoor fracture can tightly trap the muscle with little visible injury, and traction on it can trigger the oculocardiac reflex (trigeminal afferent, vagal efferent), causing bradycardia, nausea, and vomiting."},{"type":"p","text":"Chemical burns differ by agent. Alkalis (lye, ammonia, lime) saponify the lipids of cell membranes, causing liquefactive necrosis that keeps dissolving tissue and lets the alkali penetrate the stroma and anterior chamber. Acids denature and precipitate surface proteins, causing coagulative necrosis that forms a barrier and limits penetration (hydrofluoric acid is an exception). Alkali burns are therefore more severe, and blanching of the limbus means its blood supply and stem cells are destroyed, so the cornea cannot heal normally."},{"type":"table","title":"Eye injuries","cols":["Injury","Key finding","Mechanism of the finding"],"rows":[["Corneal abrasion or foreign body","Fluorescein-staining defect, severe pain","Missing epithelium admits dye; dense trigeminal innervation"],["Hyphema","Layered blood in anterior chamber, raised IOP","Iris root or ciliary vessel tear; red cells clog meshwork"],["Subconjunctival hemorrhage","Painless red patch, normal vision","Ruptured conjunctival vessel"],["Orbital floor fracture","Limited upgaze, diplopia, cheek numbness","Inferior rectus entrapment, infraorbital nerve injury"],["Alkali burn","Deep corneal damage, limbal blanching","Saponification and liquefactive necrosis"]],"caption":"Each injury's signature finding is explained by the tissue it damages."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy explains most of the patterns in this chapter. The orbital septum and the thin lamina papyracea separate a harmless lid infection from one that can reach the cavernous sinus, the central retinal artery as an end artery explains the sudden, total loss in CRAO, and the short, horizontal eustachian tube explains why otitis media is a childhood disease. Physiology adds the timing: aqueous outflow resistance, endolymph volume, the tonic balance of the vestibular nerves, and the critical period of the visual cortex each set how fast and in what order symptoms appear."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry, microbiology, immunology, and pathology supply the remaining links. Aldose reductase connects diabetes and galactosemia to cataract, fibrillin connects Marfan syndrome and homocystinuria to the lens, and VEGF connects wet AMD, proliferative diabetic retinopathy, and ischemic CRVO. The same organisms recur across sites (Pseudomonas in contact lens keratitis, otitis externa, and perichondritis; S. aureus in hordeolum, cellulitis, and dacryocystitis), and the immune status of the host decides whether CMV causes retinitis and how severe toxoplasmic disease becomes. The limit of pattern recognition is that the red eye, vertigo, and monocular vision loss each have look-alikes, including stroke, so the mechanism behind each sign is what separates them."}],"summary":["A hordeolum is an acute, painful S. aureus gland infection; a chalazion is a painless lipogranuloma from a blocked meibomian gland.","Cataract is light scattering by aggregated or swollen lens proteins; aldose reductase converts glucose and galactose to trapped, osmotically active polyols.","Watery discharge with a preauricular node suggests adenovirus, purulent discharge suggests bacteria (hyperacute with gonococcus), and itching suggests type I allergy; trachoma blinds by tarsal scarring.","Glaucoma is ganglion cell axon loss with disc cupping: open-angle is painless peripheral loss from trabecular resistance, and angle closure is a painful pupillary block emergency.","Pain, reduced vision, an abnormal pupil, or a corneal change mark keratitis, uveitis, or angle closure rather than conjunctivitis.","Dry AMD is drusen and slow atrophy; wet AMD is VEGF-driven choroidal neovascularization with rapid central loss and distortion.","HLA-B27 disease, sarcoidosis, and JIA cause anterior uveitis; Toxoplasma and CMV in AIDS cause posterior uveitis and retinitis.","Vertigo lasting seconds with position is BPPV, hours with low-frequency loss is Meniere disease, and days is vestibular neuritis, or labyrinthitis when hearing is lost.","Eustachian tube obstruction underlies otitis media; Pseudomonas causes otitis externa, malignant otitis externa in diabetics, and perichondritis that spares the lobule.","Weber lateralizes toward conductive loss and away from sensorineural loss; a negative Rinne means conductive loss in that ear."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Eye"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, sections on the ear"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Eye: optics of vision, fluid system of the eye, and receptor function of the retina"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Sense of Hearing, and vestibular sensations"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Chlamydiae, Pseudomonads, Neisseriae, herpesviruses, and free-living amebae"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolism of monosaccharides: polyol pathway and galactose metabolism"}]},"NE-3":{"unit":"NE-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01","title":"Brain and Spinal Cord Structures","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter maps the major structures of the brain and spinal cord to what they do: the primary and language cortices, the basal ganglia loops, the cerebellum, the thalamic relay nuclei, the levels of the brainstem and spinal cord, and the systems that keep us conscious. Board questions are usually localization puzzles. They describe a deficit and ask for the lesion, or name a lesion and ask for the deficit and its side. If you know what each structure normally contributes, which pathways cross, and where they cross, you can work out the answer instead of memorizing lists.","prerequisites":["Neural tube derivatives and the divisions of the brain (NE-1)","Neurons, glia, and excitatory versus inhibitory synapses (NE-2, NE-13)","Major ascending sensory and descending motor tracts and their decussations (NE-5, NE-7)","G protein signaling through Gs and Gi and cAMP"],"connections":[{"to":"Cerebral blood supply and stroke (NE-10, NE-20-A)","how":"Arterial territories decide which cortical, thalamic, or brainstem structures a stroke removes, so vascular syndromes are tested through the localization taught here."},{"to":"Neurodegenerative disease (NE-20-B)","how":"Parkinson and Huntington disease are explained by the basal ganglia circuit, and their pathology (Lewy bodies, CAG expansion) explains which neurons die."},{"to":"Cranial and peripheral nerves (NE-8, NE-20-I)","how":"The brainstem level of each cranial nerve nucleus and the position of the conus and cauda equina underlie cranial nerve and root syndromes."},{"to":"Raised intracranial pressure and herniation (NE-4, NE-20-C)","how":"Herniation causes coma by compressing the upper brainstem arousal system described in the last section."},{"to":"Higher cortical function (NE-11)","how":"The association cortex builds on the primary areas and language areas introduced here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Somatotopy","def":"The orderly mapping of body regions onto a strip of cortex or a nucleus, so that neighboring body parts are represented by neighboring neurons."},{"term":"Homonymous hemianopia","def":"Loss of the same half of the visual field (right or left) in both eyes, caused by a lesion behind the optic chiasm."},{"term":"Aphasia","def":"An acquired disorder of language (producing or understanding words), as distinct from dysarthria, which is a disorder of articulation."},{"term":"Disinhibition","def":"Excitation of a target produced by inhibiting the neurons that normally inhibit it."},{"term":"Medium spiny neuron","def":"The GABAergic projection neuron of the striatum, which forms both the direct and the indirect basal ganglia pathways."},{"term":"Ataxia","def":"Loss of coordination of voluntary movement, with errors of direction, range, force, and timing, in the absence of weakness."},{"term":"Dysdiadochokinesia","def":"Inability to perform rapidly alternating movements, such as pronation and supination of the hand, smoothly and rhythmically."},{"term":"Intention tremor","def":"Tremor that is absent at rest and grows as a limb approaches a target, typical of cerebellar hemisphere disease."},{"term":"Relay nucleus","def":"A thalamic nucleus that receives one type of input and projects it to one specific cortical area."},{"term":"Conus medullaris","def":"The tapered lower end of the spinal cord, lying near the L1 to L2 vertebral level in adults."},{"term":"Ascending reticular activating system (ARAS)","def":"Neurons of the upper pontine and midbrain tegmentum that project through the thalamus, hypothalamus, and basal forebrain to keep the cortex aroused."},{"term":"Brain death","def":"Irreversible loss of all function of the entire brain, including the brainstem, with coma, absent brainstem reflexes, and apnea."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C01","obj":"NE-3.1","title":"Primary cortical areas, language areas, and the deficits their lesions produce","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each primary cortical area handles one kind of information and is laid out as a map. The primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4) occupies the precentral gyrus and the front of the paracentral lobule; its layer V pyramidal neurons, including the giant Betz cells, contribute heavily to the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. The primary somatosensory cortex (areas 3, 1, and 2) lies just behind it in the postcentral gyrus and receives touch, position, pain, and temperature relayed from the opposite side through the thalamus. Both strips follow the same somatotopy: the leg and foot sit on the medial surface, while the trunk, arm, hand, and face run down the lateral convexity, with the hand and face given the most cortex because they need the finest control."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the motor and sensory pathways cross, lesions of either strip give contralateral signs, and the arterial territory predicts which part of the body is affected. Anterior cerebral artery occlusion damages the medial strip and weakens the leg more than the arm; middle cerebral artery occlusion damages the lateral strip and affects the face and arm more than the leg. A motor cortex lesion is an upper motor neuron lesion, with weakness that is flaccid at first and later spastic, hyperreflexia, and an extensor plantar (Babinski) response. Facial weakness spares the forehead, because the upper facial motor neurons receive input from both hemispheres. A somatosensory cortex lesion mainly removes discriminative sensation, such as position sense, two-point discrimination, and stereognosis, while crude pain and temperature awareness is relatively spared because it can reach consciousness at the thalamic level."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary visual cortex lines the calcarine sulcus. Each side receives the opposite visual field from both eyes, so a lesion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Posterior cerebral artery infarcts often spare central vision, because the occipital pole, where the macula is represented, frequently receives collateral flow from the middle cerebral artery. The primary auditory cortex sits on the transverse temporal gyri. Above the cochlear nuclei auditory pathways project to both sides, so a one-sided lesion barely reduces hearing and mainly impairs sound localization."},{"type":"p","text":"Language is lateralized to the dominant hemisphere, the left in nearly all right-handed and most left-handed people. Broca area assembles the motor program for speech, so its loss leaves comprehension intact but speech effortful, sparse, and telegraphic, and patients are aware of their errors. Wernicke area links speech sounds to meaning, so its loss leaves speech fluent but full of wrong and invented words, comprehension poor, and the patient often unaware. Both impair repetition, because each area is part of the repetition loop that the arcuate fasciculus connects."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary and language cortices","cols":["Area","Location","Lesion result","Usual artery"],"rows":[["Primary motor (area 4)","Precentral gyrus, frontal lobe","Contralateral upper motor neuron weakness; forehead spared","ACA (leg), MCA (face, arm)"],["Primary somatosensory (areas 3, 1, 2)","Postcentral gyrus, parietal lobe","Contralateral loss of position sense, fine touch, stereognosis","ACA (leg), MCA (face, arm)"],["Primary visual (area 17)","Banks of the calcarine sulcus, occipital lobe","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia, often macular sparing","PCA"],["Primary auditory (areas 41, 42)","Transverse temporal gyri","Poor sound localization; deafness only if bilateral","MCA"],["Broca (areas 44, 45)","Dominant inferior frontal gyrus","Nonfluent aphasia, comprehension intact, repetition poor, often right face and arm weakness","MCA superior division"],["Wernicke (posterior area 22)","Dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus","Fluent aphasia, comprehension poor, repetition poor, patient often unaware","MCA inferior division"],["Arcuate fasciculus","White matter linking the two language areas","Conduction aphasia: fluent, comprehension intact, repetition poor","MCA"]],"caption":"Fluency places a lesion in front of or behind the central sulcus; comprehension separates Broca from Wernicke aphasia; repetition is poor in all three."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C02","obj":"NE-3.2","title":"Direct and indirect basal ganglia pathways, Parkinson disease, and Huntington disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The basal ganglia send no fibers to the spinal cord; they adjust movement through a loop that runs from cortex to striatum to globus pallidus to thalamus and back to cortex. The key to the circuit is its output: the internal globus pallidus (GPi), together with the substantia nigra pars reticulata, fires tonically and releases GABA onto the ventral anterior and ventral lateral (VA and VL) thalamus. The motor thalamus is therefore held under a constant brake. Movement is allowed by releasing that brake selectively."},{"type":"p","text":"The striatum (caudate and putamen) receives glutamate from the cortex. Its GABAergic medium spiny neurons form two pathways. Direct pathway neurons carry D1 receptors and project to the GPi. Indirect pathway neurons carry D2 receptors and project to the external globus pallidus (GPe), which normally inhibits the subthalamic nucleus; the subthalamic nucleus in turn excites the GPi with glutamate. Counting inhibitory links gives the net effect: two in the direct route excite the thalamus, three in the indirect route inhibit it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Direct pathway (go)","steps":["Cortex excites D1 striatal neurons (glutamate)","Striatum inhibits GPi (GABA)","GPi releases its inhibition of VA and VL thalamus","Thalamus excites motor cortex","Movement is facilitated"],"caption":"Two inhibitory links in series produce net excitation of the thalamus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Indirect pathway (stop)","steps":["Cortex excites D2 striatal neurons (glutamate)","Striatum inhibits GPe (GABA)","Subthalamic nucleus is released from GPe inhibition","Subthalamic nucleus excites GPi (glutamate)","GPi inhibits VA and VL thalamus more strongly","Movement is suppressed"],"caption":"Three inhibitory links plus one excitatory link produce net inhibition of the thalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) acts on both pathways. Through D1 receptors (Gs) it makes direct pathway neurons more responsive to cortex; through D2 receptors (Gi) it makes indirect pathway neurons less responsive. Both effects favor movement, which is why D2 receptor blockers produce parkinsonism."},{"type":"p","text":"In Parkinson disease, pigmented SNc neurons degenerate, with Lewy bodies containing alpha-synuclein, and signs appear only after most nigrostriatal neurons are lost. The motor thalamus is overinhibited (see the worked example), producing bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor, postural instability, a masked face, and a shuffling gait. Huntington disease is autosomal dominant, caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the HTT gene on chromosome 4, and shows anticipation. Its polyglutamine protein kills striatal medium spiny neurons, and the indirect pathway neurons projecting to the GPe are lost first, so the GPi brake weakens and chorea appears. Caudate atrophy enlarges the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles; later loss of direct pathway neurons adds rigidity, while damage to caudate and prefrontal loops explains the dementia and psychiatric change. A small infarct of the subthalamic nucleus removes its drive to the GPi and produces contralateral hemiballismus."},{"type":"table","title":"Basal ganglia disorders by mechanism","cols":["Disorder","Lesion","Change in GPi output","Movement result"],"rows":[["Parkinson disease","Loss of SNc dopamine neurons","Increased","Too little movement: bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor"],["Huntington disease (early)","Loss of indirect pathway striatal neurons, caudate first","Decreased","Too much movement: chorea"],["Hemiballismus","Contralateral subthalamic nucleus","Decreased","Violent flinging of the contralateral limbs"]],"caption":"Hypokinetic disorders raise GPi output; hyperkinetic disorders lower it."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: predicting the signs of dopamine loss","text":"Direct pathway: D1 excitation falls, striatal inhibition of the GPi falls, and GPi output rises. Indirect pathway: D2 inhibition is removed, these striatal neurons fire more, the GPe is suppressed, and the released subthalamic nucleus drives the GPi harder. Both changes increase GPi inhibition of the VA and VL thalamus, so motor cortex receives less excitation and movements are hard to start and scale (bradykinesia)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Early Huntington disease causes chorea, yet late Huntington disease often causes rigidity and slowness. Why does the movement disorder change?","explain":"The indirect pathway neurons die first, lowering GPi output and releasing the thalamus. Later, direct pathway neurons die too, the go signal fails, and the picture becomes hypokinetic.","check":"The two pathways and the table of disorders by mechanism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C03","obj":"NE-3.3","title":"Cerebellar coordination and vermis versus hemisphere lesions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cerebellum does not start movements, and losing it does not cause weakness. It compares the movement the cortex intended with the movement that is actually happening and corrects its direction, force, range, and timing. Mossy fibers bring a copy of the motor command from the pontine nuclei through the middle cerebellar peduncle, and feedback from muscles, joints, and the vestibular system through the spinocerebellar tracts and vestibular nuclei. Climbing fibers from the contralateral inferior olive enter through the inferior peduncle and signal errors that drive motor learning."},{"type":"p","text":"All of this converges on Purkinje cells, the only output of the cerebellar cortex. Purkinje cells are GABAergic and inhibit the deep cerebellar nuclei (dentate, emboliform, globose, and fastigial) and the lateral vestibular nucleus. The deep nuclei send the cerebellum's mainly excitatory output, mostly through the superior cerebellar peduncle. The cerebellar cortex therefore works by sculpting the activity of the deep nuclei rather than by driving movement directly."},{"type":"table","title":"Functional zones of the cerebellum","cols":["Zone","Main output","Controls","Lesion signs"],"rows":[["Vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular lobe)","Vestibular nuclei","Balance and eye movements","Truncal imbalance, nystagmus"],["Vermis (medial spinocerebellum)","Fastigial nucleus to vestibulospinal and reticulospinal systems","Posture and axial muscles of both sides","Truncal ataxia, wide-based gait, both sides affected"],["Paravermis (intermediate zone)","Emboliform and globose nuclei","Ongoing limb movement","Ipsilateral limb ataxia"],["Lateral hemisphere (cerebrocerebellum)","Dentate nucleus to VL thalamus and motor cortex","Planning and timing of skilled limb movement","Ipsilateral dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia"]],"caption":"Midline structures control the trunk on both sides; lateral structures control the limbs on the same side."},{"type":"p","text":"The vermis works with the medial descending systems that control axial muscles on both sides of the body. A vermis lesion therefore causes truncal ataxia: an unsteady trunk, a wide-based staggering gait, and difficulty standing or sitting without support, while the arms may test nearly normal. The unsteadiness is present with the eyes open, because it is a failure of coordination rather than of sensory input. Chronic alcohol use degenerates the anterior superior vermis and produces this pattern of gait ataxia with relatively preserved limb coordination."},{"type":"p","text":"Each hemisphere works with the lateral corticospinal system that controls the limbs, so a hemisphere lesion causes ipsilateral limb ataxia. The patient overshoots or undershoots a target (dysmetria), shows a tremor that grows as the finger nears the target (intention tremor, absent at rest, unlike the resting tremor of Parkinson disease), cannot perform rapid alternating movements (dysdiadochokinesia), has reduced tone, and tends to veer or fall toward the side of the lesion. Speech may become slurred and irregular in rhythm (scanning dysarthria). The signs are ipsilateral because the path is crossed twice, as shown below."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why cerebellar hemisphere signs are ipsilateral","steps":["Hemisphere Purkinje cells shape dentate nucleus output","Dentate fibers leave in the superior cerebellar peduncle","First crossing in the midbrain","Contralateral VL thalamus to contralateral motor cortex","Second crossing: corticospinal tract decussates in the medulla","Corrections act on limbs on the side of the lesion"],"caption":"Two decussations cancel, so a right hemisphere lesion disturbs the right limbs."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a cerebellar hemisphere tumor and a patient with dorsal column degeneration both sway when standing. Which one gets markedly worse when the eyes close?","explain":"The patient with dorsal column disease. Vision substitutes for lost proprioception, so removing it unmasks the deficit (a positive Romberg sign). The cerebellar patient is unsteady with eyes open as well, because the problem is the coordination of movement, not the sensory input.","check":"The opening paragraph on the cerebellum as a comparator and the vermis paragraph on truncal ataxia with eyes open."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C04","obj":"NE-3.4","title":"Thalamic relay nuclei, thalamic infarction, and thalamic pain syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thalamus is the paired gray mass of the diencephalon on either side of the third ventricle. Nearly every pathway that reaches the cerebral cortex synapses here first; smell is the main exception, reaching olfactory cortex directly. The specific relay nuclei each receive one kind of input and project to one cortical area, and that area sends dense fibers back to the same nucleus to regulate the relay. The thalamus is therefore an active gate, not a simple wire, and a lesion of a relay nucleus produces nearly the same deficit as a lesion of its cortical target."},{"type":"table","title":"Thalamic relay nuclei","cols":["Nucleus","Input","Cortical target"],"rows":[["VPL (ventral posterolateral)","Medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract: contralateral body touch, position, pain, temperature","Primary somatosensory cortex"],["VPM (ventral posteromedial)","Trigeminothalamic fibers (contralateral face) and taste from the solitary nucleus","Somatosensory cortex face area; gustatory cortex (insula and frontal operculum)"],["LGN (lateral geniculate)","Optic tract","Primary visual cortex through the optic radiations"],["MGN (medial geniculate)","Inferior colliculus","Primary auditory cortex"],["VL (ventral lateral)","Dentate nucleus of the cerebellum and GPi of the basal ganglia","Primary motor and premotor cortex"]],"caption":"Lateral geniculate for vision and medial geniculate for hearing; VPL for the body and VPM for the face and taste."},{"type":"p","text":"Other nuclei serve memory, emotion, and arousal rather than relay: the anterior nucleus belongs to the limbic circuit, the mediodorsal nucleus connects with prefrontal cortex and is damaged in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, and the intralaminar nuclei carry arousal signals to the cortex (C06). Projections from VPL and VPM pass through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, which lies immediately lateral to the thalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"The ventral posterior nuclei are supplied mainly by thalamogeniculate branches of the posterior cerebral artery, small penetrating vessels that are prone to lacunar infarction in chronic hypertension. Because VPL and VPM together receive every somatic modality from the opposite side, an infarct confined to them causes contralateral loss of touch, position, pain, and temperature over the face, arm, and leg, without weakness (pure sensory stroke). A larger lesion that reaches the internal capsule adds contralateral hemiparesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Weeks to months after such an infarct, as sensation partly returns, some patients develop thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome): constant burning or aching pain on the numb side, with light touch or cold felt as pain (allodynia) and an exaggerated, delayed response to painful stimuli (hyperpathia). This is central neuropathic pain. It is thought to arise because deafferented thalamic and cortical neurons become hyperexcitable and the inhibitory influence that normal sensory input exerts on pain processing is lost."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C05","obj":"NE-3.5","title":"Brainstem levels of the cranial nerve nuclei and the regions of the spinal cord","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cranial nerve nuclei are arranged roughly in numerical order down the brainstem, so the nerves involved in a lesion give its level. The midbrain holds the oculomotor (III) and Edinger-Westphal nuclei at the level of the superior colliculus and the trochlear (IV) nucleus at the level of the inferior colliculus. The pons holds the trigeminal (V) main sensory and motor nuclei, the abducens (VI) and facial (VII) motor nuclei in its lower part, and the vestibular and cochlear (VIII) nuclei at the pontomedullary junction. The medulla holds the nuclei of the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and hypoglossal (XII) nerves. The trigeminal sensory nuclei, however, extend from the midbrain into the upper cervical cord, where the spinal accessory (XI) nucleus also lies."},{"type":"table","title":"Cranial nerve nuclei by brainstem level","cols":["Level","Nuclei","Localizing clue"],"rows":[["Midbrain","III and Edinger-Westphal (superior colliculus level); IV (inferior colliculus level)","Eye movement and pupil signs"],["Pons","V (main sensory, motor); VI and VII (lower pons); VIII (pontomedullary junction)","Jaw, lateral gaze, facial, and hearing or vestibular signs"],["Medulla","IX, X (nucleus ambiguus, dorsal motor nucleus of X, solitary nucleus); XII","Swallowing, voice, gag, and tongue signs"],["Upper cervical cord","XI (spinal accessory); spinal trigeminal nucleus continues here","Weak shoulder shrug and head turning"]],"caption":"The somatic motor nuclei for the eye and tongue muscles (III, IV, VI, XII) lie near the midline, so medial brainstem lesions tend to involve them."},{"type":"p","text":"Brainstem lesions classically produce crossed signs: an ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy, because cranial motor neurons and their exiting fibers do not cross (the trochlear nerve is the exception, crossing before it exits), with contralateral hemiparesis, because the corticospinal tract has not yet crossed. The cranial nerve gives the level; long tract signs place the lesion inside the brainstem."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a crossed brainstem lesion","text":"Right upper motor neuron hemiparesis, right loss of position and vibration sense, and a tongue deviating to the left. The tongue points toward the weak side, so left XII is affected, placing the lesion in the medulla. Right hemiparesis means the left pyramid is damaged above its decussation; right proprioceptive loss means the left medial lemniscus, already crossed, is involved. All three lie in the left medial medulla, supplied by the anterior spinal or vertebral artery."},{"type":"p","text":"In the spinal cord, central gray matter is shaped like an H. The dorsal horn receives sensory input, the ventral horn contains alpha and gamma motor neurons, and from T1 to L2 a lateral horn holds sympathetic preganglionic neurons; sacral parasympathetic neurons at S2 to S4 sit in the intermediate gray without forming a distinct horn. The gray matter swells into the cervical (about C4 to T1) and lumbosacral (about L2 to S3) enlargements, which supply the limbs through the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses."},{"type":"table","title":"Spinal cord white columns","cols":["Column","Main tract","Carries","Side of deficit below a cord lesion"],"rows":[["Dorsal","Fasciculus gracilis (legs, medial) and cuneatus (arms, above about T6)","Fine touch, vibration, position sense","Ipsilateral (crosses in the medulla)"],["Lateral","Lateral corticospinal tract","Voluntary motor commands","Ipsilateral (already crossed in the medulla)"],["Anterolateral","Spinothalamic tract","Pain and temperature","Contralateral (crosses within one to two segments)"]],"caption":"Where each tract crosses determines which side loses function below a one-sided cord lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"The vertebral column grows longer than the cord during fetal life and childhood, so the cord ends as the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 vertebral level in adults (lower, near L3, at birth). Below it, lumbar and sacral roots descend in the dural sac as the cauda equina. The dural sac continues to about S2, which is why a needle introduced at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5 reaches cerebrospinal fluid among mobile roots without striking the cord. For the same reason, cord segments lie progressively higher than their numbered vertebrae."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C06","obj":"NE-3.6","title":"The ascending reticular activating system, coma, delirium, and brain death","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Consciousness has two components. Arousal, the level of wakefulness, depends on the brainstem; awareness, the content of experience, depends on the cerebral hemispheres. Arousal is maintained by the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS), a group of neurons in the tegmentum of the upper pons and midbrain. They include cholinergic neurons of the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei, noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, and serotonergic neurons of the raphe nuclei. Their projections reach the cortex through the intralaminar and other thalamic nuclei and, by a second route, through the hypothalamus and basal forebrain, which add histamine, orexin, and acetylcholine."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the brainstem keeps the cortex awake","steps":["Reticular neurons in upper pons and midbrain fire tonically","Acetylcholine, norepinephrine, and serotonin are released rostrally","Thalamic relay and intralaminar nuclei shift to tonic firing","Hypothalamus and basal forebrain add direct cortical activation","Both hemispheres are depolarized and responsive","Arousal is maintained"],"caption":"Coma follows interruption of this system at its brainstem origin or failure of both hemispheres it activates."},{"type":"p","text":"Coma is unarousable unresponsiveness with the eyes closed. It requires one of two things: damage to the ARAS in the upper pons, midbrain, or thalamus, or dysfunction of both hemispheres. Diffuse causes include hypoxia, hypoglycemia, liver or kidney failure, drug intoxication, and seizures. A large lesion of one hemisphere does not cause coma by itself; it does so when swelling shifts the brain and compresses the midbrain, as in transtentorial herniation, often heralded by an ipsilateral dilated pupil from compression of CN III. Lesions below the upper pons spare arousal, which is why a ventral pontine infarct can leave a patient fully awake but unable to move anything except the eyes vertically and the eyelids (locked-in syndrome)."},{"type":"p","text":"Delirium is different. It is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness, often with disorganized thinking and a changing level of alertness, caused by a systemic insult such as infection, medication, alcohol withdrawal, or metabolic derangement. The patient can be roused, the course fluctuates over hours, the EEG shows diffuse slowing, and the state usually clears when the cause is corrected."},{"type":"p","text":"Brain death is the irreversible loss of all function of the entire brain, including the brainstem. It is declared only when the cause is known and confounders such as sedative drugs, hypothermia, and severe metabolic disturbance have been excluded. The findings are coma, absence of every brainstem reflex (pupillary light, corneal, oculocephalic, oculovestibular, gag, and cough), and apnea: no breathing effort even when arterial PCO2 rises to at least about 60 mmHg, a level that would strongly drive an intact medullary respiratory center. Spinal reflexes can persist because the cord is not part of the brain."},{"type":"table","title":"Disorders of consciousness","cols":["State","Arousal","Awareness","Brainstem reflexes and breathing","Usual course"],"rows":[["Delirium","Fluctuates, rousable","Impaired attention","Intact","Usually reversible"],["Coma","Unrousable, eyes closed","Absent","Variable","Recovery, vegetative state, or death"],["Persistent vegetative state","Eyes open, sleep and wake cycles","Absent","Intact, breathes spontaneously","May persist for years"],["Locked-in syndrome","Normal","Normal","Vertical eye movement and blinking preserved","Not a disorder of consciousness"],["Brain death","None","Absent","All absent, apnea","Irreversible"]],"caption":"Arousal tracks the brainstem and awareness tracks the hemispheres."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient after a basilar artery occlusion lies motionless, but blinks on command and looks up when asked. Is this patient in coma?","explain":"No. The infarct lies in the ventral pons, interrupting corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers below the ARAS, so arousal and awareness are intact. This is locked-in syndrome, and it must not be mistaken for coma or brain death.","check":"The location of the ARAS and the table of disorders of consciousness."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Localization is the common thread of this chapter. Anatomy supplies the map: which side a pathway crosses, which artery feeds a region, and where cranial nerve nuclei sit. Physiology explains the deficit: the basal ganglia gate movement through disinhibition, the cerebellum compares intention with performance, the thalamus relays and filters, and the brainstem keeps the cortex aroused. Pathology names the processes that remove each structure: lacunar infarcts in the thalamus and internal capsule, Lewy body degeneration of the nigra, polyglutamine toxicity in the caudate, alcohol injury to the vermis, and herniation that compresses the midbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"The rules have limits. Language dominance is not universal, cortical maps are larger for some body parts in some people, and real infarcts rarely respect a single nucleus, so mixed pictures are common. Reasoning from what each structure contributes and where its pathways cross is more reliable than memorizing syndromes."}],"summary":["Primary motor and sensory strips are somatotopic: leg medial (ACA), face and arm lateral (MCA), with contralateral deficits.","Calcarine cortex lesions cause contralateral homonymous hemianopia, often macular sparing; one-sided auditory cortex lesions barely reduce hearing.","Broca aphasia is nonfluent with comprehension intact; Wernicke aphasia is fluent with comprehension poor; both impair repetition.","The GPi tonically inhibits the motor thalamus; the direct pathway releases this brake and the indirect pathway, through the subthalamic nucleus, tightens it.","Dopamine excites D1 direct neurons and inhibits D2 indirect neurons, so both actions favor movement; losing it in Parkinson disease raises GPi output.","Early Huntington disease removes indirect pathway neurons, lowers GPi output, and causes chorea; a subthalamic infarct causes contralateral hemiballismus.","Vermis lesions cause bilateral truncal ataxia and a wide-based gait; hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia because of a double decussation.","VPL relays the body, VPM the face and taste, LGN vision, MGN hearing, and VL cerebellar and basal ganglia output; a VPL and VPM infarct gives contralateral sensory loss and may later cause thalamic pain.","Cranial nerve nuclei descend in order (III and IV midbrain, V to VIII pons, IX, X, XII medulla); crossed signs place a lesion in the brainstem; the adult conus ends near L1 to L2.","Coma needs upper brainstem ARAS damage or bilateral hemisphere failure; delirium fluctuates and is reversible; brain death is irreversible loss of all brain and brainstem function with apnea."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contributions of the Cerebellum and Basal Ganglia to Overall Motor Control"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Cortex, Intellectual Functions of the Brain, Learning, and Memory"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of the nervous system and motor control"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: spinal cord, meninges, and the vertebral levels of the conus medullaris"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on degenerative diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-4":{"unit":"NE-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01","title":"Cerebrospinal Fluid Compartments and Meninges","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the coverings of the brain and spinal cord, the spaces between them, the dural folds that divide the skull into compartments, the circulation of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), the barriers that separate blood from brain and CSF, and the interpretation of a CSF sample. Board questions give a mechanism of injury, a CT shape, a pupil, or a CSF panel and ask you to name the space, the vessel, the compressed structure, or the organism class. Each answer follows from which layer is attached where, which opening lies next to which structure, and what inflammation does to the barriers.","prerequisites":["Development and gross divisions of the brain and the ventricular system","Circle of Willis and the territories of the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries","Course of the oculomotor nerve and the corticospinal tract through the midbrain","Capillary types (continuous, fenestrated, sinusoidal) and Starling forces","Innate and adaptive immune cell recruitment (neutrophils versus lymphocytes)"],"connections":[{"to":"CNS blood supply and vascular disease (NE-10, NE-20-A)","how":"Herniation compresses the anterior and posterior cerebral arteries, and berry aneurysms on the circle of Willis are the usual source of spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage."},{"to":"CNS trauma (NE-20-H)","how":"Epidural and subdural hematomas and the herniation they cause are the main mechanisms of death after head injury."},{"to":"Neurological pathogens and CNS infections (NE-19, NE-20-G)","how":"The organism class in meningitis is predicted from CSF cell type, protein, and glucose before culture results return."},{"to":"Hypothalamus and posterior pituitary (endocrine)","how":"Circumventricular organs that lack a blood-brain barrier sense osmolality and angiotensin II and release ADH and oxytocin into blood."},{"to":"Control of breathing (respiratory)","how":"CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier freely while H+ crosses only slowly, so central chemoreceptors track arterial PCO2."}],"terms":[{"term":"Meninges","def":"The three membranes that cover the brain and spinal cord: dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater."},{"term":"Potential space","def":"A space that exists only when fluid or blood separates two layers that are normally in contact."},{"term":"Dural venous sinus","def":"A venous channel between the periosteal and meningeal layers of the cranial dura that drains blood and CSF from the brain."},{"term":"Bridging vein","def":"A cerebral vein that crosses the subdural space from the cortex to a dural venous sinus."},{"term":"Herniation","def":"Displacement of brain tissue from one intracranial compartment into another through a rigid dural or bony opening."},{"term":"Monro-Kellie doctrine","def":"Because the adult skull is rigid, the volumes of brain, blood, and CSF are fixed in sum, so an increase in one must be offset by a decrease in another."},{"term":"Cistern","def":"A wide region of the subarachnoid space where the arachnoid bridges over a deep surface contour."},{"term":"Arachnoid granulation","def":"A tuft of arachnoid that projects into a dural venous sinus and returns CSF to venous blood."},{"term":"Blood-brain barrier","def":"The diffusion barrier formed by tight junctions between nonfenestrated brain capillary endothelial cells."},{"term":"Circumventricular organ","def":"A small region beside the third or fourth ventricle with fenestrated capillaries and no blood-brain barrier."},{"term":"Xanthochromia","def":"Yellow discoloration of CSF from hemoglobin breakdown products, indicating blood that entered the CSF hours or more before the tap."},{"term":"Albuminocytologic dissociation","def":"Raised CSF protein with a normal or near-normal white cell count."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C01","obj":"NE-4.1","title":"Meninges, meningeal spaces, and intracranial hemorrhage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The space a bleed occupies follows from which layer is attached to which, and from which vessel breaks. The dura mater is the tough outer layer. In the skull it has an outer periosteal layer, which is the inner periosteum of the bones, and an inner meningeal layer; the two are fused except where they separate to enclose the dural venous sinuses, and the meningeal layer folds inward to form the falx and tentorium (C02). The cranial dura is pain sensitive (mainly trigeminal above the tentorium, upper cervical and vagal below)."},{"type":"p","text":"The arachnoid mater is a thin avascular membrane held against the dura by CSF pressure; its outer arachnoid barrier cells are joined by tight junctions, and fine trabeculae cross from it to the pia. The pia mater is a delicate vascular layer that adheres to the brain and spinal cord and follows every sulcus."},{"type":"p","text":"The epidural space lies between bone and dura. In the skull it is only a potential space, because the periosteal dura is the lining of the bone, so it opens only when blood strips the dura away. The spinal dura has no periosteal layer, so the vertebral canal has a real epidural space containing fat and the internal vertebral venous plexus. The subdural space is also a potential space: dura and arachnoid are joined by a weak layer of dural border cells, and this layer splits when blood collects. The subarachnoid space is a real space between arachnoid and pia that holds CSF and the large arteries and veins of the brain surface."},{"type":"table","title":"Intracranial hemorrhage by space","cols":["Feature","Epidural","Subdural","Subarachnoid"],"rows":[["Location","Between skull and dura","Between dura and arachnoid (dural border cell layer)","Between arachnoid and pia, in CSF"],["Usual source","Middle meningeal artery torn by a fracture at the pterion","Bridging veins torn by acceleration and deceleration","Trauma overall; ruptured berry aneurysm when spontaneous"],["CT shape","Biconvex (lens shaped); does not cross sutures","Crescent; crosses sutures, stops at the falx and tentorium","Blood in the basal cisterns and sulci"],["Tempo","Arterial; expands over hours","Venous; acute, or slowly over days to weeks","Sudden, severe headache at onset"]],"caption":"The vessel sets the tempo; dural attachments set the shape."},{"type":"p","text":"In epidural hematoma, the middle meningeal artery (a branch of the maxillary artery that enters through the foramen spinosum) is torn where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. Subdural blood is venous and low pressure. Brain atrophy in older adults and in chronic alcohol use stretches the bridging veins, so minor trauma tears them, and shaking injures infants the same way. Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage puts arterial blood directly into CSF, usually from a saccular aneurysm at a branch point of the circle of Willis, most often near the anterior communicating artery. Blood irritates the meninges, red cells appear in the CSF, and xanthochromia develops over hours as hemoglobin is broken down to bilirubin. Blood around the arteries later triggers vasospasm, most often about 3 to 10 days after the bleed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Epidural hematoma","steps":["Blow to the side of the head fractures the pterion","Fracture tears the middle meningeal artery","Arterial blood strips dura from bone, stopping at sutures","Some patients are lucid while the clot enlarges","Rising pressure drives uncal herniation and rapid decline"],"caption":"Arterial source explains both the lucid interval and the speed of decline."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A crescent-shaped collection over one hemisphere crosses the coronal suture but ends sharply at the midline. Which space is it in?","explain":"Subdural. Blood under the meningeal dura is not held by sutures, which bind only the periosteal dura to bone, but it cannot cross the falx, which is a fold of the meningeal dura itself.","check":"The table of hemorrhage by space and the description of the dural layers."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C02","obj":"NE-4.2","title":"Dural folds, intracranial pressure, and herniation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The meningeal dura folds inward to form rigid partitions. The falx cerebri is a sickle-shaped sheet in the longitudinal fissure between the hemispheres, running from the crista galli to the tentorium; its attached upper border carries the superior sagittal sinus, and its free lower border, which arches above the corpus callosum, carries the inferior sagittal sinus. The tentorium cerebelli roofs the posterior cranial fossa, separating the occipital lobes from the cerebellum and dividing the cranial cavity into supratentorial and infratentorial compartments. Its free anterior edge bounds the tentorial notch, the only opening between the two compartments, through which the midbrain passes."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the adult skull is rigid, brain, blood, and CSF share a fixed volume (the Monro-Kellie doctrine). An expanding mass is first offset by pushing CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and blood out of the venous sinuses, so intracranial pressure (about 5 to 15 mm Hg in a supine adult) hardly changes. Once these reserves are used, small added volumes raise pressure steeply. Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, so the rise causes ischemia. Ischemia of the medulla triggers a sympathetic surge that raises blood pressure, the baroreceptor reflex slows the heart, and breathing becomes irregular (the Cushing triad)."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Rising intracranial pressure","steps":["Mass expands after CSF and venous reserves are used","Intracranial pressure rises steeply","Perfusion pressure (MAP minus ICP) falls","Ischemia causes edema, adding volume"],"caption":"Edema from ischemia adds volume and raises pressure further, which is why decline can accelerate."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure is not shared evenly. Brain moves from the higher-pressure compartment through a rigid opening, and the structures at that opening determine the signs (table below). In uncal herniation the medial temporal lobe slides over the tentorial edge and presses the oculomotor nerve against the edge or the posterior cerebral artery. Its parasympathetic pupillary fibers run on the outside of the nerve, so the ipsilateral pupil dilates and stops reacting before ptosis and a down-and-out eye appear; ischemic third nerve palsy in diabetes damages the core of the nerve and usually spares the pupil. Compression of the ipsilateral cerebral peduncle gives contralateral hemiparesis because the corticospinal tract has not yet crossed, and midbrain compression of the reticular activating system lowers consciousness. Downward stretch can tear paramedian branches of the basilar artery, causing Duret hemorrhages in the midbrain and pons. Removing CSF by lumbar puncture when a mass is present can create the pressure gradient that drives tonsillar herniation."},{"type":"table","title":"Herniation syndromes","cols":["Type","What moves","What is compressed","Result"],"rows":[["Subfalcine","Cingulate gyrus under the free edge of the falx","Anterior cerebral artery","Medial frontal and parietal infarct; contralateral leg weakness"],["Uncal (lateral transtentorial)","Uncus of the medial temporal lobe into the tentorial notch","Ipsilateral CN III, posterior cerebral artery, cerebral peduncle; midbrain","Fixed dilated pupil, contralateral homonymous hemianopia, contralateral hemiparesis, coma"],["Tonsillar","Cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum","Medulla (respiratory and cardiovascular centers)","Apnea, circulatory collapse, death"]],"caption":"Each opening has a fixed neighbor, so the compressed structure predicts the deficit."},{"type":"example","title":"Which side is the lesion?","text":"A man with a right temporal epidural hematoma becomes drowsy. His right pupil is dilated and fixed. The uncus herniates on the side of the mass, so the right CN III is compressed, and the pupil localizes correctly. Weakness should be on the left, from the right cerebral peduncle. If instead the right arm and leg are weak, the shift has pushed the left cerebral peduncle against the opposite tentorial edge (Kernohan notch), damaging left corticospinal fibers above their crossing. Weakness then appears ipsilateral to the mass and falsely localizes. The pupil is the more reliable sign of side."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C03","obj":"NE-4.3","title":"Cisterns, CSF circulation, and the lumbar cistern","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pia follows every contour of the brain, but the arachnoid bridges over deep grooves, leaving wide pools of CSF called cisterns. They hold arteries and cranial nerves, so blood from a ruptured aneurysm collects in them, and they receive CSF as it leaves the fourth ventricle."},{"type":"table","title":"Major subarachnoid cisterns","cols":["Cistern","Location","Key contents or role"],"rows":[["Cerebellomedullary (cisterna magna)","Between the cerebellum and the dorsal medulla","Receives CSF from the median aperture of the fourth ventricle"],["Pontine (prepontine)","Anterior to the pons","Basilar artery"],["Interpeduncular","Between the cerebral peduncles","Oculomotor nerves, basilar bifurcation, posterior circle of Willis"],["Chiasmatic","Around the optic chiasm","Optic chiasm, anterior circle of Willis"],["Quadrigeminal (cistern of the great cerebral vein)","Behind the midbrain, above the cerebellum","Great cerebral vein"],["Lumbar","Conus medullaris (about L1 to L2) to about S2","Cauda equina and filum terminale; site of lumbar puncture"]],"caption":"Cisterns at the base of the brain are where subarachnoid blood is seen on CT."},{"type":"p","text":"Choroid plexus in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles secretes most CSF, about 500 mL per day. Only about 150 mL is present at once, so the volume turns over three to four times daily. The choroid epithelium moves Na+, Cl-, and HCO3- into the ventricles, using a Na+/K+-ATPase on its apical (ventricular) surface and carbonic anhydrase, and water follows through aquaporin-1. Secretion is nearly independent of CSF pressure, so a block anywhere downstream raises pressure upstream."},{"type":"flow","title":"CSF circulation","steps":["Choroid plexus secretes CSF into the lateral ventricles","Interventricular foramina (of Monro) to the third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle","Median and lateral apertures into the cisterns","Subarachnoid space around brain and spinal cord","Arachnoid granulations into the superior sagittal sinus"],"caption":"Flow is one way, from secretion in the ventricles to absorption into venous blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Arachnoid granulations are tufts of arachnoid that push through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus. Absorption is bulk flow driven by CSF pressure exceeding venous sinus pressure, and the granulations behave as one-way valves, so blood does not enter CSF when venous pressure rises. Some CSF also drains along nerve sheaths to lymphatics. Obstruction inside the ventricular system, as in aqueductal stenosis, causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus; scarring of the granulations after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage causes communicating hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"The vertebral column grows longer than the spinal cord during development, so the adult conus medullaris ends at about L1 to L2 (about L3 at birth). The dural sac and subarachnoid space continue to about S2, forming the lumbar cistern, which contains CSF, the filum terminale, and the floating roots of the cauda equina. A needle at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5 enters the cistern below the cord, and the roots usually slide aside. The line joining the tops of the iliac crests crosses about the L4 spinous process or the L4 to L5 interspace. A midline needle passes skin, subcutaneous tissue, the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, the ligamentum flavum, the epidural space, the dura, and the arachnoid before CSF flows."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor compresses the cerebral aqueduct. Which ventricles enlarge, and would a lumbar puncture relieve the pressure?","explain":"The lateral and third ventricles enlarge because they are upstream of the block, while the fourth ventricle stays normal. This is noncommunicating hydrocephalus, so the lumbar cistern does not connect with the dilated ventricles, and lowering spinal pressure risks a downward pressure gradient and herniation.","check":"The CSF circulation flow and the paragraph on obstruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C04","obj":"NE-4.4","title":"Blood-brain barrier, blood-CSF barrier, and circumventricular organs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In most tissues small solutes pass between capillary endothelial cells. Brain capillaries close this route. The blood-brain barrier has three parts: continuous, nonfenestrated endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions (claudin-5 and occludin) with very little vesicular transport; a basement membrane shared with pericytes; and astrocyte foot processes covering nearly the whole capillary surface. The endothelial tight junctions are the actual diffusion barrier. Astrocytes and pericytes induce and maintain these endothelial properties rather than block diffusion themselves."},{"type":"p","text":"With the paracellular route closed, a substance must cross the endothelial cell, so lipid solubility, small size, and lack of charge predict entry. Polar nutrients need carriers, and efflux pumps such as P-glycoprotein return many lipid-soluble drugs to blood. Unconjugated bilirubin that is not bound to albumin is lipid soluble and can enter the neonatal brain, staining the basal ganglia (kernicterus)."},{"type":"table","title":"How substances cross the blood-brain barrier","cols":["Route","Examples","Consequence"],"rows":[["Simple diffusion (lipid soluble)","O2, CO2, ethanol, general anesthetics","Rapid equilibration with blood"],["Carrier mediated","Glucose by GLUT1; large neutral amino acids and L-dopa by LAT1","Saturable; dopamine has no carrier, so only its precursor enters"],["Restricted","H+, HCO3-, proteins, most ions and polar drugs","Central chemoreceptors sense arterial PCO2 rather than acute changes in blood pH"],["Active efflux","P-glycoprotein substrates","Keeps many drugs and toxins out of the brain"]],"caption":"CO2 diffuses in and is hydrated to H+ near the chemoreceptors, while plasma H+ enters only slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumors, abscesses, infection, and infarction open the barrier, so plasma proteins and water leak into brain interstitium (vasogenic edema) and intravenous contrast enhances the lesion. This differs from cytotoxic edema, in which energy failure stops the Na+/K+-ATPase and cells swell behind an intact barrier."},{"type":"p","text":"In the choroid plexus the capillaries are fenestrated and leaky, so the barrier moves to the choroid epithelium, modified ependymal cells joined by tight junctions. This blood-CSF barrier, with the arachnoid barrier cells at the outer surface, separates blood from CSF."},{"type":"p","text":"Circumventricular organs lie beside the third and fourth ventricles and have fenestrated capillaries and no barrier, because their job requires contact with blood: they sense its composition or secrete hormones into it. Tanycyte-like ependymal cells joined by tight junctions seal them off from the rest of the brain and the CSF."},{"type":"table","title":"Circumventricular organs","cols":["Organ","Location","Function"],"rows":[["Area postrema","Floor of the fourth ventricle","Chemoreceptor trigger zone: blood-borne toxins and drugs trigger vomiting"],["Organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT)","Anterior wall of the third ventricle","Senses plasma osmolality; drives thirst and ADH release"],["Subfornical organ","Roof of the third ventricle below the fornix","Senses angiotensin II; drives thirst and ADH release"],["Median eminence","Floor of the third ventricle (base of the infundibulum)","Releases hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones into the hypophyseal portal capillaries"],["Posterior pituitary","Neurohypophysis","Releases ADH and oxytocin into systemic blood"],["Pineal gland","Roof of the third ventricle, posteriorly","Secretes melatonin"]],"caption":"Sensory organs (area postrema, OVLT, subfornical organ) sample blood; secretory organs release hormones into it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C05","obj":"NE-4.5","title":"Interpreting CSF in meningitis and Guillain-Barré syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each CSF value reflects a process. Opening pressure, measured with the patient lying on the side, is normally about 6 to 20 cm H2O. Normal CSF holds up to about 5 white cells per µL, all mononuclear, and no red cells. Protein is about 15 to 45 mg/dL, under 1% of the plasma level, because the barriers exclude large molecules. Glucose is about 50 to 80 mg/dL, roughly 60% of a simultaneous blood glucose, because it enters only by carrier-mediated GLUT1 transport and is continuously taken up by brain cells; a CSF to blood ratio below about 0.4 is clearly low."},{"type":"p","text":"Meningitis changes these values in three ways. Inflammation opens the barriers, so protein leaks in, and edema with impaired absorption raises pressure. The cell type reflects the immune response: pyogenic bacteria recruit neutrophils, while viral, fungal, and tuberculous infections are dominated by lymphocytes. Glucose falls when transport across the inflamed barrier is impaired and when leukocytes, bacteria, and inflamed brain consume it by glycolysis, which also raises CSF lactate. Viral meningitis recruits lymphocytes without major glucose consumption, so glucose stays normal."},{"type":"table","title":"CSF patterns","cols":["Condition","Opening pressure","Cells per µL (main type)","Protein","Glucose"],"rows":[["Normal","About 6 to 20 cm H2O","0 to 5 (mononuclear)","About 15 to 45 mg/dL","About 50 to 80 mg/dL; ratio about 0.6"],["Bacterial","Raised","Often 1,000 to 5,000 (neutrophils)","High, often 100 to 500 mg/dL","Low; ratio below about 0.4"],["Viral","Normal or mildly raised","About 10 to 300 (lymphocytes)","Normal or mildly raised","Normal"],["Fungal (cryptococcal)","Often very high","About 20 to 500 (lymphocytes); may be few in AIDS","Raised","Low"],["Tuberculous","Raised","About 50 to 500 (lymphocytes)","High, often very high","Low"],["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Normal","Under about 10","Raised","Normal"]],"caption":"Values are typical ranges; individual cases overlap, so the pattern is read as a whole."},{"type":"p","text":"Patterns overlap. Very early viral meningitis can show neutrophils, partially treated bacterial meningitis can look lymphocytic, and some viruses (mumps, lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus) can lower glucose modestly. Cryptococcal meningitis in advanced HIV infection may show few cells because the host cannot mount inflammation, yet pressure is often very high, thought to reflect capsular polysaccharide blocking the arachnoid granulations. Tuberculous meningitis forms a thick, protein-rich basal exudate that can entrap cranial nerves and block CSF flow. Gram stain, culture, cryptococcal antigen, acid-fast stain, and PCR identify the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barré syndrome is an immune-mediated polyradiculoneuropathy, most often demyelinating. The proximal nerve roots run through the subarachnoid space, and their inflamed blood-nerve barrier leaks protein into CSF, but the inflammation is in the nerve, not the meninges, so few cells enter. The result is albuminocytologic dissociation. Protein may still be normal in the first week, and a count above about 50 cells per µL suggests another cause, such as HIV infection, Lyme disease, or lymphoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a lymphocytic panel with low glucose","text":"Over three weeks a man develops headache, fever, and a left CN VI palsy. CSF: opening pressure 28 cm H2O, 220 white cells per µL (85% lymphocytes), protein 240 mg/dL, glucose 30 mg/dL with blood glucose 110 mg/dL. Step 1: the ratio is 30/110, about 0.27, so glucose is consumed or excluded, which rules against viral meningitis. Step 2: lymphocytes rather than neutrophils, plus a subacute course, point away from pyogenic bacteria. Step 3: very high protein and a cranial nerve palsy fit a basal exudate, favoring tuberculous meningitis. Cryptococcal antigen and acid-fast testing separate the two remaining options."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does CSF glucose stay normal in viral meningitis but fall in tuberculous meningitis, when both are lymphocytic?","explain":"Glucose falls when the inflamed barrier limits GLUT1 transport and when organisms, leukocytes, and inflamed tissue consume it. Tuberculous meningitis has an intense granulomatous basal exudate that does both; viral meningitis causes milder inflammation with little glucose use.","check":"The paragraph on the three ways meningitis changes CSF."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the rules. The periosteal dura bound to bone at sutures shapes an epidural clot, the meningeal dura folds into the falx and tentorium that channel herniation, and differential growth of the vertebral column leaves a lumbar cistern below the conus where a needle can enter safely. Physiology explains pressure: CSF is secreted at a nearly constant rate and absorbed by pressure-driven bulk flow, so any block or added mass inside a rigid skull raises pressure, lowers perfusion, and produces the Cushing triad. Biochemistry and cell biology explain the barriers, with tight junctions, GLUT1, LAT1, and P-glycoprotein deciding what reaches neurons."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology, immunology, and pathology meet in the CSF panel: the type of organism determines whether neutrophils or lymphocytes are recruited, and the intensity of inflammation determines how far glucose falls and protein rises. CSF values overlap between causes and change with timing and prior antibiotics, and a lumbar puncture in the presence of a mass lesion can itself cause herniation, so the panel is read with the clinical course and imaging."}],"summary":["The epidural space is potential in the skull and real in the spinal canal; the subdural space is a split in the dural border cell layer; the subarachnoid space is real and holds CSF and the major surface vessels.","Epidural hematoma is arterial (middle meningeal artery at the pterion), lens shaped, and stops at sutures; subdural hematoma is venous (bridging veins), crescentic, and crosses sutures but not the falx.","Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage is usually a ruptured berry aneurysm, often near the anterior communicating artery, with blood and later xanthochromia in the CSF and vasospasm days later.","In a rigid skull, pressure rises steeply once CSF and venous reserves are used; perfusion pressure equals MAP minus ICP, and medullary ischemia produces the Cushing triad.","Subfalcine herniation compresses the anterior cerebral artery; uncal herniation compresses CN III (pupil first), the posterior cerebral artery, and the cerebral peduncle; tonsillar herniation compresses the medulla.","CSF flows from choroid plexus through the ventricles and apertures to the subarachnoid space and returns through arachnoid granulations to the superior sagittal sinus.","The adult cord ends at about L1 to L2 and the dural sac at about S2, so lumbar puncture at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5 enters the lumbar cistern below the cord.","The blood-brain barrier is endothelial tight junctions; the blood-CSF barrier is choroid epithelial tight junctions; circumventricular organs such as the area postrema and posterior pituitary lack a barrier.","Bacterial meningitis gives neutrophils, high protein, and low glucose; viral gives lymphocytes with normal glucose; fungal and tuberculous give lymphocytes with low glucose and high protein.","Guillain-Barré syndrome raises CSF protein without raising cells (albuminocytologic dissociation) because the inflammation is in nerve roots, not the meninges."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, cranial meninges and dural venous sinuses; Back, spinal cord and meninges"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Neuronal Microenvironment"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue and the Nervous System, sections on meninges, the blood-brain barrier, and choroid plexus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on herniation, trauma, cerebrovascular disease, and infections"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-5":{"unit":"NE-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01","title":"Sensory Receptors, Pathways, and Reflexes","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows somatic sensation from the receptor to the cortex. It covers how receptors transduce touch, vibration, stretch, pain, and temperature, how the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways carry those signals and where each crosses, and how spinal reflexes use the same afferents. It then explains referred pain, how the dorsal horn and brainstem turn pain up or down, neuropathic pain, and the three common primary headaches. Board questions usually describe a pattern of sensory loss, an abnormal reflex, or a pain distribution and ask you to localize the lesion or name the mechanism. If you know which fibers carry what and where they cross, most of these questions become reasoning rather than recall.","prerequisites":["Resting membrane potential, graded potentials, and action potential generation (NE-13)","Spinal cord cross-sectional anatomy: gray horns, white columns, and the anterior white commissure (NE-3)","Dermatomes and the spinal nerve roots (NE-8)","Thalamic nuclei and the location of the postcentral gyrus (NE-3)","Skeletal muscle structure and the motor unit (MS-10)"],"connections":[{"to":"Brain and spinal cord structures (NE-3)","how":"The position of each tract in the cord and brainstem is what turns a sensory exam into a lesion level."},{"to":"CNS blood supply (NE-10) and vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Medullary, thalamic, and cortical strokes produce sensory losses that follow the pathways in this chapter."},{"to":"Nutritional neurological disease (NE-20-D) and CNS infections (NE-20-G)","how":"B12 deficiency and tabes dorsalis damage the dorsal columns, giving loss of vibration and position sense with a positive Romberg sign."},{"to":"Neurological pathogens (NE-19)","how":"Varicella zoster virus latent in sensory ganglia explains dermatomal shingles and postherpetic neuralgia."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and gastrointestinal systems","how":"Referred pain from the heart, diaphragm, gallbladder, and appendix follows the spinal segments that receive their visceral afferents."}],"terms":[{"term":"Receptor (generator) potential","def":"A graded depolarization of a sensory ending produced by its stimulus that triggers action potentials when it reaches threshold."},{"term":"Adaptation","def":"The decline in a receptor's firing during a constant stimulus; rapid adaptation signals change and slow adaptation signals a steady state."},{"term":"Receptive field","def":"The area of skin or tissue from which a stimulus changes the firing of a given sensory neuron."},{"term":"Intrafusal fiber","def":"A small specialized muscle fiber inside a muscle spindle, innervated by gamma motor neurons, that carries the spindle's sensory endings."},{"term":"Decussation","def":"The crossing of a pathway's axons from one side of the nervous system to the other."},{"term":"Dissociated sensory loss","def":"Loss of pain and temperature with touch, vibration, and position preserved, or the reverse, because the modalities travel in different tracts."},{"term":"Reciprocal inhibition","def":"Inhibition of the antagonist muscle through an interneuron while the agonist is reflexly excited."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain from a deep or visceral structure felt in a body surface area served by the same spinal segments."},{"term":"Allodynia","def":"Pain produced by a stimulus that is normally not painful, such as light touch."},{"term":"Hyperalgesia","def":"An exaggerated pain response to a stimulus that is normally painful."},{"term":"Neuropathic pain","def":"Pain caused by a lesion or disease of the somatosensory nervous system itself rather than by ongoing tissue injury."},{"term":"Cortical spreading depression","def":"A slowly advancing wave of neuronal and glial depolarization followed by suppressed activity that underlies migraine aura."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C01","obj":"NE-5.1","title":"Sensory receptors, adaptation, and afferent fiber types","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A sensory receptor is a transducer. Its stimulus opens mechanically, thermally, or chemically gated cation channels in the nerve ending (for light touch, mechanically gated channels such as Piezo2), producing a graded receptor potential. When that potential brings the first node of the afferent axon to threshold, action potentials fire at a rate that rises with stimulus strength. The type of stimulus is coded by which receptor and pathway is active (the labeled line principle), intensity by firing rate and the number of receptors recruited, and location by the receptor's receptive field. Small, dense receptive fields in the fingertips give fine two-point discrimination."},{"type":"h","text":"Adaptation"},{"type":"p","text":"Rapidly adapting (phasic) receptors fire at the onset and offset of a stimulus, so they signal change, movement, and vibration. Slowly adapting (tonic) receptors keep firing for as long as the stimulus lasts, so they signal steady pressure, skin stretch, position, and length. The Pacinian corpuscle shows how structure sets adaptation: its terminal is wrapped in concentric fluid-filled lamellae, and during sustained pressure the fluid redistributes so the terminal is no longer deformed, while the terminal itself also accommodates. Only rapid changes reach the nerve, which is why it detects vibration."},{"type":"table","title":"Somatic receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Best stimulus","Adaptation","Afferent fiber"],"rows":[["Meissner corpuscle","Dermal papillae of hairless (glabrous) skin","Light touch, low-frequency vibration (flutter)","Rapid","Aβ"],["Pacinian corpuscle","Deep dermis, subcutis, joint capsules","High-frequency vibration (best near 250 Hz), rapid pressure change","Very rapid","Aβ"],["Merkel disc","Basal epidermis","Sustained pressure, texture, edges","Slow","Aβ"],["Ruffini ending","Dermis and joint capsules","Skin stretch, sustained joint position","Slow","Aβ"],["Free nerve ending","Nearly all tissues","Pain, temperature, itch, crude touch","Slow; nociceptors may sensitize","Aδ and C"],["Muscle spindle","Within muscle, parallel to extrafusal fibers","Muscle length and rate of length change","Ia: rate and length; II: length (slow)","Ia and II"],["Golgi tendon organ","Musculotendinous junction, in series","Muscle tension, especially active contraction","Slow","Ib"]],"caption":"Encapsulated skin receptors use Aβ fibers and differ in adaptation and depth."},{"type":"h","text":"Pain and temperature endings"},{"type":"p","text":"Nociceptors and thermoreceptors are free nerve endings. Nociceptors adapt little and can sensitize: bradykinin, prostaglandins, and other inflammatory mediators lower their threshold, producing hyperalgesia around an injury. The heat and capsaicin channel TRPV1 opens above about 43 °C (109 °F), and TRPM8 responds to cooling and menthol. Thinly myelinated Aδ fibers carry fast, sharp, well-localized first pain and cold; unmyelinated C fibers carry slow, burning, poorly localized second pain, warmth, and itch."},{"type":"h","text":"Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs"},{"type":"p","text":"A spindle contains intrafusal fibers (nuclear bag and nuclear chain) lying in parallel with the working extrafusal fibers. Primary (Ia) endings report length and the velocity of stretch; secondary (group II) endings report mainly steady length. Gamma motor neurons contract the striated poles of the intrafusal fibers, and because alpha and gamma neurons are activated together, the spindle stays taut and sensitive while the muscle shortens. The Golgi tendon organ lies in series, its Ib endings woven between collagen strands at the musculotendinous junction. Tension straightens the strands and compresses the endings, so active contraction excites it strongly, while passive stretch of a relaxed muscle mainly excites the spindle."},{"type":"table","title":"Afferent and efferent fiber classes","cols":["Class","Myelin","Diameter","Velocity","Carries"],"rows":[["Aα (Ia, Ib)","Heavy","About 12 to 20 µm","About 70 to 120 m/s","Spindle primary, tendon organ; alpha motor neurons"],["Aβ (II)","Moderate","About 6 to 12 µm","About 30 to 70 m/s","Touch, vibration, pressure; spindle secondary endings"],["Aγ","Moderate","About 3 to 6 µm","About 15 to 30 m/s","Gamma motor neurons to intrafusal fibers (efferent)"],["Aδ (III)","Thin","About 1 to 5 µm","About 5 to 30 m/s","Fast sharp pain, cold"],["C (IV)","None","About 0.2 to 1.5 µm","About 0.5 to 2 m/s","Slow burning pain, warmth, itch; postganglionic autonomic"]],"caption":"Letter and Roman numeral classes; larger, more myelinated fibers conduct faster."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C02","obj":"NE-5.2","title":"Dorsal column medial lemniscus and spinothalamic pathways","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Somatic sensation from the body reaches the cortex by two three-neuron pathways. In both, the first-order neuron's cell body is in the dorsal root ganglion, the second-order neuron crosses the midline, and the third-order neuron in the ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus of the thalamus projects through the posterior limb of the internal capsule to the primary somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, areas 3, 1, and 2). Sensation from the face uses trigeminal equivalents that relay in the ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus."},{"type":"h","text":"Dorsal column medial lemniscus (DCML)"},{"type":"p","text":"Large Aβ fibers carrying fine touch, vibration, two-point discrimination, and conscious proprioception ascend on the same side without synapsing. Fibers from below about T6 (lower limb and lower trunk) form the medial fasciculus gracilis; fibers from T6 and above (upper limb) add laterally as the fasciculus cuneatus. They synapse in the nuclei gracilis and cuneatus of the caudal medulla, and the second-order axons sweep across as internal arcuate fibers (the sensory decussation) to form the contralateral medial lemniscus."},{"type":"flow","title":"DCML pathway","steps":["Receptor, Aβ fiber, cell body in dorsal root ganglion","Ascends ipsilaterally in fasciculus gracilis or cuneatus","Synapse in nucleus gracilis or cuneatus, caudal medulla","Crosses as internal arcuate fibers","Rises as contralateral medial lemniscus to VPL","Internal capsule to postcentral gyrus"],"caption":"The DCML stays ipsilateral throughout the spinal cord and crosses in the lower medulla."},{"type":"h","text":"Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)"},{"type":"p","text":"Aδ and C fibers carrying pain, temperature, and crude touch enter laterally and may run up or down one or two segments in the dorsolateral tract of Lissauer before synapsing in the dorsal horn (laminae I, II, and V). Second-order axons cross within one or two segments in the anterior white commissure and ascend in the contralateral anterolateral white matter. Fibers carrying fast Aδ pain run directly to VPL and allow localization. Slow C fiber pain also projects to the reticular formation, periaqueductal gray, and intralaminar thalamic nuclei, which explains its arousing, unpleasant quality. Because each new segment adds fibers medially, sacral fibers lie most lateral and cervical fibers most medial."},{"type":"flow","title":"Spinothalamic pathway","steps":["Free nerve ending, Aδ or C fiber, dorsal root ganglion","Lissauer tract, synapse in dorsal horn","Crosses in anterior white commissure within 1 to 2 segments","Ascends in contralateral anterolateral column","Synapse in VPL (and reticular, intralaminar targets)","Internal capsule to postcentral gyrus"],"caption":"The spinothalamic tract crosses almost at once, so in the cord it carries information from the opposite side."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting sensory loss from lesion level","cols":["Lesion","Touch, vibration, position","Pain and temperature","Example"],"rows":[["Dorsal root","Ipsilateral, dermatomal","Ipsilateral, dermatomal","Disc herniation compressing a root"],["Both dorsal columns","Lost bilaterally below lesion","Spared","B12 deficiency, tabes dorsalis"],["Cord hemisection","Ipsilateral below lesion","Contralateral, from 1 to 2 segments below","Brown-Séquard (C03)"],["Central cord, crossing fibers","Spared","Bilateral, only at involved segments","Syringomyelia (C03)"],["Lateral medulla","Spared","Contralateral body and ipsilateral face","Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) infarct"],["Medial medulla","Contralateral body","Spared","Medial medullary infarct"],["Pons, thalamus, internal capsule","Contralateral","Contralateral","Lacunar or thalamic stroke"],["Postcentral gyrus","Contralateral; leg if medial, face and arm if lateral","Contralateral, often milder","ACA or MCA territory infarct"]],"caption":"Below the medulla the tracts carry opposite sides; above it both carry the contralateral body."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a crossed sensory loss","text":"A patient has lost pain and temperature on the left face and the right arm, trunk, and leg; touch and vibration are normal. A crossed face and body pattern means the lesion lies where the face fibers have not crossed but the body fibers have: the lower brainstem. Trigeminal pain fibers descend ipsilaterally in the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus in the lateral medulla, beside the already crossed spinothalamic tract carrying the right body. The medial lemniscus lies medially, so it is spared. The lesion is in the left lateral medulla (vertebral or PICA territory)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C03","obj":"NE-5.3","title":"Brown-Séquard hemisection and syringomyelia","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Brown-Séquard syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"A hemisection cuts every tract on one half of the cord, usually from penetrating trauma or a tumor. The lateral corticospinal tract has already crossed at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, so weakness is ipsilateral, with upper motor neuron signs below the lesion (spasticity, hyperreflexia, an extensor plantar response) once spinal shock passes. The dorsal columns have not yet crossed, so vibration, position, and fine touch are lost ipsilaterally. The spinothalamic tract has already crossed, so pain and temperature are lost contralaterally, starting one or two segments below the lesion, because opposite-side fibers entering just below it rise one or two segments on their own side and cross above the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"At the level of the lesion itself, the damaged dorsal root entry zone and ventral horn cause a narrow ipsilateral band of loss of all sensation with lower motor neuron signs in that segment. A lesion at or above T1 can also interrupt descending sympathetic fibers on their way to the T1 to T2 preganglionic neurons, adding an ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in Brown-Séquard syndrome","cols":["Finding","Tract or structure","Side","Extent"],"rows":[["Spastic weakness, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign","Lateral corticospinal (crossed in medulla)","Ipsilateral","Below lesion"],["Loss of vibration, position, fine touch","Dorsal column (crosses in medulla)","Ipsilateral","Below lesion"],["Loss of pain and temperature","Spinothalamic (crossed in cord)","Contralateral","From 1 to 2 segments below lesion"],["Band of total anesthesia, flaccid weakness","Dorsal root entry zone, ventral horn","Ipsilateral","At lesion level"],["Horner syndrome","Descending sympathetic fibers","Ipsilateral","Lesion at or above T1"]],"caption":"Motor and dorsal column signs share a side; pain and temperature loss is opposite."},{"type":"h","text":"Syringomyelia"},{"type":"p","text":"A syrinx is a fluid-filled cavity in the central cord, most often cervical, commonly associated with a Chiari I malformation or following trauma. It first destroys the spinothalamic fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure from both sides. The result is bilateral loss of pain and temperature limited to the segments involved, classically a cape over the shoulders and arms, with touch, vibration, and position preserved because the dorsal columns lie outside the cavity. Below the cavity sensation is normal, since fibers from lower segments crossed lower down. Painless burns on the hands are typical."},{"type":"p","text":"As the cavity enlarges it reaches the ventral horns, causing lower motor neuron signs (wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles, lost arm reflexes), then the lateral corticospinal tracts, causing upper motor neuron signs in the legs. Extension into the lateral horn at T1 can add Horner syndrome, and loss of protective pain sensation can lead to neuropathic (Charcot) joints of the shoulder or elbow."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out a hemisection","text":"A man is stabbed in the back. His right leg is weak with brisk reflexes and an extensor plantar response, and he cannot feel vibration or position in the right foot. Pinprick and temperature are lost on the left from about the umbilicus down, with a narrow band of total loss on the right just above. Ipsilateral weakness and dorsal column loss put the lesion on the right. The contralateral pinprick loss begins near T10, and spinothalamic loss starts one or two segments below the lesion, so the lesion is about T8 to T9, matching the right-sided band. Diagnosis: right hemisection near T9 (Brown-Séquard)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a syrinx spanning C5 to T1 spare pain sensation in the legs, while a hemisection at C6 removes it from the opposite leg?","explain":"The syrinx damages only fibers crossing at C5 to T1; leg fibers crossed in the lumbosacral cord and ascend in the anterolateral columns outside the cavity. A hemisection cuts the ascending tract itself, which carries all fibers from the opposite side below the lesion.","check":"The crossing point of the spinothalamic tract and the somatotopy of the anterolateral column in C02."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C04","obj":"NE-5.4","title":"Spinal reflexes and reflex root levels","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A reflex arc has a receptor, an afferent neuron, one or more central synapses, an efferent neuron, and an effector. Spinal reflexes run without the brain, but descending pathways constantly adjust their gain, which is why reflex testing examines both the arc and its supervision."},{"type":"h","text":"Stretch (myotatic) reflex"},{"type":"p","text":"Tapping a tendon briefly stretches the muscle and its spindles. Ia afferents fire and synapse directly on alpha motor neurons of the same muscle and its synergists, making this the only monosynaptic reflex. Collaterals excite Ia inhibitory interneurons that suppress the antagonist (reciprocal inhibition). The reflex is negative feedback on muscle length: stretch causes contraction that restores length. Gamma motor neuron activity sets spindle sensitivity, so it raises or lowers reflex gain."},{"type":"flow","title":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex","steps":["Tendon tap stretches muscle and spindle","Ia afferent fires, enters dorsal root","Direct synapse on alpha motor neuron of same muscle","Ia inhibitory interneuron relaxes antagonist","Muscle contracts, restoring length (reflex jerk)"],"caption":"One central synapse between afferent and efferent, with a parallel disynaptic path inhibiting the antagonist."},{"type":"h","text":"Golgi tendon organ, withdrawal, and crossed extensor reflexes"},{"type":"p","text":"In the Golgi tendon organ (Ib, autogenic inhibition) reflex, rising tension excites Ib afferents, which act through inhibitory interneurons to reduce firing of the same muscle's alpha motor neurons and excite the antagonist. It regulates force and helps prevent excessive tension. The withdrawal (flexor) reflex is polysynaptic: nociceptor input excites ipsilateral flexors and inhibits extensors, pulling the limb away, and reverberating interneuron circuits keep it going briefly after the stimulus ends. Commissural interneurons simultaneously excite contralateral extensors and inhibit contralateral flexors, so the opposite leg stiffens to bear weight (crossed extensor reflex)."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of spinal reflexes","cols":["Reflex","Receptor and afferent","Central pathway","Effect"],"rows":[["Stretch","Spindle, Ia","Monosynaptic to alpha motor neuron","Same muscle contracts; antagonist inhibited"],["Golgi tendon organ","Tendon organ, Ib","Disynaptic through inhibitory interneuron","Same muscle relaxes; antagonist excited"],["Withdrawal","Nociceptors, Aδ and C","Polysynaptic, several segments","Ipsilateral flexion, extensors inhibited"],["Crossed extensor","Same input as withdrawal","Polysynaptic, crosses through commissural interneurons","Contralateral extension"]],"caption":"Only the stretch reflex is monosynaptic; the others use interneurons and spread across segments."},{"type":"table","title":"Deep tendon reflexes tested on examination","cols":["Reflex","Main root","Roots involved","Nerve and muscle"],"rows":[["Biceps","C5","C5 to C6","Musculocutaneous; biceps brachii"],["Brachioradialis","C6","C5 to C6","Radial; brachioradialis"],["Triceps","C7","C7 to C8","Radial; triceps brachii"],["Patellar","L4","L3 to L4","Femoral; quadriceps femoris"],["Achilles","S1","S1 to S2","Tibial; gastrocnemius and soleus"]],"caption":"A root lesion weakens or abolishes the reflex it serves; an S1 radiculopathy removes the ankle jerk while the knee jerk is preserved."},{"type":"h","text":"Reflexes in disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Any break in the arc (peripheral neuropathy, radiculopathy, ventral horn disease, neuromuscular disease) reduces or abolishes the reflex. An upper motor neuron lesion removes descending inhibition of alpha and gamma motor neurons, so after the acute phase of spinal shock, stretch reflexes become brisk, spread to neighboring muscles, and may show clonus, while the polysynaptic plantar reflex reverts to the extensor (Babinski) response."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a C6 cord injury has absent biceps reflexes but brisk patellar and Achilles reflexes. Explain both findings with one lesion.","explain":"At C5 to C6 the injury destroys the reflex arc itself (ventral horn and roots), a lower motor neuron pattern, so the biceps reflex is lost. Below the lesion the lumbar and sacral arcs are intact but have lost descending inhibition, an upper motor neuron pattern, so they are exaggerated.","check":"The reflex root table and the Reflexes in disease paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C05","obj":"NE-5.5","title":"Visceral and referred pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Visceral pain differs from somatic pain. Viscera are sparsely innervated, mostly by C fibers that travel with sympathetic nerves (and, for the pelvic organs, with pelvic splanchnic nerves) to the dorsal root ganglia. Cutting or burning the gut causes little pain, while distension, ischemia, inflammation, and traction on the mesentery are strong stimuli. Because the afferents are few, overlap several segments, and often arrive from both sides, visceral pain is dull, poorly localized, often felt in the midline, and accompanied by autonomic signs such as nausea, sweating, and pallor. When disease spreads to the parietal pleura or peritoneum, which have somatic innervation, pain becomes sharp and well localized."},{"type":"h","text":"Convergence and projection"},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral afferents enter the cord at the segments that supplied the organ during development and synapse on the same dorsal horn projection neurons (many in lamina V) that receive skin afferents from those dermatomes. The brain has learned to read activity in these shared neurons as coming from the skin, which supplies far more input, so it projects visceral pain onto the matching dermatomes. Referral therefore follows spinal segments, not the organ's position in the body."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart develops in the neck and upper thorax, and its afferents return with the cardiac sympathetic nerves to about T1 to T4. Ischemic pain is therefore felt as substernal pressure spreading to the left chest and the medial left arm (T1 to T2 dermatomes). The central diaphragm, along with the mediastinal pleura and pericardium, is sensory-supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5), so blood, pus, or inflammation under the diaphragm or around the heart is felt at the shoulder tip, which the supraclavicular nerves (C3 to C4) supply. The peripheral diaphragm is supplied by lower intercostal nerves and refers to the adjacent chest and abdominal wall."},{"type":"table","title":"Common referral patterns","cols":["Source","Afferent pathway and segments","Where pain is felt"],"rows":[["Heart (ischemia)","Cardiac sympathetic afferents, about T1 to T4","Substernal, left chest, medial left arm; often neck and jaw"],["Central diaphragm, pericardium","Phrenic nerve, C3 to C5","Shoulder tip and lower neck (C3 to C4 dermatomes)"],["Gallbladder","Greater splanchnic, about T6 to T9","Epigastrium and right upper quadrant; right shoulder if diaphragm irritated"],["Appendix (early)","Lesser splanchnic, about T10","Periumbilical, then right lower quadrant when parietal peritoneum is inflamed"]],"caption":"Visceral pain is felt over dermatomes of the segments that receive the organ's afferents; spread to parietal serosa makes it local and sharp."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C06","obj":"NE-5.6","title":"Pain modulation and neuropathic pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pain is not a fixed readout of tissue damage. Its main relay, the dorsal horn, is a site where incoming nociceptive signals are amplified or suppressed by local circuits and by pathways descending from the brainstem."},{"type":"h","text":"Gate control"},{"type":"p","text":"In the gate control model (Melzack and Wall), collaterals of large Aβ touch fibers excite inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa (lamina II), which release GABA, glycine, or enkephalin onto spinothalamic projection neurons and reduce their response to Aδ and C input. Activity in small pain fibers inhibits these interneurons and opens the gate. This explains why rubbing an injured area or applying transcutaneous electrical stimulation can reduce pain."},{"type":"h","text":"Descending inhibition and endogenous opioids"},{"type":"p","text":"The periaqueductal gray (PAG) of the midbrain receives input from the cortex, amygdala, and hypothalamus. It excites the rostral ventromedial medulla, including the serotonergic nucleus raphe magnus, and noradrenergic neurons of the pons such as the locus coeruleus. Their axons descend in the dorsolateral funiculus to the dorsal horn, where serotonin and norepinephrine (through alpha-2 receptors) inhibit projection neurons and activate local enkephalin interneurons. This system underlies stress-induced analgesia."},{"type":"p","text":"Endogenous opioids come from three precursors: beta-endorphin from pro-opiomelanocortin (mainly mu receptors), enkephalins from proenkephalin (mainly delta), and dynorphins from prodynorphin (mainly kappa). All three receptors couple to Gi/o proteins. Presynaptically they close voltage-gated Ca2+ channels on nociceptor terminals, reducing release of glutamate and substance P; postsynaptically they open G protein-gated inwardly rectifying K+ channels, hyperpolarizing the projection neuron. In the PAG, opioids inhibit GABAergic interneurons, which disinhibits the descending pathway."},{"type":"flow","title":"Descending pain inhibition","steps":["Cortex, amygdala, hypothalamus activate the PAG","Opioids in PAG remove local GABA inhibition","PAG excites raphe magnus (serotonin) and locus coeruleus (norepinephrine)","Fibers descend in dorsolateral funiculus to dorsal horn","Enkephalin interneurons and monoamines inhibit pain transmission","Less nociceptive signal ascends in spinothalamic tract"],"caption":"Brainstem control of the dorsal horn; opioids act both in the PAG and at the spinal synapse."},{"type":"h","text":"Sensitization and neuropathic pain"},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated C fiber input releases glutamate and substance P; the sustained depolarization removes the Mg2+ block of NMDA receptors on dorsal horn neurons, progressively increasing their output (wind-up) and producing central sensitization, so touch carried by Aβ fibers can be felt as pain (allodynia). Neuropathic pain arises when the somatosensory system itself is damaged. Injured axons accumulate Na+ channels and fire ectopically, dorsal horn inhibitory interneurons are lost, and central maps reorganize, so pain persists without ongoing tissue injury. It is typically burning, shooting, or electric, and often coexists with reduced sensation in the same area."},{"type":"table","title":"Neuropathic pain syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Site of injury","Mechanism","Typical features"],"rows":[["Postherpetic neuralgia","Dorsal root or trigeminal ganglion and nerve","Varicella zoster reactivation damages sensory neurons, leaving ectopic firing and central sensitization","Pain persisting 3 months or more after zoster in the same dermatome; allodynia; risk rises with age"],["Phantom limb pain","Severed peripheral nerves and their central maps","Neuroma discharge, central sensitization, cortical reorganization","Pain felt in the missing limb; stump stimulation may trigger it"],["Thalamic pain (Dejerine-Roussy)","VPL nucleus of the thalamus, usually infarct","Loss of normal thalamic processing with abnormal spontaneous activity","Initial contralateral sensory loss, later burning pain and allodynia on the same side"]],"caption":"All three are pain caused by injury to the nervous system rather than activation of nociceptors by tissue damage."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can thalamic pain be severe in a body region where the patient has lost the ability to feel pinprick?","explain":"The infarct removes the normal relay, so pinprick no longer reaches the cortex, but surviving thalamic and cortical neurons become hyperexcitable and fire spontaneously, generating pain that does not depend on a peripheral stimulus.","check":"The mechanisms of central sensitization and the neuropathic pain table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C07","obj":"NE-5.7","title":"Migraine, cluster, and tension headache","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Brain parenchyma has no pain receptors. Headache arises from the meninges, dural venous sinuses, and large intracranial and meningeal arteries, together with scalp muscles and other extracranial tissues. Structures above the tentorium are supplied mainly by the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1), so their pain is felt in the front of the head; those in the posterior fossa are supplied mainly by the C2 and C3 nerves, so their pain is felt at the occiput and neck. Trigeminal and C2 to C3 afferents converge in the trigeminocervical complex of the lower brainstem and upper cord."},{"type":"h","text":"Migraine"},{"type":"p","text":"Migraine is a recurrent headache lasting about 4 to 72 hours that is often unilateral and pulsating, moderate to severe, worsened by routine activity, and accompanied by nausea or by photophobia and phonophobia. It is about three times more common in adult women. Trigeminovascular activation releases calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) and substance P from trigeminal fibers around meningeal vessels, causing vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation. Sensitized trigeminal neurons then turn normal arterial pulsation into throbbing pain, and central sensitization can make the scalp painful to touch. About a third of patients have an aura, typically visual (a spreading scintillating scotoma), lasting about 5 to 60 minutes. It reflects cortical spreading depression moving across the cortex at about 3 mm per minute, which is why symptoms march gradually instead of appearing at once. Rare familial hemiplegic migraine is caused by mutations in ion channel and pump genes such as CACNA1A."},{"type":"flow","title":"Migraine mechanism","steps":["Hypothalamic and brainstem changes, with or without cortical spreading depression","Trigeminal afferents around meningeal vessels activated","CGRP and substance P released","Meningeal vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation","Peripheral then central trigeminal sensitization","Throbbing pain, nausea, photophobia, allodynia"],"caption":"Trigeminovascular model of migraine; aura is a cortical event that can precede the pain."},{"type":"h","text":"Cluster and tension headache"},{"type":"p","text":"Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia: severe, strictly unilateral orbital or temporal pain lasting about 15 to 180 minutes, recurring up to several times a day in bouts of weeks, often at the same time of night, and more common in men. Imaging during attacks shows activation of the posterior hypothalamus, which fits the circadian timing. Trigeminal pain activates a reflex parasympathetic outflow from the superior salivatory nucleus through the facial nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion, producing ipsilateral tearing, conjunctival injection, nasal congestion, and rhinorrhea. Swelling around the internal carotid artery can compress its sympathetic plexus, adding ipsilateral ptosis and miosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Tension-type headache, the most common primary headache, is bilateral, pressing or band-like, mild to moderate, and not worsened by routine activity. Nausea is absent and at most one of photophobia or phonophobia is present. Its mechanism is less defined; tender pericranial muscles and, in the chronic form, central sensitization of pain pathways are thought to contribute."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the primary headaches","cols":["Feature","Migraine","Cluster","Tension-type"],"rows":[["Location","Often unilateral","Strictly unilateral, orbital or temporal","Bilateral, band-like"],["Quality and severity","Pulsating, moderate to severe","Boring, very severe","Pressing, mild to moderate"],["Duration","About 4 to 72 hours","About 15 to 180 minutes, in bouts","About 30 minutes to 7 days"],["Associated features","Nausea, photophobia, phonophobia, aura in about a third","Ipsilateral tearing, rhinorrhea, ptosis, miosis","No nausea; at most one of photophobia or phonophobia"],["Behavior","Lies still in a dark room; worse with activity","Restless, pacing","Continues activities"],["Sex","Women more often","Men more often","Slight female predominance"],["Mechanism","Trigeminovascular activation, CGRP","Hypothalamic activation, trigeminal autonomic reflex","Pericranial muscle tenderness, sensitization"]],"caption":"Duration, laterality, autonomic signs, and behavior during an attack separate the three."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology meet in localization. Receptor type decides fiber size, fiber size decides the tract, and each tract's crossing point decides whether a lesion produces ipsilateral or contralateral loss. The same afferents serve reflexes, so a spindle Ia fiber appears in both the patellar reflex and position sense, and dorsal column and dorsal root diseases (B12 deficiency, tabes dorsalis from Treponema pallidum) cause both sensory ataxia with a positive Romberg sign and reduced reflexes. Embryology explains referred pain: organs keep the segmental innervation of the region where they formed, so the heart refers to T1 to T4 and the diaphragm, which arises in the cervical region, refers to C3 to C5."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry, microbiology, and pathology explain how the system is modulated and injured. Glutamate, substance P, and CGRP carry the pain signal, while GABA, glycine, enkephalins, serotonin, and norepinephrine suppress it, and the same Gi/o signaling explains endogenous and exogenous opioid analgesia. Varicella zoster virus, held latent in sensory ganglia by T cell immunity, reactivates as immunity wanes with age and leaves neuropathic pain behind. Patterns in this chapter are classic forms; real lesions are often incomplete, so a partial hemisection or a syrinx at an early stage may show only some findings."}],"summary":["Receptors transduce stimuli into graded receptor potentials; modality is coded by labeled lines, intensity by firing rate and recruitment.","Meissner and Pacinian corpuscles adapt rapidly and signal change and vibration; Merkel discs and Ruffini endings adapt slowly and signal steady pressure and stretch; all use Aβ fibers.","Aδ fibers carry fast pain and cold; C fibers carry slow burning pain, warmth, and itch.","Spindles lie in parallel and sense length (Ia, II); Golgi tendon organs lie in series and sense tension (Ib).","The DCML crosses in the caudal medulla; the spinothalamic tract crosses within 1 to 2 segments in the anterior white commissure; both relay in VPL to the postcentral gyrus.","Hemisection gives ipsilateral weakness and dorsal column loss with contralateral pain and temperature loss; a syrinx gives bilateral segmental pain and temperature loss with touch preserved.","The stretch reflex is the only monosynaptic reflex; biceps C5, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1.","Referred pain reflects convergence of visceral and skin afferents on shared dorsal horn neurons: heart to left arm (T1 to T4), diaphragm to shoulder (C3 to C5).","Pain is reduced by Aβ gating and by PAG-driven serotonergic, noradrenergic, and opioid inhibition of the dorsal horn; neuropathic pain arises from injury to the nervous system itself.","Migraine is trigeminovascular and CGRP mediated, with aura from cortical spreading depression; cluster headache adds ipsilateral autonomic signs; tension-type headache is bilateral and band-like."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory Receptors, Neuronal Circuits for Processing Information"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: I. General Organization, the Tactile and Position Senses"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: II. Pain, Headache, and Thermal Sensations"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory transduction and the somatic sensory system"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, malformations including syringomyelia and Chiari malformation"}]},"NE-6":{"unit":"NE-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01","title":"Special Senses: Vision, Hearing, Balance, Taste, and Smell","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows each special sense from its receptor to the cortex: the visual pathway and the pupil, phototransduction, the fluids and glands that keep the eye working, hearing, balance, taste, and smell. Board questions rarely ask for a pathway in isolation. They give a field defect, a pupil exam, a tuning fork result, a direction of nystagmus, or a lost sense after head trauma and ask you to name the lesion and explain the finding. Each of these answers comes from knowing where fibers cross or converge, which ion carries the receptor current, and how the two sides of the brain are compared.","prerequisites":["G protein coupled receptor signaling and second messengers (cAMP, cGMP, IP3)","Resting membrane potential and the Nernst equation, especially for K+","Brainstem and thalamic nuclei and the cortical lobes (NE-3)","Cranial nerve nuclei, ganglia, and foramina (NE-8)","Parasympathetic and sympathetic outflow to the head (NE-9)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cranial and spinal nerves (NE-8)","how":"Facial nerve lesions at different levels change tearing, taste, and the stapedius reflex, so the special senses localize a CN VII injury."},{"to":"CNS blood supply (NE-10) and vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Middle and posterior cerebral artery strokes produce the quadrantanopias and macular sparing hemianopia described here."},{"to":"Neurological biochemistry and nutrition (NE-14)","how":"Vitamin A supplies the retinal of rhodopsin, so deficiency causes night blindness before it damages the cornea."},{"to":"Endocrine interactions (NE-17) and neoplasms (NE-20-F)","how":"Pituitary adenomas and craniopharyngiomas reach the optic chiasm and announce themselves with bitemporal field loss."},{"to":"Diseases of the special senses (NE-20-J)","how":"Glaucoma, presbycusis, Meniere disease, and benign paroxysmal positional vertigo are disorders of the normal mechanisms taught in this chapter."}],"terms":[{"term":"Homonymous hemianopia","def":"Loss of the same half of the visual field (right or left) in both eyes, produced by any complete lesion behind the optic chiasm."},{"term":"Quadrantanopia","def":"Loss of one quarter of the visual field, usually homonymous, from a partial lesion of the optic radiations."},{"term":"Relative afferent pupillary defect","def":"A weaker pupillary response to light shone in one eye than in the other, caused by asymmetric optic nerve or severe retinal disease."},{"term":"Light-near dissociation","def":"Pupils that constrict for near focus but poorly or not at all to light."},{"term":"Phototransduction","def":"The G protein cascade by which light absorbed in a photoreceptor closes cGMP-gated cation channels and hyperpolarizes the cell."},{"term":"Dark current","def":"The steady inward cation current through open cGMP-gated channels that keeps photoreceptors depolarized in darkness."},{"term":"Endocochlear potential","def":"The positive potential of about +80 mV in the K+-rich endolymph of the scala media, generated by the stria vascularis."},{"term":"Tonotopy","def":"The orderly mapping of sound frequency to place, high frequencies at the cochlear base and low frequencies at the apex, kept throughout the auditory pathway."},{"term":"Conductive hearing loss","def":"Hearing loss from failure to carry sound through the external or middle ear to the cochlea."},{"term":"Sensorineural hearing loss","def":"Hearing loss from damage to cochlear hair cells, the cochlear nerve, or its central connections."},{"term":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex","def":"A three-neuron brainstem reflex that moves the eyes opposite to head rotation by an equal amount, keeping the image stable on the retina."},{"term":"Nystagmus","def":"Rhythmic eye movement with a slow drift in one direction and a corrective fast phase in the other, named by the direction of the fast phase."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C01","obj":"NE-6.1","title":"The visual pathway and visual field defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lens projects an inverted, left-right reversed image onto the retina. The temporal visual field therefore falls on the nasal retina, and the upper field falls on the lower retina. Retinal ganglion cell axons leave the eye at the optic disc as the optic nerve, a CNS tract wrapped in meninges."},{"type":"p","text":"At the optic chiasm, above the pituitary gland, fibers from the nasal half of each retina cross and temporal fibers stay on the same side. Each optic tract therefore carries the opposite half of the visual world from both eyes, which is why every complete lesion behind the chiasm produces a homonymous defect, while a lesion in front of it affects one eye only."},{"type":"p","text":"Most tract fibers synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus, which keeps the two eyes in separate layers; a minority leave the tract before the LGN for the pretectal area (pupillary reflex, C02) and the superior colliculus. From the LGN the optic radiations fan back to the primary visual cortex on the banks of the calcarine sulcus. Fibers from the lower retina, which carry the upper field, loop forward around the temporal horn of the lateral ventricle in the temporal lobe (the Meyer loop) and end on the lower bank (lingual gyrus). Fibers from the upper retina, carrying the lower field, run through the parietal lobe to the upper bank (cuneus)."},{"type":"p","text":"The macula, which serves central vision, is represented at the occipital pole. The posterior cerebral artery (PCA) supplies the calcarine cortex, but the pole often receives collateral flow from the middle cerebral artery (MCA). A PCA infarct therefore commonly spares central vision, whereas an optic tract lesion cannot. A pituitary adenoma presses on the chiasm from below, so it injures the inferior crossing fibers first and the upper temporal fields go first; a craniopharyngioma presses from above and tends to take the lower temporal fields first."},{"type":"table","title":"Lesion site and visual field defect (lesions on the right side)","cols":["Site","Typical cause","Field defect"],"rows":[["Right optic nerve","Optic neuritis, ischemic optic neuropathy, trauma","Blind right eye; right relative afferent pupillary defect"],["Optic chiasm, midline","Pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma","Bitemporal hemianopia"],["Right side of chiasm","Internal carotid aneurysm","Right nasal hemianopia"],["Right optic tract","Tumor, stroke, demyelination","Left homonymous hemianopia, usually incongruous"],["Right Meyer loop (temporal lobe)","MCA stroke, temporal tumor or surgery","Left upper homonymous quadrantanopia"],["Right parietal radiations","MCA stroke, parietal tumor","Left lower homonymous quadrantanopia"],["Right calcarine cortex","PCA stroke","Left homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing"]],"caption":"In front of the chiasm defects are monocular, at it heteronymous, and behind it homonymous. Lateral chiasm pressure catches uncrossed temporal fibers."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a field defect","text":"A 46-year-old woman bumps into door frames on both sides. Confrontation testing shows loss of the temporal half of the field in each eye, worse above. Step 1: both eyes are affected, so the lesion is at or behind the chiasm. Step 2: the defects are on opposite sides of the world in the two eyes (heteronymous), and only the crossing nasal fibers serve both temporal fields, so the lesion is at the chiasm. Step 3: upper fields come from the inferior retina, and inferior nasal fibers cross in the lower chiasm, so the compression comes from below. The likely cause is a pituitary macroadenoma growing upward out of the sella."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a left homonymous hemianopia with central vision preserved. Where is the lesion, and which artery is involved?","explain":"Homonymous loss places the lesion behind the chiasm on the right, and macular sparing points to the right occipital cortex: a right PCA infarct, with the occipital pole kept alive by collateral flow from the MCA.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the macula and the last two rows of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C02","obj":"NE-6.2","title":"Pupillary light reflex, accommodation, and abnormal pupils","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pupil size is set by two muscles of the iris. The sphincter pupillae constricts the pupil (miosis) under parasympathetic control through muscarinic receptors; the dilator pupillae widens it (mydriasis) under sympathetic control through alpha-1 receptors. The sympathetic route has three neurons: hypothalamus to the ciliospinal center at about C8 to T2, then to the superior cervical ganglion, then along the internal carotid artery and the long ciliary nerves."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pupillary light reflex","steps":["Light activates retinal ganglion cells","Axons run in optic nerve, chiasm, and tract","Fibers bypass the LGN to the pretectal nucleus","Each pretectal nucleus projects to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei","Preganglionic fibers travel in CN III to the ciliary ganglion","Short ciliary nerves contract both sphincter pupillae"],"caption":"Afferent limb CN II, efferent limb CN III. Bilateral pretectal projections make direct and consensual responses equal."},{"type":"p","text":"The near response (accommodation reflex) has three parts: convergence by the medial recti, pupillary constriction, and a rounder lens. The ciliary muscle, a parasympathetic target of the same CN III pathway, contracts and moves the ciliary body forward and inward, slackening the zonular fibers so the elastic lens rounds up and increases its refractive power. The near response is driven from the visual cortex and reaches the oculomotor complex by a route that does not pass through the pretectal nucleus, which explains why light and near responses can be separated by disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the efferent signal always goes to both eyes, an afferent lesion cannot make the pupils unequal. Unilateral optic nerve disease shows instead as a relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil): on the swinging flashlight test both pupils constrict when the normal eye is lit, then both dilate when the light swings to the affected eye, because that eye sends a weaker signal. A dense cataract does not cause it, since scattered light still reaches the retina."},{"type":"p","text":"Anisocoria (unequal pupils) therefore points to an efferent problem or a normal variant. Comparing the pupils in bright and dim light tells which pupil is abnormal. Pupillary fibers run on the outer surface of CN III and are supplied by pial vessels, so a compressive lesion (posterior communicating artery aneurysm, uncal herniation) dilates the pupil early, whereas diabetic ischemia of the nerve core usually spares it."},{"type":"table","title":"Interpreting abnormal pupils","cols":["Finding","Lesion and mechanism","What is seen"],"rows":[["Physiologic anisocoria","Normal variant","Small difference, about 1 mm or less, similar in light and dark; normal reactions"],["Horner syndrome","Interrupted sympathetic pathway; dilator weak","Small pupil, difference greater in the dark; mild ptosis; anhidrosis with proximal lesions"],["Compressive CN III palsy","Parasympathetic fibers lost; sphincter weak","Large poorly reactive pupil, difference greater in bright light; ptosis; eye down and out"],["Relative afferent pupillary defect","Asymmetric optic nerve or severe retinal disease","Equal pupils at rest; both dilate when light swings to the affected eye"],["Argyll Robertson pupil","Dorsal midbrain lesion near the pretectal area, classically neurosyphilis","Small, irregular, usually bilateral; constrict to near but not to light"]],"caption":"A larger difference in the dark means the small pupil is abnormal; a larger difference in bright light means the large pupil is abnormal."},{"type":"p","text":"The Argyll Robertson pupil is the classic light-near dissociation. A lesion near the pretectal area interrupts the light pathway to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus while sparing the more ventral near response fibers. Light-near dissociation also occurs with dorsal midbrain compression (Parinaud syndrome) and with a tonic (Adie) pupil, but those pupils are typically large."},{"type":"stop","predict":"The swinging flashlight test shows both pupils dilating whenever the light reaches the left eye. Are the pupils equal at rest, and where is the lesion?","explain":"Yes, because each pretectal nucleus drives both Edinger-Westphal nuclei. A weaker afferent signal from the left optic nerve or severely diseased left retina gives a left relative afferent pupillary defect.","check":"Compare with the light reflex flow and the paragraph on the swinging flashlight test."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C03","obj":"NE-6.3","title":"Phototransduction in rods and cones and vitamin A","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Photoreceptors work in reverse of most receptors: they are depolarized in the dark and hyperpolarize to light. In darkness, a high concentration of cGMP in the outer segment holds cGMP-gated cation channels open. Na+ and Ca2+ flow in (the dark current) while Na+-K+ ATPase in the inner segment pumps Na+ out. The cell rests near -40 mV and releases glutamate continuously from its synaptic terminal."},{"type":"p","text":"Rhodopsin, the rod pigment, is a G protein coupled receptor made of the protein opsin bound to 11-cis retinal, an aldehyde of vitamin A. A photon isomerizes 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal, which changes the shape of opsin into its active form (metarhodopsin II)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Phototransduction in a rod","steps":["Photon converts 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal","Activated rhodopsin activates the G protein transducin","Transducin alpha-GTP activates cGMP phosphodiesterase","Phosphodiesterase hydrolyzes cGMP; cGMP falls","cGMP-gated Na+ and Ca2+ channels close","Rod hyperpolarizes toward about -70 mV","Glutamate release onto bipolar cells falls"],"caption":"Each step multiplies the signal: one activated rhodopsin activates many transducins, and each phosphodiesterase destroys many cGMP molecules, so a rod can respond to a single photon."},{"type":"p","text":"Less glutamate is itself the signal. Bipolar cells with metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluR6) are inhibited by glutamate, so they depolarize when light arrives (ON pathway); those with ionotropic receptors hyperpolarize (OFF pathway). Splitting the signal this way lets the retina report both increases and decreases in light."},{"type":"p","text":"The response is switched off by rhodopsin kinase and arrestin, which inactivate rhodopsin, and by the intrinsic GTPase of transducin. Closing the channels also lowers intracellular Ca2+, which relieves Ca2+ inhibition of guanylyl cyclase, so cGMP is resynthesized; this negative feedback underlies light adaptation. In dark adaptation, sensitivity rises over about 20 to 30 minutes as bleached pigment is regenerated."},{"type":"p","text":"Bleached all-trans retinal is reduced to all-trans retinol, carried to the retinal pigment epithelium, isomerized back to the 11-cis form, and returned to the photoreceptors. This visual cycle needs a steady supply of vitamin A from the blood. Rods carry dim-light vision and depend almost entirely on rhodopsin, so vitamin A deficiency first appears as night blindness (nyctalopia). Vitamin A, acting through retinoic acid, also maintains specialized epithelia, so prolonged deficiency adds keratinizing metaplasia of the conjunctiva and cornea: xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, and finally keratomalacia."},{"type":"table","title":"Rods compared with cones","cols":["Feature","Rods","Cones"],"rows":[["Pigment","Rhodopsin, one type, peak about 500 nm","Three photopsins, peaks about 420 (S), 530 (M), and 560 (L) nm"],["Sensitivity","Very high; saturate in daylight","Lower; work in bright light"],["Distribution","Peripheral retina; absent from the central fovea","Concentrated in the fovea"],["Convergence onto ganglion cells","High: sensitive but blurry","Low, near one to one at the fovea: sharp acuity"],["Function","Night (scotopic) vision","Day (photopic) and color vision"]],"caption":"Both use the same cascade; they differ in pigment, sensitivity, location, and wiring."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C04","obj":"NE-6.4","title":"Aqueous humor and the tear film","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cornea and lens have no blood vessels, so aqueous humor supplies their nutrients and removes their wastes. It is formed by the ciliary processes at about 2 to 3 microliters per minute. Plasma filters out of fenestrated capillaries in the ciliary stroma, and the nonpigmented ciliary epithelium then secretes Na+, Cl-, and HCO3- into the posterior chamber, with water following osmotically. Na+-K+ ATPase powers the Na+ transport, and carbonic anhydrase supplies the HCO3-, which is why both enzymes are control points for secretion. Beta-adrenergic stimulation raises secretion through cAMP."},{"type":"p","text":"Aqueous flows from the posterior chamber through the pupil into the anterior chamber and leaves at the iridocorneal angle. Most passes through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, a circular endothelial channel that drains through collector channels to the episcleral veins. A smaller share leaves by the uveoscleral route through the ciliary muscle. Intraocular pressure, normally about 10 to 21 mmHg, is set by the balance between secretion and outflow resistance, and the trabecular meshwork supplies most of that resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"Glaucoma is optic neuropathy, usually with raised pressure, in which ganglion cell axons are lost at the optic disc, producing cupping and peripheral field loss first. In open-angle glaucoma the angle is anatomically open but trabecular resistance rises slowly, so loss is painless and gradual. In angle-closure glaucoma the peripheral iris is pushed against the trabecular meshwork. A shallow anterior chamber (often a small hyperopic eye or a thickened lens) and a mid-dilated pupil increase contact between iris and lens, aqueous builds up behind the iris, and the iris bows forward and seals the angle; pressure then rises sharply with pain, redness, and blurred vision."},{"type":"p","text":"The tear film keeps the cornea smooth, moist, and protected. Each layer has its own source, so the cause of dry eye can be predicted from which source fails. Tears spread with each blink and drain through the puncta, canaliculi, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct into the inferior meatus of the nose."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers of the tear film","cols":["Layer","Source","Function and failure"],"rows":[["Outer lipid","Meibomian glands in the tarsal plates","Slows evaporation; dysfunction gives evaporative dry eye"],["Middle aqueous","Lacrimal gland and accessory lacrimal glands","Volume, lysozyme, lactoferrin, and IgA; loss gives aqueous-deficient dry eye (for example Sjogren syndrome)"],["Inner mucin","Conjunctival goblet cells","Lets the watery layer wet the corneal surface; lost when goblet cells are lost (vitamin A deficiency, scarring)"]],"caption":"Modern descriptions merge the aqueous and mucin layers into one gel, but the three sources still predict the type of dry eye."},{"type":"flow","title":"Parasympathetic pathway to the lacrimal gland","steps":["Superior salivatory nucleus in the pons","Nervus intermedius of CN VII","Through the geniculate ganglion without synapse","Greater petrosal nerve, then nerve of the pterygoid canal","Synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion","Postganglionic fibers ride V2 and V1 branches to the gland"],"caption":"Reflex tearing uses corneal afferents in CN V1 and this CN VII efferent pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"The flow localizes facial nerve injury. A lesion at or proximal to the geniculate ganglion, as with a skull base fracture or a cerebellopontine angle tumor, removes tearing and gives a dry eye. A lesion at the stylomastoid foramen, such as a parotid tumor or local trauma, spares the greater petrosal nerve, so tear secretion is preserved; the eye still waters because a weak orbicularis oculi fails to close the lids and pump tears into the drainage system."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C05","obj":"NE-6.5","title":"Hearing: sound conduction, hair cell transduction, and hearing tests","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sound in air would mostly reflect off the higher-impedance fluid of the inner ear, and the middle ear overcomes this mismatch. The tympanic membrane has about 17 times the effective area of the stapes footplate, and the lever action of the ossicles adds about 1.3 times more, so pressure at the oval window is raised about 22 times. The stapedius (CN VII) and tensor tympani (CN V3) damp loud sound, so a facial nerve lesion can cause hyperacusis."},{"type":"p","text":"The scala vestibuli and scala tympani contain perilymph, which resembles extracellular fluid (high Na+, low K+) and connects at the apex through the helicotrema. Between them, the scala media holds endolymph, with K+ near 150 mmol/L and an endocochlear potential of about +80 mV, both produced by the stria vascularis. The organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane, with the stereocilia of its hair cells touching the overlying tectorial membrane."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sound transduction","steps":["Sound vibrates the tympanic membrane","Ossicles drive the stapes into the oval window","Traveling wave peaks at one place on the basilar membrane","Stereocilia shear against the tectorial membrane","Tip links open channels; K+ enters from endolymph","Hair cell depolarizes; Ca2+ entry releases glutamate","Spiral ganglion neurons fire into CN VIII"],"caption":"The round window bulges outward as the stapes pushes in, letting the fluid move."},{"type":"p","text":"Hair cell transduction uses K+ rather than Na+. Bending the stereocilia toward the tallest row stretches tip links and opens mechanotransduction channels at their tips. The driving force for K+ entry is the sum of the +80 mV endocochlear potential and the negative hair cell interior, about 150 mV in total, so a large current flows at little metabolic cost to the hair cell, and K+ leaves basolaterally into low-K+ perilymph. Inner hair cells, about 3,500 in number, receive about 90 to 95% of the afferent fibers and are the true sensory cells. Outer hair cells, about 12,000, change length with voltage through the motor protein prestin and amplify basilar membrane motion; they are the most vulnerable to noise and ototoxic drugs."},{"type":"p","text":"Tonotopy follows the mechanics of the basilar membrane. Near the oval window (base) it is narrow and stiff and resonates with high frequencies; toward the apex it is wide and flexible and responds to low frequencies. The normal range is about 20 to 20,000 Hz. Noise and aging (presbycusis) damage the base first, so high frequencies are lost first. Centrally, CN VIII reaches the cochlear nuclei, then both superior olivary nuclei, the lateral lemniscus, inferior colliculus, medial geniculate nucleus, and the primary auditory cortex (Heschl gyrus, superior temporal gyrus). Above the cochlear nuclei each ear is represented on both sides, so unilateral deafness means a lesion of the ear, CN VIII, or cochlear nucleus, not of the cortex."},{"type":"table","title":"Weber and Rinne tests (512 Hz tuning fork)","cols":["Hearing","Weber (fork on the vertex)","Rinne in the affected ear"],"rows":[["Normal","Heard in the midline","Air conduction louder than bone (positive)"],["Conductive loss","Lateralizes to the affected ear","Bone conduction louder than air (negative)"],["Sensorineural loss","Lateralizes to the normal ear","Air louder than bone; both reduced"]],"caption":"Rinne asks whether the middle ear is working; Weber shows which cochlea is working better."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the tuning forks","text":"Muffled hearing in the left ear; Weber lateralizes left. That fits a left conductive or a right sensorineural loss, and Rinne decides. On the left, bone is louder than air, so sound is not crossing the left middle ear: left conductive loss (cerumen, middle ear effusion, otosclerosis). Weber goes to the worse ear because bone-conducted sound reaches both cochleae equally, but the blocked ear is shielded from room noise and loses less energy out through the canal, so its intact cochlea hears it louder. Had Rinne been positive on the left with Weber going right, the loss would be left sensorineural."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C06","obj":"NE-6.6","title":"Vestibular system, the vestibulo-ocular reflex, vertigo, and motion sickness","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vestibular hair cells carry a bundle of stereocilia and one tall kinocilium. Bending toward the kinocilium depolarizes the cell and raises the firing rate of its afferent above a steady resting rate; bending away hyperpolarizes it and lowers the rate. Because each nerve fires at rest, each side reports motion as a change from baseline, and the brain reads head movement from the difference between the two labyrinths."},{"type":"table","title":"Vestibular end organs","cols":["Organ","Sensor","Adequate stimulus"],"rows":[["Semicircular canals (horizontal, anterior, posterior)","Crista in the ampulla, capped by a gelatinous cupula","Angular acceleration (head rotation) in the plane of the canal"],["Utricle","Macula, roughly horizontal, with calcium carbonate otoconia","Horizontal linear acceleration and head tilt"],["Saccule","Macula, roughly vertical, with otoconia","Vertical linear acceleration and gravity"]],"caption":"The canals lie at about right angles and work in push-pull pairs; turning to the left excites the left horizontal canal and inhibits the right."},{"type":"p","text":"When the head starts to turn, endolymph lags behind the duct by inertia and deflects the cupula. The cupula has the same density as endolymph, so gravity does not move it, and during steady rotation the fluid catches up and the signal fades; on stopping, the fluid keeps moving and the opposite rotation is felt. Otoconia are denser than endolymph, so the otolith membrane slides under linear forces and gravity, which lets the maculae signal head tilt."},{"type":"flow","title":"Horizontal vestibulo-ocular reflex (head turns left)","steps":["Left horizontal canal excited, right canal inhibited","Left vestibular nuclei fire more","Signal crosses to the right abducens nucleus","Right lateral rectus contracts","Via the medial longitudinal fasciculus, left medial rectus contracts","Both eyes move right, keeping the image stable"],"caption":"A three-neuron arc (vestibular ganglion, vestibular nucleus, ocular motor nucleus) gives the reflex a very short latency."},{"type":"p","text":"If one labyrinth or vestibular nerve fails suddenly, as in vestibular neuritis, the intact side's resting input is no longer balanced and is read as continuous rotation toward the intact side. The result is vertigo (an illusion of movement), nausea, and spontaneous nystagmus. The VOR drives the slow phase toward the damaged side, and a corrective saccade gives the fast phase away from it. Peripheral nystagmus beats in one direction and is suppressed by visual fixation; nystagmus that is purely vertical, changes direction with gaze, or is not suppressed suggests a brainstem or cerebellar lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"Caloric testing uses the same logic. With the horizontal canal brought vertical, cold water in one ear sets up convection that inhibits that canal, mimicking a turn to the opposite side, so the fast phase beats away from the cold ear; warm water does the reverse (COWS: cold opposite, warm same). In benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, loose otoconia in a canal, usually the posterior one, move with gravity and deflect the cupula, so brief vertigo follows changes in head position. In Meniere disease, excess endolymph (hydrops) causes episodic vertigo with fluctuating low-frequency hearing loss, tinnitus, and fullness."},{"type":"p","text":"Motion sickness arises when vestibular, visual, and proprioceptive signals disagree, for example reading in a moving car, where the eyes report stillness while the labyrinth reports motion. Vestibular nuclei and the cerebellar nodulus relay the mismatch to brainstem vomiting circuits in the medulla, and histamine H1 and muscarinic acetylcholine receptors carry the signal in these relays."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has sudden vertigo from a left vestibular neuritis. Which way does the fast phase of the nystagmus beat, and toward which side does the patient tend to fall?","explain":"The intact right side dominates, so the brain senses rotation to the right. The slow phase drifts left and the fast phase beats right, away from the lesion, and the patient leans or falls toward the left, the side of the lesion.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on unilateral loss and the VOR flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-06-01-C07","obj":"NE-6.7","title":"Taste and smell","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Taste receptor cells sit in taste buds on the fungiform papillae of the anterior tongue, the foliate papillae on its sides, and the circumvallate papillae just in front of the sulcus terminalis, with a few on the epiglottis and pharynx. They are modified epithelial cells, replaced from basal cells about every 10 days. Sweet, umami, and bitter tastes act on G protein coupled receptors that release Ca2+ through the IP3 pathway, open TRPM5 channels, and release ATP as the transmitter. Salt depolarizes the cell through Na+ entry by epithelial Na+ channels, and sour through H+ entry."},{"type":"p","text":"The anterior two thirds of the tongue sends taste in the chorda tympani branch of CN VII, which runs with the lingual nerve and then crosses the middle ear; its cell bodies lie in the geniculate ganglion. The posterior third, including the circumvallate papillae even though they lie in front of the sulcus, sends taste in CN IX, and the epiglottis in CN X. All three end in the rostral part of the nucleus of the solitary tract, which projects to the ipsilateral ventral posteromedial nucleus (VPM) of the thalamus and then to the gustatory cortex in the anterior insula and frontal operculum. Touch and pain from the anterior tongue travel separately in the lingual nerve of CN V3, so a chorda tympani lesion removes taste but not sensation."},{"type":"p","text":"Olfactory receptor neurons are bipolar neurons in the nasal roof epithelium, replaced throughout life from basal cells. Each expresses one of several hundred odorant receptor types, all coupled to the G protein Golf. Odorant binding activates adenylyl cyclase, cAMP opens cyclic nucleotide-gated cation channels, and Ca2+ entering through them opens Ca2+-activated Cl- channels; because these neurons hold a high internal Cl-, Cl- leaves and adds to the depolarization."},{"type":"p","text":"Their unmyelinated axons cross the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone in bundles (the olfactory nerve, CN I) and synapse in glomeruli of the olfactory bulb, where neurons expressing the same receptor converge. Mitral and tufted cells project through the olfactory tract and lateral olfactory stria directly to the piriform cortex, the amygdala, and the entorhinal cortex. Smell is the only sense that reaches its primary cortex without a thalamic relay, and its direct limbic links tie odor to emotion and memory."},{"type":"p","text":"Head trauma can shear the olfactory fila at the cribriform plate, causing anosmia; a fracture that tears the dura also causes CSF rhinorrhea and opens a route for meningitis. Anosmia also occurs with viral upper respiratory infections, early Parkinson and Alzheimer disease, and Kallmann syndrome, in which GnRH neurons fail to migrate with the olfactory axons, giving anosmia with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. Flavor depends largely on retronasal smell, so patients with anosmia often report lost taste."},{"type":"table","title":"Taste and smell compared","cols":["Feature","Taste","Smell"],"rows":[["Receptor cell","Modified epithelial cell","Bipolar neuron (the first-order neuron itself)"],["Cranial nerves","CN VII (anterior two thirds), IX (posterior third), X (epiglottis)","CN I through the cribriform plate"],["First central relay","Nucleus of the solitary tract","Olfactory bulb"],["Thalamus","VPM","No obligatory relay"],["Primary cortex","Anterior insula and frontal operculum","Piriform cortex, with amygdala and entorhinal cortex"],["Classic lesion","Chorda tympani or facial nerve injury","Cribriform plate fracture"]],"caption":"Taste follows the usual brainstem to thalamus to cortex sequence; smell bypasses the thalamus."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology meet at the same few junctions. Crossing at the chiasm, bilateral pretectal projections, bilateral auditory relays above the cochlear nuclei, and two vestibular labyrinths compared against each other explain why vision gives homonymous defects, why an afferent pupil lesion never causes anisocoria, why cortical lesions do not cause one-sided deafness, and why one failed labyrinth produces vertigo. The facial nerve ties several senses together: tearing, the stapedius reflex, and taste leave it at successive levels, so testing them localizes a CN VII lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pathology explain the failures. Phototransduction and olfaction use cyclic nucleotide cascades, hair cells use a K+ gradient built by the stria vascularis, and the eye depends on vitamin A for both rhodopsin and healthy conjunctival epithelium. Tumors (pituitary adenoma, schwannoma of CN VIII), vascular disease (MCA and PCA strokes), infection (neurosyphilis, viral neuritis), trauma (cribriform fracture), and genetic disease (Kallmann syndrome) each strike a predictable point. These pathways are simplified: real lesions are often partial, and bedside tests such as tuning forks screen rather than measure."}],"summary":["Nasal retinal fibers cross at the chiasm, so lesions in front of it are monocular, at it bitemporal, and behind it homonymous.","The Meyer loop in the temporal lobe carries the upper field and parietal radiations the lower field; PCA stroke gives hemianopia with macular sparing.","The light reflex runs CN II to the pretectal nucleus to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei to CN III, so an afferent lesion gives a relative afferent pupillary defect, never anisocoria.","Horner syndrome gives a small pupil, compressive CN III palsy a large one, and the Argyll Robertson pupil of neurosyphilis reacts to near but not to light.","Light isomerizes 11-cis retinal, transducin activates phosphodiesterase, cGMP falls, cation channels close, and the photoreceptor hyperpolarizes and releases less glutamate.","Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness first because rods depend on regenerated rhodopsin, then xerophthalmia from epithelial keratinization.","Ciliary epithelium secretes aqueous humor that drains through the trabecular meshwork and canal of Schlemm; tears come from meibomian (lipid), lacrimal (aqueous, CN VII), and goblet cells (mucin).","Hair cells depolarize by K+ entry from +80 mV endolymph; the cochlear base senses high and the apex low frequencies.","Weber lateralizes to a conductive ear and away from a sensorineural ear; Rinne is negative (bone louder than air) in conductive loss.","Canals sense angular and otoliths linear acceleration; unilateral vestibular loss gives nystagmus beating away from the lesion, and taste relays in the thalamus while smell does not."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Eye: I. Optics of Vision; II. Receptor and Neural Function of the Retina; III. Central Neurophysiology of Vision"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Sense of Hearing"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Chemical Senses: Taste and Smell"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory Transduction"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, sections on the orbit, the ear, and the nasal cavity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Eye"}]},"NE-7":{"unit":"NE-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07-01","title":"Somatic and Visceral Motor and Sensory Pathways","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows motor commands out of the central nervous system and visceral sensory signals back in. It traces the lateral corticospinal tract from cortex to the anterior horn, explains why upper and lower motor neuron lesions look so different, contrasts the one-neuron somatic and two-neuron autonomic motor pathways, and shows how visceral pain and baroreceptor signals reach the cord and medulla. Board questions usually describe a patient and ask you to place the lesion. If you know where each tract runs, where it crosses, and which artery supplies it, you can predict the findings of motor neuron disease and the classic spinal cord syndromes from first principles.","prerequisites":["Neuron structure, synaptic transmission, and fiber classes (A alpha, A delta, B, C)","Cross-sectional anatomy of the spinal cord: gray horns, white funiculi, dorsal and ventral roots","Dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways (NE-5)","The stretch reflex: muscle spindle, Ia afferent, and alpha motor neuron","Cholinergic and adrenergic receptor classes"],"connections":[{"to":"Sensory pathways and reflexes (NE-5)","how":"Spinal cord syndromes combine motor loss from this chapter with the dorsal column and spinothalamic losses taught there."},{"to":"Cerebrovascular anatomy and stroke","how":"The arteries supplying motor cortex, internal capsule, brainstem, and anterior cord decide which corticospinal segment an infarct removes."},{"to":"Cardiovascular regulation","how":"The baroreceptor reflex links CN IX and CN X afferents and the nucleus solitarius to moment-to-moment control of heart rate and vascular tone."},{"to":"Hematology and biochemistry","how":"Vitamin B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so its deficiency causes both megaloblastic anemia and myelin failure in the cord."},{"to":"Microbiology and genetics","how":"Poliovirus, Treponema pallidum, and SMN1 loss each damage a specific neuronal population, which explains their distinct patterns of weakness and sensory loss."}],"terms":[{"term":"Upper motor neuron (UMN)","def":"A neuron whose cell body lies in the cerebral cortex or brainstem and whose axon descends to influence lower motor neurons."},{"term":"Lower motor neuron (LMN)","def":"An alpha motor neuron in the anterior horn or a brainstem motor nucleus whose axon directly innervates skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Final common pathway","def":"The alpha motor neuron, through which all descending and reflex influences must pass to move skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Pyramidal decussation","def":"The crossing of most corticospinal axons at the junction of the medulla and spinal cord."},{"term":"Spasticity","def":"Velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone caused by exaggerated stretch reflexes after an upper motor neuron lesion."},{"term":"Babinski sign","def":"Extension of the great toe, often with fanning of the other toes, when the lateral sole is stroked; an extensor plantar response."},{"term":"Fasciculation","def":"A visible twitch caused by spontaneous discharge of a single motor unit, typical of lower motor neuron disease."},{"term":"Preganglionic neuron","def":"An autonomic neuron with its cell body in the brainstem or spinal cord whose axon synapses in an autonomic ganglion."},{"term":"Spinal shock","def":"Temporary flaccidity and loss of reflexes below an acute spinal cord lesion, followed later by spasticity and hyperreflexia."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain from an internal organ felt in a body wall region served by the same spinal segments."},{"term":"Nucleus solitarius","def":"The nucleus of the solitary tract in the dorsal medulla, which receives visceral afferents from CN VII, IX, and X."},{"term":"Baroreceptor","def":"A stretch receptor in the wall of the carotid sinus or aortic arch that signals arterial pressure."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07-01-C01","obj":"NE-7.1","title":"The lateral corticospinal tract from cortex to anterior horn","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lateral corticospinal tract carries voluntary, skilled movement, especially of the distal limbs. Its neurons are upper motor neurons (UMNs): their cell bodies lie in cerebral cortex and their axons descend without interruption to the spinal cord. About one third of the fibers arise in primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus, Brodmann area 4), about one third in premotor and supplementary motor cortex (area 6), and the rest in parietal somatosensory cortex. Giant pyramidal (Betz) cells in layer V of area 4 supply only about 3% of the fibers, but they are the largest and fastest."},{"type":"p","text":"The precentral gyrus is mapped by body part. The leg and foot lie on the medial surface in the paracentral lobule, supplied by the anterior cerebral artery, while the trunk, arm, hand, and face lie on the lateral convexity, supplied by the middle cerebral artery. This is why an anterior cerebral artery infarct weakens mainly the opposite leg and a middle cerebral artery infarct weakens mainly the opposite face and arm."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of the lateral corticospinal tract","steps":["Precentral gyrus, layer V pyramidal neurons","Corona radiata","Posterior limb of the internal capsule","Middle part of the cerebral peduncle (crus cerebri)","Scattered bundles in the basilar pons","Medullary pyramid","Pyramidal decussation: about 85% to 90% cross","Lateral funiculus of the cord (lateral corticospinal tract)","Anterior horn interneurons and alpha motor neurons"],"caption":"Above the decussation the tract serves the opposite side of the body; below it, the tract serves the same side."},{"type":"p","text":"In the internal capsule the corticospinal fibers for arm and leg are packed into the posterior limb, with corticobulbar fibers for the face near the genu. This compact bundle is supplied by lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery and by the anterior choroidal artery, so a small lacunar infarct can produce dense pure motor hemiparesis of the opposite face, arm, and leg. In the pons the tract is broken up by pontine nuclei, and it regroups on the ventral medulla as the pyramid."},{"type":"p","text":"At the cervicomedullary junction most fibers cross and descend in the lateral funiculus. The uncrossed remainder (about 10%) forms the anterior corticospinal tract, and most of those fibers cross at their own segment in the anterior white commissure to reach motor neurons for axial and proximal muscles. Lateral tract axons end mainly on interneurons, but some synapse directly on alpha motor neurons in the lateral anterior horn, the cells that drive the fine finger movements this tract is best known for. The alpha motor neuron is the lower motor neuron (LMN) and the final common pathway: its axon leaves in the ventral root and spinal nerve to the motor end plate."},{"type":"table","title":"Where a corticospinal lesion lies and what it produces","cols":["Lesion site","Usual artery","Weakness and associated findings"],"rows":[["Precentral gyrus, medial surface","Anterior cerebral","Opposite leg weaker than arm"],["Precentral gyrus, lateral surface","Middle cerebral","Opposite face and arm weaker than leg"],["Posterior limb of internal capsule","Lenticulostriate branches, anterior choroidal","Opposite face, arm, and leg equally (pure motor)"],["Cerebral peduncle (midbrain)","Branches of posterior cerebral","Opposite hemiparesis with same-side CN III palsy"],["Medullary pyramid","Anterior spinal or vertebral branches","Opposite arm and leg (face spared) with same-side tongue weakness"],["Lateral funiculus of the cord","Anterior spinal, mainly","Same-side weakness below the level"]],"caption":"The face is spared in a medullary lesion because corticobulbar fibers to the facial nucleus have already left the tract in the pons."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07-01-C02","obj":"NE-7.2","title":"Upper versus lower motor neuron lesions, ALS, poliomyelitis, and SMA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Resting muscle tone is the background activity of the stretch reflex: spindle Ia afferents excite alpha motor neurons, and descending pathways set how excitable that loop is. Upper motor neuron lesions and lower motor neuron lesions disturb this loop in opposite ways, which is why their signs are near mirror images."},{"type":"p","text":"A UMN lesion removes descending control while the reflex arc stays intact. The lesion usually damages corticospinal fibers together with the cortical fibers that drive the inhibitory medullary reticulospinal tract, leaving facilitatory vestibulospinal and pontine reticulospinal influences unopposed. Alpha and gamma motor neurons become hyperexcitable, so stretch reflexes are exaggerated: tone rises in a velocity-dependent way (spasticity, often with a clasp-knife quality), reflexes are brisk, and clonus may appear. Loss of corticospinal input also releases the Babinski sign, an extensor plantar response that is normal in infants before the tract is fully myelinated. Atrophy is mild, from disuse. An acute UMN lesion is often flaccid and areflexic for days before spasticity develops. A lesion confined to the pyramid gives weakness and a Babinski sign with little spasticity, because the reticulospinal fibers are spared."},{"type":"p","text":"An LMN lesion (anterior horn cell, ventral root, or peripheral nerve) cuts the efferent limb of the reflex arc, so the muscle is weak, flaccid, and hyporeflexic or areflexic. Denervated muscle loses neural activity and trophic support and wastes markedly within weeks. Irritable motor neurons and axons fire spontaneously, and each discharge of a whole motor unit shows as a fasciculation."},{"type":"table","title":"Upper versus lower motor neuron signs","cols":["Sign","UMN lesion","LMN lesion"],"rows":[["Tone","Increased (spastic), after an initial flaccid phase","Decreased (flaccid)"],["Deep tendon reflexes","Increased, may have clonus","Decreased or absent"],["Babinski sign","Present","Absent"],["Fasciculations","Absent","Present"],["Atrophy","Mild, from disuse","Marked, from denervation"],["Distribution","Groups of muscles, one side or below a level","Muscles of the affected segment, root, or nerve"]],"caption":"Both columns describe weakness; the other signs locate the lesion above or at the anterior horn."},{"type":"p","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) destroys both populations: Betz cells and corticospinal axons (scarring the lateral columns) and anterior horn and brainstem motor neurons (causing amyotrophy, muscle wasting). The result is a mixture of UMN and LMN signs, often in the same limb, such as a wasted, fasciculating arm with brisk reflexes and a Babinski sign. Sensory neurons are spared, and so, usually, are the oculomotor nuclei and the sacral motor neurons controlling the sphincters. Most cases are sporadic; about 10% are familial, with C9orf72 and SOD1 mutations among the known causes."},{"type":"p","text":"Poliomyelitis and spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) damage only anterior horn cells, so they give pure LMN signs with intact sensation. Poliovirus, an enterovirus spread by the fecal-oral route, replicates in pharyngeal and intestinal lymphoid tissue, reaches the bloodstream, and in a small minority of infections destroys anterior horn cells, causing asymmetric flaccid paralysis. SMA is autosomal recessive loss of the SMN1 gene on chromosome 5q. Its protein is needed to assemble small nuclear ribonucleoproteins for splicing, and motor neurons degenerate without it; the number of copies of the similar SMN2 gene modifies severity. The severe infantile form presents as a floppy infant with tongue fasciculations and absent reflexes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A small infarct destroys only the medullary pyramid on one side. Will the opposite limbs be strongly spastic?","explain":"Spasticity depends mainly on loss of the cortical drive to inhibitory reticulospinal pathways, which do not run in the pyramid. A pure pyramidal lesion causes weakness, loss of fine finger movement, and a Babinski sign, but only mild changes in tone.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on UMN lesions and the table of lesion sites in the previous section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07-01-C03","obj":"NE-7.3","title":"Somatic versus visceral motor pathways","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Somatic motor output uses one neuron. The alpha motor neuron in the anterior horn, or in a cranial nerve motor nucleus, sends a large myelinated axon straight to skeletal muscle. At the neuromuscular junction acetylcholine (ACh) binds muscle-type nicotinic receptors (NM), ligand-gated cation channels that depolarize the end plate. Skeletal muscle has no intrinsic activity, so this input is always excitatory: without it the muscle is paralyzed."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral (autonomic) motor output uses two neurons. A preganglionic neuron in the CNS sends a thinly myelinated B fiber to an autonomic ganglion, where it releases ACh onto neuronal nicotinic receptors (NN). The postganglionic neuron sends an unmyelinated C fiber to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or glands. Instead of a specialized end plate, it releases transmitter from varicosities along its terminal branches, so the signal spreads to many cells. Visceral targets already have their own tone, rhythm, or secretion, so autonomic input modulates them, and the receptor on the target decides whether the effect is excitatory or inhibitory."},{"type":"table","title":"Somatic and visceral motor pathways compared","cols":["Feature","Somatic motor","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic"],"rows":[["Neurons from CNS to target","One","Two","Two"],["CNS cell bodies","Anterior horn; cranial motor nuclei","Intermediolateral cell column, T1 to L2","Brainstem nuclei of CN III, VII, IX, X; S2 to S4"],["Ganglion location","None","Paravertebral chain or prevertebral (on the aorta)","In or near the target organ"],["Transmitter at ganglion","Not applicable","ACh on NN","ACh on NN"],["Transmitter at target","ACh on NM","Norepinephrine on adrenergic receptors (sweat glands: ACh on muscarinic)","ACh on muscarinic receptors"],["Targets","Skeletal muscle","Smooth and cardiac muscle, glands","Smooth and cardiac muscle, glands"],["Effect","Always excitatory","Excitatory or inhibitory","Excitatory or inhibitory"]],"caption":"Every preganglionic neuron is cholinergic and nicotinic; the divisions differ at the postganglionic synapse."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sympathetic route from cord to a limb blood vessel","steps":["Preganglionic cell in the intermediolateral column (T1 to L2)","Ventral root and spinal nerve","White ramus communicans (myelinated) to the chain","Synapse in a paravertebral ganglion, ACh on NN","Postganglionic axon leaves in a gray ramus (unmyelinated)","Spinal nerve to the vessel; norepinephrine on alpha-1 receptors"],"caption":"White rami exist only from T1 to L2, while gray rami join every spinal nerve, so sympathetic fibers reach the whole body wall."},{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal medulla is the main exception to the two-neuron rule. Preganglionic sympathetic fibers in the splanchnic nerves end directly on chromaffin cells, which come from neural crest like postganglionic neurons and release mostly epinephrine into the blood as a hormone."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the NM and NN receptors have different subunits, disease and drugs can separate the two systems. Myasthenia gravis antibodies target the muscle nicotinic receptor, so weakness occurs without autonomic symptoms, while a ganglionic nicotinic blocker such as hexamethonium interrupts both sympathetic and parasympathetic output and leaves skeletal muscle untouched. Lambert-Eaton syndrome targets presynaptic P/Q-type Ca2+ channels used at both kinds of terminal, so it causes both weakness and autonomic signs such as dry mouth."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a toxin that blocks ACh release from all cholinergic terminals affect only skeletal muscle?","explain":"No. Every somatic motor terminal, every preganglionic terminal, and every parasympathetic postganglionic terminal releases ACh, so the toxin would cause skeletal paralysis together with loss of autonomic ganglionic transmission and parasympathetic effects, such as dry mouth, dilated pupils, and urinary retention.","check":"Check the transmitter rows in the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07-01-C04","obj":"NE-7.4","title":"Spinal cord lesion patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The dorsal columns (fine touch, vibration, and position sense) ascend on the same side and cross in the medulla. The lateral corticospinal tract, having crossed in the medulla, descends on the side of the muscles it controls. The spinothalamic tract (pain and temperature) crosses within one or two segments of entry and ascends on the opposite side. Blood supply divides the cord the same way: one anterior spinal artery supplies the anterior two thirds, and paired posterior spinal arteries supply the dorsal columns and dorsal horns."},{"type":"p","text":"Subacute combined degeneration results from vitamin B12 deficiency. B12 is the cofactor for methionine synthase, which supplies S-adenosylmethionine for methylation reactions, and for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so methylmalonic acid and homocysteine rise. Myelin of long, heavily myelinated axons fails, with vacuolar change beginning in the cervical and upper thoracic cord. Losing the dorsal columns causes symmetric paresthesias, loss of vibration and position sense, and sensory ataxia with a positive Romberg sign; losing the lateral corticospinal tracts adds spastic weakness and Babinski signs. Knee reflexes are often brisk, but ankle reflexes may be lost when a peripheral neuropathy coexists. Anemia can be absent."},{"type":"p","text":"Tabes dorsalis is a late form of neurosyphilis (Treponema pallidum) in which the dorsal roots, mainly lumbosacral, are inflamed and degenerate. The dorsal columns degenerate secondarily because they are the central branches of the same dorsal root ganglion neurons. Position sense and reflexes are lost together, because the afferent limb of the stretch reflex is cut, while strength and plantar responses stay normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Anterior spinal artery infarction removes the anterior two thirds: corticospinal tracts, spinothalamic tracts, anterior horns, and descending autonomic fibers to the bladder. Vibration and position sense survive because the posterior spinal arteries are intact. The mid-thoracic cord is a watershed, so aortic dissection, aortic surgery, or severe hypotension that compromises the great anterior radicular artery (artery of Adamkiewicz) is the classic cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Complete transection removes every tract at the level. At first, sudden loss of descending facilitation silences the cord below (spinal shock), giving flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and an atonic bladder; lesions above about T6 also remove sympathetic outflow, causing hypotension and bradycardia. Over days to weeks spinal reflexes recover and become exaggerated, so UMN signs appear below the level, with LMN signs in muscles supplied by the damaged segments."},{"type":"table","title":"Spinal cord syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Structures lost","Key findings below the lesion","Spared"],"rows":[["Subacute combined degeneration","Dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal (often spinocerebellar)","Lost vibration and position, Romberg sign, spastic weakness, Babinski","Pain and temperature"],["Tabes dorsalis","Dorsal roots, dorsal columns","Sensory ataxia, Romberg sign, lightning pains, areflexia, Charcot joints, Argyll Robertson pupils","Strength; plantar response normal"],["Anterior spinal artery infarct","Anterior two thirds: corticospinal, spinothalamic, anterior horns","Bilateral weakness, bilateral pain and temperature loss, bladder dysfunction; LMN signs at the level","Vibration and position sense"],["Complete transection","All tracts","All movement and sensation lost; spinal shock, then spasticity and Babinski","Nothing below the level"]],"caption":"Ask which column is lost, whether reflexes are up or down, and whether the dorsal columns survive."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: paraplegia after aortic repair","text":"After thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm repair, a 68-year-old cannot move his legs. Pinprick and temperature are absent below the umbilicus on both sides, yet he feels a tuning fork at both ankles and knows which way his toes are moved. Step 1: both legs weak means both corticospinal tracts. Step 2: bilateral loss of pain and temperature means both spinothalamic tracts. Step 3: preserved vibration and position means intact dorsal columns, so the posterior spinal arteries were spared. Step 4: the umbilicus is the T10 dermatome, so the lesion lies at or above about T10. Only loss of the anterior spinal artery territory fits, from interrupted flow through the great anterior radicular artery during aortic clamping. Expect flaccid, areflexic legs at first (spinal shock), then spasticity and Babinski signs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-07-01-C05","obj":"NE-7.5","title":"Visceral sensory pathways: pain and baroreceptor reflexes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Visceral afferents are sparse, thinly myelinated A delta and unmyelinated C fibers. Their endings respond to distension, ischemia, inflammation, and traction, but not to cutting or burning, which is why a bowel can be cut without pain while distension hurts. Two routes carry them: pain afferents travel back with sympathetic nerves, while afferents for unconscious reflexes travel with parasympathetic nerves, chiefly CN IX and CN X."},{"type":"flow","title":"Route of visceral pain from a thoracic or abdominal organ","steps":["Nociceptor in organ wall: distension, ischemia, inflammation","Visceral plexus and prevertebral ganglion, passing without synapse","Splanchnic nerve to the sympathetic chain","White ramus to the spinal nerve","Cell body in a T1 to L2 dorsal root ganglion","Dorsal horn neuron that also receives skin input","Spinothalamic tract to thalamus and cortex","Pain felt in that segment's dermatome"],"caption":"Visceral pain follows sympathetic nerves backward, so its segment matches the organ's sympathetic supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Referred pain arises from convergence: visceral afferents end on dorsal horn neurons that also carry input from a dermatome, and the brain, more familiar with skin, assigns the signal to the body wall. Early visceral pain is therefore dull, poorly localized, and midline, because most gut organs are midline in origin and have bilateral innervation. In the pelvis, organs that touch the peritoneum send pain with sympathetic fibers, but organs below the pelvic pain line send it with the pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2 to S4."},{"type":"table","title":"Common referral patterns","cols":["Organ","Approximate cord segments","Where pain is felt"],"rows":[["Heart","T1 to T4","Substernal, left chest, medial left arm"],["Stomach and gallbladder","T6 to T9","Epigastrium"],["Appendix","T10","Periumbilical"],["Kidney and ureter","T10 to L2","Flank toward groin"],["Diaphragm (central part)","C3 to C5 via phrenic nerve","Shoulder"]],"caption":"The diaphragm is the exception: its central pain travels in the somatic phrenic nerve, not with sympathetics."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why appendicitis pain moves","text":"Appendicitis often begins as vague periumbilical pain that later shifts to the right lower quadrant. Step 1: the inflamed, distended appendix stimulates visceral afferents that travel with sympathetic fibers to the T10 segment. Step 2: the T10 dorsal horn also serves the umbilical dermatome, so the pain is referred there, dull and midline. Step 3: as inflammation reaches the overlying parietal peritoneum, somatic nerves of the abdominal wall are stimulated. Step 4: somatic afferents are dense and well mapped, so the pain becomes sharp and localized over the appendix."},{"type":"p","text":"Baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive endings in the wall of the carotid sinus, at the origin of the internal carotid artery, and in the aortic arch. Carotid sinus fibers run in the carotid sinus nerve, a branch of CN IX, and aortic arch fibers in CN X. Both enter the medulla, run in the solitary tract, and synapse in the nucleus solitarius (nucleus of the solitary tract). Carotid receptors fire little below about 50 to 60 mmHg, respond most steeply near normal mean pressure, and saturate near 180 mmHg; they respond more to a changing pressure than to a steady one. Carotid body and aortic body chemoreceptors use the same nerves."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Baroreceptor reflex","steps":["Arterial pressure rises; vessel walls stretch","Carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) firing increases","Nucleus solitarius is excited","Vagal output rises; medullary sympathetic drive falls","Heart rate, contractility, and vascular resistance fall","Pressure returns toward the set point"],"caption":"The same loop runs in reverse when pressure falls, as on standing."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If both carotid sinus nerves and the aortic baroreceptor fibers are cut, what happens to blood pressure?","explain":"Tonic inhibition from the nucleus solitarius falls, so sympathetic drive rises and pressure increases at first. Over time the average pressure changes little, but it swings widely with posture and activity because no fast negative feedback remains. Baroreceptors buffer short-term changes; they also reset within days, so they do not set the long-term level.","check":"Compare with the baroreceptor reflex cycle."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and blood supply decide the pattern of deficit. The corticospinal tract crosses once, low in the medulla, so laterality depends on whether a lesion lies above or below the decussation, and each named artery removes a predictable segment: the lenticulostriate branches take the internal capsule, the anterior spinal artery the anterior cord. Biochemistry, microbiology, and genetics explain which cells fail: B12 deficiency attacks myelin, Treponema pallidum the dorsal roots, poliovirus and SMN1 loss the anterior horn cells, and ALS both motor neuron populations while sensory neurons survive."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology links the visceral pathways to other systems. The same nucleus solitarius receives baroreceptor, chemoreceptor, and taste input, so the medulla integrates cardiovascular and respiratory reflexes, while visceral pain referral reflects embryologic segment origin rather than the organ's adult position. The rules have limits: early lesions may be flaccid before spasticity appears, overlapping neuropathy can blunt expected hyperreflexia, and dermatome and referral maps vary between individuals and sources."}],"summary":["The lateral corticospinal tract runs precentral gyrus, posterior limb of the internal capsule, middle cerebral peduncle, basilar pons, pyramid, decussation, lateral funiculus, anterior horn.","About 85% to 90% of corticospinal fibers cross in the caudal medulla; lesions above the decussation cause contralateral weakness, cord lesions cause ipsilateral weakness.","UMN lesions release the stretch reflex: spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign, mild atrophy; LMN lesions cut it: flaccidity, areflexia, fasciculations, marked atrophy.","ALS gives mixed UMN and LMN signs with spared sensation; poliomyelitis and SMA destroy anterior horn cells and give pure LMN signs.","Somatic motor is one neuron with ACh on NM receptors and always excites; visceral motor is two neurons with ACh on NN in the ganglion and excitatory or inhibitory effects at the target.","Subacute combined degeneration takes dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts; tabes takes dorsal roots and dorsal columns with areflexia and normal strength.","Anterior spinal artery infarction spares only the dorsal columns; complete transection removes everything below the level, starting with spinal shock.","Visceral pain travels with sympathetic nerves and is referred to the dermatomes of the same segments; reflex afferents travel with CN IX and CN X.","Carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) baroreceptors excite the nucleus solitarius, which raises vagal and lowers sympathetic output when pressure rises."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cortical and Brain Stem Control of Motor Function; Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes; The Autonomic Nervous System and the Adrenal Medulla"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Nervous Regulation of the Circulation and Rapid Control of Arterial Pressure"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back (spinal cord and its arteries) and overview of the autonomic nervous system and visceral afferents"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The autonomic nervous system and spinal motor control"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Central Nervous System: degenerative diseases affecting motor neurons, infections, and nutritional disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins: vitamin B12"}]},"NE-8":{"unit":"NE-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01","title":"Cranial and Spinal Nerves","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the twelve cranial nerves, the brachial plexus and the major nerves of the upper and lower limbs, the dermatomes, and the brainstem circuit for horizontal gaze. Board questions almost always start from a deficit, such as a down-and-out eye, a drooping lower face, a wrist drop, a foot drop, or a numb lateral foot, and ask which nerve, root, or tract is damaged and where. The reliable way to answer is to know what each nerve supplies, where it runs, and which muscles are left unopposed when it fails, so that every sign can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Organization of the brainstem and spinal cord, including the location of cranial nerve nuclei (NE-3)","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs (NE-7)","Actions of the extraocular, facial, upper limb, and lower limb muscles (MS-5)","Bones and landmarks of the skull base, humerus, and fibula (MS-3)","Spinal nerve formation: dorsal and ventral roots, dorsal root ganglion, and rami"],"connections":[{"to":"Autonomic nervous system (NE-9)","how":"Parasympathetic fibers ride in CN III, VII, IX, and X, and T1 sympathetic outflow explains Horner syndrome in lower plexus injuries."},{"to":"CNS blood supply and vascular disease (NE-10, NE-20-A)","how":"Aneurysms and brainstem infarcts produce cranial nerve palsies and internuclear ophthalmoplegia."},{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Multiple sclerosis plaques in the medial longitudinal fasciculus are the classic cause of bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia."},{"to":"PNS trauma and compression (NE-20-I)","how":"Fractures, dislocations, and entrapment sites predict which peripheral nerve is injured."},{"to":"Neurological pathogens (NE-19)","how":"Varicella zoster and Borrelia burgdorferi affect cranial nerves and dorsal root ganglia in dermatomal or nerve patterns."}],"terms":[{"term":"Lower motor neuron (LMN)","def":"A motor neuron in a brainstem nucleus or the spinal ventral horn whose axon reaches skeletal muscle directly; its loss causes weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, and reduced reflexes."},{"term":"Upper motor neuron (UMN)","def":"A cortical or brainstem neuron whose axon descends to synapse on lower motor neurons; its loss causes weakness with spasticity and hyperreflexia below the lesion."},{"term":"Corticobulbar tract","def":"The UMN pathway from motor cortex to the motor nuclei of the cranial nerves."},{"term":"Branchial motor fibers","def":"Motor fibers to muscles derived from the pharyngeal arches, carried by CN V, VII, IX, and X."},{"term":"Dermatome","def":"The area of skin supplied by the sensory fibers of a single spinal nerve root."},{"term":"Myotome","def":"The group of muscles supplied mainly by a single spinal nerve root."},{"term":"Brachial plexus","def":"The network formed by the ventral rami of C5 to T1 that supplies the upper limb."},{"term":"Trendelenburg sign","def":"Drop of the pelvis on the unsupported side when standing on one leg, caused by weak hip abductors on the stance side."},{"term":"Medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)","def":"A paired brainstem tract that, among other connections, carries axons from the abducens nucleus on one side to the medial rectus motor neurons of the oculomotor nucleus on the other side."},{"term":"Paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)","def":"The pontine gaze center that generates horizontal saccades toward its own side."},{"term":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO)","def":"Impaired adduction of one eye on lateral gaze, with nystagmus of the opposite abducting eye, caused by a lesion of the MLF."},{"term":"Radiculopathy","def":"Dysfunction of a spinal nerve root, producing pain, sensory loss, weakness, and reflex change in that root's distribution."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01-C01","obj":"NE-8.1","title":"The cranial nerves: foramina, components, and deficits","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cranial nerves carry the fiber types of spinal nerves plus special senses and branchial motor fibers, but each carries only some of them, so each lesion produces a distinctive combination of losses. Their attachments follow a fixed order: I and II to the forebrain, III and IV to the midbrain, V to the pons, VI, VII, and VIII at the pontomedullary junction, IX, X, and XII to the medulla, and the spinal part of XI to the upper cervical cord. That order, together with the foramen each nerve uses, places a lesion in the brainstem, at the skull base, or in the periphery."},{"type":"table","title":"The cranial nerves","cols":["Nerve","Skull exit","Components","Main functions","Sign of injury"],"rows":[["I Olfactory","Cribriform plate","Special sense","Smell","Anosmia"],["II Optic","Optic canal","Special sense","Vision; afferent limb of the light reflex","Visual loss; relative afferent pupillary defect"],["III Oculomotor","Superior orbital fissure","Somatic motor; parasympathetic","Most extraocular muscles, levator palpebrae; pupil constriction, accommodation","Eye down and out, ptosis, dilated unreactive pupil"],["IV Trochlear","Superior orbital fissure","Somatic motor","Superior oblique (depression in adduction, intorsion)","Vertical diplopia on looking down and in; head tilt away from lesion"],["V Trigeminal","V1 superior orbital fissure; V2 foramen rotundum; V3 foramen ovale","Somatic sensory; branchial motor (V3)","Facial sensation; muscles of mastication","Facial numbness; jaw deviates toward lesion"],["VI Abducens","Superior orbital fissure","Somatic motor","Lateral rectus","Eye cannot abduct; horizontal diplopia"],["VII Facial","Internal acoustic meatus, then stylomastoid foramen","Branchial motor; parasympathetic; taste; small somatic sensory","Facial expression, stapedius; lacrimal and salivary glands; taste anterior two thirds of tongue","Whole ipsilateral face weak; other signs by site (C02)"],["VIII Vestibulocochlear","Internal acoustic meatus","Special sense","Hearing and balance","Sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo"],["IX Glossopharyngeal","Jugular foramen","Branchial motor; parasympathetic; sensory; taste","Stylopharyngeus; parotid; posterior third of tongue; pharynx; carotid sinus","Lost gag reflex on that side (afferent limb)"],["X Vagus","Jugular foramen","Branchial motor; parasympathetic; sensory","Palate, pharynx, larynx; thoracic and abdominal viscera","Uvula deviates away from lesion; hoarseness; dysphagia"],["XI Accessory","Jugular foramen (spinal root enters through foramen magnum)","Motor","Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius","Shoulder droop, weak shrug; weak head turn away from lesion"],["XII Hypoglossal","Hypoglossal canal","Somatic motor","Intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles","Tongue deviates toward lesion, with atrophy and fasciculations"]],"caption":"A lesion at the superior orbital fissure or jugular foramen affects every nerve passing through it."},{"type":"p","text":"Eye signs follow from the muscle that is lost and the muscles left unopposed. When CN III fails, the lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV) pull the eye down and out, the lid falls because the levator palpebrae is paralyzed, and the pupil dilates and stops reacting because the parasympathetic supply to the sphincter is lost while sympathetic dilator tone remains. Pupillary fibers run on the surface of the nerve, so external compression by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm or a herniating uncus dilates the pupil early. Diabetic microvascular infarction damages the core of the nerve and usually spares the pupil."},{"type":"flow","title":"CN III compression by an aneurysm","steps":["Aneurysm at the internal carotid and posterior communicating junction","Expanding sac presses on the adjacent CN III","Superficial parasympathetic pupillary fibers fail first","Pupil dilates and reacts poorly to light","Deeper somatic motor fibers then fail","Ptosis and a down-and-out eye follow"],"caption":"A painful third nerve palsy with a dilated pupil indicates compression until proven otherwise."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior oblique depresses the eye most effectively when it is adducted and also intorts it. A CN IV lesion therefore causes vertical diplopia worst when reading or descending stairs. The patient tilts the head away from the lesion, because tilting toward it demands intorsion from the weak muscle and lets the superior rectus elevate the eye further. CN IV is the only nerve to leave the dorsal brainstem, and its fibers cross first, so a trochlear nucleus lesion weakens the opposite superior oblique. CN VI supplies only the lateral rectus, leaving the eye adducted. Its long course over the clivus makes it easily stretched by raised intracranial pressure, so a sixth nerve palsy can be a false localizing sign."},{"type":"p","text":"Each lateral pterygoid (CN V3) pulls the jaw forward and toward the opposite side, so with one weak the jaw deviates toward the lesion on opening. Each genioglossus (CN XII) protrudes the tongue toward the opposite side, so after a CN XII lesion the intact muscle pushes the tongue toward the lesion, with atrophy and fasciculations because the LMN is lost. In the palate the intact levator veli palatini (CN X) draws the uvula away from the lesion. CN XI weakness drops the ipsilateral shoulder and weakens turning the head to the opposite side, since each sternocleidomastoid turns the face away from its own side."},{"type":"table","title":"Reflexes that test pairs of cranial nerves","cols":["Reflex","Afferent limb","Efferent limb"],"rows":[["Pupillary light reflex","CN II","CN III (parasympathetic)"],["Corneal blink reflex","CN V1","CN VII"],["Gag reflex","CN IX","CN X"],["Carotid sinus reflex","CN IX","CN X (heart)"]],"caption":"If one eye never blinks whichever cornea is touched, that eye's CN VII is damaged; if neither eye blinks when one cornea is touched, that side's V1 is damaged."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with long-standing diabetes and a patient with sudden severe headache both have ptosis and a down-and-out eye. Which one probably has a normal pupil, and why?","explain":"The diabetic patient. Microvascular ischemia infarcts the center of the nerve and spares the superficial pupillary fibers, whereas an aneurysm compresses from outside and affects the pupil first.","check":"The CN III paragraph and the compression flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01-C02","obj":"NE-8.2","title":"Central versus peripheral facial weakness","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The facial motor nucleus in the caudal pons holds the LMNs for every muscle of facial expression on its own side. The part serving the upper face (frontalis and orbicularis oculi) receives corticobulbar fibers from both hemispheres, while the part serving the lower face receives them mainly from the opposite hemisphere. This difference in UMN input, not any difference in the nerve, separates the two patterns of facial weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"A lesion of the motor cortex or of corticobulbar fibers in the internal capsule removes one of the two inputs to the upper face. The input from the other hemisphere keeps the forehead wrinkling and the eye closing, while the opposite lower face loses its main input, so the nasolabial fold flattens and the mouth is pulled toward the normal side. Arm weakness on the drooping side often accompanies it. Smiling in response to genuine emotion may be partly preserved, because emotional facial movement uses pathways separate from voluntary corticobulbar control."},{"type":"p","text":"A lesion of the facial nucleus or nerve removes the final common pathway for the entire half of the face. The forehead cannot wrinkle, the eye cannot close and rolls upward on attempted closure (Bell phenomenon), the corneal blink is lost on that side, and the mouth sags. The usual cause is Bell palsy, an acute idiopathic CN VII palsy thought to arise from viral inflammation (reactivated herpes simplex virus is suspected) with swelling of the nerve inside the narrow facial canal. Other causes include varicella zoster reactivation in the geniculate ganglion (Ramsay Hunt syndrome, with vesicles in the ear), Lyme disease (sometimes bilateral), parotid tumors, temporal bone fracture, and tumors in the internal acoustic meatus. Within the pons, CN VII fibers loop around the abducens nucleus beneath the facial colliculus, so a pontine lesion there often adds an ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy."},{"type":"table","title":"Upper motor neuron versus CN VII facial weakness","cols":["Feature","UMN lesion (cortex, internal capsule)","CN VII lesion (nucleus or nerve)"],"rows":[["Side of weakness","Contralateral to lesion","Ipsilateral to lesion"],["Forehead and eye closure","Spared","Weak"],["Lower face","Weak","Weak"],["Corneal blink on that side","Present","Absent (efferent lost)"],["Taste, tearing, hyperacusis","Normal","May be affected, depending on site"],["Typical companions","Contralateral arm weakness, aphasia if dominant hemisphere","Ear pain or vesicles, ipsilateral CN VI or VIII signs, parotid mass"]],"caption":"Forehead strength is the single most useful bedside distinction."},{"type":"p","text":"Branches leave CN VII at fixed points in the temporal bone, so the accompanying findings place a peripheral lesion along its course."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of CN VII and the branches that localize a lesion","steps":["Pons: motor nucleus; fibers loop around abducens nucleus","Internal acoustic meatus with CN VIII","Geniculate ganglion: greater petrosal nerve to lacrimal gland","Facial canal: nerve to stapedius","Just above exit: chorda tympani (taste, submandibular and sublingual glands)","Stylomastoid foramen, then through parotid to facial muscles"],"caption":"A lesion affects every branch at or beyond its site and spares branches that left earlier."},{"type":"example","title":"Placing a CN VII lesion","text":"A patient cannot wrinkle the right forehead or close the right eye, and the right mouth droops. Forehead involvement makes this a CN VII lesion, not a stroke. Sounds are painfully loud in the right ear and taste is lost on the right anterior tongue, but tearing is normal. Hyperacusis means the nerve to stapedius is involved, and lost taste means the chorda tympani is involved, so the lesion lies at or above the stapedius branch. Normal tearing means the greater petrosal nerve, which leaves at the geniculate ganglion, is spared. The lesion is in the facial canal between the geniculate ganglion and the stapedius branch. A lesion at the stylomastoid foramen, such as a parotid tumor, would cause facial weakness alone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01-C03","obj":"NE-8.3","title":"The brachial plexus and upper limb nerve injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brachial plexus is formed by the ventral rami of C5 to T1. The roots join into three trunks above the clavicle, each trunk splits into an anterior and a posterior division behind the clavicle, the divisions regroup into three cords in the axilla, and the cords end in five terminal branches. Anterior divisions supply the flexor (anterior) compartments and posterior divisions supply the extensor (posterior) compartments, which is why the posterior cord gives rise to both the axillary and radial nerves. The cords are named for their position around the axillary artery."},{"type":"table","title":"Organization of the brachial plexus","cols":["Level","Composition","Important branches"],"rows":[["Roots (ventral rami)","C5, C6, C7, C8, T1","Dorsal scapular (C5); long thoracic (C5 to C7)"],["Trunks","Upper C5 to C6; middle C7; lower C8 to T1","Suprascapular from upper trunk"],["Divisions","Anterior (flexor) and posterior (extensor) from each trunk","None"],["Lateral cord","Anterior divisions of upper and middle trunks","Musculocutaneous; lateral root of median"],["Medial cord","Anterior division of lower trunk","Ulnar; medial root of median"],["Posterior cord","All three posterior divisions","Axillary; radial; thoracodorsal; subscapular nerves"]],"caption":"The median nerve receives roots from both the lateral and medial cords."},{"type":"p","text":"Forcing the head and shoulder apart, as in shoulder dystocia or a fall onto the shoulder, stretches the upper trunk (Erb palsy, C5 to C6). The deltoid and supraspinatus (abduction), infraspinatus and teres minor (lateral rotation), and biceps and brachialis (elbow flexion and supination) fail, so the arm hangs adducted and medially rotated with the elbow extended and forearm pronated (waiter's tip). Sensation is reduced over the lateral arm and forearm, and the biceps reflex is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulling the abducted arm upward, as when grabbing a support during a fall or in a breech delivery, stretches the lower trunk (Klumpke palsy, C8 to T1). The intrinsic hand muscles fail. Because the lumbricals and interossei normally flex the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and extend the interphalangeal (IP) joints, their loss lets the long extensors hyperextend the MCPs and the long flexors flex the IPs, producing a claw hand, with sensory loss along the medial forearm and hand. Avulsion of the T1 root also interrupts preganglionic sympathetic fibers bound for the head, adding ipsilateral Horner syndrome (mild ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis). A cervical rib or an apical lung (Pancoast) tumor compresses the same trunk gradually."},{"type":"table","title":"Terminal and collateral nerve injuries in the upper limb","cols":["Nerve (roots)","Typical injury","Motor deficit","Sensory loss"],"rows":[["Axillary (C5 to C6)","Surgical neck fracture; anterior shoulder dislocation","Weak abduction (deltoid) and lateral rotation (teres minor)","Skin over the lower deltoid"],["Musculocutaneous (C5 to C7)","Rare; penetrating injury","Weak elbow flexion and supination; biceps reflex lost","Lateral forearm"],["Radial (C5 to T1)","Midshaft humerus fracture in the radial groove","Wrist drop, weak finger and thumb extension; triceps largely spared","Dorsum of hand near the thumb"],["Median at wrist (C5 to T1)","Carpal tunnel compression","Weak thumb abduction and opposition; thenar atrophy","Palmar lateral three and a half digits; thenar palm spared"],["Median at elbow","Supracondylar humerus fracture","Above plus weak pronation and flexion of thumb, index, and middle fingers","As above plus thenar palm"],["Ulnar (C8 to T1)","Medial epicondyle or cubital tunnel; hook of hamate or Guyon canal","Weak finger abduction and adduction, claw of digits 4 and 5, weak thumb adduction; an elbow lesion also weakens the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus","Medial one and a half digits"],["Long thoracic (C5 to C7)","Axillary node dissection or mastectomy","Winged scapula; difficulty raising arm above horizontal","None"]],"caption":"Each nerve is injured where it lies on bone or passes through a tunnel."},{"type":"p","text":"The radial nerve gives its branches to the triceps before it enters the radial groove, so a midshaft fracture spares elbow extension but paralyzes the wrist and finger extensors. The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum, so carpal tunnel syndrome numbs the fingers but spares the skin over the thenar eminence. The serratus anterior holds the scapula against the chest wall and rotates it upward, so its loss lets the medial border lift away when the patient pushes against a wall and limits elevation of the arm."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is clawing of the ring and little fingers more obvious after an ulnar lesion at the wrist than after one at the elbow?","explain":"An elbow lesion also paralyzes the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus, so the distal IP joints of digits 4 and 5 are not pulled into flexion. A wrist lesion spares that muscle, so the unopposed long flexor bends the fingers more and the claw is more marked.","check":"The Klumpke paragraph on what the lumbricals and interossei do, and the ulnar row of the nerve injury table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01-C04","obj":"NE-8.4","title":"Lower limb nerve injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lower limb nerves are injured where they cross bone or pass close to surgical and injection sites, and each deficit follows from the muscle compartment the nerve supplies. The sciatic nerve divides above the knee into the tibial nerve, which supplies the posterior compartment of the leg and the sole, and the common fibular (peroneal) nerve, which supplies the anterior and lateral compartments."},{"type":"p","text":"The common fibular nerve winds superficially around the neck of the fibula, where a fracture, a tight cast, a knee brace, or prolonged leg crossing compresses it. Its deep branch supplies the anterior compartment (tibialis anterior and the toe extensors, which dorsiflex the foot and toes) and the skin of the first web space. Its superficial branch supplies the lateral compartment (fibularis longus and brevis, which evert the foot) and most of the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg. Injury causes foot drop: the toes catch during swing, so the patient lifts the knee high (steppage gait) and the foot slaps at heel strike. Inversion is largely preserved because tibialis posterior, the main invertor, is supplied by the tibial nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"The tibial nerve supplies gastrocnemius, soleus, tibialis posterior, and the long toe flexors. A proximal injury, as in posterior knee dislocation, severely weakens plantar flexion and inversion, so the patient cannot stand on the toes, the Achilles reflex falls, and the sole is numb. Compression in the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus affects only the plantar nerves, numbing the sole and weakening the intrinsic foot muscles."},{"type":"p","text":"The femoral nerve (L2 to L4) passes under the inguinal ligament to the quadriceps. Its loss makes the knee buckle, especially on stairs, reduces the patellar reflex, and numbs the anterior thigh and, through the saphenous branch, the medial leg. The obturator nerve (L2 to L4) runs along the lateral pelvic wall to the thigh adductors and can be compressed during pelvic surgery, pelvic fracture, or a difficult labor, causing weak adduction and a numb patch on the medial thigh."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior gluteal nerve (L4 to S1) supplies gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae, which abduct the hip and, during single-leg stance, hold the pelvis level against body weight. It is injured by a poorly placed gluteal injection or hip surgery. The sign appears on the opposite side from the lesion, as the flow shows."},{"type":"flow","title":"Trendelenburg sign after right superior gluteal nerve injury","steps":["Patient stands on the right leg","Right gluteus medius and minimus must hold the pelvis level","Weak abductors cannot resist body weight","Unsupported left side of the pelvis drops","Trunk lurches right to shift weight over the hip"],"caption":"The pelvis drops on the healthy side; the weak muscle is on the stance side."},{"type":"table","title":"Lower limb nerve injuries","cols":["Nerve (roots)","Typical injury","Motor deficit","Sensory loss","Reflex"],"rows":[["Common fibular (L4 to S2)","Fibular neck fracture or compression","Foot drop; weak dorsiflexion, toe extension, eversion; inversion largely intact","Lateral leg and dorsum of foot","None lost"],["Tibial (L4 to S3)","Posterior knee trauma; tarsal tunnel distally","Weak plantar flexion, inversion, toe flexion","Sole of foot","Achilles reduced"],["Femoral (L2 to L4)","Pelvic or inguinal trauma; psoas hematoma","Weak knee extension; some hip flexion","Anterior thigh, medial leg","Patellar reduced"],["Obturator (L2 to L4)","Pelvic surgery; pelvic fracture; difficult labor","Weak thigh adduction","Medial thigh","None routinely tested"],["Superior gluteal (L4 to S1)","Misplaced gluteal injection; hip surgery","Weak hip abduction; Trendelenburg sign","None","None"]],"caption":"Foot drop with intact inversion points to the common fibular nerve rather than the L5 root."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01-C05","obj":"NE-8.5","title":"Dermatomes and localizing a nerve root lesion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each spinal nerve root carries sensory fibers whose cell bodies lie in one dorsal root ganglion, and the strip of skin those fibers supply is its dermatome. Dermatomes reflect segmental development of the embryo: the limbs grow out carrying their segments with them, so dermatomes run as bands around the trunk but as long strips down the limbs. A root lesion produces sensory change in a dermatome, weakness in a myotome, and loss of the reflex served by that root, whereas a peripheral nerve lesion stays within that nerve's own sensory field and muscles."},{"type":"table","title":"Root landmarks for localization","cols":["Root","Dermatome landmark","Key motor action","Reflex"],"rows":[["C5","Lateral shoulder and upper arm","Shoulder abduction","Biceps (C5 to C6)"],["C6","Lateral forearm and thumb","Elbow flexion, wrist extension","Brachioradialis, biceps"],["C7","Middle finger","Elbow and finger extension","Triceps"],["C8","Little finger and medial hand","Finger flexion","None routinely tested"],["T1","Medial arm near the elbow","Finger abduction","None"],["T4","Nipple","None","None"],["T10","Umbilicus","None","None"],["L4","Knee and medial leg","Knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion","Patellar (L3 to L4)"],["L5","Lateral leg and dorsum of foot, great toe","Great toe extension, foot inversion, hip abduction","None reliable"],["S1","Lateral foot and little toe, sole","Plantar flexion, foot eversion","Achilles"],["S2 to S5","Perineum and perianal skin (saddle area)","Anal and urethral sphincters (S2 to S4)","Anal reflex (S2 to S4)"]],"caption":"Matching dermatome, myotome, and reflex together localizes a root best."},{"type":"p","text":"Adjacent dermatomes overlap, especially for light touch, so a single root lesion usually produces reduced rather than absent sensation and is best detected with pinprick. For spinal cord lesions, a sensory level at the nipple places a cord lesion at or above T4, and one at the umbilicus at or above T10, because ascending tracts carry everything from below."},{"type":"p","text":"Root numbering explains which root a herniated disc compresses. In the cervical spine each root exits above the vertebra of the same number (C8 exits between C7 and T1), so a C5 to C6 disc compresses the C6 root and numbs the thumb. In the lumbar spine each root exits below its pedicle, high in its foramen, above the disc. The usual posterolateral herniation therefore misses the root exiting at that level and compresses the root descending to the next foramen."},{"type":"flow","title":"L4 to L5 disc herniation","steps":["Annulus fibrosus tears posterolaterally, beside the posterior longitudinal ligament","Nucleus pulposus herniates at the L4 to L5 disc","Descending L5 root is compressed in the lateral recess","Pain radiates down the lateral leg to the dorsum of the foot","Weak great toe extension and dorsiflexion; no reflex lost"],"caption":"An L5 to S1 herniation compresses S1 instead, numbing the lateral foot and reducing the Achilles reflex."},{"type":"p","text":"Varicella zoster virus lies latent in a dorsal root ganglion, so its reactivation (shingles) produces a painful vesicular band confined to one dermatome. Visceral pain is referred to the dermatome sharing the visceral afferents' spinal segment: early appendicitis is felt at the umbilicus (T10), and diaphragmatic irritation is felt at the shoulder because the phrenic nerve arises from C3 to C5."},{"type":"example","title":"Root or nerve? Two patients with foot drop","text":"Patient A has foot drop, weak eversion, normal inversion, normal hip abduction, and numbness on the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg. Patient B has foot drop, weak inversion and hip abduction, and numbness of the dorsum of the foot with back pain radiating down the leg. Both lack dorsiflexion (deep fibular nerve, L4 and L5). Inversion (tibialis posterior) is supplied by the tibial nerve and hip abduction (gluteus medius) by the superior gluteal nerve, yet both draw on L5. Weakness across three nerves sharing one root places Patient B's lesion at the L5 root, most likely an L4 to L5 disc. Patient A's deficit stays within the common fibular nerve, consistent with compression at the fibular neck."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-08-01-C06","obj":"NE-8.6","title":"Horizontal gaze and internuclear ophthalmoplegia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Looking to one side requires the lateral rectus of one eye (CN VI) and the medial rectus of the other (CN III) to contract together, although their nuclei lie in different parts of the brainstem. The abducens nucleus coordinates them. It contains motor neurons for the ipsilateral lateral rectus and also internuclear neurons whose axons cross the midline and ascend in the opposite medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) to the medial rectus motor neurons of the oculomotor nucleus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Circuit for horizontal gaze to the right","steps":["Left frontal eye field signals a rightward saccade","Right PPRF generates the horizontal saccade command","Right abducens nucleus: motor neurons fire to the right lateral rectus","Right abducens internuclear axons cross to the left MLF","Left MLF ascends to the left oculomotor medial rectus neurons","Left medial rectus contracts; both eyes turn right"],"caption":"Each abducens nucleus is a gaze center for its own side, not just the nucleus of one muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"A lesion of the MLF cuts this link on one side. On gaze away from the lesion, the eye on the side of the lesion fails to adduct or adducts slowly, while the opposite, abducting eye overshoots with horizontal nystagmus. The leading explanation is that the brain increases the command to overcome the weak adduction, and because yoked muscles receive equal innervation, the abducting eye receives the excess too. The syndrome is named for the side of the lesion, which is the side of the eye that fails to adduct."},{"type":"p","text":"Convergence is preserved because it uses a separate midbrain vergence pathway that reaches the medial rectus neurons directly, without the MLF. An eye that cannot adduct on lateral gaze but adducts normally during convergence therefore has an intact medial rectus, CN III, and oculomotor nucleus, which proves the lesion is internuclear. The abducting eye is normal, distinguishing INO from a CN VI palsy, and there is no ptosis or pupil change, distinguishing it from a CN III palsy."},{"type":"p","text":"The MLF is a compact, heavily myelinated tract near the floor of the fourth ventricle, where multiple sclerosis (MS) plaques commonly form. Demyelination slows or blocks conduction in the internuclear axons, so mild cases show only slowed adduction during saccades. Bilateral INO in a young adult strongly suggests MS. A unilateral INO in an older adult more often reflects a small brainstem infarct in paramedian branches of the basilar artery. If a lesion damages the PPRF or abducens nucleus together with the adjacent MLF on the same side, the result is the one-and-a-half syndrome: no horizontal gaze toward the lesion and, on gaze away, no adduction of the eye on the lesion side, leaving abduction of the opposite eye as the only horizontal movement."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing a horizontal gaze problem (right-sided lesions)","cols":["Lesion","Gaze to the right","Gaze to the left","Convergence"],"rows":[["Right CN VI (nerve)","Right eye fails to abduct","Normal","Normal"],["Right abducens nucleus or PPRF","Neither eye moves right (gaze palsy)","Normal","Normal"],["Right MLF (right INO)","Normal","Right eye fails to adduct; left eye abducts with nystagmus","Normal"],["Right PPRF or abducens nucleus plus right MLF","Neither eye moves right","Only the left eye abducts, with nystagmus","Usually normal"],["Right CN III","Right eye abducts normally","Right eye fails to adduct, no nystagmus","Right eye fails to adduct; ptosis and dilated pupil"]],"caption":"Convergence and the other cranial nerve signs separate an MLF lesion from a CN III or CN VI palsy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 28-year-old cannot adduct either eye on lateral gaze, each abducting eye shows nystagmus, and both eyes converge normally. Where is the lesion and what disease is most likely?","explain":"Both MLFs are damaged (bilateral INO). Normal convergence shows that both medial recti and oculomotor nuclei work, so the defect is in the internuclear pathway, and bilateral involvement in a young adult points to MS.","check":"The convergence paragraph and the MS paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology predict where nerves are injured. Cranial nerves crowd through shared foramina, so a skull base lesion at the superior orbital fissure or jugular foramen takes out a group, while a brainstem lesion combines a cranial nerve deficit on one side with long tract signs on the other. Limb nerves fail where they press against bone or pass through tunnels, and segmental development of the embryo explains why roots map to dermatomes and myotomes that cross several peripheral nerve territories. That overlap is how root lesions are told apart from nerve lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology, microbiology, and immunology supply the causes. Aneurysms and microvascular ischemia separate pupil-involving from pupil-sparing third nerve palsies, herpes viruses and Borrelia burgdorferi cause facial palsy, varicella zoster reactivates in a single dorsal root ganglion, and immune-mediated demyelination in MS targets the MLF. Bedside localization has limits: dermatome maps differ among sources, single roots overlap, and anomalous innervation is common in the hand, so a pattern that does not fit should prompt a search for more than one lesion."}],"summary":["Each cranial nerve has a fixed brainstem level and skull exit; III, IV, V1, and VI share the superior orbital fissure, and IX, X, and XI share the jugular foramen.","CN III palsy gives a down-and-out eye with ptosis; compression dilates the pupil early, while diabetic ischemia usually spares it.","The jaw (V3) and tongue (XII) deviate toward the lesion; the uvula deviates away from a CN X lesion.","The upper face receives bilateral cortical input, so a UMN lesion spares the forehead and weakens only the contralateral lower face; a CN VII lesion weakens the entire ipsilateral face.","Taste, tearing, and hyperacusis localize a CN VII lesion along its course through the temporal bone.","Erb palsy (C5 to C6) gives the waiter's tip; Klumpke palsy (C8 to T1) gives a claw hand, sometimes with Horner syndrome.","Distal nerves fail at bony sites: axillary at the surgical neck, radial at the midshaft, ulnar at the medial epicondyle, common fibular at the fibular neck.","Superior gluteal nerve injury lets the pelvis drop on the opposite, unsupported side (Trendelenburg sign).","C6 thumb, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L4 knee and medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot; a posterolateral L4 to L5 disc compresses the L5 root.","An MLF lesion impairs adduction of the ipsilateral eye on lateral gaze with nystagmus of the abducting eye and spares convergence; bilateral INO suggests MS."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Summary of cranial nerves"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper limb: brachial plexus and nerve injuries"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower limb: nerves of the gluteal region, thigh, and leg"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: spinal nerves, dermatomes, and herniation of intervertebral discs"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Eye: Central Neurophysiology of Vision, control of eye movements"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles; Central Nervous System, demyelinating diseases"}]},"NE-9":{"unit":"NE-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09-01","title":"The Autonomic Nervous System","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers how the autonomic nervous system reaches smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands: where sympathetic and parasympathetic neurons sit, which transmitter and receptor act at each synapse, and how the receptor's G protein decides the response. It then applies that wiring to three high-yield problems: the oculosympathetic pathway and Horner syndrome, the parasympathetic ganglia of the head, and autonomic dysreflexia after high spinal cord injury. Board questions usually give a set of signs and ask you to locate the lesion, or give a receptor and ask you to predict the effect. Both tasks depend on tracing the two-neuron chain correctly.","prerequisites":["Spinal cord cross-sectional anatomy, including the lateral horn and ventral roots (NE-3)","Cranial nerve nuclei and the course of CN III, VII, IX, and X","G protein-coupled receptor signaling: Gs, Gi, Gq, cAMP, IP3, and DAG","Smooth muscle excitation-contraction coupling through calcium-calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase","The arterial baroreceptor reflex"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiovascular physiology (CV units on pacemaker activity and blood pressure control)","how":"Beta1 and M2 receptors set heart rate and contractility, and sympathetic tone to the splanchnic bed sets much of total peripheral resistance."},{"to":"Endocrine system (adrenal medulla)","how":"Chromaffin cells are modified sympathetic postganglionic neurons whose epinephrine output depends on cortisol-induced PNMT."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy","how":"Cranial parasympathetic fibers travel on branches of the trigeminal nerve, so gland function helps localize lesions of CN VII and IX."},{"to":"Respiratory pathology","how":"A superior sulcus (Pancoast) tumor at the lung apex produces Horner syndrome by compressing the sympathetic chain."},{"to":"Renal and urinary physiology","how":"Bladder filling and emptying depend on beta3 and alpha1 sympathetic and M3 parasympathetic receptors, and bladder distension is the most common trigger of autonomic dysreflexia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Preganglionic neuron","def":"An autonomic neuron with its cell body in the brainstem or spinal cord whose thinly myelinated axon ends in an autonomic ganglion."},{"term":"Postganglionic neuron","def":"An autonomic neuron with its cell body in a ganglion whose unmyelinated axon ends on the target tissue."},{"term":"Intermediolateral cell column","def":"The lateral horn of spinal gray matter from T1 to L2 that contains sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies."},{"term":"White ramus communicans","def":"The connection, present only from T1 to L2, that carries myelinated preganglionic fibers from a spinal nerve into the sympathetic chain."},{"term":"Gray ramus communicans","def":"The connection, present at every spinal level, that returns unmyelinated postganglionic fibers from the chain to a spinal nerve."},{"term":"Splanchnic nerve","def":"A visceral nerve; thoracic and lumbar splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers, while pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) carry parasympathetic fibers."},{"term":"Prevertebral ganglion","def":"A sympathetic ganglion on the front of the abdominal aorta (celiac, aorticorenal, superior and inferior mesenteric) where splanchnic preganglionic fibers synapse."},{"term":"Chromaffin cell","def":"An adrenal medullary cell, derived from neural crest, that acts as a postganglionic neuron and secretes mainly epinephrine into the blood."},{"term":"Ciliospinal center","def":"The group of sympathetic preganglionic neurons at about C8 to T2 whose axons ascend to the superior cervical ganglion to supply the eye."},{"term":"Horner syndrome","def":"Ipsilateral ptosis, miosis, and, depending on the lesion level, facial anhidrosis caused by interruption of the oculosympathetic pathway."},{"term":"Autonomic dysreflexia","def":"Episodic severe hypertension in spinal cord injury at or above T6, caused by an uncontrolled spinal sympathetic reflex to a stimulus below the lesion."},{"term":"Denervation supersensitivity","def":"Increased responsiveness of a target to its transmitter after loss of innervation, partly from receptor upregulation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09-01-C01","obj":"NE-9.1","title":"Sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Autonomic output to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands uses a two-neuron chain. A preganglionic neuron in the brainstem or spinal cord sends a thinly myelinated B fiber to a ganglion outside the central nervous system, where it synapses on a postganglionic neuron whose unmyelinated C fiber reaches the target."},{"type":"h","text":"Sympathetic (thoracolumbar) outflow"},{"type":"p","text":"Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies form the intermediolateral cell column (lateral horn) of segments T1 to L2. Their axons leave in the ventral root and pass through a white ramus communicans into the paravertebral chain. White rami exist only at T1 to L2, because only those segments hold preganglionic cells. In the chain a fiber may synapse at its own level, travel up or down the chain to synapse at another level, or pass straight through as a splanchnic nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibers that synapse in the chain return postganglionic axons through gray rami communicantes to all 31 pairs of spinal nerves, reaching sweat glands, arrector pili, and vessels of the body wall and limbs. Fibers for the head ascend to the superior cervical ganglion, and postganglionic cardiopulmonary nerves from the cervical and upper thoracic ganglia supply the heart and lungs. Abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves (greater, lesser, least, and lumbar) carry preganglionic fibers to the prevertebral ganglia on the aorta. The adrenal medulla is the exception to the two-neuron rule: preganglionic fibers end directly on chromaffin cells, which release hormone into the blood."},{"type":"h","text":"Parasympathetic (craniosacral) outflow"},{"type":"p","text":"Parasympathetic preganglionic cells lie in brainstem nuclei of CN III (Edinger-Westphal nucleus), CN VII (superior salivatory nucleus), CN IX (inferior salivatory nucleus), and CN X (dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, plus cardiac neurons near the nucleus ambiguus), and in the lateral gray matter of S2 to S4. The vagus supplies the thoracic viscera and the gut as far as about the distal third of the transverse colon, the end of the midgut. Pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2 to S4 supply the descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, bladder, and erectile tissue. The ganglia sit beside or within the target, so preganglionic fibers are long and postganglionic fibers short."},{"type":"p","text":"Ganglion position sets the reach of each division. One sympathetic preganglionic fiber diverges onto many postganglionic neurons at several chain levels, and the adrenal medulla adds circulating epinephrine, so sympathetic activation is widespread. Parasympathetic divergence is small, so a pupil can constrict without any change in gut motility. Most viscera receive both divisions, but most blood vessels, sweat glands, arrector pili, and the adrenal medulla receive sympathetic fibers only, so their activity is graded by raising or lowering sympathetic discharge."},{"type":"table","title":"Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions compared","cols":["Feature","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic"],"rows":[["Preganglionic cell bodies","Intermediolateral column, T1 to L2","Nuclei of CN III, VII, IX, X; lateral gray of S2 to S4"],["Ganglia","Paravertebral chain; prevertebral (celiac, aorticorenal, superior and inferior mesenteric)","Head ganglia (ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, otic) or in the organ wall"],["Preganglionic fiber","Short, myelinated (B)","Long, myelinated (B)"],["Postganglionic fiber","Long, unmyelinated (C)","Short, unmyelinated (C)"],["Divergence","High: widespread response","Low: discrete response"],["Route to abdominopelvic viscera","Thoracic and lumbar splanchnic nerves to prevertebral ganglia","Vagus to the midgut end; pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) beyond it"]],"caption":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves are parasympathetic; every other splanchnic nerve is sympathetic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A surgeon cuts one sympathetic chain between the stellate and T2 ganglia. Does hand sweating change, and is the eye affected?","explain":"Hand sweating is largely lost. Upper limb preganglionic fibers leave at about T2 to T6 and must ascend the chain to the stellate and cervical ganglia, whose gray rami join the brachial plexus; the cut blocks that ascent. The eye is usually spared because most oculosympathetic fibers leave at T1, above the cut.","check":"White rami only at T1 to L2; fibers ascend the chain to other levels."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09-01-C02","obj":"NE-9.2","title":"Transmitters, receptors, and G proteins at autonomic synapses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every autonomic ganglion uses the same synapse: preganglionic neurons of both divisions release acetylcholine (ACh) onto neuronal nicotinic receptors (NN), ligand-gated channels permeable to Na+ and K+ whose opening produces a fast excitatory postsynaptic potential. Adrenal chromaffin cells carry the same receptor, which is why they behave like postganglionic neurons. NN receptors differ in subunits from the muscle-type NM receptors, so a ganglionic blocker silences both divisions without paralyzing skeletal muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"At the target, parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors, and most sympathetic postganglionic neurons release norepinephrine (NE) onto adrenergic receptors. Eccrine sweat glands used for heat loss are the main exception: their sympathetic postganglionic fibers release ACh onto M3 receptors, so muscarinic blockade produces hot, dry, flushed skin. The adrenal medulla secretes about 80% epinephrine because its phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PNMT), which methylates NE, is induced by cortisol reaching it through intra-adrenal portal vessels."},{"type":"table","title":"Transmitter and receptor at each synapse","cols":["Pathway","Ganglionic synapse","Target synapse"],"rows":[["Sympathetic to most targets","ACh on NN","NE on alpha or beta receptors"],["Sympathetic to eccrine sweat glands","ACh on NN","ACh on M3"],["Sympathetic to adrenal medulla","None: preganglionic fiber ends on chromaffin cell (ACh on NN)","Epinephrine (about 80%) and NE released into blood"],["Parasympathetic to all targets","ACh on NN","ACh on M2 (heart) or M3 (smooth muscle, glands)"],["Somatic motor, for contrast","No ganglion","ACh on NM"]],"caption":"Every ganglion is cholinergic and nicotinic."},{"type":"p","text":"Adrenergic and muscarinic receptors are G protein-coupled. Gq activates phospholipase C, which cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and diacylglycerol (DAG); IP3 releases Ca2+ from the endoplasmic or sarcoplasmic reticulum and DAG activates protein kinase C. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A (PKA). Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase, and its beta-gamma subunits open K+ channels and inhibit Ca2+ channels."},{"type":"table","title":"Autonomic receptors and their G proteins","cols":["Receptor","G protein","Second messenger","Main effects"],"rows":[["Alpha1","Gq","Increased IP3, DAG, Ca2+","Vasoconstriction, pupil dilation (dilator pupillae), internal urethral sphincter contraction"],["Alpha2","Gi","Decreased cAMP","Less NE release from terminals, less insulin release"],["Beta1","Gs","Increased cAMP","Increased heart rate, contractility, and AV conduction; renin release"],["Beta2","Gs","Increased cAMP","Bronchodilation, skeletal muscle arteriolar dilation, uterine relaxation, glycogenolysis"],["M1","Gq","Increased IP3, DAG, Ca2+","Central nervous system and enteric neurons, gastric secretion"],["M2","Gi","Decreased cAMP; K+ channels open","Slower SA node firing and AV conduction"],["M3","Gq","Increased IP3, DAG, Ca2+","Gland secretion, gut motility, bronchoconstriction, detrusor contraction, miosis, accommodation"]],"caption":"Alpha1 and M3 share Gq; alpha2 and M2 share Gi; beta receptors use Gs."},{"type":"flow","title":"How alpha1 stimulation contracts vascular smooth muscle","steps":["NE binds alpha1 receptor","Gq activates phospholipase C","IP3 releases Ca2+ from sarcoplasmic reticulum","Ca2+-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase","Phosphorylated myosin forms cross-bridges","Arteriole narrows; resistance rises"],"caption":"M3 contraction of bronchial, gut, and detrusor muscle uses the same pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"The same messenger can act in opposite directions. In cardiac muscle, PKA phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels and phospholamban, so beta1 stimulation strengthens contraction; in smooth muscle, PKA phosphorylates and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, so beta2 stimulation relaxes it. In the SA node, M2 beta-gamma subunits open acetylcholine-activated K+ channels and lower cAMP, which flattens phase 4 and slows the heart. Most vessels lack parasympathetic supply, yet ACh dilates them through endothelial M3 receptors that release nitric oxide. Presynaptic alpha2 receptors provide negative feedback on NE release. Acetylcholinesterase ends ACh action; NE is removed mainly by reuptake through the NE transporter and degraded by monoamine oxidase and catechol-O-methyltransferase."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict heart rate, pupil size, and skin temperature after a large dose of atropine, a muscarinic antagonist.","explain":"Heart rate rises because M2 blockade removes vagal tone. The pupil dilates and accommodation fails because sphincter and ciliary muscle M3 receptors are blocked. Skin becomes hot and dry because sympathetic cholinergic sweat fibers act through M3, so sweating stops.","check":"The synapse table and the sweat gland exception."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09-01-C03","obj":"NE-9.3","title":"The oculosympathetic pathway and Horner syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sympathetic fibers to the eye and face supply the dilator pupillae (alpha1), the superior tarsal muscle (smooth muscle attached to the levator palpebrae superioris), and facial sweat glands and arterioles. They travel in a three-neuron chain, and a break at any link removes sympathetic tone on the same side."},{"type":"flow","title":"The three-neuron oculosympathetic pathway","steps":["First order: posterolateral hypothalamus","Descends uncrossed through lateral brainstem and cervical cord","Synapses in ciliospinal center, C8 to T2","Second order: exits mainly at T1, white ramus","Ascends chain over lung apex, through stellate ganglion","Synapses in superior cervical ganglion near carotid bifurcation","Third order: plexus on internal carotid artery","Through cavernous sinus, with V1, to dilator and tarsal muscle"],"caption":"Facial sweat fibers follow the external carotid artery instead."},{"type":"p","text":"Each sign follows from a lost target. Losing the superior tarsal muscle drops the upper lid only 1 to 2 mm, because the levator, supplied by CN III, still works. Loss of the inferior tarsal muscle lets the lower lid rise slightly, narrowing the fissure so the eye looks sunken (apparent enophthalmos). Miosis results from the parasympathetic sphincter acting unopposed; anisocoria is greatest in dim light, when the normal pupil dilates and the affected one lags, yet the affected pupil still constricts briskly to light. In congenital Horner syndrome the affected iris is often lighter, because iris pigmentation needs sympathetic input during development."},{"type":"p","text":"Whether the face sweats depends on the lesion level, which makes anhidrosis a localizing sign. First- and second-order lesions interrupt sudomotor fibers before they branch off at the carotid bifurcation, so the face is dry. Third-order lesions on the internal carotid spare the external carotid fibers, so facial sweating is spared except perhaps over the medial forehead."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing Horner syndrome","cols":["Neuron","Typical causes","Facial anhidrosis","Associated findings"],"rows":[["First order (central)","Lateral medullary infarct, cervical cord lesion such as a syrinx","Present, often over the whole ipsilateral body","Brainstem signs, such as crossed pain and temperature loss"],["Second order (preganglionic)","Pancoast tumor, neck or chest trauma or surgery","Present over the face","Shoulder and medial arm pain, weak intrinsic hand muscles (lower brachial plexus)"],["Third order (postganglionic)","Internal carotid dissection, cavernous sinus lesion","Absent or limited to the medial forehead","Ipsilateral head or neck pain; stroke risk; CN III, IV, V1, VI deficits if cavernous sinus"]],"caption":"Facial sweating separates postganglionic lesions from the others."},{"type":"p","text":"A Pancoast tumor is a carcinoma in the superior pulmonary sulcus at the lung apex. Growing upward, it compresses the stellate ganglion and second-order fibers over the pleural dome and invades the lower trunk of the brachial plexus (C8, T1), causing medial arm pain and wasting of intrinsic hand muscles. In internal carotid dissection, blood enters the arterial wall through an intimal tear, and the expanding hematoma stretches the carotid plexus, giving an acute painful Horner syndrome without facial anhidrosis; narrowing or thrombus can cause stroke."},{"type":"table","title":"Horner syndrome compared with oculomotor (CN III) palsy","cols":["Feature","Horner syndrome","Compressive CN III palsy"],"rows":[["Ptosis","Mild, 1 to 2 mm","Marked, often complete"],["Pupil","Small, reacts to light","Large, sluggish or unreactive"],["Anisocoria","Greater in dim light","Greater in bright light"],["Eye movements","Normal","Down and out; diplopia"]],"caption":"In Horner syndrome the smaller pupil is abnormal; in CN III palsy the larger one is."},{"type":"example","title":"Locating a Horner lesion from the history","text":"A 52-year-old smoker has 2 mm right ptosis, a right pupil 2 mm smaller than the left in dim light, a dry right face, and inner right arm pain with weak finger abduction. Step 1: mild ptosis with a small reactive pupil means Horner syndrome. Step 2: the dry face places the lesion before the carotid bifurcation (first or second order). Step 3: no brainstem signs, but medial arm pain and weak interossei implicate the lower trunk (C8, T1), which lies beside second-order fibers at the lung apex. Conclusion: a second-order lesion, likely a Pancoast tumor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09-01-C04","obj":"NE-9.4","title":"Parasympathetic ganglia of the head","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cranial parasympathetic preganglionic fibers leave the brainstem in CN III, VII, and IX and synapse in four ganglia. The postganglionic fibers then ride on branches of the trigeminal nerve to reach their targets. Sympathetic and sensory fibers also pass through these ganglia, but only the parasympathetic fibers synapse there. Learning each route as nucleus, nerve, ganglion, carrier branch, and target makes lesion questions predictable."},{"type":"table","title":"Cranial parasympathetic ganglia","cols":["Ganglion","Preganglionic nucleus and nerve","Preganglionic route","Postganglionic route","Target and effect"],"rows":[["Ciliary (orbit, lateral to optic nerve)","Edinger-Westphal nucleus, CN III","Inferior division of CN III (branch to inferior oblique)","Short ciliary nerves","Sphincter pupillae (miosis) and ciliary muscle (accommodation)"],["Pterygopalatine (pterygopalatine fossa)","Superior salivatory nucleus, CN VII","Greater petrosal nerve, then nerve of the pterygoid canal","Branches of V2; zygomatic branch to the lacrimal nerve","Lacrimal gland (tears); nasal, palatine, and pharyngeal glands"],["Submandibular (hanging from lingual nerve)","Superior salivatory nucleus, CN VII","Chorda tympani, joining the lingual nerve (V3)","Directly to the submandibular gland; via the lingual nerve to the sublingual gland","Submandibular and sublingual glands (saliva)"],["Otic (below foramen ovale, medial to V3)","Inferior salivatory nucleus, CN IX","Tympanic nerve, tympanic plexus, lesser petrosal nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Parotid gland (saliva)"]],"caption":"CN VII serves two ganglia (tears and two salivary glands); CN IX serves only the parotid."},{"type":"p","text":"At the ciliary ganglion the targets act through M3 receptors. Contraction of the circular sphincter pupillae constricts the pupil. Contraction of the ciliary muscle moves the ciliary body inward, which slackens the zonular fibers and lets the elastic lens round up for near vision. In the pupillary light reflex, optic nerve fibers project to both pretectal nuclei, and each pretectal nucleus projects to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, so light in one eye constricts both pupils (direct and consensual responses)."},{"type":"p","text":"Gland function localizes facial nerve lesions along the canal. The greater petrosal nerve leaves at the geniculate ganglion, the chorda tympani leaves lower in the facial canal, and only motor fibers remain beyond the stylomastoid foramen. The parotid is never affected by a facial nerve lesion, even though the nerve runs through the gland, because its secretomotor supply comes from CN IX."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a facial nerve lesion by tears, saliva, and taste","text":"Three patients have complete right facial weakness. Patient A also has a dry right eye, loss of taste on the anterior two thirds of the right tongue, and reduced submandibular flow: the lesion is at or proximal to the geniculate ganglion, before the greater petrosal nerve leaves, as with a tumor in the internal acoustic meatus. Patient B tears normally but has lost taste and submandibular flow: the lesion lies in the facial canal after the greater petrosal nerve leaves but before the chorda tympani leaves. Patient C has weakness alone: the lesion is at or beyond the stylomastoid foramen, as with a parotid tumor. Parotid flow is normal in all three."},{"type":"p","text":"Misdirected regrowth of cholinergic fibers explains three classic syndromes. In Frey syndrome, after parotid surgery, cut postganglionic parasympathetic fibers in the auriculotemporal nerve regrow into the sympathetic cholinergic sweat glands of the overlying skin; both systems use ACh on muscarinic receptors, so eating causes sweating and flushing over the cheek. In crocodile tears, after a proximal facial nerve injury, salivary fibers regrow along the greater petrosal pathway, so eating causes tearing. In Adie tonic pupil, damage to the ciliary ganglion, often after a viral illness, leaves a dilated pupil with a poor light response; regenerating fibers meant for the ciliary muscle reach the sphincter, so the pupil constricts slowly during sustained near focus (light-near dissociation), and the denervated sphincter becomes supersensitive to a dilute cholinergic agonist."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-09-01-C05","obj":"NE-9.5","title":"Autonomic dysreflexia after spinal cord injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Autonomic dysreflexia is a sudden, potentially dangerous rise in blood pressure in a person with spinal cord injury at or above T6, triggered by a stimulus below the lesion. It usually appears after spinal shock resolves and segmental reflexes return, weeks to months after injury. Resting systolic pressure in these patients is often low, about 90 to 110 mmHg, because tonic sympathetic drive from the brainstem is lost, so a rise of 20 mmHg or more above baseline is significant, and pressures above 200 mmHg can occur. Bladder distension is the most common trigger; bowel impaction, pressure injuries, ingrown toenails, and labor are others."},{"type":"p","text":"The T6 level matters because of the splanchnic circulation. The greater splanchnic nerve (about T5 to T9) and the lesser and least splanchnic nerves control the splanchnic vascular bed, the largest pool of resistance and capacitance vessels in the body. With a lesion at or above T6, most of that outflow lies below the injury and is cut off from brainstem control. With a lower lesion, the brainstem still governs splanchnic outflow and can dilate that bed to offset vasoconstriction in the legs."},{"type":"flow","title":"From a full bladder to a hypertensive crisis","steps":["Bladder distends below the lesion","Visceral afferents enter cord below the injury","Segmental sympathetic preganglionic neurons fire massively","Splanchnic and lower-body vessels constrict","Blood pressure rises sharply","Baroreceptors signal the nucleus tractus solitarius","Vagal outflow slows the heart","Descending inhibition blocked at the lesion; pressure stays high"],"caption":"The reflex continues until the stimulus is removed, because the brainstem can act only above the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"Below the lesion, isolated cord segments still respond to segmental afferent input. Without descending inhibition the reflex is exaggerated, and changes after injury add to it: sprouting of primary afferent terminals in the cord, and increased sensitivity of denervated vessels to alpha1 stimulation. The carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) baroreceptors detect the rise. The brainstem responds in the two ways still open to it: vagal slowing of the heart, which works because the vagus leaves the brainstem and never passes through the injured cord, and withdrawal of sympathetic tone above the lesion. Neither can reach the constricted vessels below, so the pressure stays high."},{"type":"p","text":"The signs divide at the lesion. Above it, vasodilation produces flushing, nasal congestion, and sweating, and the pressure surge with cerebral vasodilation produces a pounding headache and sometimes blurred vision. Below it, sympathetic discharge produces pale, cool skin and piloerection. Bradycardia is typical but not universal. Uncontrolled episodes can cause intracerebral hemorrhage, seizures, myocardial ischemia, and pulmonary edema."},{"type":"table","title":"Autonomic dysreflexia compared with neurogenic shock","cols":["Feature","Autonomic dysreflexia","Neurogenic shock"],"rows":[["Timing","After spinal shock resolves (weeks to months)","Hours to days after acute injury above about T6"],["Mechanism","Uncontrolled spinal sympathetic reflex below the lesion","Loss of sympathetic tone with intact vagal tone"],["Blood pressure","Sharply raised, episodic","Low, sustained"],["Heart rate","Reflex bradycardia (baroreceptor)","Bradycardia (unopposed vagus)"],["Skin below lesion","Pale, cool, piloerection","Warm, dry, vasodilated"]],"caption":"Both need a high lesion and both may show bradycardia, but the blood pressure moves in opposite directions."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients with complete injuries, one at C6 and one at T10, each develop a distended bladder. Which one becomes severely hypertensive, and why?","explain":"The C6 patient. Below C6 the whole splanchnic outflow is cut off from the brainstem, so the reflex vasoconstriction is unopposed. At T10, splanchnic outflow from about T5 to T9 remains under brainstem control, so baroreflex withdrawal of sympathetic tone dilates the splanchnic bed and limits the rise.","check":"The paragraph on why the T6 level matters."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the wiring. Sympathetic preganglionic neurons are confined to T1 to L2, so any target outside those levels, including the eye, depends on fibers that travel through the chain, which is why a lung apex tumor or a neck injury can disturb the pupil. Neural crest gives rise to the chain ganglia, the parasympathetic ganglia, and the adrenal chromaffin cells, so a chromaffin cell behaves like a postganglionic neuron. Cranial parasympathetic fibers borrow trigeminal branches, which turns gland function into a map for localizing lesions of CN VII and IX."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology follow the receptors. One transmitter can contract or relax a tissue depending on whether Gq, Gs, or Gi carries the signal, and the same receptor logic predicts the effects of toxins and drugs used to probe the system. Horner syndrome and autonomic dysreflexia are both disorders of interrupted descending sympathetic control, with opposite results: loss of tone at a single target in one, and an unrestrained segmental reflex in the other. The limits are that localization by signs is probabilistic, not every patient shows every sign, and reference values such as the dysreflexia threshold vary between sources."}],"summary":["Sympathetic preganglionic neurons lie in the T1 to L2 lateral horn and synapse in chain or prevertebral ganglia; parasympathetic preganglionic neurons lie in CN III, VII, IX, X nuclei and S2 to S4 and synapse near the target.","White rami (T1 to L2) carry preganglionic fibers into the chain; gray rami return postganglionic fibers to every spinal nerve.","All autonomic ganglia use ACh on NN receptors; sympathetic targets use NE except sweat glands (ACh on M3), and the adrenal medulla secretes mostly epinephrine.","Alpha1 and M3 use Gq to raise Ca2+; beta receptors use Gs to raise cAMP; alpha2 and M2 use Gi to lower cAMP, and M2 also opens K+ channels in the SA node.","Horner syndrome is ipsilateral mild ptosis and miosis greatest in dim light, from a break in the three-neuron hypothalamus to C8 to T2 to superior cervical ganglion to carotid plexus pathway.","Facial anhidrosis marks a first- or second-order lesion; a third-order lesion such as carotid dissection spares facial sweating and is painful.","A Pancoast tumor causes a second-order Horner syndrome with lower brachial plexus pain and hand weakness.","Ciliary (III) controls pupil and lens, pterygopalatine (VII) tears, submandibular (VII) submandibular and sublingual saliva, and otic (IX) parotid saliva.","Autonomic dysreflexia needs a lesion at or above T6, so the splanchnic bed escapes brainstem control; a stimulus below the lesion causes severe hypertension with baroreflex bradycardia and flushing above the lesion."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Autonomic Nervous System and the Adrenal Medulla"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The autonomic nervous system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Overview of the autonomic nervous system; head, parasympathetic ganglia and the orbit"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Conversion of amino acids to specialized products, catecholamine synthesis"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]}});
